/* Question bank for lab/java-backend-guide.html. One object per question; level is Basic or Advanced. */
window.JAVA_GUIDE = [{"id":1,"category":"Java Core","question":"Two `Money` objects print identically, yet `a == b` is false. Explain why, and when `equals()` would return true.","level":"Basic","tldr":"For primitives `==` checks the value and for objects it checks identity (same instance), whereas `equals()` asks whether two instances mean the same thing as the class defines it; a value type that overrides one of `equals` and `hashCode` must override the other.","answer":["For primitives, `==` compares values (with numeric promotion, so `1 == 1.0` is true). For references, `==` asks whether both variables point to the same object. `equals()` is a method: `Object.equals` defaults to identity, while `String`, `Integer`, `LocalDate`, `UUID` and every record override it to compare content. Two distinct `String` objects with the same characters are therefore `equals` but usually not `==`.","The nasty production bugs come from `==` appearing to work. String literals are interned, so `==` passes in unit tests but fails for strings read from JSON or the database. Autoboxing caches `Integer`, `Long` and friends in the range -128 to 127, so comparing two boxed `Long` ids with `==` works for small test ids and fails for real ones. And `==` between a wrapper and a primitive unboxes, throwing `NullPointerException` when the wrapper is null.","When you define a value type (`Money`, `AccountId`, an order line key) you override `equals` and `hashCode` together, because hash-based collections use `hashCode` to find the bucket and `equals` to confirm the match; records do this for you. Use `==` deliberately only for identity, primitives and enums (where it is null-safe and preferred), and `Objects.equals(a, b)` for null-safe logical comparison."],"keyPoints":["identity comparison asks whether two references point to one instance","the default `Object.equals` is identity, so value classes must override it","hash-based collections need a matching `hashCode` for equal instances","records generate correct value equality without hand-written code"],"examples":[{"title":"Boxed ids, strings and BigDecimal: == passes in tests, fails in production","lang":"java","code":"Long testId = 42L;\nLong sameTestId = 42L;\nSystem.out.println(testId == sameTestId);        // true: both come from the Long cache\n\nLong orderId = 1_000_000L;\nLong lookedUpId = 1_000_000L;\nSystem.out.println(orderId == lookedUpId);       // false: two objects (BAD)\nSystem.out.println(orderId.equals(lookedUpId));  // true (GOOD)\n\nString status = new String(\"SETTLED\");           // like a value read from a DB row\nSystem.out.println(status == \"SETTLED\");         // false: different objects (BAD)\nSystem.out.println(\"SETTLED\".equals(status));    // true, and null-safe in this order (GOOD)\n\nBigDecimal a = new BigDecimal(\"10.0\");\nBigDecimal b = new BigDecimal(\"10.00\");\nSystem.out.println(a.equals(b));                 // false: equals includes scale\nSystem.out.println(a.compareTo(b) == 0);         // true: numeric comparison\n\nSystem.out.println(Long.valueOf(7).equals(7));   // false: 7 autoboxes to Integer","note":"Every line compiles and most would pass a test with small ids and literals. The last two lines are why money and id comparisons need `compareTo` and consistent id types."},{"title":"equals without hashCode breaks HashSet","lang":"java","code":"final class Money {\n    private final BigDecimal amount;\n    private final String currency;\n\n    Money(BigDecimal amount, String currency) {\n        this.amount = amount;\n        this.currency = currency;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        return o instanceof Money m\n                && amount.compareTo(m.amount) == 0\n                && currency.equals(m.currency);\n    }\n    // BAD: hashCode not overridden, equal Money objects get identity hash codes\n    // FIX: return Objects.hash(amount.stripTrailingZeros(), currency);\n}\n\nclass MoneyDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<Money> seen = new HashSet<>();\n        seen.add(new Money(new BigDecimal(\"9.99\"), \"SEK\"));\n        System.out.println(seen.contains(new Money(new BigDecimal(\"9.99\"), \"SEK\"))); // almost always false\n        seen.add(new Money(new BigDecimal(\"9.99\"), \"SEK\"));\n        System.out.println(seen.size()); // 2: logical duplicate stored\n    }\n}","note":"The fix hashes `amount.stripTrailingZeros()`, not `amount`, because `equals` uses `compareTo` and treats 9.99 and 9.990 as equal while `BigDecimal.hashCode` does not; hashing must follow whatever notion of equality `equals` uses."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does `Integer.valueOf(127) == Integer.valueOf(127)` return true while the same check with 128 usually fails?","a":"`Integer.valueOf` and autoboxing return cached instances for values from `-128` to `127`, so small boxed values share an instance and `==` happens to succeed. Above that range each boxing normally creates a new object, so `==` compares two different references and returns false (the upper bound can be raised with `-XX:AutoBoxCacheMax`). Code that passes tests with small IDs and breaks in production with large ones is the classic symptom; compare boxed numbers with `equals` or unbox them first."},{"q":"A class overrides `equals` but keeps the inherited `hashCode`. What breaks when you put instances in a `HashSet`?","a":"Equal instances keep identity-based hash codes, so they nearly always land in different buckets and `equals` is never even consulted. `contains` with an equal but distinct instance returns false, and adding it again makes `size()` grow to 2. Implement `hashCode` from the same fields as `equals`, or make the class a record."}],"extra":["**Integer cache is tunable:** the upper bound of the `Integer` cache can be raised with `-XX:AutoBoxCacheMax`, so `==` on boxed values can even behave differently between environments; never rely on it.","**Enums and ==:** for enums, `==` is preferred: there is exactly one instance per constant, it is null-safe, and comparing an enum with an unrelated type fails at compile time instead of quietly returning false.","**Cross-type equals:** `Long.valueOf(7).equals(7)` is false, so `map.get(intId)` on a `Map<Long, Order>` silently returns `null`; keep id types consistent or wrap them in records like `OrderId`.","**BigDecimal in value objects:** `equals` is scale-sensitive (10.0 versus 10.00), which bites money comparisons and `HashSet<BigDecimal>`; compare with `compareTo` or normalize the scale once in the value object's constructor.","**Objects.equals:** it handles nulls on both sides and is the right building block for comparing fields inside your own `equals` implementations."],"redFlags":["uses `==` on `String` or `Integer` values and relies on caching or interning","calls `a.equals(b)` without considering that `a` might be null","cannot say what `equals` does when a class never overrides it"]},{"id":2,"category":"Java Core","question":"After a deploy, a `HashSet<CustomerKey>` starts holding duplicates. Which rules of `equals` and `hashCode` might the key class break?","level":"Basic","tldr":"If `a.equals(b)` then `a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()` must hold; unequal objects may collide, but breaking the rule, or changing hashed fields after insertion, makes hash collections miss entries and store duplicates.","answer":["The contract has two halves. `equals` must be reflexive (`x.equals(x)`), symmetric (`x.equals(y)` exactly when `y.equals(x)`), transitive, consistent across repeated calls while the objects do not change, and return false for `null`. `hashCode` must stay consistent while the fields used by `equals` do not change, and equal objects must return equal hash codes. The converse does not hold: two objects that are not equal are allowed to collide on the same hash code, and that only costs performance.","To see why it matters, follow a `HashMap` lookup: hash the key, pick a bucket, compare the stored hash, then call `equals` on candidates in that bucket. If equal objects produce different hashes, the lookup goes to the wrong bucket and `equals` is never called: `get` returns `null`, `HashSet.add` accepts a duplicate and `remove` does nothing. If `equals` is not symmetric or transitive, results depend on which object is the argument and on insertion order, producing flaky, data-dependent bugs.","Practical rules: compute both methods from the same immutable fields; prefer `final` classes or records so subclasses cannot break symmetry; use `instanceof` pattern matching for the type check (or `getClass()` when subclasses must never equal their parent); and never mutate a field that feeds `hashCode` while the object sits in a hash-based collection, because it becomes unreachable in its old bucket."],"keyPoints":["a lookup filters by hash first and only calls `equals` inside the matching bucket","breaking the rule makes equal keys miss each other, so sets keep duplicates","collisions between unequal keys are legal and only cost lookup time","hash only fields that never change while the object sits in a collection"],"examples":[{"title":"Broken symmetry versus a normalized record","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: tries to be equal to plain Strings, which breaks symmetry\nfinal class CurrencyCode {\n    private final String code;\n\n    CurrencyCode(String code) { this.code = code; }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (o instanceof CurrencyCode c) return code.equalsIgnoreCase(c.code);\n        if (o instanceof String s) return code.equalsIgnoreCase(s); // one-way equality\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int hashCode() { return code.toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT).hashCode(); }\n}\n\n// new CurrencyCode(\"sek\").equals(\"SEK\")  is true\n// \"SEK\".equals(new CurrencyCode(\"sek\"))  is false: symmetry broken, so\n// list.contains(x) and set lookups depend on which side calls equals\n\n// GOOD: normalize once, compare only with the same type\nrecord CurrencyId(String code) {\n    CurrencyId {\n        code = code.toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT);\n    }\n}","note":"Trying to be clever across types is the most common way to break symmetry. Normalizing in a compact constructor gives the same case-insensitive behavior with generated, contract-safe `equals` and `hashCode`."},{"title":"Mutating a hashed field after insertion","lang":"java","code":"class Customer {\n    private String email; // BAD: mutable and used in equals and hashCode\n\n    Customer(String email) { this.email = email; }\n    void changeEmail(String email) { this.email = email; }\n\n    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        return o instanceof Customer c && email.equals(c.email);\n    }\n    @Override public int hashCode() { return email.hashCode(); }\n}\n\nclass NotificationDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<Customer> notified = new HashSet<>();\n        Customer c = new Customer(\"ana@example.com\");\n        notified.add(c);\n        c.changeEmail(\"ana.b@example.com\");\n        System.out.println(notified.contains(c)); // false: searched under the new hash\n        System.out.println(notified.add(c));      // true: same object stored twice\n        System.out.println(notified.size());      // 2\n    }\n}","note":"`remove(c)` now only removes the copy under the new hash; the old one leaks forever. Base equality on immutable identity (a customer id), not on editable attributes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why do `\"Aa\"` and `\"BB\"` end up in the same `HashMap` bucket, and is that a problem?","a":"Both strings have hash code 2112, so they collide; with only about 4 billion `int` values for an unlimited number of objects, collisions are unavoidable. The contract only demands equal hashes for equal objects, so this is legal: the map keeps both in one bucket and tells them apart with `equals`. It only hurts when collisions are common, for example with attacker-chosen keys, which is one reason long buckets turn into trees."},{"q":"Should `hashCode` include a field like `lastLoginAt` that changes during the object's life?","a":"Not if instances live in hash collections or serve as map keys. Once the field changes, the object sits in the bucket of its old hash, so `contains` and `remove` miss it while `size()` and iteration still count it, and a later `add` stores it twice. Base equality and hashing on immutable identifying fields, or remove the object, change it and reinsert it."}],"extra":["**Consistency with compareTo:** `TreeSet` and `TreeMap` use `compareTo`, not `equals`, so `BigDecimal` values 1.0 and 1.00 are two elements in a `HashSet` but one in a `TreeSet`; keep `compareTo` consistent with `equals` or document the difference.","**Records and arrays:** a record's generated `equals` compares array components by reference, so `record Batch(byte[] payload)` is almost never equal to another instance; use a `List`, or override both methods with `Arrays.equals` and `Arrays.hashCode`.","**Inheritance trap:** a subclass that adds a field to `equals` cannot keep both symmetry and transitivity with a parent that uses `instanceof`; prefer composition over inheritance for value types.","**Cost of Objects.hash:** `Objects.hash(a, b, c)` allocates a varargs array per call; that is fine in general, but for keys hashed on very hot paths a hand-written `31 * h + field.hashCode()` avoids relying on escape analysis.","**Test it once, properly:** the EqualsVerifier library checks symmetry, transitivity, null handling and hashCode consistency in a single assertion and catches most hand-written mistakes."],"redFlags":["derives `hashCode` from a field that `equals` ignores","writes an `equals` that accepts subclasses on one side only, so symmetry breaks"]},{"id":3,"category":"Java Core","question":"String vs StringBuilder vs StringBuffer, and how does the string pool work?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`String` is immutable and safe to share; `StringBuilder` is a mutable, unsynchronized buffer for building text within one thread; `StringBuffer` is the legacy synchronized version you rarely need. The string pool keeps one canonical instance per literal and compile-time constant, and `intern()` adds runtime strings to it, but it is never a reason to compare strings with `==`.","answer":["Use `String` for values, `StringBuilder` for assembling text step by step, and `StringBuffer` essentially never. Its `synchronized` methods make each single call atomic, but builders are almost always local to one method, and a sequence of appends from several threads still interleaves. All three implement `CharSequence`. A builder grows an internal array (default capacity 16, roughly doubling when full), so presizing with `new StringBuilder(expectedLength)` avoids repeated copying when building large payloads. Concatenation with `+` inside a single expression is fine, because `javac` compiles it to an `invokedynamic` call to `StringConcatFactory`; builders matter in loops and incremental assembly.","The string pool (the JVM's string table) holds canonical `String` instances. Every string literal and every compile-time constant expression resolves to the pooled instance, so `\"SEK\" == \"SEK\"` is true even across classes. The compiler folds constant expressions: `PREFIX + \"ment\"` with `static final String PREFIX = \"pay\"` becomes the literal `\"payment\"`, while the same concatenation with a non-final variable creates a new object at run time. `new String(\"SEK\")` always creates a separate object (the literal itself is pooled), and `s.intern()` returns the pooled instance with `s`'s content, adding one if absent. Since Java 7 the pool lives in the ordinary heap, so pooled strings are garbage collected when nothing references them.","In production the pool matters less than people expect. Never use `==` on strings: values from JSON, JDBC or Kafka are never the pooled instances. Interning runtime data is a niche memory optimization for very many long-lived copies of a small set of values (currency codes, country codes, statuses in millions of cached rows), and it has costs: a JVM-wide hash table (initial size set by `-XX:StringTableSize`), a lookup on every call, and the risk of filling it with high-cardinality data such as ids. A bounded canonicalizing map, an enum, or `-XX:+UseStringDeduplication` (which shares backing arrays, not the objects) is usually the better tool. `jcmd <pid> VM.stringtable` shows how large the pool really is."],"keyPoints":["String is immutable, StringBuilder is a fast local buffer, StringBuffer is synchronized legacy","literals and compile-time constants are pooled, runtime strings are not","intern is a niche memory optimization, never a reason to use =="],"examples":[{"title":"What is pooled and what is not","lang":"java","code":"class StringPoolDemo {\n    static final String PREFIX = \"pay\";                // compile-time constant\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String literal = \"payment\";                    // pooled\n        String folded = PREFIX + \"ment\";               // folded by javac into \"payment\"\n        String prefix = loadPrefix();                  // runtime value, like a config read\n        String built = prefix + \"ment\";                // new object at run time\n        String copy = new String(\"payment\");           // always a new object\n\n        System.out.println(literal == folded);         // true: same pooled instance\n        System.out.println(literal == built);          // false\n        System.out.println(literal == copy);           // false\n        System.out.println(literal == built.intern()); // true: intern returns the pooled one\n        System.out.println(literal.equals(built));     // true: the comparison to use\n\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        System.out.println(sb.capacity());             // 16: grows by copying when full\n    }\n\n    static String loadPrefix() {\n        return System.getProperty(\"payments.prefix\", \"pay\");\n    }\n}","note":"`folded` is pooled only because `PREFIX` is a `static final` constant initialized with a literal; remove `final` and it behaves like `built`. Tests full of literals therefore hide `==` bugs that production data exposes."},{"title":"Building text: presize, keep it local, beware the char constructor","lang":"java","code":"@Component\npublic class StatementFormatter {\n\n    // GOOD: local builder, presized, one toString at the end\n    public String format(List<LedgerEntry> entries) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(entries.size() * 64);\n        for (LedgerEntry e : entries) {\n            sb.append(e.bookingDate()).append(';')\n              .append(e.reference()).append(';')\n              .append(e.amount().toPlainString()).append('\\n');\n        }\n        return sb.toString();\n    }\n\n    // BAD: 'P' widens to int, so this is new StringBuilder(80): an empty buffer\n    public String header() {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder('P');\n        sb.append(\"AYMENT STATEMENT\");\n        return sb.toString();                     // \"AYMENT STATEMENT\"\n    }\n\n    // BAD: one buffer shared by all requests in a singleton bean\n    private final StringBuilder shared = new StringBuilder();\n\n    public String summary(Account account) {\n        shared.setLength(0);\n        shared.append(account.id()).append(\": \").append(account.balance());\n        return shared.toString();                 // races under concurrent requests\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: a single expression needs no buffer at all\n    public String summaryFixed(Account account) {\n        return account.id() + \": \" + account.balance();\n    }\n}","note":"Switching `shared` to a `StringBuffer` would make each `append` atomic, but two requests could still interleave between `setLength(0)` and `toString()`; the fix is not sharing the buffer. The char constructor trap compiles silently because `char` widens to the `int` capacity parameter."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How many objects does new String(\"SEK\") create?","a":"Each execution creates one new `String` object, which in current JDKs shares the literal's internal byte array rather than copying it. The literal `\"SEK\"` is a pooled instance created or found once, when that literal is first resolved, not on every call. So the precise answer is one new object per execution plus the pooled literal once, and the explicit constructor is almost always pointless."},{"q":"When would you actually call intern(), and what are the risks?","a":"Only when a heap dump or profiler shows many long-lived duplicate strings and the set of distinct values is small and bounded, for example millions of cached rows each holding one of a few currency codes. The risks are a JVM-wide table that keeps growing with high-cardinality input such as user or request ids, a hash lookup on every call, and the false impression that `==` is now safe. A bounded canonicalizing map, an enum, or JVM string deduplication usually delivers the saving with fewer surprises."}],"extra":["**Text blocks are constants:** a text block is a compile-time constant like any other literal, so SQL or JSON templates written as text blocks are pooled and cost nothing per call.","**switch on strings:** a `switch` over a `String` compiles to a switch on `hashCode()` followed by `equals` checks, so it is safe with runtime values and does not depend on interning.","**Concatenation in logs:** `log.debug(\"Order \" + id + \" failed\")` builds the string even when debug logging is off; parameterized logging (`log.debug(\"Order {} failed\", id)`) formats only when the level is enabled.","**Do not cache builders:** keeping a `StringBuilder` in a field or a `ThreadLocal` to save allocations creates races or leaks; short-lived allocations are cheap for modern collectors.","**Locale-safe normalization:** `toUpperCase()` without an argument uses the default locale, which turns `\"i\"` into a dotted capital I under a Turkish locale; use `toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT)` when normalizing codes and keys."],"redFlags":["Recommends StringBuffer for ordinary string building","compares strings with == because literals are pooled","suggests interning every runtime string to save memory"]},{"id":4,"category":"Java Core","question":"If `String` were mutable, what would break in a `HashMap` key, the string pool, or a permission check? Explain the design.","level":"Basic","tldr":"`String` is immutable (final class, private final internal array, no mutators), which makes it thread-safe, poolable, a stable hash key and safe to pass through security checks; the price is a new object on every modification.","answer":["`String` is a `final` class whose content lives in a private `final byte[]` (compact strings since Java 9, Latin-1 or UTF-16) that is never exposed or modified. Methods like `concat`, `replace` and `toUpperCase` return new strings. The class being `final` matters too: no subclass can add mutable behavior while posing as a `String`.","The benefits are concrete. Thread safety: strings can be shared across threads without synchronization, and final fields give safe publication. Pooling: literals and `intern()` share one instance per value, which is only safe because nobody can change it. Stable hashing: the hash is computed once, cached in the object and can never go stale, which is why `String` is the ideal `HashMap` key. Security: a file path, class name, JDBC URL or permission string validated by one component cannot be altered by the caller afterwards, closing time-of-check versus time-of-use holes. Sharing: APIs can store or return strings without defensive copies.","The cost: every modification allocates. `s += x` in a loop copies the entire accumulated string on each iteration, which is quadratic time and a lot of garbage when building CSV exports or log payloads. Use `StringBuilder`, `String.join` or `Collectors.joining` for loops; a single-expression concatenation is fine because since Java 9 `javac` compiles `+` to an optimized `invokedynamic` call. And remember that a `final String` variable only fixes the reference; immutability comes from the `String` class itself."],"keyPoints":["a final class with a private array and no mutators keeps contents fixed","shared instances need no copies or locks across threads","the cached hash stays valid for the object's whole life","repeated concatenation in a loop allocates new strings, so use a builder"],"examples":[{"title":"Building a settlement CSV","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: each += copies everything built so far (quadratic for large batches)\nString csvBad = \"\";\nfor (Settlement s : settlements) {\n    csvBad += s.merchantId() + \";\" + s.amount() + \";\" + s.currency() + \"\\n\";\n}\n\n// GOOD: one growable buffer, presized\nStringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(settlements.size() * 48);\nfor (Settlement s : settlements) {\n    sb.append(s.merchantId()).append(';')\n      .append(s.amount().toPlainString()).append(';')\n      .append(s.currency()).append('\\n');\n}\nString csv = sb.toString();\n\n// GOOD: declarative version for simple joins\nString csv2 = settlements.stream()\n        .map(s -> String.join(\";\", s.merchantId(), s.amount().toPlainString(), s.currency()))\n        .collect(Collectors.joining(\"\\n\"));","note":"With 100k settlements the BAD loop copies gigabytes of characters in total; the builder appends in place and copies only when it grows."},{"title":"final reference versus immutable object","lang":"java","code":"final StringBuilder note = new StringBuilder(\"PENDING\");\nnote.append(\"_REVIEW\");                 // allowed: final only blocks reassignment\n// note = new StringBuilder();          // compile error\n\nString status = \"PENDING\";\nstatus.concat(\"_REVIEW\");               // BAD: result discarded, status is still \"PENDING\"\nstatus = status.concat(\"_REVIEW\");      // GOOD: rebind the variable to the new String\n\nMap<String, Integer> retries = new HashMap<>();\nString key = \"pay-1001\";\nretries.put(key, 1);\n// No code path can change the characters of key, so its cached hash and bucket stay valid","note":"A final reference to a mutable object is still mutable, and an immutable object behind a non-final variable is still immutable: the two concepts are independent."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is a `char[]` sometimes preferred over a `String` for passwords?","a":"A `String` cannot be wiped: its characters stay in the heap until the garbage collector reclaims the object, and can show up in a heap dump meanwhile. A `char[]` can be overwritten with zeros right after use, which is why `Console.readPassword()` and `JPasswordField.getPassword()` return arrays. It narrows the exposure window rather than closing it, since the GC may already have copied the array during compaction."},{"q":"A loop builds a large report with `+=` on a `String`. What happens, and what would you use?","a":"Each `+=` creates a new `String` and copies everything accumulated so far, so building n pieces costs roughly quadratic copying plus a pile of short-lived garbage. The compiler cannot merge loop iterations into one buffer, so declare a `StringBuilder` once before the loop (or use `StringJoiner`, `String.join` or `Collectors.joining`). Prefer `StringBuilder` over `StringBuffer`, whose synchronization buys nothing for a method-local buffer."}],"extra":["**Compact strings:** since Java 9 (JEP 254), Latin-1 strings use one byte per character instead of two, roughly halving heap for ASCII-heavy data such as ids, JSON keys and log messages.","**Hash caching detail:** the hash is computed lazily on the first `hashCode()` call and stored in a field; Java 13 added a flag so strings whose hash really is 0 are not recomputed on every call.","**Passwords in char[]:** a `String` cannot be wiped and may linger in the heap or a heap dump until GC, which is why security APIs such as `KeyStore.load` take a `char[]` that you can zero after use.","**substring no longer leaks:** since Java 7u6, `substring` copies the characters instead of sharing the parent's array, so keeping a small substring of a huge payload no longer pins the whole payload in memory.","**String deduplication:** `-XX:+UseStringDeduplication` lets the GC make equal strings share one backing array, which can noticeably cut heap in services that hold many duplicate values (currency codes, statuses) loaded from databases."],"redFlags":["thinks `s.concat(x)` or `s.toUpperCase()` changes `s` in place","uses `+=` inside a large loop and assumes the compiler makes it free"]},{"id":5,"category":"Java Core","question":"Is Java pass-by-value or pass-by-reference? Show what happens when a method reassigns or mutates a parameter.","level":"Basic","tldr":"Java is always pass-by-value. For objects the value that gets copied is the reference, so a method can mutate the object the caller also sees, but reassigning the parameter only changes the method's own copy and never the caller's variable.","answer":["Every argument is copied into its parameter. For a primitive the copy is the number itself; for an object it is the reference, a pointer-like value that identifies the object on the heap. Inside the method there are now two variables, the caller's and the parameter, pointing at the same object. Calling `order.addLine(...)` or `lines.clear()` through the parameter changes that shared object, so the caller sees the change. Assigning `order = new Order()` only repoints the parameter; the caller's variable still refers to the original. True pass-by-reference (C++ `&`, C# `ref`) would let the method rebind the caller's variable, and Java has no way to do that, which is why a general `swap(a, b)` method cannot be written.","The phrase \"objects are passed by reference\" is the usual source of confusion, and the precise wording matters in an interview: references are passed by value. Immutable types such as `String`, `BigDecimal`, `Instant` and records with immutable components behave as if they were passed by value, because nothing can change them; `total.add(fee)` returns a new object and the caller's `total` is untouched unless you return the result and the caller reassigns it. Arrays and collections are the opposite: they are mutable objects, so a method that sorts, clears or fills a list it was given changes the caller's data. Varargs follow the same rule: when the caller passes an existing array, the method receives that very array.","In production this shows up as aliasing bugs. A service \"normalizes\" a request by mutating the DTO it received, and a later step or a retry sees altered data. A caller filters the `List` returned by a `@Cacheable` method backed by an in-memory cache (Caffeine and `ConcurrentMapCache` store the object reference), and every other caller now gets the shortened list. A helper sets a field on a managed JPA entity it was handed \"just for a calculation\", and dirty checking writes it at commit. The defenses are the same each time: prefer immutable parameters and return values, return new objects instead of mutating inputs (`withStatus(...)`), copy at boundaries with `List.copyOf`, and make any method that does mutate its argument say so in its name."],"keyPoints":["java copies every argument, including references","mutation through a parameter is visible to the caller, reassignment is not","immutable types make the distinction harmless"],"examples":[{"title":"Reassigning vs mutating a parameter","lang":"java","code":"final class Order {\n    private final List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>();\n    private OrderStatus status = OrderStatus.NEW;\n\n    void addLine(OrderLine line) { lines.add(line); }\n    void markPaid() { status = OrderStatus.PAID; }\n    OrderStatus status() { return status; }\n    int lineCount() { return lines.size(); }\n}\n\nclass ParameterDemo {\n    static void reassign(Order order) {\n        order = new Order();              // only the local copy of the reference changes\n        order.markPaid();\n    }\n\n    static void mutate(Order order) {\n        order.markPaid();                 // same object the caller holds\n        order.addLine(new OrderLine(\"SKU-1\", 2));\n    }\n\n    static void addFee(BigDecimal total) {\n        total = total.add(new BigDecimal(\"4.90\")); // new object; caller's total untouched\n    }\n\n    static void increment(int retries) {\n        retries++;                        // copy of the primitive\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Order order = new Order();\n        reassign(order);\n        System.out.println(order.status());                          // NEW\n        mutate(order);\n        System.out.println(order.status() + \" \" + order.lineCount()); // PAID 1\n\n        BigDecimal total = new BigDecimal(\"100.00\");\n        addFee(total);\n        System.out.println(total);                                   // 100.00\n\n        int retries = 0;\n        increment(retries);\n        System.out.println(retries);                                 // 0\n    }\n}","note":"`reassign` creates and pays a different order that nobody else can see. To change what the caller holds, return the new value and let the caller assign it: `total = withFee(total);`."},{"title":"Aliasing through an in-memory cache","lang":"java","code":"@Service\nclass CurrencyCatalog {\n    private final RateClient rateClient;\n\n    CurrencyCatalog(RateClient rateClient) { this.rateClient = rateClient; }\n\n    @Cacheable(\"supported-currencies\")\n    public List<String> supportedCurrencies() {\n        return new ArrayList<>(rateClient.fetchCurrencies()); // BAD: mutable list goes into the cache\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass CheckoutService {\n    private final CurrencyCatalog catalog;\n\n    CheckoutService(CurrencyCatalog catalog) { this.catalog = catalog; }\n\n    List<String> currenciesFor(Market market) {\n        List<String> currencies = catalog.supportedCurrencies(); // the object the cache holds\n        currencies.removeIf(c -> !market.allows(c));             // BAD: shrinks it for everyone\n        return currencies;\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: cache an immutable value and derive new lists instead of mutating\n@Cacheable(\"supported-currencies\")\npublic List<String> supportedCurrencies() {\n    return List.copyOf(rateClient.fetchCurrencies());\n}\n\nList<String> currenciesFor(Market market) {\n    return catalog.supportedCurrencies().stream()\n            .filter(market::allows)\n            .toList();\n}","note":"With an in-memory cache the cached \"value\" is a reference, so the first market to call `currenciesFor` silently removes currencies for every later request until eviction. With `List.copyOf`, the same `removeIf` throws `UnsupportedOperationException` immediately, which is the failure you want."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you return several values, or emulate an out parameter, in Java?","a":"Return a small record that carries all results, such as `record Settlement(BigDecimal net, BigDecimal fee)`, or a sealed result type when the outcomes differ in shape. Holder tricks such as a one-element array or an `AtomicReference` passed in to be filled work, but they hide the data flow. A method that fills a caller-supplied collection, such as `collectErrors(List<String> errors)`, is acceptable when its name makes the mutation obvious."},{"q":"Why can a lambda not assign a local variable of the enclosing method?","a":"A lambda captures the value of a local, not the variable itself, because the local lives in the creating method's stack frame and may be gone when the lambda runs, possibly on another thread. Java therefore requires captured locals to be final or effectively final, so the copy can never disagree with the original. Mutating an object through a captured reference is allowed, which is why an `AtomicInteger` counter inside `forEach` compiles, although `count()` or `reduce` is usually the cleaner answer."}],"extra":["**Serialization breaks aliasing:** a Redis-backed cache, an HTTP call or a message hands the receiver a deserialized copy, so mutations never travel back; moving a cache from Caffeine to Redis can change the behavior of code that mutated cached values.","**ArgumentCaptor sees the final state:** Mockito keeps the reference passed to a mock, so if production code mutates the object after the call, `captor.getValue()` shows the mutated state rather than what was passed at the time; immutable arguments make such tests trustworthy.","**Varargs share the array:** calling `audit(ids)` with an existing `long[]` passes that array itself, so a varargs method that sorts or overwrites its parameter changes the caller's array.","**Managed entities as parameters:** passing a managed JPA entity into a helper that sets a field is a database write, because dirty checking flushes any change to a managed entity at commit; calculate on DTOs or value objects instead.","**Defensive copies at the boundary:** a constructor that stores a caller's `List` without copying shares it forever, so the caller can change your object after you validated it; `List.copyOf` on the way in and unmodifiable return values on the way out close both directions."],"redFlags":["Says objects are passed by reference without qualification","thinks reassigning a parameter changes the caller's variable","cannot explain why a method can still mutate the caller's object"]},{"id":6,"category":"Java Core","question":"Abstract class vs interface: how do you choose now that interfaces can have default and static methods?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Choose an interface for a contract or capability: a class can implement many, they are easy to fake in tests and wrap in decorators, and default methods let them evolve. Choose an abstract class when implementations must share state, constructor logic or a fixed algorithm with protected hooks; often the best design offers both, an interface plus an optional skeletal base class.","answer":["The remaining differences are about state and control. An interface cannot have instance fields (only `public static final` constants) or constructors, and all its members are public apart from private helper methods (Java 9+); a default method can only work through other interface methods. An abstract class can hold fields, run constructor logic, use `protected` and package-private members, and declare `final` methods that subclasses cannot override. A class extends one class but implements any number of interfaces, so choosing an abstract class spends the only superclass slot your implementers get.","Default methods were added for interface evolution: Java 8 needed to add `stream()` and `removeIf` to `Collection` without breaking every existing implementation. They suit convenience methods derived from the abstract ones (`isEmpty()` from `size()`), not shared state or an enforced algorithm, since any implementer can override them and they cannot be `final` or `synchronized`. Conflicts follow three rules: a method inherited from a superclass beats any default, a more specific interface beats the one it extends, and otherwise the class must override and choose explicitly with `Auditable.super.auditLabel()`. Static interface methods are not inherited by implementing classes; they serve as factories and helpers called through the interface name, as in `Comparator.comparing`.","In backend code, interfaces define the ports: `PaymentGateway`, `OrderRepository`, `NotificationSender`. They keep callers decoupled, let tests use simple fakes, and let you wrap an implementation in a decorator for metrics, retries or caching. Use an abstract class when implementations genuinely share mechanics you want to own, such as a provider adapter base that times calls and maps errors in a `final` template method while subclasses fill in the provider-specific hooks. The JDK pattern of an interface plus a skeletal implementation (`List` and `AbstractList`) gives you both: callers depend on the interface, implementers may extend the base for convenience, and nobody is forced to. For a closed set of variants, a sealed interface with record implementations is often a better model than either."],"keyPoints":["interfaces define contracts, abstract classes share state and enforced algorithms","default methods exist for API evolution, not for state","an interface plus a skeletal base class offers both"],"examples":[{"title":"A provider port with an optional template base class","lang":"java","code":"public interface PaymentGateway {\n    String providerId();\n\n    PaymentResult charge(ChargeRequest request);\n\n    default boolean supports(String currency) {      // derived convenience, overridable\n        return supportedCurrencies().contains(currency);\n    }\n\n    default Set<String> supportedCurrencies() {\n        return Set.of(\"SEK\", \"EUR\");\n    }\n\n    static PaymentGateway unavailable(String providerId) { // factory, not inherited\n        return new PaymentGateway() {\n            public String providerId() { return providerId; }\n            public PaymentResult charge(ChargeRequest r) { throw new ProviderDownException(providerId); }\n        };\n    }\n}\n\npublic abstract class HttpPaymentGateway implements PaymentGateway {\n    private final MeterRegistry meters;               // shared state needs a class\n    private final RestClient client;\n\n    protected HttpPaymentGateway(MeterRegistry meters, RestClient client) {\n        this.meters = meters;\n        this.client = client;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public final PaymentResult charge(ChargeRequest request) {  // fixed algorithm\n        Timer.Sample sample = Timer.start(meters);\n        try {\n            return toResult(send(client, request));\n        } catch (RestClientResponseException e) {\n            return mapError(e);\n        } finally {\n            sample.stop(meters.timer(\"payment.charge\", \"provider\", providerId()));\n        }\n    }\n\n    protected abstract ProviderResponse send(RestClient client, ChargeRequest request);\n    protected abstract PaymentResult toResult(ProviderResponse response);\n    protected abstract PaymentResult mapError(RestClientResponseException e);\n}","note":"Callers and tests depend only on `PaymentGateway`; a provider with unusual needs implements it directly instead of extending the base. The base class earns its place by owning state (`meters`, `client`) and a `final` algorithm, which an interface cannot do."},{"title":"How default method conflicts resolve","lang":"java","code":"interface Auditable {\n    default String auditLabel() { return \"entity:\" + getClass().getSimpleName(); }\n}\n\ninterface Exportable {\n    default String auditLabel() { return \"export\"; }\n}\n\n// Two unrelated defaults: the class must override and choose\nclass Invoice implements Auditable, Exportable {\n    @Override\n    public String auditLabel() {\n        return Auditable.super.auditLabel();      // \"entity:Invoice\"\n    }\n}\n\n// A superclass method beats any default\nabstract class BaseEntity {\n    public String auditLabel() { return \"base-entity\"; }\n}\nclass Refund extends BaseEntity implements Auditable { }   // \"base-entity\"\n\n// The more specific interface wins\ninterface VersionedAuditable extends Auditable {\n    @Override\n    default String auditLabel() { return \"v2:\" + Auditable.super.auditLabel(); }\n}\nclass Payout implements Auditable, VersionedAuditable { }  // \"v2:entity:Payout\"\n\n// Compile errors: a default method cannot be final or synchronized,\n// and cannot override Object methods such as equals or hashCode","note":"Without the override, `Invoice` fails to compile with \"types Auditable and Exportable are incompatible\". The superclass rule is the silent one: if a base class already has a method with the same signature, a newly added default is simply ignored for that class."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Interfaces can declare fields, so can they hold state?","a":"Interface fields are implicitly `public static final`, so they are constants shared by everyone, not per-instance state. A default method can reach instance data only through abstract accessors the implementer provides, which is why behavior that depends on fields (counters, caches, injected collaborators) belongs in an abstract class or in a collaborator the implementer composes. Putting a mutable object in an interface constant, such as a `Map` used as a registry, creates hidden global state."},{"q":"Why do libraries often ship both an interface and an abstract base class?","a":"The interface is the stable contract that callers, proxies and test doubles depend on; the abstract class is an optional convenience that implements the boilerplate, as `AbstractList` does for `List`. Implementers that already extend something else, or that need different mechanics, implement the interface directly. Evolution is easier too: a new default method on the interface or a new concrete method on the base reaches existing implementations without breaking them."}],"extra":["**Private interface methods:** since Java 9 interfaces can declare private and private static methods, so default methods can share helper code without exposing it as API.","**Functional interfaces:** an interface with exactly one abstract method can be implemented with a lambda, a strong reason to keep small strategy contracts as interfaces; `@FunctionalInterface` makes the compiler enforce the single abstract method.","**Constructors in abstract classes:** calling an overridable method from the base constructor runs the subclass override before the subclass fields are initialized, a classic source of null fields in template base classes; keep base constructors to assignments.","**Sealed interfaces:** `sealed interface PaymentResult permits ...` keeps an interface's flexibility (records can implement it) while closing the set of implementations, which enables exhaustive switches.","**Binary compatibility:** adding an abstract method to a published interface breaks every implementer at compile time and fails with `AbstractMethodError` when old binaries are called through it; adding a default method avoids both, which is how library APIs evolve."],"redFlags":["Says interfaces cannot contain method bodies","uses default methods to share mutable state or enforce an algorithm","picks an abstract class by habit and spends the single superclass slot"]},{"id":7,"category":"Java Core","question":"Method overloading vs overriding: what is resolved at compile time and what at run time?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Overloading picks a method by name and the compile-time types of the arguments, so the choice is fixed in the bytecode. Overriding replaces an inherited instance method, and the JVM picks the implementation at run time from the actual class of the receiver; fields and static, private and final methods are never dispatched dynamically.","answer":["**Overloading** means several methods share a name with different parameter lists. The compiler chooses one from the declared (static) types of the arguments, in three phases: first exact matches and primitive widening, then boxing and unboxing, then varargs. So `send(5)` prefers `send(long)` over `send(Integer)` over `send(int...)`. With several candidates in the same phase it picks the most specific one, which is why `route(null)` calls `route(String)` rather than `route(Object)`, while two unrelated candidates such as `m(Integer)` and `m(String)` make `m(null)` a compile error. The return type plays no part, and because generics are erased, `process(List<String>)` and `process(List<Integer>)` cannot coexist.","**Overriding** means a subclass provides its own implementation of an inherited instance method with the same signature. The call site is compiled against the declared type, but `invokevirtual` and `invokeinterface` dispatch on the receiver's runtime class, so `Gateway g = new CardGateway(); g.charge()` runs `CardGateway.charge`. The rules: same name and parameter types, a return type that is the same or a subtype (covariant return), no reduced visibility, and no new or broader checked exceptions. Static methods are hidden, not overridden: the call is resolved from the declared type, and fields are resolved the same way. `private`, `static` and `final` methods are bound at compile time.","The two meet in a classic bug: the argument's static type selects the overload, even when the object is more specific at run time. Pass an `Object` variable holding a `RefundEvent` to overloads `route(Object)` and `route(RefundEvent)`, and `route(Object)` runs. Writing `equals(OrderKey other)` instead of `equals(Object o)` creates an overload that collections never call. `@Override` turns the second mistake into a compile error and belongs on every overriding method. When behavior should depend on the runtime type of an argument, use real dispatch: a method on the argument itself, a sealed type with a pattern `switch`, or a visitor. Performance is not a reason to avoid overriding: HotSpot uses type profiles and class hierarchy analysis to devirtualize and inline most call sites."],"keyPoints":["overloads are chosen at compile time from declared argument types","overrides are dispatched at run time on the receiver's class","static methods and fields are hidden, never overridden"],"examples":[{"title":"Which method runs: resolution phases and dynamic dispatch","lang":"java","code":"class Notifier {\n    String send(long userId)    { return \"long\"; }\n    String send(Integer userId) { return \"Integer\"; }\n    String send(int... userIds) { return \"varargs\"; }\n\n    String route(Object event)      { return \"generic handler\"; }\n    String route(RefundEvent event) { return \"refund handler\"; }\n}\n\nclass Gateway {\n    static String kind() { return \"gateway\"; }\n    String name = \"gateway\";\n    String charge() { return \"Gateway.charge\"; }\n}\n\nclass CardGateway extends Gateway {\n    static String kind() { return \"card\"; }   // hides Gateway.kind, does not override\n    String name = \"card\";                     // a second field that hides the first\n    @Override String charge() { return \"CardGateway.charge\"; }\n}\n\nclass DispatchDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Notifier n = new Notifier();\n        System.out.println(n.send(5));                    // long: widening beats boxing beats varargs\n\n        Object event = new RefundEvent(\"r-17\");\n        System.out.println(n.route(event));               // generic handler: static type is Object\n        System.out.println(n.route((RefundEvent) event)); // refund handler\n\n        Gateway g = new CardGateway();\n        System.out.println(g.charge());                   // CardGateway.charge: runtime dispatch\n        System.out.println(g.name);                       // gateway: fields use the declared type\n        System.out.println(Gateway.kind());               // gateway: bound at compile time\n    }\n}","note":"Only `charge()` looks at the object; everything else was decided by `javac` from declared types. Calling a static method through an instance (`g.kind()`) compiles (javac warns only with `-Xlint:static`, IDEs flag it) and still uses the declared type, which is why style guides ban it."},{"title":"An equals overload: compiles, passes a direct test, breaks HashSet","lang":"java","code":"public final class OrderKey {\n    private final String merchantId;\n    private final String orderNo;\n\n    public OrderKey(String merchantId, String orderNo) {\n        this.merchantId = merchantId;\n        this.orderNo = orderNo;\n    }\n\n    // BAD: overloads equals(Object) instead of overriding it\n    public boolean equals(OrderKey other) {\n        return merchantId.equals(other.merchantId) && orderNo.equals(other.orderNo);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int hashCode() {\n        return Objects.hash(merchantId, orderNo);\n    }\n}\n\nclass OrderKeyDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        OrderKey a = new OrderKey(\"m-1\", \"1001\");\n        OrderKey b = new OrderKey(\"m-1\", \"1001\");\n        System.out.println(a.equals(b));        // true: static type OrderKey picks the overload\n\n        Object o = b;\n        System.out.println(a.equals(o));        // false: Object.equals, identity\n\n        Set<OrderKey> seen = new HashSet<>(List.of(a));\n        System.out.println(seen.contains(b));   // false: HashSet calls equals(Object)\n    }\n}\n// FIX: @Override public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof OrderKey k && ... }\n// With @Override on the original method, javac would have rejected it immediately.","note":"The hash codes match, so `HashSet` finds the right bucket and then calls `equals(Object)`, which this class never overrode. A record would have generated the correct method."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can you overload or override a method by changing only the return type?","a":"Neither. Two methods with the same name and parameter types are a compile error whatever their return types, because a call site could not tell them apart. An override may return a subtype of the original return type (a covariant return, such as `CardPaymentResult` where the base method returns `PaymentResult`), and `javac` emits a bridge method with the old signature so existing callers still link."},{"q":"How do overloading and lambdas interact?","a":"When overloads differ by functional interface, the compiler uses the lambda's shape to choose: `executor.submit(() -> computeTotal())` picks `submit(Callable)` because the lambda returns a value, while a lambda calling a `void` method picks `submit(Runnable)`, and its `Future` yields `null`. Overloads taking different functional interfaces of the same arity can be ambiguous for implicitly typed lambdas, which is one reason APIs such as `Comparator` use distinct names like `comparingInt` and `comparingLong`."}],"extra":["**Generic code sees erased types:** inside `<T> void log(T value)`, a call to overloaded `format(value)` always binds to `format(Object)`, even when `T` is `String` at run time, because the compiler only knows `T` as `Object`.","**Access and exceptions in overrides:** an override may widen access (protected to public) and drop checked exceptions, but never the reverse; this is substitutability enforced by the compiler.","**Private methods are not overridden:** a subclass method with the same signature as a superclass `private` method is a new, unrelated method, the superclass keeps calling its own version, and `@Override` on it is a compile error.","**Proxies rely on overriding:** CGLIB-based Spring proxies subclass your bean and override its methods to add transactions or caching, so `final` and `private` methods silently get no `@Transactional` behavior.","**Dispatch cost:** HotSpot inlines virtual calls at sites that see one or two receiver types; a megamorphic site with many types falls back to a table lookup, which matters only in very hot loops."],"redFlags":["Says overloading is resolved at run time","believes static methods or fields are polymorphic","omits `@Override` and cannot name the bug it prevents"]},{"id":8,"category":"Java Core","question":"final, finally and finalize: what does each do, and what replaced finalize for cleanup?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`final` prevents reassignment, overriding or subclassing; `finally` is a block that runs after `try` whether it completes normally or throws; `finalize` was a GC callback and is deprecated for removal. Deterministic cleanup now uses `AutoCloseable` with try-with-resources, and `java.lang.ref.Cleaner` serves only as a safety net for resources someone forgot to close.","answer":["`final` is a compile-time restriction: one assignment for a variable, no override for a method, no subclass for a class. `finally` is run-time control flow: its block runs when the `try` or `catch` completes normally, returns, breaks or throws, which makes it the place to release locks and reset per-request state such as MDC keys. It does not run if the JVM stops underneath it (`System.exit` inside the `try`, `Runtime.halt`, `kill -9`, a crash). Two traps: a `return` in `finally` replaces the `try`'s result and silently discards any exception in flight (`javac -Xlint:finally` warns about it), and an exception thrown from `finally` replaces the original one.","Try-with-resources is the modern answer to cleanup. Every `AutoCloseable` declared in the header is closed automatically, in reverse declaration order, after the block and before any `catch` or `finally` of the same statement. If the body throws and `close()` also throws, the close failure is attached to the primary exception as a suppressed exception (`getSuppressed()`) instead of hiding it, which was the flaw of hand-written `finally { conn.close(); }`. Since Java 9 an effectively final variable can be listed directly (`try (connection) { ... }`), and a `null` resource is skipped. `Closeable` is the I/O subtype whose `close` throws `IOException` and must be idempotent; `AutoCloseable.close` may throw `Exception`, so declare a narrower exception in your own implementations.","`finalize` failed because it had no timing guarantee, delayed reclamation by at least one extra GC cycle, ran on a finalizer thread that could fall behind, and could resurrect objects. It has been deprecated for removal since Java 18 (JEP 421); `--finalization=disabled` lets you test whether anything still relies on it. The replacement for safety-net cleanup is `Cleaner`: you register an object with a cleanup action, and the action runs once, either when `close()` calls `cleanable.clean()` or after the object becomes phantom reachable. The action must not reference the object it cleans, so put the native handle in a static nested class; a lambda that captures `this` keeps the object reachable forever. Application code rarely needs `Cleaner`; it is for libraries wrapping native memory, file descriptors or off-heap buffers."],"keyPoints":["final restricts, finally runs after try, finalize is deprecated","try-with-resources closes in reverse order and keeps close failures as suppressed","use Cleaner only as a safety net behind close()"],"examples":[{"title":"finally traps, and what try-with-resources does instead","lang":"java","code":"class ReportJob {\n    // BAD: return in finally swallows the exception from the try block\n    static String exportStatus() {\n        try {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"export failed\");\n        } finally {\n            return \"OK\";                  // caller sees \"OK\"; javac -Xlint:finally warns\n        }\n    }\n\n    record AuditFile(String name) implements AutoCloseable {\n        @Override\n        public void close() {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"flush failed for \" + name);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(exportStatus());           // OK\n\n        try (AuditFile ledger = new AuditFile(\"ledger.csv\");\n             AuditFile payouts = new AuditFile(\"payouts.csv\")) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"bad row 17\");\n        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {\n            System.out.println(e.getMessage());       // bad row 17: primary exception kept\n            for (Throwable s : e.getSuppressed()) {\n                System.out.println(\"  suppressed: \" + s.getMessage());\n            }\n            // suppressed: flush failed for payouts.csv   (closed first: reverse order)\n            // suppressed: flush failed for ledger.csv\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"A hand-written `finally { file.close(); }` would have thrown the close failure and lost \"bad row 17\", the error you actually need in the logs. `printStackTrace` and the common logging frameworks print suppressed exceptions as part of the trace."},{"title":"Cleaner as a safety net behind close()","lang":"java","code":"public final class NativeSigner implements AutoCloseable {\n    private static final Cleaner CLEANER = Cleaner.create();  // owns a daemon thread: share it\n\n    // Holds only the native handle; must not reference NativeSigner\n    private static final class State implements Runnable {\n        private final long handle;\n        State(long handle) { this.handle = handle; }\n\n        @Override\n        public void run() {                     // invoked at most once\n            NativeLib.release(handle);\n        }\n    }\n\n    private final State state;\n    private final Cleaner.Cleanable cleanable;\n\n    public NativeSigner(byte[] keyMaterial) {\n        this.state = new State(NativeLib.load(keyMaterial));\n        this.cleanable = CLEANER.register(this, state);\n        // BAD alternative: register(this, () -> NativeLib.release(this.handle())) keeps this alive\n    }\n\n    public byte[] sign(byte[] payload) {\n        try {\n            return NativeLib.sign(state.handle, payload);\n        } finally {\n            Reference.reachabilityFence(this);  // keep this reachable during the native call\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void close() {\n        cleanable.clean();                      // deterministic path; unregisters the action\n    }\n}\n\n// Usage: try-with-resources is the real cleanup, the Cleaner only catches leaks\ntry (NativeSigner signer = new NativeSigner(keyMaterial)) {\n    return signer.sign(statementBytes);\n}","note":"The state object holds the handle, not the wrapper, so the wrapper can become unreachable and the action can run. `reachabilityFence` stops the JIT from treating `this` as dead while the native call still uses the handle, which would let the Cleaner free it mid-call."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Does finally always run?","a":"It runs on normal completion, `return`, `break`, `continue` and any exception, including errors such as `OutOfMemoryError` as long as the thread can still execute. It does not run when the JVM stops underneath it: `System.exit` inside the `try` (shutdown hooks run instead), `Runtime.halt`, `kill -9`, a native crash or a lost machine. Durable effects therefore need to be safe by design, through transactions and idempotent retries, not only through cleanup code."},{"q":"What should close() do when it is called twice or fails?","a":"Make it idempotent: a second call should do nothing, which `Closeable` requires and pools and containers assume, since they may close a resource you already closed. If release can fail, throw a specific exception and let try-with-resources attach it as suppressed rather than swallowing it, because leaked connections and file handles surface later as pool exhaustion or \"too many open files\". Keep `close()` fast, since it often runs on shutdown paths."}],"extra":["**Locks use finally, not try-with-resources:** `ReentrantLock` is not `AutoCloseable`, so the idiom is `lock.lock(); try { ... } finally { lock.unlock(); }`, with `lock()` before the `try` so a failed acquisition never calls `unlock`.","**MDC and ThreadLocal cleanup:** values put into `MDC` or a `ThreadLocal` on pooled threads must be removed in `finally`, or the next request on that thread inherits a stale user or correlation id; SLF4J's `MDC.putCloseable` returns an `AutoCloseable` for try-with-resources.","**Streams hold resources too:** `Files.lines`, `Files.list` and `Files.walk` return streams backed by open file handles, as do Spring Data repository methods returning `Stream<T>`, so they belong in try-with-resources; `Stream` implements `AutoCloseable` for this case.","**Spring bean cleanup:** beans release resources through `@PreDestroy`, `DisposableBean` or `AutoCloseable.close`; for `@Bean` methods Spring infers a public `close` or `shutdown` method as the destroy method by default.","**Finding old finalizers:** `jcmd <pid> GC.finalizer_info` and the JFR event `jdk.FinalizerStatistics` show which classes still have finalizers, often old drivers or libraries, before you run with `--finalization=disabled`."],"redFlags":["Treats finalize as a valid cleanup hook","returns from finally or closes resources by hand and loses the original exception","cannot explain suppressed exceptions or close order in try-with-resources"]},{"id":9,"category":"Java Core","question":"A colleague marks a `List` field `final` and calls the class immutable. Is that right, and what does `final` actually guarantee?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`final` means cannot be reassigned (variables and fields), cannot be overridden (methods) or cannot be subclassed (classes); it freezes a reference, not the object behind it, so on its own it does not guarantee immutability.","answer":["On a local variable or field, `final` means it is assigned exactly once, enforced by the compiler's definite assignment rules; lambdas and inner classes may only capture final or effectively final locals. On a method, subclasses cannot override it. On a class, nobody can extend it, as with `String`, `Integer` and every record.","`final` does not make the referenced object immutable. A `private final List<OrderLine> lines` still accepts `lines.add(...)`, and the elements of a `final` array can be overwritten. Immutability is a property of the whole object graph: the class must close every path to changing state, including references it hands out and references it received from callers.","`final` fields still matter beyond documentation: the Java Memory Model guarantees that once a constructor completes without leaking `this`, every thread sees the correctly initialized values of final fields, even if the object was published without synchronization. That is the foundation of thread-safe immutable objects. Also keep the keywords apart: `finally` is the block that runs after `try` and `catch`, while `finalize()` is the deprecated GC callback that should not be used at all."],"keyPoints":["on a variable or field it blocks reassignment of the reference only","on a method it blocks overriding, and on a class it blocks subclassing","the object behind a final reference can still change through its own methods","real immutability also needs no mutators and defensive copies of mutable inputs"],"examples":[{"title":"Final fields, mutable object, and the immutable fix","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: every field is final, yet the invoice is mutable through its list\npublic final class Invoice {\n    private final String invoiceNo;\n    private final List<InvoiceLine> lines;\n\n    public Invoice(String invoiceNo, List<InvoiceLine> lines) {\n        this.invoiceNo = invoiceNo;\n        this.lines = lines;        // the caller keeps a reference and can change it\n    }\n\n    public List<InvoiceLine> lines() {\n        return lines;              // callers can add or clear lines\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: final class, final fields, defensive copy, immutable component types\nrecord ImmutableInvoice(String invoiceNo, List<InvoiceLine> lines, Instant issuedAt) {\n    ImmutableInvoice {\n        Objects.requireNonNull(invoiceNo);\n        Objects.requireNonNull(issuedAt);\n        lines = List.copyOf(lines); // unmodifiable snapshot; InvoiceLine is itself a record\n    }\n}","note":"The BAD class would pass a review that only checks for `final` keywords. The GOOD version also relies on `Instant` and `InvoiceLine` being immutable; one mutable component breaks the guarantee."},{"title":"finally versus finalize for resource cleanup","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: relies on the GC to close the connection someday (deprecated for removal)\nclass LegacyExporter {\n    private final Connection connection;\n\n    LegacyExporter(Connection connection) { this.connection = connection; }\n\n    @Override\n    @SuppressWarnings(\"removal\")\n    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {\n        connection.close();\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: deterministic cleanup; try-with-resources compiles to finally blocks\nclass PaymentExporter {\n    void export(DataSource dataSource) throws SQLException {\n        try (Connection connection = dataSource.getConnection();\n             PreparedStatement ps = connection.prepareStatement(\"select id, amount from payments\");\n             ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {\n            while (rs.next()) {\n                write(rs.getLong(\"id\"), rs.getBigDecimal(\"amount\"));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private void write(long id, BigDecimal amount) { /* append to the export file */ }\n}","note":"Under load the finalizer version exhausts the connection pool long before the GC runs finalizers; the try-with-resources version returns the connection the moment the block exits, even on exceptions."}],"followUps":[{"q":"A class holds a `java.util.Date` and a `List<String>`. What changes make it safe to share as immutable?","a":"Make the class `final` and its fields `private final`, remove setters, and replace `Date` with an immutable type such as `Instant`, or copy it on the way in and out. Copy the list with `List.copyOf` in the constructor and return that unmodifiable list, never the caller's original. State changes then return new instances, for example `withTags(...)`, and a record with a compact constructor can do most of this for you."},{"q":"Why is relying on `finalize` to close connections a bad idea?","a":"Nothing guarantees when, or whether, the GC runs it, so connections can run out long before cleanup happens. Finalizable objects take extra GC cycles to reclaim, depend on a finalizer thread that can fall behind, and silently drop exceptions; finalization has been deprecated for removal since JDK 18. Close resources deterministically with try-with-resources and keep `Cleaner` only as a safety net."}],"extra":["**finalize is on its way out:** `Object.finalize` was deprecated in Java 9 and deprecated for removal in Java 18 (JEP 421); running with `--finalization=disabled` tells you whether anything in your application still depends on it.","**Compile-time constants are inlined:** a `static final` primitive or `String` initialized with a constant expression is copied into every class that uses it, so changing it in a shared library without recompiling dependents leaves stale values in production.","**Sealed classes as a middle ground:** Java 17 `sealed` classes allow extension only by the subclasses listed in `permits`, which is often what you want instead of the all-or-nothing choice of `final`.","**final is not a performance tool:** HotSpot does not need `final` on locals or parameters to optimize, and it inlines non-final methods using class hierarchy analysis; use `final` for design intent and safe publication, not speed.","**Leaking this voids the guarantee:** registering `this` as a listener or starting a thread from the constructor lets other threads see the object before its final fields are frozen, which breaks the safe-publication guarantee."],"redFlags":["returns a `final` list field from a getter and assumes callers cannot add to it","claims `final` exists mainly as a performance hint for the JIT"]},{"id":10,"category":"Java Core","question":"How do you design an immutable class in Java?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Prevent subclassing, make every field private and final, provide no setters, and express changes as methods that return new instances. Copy mutable inputs on the way in, never hand out internal mutable state, prefer immutable component types, and finish construction before anything can see `this`; a record does most of this for you but is only shallowly immutable.","answer":["The recipe has five parts. Nobody can subclass the class: declare it `final`, or use private constructors with static factories, because a subclass could add mutable state or override accessors and still be passed around as your type. Every field is `private final`, which also gives the memory model's guarantee that other threads see the fields initialized. There are no mutators: state transitions return new objects (`withStatus`, `plus`), the way `BigDecimal` and `java.time` work. Mutable inputs are copied in, and internal mutable state is never returned. And the constructor validates the copies, not the caller's originals, so nobody can change the data between your check and its use.","What breaks immutability is almost always a reference. A `List`, array or `java.util.Date` component makes the object only as immutable as that component, and a record does nothing about it: `List.copyOf` in the compact constructor fixes lists (and rejects nulls), arrays need `clone()` both on the way in and on the way out, and `Date` should become `Instant`. `Collections.unmodifiableList(callerList)` is not a copy; it is a read-only view that still changes when the caller changes the list. Leaking `this` from the constructor (registering a listener, starting a thread, handing `this` to a collaborator) lets other code see the object before its fields are assigned, and calling an overridable method from a constructor does the same to subclasses. A lazily computed cache is acceptable only if it is invisible from outside, like `String`'s cached hash.","Records are the default way to write immutable types today: final class, private final fields, value-based `equals` and `hashCode`, and a compact constructor for validation and defensive copies. Write a class by hand when you need to hide the representation (a record exposes every component through an accessor), store derived fields, or offer a different construction API. In a Spring service, immutable configuration (`@ConfigurationProperties` bound to a record), DTOs, domain events and value objects remove whole classes of bugs: they can be cached, shared across threads, used as map keys and logged without anyone changing them afterwards. JPA entities are the exception because the provider needs mutable state; encapsulate them instead."],"keyPoints":["final class, private final fields, no setters, a new instance per change","copy mutable inputs in and never return internal mutable state","records help but are only shallowly immutable"],"examples":[{"title":"An immutable payment instruction written by hand","lang":"java","code":"public final class PaymentInstruction {\n    private final String instructionId;\n    private final Money amount;                  // immutable record\n    private final List<String> remittanceLines;\n    private final byte[] signature;\n    private final Instant requestedAt;           // Instant, not java.util.Date\n\n    private PaymentInstruction(String instructionId, Money amount, List<String> remittanceLines,\n                               byte[] signature, Instant requestedAt) {\n        this.instructionId = Objects.requireNonNull(instructionId);\n        this.amount = Objects.requireNonNull(amount);\n        this.remittanceLines = List.copyOf(remittanceLines); // copy first, then validate the copy\n        if (this.remittanceLines.size() > 4) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"at most 4 remittance lines\");\n        }\n        this.signature = signature.clone();                  // arrays have no immutable form\n        this.requestedAt = Objects.requireNonNull(requestedAt);\n    }\n\n    public static PaymentInstruction of(String id, Money amount, List<String> lines,\n                                        byte[] signature, Instant requestedAt) {\n        return new PaymentInstruction(id, amount, lines, signature, requestedAt);\n    }\n\n    public List<String> remittanceLines() { return remittanceLines; } // already unmodifiable\n    public byte[] signature() { return signature.clone(); }           // copy out, too\n    public Money amount() { return amount; }\n\n    public PaymentInstruction withAmount(Money newAmount) {          // a change is a new object\n        return new PaymentInstruction(instructionId, newAmount, remittanceLines, signature, requestedAt);\n    }\n}","note":"Every field is final and either immutable or copied, and the array is copied in both directions because a caller could otherwise overwrite the signature after it was verified. `List.copyOf` usually returns its argument unchanged when it is already an unmodifiable list, so `withAmount` does not pay for a second copy."},{"title":"Four ways immutability leaks","lang":"java","code":"// 1. Record with mutable components: final fields, mutable state\nrecord Refund(String refundId, Date issuedAt, int[] lineIds) { }\n// refund.issuedAt().setTime(0) and refund.lineIds()[0] = 99 both change the \"immutable\" record\n\n// 2. Read-only view instead of a copy\nfinal class Basket {\n    private final List<String> skus;\n    Basket(List<String> skus) {\n        this.skus = Collections.unmodifiableList(skus); // BAD: the caller can still change skus\n    }\n    List<String> skus() { return skus; }\n}\n\n// 3. Non-final class: a mutable subclass still passes as CurrencyCode\nclass CurrencyCode {\n    private final String code;\n    CurrencyCode(String code) { this.code = code; }\n    public String code() { return code; }\n}\nclass SwitchableCurrency extends CurrencyCode {\n    private String override;\n    SwitchableCurrency(String code) { super(code); }\n    void set(String code) { override = code; }\n    @Override public String code() { return override != null ? override : super.code(); }\n}\n\n// 4. this escapes before the fields are assigned\nfinal class RateSnapshot {\n    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> rates;\n    RateSnapshot(RateBus bus, Map<String, BigDecimal> rates) {\n        bus.register(this);                 // BAD: listeners may read rates while it is null\n        this.rates = Map.copyOf(rates);\n    }\n}","note":"Each class compiles, and every type posing as immutable has only final fields. The fixes: `Instant` and a copied `List<Integer>` instead of `Date` and `int[]`, `List.copyOf` instead of a view, a `final` class, and registering from a static factory after construction completes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you build an immutable object with many fields, some optional?","a":"Use a builder that collects values in mutable fields and calls one private constructor from `build()`, where validation and defensive copies happen, so the product is immutable even though the builder is not. For records, a static nested builder or a few `with` methods work well; Lombok's `@Builder` and `@With` generate the same shape, but they validate nothing unless the constructor does. Avoid telescoping constructors full of nullable parameters, which push invalid combinations onto callers."},{"q":"Can reflection or serialization break an immutable class?","a":"A plain class's `final` instance fields can still be changed through reflection after `setAccessible(true)`, so immutability protects well-behaved code and is not a security boundary; record fields are protected, and `Field.set` on them throws `IllegalAccessException`. Classic Java serialization creates ordinary classes without running their constructors, so a crafted stream can skip validation and defensive copies unless `readObject` checks them or a serialization proxy is used. Records are always deserialized through their canonical constructor."}],"extra":["**Unmodifiable is not deep:** `List.copyOf`, `Map.copyOf` and `Stream.toList()` return unmodifiable collections, but the elements are as mutable as they were; a deeply immutable object needs immutable elements too.","**Benign caching:** an immutable class may cache a derived value, as `String` caches its hash, when the computation is deterministic and a race can only cause duplicate work; anything observable must be fixed in the constructor.","**Ideal map keys:** immutable value objects make safe `HashMap` keys only if `equals` and `hashCode` use the same fields; records generate both, hand-written classes must override both.","**Immutable configuration:** binding `@ConfigurationProperties` to a record, or to a class with a single constructor, gives settings that no bean can change at run time.","**Ignored withers:** `instruction.withAmount(newAmount);` on its own line discards the new object and changes nothing; annotating such methods with Error Prone's `@CheckReturnValue` turns that mistake into a build error."],"redFlags":["Thinks final fields alone make a class immutable","returns internal lists or arrays directly, or wraps the caller's list in an unmodifiable view","assumes every record is deeply immutable"]},{"id":11,"category":"Java Core","question":"A new module declares `throws Exception` on most service methods. How would you guide the team on checked versus unchecked exceptions?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Use unchecked exceptions for most service code (bugs, domain rule violations, infrastructure failures), reserve checked ones for rare recoverable conditions the direct caller must handle, and translate at layer boundaries while always preserving the cause.","answer":["Checked exceptions make the compiler force callers to handle or declare them. That fits a boundary condition the direct caller can realistically recover from, like a file that may be missing in a batch job or a parser signaling malformed input. In a layered Spring service, though, most failures cannot be fixed by the immediate caller, and checked exceptions leak through every signature, do not compose with lambdas and streams, and tempt people into empty `catch` blocks.","Unchecked exceptions fit three cases: programming errors (`IllegalArgumentException`, `IllegalStateException`), domain rule violations (an `InsufficientFundsException extends RuntimeException` carrying the account and amount), and infrastructure failures that should propagate to a single handler. Spring follows this design: `DataAccessException` and the HTTP client exceptions are all unchecked. For expected business outcomes that are not errors, such as a declined credit application, a return type like a sealed `CreditDecision` is often better than any exception.","The non-negotiables: never swallow, and always chain the cause (`new PaymentGatewayException(msg, e)`) so the root stack trace survives. Translate at boundaries: outbound adapters turn `SQLException`, `RestClientException` or Kafka errors into domain or application exceptions, and the web edge maps those to HTTP responses with `@RestControllerAdvice` and `ProblemDetail`. Log once, where the exception is handled, not at every layer that rethrows it."],"keyPoints":["a checked exception is a compile-time demand on every caller up the chain","most service failures are bugs, rule violations or outages nobody nearby can fix","each layer boundary maps foreign exceptions into its own vocabulary","wrapping must keep the original exception attached for diagnosis"],"examples":[{"title":"Adapter translating HTTP client failures","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass CreditBureauAdapter implements CreditBureauPort {\n\n    private final RestClient restClient;\n\n    CreditBureauAdapter(RestClient.Builder builder) {\n        this.restClient = builder.baseUrl(\"https://bureau.internal\").build();\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public CreditScore scoreFor(CustomerId customerId) {\n        try {\n            return restClient.get()\n                    .uri(\"/v1/scores/{id}\", customerId.value())\n                    .retrieve()\n                    .body(CreditScore.class);\n        } catch (HttpClientErrorException.NotFound e) {\n            throw new UnknownCustomerException(customerId, e);         // GOOD: domain meaning, cause kept\n        } catch (RestClientException e) {\n            throw new CreditBureauUnavailableException(customerId, e); // GOOD: retryable dependency failure\n        }\n        // BAD: catch (Exception e) { return null; }                     swallowed\n        // BAD: throw new RuntimeException(\"bureau error\");              cause lost\n    }\n}","note":"The domain only sees `UnknownCustomerException` and `CreditBureauUnavailableException`; the Spring HTTP exception survives as the cause, so logs still show status codes and the original stack trace."},{"title":"Translating to HTTP once, at the web edge","lang":"java","code":"@RestControllerAdvice\nclass ApiExceptionHandler {\n\n    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ApiExceptionHandler.class);\n\n    @ExceptionHandler(UnknownCustomerException.class)\n    ProblemDetail unknownCustomer(UnknownCustomerException e) {\n        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, e.getMessage());\n    }\n\n    @ExceptionHandler(CreditBureauUnavailableException.class)\n    ProblemDetail bureauDown(CreditBureauUnavailableException e) {\n        log.warn(\"Credit bureau unavailable\", e); // logged once, with the full cause chain\n        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(\n                HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE, \"Credit check temporarily unavailable\");\n        pd.setProperty(\"retryable\", true);\n        return pd;\n    }\n}","note":"Controllers and services stay free of try/catch; the advice is the single place that knows about status codes and response bodies, and it is also the single place that logs."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Where in a layered service should a `DataIntegrityViolationException` become something the business understands?","a":"In the outbound adapter that talks to the database, such as the repository implementation, which can map it to a domain exception like `DuplicatePaymentException` and keep the original attached as the cause. The domain layer then reasons about duplicates without importing Spring or JDBC types. At the inbound edge, `@RestControllerAdvice` maps that domain exception to a 409 with a `ProblemDetail` body."},{"q":"When does a checked exception still earn its place in modern Java service code?","a":"When the condition is expected and recoverable and the immediate caller is the one who can act on it, for example an `InsufficientFundsException` whose callers must offer another payment method. It also fits library APIs where ignoring the case is a real bug, like `IOException` on file access. If most callers just rethrow or wrap it, it should be unchecked."}],"extra":["**@Transactional rollback rules:** Spring rolls back by default only on `RuntimeException` and `Error`; a checked exception thrown from a `@Transactional` method commits the transaction unless you declare `rollbackFor`, a classic source of half-applied payments.","**InterruptedException:** if you catch it and cannot rethrow it, restore the flag with `Thread.currentThread().interrupt()` before translating, otherwise shutdown and cancellation silently stop working.","**Checked exceptions in lambdas:** when an `IOException` must cross a lambda, wrap it in the purpose-built `UncheckedIOException` (or your own unchecked type) instead of sneaky-throw tricks that hide it from the compiler.","**Stack trace cost:** exceptions used for high-volume control flow spend CPU in `fillInStackTrace`; a subclass can call the protected `RuntimeException(message, cause, enableSuppression, writableStackTrace)` constructor, but usually the better fix is a result type.","**Suppressed exceptions:** try-with-resources attaches failures from `close()` through `addSuppressed`, so the primary exception survives; a `finally` block that throws would replace it and hide the real failure."],"redFlags":["logs and rethrows the same exception at every layer, flooding logs with duplicates","catches `Exception` broadly and turns every failure into the same 500","wraps checked exceptions in a bare `RuntimeException` with no message or context"]},{"id":12,"category":"Java Core","question":"What does static mean for fields, methods, initializer blocks and nested classes?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`static` ties a member to the class instead of an instance: one field per loaded class, methods without `this` that are bound at compile time, initializer blocks that run once when the class is initialized, and nested classes that hold no reference to an outer instance. It suits constants, pure helpers, factories and builders, and it is dangerous for mutable shared state.","answer":["A static field exists once per class, more precisely once per class per class loader, and is shared by every instance and thread. A static method has no `this`, so it can reach instance state only through a parameter, and it is not dispatched dynamically: a subclass with the same static method hides it, and the call is resolved from the declared type. A static initializer block (`static { ... }`) and static field initializers run once, top to bottom, when the class is initialized, which happens lazily on first active use (creating an instance, calling a static method, using a non-constant static field), not at JVM startup. The JVM runs initialization under a lock, so the initializer itself is thread-safe, which is why the holder idiom gives a lazy, thread-safe singleton without `synchronized`.","Initialization failures are a production classic. If a static initializer throws, the first caller gets `ExceptionInInitializerError` carrying the real cause; the class is then marked as failed, and every later use throws `NoClassDefFoundError: Could not initialize class FeeTable`, which sends people looking for a missing jar. Search the logs for the first error. For nested types, a **static nested** class is an ordinary class scoped inside another, ideal for builders, DTOs and private helpers. An **inner** (non-static) class instance belongs to an outer instance and keeps a reference to it, so an inner or anonymous class handed to a long-lived executor, cache or listener registry keeps the whole outer object alive. Default to static nested classes, nested records (implicitly static) or lambdas that do not capture `this`.","In Spring applications, static mutable state is the thing to avoid. Beans are already singletons, so a `static` cache or counter adds nothing except state that bypasses the container, survives between test contexts in the same JVM, cannot be replaced in tests, and is shared across tenants. Spring does not inject into static fields (`@Autowired` on one is ignored with a log message), and putting the `ApplicationContext` into a static holder so any class can fetch beans hides dependencies. The good uses of `static` are constants (`static final` immutable values), pure functions of their arguments (`Money.sum(amounts)`), factories (`OrderId.of(...)`), loggers and nested builders."],"keyPoints":["static members belong to the class, once per class loader","static methods and fields are bound at compile time, not overridden","prefer static nested classes; inner classes capture the outer instance"],"examples":[{"title":"A failing static initializer: the first error and every error after it","lang":"java","code":"final class FeeTable {\n    private static final Map<String, BigDecimal> FEES = load();  // runs during class initialization\n\n    private static Map<String, BigDecimal> load() {\n        String raw = System.getenv(\"FEE_TABLE\");                 // null when the variable is missing\n        return Arrays.stream(raw.split(\",\"))\n                .map(entry -> entry.split(\"=\"))\n                .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableMap(kv -> kv[0], kv -> new BigDecimal(kv[1])));\n    }\n\n    static BigDecimal feeFor(String method) {\n        return FEES.getOrDefault(method, BigDecimal.ZERO);\n    }\n}\n\nclass CheckoutJob {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= 2; attempt++) {\n            try {\n                FeeTable.feeFor(\"CARD\");\n            } catch (Throwable t) {\n                System.out.println(attempt + \": \" + t);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n// 1: java.lang.ExceptionInInitializerError       (cause: NullPointerException on raw.split)\n// 2: java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError: Could not initialize class FeeTable\n// GOOD: load configuration in a bean at startup and fail fast with a clear message","note":"Only the first failure shows the real cause directly; recent JDKs attach it as the cause of the later `NoClassDefFoundError`, but log searches and alerts often show only the top line. Static initializers are the wrong place for I/O and configuration."},{"title":"Inner class leak vs static nested class, and the holder idiom","lang":"java","code":"public final class ExportSession {\n    private final byte[] buffer = new byte[8 * 1024 * 1024];  // per export, large\n    private int rowsWritten;\n\n    // BAD: inner class; each listener keeps the whole session, buffer included, reachable\n    class ProgressListener implements Consumer<ExportEvent> {\n        public void accept(ExportEvent e) { rowsWritten += e.rows(); }\n    }\n\n    void start(EventBus globalBus) {\n        globalBus.subscribe(new ProgressListener());  // forgotten unsubscribe leaks 8 MB per export\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: static nested class with explicit, minimal state\n    static final class ProgressCounter implements Consumer<ExportEvent> {\n        private final LongAdder rows = new LongAdder();\n        public void accept(ExportEvent e) { rows.add(e.rows()); }\n        long rows() { return rows.sum(); }\n    }\n\n    // Lazy and thread-safe: TemplateHolder is initialized once, on the first templates() call\n    private static final class TemplateHolder {\n        static final StatementTemplates INSTANCE = StatementTemplates.compile(\"statement.ftl\");\n    }\n\n    static StatementTemplates templates() {\n        return TemplateHolder.INSTANCE;\n    }\n}","note":"Both listeners should be unsubscribed when the export ends, but the inner class turns a forgotten unsubscribe into a leak of the entire session. The holder idiom relies on the JVM's class initialization lock, so no `volatile` or double-checked locking is needed."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why are static methods hard to test, and what do you do about it?","a":"A static call is a hard-wired dependency: callers cannot be given another implementation, so `Instant.now()`, `UUID.randomUUID()` or a static HTTP helper inside business logic makes tests slow or nondeterministic. Mockito can mock statics with `mockStatic` (its inline mock maker is the default since Mockito 5), but that is a workaround; inject a `Clock`, an id generator or a client interface instead. Pure static functions of their inputs need no mocking at all."},{"q":"When is a class initialized, and can it happen twice?","a":"On first active use: `new`, a static method call, reading or writing a non-constant static field, `Class.forName`, or initialization of a subclass, which initializes the superclass first. Reading a `static final` compile-time constant does not trigger it, because the value is inlined into the caller. A class is initialized once per class loader, so the same class loaded by two loaders (application servers, plugin systems, Spring Boot DevTools restarts) has two independent copies of its static state."}],"extra":["**Initialization order:** static initializers run once per class, superclass first; instance field initializers and instance blocks run on every `new`, after the superclass constructor and before the rest of the constructor body.","**Unused outer references:** recent `javac` versions omit the hidden outer-instance field when an inner class body never uses the outer object, but the reference comes back as soon as it touches an outer field or method, so do not rely on it.","**Static state and tests:** static caches, counters and `ThreadLocal`s survive between tests in the same JVM, including across Spring test contexts, a common cause of order-dependent test failures.","**Static @Bean methods:** declare `@Bean` methods that return a `BeanFactoryPostProcessor`, such as `PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer`, as `static`, so Spring can create them without instantiating the configuration class too early.","**Static loggers:** `private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(OrderService.class)` is the accepted exception to avoiding static state: callers cannot change it, it is cheap, and it needs no injection."],"redFlags":["Uses static fields for request, user or tenant data","thinks static methods can be overridden","cannot explain how an inner class causes a memory leak"]},{"id":13,"category":"Java Core","question":"Autoboxing pitfalls: why can two Integer values compare equal with == at 127 but not at 128?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Autoboxing calls `Integer.valueOf`, which returns cached instances for -128 to 127 and new objects outside that range, so `==` compares references that happen to match only for small values. Beyond `==`, boxing causes `NullPointerException` on unboxing, overload surprises, broken comparators, and memory and CPU overhead in hot paths.","answer":["The compiler turns `Integer a = 127;` into `Integer.valueOf(127)` and `int x = a;` into `a.intValue()`. The JLS requires boxing of small values (-128 to 127, `true` and `false`, and `char` values up to 127) to yield identical objects, so `Integer.valueOf` keeps a cache; 128 falls outside it and each boxing creates a new `Integer`. `==` between two `Integer` references compares identity, so it is true at 127 and false at 128. `Byte`, `Short`, `Long` and `Character` cache small values too, `Float` and `Double` cache nothing, and the constructor `new Integer(...)`, deprecated for removal, never uses the cache.","Mixed comparisons hide the problem. When one operand is primitive, `==` unboxes and compares values, so `boxed == 128` is true; the relational operators always unbox, so `a <= b && a >= b` can be true while `a == b` is false. Unboxing `null` throws `NullPointerException`, and the source is not always obvious: `int retries = overrides.get(merchantId);` on a missing key, a `Long` field mapped from a nullable column, or a conditional such as `flag ? map.get(key) : 3`, whose type is `int` because one branch is primitive, so the map value is unboxed even when the result is assigned to an `Integer`. Helpful NPE messages, such as `Cannot invoke \"java.lang.Integer.intValue()\" because the return value of \"java.util.Map.get(Object)\" is null`, point straight at these.","The other costs are subtle. Overload resolution prefers no boxing, so `List<Integer>.remove(1)` removes the element at index 1, not the value 1. A `Comparator` written as `(x, y) -> x < y ? -1 : (x == y ? 0 : 1)` on boxed values works for small numbers and breaks the comparator contract for large ones, which surfaces as \"Comparison method violates its general contract!\" from `sort`. Every boxed value is a separate heap object of 16 to 24 bytes plus a reference, so a `Map<Long, Long>` with ten million entries or a `Stream<Integer>` reduction in a hot loop creates real GC pressure; use primitive streams (`mapToLong`), arrays, or a primitive collections library where profiling shows it matters. And never synchronize on a wrapper: wrappers are value-based classes shared through the cache, so unrelated code may lock the same object, and `javac` warns about it."],"keyPoints":["autoboxing calls valueOf, which caches -128 to 127","== on two wrappers compares identity; use equals or unbox","unboxing null throws, and boxing costs memory in hot paths"],"examples":[{"title":"What the compiler generates, and why 128 differs","lang":"java","code":"class BoxingDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Integer a = 127;                        // Integer.valueOf(127): cached instance\n        Integer b = 127;\n        System.out.println(a == b);             // true: same cached object\n\n        Integer c = 128;                        // Integer.valueOf(128): new object\n        Integer d = 128;\n        System.out.println(c == d);             // false: two objects\n        System.out.println(c.equals(d));        // true\n        System.out.println(c == 128);           // true: one side is primitive, c is unboxed\n        System.out.println(c <= d && c >= d);   // true: relational operators always unbox\n\n        Double rate = 0.5, sameRate = 0.5;\n        System.out.println(rate == sameRate);   // false: Double has no cache\n\n        long total = 0;                         // GOOD: primitive accumulator\n        Long boxedTotal = 0L;                   // BAD: each += unboxes, adds and boxes again\n        for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {\n            total += i;\n            boxedTotal += i;\n        }\n        System.out.println(total == boxedTotal); // true: unboxed for the comparison\n    }\n}","note":"Tests usually use ids and amounts below 128, so `==` on wrappers passes there and fails with production values. `Objects.equals(c, d)` or comparing primitives is always correct."},{"title":"Production traps: null unboxing, overloads and comparators","lang":"java","code":"class RetryPolicy {\n    private final Map<String, Integer> overrides = new HashMap<>();\n\n    // BAD: the conditional's type is int, so a missing override unboxes null\n    Integer maxRetries(String merchantId, boolean hasOverride) {\n        return hasOverride ? overrides.get(merchantId) : 3;   // NullPointerException\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: keep the lookup boxed, or use getOrDefault\n    Integer maxRetriesFixed(String merchantId) {\n        return overrides.getOrDefault(merchantId, 3);\n    }\n}\n\nclass BoxingTraps {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Integer> orderIds = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20, 1));\n        orderIds.remove(1);                     // remove(int index): drops 20, leaves [10, 1]\n        orderIds.remove(Integer.valueOf(1));    // remove(Object): drops the value 1, leaves [10]\n\n        // BAD: == on boxed values, so compare(500, 500) returns 1\n        Comparator<Integer> byPriority = (x, y) -> x < y ? -1 : (x == y ? 0 : 1);\n        // GOOD\n        Comparator<Integer> fixed = Integer::compare;\n\n        Long accountId = null;                  // e.g. a nullable foreign key column\n        // long id = accountId;                 // NullPointerException on unboxing\n\n        // synchronized (Integer.valueOf(42)) { }  // javac warns: value-based class\n    }\n}","note":"The conditional rule is the least known: if one branch is `int` and the other `Integer`, the result type is `int`, so the `Integer` branch is unboxed even though the method returns `Integer`. Sorting a large list with `byPriority` can fail with \"Comparison method violates its general contract!\"."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Is the Integer cache range fixed?","a":"The JLS guarantees at least -128 to 127. The upper bound for `Integer` can be raised with `-XX:AutoBoxCacheMax`, while the other wrappers use fixed ranges, so code that relies on `==` can even behave differently between environments. Treat the cache as an allocation optimization you never depend on for correctness."},{"q":"How do you avoid boxing costs in data-heavy code?","a":"Use primitive types for fields, counters and accumulators, primitive streams (`IntStream`, `LongStream`, `mapToLong(...).sum()`) instead of `Stream<Long>` reductions, and arrays or a primitive collections library for large id sets and maps. Measure first with a profiler or JFR allocation events, since boxing in code that runs a few times per request rarely matters. JPA entities and JSON DTOs often need wrappers to represent null, which is a legitimate reason to keep them at the edges."}],"extra":["**Wrappers model null, primitives do not:** use `Long` for a nullable column and `long` when a value is always present; unboxing NPEs come from mixing the two, so convert and validate at the boundary.","**Generic code always boxes:** `List<Integer>`, `Map<Long, ...>` and `Optional<Integer>` hold objects, which is why `OptionalInt`, `IntStream` and other primitive specializations exist.","**Switch on a wrapper:** a `switch` on an `Integer` unboxes it and throws on `null`, unless it is a pattern switch with an explicit `case null`.","**Value-based classes:** wrappers, `Optional` and the `java.time` types are value-based, so identity operations (`==`, `synchronized`, identity hash maps) on them are unreliable; the diagnostic flag `-XX:DiagnoseSyncOnValueBasedClasses` can log or fail on such synchronization at run time.","**Counting with maps:** `counts.merge(sku, 1, Integer::sum)` boxes on every update; that is fine for a report, while hot counters are better served by `LongAdder` values or a primitive map."],"redFlags":["Says == compares Integer values","does not know where the compiler inserts valueOf and intValue calls","ignores null unboxing from map lookups and nullable columns"]},{"id":14,"category":"Java Core","question":"For an API response, a domain event and a JPA entity, which would you model as a record, and why?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Reach for a record when a type is just transparent data whose fields never get reassigned (DTOs, value objects, events, projections); the compiler writes the canonical constructor, component accessors, `toString` and value-based equality, while JPA entities and classes rich in mutable behavior stay regular classes.","answer":["A record (final since Java 16) declares its state in the header, and the compiler generates private final fields, a canonical constructor, accessors named after the components (`amount()`, not `getAmount()`), and `equals`, `hashCode` and `toString` over all components. Records are implicitly final, extend `java.lang.Record` and cannot declare extra instance fields, but they can implement interfaces and have static members, instance methods and additional constructors.","They fit request and response DTOs, value objects like `Money` or `CustomerId`, domain events and Kafka payloads, query projections (JPQL constructor expressions and Spring Data projections) and composite map keys. In Java 21, records plus sealed interfaces and record patterns make algebraic data types practical, for example an exhaustive `switch` over a `CreditDecision`.","Two limits matter. Records are only shallowly immutable: a `List` component can still be mutated by whoever holds it, so copy it in a compact constructor with `List.copyOf`. And records are the wrong tool for JPA entities, which need a no-arg constructor, mutable state, proxyable (non-final) classes and identity that is not value-based, and for rich models whose invariants evolve through state transitions. Validation belongs in the compact constructor, so an invalid instance can never exist; Jackson and Bean Validation (`@Valid` on a `@RequestBody`) both work with records in Spring Boot 3."],"keyPoints":["the compiler derives construction, accessors and value-based equality from the header","fields are final, but objects they reference may still change","a compact constructor is the one place to validate and normalize inputs","entities need no-arg constructors, mutable state and proxies, which records cannot offer"],"examples":[{"title":"DTO with validation, normalization and a defensive copy","lang":"java","code":"public record CreditApplication(\n        String applicationId,\n        String customerId,\n        BigDecimal requestedAmount,\n        String currency,\n        List<String> productCodes) {\n\n    public CreditApplication {                        // compact canonical constructor\n        Objects.requireNonNull(applicationId, \"applicationId\");\n        Objects.requireNonNull(customerId, \"customerId\");\n        Objects.requireNonNull(currency, \"currency\");\n        if (requestedAmount == null || requestedAmount.signum() <= 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"requestedAmount must be positive\");\n        }\n        currency = currency.toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT);  // reassigns the parameter, not the field\n        productCodes = List.copyOf(productCodes);       // shallow immutability made real\n    }                                                   // fields are assigned here implicitly\n\n    public boolean requiresManualReview() {\n        return requestedAmount.compareTo(new BigDecimal(\"500000\")) > 0;\n    }\n}","note":"Every construction path, including Jackson deserialization of a request body, runs through the compact constructor, so an invalid or externally mutable `CreditApplication` cannot exist."},{"title":"Sealed hierarchy with record patterns (Java 21)","lang":"java","code":"public sealed interface CreditDecision permits Approved, Declined, Referred { }\n\npublic record Approved(String applicationId, BigDecimal limit) implements CreditDecision { }\npublic record Declined(String applicationId, String reasonCode) implements CreditDecision { }\npublic record Referred(String applicationId, String queue) implements CreditDecision { }\n\nclass DecisionMessages {\n    static String toCustomerMessage(CreditDecision decision) {\n        return switch (decision) {                    // exhaustive: no default branch needed\n            case Approved(var id, var limit) -> \"Approved with a limit of \" + limit;\n            case Declined(var id, var reason) -> \"Declined (\" + reason + \")\";\n            case Referred(var id, var queue) -> \"Your application is under review\";\n        };\n    }\n}","note":"Adding a fourth decision type makes this `switch` fail to compile until it is handled, which is the safety net you want in credit decision code."}],"followUps":[{"q":"A record component is a `List<OrderLine>`. Can a caller still change the record's contents?","a":"Yes. The field itself cannot be reassigned, but the accessor returns the same list object, so anyone holding it, including the code that passed it in, can add or remove lines. Copy it in the compact constructor with `lines = List.copyOf(lines);`, which yields an unmodifiable list and also rejects `null` elements."},{"q":"Where would you reject a negative amount in a `Money` record, and why there?","a":"In a compact constructor such as `public Money { if (amount.signum() < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"negative amount\"); }`, which runs before the compiler assigns the fields. You can also normalize there by reassigning the parameter, for example `amount = amount.setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);`, but assigning `this.amount` is a compile error. Every construction path, including Jackson and Java deserialization, goes through the canonical constructor, so the invariant cannot be bypassed."}],"extra":["**Safer serialization:** a serializable record is always deserialized through its canonical constructor, so compact constructor validation runs on deserialization too, unlike classic `Serializable` classes that bypass constructors.","**Array components:** generated `equals` and `hashCode` compare arrays by reference, so a `byte[]` component breaks value semantics; prefer a `List` or override both methods.","**BigDecimal components:** generated `equals` uses `BigDecimal.equals`, which is scale-sensitive, so 10.0 and 10.00 make two different `Money` records unless you normalize the scale in the compact constructor.","**JPA fit:** records cannot be entities but work well as DTO projections (`select new com.acme.OrderSummary(o.id, o.total) from Order o`) and, with Hibernate 6.2 or later, as `@Embeddable` value types.","**Local records:** you can declare a record inside a method, for example `record MerchantTotal(String merchantId, BigDecimal total)`, to carry intermediate stream results instead of abusing `Map.Entry` or `Object[]`."],"redFlags":["tries to add extra instance fields or extend another class from a record","puts validation in a separate `validate()` method that callers may skip","assumes records cannot have methods or implement interfaces"]},{"id":15,"category":"Java Core","question":"Why does `sum(List<Number>)` reject a `List<Integer>`, and how do `extends` and `super` wildcards fix such signatures?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Generics are invariant, so `List<Integer>` is not a `List<Number>`; use `? extends T` when a parameter only produces values you read, `? super T` when it only consumes values you write, and never use raw types.","answer":["Arrays are covariant: `Integer[]` is a `Number[]`, and storing a `Double` into it compiles but throws `ArrayStoreException` at runtime. Generics were designed to catch that mistake during compilation, and since erasure leaves no type arguments to check at runtime, parameterized types are invariant: a `List<Integer>` cannot be used where a `List<Number>` is expected. If it were, you could add a `Double` to a list of integers through the `List<Number>` alias.","Wildcards restore flexibility at the use site. `List<? extends Number>` accepts a `List<Integer>` or `List<BigDecimal>`: you can read elements as `Number` but cannot add anything except `null`, because the compiler does not know the actual element type. `List<? super Integer>` accepts `List<Integer>`, `List<Number>` or `List<Object>`: you can add `Integer`s, but reads only give you `Object`. That is PECS: a parameter that produces values for you uses `extends`, one that consumes your values uses `super`, and one that does both stays exact as `List<T>`.","This is why JDK signatures look like `Collections.copy(List<? super T> dest, List<? extends T> src)` and `List.sort(Comparator<? super E>)`, and why a `Comparator<Object>` can sort a `List<Payment>`. Put wildcards in parameters, not return types, where they only push complexity onto callers. Avoid raw types entirely: `List raw = orders` switches off type checking, allows heap pollution and turns a wrong insert into a `ClassCastException` far away from the actual bug."],"keyPoints":["if lists were covariant, code could slip a `Double` into a list of integers","an upper-bounded wildcard lets you read elements as the bound but not add them","a lower-bounded wildcard accepts writes of the bound but reads only `Object`","raw types switch off these checks and push failures to runtime"],"examples":[{"title":"PECS in a payment API","lang":"java","code":"sealed interface Payment permits CardPayment, BankTransfer {\n    BigDecimal amount();\n}\nrecord CardPayment(BigDecimal amount, String maskedPan) implements Payment { }\nrecord BankTransfer(BigDecimal amount, String iban) implements Payment { }\n\nfinal class PaymentOps {\n\n    // Producer: we only read Payments out, so accept a list of any subtype\n    static BigDecimal total(Collection<? extends Payment> payments) {\n        BigDecimal sum = BigDecimal.ZERO;\n        for (Payment p : payments) {\n            sum = sum.add(p.amount());\n        }\n        return sum;\n    }\n\n    // Consumer: we only put CardPayments in, so accept a sink of any supertype\n    static void extractCards(Collection<? extends Payment> source, Collection<? super CardPayment> sink) {\n        for (Payment p : source) {\n            if (p instanceof CardPayment card) {\n                sink.add(card);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    static void demo() {\n        List<CardPayment> cards = List.of(new CardPayment(new BigDecimal(\"25.00\"), \"****4242\"));\n        BigDecimal t = total(cards);     // compiles only because of \"? extends\"\n        List<Payment> audit = new ArrayList<>();\n        extractCards(cards, audit);      // compiles only because of \"? super\"\n    }\n}","note":"With `Collection<Payment>` as the parameter types, both calls in `demo` would fail to compile, forcing callers into copies or casts. Wildcards make the API accept exactly what is type-safe."},{"title":"Covariant arrays, invariant generics and raw types","lang":"java","code":"Number[] amounts = new Integer[2];\namounts[0] = 1.5;                         // compiles, throws ArrayStoreException at runtime\n\nList<Integer> ints = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2));\n// List<Number> nums = ints;              // does not compile: generics are invariant\nList<? extends Number> view = ints;       // OK: read elements as Number\nNumber first = view.get(0);\n// view.add(3);                           // does not compile: actual element type unknown\n\nList<? super Integer> sink = new ArrayList<Number>();\nsink.add(42);                             // OK: an Integer fits any supertype list\nObject out = sink.get(0);                 // reads come back as Object\n\n// BAD: a raw type silences the compiler and the failure surfaces far away\nList raw = ints;\nraw.add(\"oops\");                          // only an unchecked warning\nint boom = ints.get(2);                   // ClassCastException here","note":"The array bug is caught at runtime, the generic one at compile time, and the raw type defeats both, so the exception appears at a read site that has nothing to do with the faulty write."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does `Comparator<? super E>` appear in JDK signatures such as `List.sort`?","a":"A comparator only consumes elements, so one written for a supertype works for every subtype: a `Comparator<Payment>` can sort a `List<CardPayment>`. Declaring the parameter as `Comparator<? super E>` allows that reuse, while a plain `Comparator<E>` would force a separate comparator per subtype. It is the same producer and consumer rule applied to a functional interface rather than a collection."},{"q":"Show a signature that copies elements from one list into another as flexibly as possible.","a":"`static <T> void copyInto(List<? extends T> src, List<? super T> dest) { for (T item : src) { dest.add(item); } }` The source only hands out `T`s, so it is bounded with `extends`; the destination only receives them, so it is bounded with `super`. That lets a `List<CardPayment>` be copied into a `List<Payment>` or a `List<Object>`, which `List<T>` on both sides would reject."}],"extra":["**Wildcard capture:** a method taking `List<?>` cannot call `list.set(i, list.get(j))`; delegate to a private generic helper such as `<T> void swap(List<T> list, int i, int j)` so the compiler captures the unknown type.","**Recursive bounds:** the shape of `Collections.max`, `<T extends Comparable<? super T>> T max(Collection<? extends T> c)`, is PECS applied twice; the `super` lets a subclass rely on its parent's `compareTo`, as `java.sql.Timestamp` does through `java.util.Date`.","**Erasure consequences:** you cannot write `new T[]`, `instanceof List<String>` on an `Object` or overload `process(List<Order>)` with `process(List<Refund>)`; pass a `Class<T>` or a type token (like Jackson's `TypeReference`) when runtime type information is needed.","**Use-site versus declaration-site variance:** Java expresses variance at each use with wildcards, while Kotlin declares it once on the type (`out T`, `in T`); the PECS reasoning is identical.","**@SafeVarargs:** generic varargs create a generic array and a possible heap pollution warning; methods that only read their varargs, like `List.of`, can be annotated with `@SafeVarargs` when they are static, final or private."],"redFlags":["treats generic lists like arrays and does not know arrays fail at runtime with `ArrayStoreException`","puts wildcards in return types, forcing every caller to deal with them","declares `List<Object>` as if it accepted any list"]},{"id":16,"category":"Java Core","question":"Sealed types, records, pattern matching and switch expressions: what problems do they solve in domain code?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Together they let you model a closed set of outcomes as plain data: a sealed interface lists every variant, records carry exactly the fields each variant needs, and a pattern-matching switch expression deconstructs them and must cover every case. The compiler then turns \"we forgot to handle the new payment state\" from a production incident into a compile error.","answer":["Domain code is full of closed alternatives: a payment is authorized, declined, pending review or failed at the provider; a credit application is approved, declined or referred. The traditional encodings are weak. A status enum plus nullable fields allows illegal combinations (a declined payment with an auth code) and forces null checks everywhere. Exceptions for expected outcomes mix business results with failures. Classic polymorphism pushes HTTP mapping, metrics and persistence methods onto domain classes. Sealed types with records model the alternatives directly: `sealed interface PaymentResult permits Authorized, Declined, PendingReview, ProviderError`, where each record holds only its own data, so an `Authorized` without an auth code cannot be constructed.","A switch expression yields a value, uses `->` arms without fall-through, and must be exhaustive. Over a sealed type the compiler knows every permitted subtype, so a switch that covers them all needs no `default`, and adding a fifth variant breaks the build at every switch that does not handle it. Type patterns (`case Declined d`) bind a typed variable, record patterns (`case Authorized(var id, Money(var value, var currency), var code)`) deconstruct nested records, and guards (`when value.compareTo(LIMIT) > 0`) refine a case. A case dominated by an earlier, broader one is a compile error, so specific cases come first. `case null` handles null explicitly; without it the switch throws `NullPointerException`. Since Java 22 the unnamed pattern `_` skips components you do not need. Permitted subclasses must be `final`, `sealed` or `non-sealed` and live in the same module (or package, in the unnamed module); records are implicitly final.","The value comes from not writing `default`. A `default` arm switches off exhaustiveness checking, so a new `PartiallyAuthorized` variant falls into \"unknown\" at run time instead of failing compilation. Put the switches in the layers that need a decision (mapping to HTTP status, choosing a retry policy, writing an audit event) and keep the records plain data. If code compiled against an older hierarchy meets a new subtype at run time, for example a shared library upgraded without recompiling its users, the switch throws `MatchException`; switch expressions over enums behave the same way. At the boundaries, configure serialization explicitly: map the sealed interface for Jackson with `@JsonTypeInfo` and `@JsonSubTypes` (or a custom deserializer), test the round trip, and decide what an unknown type name in a payload should do."],"keyPoints":["sealed types with records make illegal states unrepresentable","an exhaustive switch without default turns a new variant into a compile error","record patterns and guards replace instanceof chains and casts"],"examples":[{"title":"An exhaustive switch over payment results","lang":"java","code":"public sealed interface PaymentResult permits Authorized, Declined, PendingReview, ProviderError {}\n\npublic record Authorized(String paymentId, Money amount, String authCode) implements PaymentResult {}\npublic record Declined(String paymentId, DeclineReason reason) implements PaymentResult {}\npublic record PendingReview(String paymentId, Duration sla) implements PaymentResult {}\npublic record ProviderError(String provider, int httpStatus, boolean retryable) implements PaymentResult {}\n\npublic record Money(BigDecimal value, String currency) {}\npublic enum DeclineReason { INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS, CARD_EXPIRED, FRAUD_SUSPECTED }\n\nfinal class PaymentOutcomeHandler {\n    private static final BigDecimal TREASURY_LIMIT = new BigDecimal(\"10000\");\n\n    NextStep next(PaymentResult result) {\n        return switch (result) {                           // no default: every variant is listed\n            case Authorized(var id, Money(var value, var currency), var code)\n                    when value.compareTo(TREASURY_LIMIT) > 0 -> NextStep.notifyTreasury(id, value, currency);\n            case Authorized a -> NextStep.capture(a.paymentId(), a.authCode());\n            case Declined(var id, var reason) -> switch (reason) {  // exhaustive over the enum too\n                case INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS, CARD_EXPIRED -> NextStep.askCustomer(id, reason);\n                case FRAUD_SUSPECTED -> NextStep.block(id);\n            };\n            case PendingReview(var id, var sla) -> NextStep.scheduleCheck(id, sla);\n            case ProviderError(var provider, var status, var retryable)\n                    when retryable -> NextStep.retry(provider);\n            case ProviderError e -> NextStep.fail(e.provider(), e.httpStatus());\n        };\n    }\n}","note":"Guarded cases must come before the unguarded case for the same type; swapping the two `Authorized` arms is a compile error because the guarded one would be dominated. Add a `PartiallyAuthorized` record to `permits` and this method stops compiling until someone decides what it means."},{"title":"Mapping to HTTP at the edge, and why default hurts","lang":"java","code":"@RestController\nclass PaymentController {\n    private final PaymentService payments;\n\n    PaymentController(PaymentService payments) { this.payments = payments; }\n\n    @PostMapping(\"/payments\")\n    ResponseEntity<?> create(@Valid @RequestBody CreatePaymentRequest request) {\n        return switch (payments.authorize(request)) {\n            case Authorized a -> ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(PaymentView.from(a));\n            case Declined(_, var reason) -> ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.PAYMENT_REQUIRED)\n                    .body(ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.PAYMENT_REQUIRED, reason.name()));\n            case PendingReview(var id, _) -> ResponseEntity.accepted()      // _ needs Java 22+\n                    .location(URI.create(\"/payments/\" + id)).build();\n            case ProviderError(_, _, var retryable) when retryable ->\n                    ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE).header(\"Retry-After\", \"5\").build();\n            case ProviderError e -> ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_GATEWAY).build();\n        };\n    }\n}\n\n// BAD: compiles today, and keeps compiling when a new variant is added\nString label(PaymentResult r) {\n    return switch (r) {\n        case Authorized a -> \"authorized\";\n        case Declined d -> \"declined\";\n        default -> \"unknown\";              // PartiallyAuthorized silently becomes \"unknown\"\n    };\n}\n\n// The old style the patterns replace: casts and no exhaustiveness\nif (r instanceof Declined) {\n    Declined d = (Declined) r;\n    log.info(\"payment {} declined: {}\", d.paymentId(), d.reason());\n}","note":"The controller switch has no `default`, so a new variant fails compilation exactly where an HTTP mapping decision is needed. The `default` version and the `instanceof` chain both keep compiling forever, which is the problem."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Sealed types with pattern matching or polymorphic methods: how do you choose?","a":"Polymorphism makes adding variants cheap and adding operations expensive, because each new operation touches every class; sealed types with switches make the opposite trade. When the set of variants is closed and stable (payment outcomes, decision results) but many layers need different operations on it (HTTP mapping, metrics, audit), pattern matching keeps those concerns out of the domain records. When other teams or plugins add variants, keep an open interface with polymorphic methods."},{"q":"What happens when a sealed hierarchy gets a new subtype but a client is not recompiled?","a":"The old bytecode has no arm for the new class, so the switch throws `MatchException` when it meets one at run time; exhaustiveness is guaranteed only for code compiled against the current hierarchy. Across service boundaries the equivalent problem is a new JSON type name reaching an old consumer, so treat a new variant like any contract change: deploy readers before writers and decide explicitly how unknown types are handled."}],"extra":["**Exhaustive enums too:** a switch expression over an enum without `default` must list every constant, which gives simple closed sets without data the same compile-time safety.","**instanceof patterns:** `if (result instanceof Authorized(var id, var amount, var code))` binds components without a cast, and the bindings are in scope wherever the match is definitely true, including after `if (!(x instanceof Declined d)) return;`.","**yield in block arms:** an arm with a block returns its value with `yield`, for example `case Declined d -> { audit(d); yield NextStep.block(d.paymentId()); }`; `return` is not allowed inside a switch expression.","**Persistence mapping:** records cannot be JPA entities, so persist an outcome as an entity with a type column (or a JSON column) and convert it to the sealed type at the repository boundary.","**Keep records as data:** validation belongs in compact constructors and behavior specific to one variant can be a method on that record, while cross-cutting decisions (HTTP, retries, metrics) live in switches at the edges."],"redFlags":["Adds a default arm to every switch over a sealed type","models outcomes as a status enum plus nullable fields and cannot say why that is weaker","confuses switch expressions with fall-through switch statements"]},{"id":17,"category":"Java Core","question":"An entity hashes on its generated id, and children vanish from a `Set` after save. Why, and what would you use?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A generated id is null until persist and then changes, so a `hashCode` built on it corrupts hash collections; prefer an immutable business key or assigned UUID, or id-based `equals` with a constant `hashCode`.","answer":["With `@GeneratedValue`, a new entity has `id == null` until `persist` (for `IDENTITY`, until the insert runs). If `hashCode` is derived from the id, its value changes during the entity's lifecycle, so any `HashSet` or `HashMap` that already holds the entity, including the `Set` behind a `@OneToMany`, now has it in the wrong bucket. And if `equals` treats two null ids as equal, two different new order lines collapse into one set element before they are saved.","Three approaches work. First, a stable business key set at construction and never changed (payment reference, external order number), mapped with Hibernate's `@NaturalId` and a unique constraint. Second, an application-assigned UUID generated in the constructor and used as the id, so identity exists before persistence. Third, if you must use the generated id, make `equals` compare ids only when non-null and return a constant `hashCode` per class; that stays correct across the lifecycle, and child entity sets are small enough that one bucket does not matter.","Hibernate adds two more concerns. Lazy references are proxies (runtime subclasses), so `getClass() != o.getClass()` fails when a proxy meets the real entity; use `instanceof` or `Hibernate.getClass`, and read the other side through getters because a proxy's own fields are empty. And never generate entity equality with Lombok `@Data` or `@EqualsAndHashCode` over all fields: it touches lazy associations, causing extra queries, `LazyInitializationException` or infinite recursion across bidirectional links."],"keyPoints":["the id is null for a new entity and only assigned on persist or flush","an object whose hash moves while stored is lost to its own collection","an assigned UUID or natural key gives identity from construction","id-based `equals` with a constant `hashCode` trades lookup speed for safety"],"examples":[{"title":"Business key equality that is proxy-safe","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\n@Table(name = \"payments\")\npublic class Payment {\n\n    @Id\n    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE)\n    private Long id;\n\n    @NaturalId\n    @Column(name = \"payment_reference\", nullable = false, unique = true, updatable = false)\n    private String paymentReference;\n\n    protected Payment() { } // required by JPA\n\n    public Payment(String paymentReference) {\n        this.paymentReference = Objects.requireNonNull(paymentReference);\n    }\n\n    public Long getId() { return id; }\n    public String getPaymentReference() { return paymentReference; }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (this == o) return true;\n        if (!(o instanceof Payment other)) return false; // also matches Hibernate proxies\n        return paymentReference.equals(other.getPaymentReference()); // getter, not field\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int hashCode() {\n        return paymentReference.hashCode(); // known before persist, never changes\n    }\n}","note":"The reference exists from construction, so the hash is identical before and after `persist`, across sessions and for detached copies. Accessing `other` through its getter makes the comparison work when `other` is an uninitialized proxy."},{"title":"Generated id only: the safe variant and what the naive one breaks","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\npublic class OrderLine {\n\n    @Id\n    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE)\n    private Long id;\n\n    public Long getId() { return id; }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (this == o) return true;\n        if (!(o instanceof OrderLine other)) return false;\n        return id != null && id.equals(other.getId()); // transient lines equal only themselves\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int hashCode() {\n        return OrderLine.class.hashCode(); // GOOD: constant across persist and merge\n    }\n\n    // BAD alternative: return Objects.hash(id);\n    // With it:\n    //   order.getLines().add(line);       hashed while id is null\n    //   entityManager.persist(order);     cascade assigns line.id\n    //   order.getLines().contains(line);  false, it sits under the old hash\n}","note":"The constant hash puts all lines of one order in a single bucket, which is irrelevant for the small collections entities hold, and in exchange the set never loses an element when ids are assigned."}],"followUps":[{"q":"If `equals` compares ids, how should it treat two unsaved entities whose ids are both null?","a":"As different, unless they are the same instance: the usual pattern returns true for `this == o`, and otherwise only when `id != null && id.equals(other.getId())`. Comparing with `Objects.equals(id, other.id)` instead makes every new entity equal to every other new entity, so adding three new line items to a `Set` keeps only one. Pair it with a constant `hashCode` so the bucket does not move when the id is assigned."},{"q":"Why can a lazy-loaded reference fail an equality check against the same row loaded eagerly?","a":"A lazy `@ManyToOne` or `getReference()` returns a generated subclass of the entity, so a `getClass()` comparison fails, and reading `other.id` directly sees the proxy's own uninitialized fields. Use `instanceof` or `Hibernate.getClass(o)` for the type check and call getters such as `other.getId()`, which a proxy answers without loading the row. Within one persistence context Hibernate returns one instance per row, so this mainly bites detached objects and cross-session comparisons."}],"extra":["**Persistence context guarantee:** within one `EntityManager`, Hibernate returns the same Java instance for the same row, so identity equality already works inside a single transaction; custom `equals` matters when you compare detached entities, mix sessions or keep entities in sets across requests.","**Assigned UUIDs and Spring Data:** if you assign the id in the constructor, `SimpleJpaRepository.save` sees a non-null id, treats the entity as existing and calls `merge`, which costs an extra `SELECT`; implement `Persistable<UUID>` with an `isNew` flag, or add a `@Version` field, to get a plain `persist`.","**Time-ordered UUIDs:** random version 4 UUID primary keys scatter inserts across a PostgreSQL B-tree index; time-ordered version 7 UUIDs keep inserts mostly append-only while still being generated in the application.","**Keep equals and hashCode non-final:** a proxy cannot intercept `final` methods, so a `final equals` runs against the proxy's empty fields; if you make them final, access state only through getters.","**Natural id lookups:** with `@NaturalId`, `session.bySimpleNaturalId(Payment.class).load(reference)` (deprecated since Hibernate 7.3 in favor of `session.find(Payment.class, reference, KeyType.NATURAL)`) resolves the entity by its business key through Hibernate's natural id resolution, so the key used for equality doubles as an efficient lookup path."],"redFlags":["relies on Lombok `@Data` or generated equality over every field, including lazy associations","treats two unsaved entities as equal because both ids are null","uses `getClass()` in `equals` without thinking about Hibernate proxies"]},{"id":18,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"Explain encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance and polymorphism with backend examples.","level":"Basic","tldr":"Encapsulation keeps state and its invariants behind methods (an `Account` whose balance changes only through `withdraw`); abstraction exposes what a component does and hides how (`PaymentGateway`); inheritance reuses and specializes a type (an exception hierarchy); polymorphism lets one call run different implementations chosen at run time. In backend code they keep rules in one place and keep callers independent of details.","answer":["**Encapsulation** is about protecting invariants, not about writing getters and setters. An `Account` with `setBalance` is a data bag: any service can make the balance negative or skip the ledger entry. An encapsulated `Account` exposes `withdraw(amount)`, checks status and limits inside, and keeps its fields private, so the rule lives in exactly one place. **Abstraction** is about what callers need to know: a `PaymentGateway.charge(request)` interface hides HTTP clients, request signing, retries and provider error codes behind a simple model. The two are related but distinct: encapsulation hides an object's internal state, abstraction hides a component's implementation behind a simpler contract.","**Inheritance** creates an is-a relationship in which a subtype reuses and specializes a supertype. Its legitimate backend uses are narrow: exception hierarchies (`PaymentDeclinedException extends PaymentException`, so one handler covers the family), framework base classes designed for extension, and interface hierarchies. Implementation inheritance just to reuse code couples subclasses to parent internals, which is why composition is the default. **Polymorphism** is the payoff: code written against `PaymentGateway` calls `charge`, and the runtime class decides what happens. Java has three forms: subtype polymorphism (overriding, dispatched at run time), parametric polymorphism (generics such as `Repository<T, ID>`), and ad hoc polymorphism (overloading, resolved at compile time).","In a Spring service these ideas appear everywhere. Encapsulation decides whether business rules live in entities and value objects or are scattered across services that call setters, the anemic domain model. Abstraction is why services depend on repository and client interfaces, and why tests can swap in fakes. Polymorphism is how much of Spring works: an injected `List<PaymentGateway>` lets new providers plug in, and the proxy that adds `@Transactional` or caching is a different class standing in for yours behind the same type. A strong answer names each pillar's benefit (invariants in one place, decoupling, reuse of a contract, substitutability) and its failure mode: public setters everywhere, leaky abstractions that expose provider-specific errors, deep inheritance trees, and `instanceof` chains that replace polymorphism."],"keyPoints":["encapsulation protects invariants, not just fields","abstraction hides how a component works behind what it does","inheritance is for real is-a relationships; polymorphism keeps callers unchanged as implementations vary"],"examples":[{"title":"Encapsulation: setters vs an entity that guards its invariants","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: anemic entity; every caller must remember the rules\n@Entity\nclass AccountRecord {\n    @Id Long id;\n    BigDecimal balance;\n    AccountStatus status;\n    public void setBalance(BigDecimal balance) { this.balance = balance; }\n    public void setStatus(AccountStatus status) { this.status = status; }\n}\n\n// GOOD: state changes only through methods that enforce the rules\n@Entity\npublic class Account {\n    @Id private Long id;\n    private BigDecimal balance = BigDecimal.ZERO;\n    private BigDecimal overdraftLimit = BigDecimal.ZERO;\n    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING) private AccountStatus status = AccountStatus.ACTIVE;\n\n    protected Account() { }                          // for JPA only\n\n    public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {\n        if (amount.signum() <= 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"amount must be positive\");\n        }\n        if (status != AccountStatus.ACTIVE) {\n            throw new AccountFrozenException(id);\n        }\n        if (balance.subtract(amount).compareTo(overdraftLimit.negate()) < 0) {\n            throw new InsufficientFundsException(id, amount);\n        }\n        balance = balance.subtract(amount);\n    }\n\n    public void freeze() { status = AccountStatus.FROZEN; }\n\n    public BigDecimal balance() { return balance; }  // read access, no setter\n}","note":"Every withdrawal rule lives in one method, so a new endpoint or a batch job cannot bypass it. JPA still populates the private fields through field access; the protected constructor exists only for the persistence provider."},{"title":"Abstraction, polymorphism and inheritance working together","lang":"java","code":"// Abstraction: callers see what a gateway does, not how\npublic interface PaymentGateway {\n    String providerId();\n    ChargeResult charge(ChargeRequest request);\n}\n\n@Component\nclass CardGateway implements PaymentGateway {\n    public String providerId() { return \"card\"; }\n    public ChargeResult charge(ChargeRequest request) {\n        // HTTP call, request signing and card network error codes stay in here\n        return ChargeResult.approved(request.orderId());\n    }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass BankTransferGateway implements PaymentGateway {\n    public String providerId() { return \"bank-transfer\"; }\n    public ChargeResult charge(ChargeRequest request) {\n        throw new PaymentDeclinedException(request.orderId(), \"account closed\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Inheritance: an exception family that callers can handle as one type\npublic class PaymentException extends RuntimeException {\n    public PaymentException(String message) { super(message); }\n}\n\npublic class PaymentDeclinedException extends PaymentException {\n    public PaymentDeclinedException(String orderId, String reason) {\n        super(\"Order \" + orderId + \" declined: \" + reason);\n    }\n}\n\n@RestControllerAdvice\nclass PaymentErrors {\n    @ExceptionHandler(PaymentException.class)       // handles every subtype\n    ProblemDetail handle(PaymentException e) {\n        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.PAYMENT_REQUIRED, e.getMessage());\n    }\n}\n// Polymorphism: CheckoutService calls gateway.charge(request) and the runtime class decides","note":"Adding a provider is a new class, not an edit to the checkout code, and a decline from any gateway maps to the same HTTP response because the handler targets the base exception type. The interface is also the test seam: a fake gateway needs no HTTP at all."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Is a class with private fields and public getters and setters encapsulated?","a":"Only technically. If every field has a public setter, callers can still put the object into any state, so the invariants live in whatever services call the setters and the class protects nothing. Real encapsulation exposes operations that mean something in the domain (`withdraw`, `freeze`, `addLine`), validates inside them, and offers read access without exposing mutable internals. Getters and setters are fine on DTOs, which are data carriers by design."},{"q":"Where does polymorphism show up in a Spring application?","a":"Injection by interface means the bean you receive may not be your class at all: it can be a proxy that adds transactions, security or caching around your implementation, while callers keep calling the same methods. Event listeners, `HandlerInterceptor`s, `Converter`s and `@ExceptionHandler` selection by exception type are all dispatch on runtime type. It is also why `getClass()` checks on beans are fragile: the runtime class is often a generated subclass."}],"extra":["**Tell, don't ask:** calling `account.withdraw(amount)` instead of reading the balance, checking it in a service and calling a setter keeps the decision next to the data and removes duplicated check-then-act logic.","**Leaky abstractions:** a `PaymentGateway` that throws a provider SDK's exception types or returns its raw error codes couples every caller to that provider; translate errors into your own types inside the adapter.","**Substitutability is the test:** a subtype must work everywhere its base type is accepted; if it throws on inherited methods or ignores parameters, the hierarchy is wrong and composition is the better tool.","**Polymorphism vs instanceof:** a chain of `instanceof` checks over an open hierarchy suggests the behavior belongs on the types; over a closed, sealed hierarchy an exhaustive pattern switch is the deliberate alternative.","**Package-private is encapsulation too:** keeping implementation classes package-private and exposing only interfaces and a few public types stops other packages from depending on internals; Spring can still instantiate package-private `@Component` classes."],"redFlags":["Defines encapsulation as private fields plus getters and setters","uses inheritance mainly for code reuse","cannot give examples beyond animals and shapes"]},{"id":19,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"A `PremiumAccount extends Account` hierarchy keeps breaking when the base class changes. Would you refactor it, and toward what?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Start with composition, which ties you only to another type's public contract and lets you swap collaborators at runtime; subclass only when every child can truly stand in for its parent and the parent was deliberately built and documented for extension.","answer":["Inheritance couples a subclass to its parent's implementation, not just its API. When the base class changes how its methods call each other, subclasses break: the **fragile base class** problem. The classic case is overriding `add` and `addAll` on a `HashSet` to count insertions and getting double counts, because the inherited `addAll` calls `add`. Inheritance also fixes behavior at compile time and exposes every inherited public method, including ones that make no sense for the subtype.","Composition holds a reference to a collaborator and forwards to its public contract. You choose which operations to expose, can swap implementations at runtime (strategy, decorator), test with a simple fake, and the collaborator can change internally without breaking you. That is why Effective Java says to favor composition over inheritance, and to design and document for inheritance or else prohibit it by making classes `final`.","Inheritance is right when the subtype is truly substitutable (it honors every contract of the base), the base contract is stable, and the base class is designed for extension, typically a template method with protected hooks, as in Spring's `OncePerRequestFilter` or `AbstractRoutingDataSource`. Inheriting just to reuse a few helper methods is the red flag. In modern Java, sealed interfaces with records model closed hierarchies (payment methods, decision outcomes) through interface subtyping rather than implementation inheritance."],"keyPoints":["a subclass depends on its parent's internals, so base changes ripple down","a wrapped collaborator is reached only through its public interface","delegation lets behavior be swapped or combined at runtime","subclassing fits only when every child honors the parent's contract"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: fragile base class vs composition","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: inheriting for reuse couples you to HashSet's internal use of add()\nclass CountingOrderSet extends HashSet<Order> {\n    private int added;\n\n    @Override public boolean add(Order order) { added++; return super.add(order); }\n\n    @Override public boolean addAll(Collection<? extends Order> orders) {\n        added += orders.size();\n        return super.addAll(orders);     // inherited addAll calls add(): every order counted twice\n    }\n\n    int added() { return added; }\n}\n\n// GOOD: compose, and expose only what the domain needs\nfinal class OrderBatch {\n    private final Set<Order> orders = new HashSet<>();\n    private int added;\n\n    boolean add(Order order) {\n        boolean changed = orders.add(order);\n        if (changed) added++;\n        return changed;\n    }\n\n    void addAll(Collection<Order> batch) { batch.forEach(this::add); }\n\n    int added() { return added; }\n\n    Set<Order> view() { return Collections.unmodifiableSet(orders); }\n}","note":"The BAD class also exposes `remove`, `clear` and `retainAll`, which the domain never wanted. The GOOD class does not care how `HashSet` is implemented and could switch to a `LinkedHashSet` without affecting callers."},{"title":"Subtyping that is appropriate: a sealed hierarchy","lang":"java","code":"sealed interface PaymentMethod permits Card, BankTransfer, Wallet {}\n\nrecord Card(String token, YearMonth expiry) implements PaymentMethod {}\nrecord BankTransfer(String iban) implements PaymentMethod {}\nrecord Wallet(String provider, String walletId) implements PaymentMethod {}\n\nfinal class FeeCalculator {\n\n    BigDecimal feeFor(PaymentMethod method, BigDecimal amount) {\n        return switch (method) {\n            case Card card -> amount.multiply(new BigDecimal(\"0.014\"));\n            case BankTransfer transfer -> new BigDecimal(\"0.20\");\n            case Wallet wallet -> amount.multiply(new BigDecimal(\"0.019\"));\n        };\n    }\n}","note":"Each record truly is a payment method and shares no implementation, only a type. Because the hierarchy is sealed, the `switch` is exhaustive and adding a new method is a compile error until every switch handles it."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does an `InstrumentedHashSet extends HashSet` overcount when `addAll` is called?","a":"`HashSet.addAll` is inherited from `AbstractCollection`, which calls `add` for each element, so overriding both `addAll` and `add` to increment a counter counts every element twice. The subclass silently depends on a self-use detail the base class never promised, and a future JDK could change it. Wrapping a `Set` and forwarding calls counts correctly regardless of how the wrapped set is implemented."},{"q":"When is a template-method superclass still a good idea in your own code?","a":"When subclasses share state and a fixed algorithm you control, such as a `final charge()` method calling a protected `doCharge()` hook, and all subclasses live in one module or team. Document the extension points and keep everything else `final` or private. Outside that situation, an interface plus composition is the safer default."}],"extra":["**Default methods are not free reuse:** interface default methods share behavior without state, but a default added later is silently overridden by any implementation that already has a method with that signature, possibly with different semantics.","**JPA inheritance cost:** `SINGLE_TABLE`, `JOINED` and `TABLE_PER_CLASS` mappings add nullable columns, joins or unions to every polymorphic query; many teams prefer composition with an embedded type or a type column plus a strategy.","**Decorators beat subclasses for cross-cutting concerns:** caching, retries or metrics around a `PaymentGateway` are cleaner as a decorator implementing the same interface than as a subclass of one concrete gateway.","**Spring proxies:** Spring AOP uses composition (JDK dynamic proxies) or generated subclasses (CGLIB); the subclass approach is why `final` classes and methods cannot be advised through CGLIB proxies."],"redFlags":["extends a concrete class such as `HashSet` to add counting and gets double counts","treats `protected` fields as a safe, stable extension point"]},{"id":20,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"An `OrderService` changes whenever finance, ops or marketing asks for something. What principle is strained, and how would you reason about it?","level":"Basic","tldr":"SRP means a module has one reason to change because it serves one actor or business responsibility; it is about cohesion around who requests changes, not about making classes small.","answer":["Robert Martin's refined wording is that a module should be responsible to one, and only one, **actor**: the group of stakeholders whose requests change it. The useful question is not \"does this class do one thing?\" but \"who would ask me to change this code, and for what reasons?\" If pricing rules come from the commercial team, the settlement file format from finance and the email template from marketing, putting them in one class means a change for one actor risks breaking the others, and their changes collide in merges and releases.","The goal is **high cohesion**: things that change together live together; things that change for different reasons are separated. A 400-line `CreditPolicy` class encoding one coherent set of lending rules can satisfy SRP perfectly, while ten tiny classes that must always change together violate its spirit and cause shotgun surgery. Size is a symptom, not the rule.","Practical signals of a violation: a class named with \"And\" or \"Manager\", tests that need many unrelated mocks, git history where one file changes for unrelated tickets, and several teams editing the same service. The fix is to split along change axes (domain rules, persistence, integration, notification) with a thin application service that orchestrates the use case."],"keyPoints":["responsibility is measured by who asks for changes, not by line count","code serving different stakeholders should live apart so their changes do not collide","an orchestrating use case can call many parts and still have one job"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: one service changed by three different actors","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: validation rules (product), SQL (DBA) and customer emails (CRM) in one class\n@Service\npublic class OrderServiceBad {\n    private final JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;\n    private final JavaMailSender mailSender;\n\n    public OrderServiceBad(JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate, JavaMailSender mailSender) {\n        this.jdbcTemplate = jdbcTemplate;\n        this.mailSender = mailSender;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public Order placeOrder(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {\n        if (cmd.lines().isEmpty()) throw new InvalidOrderException(\"order has no lines\");\n        Order order = Order.from(cmd);\n        jdbcTemplate.update(\"insert into orders (id, customer_id, total) values (?, ?, ?)\",\n                order.id(), order.customerId(), order.total());\n        mailSender.send(confirmationEmail(order));   // SMTP failure rolls back a valid order\n        return order;\n    }\n\n    private SimpleMailMessage confirmationEmail(Order order) {\n        SimpleMailMessage message = new SimpleMailMessage();\n        message.setSubject(\"Order \" + order.id() + \" confirmed\");\n        return message;\n    }\n}","note":"Beyond mixed responsibilities, the failure semantics are wrong: an email outage now blocks order placement."},{"title":"GOOD: orchestrating use case plus focused collaborators","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class PlaceOrderService {\n    private final OrderValidator validator;           // business rules\n    private final OrderRepository orders;             // persistence\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;   // integration boundary\n\n    public PlaceOrderService(OrderValidator validator, OrderRepository orders,\n                             ApplicationEventPublisher events) {\n        this.validator = validator;\n        this.orders = orders;\n        this.events = events;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public Order placeOrder(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {\n        validator.validate(cmd);\n        Order order = orders.save(Order.from(cmd));\n        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlaced(order.id(), order.customerId()));\n        return order;\n    }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass OrderConfirmationNotifier {\n    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)\n    void on(OrderPlaced event) {\n        // render the template and send the email; failures are retried, never roll back the order\n    }\n}","note":"`AFTER_COMMIT` sends the email only for committed orders. If the notification must never be lost, write an outbox row in the same transaction instead of relying on an in-memory event."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Validation, saving and the confirmation email all sit in one method. Which piece would you separate first, and why?","a":"The email, because it changes for a different team and has different failure semantics: a failed email must not roll back a valid order. Move it behind an `OrderPlaced` event handled after commit with `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)`, or an outbox if delivery must be guaranteed. Then push business rules into the `Order` aggregate and persistence behind a repository, leaving the service to orchestrate."},{"q":"How can version control history reveal a class with too many responsibilities?","a":"Look for a file that changes in commits for unrelated tickets or teams, and for merge conflicts between people working on unrelated features. Change-coupling analysis over `git log` shows which concerns keep moving together. A class touched by both pricing work and notification work is a strong candidate to split."}],"extra":["**The actor test:** ask who requests changes to this code and who approves them; if the answer is two different teams or roles, you likely have two responsibilities in one module.","**SRP scales up:** the same reasoning applies to packages and services; a microservice owned by one team but changed by three is an SRP problem at the architecture level, closely tied to Conway's law.","**Do not split what changes together:** the Common Closure Principle is SRP applied to packages; spreading one cohesive rule set across many classes means every business change touches five files.","**AFTER_COMMIT listener detail:** when a `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)` runs, the transaction has already committed, so database writes it makes need `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)` or they will not be committed.","**Testing payoff:** once split, validation rules get fast unit tests with no mocks, persistence gets `@DataJpaTest` or Testcontainers tests, and the orchestrator needs only a few collaborators."],"redFlags":["splits by technical layer only and still mixes billing and shipping rules in one service","cannot explain why a failed email should not roll back an order"]},{"id":21,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"Each new market adds another branch to a central `calculateFee` method. How would you restructure it so rules arrive as new code?","level":"Basic","tldr":"OCP means designing stable extension points, such as policy or strategy interfaces, so a new business rule is added as new code rather than another branch in a central if/else, without abstracting what never varies.","answer":["The principle says code should be open for extension but closed for modification. In business systems it matters most where variation is frequent: discount types, credit policies per product or market, fee schedules, payment providers. If every new rule means editing the same `switch` in a central `PricingService`, each change risks breaking existing rules, forces retesting all of them and creates merge conflicts between teams.","The usual tool is a stable abstraction at the point of variation: a `DiscountPolicy` or `CreditPolicy` interface, one implementation per rule, and a coordinator that iterates over them or looks them up. In Spring, injecting `List<DiscountPolicy>` means a new rule is a new `@Component` and the coordinator never changes. How results combine (sum, best single discount, first rejection wins) is itself a policy and should be explicit.","The trade-off is indirection. Wrapping each class in its own interface, or building a plugin framework for a rule that has never varied, is speculative generality. Introduce the extension point when the second or third variant appears, where the business actually varies. Consider alternatives too: rules business users change weekly may belong in data (a rule table or decision engine) rather than code, and for a closed set of types where you add operations more often than types, a sealed interface with an exhaustive `switch` is the better trade."],"keyPoints":["a small policy interface is the stable point that new rules plug into","adding a rule means adding a class while the dispatcher stays untouched","only abstract where variation has actually happened or is clearly coming"],"examples":[{"title":"Discount policies injected as a list","lang":"java","code":"public interface DiscountPolicy {\n    boolean appliesTo(Order order);\n    BigDecimal discountFor(Order order);\n}\n\n@Component\nclass LoyaltyTierDiscount implements DiscountPolicy {\n    public boolean appliesTo(Order order) { return order.customer().tier() == Tier.GOLD; }\n    public BigDecimal discountFor(Order order) { return order.subtotal().multiply(new BigDecimal(\"0.05\")); }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass BulkOrderDiscount implements DiscountPolicy {\n    public boolean appliesTo(Order order) { return order.lines().size() >= 20; }\n    public BigDecimal discountFor(Order order) { return new BigDecimal(\"25.00\"); }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass PricingService {\n    private final List<DiscountPolicy> policies;   // Spring injects every DiscountPolicy bean\n\n    PricingService(List<DiscountPolicy> policies) { this.policies = policies; }\n\n    BigDecimal totalDiscount(Order order) {\n        BigDecimal sum = policies.stream()\n                .filter(policy -> policy.appliesTo(order))\n                .map(policy -> policy.discountFor(order))\n                .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);\n        return sum.min(order.subtotal().multiply(new BigDecimal(\"0.30\")));   // explicit cap: 30%\n    }\n}","note":"A new campaign is a new `@Component`; `PricingService` and the existing policies are untouched. The combination rule (sum with a 30% cap) is explicit and tested once."},{"title":"Credit policies keyed by product","lang":"java","code":"public interface CreditPolicy {\n    String product();                                   // \"CARD\", \"LOAN\", \"BNPL\"\n    Decision evaluate(CreditApplication application);\n}\n\n@Service\nclass CreditDecisionService {\n    private final Map<String, CreditPolicy> byProduct;\n\n    CreditDecisionService(List<CreditPolicy> policies) {\n        this.byProduct = policies.stream()\n                .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableMap(CreditPolicy::product, Function.identity()));\n    }\n\n    Decision decide(CreditApplication application) {\n        CreditPolicy policy = byProduct.get(application.product());\n        if (policy == null) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"No credit policy for \" + application.product());\n        }\n        return policy.evaluate(application);\n    }\n}","note":"`toUnmodifiableMap` throws on duplicate keys, so two beans claiming the same product fail at startup instead of in production traffic."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Two fee policies both apply to an order. Where should the combination rule live?","a":"In one explicit place, such as the pricing service or a dedicated combiner, never implied by bean order. State the rule as code (sum with a cap, best single discount, or first rejection wins) and test it separately from the individual policies. Record which policies applied in the result so support and audit can explain the outcome."},{"q":"Which parts of a fee rule belong in configuration rather than in a new class?","a":"Numbers and lists that change without new logic: percentages, thresholds, currency lists and effective dates. A new class is justified only when the calculation itself differs, such as tiered pricing versus a flat rate. Keeping parameters in configuration or a table lets the business tune rules without a deploy, while the code keeps a small, testable set of algorithms."}],"extra":["**Ordering is part of the contract:** when policy order matters (first match wins), make it explicit with `@Order` or an `order()` method and test it; relying on bean registration order is fragile.","**Expression problem:** polymorphic OCP makes new types cheap and new operations expensive; sealed types plus pattern matching make the opposite trade, so choose by which axis changes more often.","**Shadow mode:** separate policy classes make it easy to run a new credit rule in parallel, logging its decision next to the live one, before switching traffic to it.","**Auditability:** in credit and payments, record which policy and version produced each decision; policy objects make that a natural field instead of a reconstruction from branches.","**Closed means verified:** the point is not that code never changes, but that tested, deployed behavior is not edited to add a rule, which shrinks review scope and regression risk."],"redFlags":["replaces the `if/else` chain with a `switch` and calls the design open for extension","builds a plugin framework before a second rule exists","hard-codes thresholds in each policy class, so tuning needs a deploy"]},{"id":22,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"A reporting module depends on a 30-method `PaymentService` but calls only two. What does that cost, and how would you fix it?","level":"Basic","tldr":"A caller should see only the operations it actually needs; a wide interface couples it to unrelated changes, inflates test doubles and invites stub methods that throw `UnsupportedOperationException`, so carve interfaces along the roles their clients play rather than one method at a time.","answer":["A large interface couples every client to every operation. A reporting job that needs `dailyStats` also compiles against `refund`, so it can call it, its tests must fake a 30-method type, and a signature change it never uses still forces a change and redeploy. Implementations suffer too: a read-only replica adapter or an in-memory fake must implement write methods it cannot support, which produces `UnsupportedOperationException` stubs, an LSP violation waiting to happen.","Interface Segregation says to shape interfaces from the **client's** point of view: role interfaces such as `PaymentCommands` for checkout and refunds, and `PaymentQueries` for status pages and support tools. One class can still implement several roles, so segregation does not force duplication; it limits what each client can see. It also clarifies permissions and boundaries: a query endpoint that only has `PaymentQueries` injected cannot refund anything.","The opposite mistake is mechanical splitting: one interface per method, grouped by nothing. That multiplies types without reducing coupling. Group operations used together by the same kind of client and changing together. The signals that a split is due are concrete: different clients using disjoint subsets, fakes full of unused stubs, or implementations that can only support half the methods."],"keyPoints":["every method on a wide interface is a reason for unrelated clients to recompile or redeploy","test doubles must stub methods the code under test never calls","role interfaces follow how clients use a type, not how it is implemented"],"examples":[{"title":"Refactoring a fat PaymentService into role interfaces","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: every client sees every operation\npublic interface PaymentService {\n    Payment authorize(AuthorizeCommand command);\n    Payment capture(String paymentId);\n    Refund refund(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount);\n    Optional<Payment> findById(String paymentId);\n    Page<Payment> search(PaymentFilter filter, Pageable pageable);\n    PaymentStats dailyStats(LocalDate day);\n}\n\n// GOOD: interfaces shaped by the clients that use them\npublic interface PaymentCommands {\n    Payment authorize(AuthorizeCommand command);\n    Payment capture(String paymentId);\n    Refund refund(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount);\n}\n\npublic interface PaymentQueries {\n    Optional<PaymentView> findById(String paymentId);\n    Page<PaymentView> search(PaymentFilter filter, Pageable pageable);\n}\n\n@RestController\nclass PaymentStatusController {\n    private final PaymentQueries payments;   // cannot call refund(), and its test fake has two methods\n\n    PaymentStatusController(PaymentQueries payments) {\n        this.payments = payments;\n    }\n}","note":"The query side now returns a read model (`PaymentView`) and can be served by a read replica or projection without touching the command side. `dailyStats` moves to a reporting interface used only by the reporting job."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you split such an interface without breaking every client at once?","a":"Group methods by the clients that call them, typically commands such as authorize and refund versus queries such as find and search, and extract `PaymentCommands` and `PaymentQueries`. Let the existing class implement both and make the old interface extend both for a while, so nothing breaks. Move clients to the narrow role one at a time, then delete the old interface once nothing uses it."},{"q":"Where do oversized interfaces show up in unit tests?","a":"Mocks and fakes have to implement or stub methods the test never exercises, and hand-written fakes fill up with methods that throw. When a test needs a 30-method fake to check two calls, the class under test depends on far more than it uses. Narrow interfaces keep fakes tiny and make the real dependencies visible in the constructor."}],"extra":["**Spring Data supports it:** extend the marker interface `Repository<Order, Long>` and declare only `findById` and `save`, instead of inheriting the long method list of `JpaRepository`, including `deleteAll`.","**Read models:** a separate query interface can return DTOs or projections under `@Transactional(readOnly = true)`, which is hard while one interface returns managed entities to both sides.","**Migrating without breaking clients:** temporarily keep `interface PaymentService extends PaymentCommands, PaymentQueries`, mark it `@Deprecated`, and move clients over one at a time.","**Security surface:** narrower injected types reduce the blast radius of a bug or misuse, which matters in payment and credit systems where refund or approval rights are sensitive.","**One-method roles are fine when a client needs exactly that:** an `ExchangeRateProvider` or a `Supplier<Instant>` used as a clock is a legitimate ISP result; the problem is splitting with no client in mind."],"redFlags":["argues one big interface is simpler because there is only one bean to inject","splits by implementation detail, like `PaymentJdbcOps`, instead of by client need"]},{"id":23,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"A `CachedAccountRepository` subclass sometimes throws where the base repository never did, and callers break. What principle is violated, and how exactly?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Liskov Substitution means code written against a base type must keep working with any subtype: a subtype may not demand more, promise less, break invariants, or throw exceptions callers were never told to expect.","answer":["LSP is about behavior, not signatures. The compiler checks that a subtype has the right methods; LSP asks whether it honors the base type's **contract**: preconditions (what callers must provide), postconditions (what the method guarantees), invariants (what always holds for the object) and the exceptions callers are prepared for. A subtype may accept more and guarantee more, never the reverse: no **stronger preconditions**, no **weaker postconditions**.","A real failure: the `PaymentGateway.refund(paymentId, amount)` contract allows partial refunds. A new voucher provider adapter only supports full refunds, so it throws when the amount is less than the captured amount. It compiles, passes its own tests, and the first partial refund on that provider fails in production inside a flow that never expected the exception. The subtype strengthened the precondition. Similar failures: a caching repository whose `save` is asynchronous, so `findById` right after `save` returns empty (weakened postcondition), or an adapter that returns null where the contract says never null.","Rectangle and Square only illustrates this if you name the contract: `setWidth` promises height stays unchanged, and a square cannot keep that promise. The fix is usually to model capabilities honestly, for example a separate interface for partial refunds, and to run one **contract test suite** against every implementation."],"keyPoints":["callers rely on the base type's promises, not on the concrete class","a subtype may accept more inputs and promise more, never the reverse","new exceptions or broken invariants surface only at runtime in caller code"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: a gateway that strengthens the precondition","lang":"java","code":"public interface PaymentGateway {\n    /** Refunds any amount up to the captured amount. Never returns null. */\n    RefundResult refund(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount);\n}\n\n// BAD: callers written against PaymentGateway now fail at runtime for this provider\nabstract class VoucherGateway implements PaymentGateway {\n\n    @Override\n    public RefundResult refund(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount) {\n        if (amount.compareTo(capturedAmount(paymentId)) != 0) {\n            throw new UnsupportedOperationException(\"voucher provider supports full refunds only\");\n        }\n        return fullRefund(paymentId);\n    }\n\n    abstract BigDecimal capturedAmount(String paymentId);\n\n    abstract RefundResult fullRefund(String paymentId);\n}","note":"Nothing in the type system warns you; only a contract test that runs a partial refund against every gateway implementation would catch it before production."},{"title":"GOOD: make the capability part of the contract","lang":"java","code":"// The base contract promises only what every provider can do\npublic interface PaymentGateway {\n    RefundResult refundInFull(String paymentId);\n}\n\n// Optional capability, discoverable before calling\npublic interface PartialRefunds {\n    RefundResult refundPartially(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount);\n}\n\nclass RefundService {\n\n    RefundResult refund(PaymentGateway gateway, String paymentId,\n                        BigDecimal amount, BigDecimal captured) {\n        if (amount.compareTo(captured) == 0) {\n            return gateway.refundInFull(paymentId);\n        }\n        if (gateway instanceof PartialRefunds partial) {\n            return partial.refundPartially(paymentId, amount);\n        }\n        return RefundResult.storeCredit(paymentId, amount);   // explicit business fallback\n    }\n}","note":"Every `PaymentGateway` now honors its whole contract. Providers that can do more implement the extra interface, and the business decides the fallback instead of discovering it from a stack trace."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is a `ReadOnlyAccount` that throws on `withdraw` a design smell even though it compiles?","a":"Code written against `Account` expects `withdraw` to work for valid input, so the subtype has added a hidden precondition and every caller can now fail at runtime. It usually means the base type is too broad: split it into a read interface and a write interface, and let read-only accounts implement only the first. The compiler cannot catch this, which is why behavioral contracts need tests."},{"q":"A method receives a `List` and calls `add` on it. Why can that fail, and what rule follows?","a":"The caller may pass `List.of(...)`, `Stream.toList()` or an unmodifiable view, which implement `List` but throw `UnsupportedOperationException` on mutators. The JDK documents those methods as optional operations, so it is not formally a substitution break, but the receiver cannot tell from the type. Treat received collections as read-only and return new ones, unless the contract explicitly says the method may modify its argument."}],"extra":["**Contract tests:** an abstract JUnit class holding the base-type expectations, extended once per implementation (including fakes used in other tests), turns LSP from a review opinion into a failing build.","**Exceptions are part of the contract:** a subtype that throws a new unchecked exception type the base never documented breaks callers' error handling just as surely as a wrong return value.","**equals and inheritance:** a subclass that adds a value field cannot preserve the `equals` contract (symmetry, transitivity) with its parent; `java.sql.Timestamp` and `java.util.Date` are the JDK's own example, and records avoid it by being final.","**What Java does enforce:** overrides may return a narrower type and declare fewer checked exceptions, which is LSP expressed in the type system; everything else must come from documentation and tests.","**History constraint:** a mutable subtype of a type documented as immutable violates LSP even without overriding anything, because callers rely on the value never changing."],"redFlags":["thinks the compiler enforces substitutability because the subclass compiles","patches violations with `instanceof` checks in the calling code"]},{"id":24,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"Everything is injected by Spring, yet domain classes import JPA repositories directly. Is this Dependency Inversion, and why or why not?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Dependency Inversion is a design rule: high-level policy owns abstractions that low-level details implement; dependency injection is merely how objects receive collaborators, and a DI container does not create those boundaries for you.","answer":["**DIP** is about the direction of source-code dependencies. High-level modules (credit decision rules, order workflow) should not depend on low-level modules (HTTP clients, JPA, Kafka); both depend on abstractions, and the abstraction belongs to the policy side, expressed in its language. `CreditDecisionService` depends on a `CreditBureau` port returning a domain `CreditScore`; the HTTP adapter in the infrastructure package implements it. The source dependency points from infrastructure to domain, opposite to the call direction.","**DI** is a mechanism: an object receives its collaborators, ideally through the constructor, instead of creating or looking them up, and Spring's container automates the wiring. DI is how you usually realize DIP, but they are independent: you can inject a concrete `JdbcOrderDao`, or an interface returning `Page<OrderEntity>` that leaks JPA, and Spring wires it happily with no inversion at all. You can also satisfy DIP without any container by wiring objects by hand in a composition root.","So `@Autowired` everywhere proves nothing. What matters is where interfaces live and which types they expose: ports in the core module using domain types, adapters outside, and ideally no persistence or HTTP types in the domain. Enforce it with Gradle or Maven module boundaries or ArchUnit tests, because conventions alone erode."],"keyPoints":["inversion is about which module owns the abstraction, not how objects are wired","the core declares the interfaces it needs and adapters implement them","a container can wire concrete classes everywhere and still leave the core coupled"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: dependency injection without dependency inversion","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: wired by Spring, yet the policy depends directly on infrastructure details\n@Service\npublic class CreditDecisionServiceBad {\n\n    private final BureauRestClient bureauClient;                  // concrete HTTP client class\n    private final JpaRepository<ApplicationEntity, Long> repo;    // persistence types in the policy\n\n    public CreditDecisionServiceBad(BureauRestClient bureauClient,\n                                    JpaRepository<ApplicationEntity, Long> repo) {\n        this.bureauClient = bureauClient;\n        this.repo = repo;\n    }\n\n    public Decision decide(long applicationId) {\n        ApplicationEntity application = repo.findById(applicationId).orElseThrow();\n        BureauScoreResponse response = bureauClient.getScore(application.getCustomerRef());  // vendor DTO\n        return response.getScore() >= 620 ? Decision.APPROVE : Decision.REFER;\n    }\n}","note":"Changing bureau vendor or persistence technology means editing the credit policy, and unit testing it needs HTTP and JPA fakes."},{"title":"GOOD: port owned by the domain, adapter in infrastructure","lang":"java","code":"// domain: the policy owns the port, expressed in domain types; no Spring, no HTTP\npublic interface CreditBureau {\n    CreditScore scoreFor(CustomerId customerId);\n}\n\npublic class CreditDecisionService {\n    private final CreditBureau bureau;\n    public CreditDecisionService(CreditBureau bureau) { this.bureau = bureau; }\n\n    public Decision decide(CreditApplication application) {\n        CreditScore score = bureau.scoreFor(application.customerId());\n        return score.value() >= 620 ? Decision.APPROVE : Decision.REFER;\n    }\n}\n\n// infrastructure: the detail depends on the domain abstraction\n@Component\nclass HttpCreditBureauAdapter implements CreditBureau {\n    private final RestClient restClient;\n    HttpCreditBureauAdapter(RestClient.Builder builder) {\n        this.restClient = builder.baseUrl(\"https://bureau.internal\").build();\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public CreditScore scoreFor(CustomerId customerId) {\n        BureauScoreResponse body = restClient.get().uri(\"/v2/scores/{id}\", customerId.value())\n                .retrieve().body(BureauScoreResponse.class);\n        return new CreditScore(body.score());\n    }\n}\n\n// configuration: DI wires the plain domain service to the adapter\n@Configuration\nclass CreditConfig {\n    @Bean\n    CreditDecisionService creditDecisionService(CreditBureau bureau) { return new CreditDecisionService(bureau); }\n}","note":"The domain class has no annotations and can be unit tested with a lambda such as `id -> new CreditScore(700)`. Spring (DI) only connects the pieces; the inversion comes from where `CreditBureau` lives and what it returns."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Where does the `OrderRepository` interface belong, and what types should its methods use?","a":"In the application or domain core, beside the use cases that need it, expressed in domain terms such as `findById(OrderId)` returning a domain `Order`. The JPA or jOOQ implementation lives in an infrastructure adapter that maps between persistence entities and the domain model. The core then compiles and tests without a database, and swapping persistence touches only the adapter."},{"q":"Is calling `new JdbcOrderRepository(dataSource)` in a configuration class a violation?","a":"No. The principle governs which modules the business policy depends on, and a composition root in the outer layer is exactly where concrete classes are supposed to be chosen and constructed. It becomes a violation when the domain class itself instantiates the JDBC implementation. If the domain must create objects at runtime, give it a factory interface it owns, implemented in infrastructure."}],"extra":["**Ownership is the test:** ask which module ships the interface; if the domain must import the adapter's package to see it, the dependency was hidden behind an interface, not inverted.","**ArchUnit rule:** `noClasses().that().resideInAPackage(\"..domain..\").should().dependOnClassesThat().resideInAnyPackage(\"org.springframework..\", \"jakarta.persistence..\")` fails the build when a detail leaks into the core.","**Pragmatic middle ground:** many Spring teams let the domain use a Spring Data interface directly; that trades purity for less mapping code and is reasonable when the persistence and domain models are the same and unlikely to diverge, as long as it is a conscious decision.","**Constructor injection:** dependencies are explicit and `final`, the class works without the container in unit tests, and a constructor that keeps growing is an honest SRP warning.","**Service locator is not DI:** calling `applicationContext.getBean(...)` in business code hides dependencies and makes the domain depend on the container, which is the opposite of the goal."],"redFlags":["believes any use of `new` breaks the principle","adds an interface per class and calls that inversion","lets domain methods return JPA entities or Spring `Page` types"]},{"id":25,"category":"Java Collections","question":"A `HashMap` cache slows down as it grows and some entries seem to vanish. Walk through a lookup and the likely causes.","level":"Basic","tldr":"`HashMap` picks a bucket from the key's spread `hashCode()` and finds the key with `equals()`; collisions chain into a list that may treeify, and bad hashes, mutable keys or expensive equality degrade lookups.","answer":["On `put` and `get`, `HashMap` computes `h ^ (h >>> 16)` from `hashCode()` to mix high bits into low bits, then indexes the table with `(n-1) & hash`; the table size is a power of two (default 16) and doubles when size exceeds capacity times the load factor (0.75). Inside the bucket it compares the cached hash first, then `==` or `equals()`. So `hashCode` only chooses the bucket and `equals` decides whether the key matches. Hash codes are not unique: unequal keys sharing a bucket is normal.","Colliding entries chain as a linked list. Since Java 8, when one bucket reaches 8 entries and the table has at least 64 slots, the chain becomes a red-black tree of `TreeNode`s, so worst-case lookup is O(log n) instead of O(n); on smaller tables the map resizes instead. Trees order by hash, then by `compareTo` when keys are mutually `Comparable`, so non-comparable keys with identical hashes still degrade toward a linear search.","What makes lookups slow in production: a weak `hashCode` (constant, or built from a low-cardinality field like country or status) that funnels keys into few buckets; expensive `hashCode` or `equals` computed on every lookup; repeated resizing when the map is not presized; and adversarial keys crafted to collide when keys come from user input. Mutable keys are worse than slow: if a hashed field changes after insertion, the entry becomes unreachable. The fix is immutable keys with cheap, well-distributed hashes, typically records, `String` or `Long` ids."],"keyPoints":["the spread hash picks a bucket index, then equality confirms the match","colliding keys share a bucket chain that becomes a red-black tree once long enough","a key whose hashed fields change after insertion becomes unreachable","slow or weak `equals` and `hashCode` implementations cost time on every lookup"],"examples":[{"title":"Mutable key makes an entry unreachable","lang":"java","code":"final class AccountKey {\n    private String iban; // BAD: mutable field that drives hashCode\n\n    AccountKey(String iban) { this.iban = iban; }\n    void setIban(String iban) { this.iban = iban; }\n\n    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        return o instanceof AccountKey k && iban.equals(k.iban);\n    }\n    @Override public int hashCode() { return iban.hashCode(); }\n}\n\nrecord AccountId(String iban) { } // GOOD: immutable, equals and hashCode generated\n\nclass BalanceCacheDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map<AccountKey, BigDecimal> balances = new HashMap<>();\n        AccountKey key = new AccountKey(\"SE3550000000054910000003\");\n        balances.put(key, new BigDecimal(\"100.00\"));\n\n        key.setIban(\"SE4550000000058398257466\"); // hash changes, entry stays in the old bucket\n\n        System.out.println(balances.get(key));         // null\n        System.out.println(balances.containsKey(key)); // false\n        System.out.println(balances.size());           // 1: unreachable entry, a slow leak\n    }\n}","note":"Each node caches the hash computed at insertion, so even if the new hash lands in the same bucket, the cached hash no longer matches and the lookup misses."},{"title":"Poor distribution and presizing","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: all merchants in one country share a hash, so a few buckets hold thousands of keys\nrecord MerchantKey(String merchantId, String country) {\n    @Override public int hashCode() { return country.hashCode(); }\n}\n\n// GOOD: the generated hashCode combines all components\nrecord MerchantRef(String merchantId, String country) { }\n\nclass MerchantRegistry {\n    // GOOD: sized for the expected number of mappings, no resize while loading (Java 19+)\n    private final Map<MerchantRef, MerchantConfig> configs = HashMap.newHashMap(50_000);\n\n    void load(List<MerchantConfig> all) {\n        for (MerchantConfig c : all) {\n            configs.put(new MerchantRef(c.merchantId(), c.country()), c);\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The BAD key still honors the contract (equal keys have equal hashes), which is why this bug survives code review: correctness is fine, only latency and CPU degrade as the map grows."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does `HashMap` mix the high bits of `hashCode()` before choosing a bucket?","a":"The table size is a power of two, so the bucket index is `hash & (n-1)`, which uses only the low bits of the hash. Keys whose hash codes differ only in the high bits would all land in a few buckets, so `HashMap.hash()` XORs the upper 16 bits into the lower 16 (`h ^ (h >>> 16)`). It is a cheap defense against weak hash functions, but it cannot rescue a hash that is constant or ignores most fields."},{"q":"An entry's key is mutated after `put`. What does the map do on the next `get`?","a":"The entry stays in the bucket chosen from its original hash, and the node caches that original value. The next `get` with the mutated key computes a new hash, probes a different bucket or fails the cached hash check, and returns `null`, while `size()` and iteration still show the entry. In a cache this becomes a slow leak plus duplicates, because a later `put` of the same logical key adds a second entry."}],"extra":["**Treeify numbers:** `TREEIFY_THRESHOLD` is 8, `UNTREEIFY_THRESHOLD` is 6 (a split bin shrinks back to a list during resize) and `MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY` is 64; below 64 slots a long chain triggers a resize instead of a tree.","**Hash flooding:** strings like `\"Aa\"` and `\"BB\"` share a hash code, so an attacker can generate many colliding keys in request parameters or JSON field names; treeification limits the damage to O(log n) because `String` is `Comparable`, but capping input size is still your job.","**Presizing correctly:** `new HashMap<>(n)` takes a capacity, not an expected size, so it still resizes at 75% load; `HashMap.newHashMap(expectedMappings)` (Java 19+) computes the right capacity for you.","**Resize cost:** a resize allocates a doubled table and splits each bucket into a low and high half, which is O(n); a large map built without presizing on a hot path shows up as latency spikes and allocation churn.","**Hash caching:** `String` caches its hash after the first computation, so repeated lookups are cheap, whereas a record key containing a `List` recomputes the whole list hash on every `get`."],"redFlags":["claims a lookup is always O(1) regardless of hash quality","thinks the map compares keys only by reference","cannot say why a constant `hashCode` turns the map into a slow list"]},{"id":26,"category":"Java Collections","question":"HashMap vs LinkedHashMap vs TreeMap: how do ordering, cost and use cases differ?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`HashMap` gives average O(1) lookups and no iteration order, `LinkedHashMap` keeps insertion order (or access order, which makes an LRU cache) at a small memory cost, and `TreeMap` keeps keys sorted with O(log n) operations plus range queries. Pick the map by the ordering the use case needs.","answer":["`HashMap` hashes keys into buckets for average O(1) `get` and `put`; its iteration order depends on hash codes and capacity and can change after a resize. `LinkedHashMap` is a `HashMap` that also links its entries in a doubly linked list, so it iterates in insertion order, and putting an existing key again keeps its position. `TreeMap` is a red-black tree ordered by `compareTo` or a `Comparator`: O(log n) operations, always sorted iteration, and no null key under natural ordering.","`LinkedHashMap` costs two extra references per entry and is otherwise about as fast as `HashMap`. Built with `accessOrder = true`, every `get` or `put` moves the entry to the tail, and overriding `removeEldestEntry` evicts from the head: a complete LRU cache in a dozen lines. `TreeMap` needs about 20 comparisons to find a key among a million, so it loses on plain lookups, but as a `NavigableMap` it answers range questions directly: `floorKey` finds the rule in effect on a date, `subMap` returns a time window, `headMap` and `tailMap` everything before or after a key.","The common production bug is depending on `HashMap` order without noticing: JSON fields or report columns that shift, tests asserting on `toString()`. Use `LinkedHashMap` when output must follow input order, `TreeMap` for sorted output and range lookups, `HashMap` for everything else. None is thread-safe, and an access-order `LinkedHashMap` mutates even on `get`, so a shared LRU needs a lock; real service caches belong in Caffeine, and a concurrent sorted map is `ConcurrentSkipListMap`."],"keyPoints":["choose by the iteration order you need","access order plus removeEldestEntry gives an LRU cache","TreeMap trades O(log n) for floor, ceiling and range views"],"examples":[{"title":"Three iteration orders, and an access-ordered LinkedHashMap as an LRU cache","lang":"java","code":"final class RateCache extends LinkedHashMap<String, BigDecimal> {\n    private final int maxEntries;\n\n    RateCache(int maxEntries) {\n        super(16, 0.75f, true);              // true: access order instead of insertion order\n        this.maxEntries = maxEntries;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<String, BigDecimal> eldest) {\n        return size() > maxEntries;          // runs after every put that adds a new key\n    }\n}\n\nRateCache rates = new RateCache(2);\nrates.put(\"EUR/SEK\", new BigDecimal(\"11.42\"));\nrates.put(\"USD/SEK\", new BigDecimal(\"10.61\"));\nrates.get(\"EUR/SEK\");                        // moves EUR/SEK to the tail\nrates.put(\"GBP/SEK\", new BigDecimal(\"13.37\"));\nSystem.out.println(rates.keySet());          // [EUR/SEK, GBP/SEK]: USD/SEK evicted\n\nMap<String, Integer> hash = new HashMap<>(), linked = new LinkedHashMap<>(), sorted = new TreeMap<>();\nfor (String s : List.of(\"SHIPPED\", \"CREATED\", \"PAID\")) {\n    hash.put(s, 0); linked.put(s, 0); sorted.put(s, 0);\n}\nSystem.out.println(hash.keySet());           // depends on hash codes: never rely on it\nSystem.out.println(linked.keySet());         // [SHIPPED, CREATED, PAID]\nSystem.out.println(sorted.keySet());         // [CREATED, PAID, SHIPPED]","note":"Access order plus `removeEldestEntry` does all the LRU bookkeeping. The cache is not thread-safe, and in access order even `get` is a structural change."},{"title":"TreeMap navigation: the fee in effect on a date and events in a window","lang":"java","code":"NavigableMap<LocalDate, BigDecimal> feeByEffectiveDate = new TreeMap<>(Map.of(\n        LocalDate.of(2025, 1, 1), new BigDecimal(\"0.0180\"),\n        LocalDate.of(2026, 3, 1), new BigDecimal(\"0.0165\"),\n        LocalDate.of(2026, 9, 1), new BigDecimal(\"0.0150\")));\n\nBigDecimal feeOn(LocalDate bookingDate) {\n    // greatest key <= bookingDate\n    Map.Entry<LocalDate, BigDecimal> rule = feeByEffectiveDate.floorEntry(bookingDate);\n    if (rule == null) throw new IllegalStateException(\"No fee rule on \" + bookingDate);\n    return rule.getValue();\n}\n// 2026-05-20 -> 0.0165, 2026-09-01 -> 0.0150, 2024-01-01 -> exception\n\nNavigableMap<Instant, String> eventsByTime = new TreeMap<>();\neventsByTime.put(Instant.parse(\"2026-10-30T09:59:58Z\"), \"payment-981 captured\");\neventsByTime.put(Instant.parse(\"2026-10-30T10:15:00Z\"), \"payment-982 captured\");\neventsByTime.put(Instant.parse(\"2026-10-30T11:00:00Z\"), \"payment-984 captured\");\n\nInstant from = Instant.parse(\"2026-10-30T10:00:00Z\");\nSortedMap<Instant, String> hour = eventsByTime.subMap(from, from.plus(1, ChronoUnit.HOURS));\nSystem.out.println(hour.values());           // [payment-982 captured]: from inclusive, to exclusive\nSystem.out.println(eventsByTime.headMap(from).size());   // 1","note":"Each question is an O(log n) lookup with no scan. With a `HashMap` you would sort the keys or loop over every entry each time."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can request threads share that LinkedHashMap LRU cache?","a":"Not as is. Every `get` relinks an entry, so concurrent reads corrupt the list just like writes. `Collections.synchronizedMap` makes it correct but serializes every read; a production cache should use Caffeine, which gives concurrent access, size and time eviction, and hit-rate statistics."},{"q":"What would you use for a sorted map that several threads update?","a":"`ConcurrentSkipListMap`, a concurrent `NavigableMap` with expected O(log n) operations and weakly consistent iterators, so range queries keep working while others write. It rejects null keys and values. A synchronized `TreeMap` also works, but serializes every call and needs manual locking around iteration."}],"extra":["**Map.of order:** `Map.of` and `Set.of` randomize iteration order per JVM run on purpose, so tests asserting their order can pass locally and fail in CI.","**Sequenced collections:** since Java 21 `LinkedHashMap` and `TreeMap` implement `SequencedMap` (`firstEntry`, `pollFirstEntry`, `reversed()`); `putLast` moves a `LinkedHashMap` entry but throws `UnsupportedOperationException` on `TreeMap`.","**Live views:** `subMap`, `headMap` and `tailMap` write through to the `TreeMap`, and putting a key outside the view's range throws `IllegalArgumentException`.","**Iteration cost:** `HashMap` iteration walks every bucket, including empty ones, so an oversized map iterates slowly; `LinkedHashMap` iterates only its entries.","**Nulls:** `HashMap` and `LinkedHashMap` allow one null key, `TreeMap` rejects it unless the comparator handles nulls, and `ConcurrentHashMap` rejects null keys and values."],"redFlags":["Relies on HashMap iteration order","Uses TreeMap with no need for ordering or ranges","Shares an unsynchronized LRU LinkedHashMap between threads"]},{"id":27,"category":"Java Collections","question":"A teammate replaces `ArrayList` with `LinkedList` on a hot request path, citing faster inserts. Would you approve that change?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Default to `ArrayList`: O(1) indexed access, compact contiguous storage and cache-friendly iteration; `LinkedList` pays a node object per element and O(n) access, and for queues or stacks `ArrayDeque` beats both.","answer":["`ArrayList` stores references in one contiguous `Object[]`. `get(i)` is O(1), appends are amortized O(1) (the array grows by about 50% when full), and iteration walks memory sequentially, which CPU caches and prefetchers love. Inserting or removing in the middle is O(n), but it is a single `System.arraycopy`, which is very fast for the sizes backend code usually handles.","`LinkedList` is a doubly linked list: every element lives in a separate `Node` with `item`, `next` and `prev`, roughly 24 extra bytes per element with compressed oops, scattered across the heap. `get(i)` is O(n) (it walks from the nearer end) and each hop is a likely cache miss. The famous O(1) insertion only holds once you already hold a position through a `ListIterator`; finding that position is O(n). So the claim that LinkedList is faster for inserts is usually false in practice, and the extra allocations add GC pressure.","For FIFO queues and stacks, use `ArrayDeque`: a resizable circular array with O(1) `offerLast`, `pollFirst`, `push` and `pop` and no per-element nodes. It replaces both `LinkedList` used as a queue and the legacy synchronized `Stack`. The production rule: default to `ArrayList`, presize it when the size is known, and reach for `LinkedList` only with a benchmark that proves it wins for your access pattern."],"keyPoints":["array storage keeps elements contiguous, so iteration stays in CPU cache","each linked node is a separate heap object with two extra references","reaching a middle position in a linked list means walking nodes first","for FIFO or LIFO work, a circular array deque avoids per-element allocation"],"examples":[{"title":"Indexed loop turns quadratic on LinkedList","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: looks linear, is O(n^2) because get(i) walks the nodes every time\nList<Payment> payments = new LinkedList<>(repository.findSettledToday());\nBigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;\nfor (int i = 0; i < payments.size(); i++) {\n    total = total.add(payments.get(i).amount());\n}\n\n// GOOD: array-backed, presized copy, O(1) access and sequential memory\nList<Payment> settled = new ArrayList<>(repository.findSettledToday());\nBigDecimal sum = BigDecimal.ZERO;\nfor (Payment p : settled) {\n    sum = sum.add(p.amount());\n}","note":"With a six-figure list the BAD loop performs billions of pointer hops, and it often hides behind a method that accepts `List` and uses an index loop. Code against `List`, but never assume `get(i)` is cheap unless the list implements `RandomAccess`."},{"title":"ArrayDeque as a retry queue and a stack","lang":"java","code":"Deque<RetryTask> retryQueue = new ArrayDeque<>(); // GOOD: circular array, no nodes\n\nretryQueue.offerLast(new RetryTask(\"pay-1001\", 1));\nretryQueue.offerLast(new RetryTask(\"pay-1002\", 1));\n\nRetryTask next;\nwhile ((next = retryQueue.pollFirst()) != null) {\n    boolean ok = gateway.retry(next.paymentId());\n    if (!ok && next.attempt() < 3) {\n        retryQueue.offerLast(next.nextAttempt());\n    }\n}\n\nDeque<String> steps = new ArrayDeque<>(); // GOOD: replaces legacy java.util.Stack\nsteps.push(\"validate\");\nsteps.push(\"authorize\");\nString top = steps.pop(); // \"authorize\"","note":"`ArrayDeque` rejects `null` (null means empty for `poll`) and is not thread-safe, which is exactly right for single-threaded work queues and stacks."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does `ArrayList` grow when it runs out of capacity, and when would you presize it?","a":"When the backing array is full, `add` allocates a larger array (about 1.5 times the old capacity) and copies the elements over, so appends are amortized O(1) even though a single append can cost O(n). If you know the final size, such as when mapping a page of 500 rows, pass it to the constructor or call `ensureCapacity` to skip the intermediate copies and garbage. `trimToSize()` releases unused capacity for long-lived lists."},{"q":"Is there any case where a linked list still earns its place?","a":"Mainly when you hold a `ListIterator` in the middle of a long list and insert or remove many elements at that spot during one pass, because each iterator `add` or `remove` relinks nodes without shifting anything. It is also the only standard `List` that doubles as a `Deque` and permits `null` elements. Even then, benchmark it against `ArrayList.removeIf`, a rebuilt list or an `ArrayDeque`, which usually win on locality."}],"extra":["**Memory math:** a million-element `ArrayList` holds about 4 MB of references (compressed oops) in one array, while a `LinkedList` adds a million 24-byte `Node` objects on top, roughly 24 MB of extra heap and a million more objects for the GC to trace.","**Java 21 sequenced collections:** `List` now has `getFirst`, `getLast`, `addFirst` and `removeFirst`; on `ArrayList`, `addFirst` and `removeFirst` are O(n) shifts, so seeing them in a hot loop is a hint you actually want an `ArrayDeque`.","**Presizing:** `new ArrayList<>(expectedSize)` avoids repeated grow and copy cycles when you know the batch size (for example a page of 500 rows), and `trimToSize()` releases slack on long-lived lists.","**Bulk removal:** removing many elements with `iterator.remove()` on an `ArrayList` is O(n^2) because of repeated shifting; `removeIf` compacts the array in one pass, which removes the main argument people make for `LinkedList`.","**Concurrent queues are a different choice:** across threads use `ArrayBlockingQueue` (bounded, gives backpressure), `LinkedBlockingQueue` (optionally bounded, one node per element) or `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` (lock-free, unbounded); unbounded queues under sustained load are a classic cause of `OutOfMemoryError`."],"redFlags":["picks a structure from Big O tables alone and never mentions measuring","suggests `Stack` or `LinkedList` for a work queue without knowing `ArrayDeque`","treats every `ArrayList` append as a full array copy instead of amortized O(1)"]},{"id":28,"category":"Java Collections","question":"Under load, a `Collections.synchronizedMap` cache shows threads blocked on one monitor. Would `ConcurrentHashMap` fix that, and what changes?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`Collections.synchronizedMap` puts one lock around every call so all threads serialize; `ConcurrentHashMap` locks only single bins on writes, reads without locking, and offers atomic `compute`, `merge` and `putIfAbsent` for compound updates.","answer":["`Collections.synchronizedMap(map)` wraps every method in `synchronized` on a single mutex. Each call is thread-safe, but every `get` and `put` from every thread queues on that one lock, so throughput flattens or even drops as you add cores. Iteration must be wrapped manually in `synchronized (map) { ... }` or you risk `ConcurrentModificationException`, and every check-then-act sequence needs that same external lock.","`ConcurrentHashMap` (Java 8+) has no global lock. Inserting into an empty bin is a CAS; updating a non-empty bin synchronizes only on that bin's first node; `get` takes no lock at all and relies on volatile reads, so readers never block writers or each other. Resizing is cooperative, with writer threads helping transfer bins, and the size is tracked with striped counters. Iterators are weakly consistent: they never throw `ConcurrentModificationException` but may or may not reflect concurrent updates.","Thread-safe single operations do not make compound logic atomic: `if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k, v)` is a race in either map. Use `putIfAbsent`, `computeIfAbsent`, `compute`, `merge` or `replace(key, expected, newValue)`, which are atomic per key. Keep the remapping functions short and free of side effects: they run while holding the bin lock, and modifying the same map from inside them is forbidden (it can deadlock or throw `IllegalStateException`). Also, `ConcurrentHashMap` rejects `null` keys and values, which breaks code ported from `HashMap` that used `null` as a marker."],"keyPoints":["a single monitor makes every reader and writer wait in line","writes lock just one bin while reads use volatile loads without locking","atomic per-key methods replace separate read and write calls","null keys and values are rejected to keep results unambiguous"],"examples":[{"title":"Check-then-act race versus computeIfAbsent","lang":"java","code":"private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, MerchantThrottle> throttles = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n// BAD: two threads can both see \"absent\" and create two throttles\nMerchantThrottle throttleBad(String merchantId) {\n    MerchantThrottle throttle = throttles.get(merchantId);\n    if (throttle == null) {\n        throttle = MerchantThrottle.forMerchant(merchantId);\n        throttles.put(merchantId, throttle); // the second put overwrites the first\n    }\n    return throttle;\n}\n\n// GOOD: created at most once per key, atomically\nMerchantThrottle throttle(String merchantId) {\n    return throttles.computeIfAbsent(merchantId, MerchantThrottle::forMerchant);\n}","note":"In the BAD version two requests for the same merchant can end up with different throttle instances, so the rate limit is silently doubled. `computeIfAbsent` guarantees one instance per key."},{"title":"Atomic per-key counters","lang":"java","code":"private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Long> declinesByReason = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\nprivate final ConcurrentHashMap<String, LongAdder> requestsByMerchant = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\nvoid recordDecline(String reasonCode) {\n    declinesByReason.merge(reasonCode, 1L, Long::sum); // atomic read, modify, write per key\n}\n\nvoid recordRequest(String merchantId) {\n    requestsByMerchant.computeIfAbsent(merchantId, id -> new LongAdder())\n                      .increment();                  // scales for very hot keys\n}\n\nlong requests(String merchantId) {\n    LongAdder adder = requestsByMerchant.get(merchantId);\n    return adder == null ? 0 : adder.sum();\n}\n\n// BAD: lost updates, two threads read 5 and both write 6\n// declinesByReason.put(code, declinesByReason.getOrDefault(code, 0L) + 1);","note":"`merge` is enough for moderate traffic; `LongAdder` spreads increments over internal cells so a hot key does not serialize on one bin lock, at the cost of a slightly more expensive `sum()`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What do iterators over a `ConcurrentHashMap` guarantee while other threads write?","a":"They are weakly consistent: they never throw `ConcurrentModificationException`, they traverse the elements that existed when they were created exactly once, and they may or may not reflect changes made afterwards. That is fine for reporting or periodic cache sweeps, but a pass over the map is not an atomic snapshot, and `size()` is only an estimate while writers are active."},{"q":"Two threads increment a per-key counter in the same map. How do you avoid losing updates?","a":"Do the read-modify-write inside the map: `counts.merge(key, 1L, Long::sum)` inserts 1 or adds to the current value atomically for that key. For very hot keys, store a `LongAdder` with `counts.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new LongAdder()).increment()` and read it with `sum()`. A separate `get` followed by `put` lets both threads read the same value, so one increment disappears."}],"extra":["**Java 7 versus 8 internals:** Java 7 split the map into `Segment` locks (16 by default, the `concurrencyLevel`); Java 8 replaced segments with per-bin locking plus CAS, and `concurrencyLevel` survives only as a sizing hint.","**size() is an estimate under load:** with concurrent writers, `size()` and `isEmpty()` are momentary snapshots; `mappingCount()` returns a `long` and is preferred for large maps, and neither should drive correctness decisions.","**Not a loading cache:** the `computeIfAbsent` function blocks other writers to that bin (including unrelated keys that collide with it), so a slow HTTP or database call inside it serializes traffic; for loading caches with expiry use Caffeine, which is built on these primitives.","**Concurrent sets and bulk operations:** `ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()` gives a scalable concurrent `Set`, and `forEach`, `reduceValues` and `search` with a `parallelismThreshold` argument run bulk operations safely while the map is being updated.","**Treeified bins exist here too:** heavily colliding bins convert to a balanced `TreeBin` at the same threshold of 8, so bad key hashes cost O(log n) rather than O(n), but they still concentrate lock contention on that bin."],"redFlags":["expects iteration over the concurrent map to throw or to show a frozen snapshot","assumes `size()` is exact while other threads are writing","wraps every call in its own `synchronized` block on top of the concurrent map"]},{"id":29,"category":"Java Collections","question":"Comparable vs Comparator: when do you use each?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`Comparable` lives on the class and defines its one natural ordering; a `Comparator` is a separate object, so a type can have many orderings and you can sort types you do not own. Implement `Comparable` only when the ordering is obvious and agrees with `equals`, and build everything else with `Comparator.comparing` chains.","answer":["`Comparable<T>` adds `compareTo` to the type, returning negative, zero or positive. It is what `List.sort(null)`, `TreeMap`, `TreeSet` and `PriorityQueue` use without a comparator, and `String`, `Integer`, `LocalDate`, `BigDecimal` and every enum (declaration order) implement it. `Comparator<T>` is a functional interface you pass in, so orders can sort by due date on one screen and by amount on another. Rule of thumb: `Comparable` for the one ordering nobody would argue about, `Comparator` for anything business-specific.","You rarely write `compare` by hand. `Comparator.comparing` builds from a key, `comparingInt` and `comparingLong` avoid boxing, `thenComparing` adds tie-breakers, `reversed()` flips the chain so far, `Comparator.reverseOrder()` flips one key, and `nullsFirst` and `nullsLast` put nulls at an end instead of throwing. The contract still matters: sign flips when arguments swap, and the ordering is transitive. A comparator that breaks it, classically by subtracting ints that overflow, mis-sorts, and TimSort may throw 'Comparison method violates its general contract!' on large inputs.","The drill-down is consistency with `equals`. `TreeSet` and `TreeMap` never call `equals`: `compare(a, b) == 0` means same element. A `TreeSet<Payment>` ordered only by `capturedAt` silently drops a second payment captured in the same millisecond, and `BigDecimal` 2.0 and 2.00 are two elements in a `HashSet` but one in a `TreeSet`. End every chain with a unique key such as the id. `List.sort` tolerates ties; sorted sets and map keys lose data."],"keyPoints":["Comparable is the natural ordering, Comparator any external one","compose with comparing, thenComparing, reversed and nullsLast","sorted sets treat compare == 0 as a duplicate"],"examples":[{"title":"Comparator chains: multiple keys, nulls, partial reversal and a tie-breaker","lang":"java","code":"enum Priority { URGENT, HIGH, NORMAL }\n\nrecord Order(UUID id, String customerName, Priority priority,\n             LocalDate dueDate, BigDecimal total, int lineCount) {}\n\nfinal class OrderOrderings {\n    // Urgent first, earliest due date (missing last), largest total, then id\n    static final Comparator<Order> DASHBOARD = Comparator\n            .comparing(Order::priority)                                // enum declaration order\n            .thenComparing(Order::dueDate, Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()))\n            .thenComparing(Order::total, Comparator.reverseOrder())    // reverses this key only\n            .thenComparing(Order::id);                                 // total, deterministic order\n\n    static final Comparator<Order> BY_CUSTOMER = Comparator\n            .comparing(Order::customerName, Comparator.nullsFirst(String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER))\n            .thenComparing(Order::id);\n\n    // BAD: subtraction can overflow and flip the sign\n    static final Comparator<Order> BY_LINES_BAD = (a, b) -> a.lineCount() - b.lineCount();\n    // GOOD\n    static final Comparator<Order> BY_LINES = Comparator.comparingInt(Order::lineCount);\n}\n\nopenOrders.sort(OrderOrderings.DASHBOARD);","note":"The final `thenComparing(Order::id)` makes the order total and repeatable between runs. `reverseOrder()` on one key differs from `reversed()`, which would flip every key before it."},{"title":"Inconsistent with equals: TreeSet silently drops elements","lang":"java","code":"record Payment(String id, BigDecimal amount, Instant capturedAt) {}\n\nInstant now = Instant.parse(\"2026-10-30T10:00:00Z\");\nList<Payment> batch = List.of(\n        new Payment(\"pay-1\", new BigDecimal(\"250.00\"), now),\n        new Payment(\"pay-2\", new BigDecimal(\"99.50\"), now),        // same instant\n        new Payment(\"pay-3\", new BigDecimal(\"12.00\"), now.plusSeconds(1)));\n\n// BAD: compare() == 0 for pay-1 and pay-2, so pay-2 counts as a duplicate\nSet<Payment> byTime = new TreeSet<>(Comparator.comparing(Payment::capturedAt));\nbyTime.addAll(batch);\nSystem.out.println(byTime.size());           // 2, no exception, no log line\n\n// GOOD: tie-break on a unique key\nSet<Payment> byTimeThenId = new TreeSet<>(\n        Comparator.comparing(Payment::capturedAt).thenComparing(Payment::id));\nbyTimeThenId.addAll(batch);\nSystem.out.println(byTimeThenId.size());     // 3\n\n// The JDK's own example: compareTo ignores scale, equals does not\nSet<BigDecimal> hashed = new HashSet<>(List.of(new BigDecimal(\"2.0\"), new BigDecimal(\"2.00\")));\nSet<BigDecimal> sorted = new TreeSet<>(List.of(new BigDecimal(\"2.0\"), new BigDecimal(\"2.00\")));\nSystem.out.println(hashed.size() + \" vs \" + sorted.size());   // 2 vs 1","note":"Nothing fails loudly; the set is just smaller. The same applies to `TreeMap` keys, where the second `put` overwrites the first value."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Should a JPA entity like Order implement Comparable?","a":"Usually not. An order has no single obvious ordering, and a natural ordering that disagrees with `equals` makes sorted sets and `TreeMap` keys lose entries. Keep named `Comparator` constants near the use case, and when the data comes from the database, sort there with `ORDER BY` so pagination stays stable."},{"q":"Where exactly do you put nullsLast?","a":"Around the key comparator: `comparing(Order::dueDate, nullsLast(naturalOrder()))` handles orders with no due date. `nullsLast(comparing(Order::dueDate))` only handles null orders and still throws `NullPointerException` when a due date is null."}],"extra":["**reversed() scope:** `comparing(a).thenComparing(b).reversed()` reverses both keys; to reverse only the second, pass `Comparator.reverseOrder()` to `thenComparing`.","**Inference gotcha:** `Comparator.comparing(o -> o.total()).reversed()` does not compile because `o` is inferred as `Object`; a method reference or a typed lambda fixes it.","**Human text:** `String.compareTo` compares UTF-16 code units, so uppercase sorts before lowercase and Swedish Ä before Å; use `Collator.getInstance(Locale.forLanguageTag(\"sv-SE\"))`, which is a `Comparator`.","**UUID ordering:** `UUID.compareTo` compares signed 64-bit halves, so its order differs from the string form and from PostgreSQL's; never mix them in keyset pagination.","**Integer.compare:** for hand-written comparators use `Integer.compare` or `Long.compare`, never subtraction."],"redFlags":["Subtracts values in compare","Does not know TreeSet uses compare, not equals","Adds Comparable to entities for one screen's sort"]},{"id":30,"category":"Java Collections","question":"Fail-fast vs fail-safe iterators: what causes ConcurrentModificationException and how do you avoid it?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Fail-fast iterators throw `ConcurrentModificationException` when the collection is structurally changed by anything but the iterator, most often by the same thread inside a for-each loop. Use `Iterator.remove`, `removeIf` or a new collection within one thread, and concurrent collections, whose iterators are weakly consistent or snapshots, when other threads write.","answer":["`ArrayList`, `HashMap` and friends increment a `modCount` on every structural change (add, remove, clear, resize; not `set` or replacing a value). An iterator records the count and checks it in `next()`; a mismatch throws `ConcurrentModificationException`. The name misleads: the classic case is one thread calling `list.remove(order)` inside `for (Order order : list)`. The check is best effort: removing the second-to-last element of an `ArrayList` ends the loop silently and skips the last element.","Within one thread, change the collection only through the iterator (`Iterator.remove`, `ListIterator.set`), use bulk methods (`removeIf`, `replaceAll`, `map.values().removeIf`), or build a new collection with a stream. `removeIf` is also faster on `ArrayList`: one pass instead of shifting the tail per removal. Across threads, `CopyOnWriteArrayList` iterates over a snapshot of its array, so it never throws and never sees later writes. `ConcurrentHashMap` and `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` iterators are weakly consistent: they never throw, return each element at most once, and may or may not reflect concurrent updates.","In production a CME usually means a plain collection is shared: a singleton bean's `HashMap` written by request threads and scanned by a `@Scheduled` job. The exception is the lucky outcome; the same race can silently lose entries, so catching and retrying fixes nothing. Confine the collection to one thread, publish immutable snapshots, or use a concurrent collection, and remember that iterating a `ConcurrentHashMap` while it changes is not a point-in-time view."],"keyPoints":["modCount detects structural changes outside the iterator","Iterator.remove, removeIf or rebuild within one thread","concurrent collections iterate weakly consistently or over snapshots"],"examples":[{"title":"Single-threaded CME and the safe ways to remove","lang":"java","code":"List<Order> pending = new ArrayList<>(orderRepository.findPending());\n\n// BAD: structural change behind the iterator, next() throws ConcurrentModificationException\nfor (Order order : pending) {\n    if (order.isExpired(clock)) {\n        pending.remove(order);\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: remove through the iterator\nfor (Iterator<Order> it = pending.iterator(); it.hasNext(); ) {\n    if (it.next().isExpired(clock)) {\n        it.remove();\n    }\n}\n\n// BETTER: one call, one O(n) pass\npending.removeIf(order -> order.isExpired(clock));\n\n// Maps: remove through a view, never map.remove(key) inside a keySet loop\nInstant cutoff = clock.instant().minus(Duration.ofMinutes(30));\nlastSeenBySession.values().removeIf(lastSeen -> lastSeen.isBefore(cutoff));\n\n// Often cleanest: do not mutate at all\nList<Order> stillValid = pending.stream().filter(o -> !o.isExpired(clock)).toList();","note":"All of these are single-threaded fixes; none makes the list safe to share between threads."},{"title":"Shared state: weakly consistent and snapshot iteration","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass MerchantActivityTracker {\n    // Written by request threads, scanned by a scheduled job\n    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Instant> lastPaymentByMerchant = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    // Read often, changed rarely: every write copies the array\n    private final List<Consumer<String>> inactivityListeners = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n\n    void onPayment(String merchantId, Instant at) {\n        lastPaymentByMerchant.merge(merchantId, at, (old, now) -> now.isAfter(old) ? now : old);\n    }\n\n    @Scheduled(fixedDelay = 60_000)\n    void evictInactive() {\n        Instant cutoff = Instant.now().minus(Duration.ofDays(30));\n        // Weakly consistent: never throws, may or may not see concurrent payments\n        for (Map.Entry<String, Instant> e : lastPaymentByMerchant.entrySet()) {\n            if (e.getValue().isBefore(cutoff)\n                    && lastPaymentByMerchant.remove(e.getKey(), e.getValue())) {   // only if unchanged\n                inactivityListeners.forEach(l -> l.accept(e.getKey()));        // snapshot iteration\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"`remove(key, expectedValue)` deletes only if no payment updated the entry since it was read, which is how you act safely on weakly consistent iteration."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why might removing inside a for-each loop not throw?","a":"`ArrayList`'s `hasNext()` is just `cursor != size`. Removing the second-to-last element makes size equal the cursor, so the loop ends normally and the last element is never visited. That is why the rule is to never modify outside the iterator, not to rely on the exception."},{"q":"Is Collections.synchronizedList enough?","a":"Only for single calls. Iterators and streams from the wrapper are still fail-fast, so you must hold `synchronized (list)` for the whole loop, which blocks writers meanwhile. For read-heavy data a `CopyOnWriteArrayList` or an immutable snapshot swapped through an `AtomicReference` usually fits better."}],"extra":["**Hidden triggers:** `get` on an access-order `LinkedHashMap` during iteration, a `HashMap.computeIfAbsent` that writes to the same map, and a `subList` used after its parent changed all throw CME.","**Fail-safe is not a JDK term:** the Javadoc says weakly consistent for concurrent collections and snapshot for copy-on-write ones.","**Snapshot cost:** copy-on-write iteration is free but each write copies the array, so it suits listener lists, not per-request growth.","**Streams:** modifying the source inside `stream().forEach` throws CME, for `ArrayList` usually after the traversal, when the change has already happened.","**Iterator.remove on copy-on-write lists:** it throws `UnsupportedOperationException`; use `list.removeIf`."],"redFlags":["Thinks CME needs multiple threads","Catches the exception instead of fixing ownership","Makes every list a CopyOnWriteArrayList"]},{"id":31,"category":"Java Collections","question":"A method fills a local list and returns it to callers. When is `List.of()` the right choice, and when is `new ArrayList<>()`?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`List.of()` gives a compact, unmodifiable, null-rejecting list for constants and API return values; `new ArrayList<>()` is a mutable, null-tolerant working buffer you fill locally and then expose as an unmodifiable copy.","answer":["`List.of(...)` (Java 9+) returns an unmodifiable list: `add`, `remove`, `set` and `sort` throw `UnsupportedOperationException`. It also rejects `null` eagerly, so `List.of(a, null)` throws `NullPointerException` at creation, and even `contains(null)` throws. `new ArrayList<>()` is a resizable, array-backed list that accepts `null` and duplicates and is not thread-safe.","Declaring a field `final` does not make the list immutable; it only stops reassignment of the reference. Immutability comes from the list implementation. That matters at API boundaries: if a service returns its internal `ArrayList`, any caller can mutate your state, and if it stores a caller's list, the caller can change it later. Returning or storing `List.copyOf(...)` removes that whole class of aliasing bugs and makes the value safe to share across threads without locking, provided the elements are themselves immutable.","In practice: use `ArrayList` as a local accumulator inside a method (building a result, batching rows, sorting) and for JPA entity collections, which must be mutable. Use `List.of` for constants, test fixtures and small fixed lists, and `List.copyOf` to freeze a list at the edge (constructor, record compact constructor, return value). The production payoff is fail-fast behavior: an accidental write or a stray `null` blows up where the bug is, instead of corrupting shared state or leaking into a JSON response three layers later."],"keyPoints":["unmodifiable lists fail fast on writes, so callers cannot corrupt shared state","a mutable list works as a local buffer while results are being built","copy the buffer into an unmodifiable list before it leaves the method","null elements are rejected when the list is created, not at first use"],"examples":[{"title":"Mutable buffer inside, unmodifiable copy at the boundary","lang":"java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic final class Order {\n    private final String orderId;\n    private final List<OrderLine> lines; // final reference only; the list decides mutability\n\n    public Order(String orderId, List<OrderLine> lines) {\n        this.orderId = orderId;\n        this.lines = List.copyOf(lines); // GOOD: snapshot, rejects null lines\n    }\n\n    public List<OrderLine> lines() {\n        return lines; // safe to hand out: callers cannot mutate it\n    }\n}\n\nclass OrderAssembler {\n    Order assemble(String orderId, List<CartItem> cart) {\n        List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>(cart.size()); // mutable local buffer\n        for (CartItem item : cart) {\n            if (item.quantity() > 0) {\n                lines.add(new OrderLine(item.sku(), item.quantity()));\n            }\n        }\n        return new Order(orderId, lines);\n    }\n}","note":"Build with a presized `ArrayList` inside the method, then freeze with `List.copyOf` in the constructor. A caller doing `order.lines().add(...)` gets an immediate `UnsupportedOperationException` instead of silently changing the order."},{"title":"Unmodifiable view versus real copy","lang":"java","code":"List<String> source = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"PENDING\", \"AUTHORIZED\"));\n\nList<String> view = Collections.unmodifiableList(source);\nList<String> copy = List.copyOf(source);\n\nsource.add(\"CAPTURED\");\n\nSystem.out.println(view);  // [PENDING, AUTHORIZED, CAPTURED]: the view sees the change\nSystem.out.println(copy);  // [PENDING, AUTHORIZED]: independent snapshot\n\nview.add(\"REFUNDED\");      // UnsupportedOperationException\nList.of(\"A\", null);        // NullPointerException at creation\nnew ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(\"A\", null)); // fine: ArrayList accepts null","note":"`Collections.unmodifiableList` only blocks writes through the wrapper; whoever holds `source` can still change what readers of `view` see. `List.copyOf` detaches completely."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does `Stream.toList()` return, and how is it different from `Collectors.toList()`?","a":"`Stream.toList()` (Java 16+) returns an unmodifiable list, and unlike `List.of` it accepts `null` elements. `Collectors.toList()` makes no promise about mutability, type or thread safety, even though it returns an `ArrayList` today. Prefer `toList()` for results you hand back, and `Collectors.toCollection(ArrayList::new)` when you really need to modify the result."},{"q":"A caller needs a read-only list that still reflects later updates. What would you return?","a":"A view from `Collections.unmodifiableList(internal)`, which delegates every read to the backing list, so callers see changes but cannot write through it. Make the live behavior part of the contract: its size can change between calls, and iterating it while the owner modifies the list can throw `ConcurrentModificationException`. If callers need a stable snapshot instead, return `List.copyOf(internal)`."}],"extra":["**Stream.toList() vs Collectors.toList():** `stream.toList()` (Java 16) returns an unmodifiable list that still permits `null` elements, `Collectors.toUnmodifiableList()` rejects nulls, and `Collectors.toList()` makes no mutability promise (it is an `ArrayList` today, but do not rely on it).","**Arrays.asList is a third behavior:** it is fixed-size and writes through to the backing array, so `set` works and changes the array, while `add` and `remove` throw `UnsupportedOperationException`.","**List.copyOf is cheap when already immutable:** passing a list created by `List.of` or `List.copyOf` returns the same instance without copying, so defensive copying at every layer costs almost nothing.","**Shallow only:** an unmodifiable list of mutable `Order` objects still lets callers mutate each order; real immutability needs immutable elements too (records with immutable components, `BigDecimal`, `String`, `Instant`).","**JPA collections must stay mutable:** initialize `@OneToMany` fields with `new ArrayList<>()` or `new HashSet<>()`, because your domain methods and Hibernate both mutate them; initialize with `List.of()` and the first `order.addLine(...)` throws `UnsupportedOperationException`."],"redFlags":["returns the internal mutable list directly and lets callers edit it","believes `Collections.unmodifiableList` produces a frozen snapshot","does not realize writes to an unmodifiable list fail only at runtime, with `UnsupportedOperationException`"]},{"id":32,"category":"Java Collections","question":"Which Queue or Deque would you use for a stack, a FIFO buffer and a priority scheduler?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Use `ArrayDeque` for both a stack (`push`/`pop`) and a single-threaded FIFO buffer (`offer`/`poll`), not the legacy `Stack` or `LinkedList`; use `PriorityQueue` with an explicit comparator for priority work. When producers and consumers are different threads, switch to a bounded `BlockingQueue`, or `PriorityBlockingQueue` for priorities.","answer":["`ArrayDeque` is a resizable circular array with O(1) operations at both ends, so it is the default stack and the default FIFO queue. `Stack` extends `Vector`: every method is synchronized, it inherits index-based methods such as `add(0, x)` that break the stack abstraction, and it iterates bottom to top. `LinkedList` also implements `Deque`, but it allocates a node per element, scatters them across the heap and accepts nulls, so it is slower and uses more memory for the same job. As a stack, call `push`, `pop` and `peek`; as a FIFO, call `offer` at the tail and `poll` at the head.","`PriorityQueue` is a binary heap: `offer` and `poll` are O(log n), `peek` is O(1), and the head is always the smallest element by its comparator, so lower numbers mean more urgent. Two caveats bite in practice. Iterating it or calling `toString` shows heap layout, not priority order; only repeated `poll` yields sorted order. And a heap is not stable, so jobs with equal priority come out in no particular order; if submission order matters within a priority, add a sequence number as a tie-breaker. There is no efficient way to change a queued element's priority: `remove(Object)` is O(n).","None of these classes is thread-safe. When one thread produces and another consumes, use a `BlockingQueue`: `ArrayBlockingQueue` or `LinkedBlockingQueue` with an explicit capacity, where `put` blocks when full, `take` blocks when empty, and `offer` with a timeout lets you reject instead of waiting. The capacity is your backpressure; `LinkedBlockingQueue` without one is bounded only by `Integer.MAX_VALUE`, which in practice means by the heap. `PriorityBlockingQueue` is the thread-safe heap, and it is unbounded. Everything in these queues disappears on a crash or redeploy, so work that must survive belongs in a broker or a database table."],"keyPoints":["ArrayDeque for stacks and FIFO buffers","PriorityQueue iterates in heap order and is not stable","bounded BlockingQueue between threads"],"examples":[{"title":"ArrayDeque as a stack and as a FIFO buffer","lang":"java","code":"// Stack: compensations run in reverse order of the steps that succeeded\nDeque<Runnable> compensations = new ArrayDeque<>();\ntry {\n    inventory.reserve(order);\n    compensations.push(() -> inventory.release(order));\n    payments.authorize(order);\n    compensations.push(() -> payments.voidAuthorization(order));\n    shipping.book(order);\n} catch (RuntimeException e) {\n    while (!compensations.isEmpty()) {\n        compensations.pop().run();           // void the payment first, then release stock\n    }\n    throw e;\n}\n\n// FIFO owned by one thread: offer at the tail, poll at the head\nDeque<String> pendingWebhooks = new ArrayDeque<>();\npendingWebhooks.offer(\"evt-1\");\npendingWebhooks.offer(\"evt-2\");\nString next = pendingWebhooks.poll();        // evt-1; null when empty, while pop() would throw\n\n// BAD: legacy Stack is a synchronized Vector and leaks list operations\nStack<String> legacy = new Stack<>();\nlegacy.push(\"a\");\nlegacy.push(\"b\");\nlegacy.add(0, \"c\");                          // compiles, breaks the stack\nSystem.out.println(legacy);                  // [c, a, b]: bottom to top","note":"`push` and `pop` work at the head of the deque, so `ArrayDeque` iterates top first, the opposite of `Stack`. `ArrayDeque` rejects null because `poll` uses null to mean empty."},{"title":"Priority scheduler with a FIFO tie-breaker, then a bounded handoff between threads","lang":"java","code":"record Job(String id, int priority, long seq) {}\n\nAtomicLong sequence = new AtomicLong();\n// Lower number = more urgent; seq keeps submission order within a priority\nPriorityQueue<Job> jobs = new PriorityQueue<>(\n        Comparator.comparingInt(Job::priority).thenComparingLong(Job::seq));\n\njobs.offer(new Job(\"refund-17\", 2, sequence.getAndIncrement()));\njobs.offer(new Job(\"fraud-check-9\", 0, sequence.getAndIncrement()));\njobs.offer(new Job(\"report-3\", 5, sequence.getAndIncrement()));\njobs.offer(new Job(\"refund-18\", 2, sequence.getAndIncrement()));\n\nSystem.out.println(jobs.stream().map(Job::id).toList());\n// [fraud-check-9, refund-17, report-3, refund-18]: heap layout, NOT priority order\nwhile (!jobs.isEmpty()) {\n    process(jobs.poll());                    // fraud-check-9, refund-17, refund-18, report-3\n}\n\n// Between threads: the capacity is the backpressure\nBlockingQueue<Job> handoff = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1_000);\n// producer: wait briefly, then reject instead of growing without limit\nif (!handoff.offer(job, 50, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {\n    throw new RejectedExecutionException(\"Job queue full, rejected \" + job.id());\n}\n// consumer\nJob taken = handoff.take();                  // blocks until a job arrives","note":"Without `seq`, the two refunds could run in either order. In a thread pool the same choice reappears as the executor's work queue."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why not LinkedList for a queue? It has O(1) add and remove at both ends.","a":"Same big O, worse constants: every element is a separate node object with two links, which costs memory, adds garbage collection work and destroys cache locality, while `ArrayDeque` stores references contiguously. `LinkedList` also accepts null, which makes `poll` returning null ambiguous. It only wins if you need the `List` interface as well, which is rare for a queue."},{"q":"How do you choose between the BlockingQueue implementations?","a":"`ArrayBlockingQueue` is a fixed array with one lock, always bounded, predictable memory. `LinkedBlockingQueue` uses separate put and take locks and can give more throughput, but pass a capacity. `SynchronousQueue` has no capacity and hands each element directly to a waiting consumer; `PriorityBlockingQueue` orders by priority but is unbounded, and `DelayQueue` releases elements only after their delay expires."}],"extra":["**Two method families:** `add`, `remove`, `element`, `push` and `pop` throw on a full or empty queue, while `offer`, `poll` and `peek` return false or null; pick one family and use it consistently.","**PriorityBlockingQueue in a ThreadPoolExecutor:** `submit` wraps tasks in a `FutureTask`, which is not `Comparable`, so queuing it throws `ClassCastException`; use `execute` with tasks that implement `Comparable`.","**Changing priority:** a heap has no update operation; remove and re-add, or enqueue a new entry and skip stale ones when they are polled.","**Scheduling by time:** for run-at-a-time work use a `ScheduledExecutorService` or a database-backed scheduler, not a hand-rolled `PriorityQueue` loop.","**Durability:** in-memory queues lose their contents on crash or redeploy; use a broker or an outbox table for anything that must be processed exactly once."],"redFlags":["Reaches for Stack or LinkedList by default","Expects PriorityQueue iteration to be sorted","Uses an unbounded queue between producer and consumer threads"]},{"id":33,"category":"Java Collections","question":"A team uses `CopyOnWriteArrayList` for a listener registry and for a per-request audit buffer. Why does it suit only one?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"`CopyOnWriteArrayList` gives lock-free reads and snapshot iterators by copying the whole array on every write, so it shines for small, read-mostly lists like listeners and becomes a CPU and memory hog under frequent writes.","answer":["Every mutation (`add`, `set`, `remove`) takes a lock, copies the entire backing array, applies the change and publishes the new array through a volatile field. Reads and iteration take no lock; they simply read the current array. An iterator captures the array at creation, so it never throws `ConcurrentModificationException`, never blocks writers and never sees later changes; `iterator.remove()` is unsupported.","That makes it ideal for small collections that are read constantly and written rarely: event listeners, interceptor or filter chains, a list of downstream endpoints refreshed from configuration, subscribers registered at startup. Readers on hot paths pay no synchronization cost, and you can iterate while other threads register or remove listeners without extra locking.","It becomes dangerous when writes are frequent or the list is large. Each write is an O(n) copy plus a new array allocation, so appending n elements one by one is quadratic work and garbage; with thousands of elements and steady writes you get allocation churn and GC pressure, and writers serialize on one lock. Snapshot semantics can surprise you too: an iteration in progress keeps calling a listener that was just removed, and a reader holding an old snapshot may act on stale data. Use it as a registry, never as a buffer, queue or growing log."],"keyPoints":["every add or remove allocates and copies the full backing array","readers and iterators work on an immutable array with no locking","an iterator shows the list as it was when the iterator started","fits small sets that change rarely, such as listeners or interceptors"],"examples":[{"title":"Listener registry: the intended use","lang":"java","code":"@Component\npublic class PaymentEventPublisher {\n\n    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(PaymentEventPublisher.class);\n\n    private final CopyOnWriteArrayList<PaymentListener> listeners = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n\n    public void register(PaymentListener listener) {  // rare: startup or plugin reload\n        listeners.addIfAbsent(listener);\n    }\n\n    public void unregister(PaymentListener listener) {\n        listeners.remove(listener);\n    }\n\n    public void publish(PaymentSettled event) {        // hot path: every settled payment\n        for (PaymentListener listener : listeners) {   // snapshot: no lock, no CME\n            try {\n                listener.onSettled(event);\n            } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n                log.warn(\"Listener {} failed for {}\", listener, event.paymentId(), e);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Writes happen a handful of times per deployment while `publish` runs thousands of times per second, which is exactly the ratio copy-on-write is designed for."},{"title":"Write-heavy misuse and a better structure","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: one full array copy per request; at 10k entries every add copies 10k references\nprivate final List<UsageEvent> usageBuffer = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n\nvoid onRequest(UsageEvent event) {\n    usageBuffer.add(event);\n}\n\n// GOOD: bounded queue for concurrent producers, drained in batches\nprivate final BlockingQueue<UsageEvent> usageQueue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(50_000);\n\nvoid onRequestQueued(UsageEvent event) {\n    if (!usageQueue.offer(event)) {\n        droppedEvents.increment(); // explicit backpressure instead of unbounded growth\n    }\n}\n\n@Scheduled(fixedDelay = 1000)\nvoid flush() {\n    List<UsageEvent> batch = new ArrayList<>(500);\n    usageQueue.drainTo(batch, 500);\n    if (!batch.isEmpty()) {\n        usageRepository.saveAll(batch);\n    }\n}","note":"The queue gives O(1) inserts, a bounded memory footprint and batch draining, while the copy-on-write buffer degrades quadratically as the buffer grows."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does adding 10,000 entries one at a time to a `CopyOnWriteArrayList` cost?","a":"Each `add` copies the whole current array, so adding 10,000 elements one by one performs roughly 50 million element copies and leaves 10,000 discarded arrays for the GC. Pass a prebuilt collection to the constructor or to `addAll`, which copies once. If writes are routine rather than rare, this is the wrong type."},{"q":"Writes become frequent in that audit path. What would you replace the list with?","a":"Pick by access pattern: `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` or a bounded `ArrayBlockingQueue` for buffers that one side fills and another drains, `ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()` for membership checks, and an `ArrayList` behind a `ReentrantReadWriteLock` when you truly need list semantics. If updates arrive as whole batches, keep an immutable list in an `AtomicReference` and swap it once per batch."}],"extra":["**Batch your writes:** `addAll`, `addAllAbsent` and `removeIf` copy the array once for the whole batch, so rebuilding a listener list from configuration should be one bulk call, not a loop of `add`.","**CopyOnWriteArraySet:** it is backed by a `CopyOnWriteArrayList`, so `contains` and `add` are linear scans; fine for a dozen listeners, wrong for thousands of ids.","**Humongous allocations:** in G1, an array larger than half a region is allocated as a humongous object, so copying a large array on every write can trigger frequent humongous allocations and extra GC cycles.","**Immutable snapshot alternative:** for configuration that is replaced wholesale, a `volatile List<Route>` or `AtomicReference<List<Route>>` holding a `List.copyOf(...)` gives the same lock-free reads with one explicit swap and no per-element write API to misuse.","**Only references are snapshotted:** if the elements are mutable objects, concurrent changes to their fields are not protected by the list at all."],"redFlags":["thinks writes are cheap because reads take no lock","expects an iterator to see elements added after it was created","calls `iterator().remove()` and is surprised by `UnsupportedOperationException`"]},{"id":34,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"Why do nested results like `Stream<List<LineItem>>` or `Optional<Optional<Card>>` appear with `map`, and how do you avoid them?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`map` applies a one-to-one transformation, while `flatMap` maps each element to a stream (or `Optional`) and flattens the results into one level, which is what you need for nested collections and chained optional lookups.","answer":["`map(Function<T, R>)` produces exactly one output per input: `orders.stream().map(Order::orderId)` gives a stream of ids. If the function itself returns a collection or a stream, `map` gives you a nested structure such as `Stream<List<LineItem>>`, which is rarely what you want.","`flatMap(Function<T, Stream<R>>)` lets each element produce zero, one or many results and concatenates them into a single `Stream<R>`. Turning a `List<Order>` into all of its line items is `flatMap(o -> o.lineItems().stream())`. Returning `Stream.empty()` drops an element, so `flatMap` can also act as a combined map and filter.","The same idea applies to `Optional`. When the mapping function already returns an `Optional`, `map` yields `Optional<Optional<T>>`, while `flatMap` keeps it flat: `findCustomer(id).flatMap(Customer::defaultPaymentMethod)`. That lets you chain lookups that may each be absent without null checks. `flatMap` is about shape, not performance: it creates a stream per element, so it is slightly more expensive than `map`, and for small fan-out on hot paths `mapMulti` (Java 16) avoids those intermediate streams."],"keyPoints":["a one-to-one function keeps both the element count and the nesting level","a function that returns a stream or optional needs flattening to stay one level deep","flattening an optional chain short-circuits at the first empty step","a `flatMap(Optional::stream)` step keeps only present values in a stream"],"examples":[{"title":"Orders to line items and revenue per SKU","lang":"java","code":"record LineItem(String sku, int quantity, BigDecimal unitPrice) {\n    BigDecimal total() { return unitPrice.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(quantity)); }\n}\nrecord Order(String orderId, List<LineItem> lineItems) { }\n\nclass RevenueReport {\n    Map<String, BigDecimal> revenueBySku(List<Order> orders) {\n        // map: one result per order\n        List<String> orderIds = orders.stream().map(Order::orderId).toList();\n\n        // map with a list-returning function gives nesting\n        Stream<List<LineItem>> nested = orders.stream().map(Order::lineItems);\n\n        // flatMap: one flat stream of every line item across all orders\n        return orders.stream()\n                .flatMap(order -> order.lineItems().stream())\n                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(\n                        LineItem::sku,\n                        Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, LineItem::total, BigDecimal::add)));\n    }\n}","note":"`flatMap` is the only step that changes the element type from `Order` to `LineItem`; everything after it works on a flat stream, so grouping and summing need no nested loops."},{"title":"Optional pipelines with flatMap","lang":"java","code":"@Service\nclass PaymentMethodService {\n\n    private final CustomerRepository customerRepository; // findById returns Optional<Customer>\n\n    PaymentMethodService(CustomerRepository customerRepository) {\n        this.customerRepository = customerRepository;\n    }\n\n    public Optional<String> cardTokenFor(String customerId) {\n        return customerRepository.findById(customerId)   // Optional<Customer>\n                .flatMap(Customer::defaultPaymentMethod) // Optional<PaymentMethod>, not nested\n                .filter(PaymentMethod::isActive)\n                .map(PaymentMethod::token);              // plain value: map is enough\n    }\n\n    // BAD: map with an Optional-returning function nests the result\n    // Optional<Optional<PaymentMethod>> nested = repo.findById(id).map(Customer::defaultPaymentMethod);\n\n    public List<String> activeTokens(List<String> customerIds) {\n        return customerIds.stream()\n                .map(this::cardTokenFor)                 // Stream<Optional<String>>\n                .flatMap(Optional::stream)               // keep present values only (Java 9+)\n                .toList();\n    }\n}","note":"If any step is empty the chain short-circuits to `Optional.empty()`, so there is no null check anywhere, and `flatMap(Optional::stream)` bridges from optionals back into a stream."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you keep the order id alongside each line item while flattening?","a":"Map inside the inner stream to a small record that carries the parent: `orders.stream().flatMap(o -> o.lineItems().stream().map(li -> new OrderLineRef(o.orderId(), li))).toList()`. The inner lambda closes over `o`, so the parent is in scope for every child. Java 16's `mapMulti` is an alternative when you want to push zero or more results imperatively without creating an inner stream."},{"q":"When would you use `Optional.flatMap` instead of `Optional.map`?","a":"When the function you apply already returns an `Optional`, such as `findCustomer(id).flatMap(Customer::defaultPaymentMethod)`; `map` there would produce `Optional<Optional<PaymentMethod>>`. If any step is empty, the remaining steps are skipped and the result is `Optional.empty()`. Use `map` when the function returns a plain value, which `map` wraps for you and turns into empty if it is `null`."}],"extra":["**Optional.stream():** since Java 9, `flatMap(Optional::stream)` turns a `Stream<Optional<T>>` into only the present values, replacing the older `filter(Optional::isPresent).map(Optional::get)` idiom.","**Stream.ofNullable:** `flatMap(o -> Stream.ofNullable(o.couponCode()))` (Java 9) maps a nullable field to zero or one elements without an explicit null filter.","**Laziness fix in Java 10:** before JDK 10, `flatMap` fully consumed each inner stream even under short-circuiting operations like `findFirst`, which was costly and could hang with infinite inner streams; since Java 10 it respects short-circuiting.","**Reactive flatMap is different:** in Project Reactor, `flatMap` subscribes to inner publishers concurrently and interleaves their results with no order guarantee, while `concatMap` preserves order; assuming `Stream.flatMap` semantics there is a common source of reordering bugs in WebFlux code.","**flatMap for validation:** mapping each input to a stream of errors and flattening (`flatMap(row -> validate(row).stream())`) collects all validation errors across a batch without any shared mutable state."],"redFlags":["calls `.get()` on an inner optional instead of flattening the chain","thinks `flatMap` reorders elements or removes duplicates","triggers a lazy load per order inside the stream without noticing N+1 queries"]},{"id":35,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"What is a functional interface, and how do lambdas and method references relate to it?","level":"Basic","tldr":"A functional interface has exactly one abstract method, and a lambda or method reference is a compact implementation of that method: it has no type of its own and takes the functional interface type its context expects. The compiler checks the shape and the runtime creates the object through `invokedynamic`.","answer":["A functional interface declares exactly one abstract method. Default methods, static methods and abstract methods that match public `Object` methods do not count, which is why `Comparator` is functional even though it declares `equals`. `@FunctionalInterface` is optional, but it makes the compiler reject a second abstract method. `java.util.function` covers the common shapes: `Function`, `BiFunction`, `Supplier`, `Consumer`, `Predicate`, `UnaryOperator`, `BinaryOperator`, plus primitive variants such as `IntPredicate` and `ToLongFunction` that avoid boxing. Older interfaces such as `Runnable`, `Callable` and `Comparator` qualify too.","A lambda is typed by its target, the variable, parameter or return type it is assigned to, so the same text `s -> s.isBlank()` can be a `Predicate<String>` or a `Function<String, Boolean>`. At runtime the body becomes a private synthetic method, and an `invokedynamic` call site asks `LambdaMetafactory` to create the implementing class on first use. Non-capturing lambdas typically reuse one instance; capturing ones allocate per evaluation, and captured locals must be effectively final. Inside a lambda `this` is the enclosing object. Method references are shorthand when a lambda only calls one method: static (`Integer::parseInt`), bound (`auditLog::record`), unbound (`Payment::amount`, the first argument becomes the receiver) and constructor (`PaymentId::new`).","In production code three things matter. The `java.util.function` types declare no checked exceptions, so I/O inside a lambda must be wrapped, typically in `UncheckedIOException`, or you define your own interface that declares `throws`. Lambdas have no useful identity: `this::onPayment` evaluated twice gives two different objects, so unsubscribing with a fresh reference silently removes nothing. And a domain interface such as `FeeRule` reads better than a raw `Function<Payment, BigDecimal>` and can carry default methods for composition, the way `Comparator.thenComparing` does."],"keyPoints":["one abstract method, typed by context","four kinds of method references","no checked exceptions and no reliable identity"],"examples":[{"title":"A domain functional interface, lambdas and the four method reference kinds","lang":"java","code":"@FunctionalInterface\npublic interface FeeRule {\n    BigDecimal feeFor(Payment payment);               // the single abstract method\n\n    default FeeRule plus(FeeRule next) {              // defaults do not count\n        return p -> feeFor(p).add(next.feeFor(p));\n    }\n\n    static FeeRule flat(BigDecimal amount) {          // statics do not count\n        return p -> amount;\n    }\n\n    String toString();                                // Object method: does not count\n}\n\nFeeRule cardFee = p -> p.amount().multiply(new BigDecimal(\"0.0150\"));\nFeeRule total = cardFee.plus(FeeRule.flat(new BigDecimal(\"1.80\")));\ntotal.feeFor(new Payment(new BigDecimal(\"100.00\")));  // 3.300000\n\n// Same lambda text, different target types\nPredicate<String> isBlank = s -> s.isBlank();\nFunction<String, Boolean> isBlankFn = s -> s.isBlank();\n\n// Method references\nFunction<String, Integer> parse = Integer::parseInt;      // static\nConsumer<Payment> audit = auditLog::record;               // bound: receiver fixed now\nFunction<Payment, BigDecimal> amount = Payment::amount;   // unbound: argument is receiver\nFunction<String, PaymentId> toId = PaymentId::new;        // constructor","note":"`plus` composes rules the way `Comparator.thenComparing` composes comparators; named domain interfaces give you a place to put that behavior."},{"title":"Lambda gotchas: identity, checked exceptions, eager receivers, captured variables","lang":"java","code":"class FraudMonitor {\n    private final Consumer<PaymentEvent> handler = this::onPayment;   // keep one instance\n\n    void start() { bus.subscribe(this::onPayment); }\n    void stop()  { bus.unsubscribe(this::onPayment); }   // BAD: new object, nothing removed\n    // GOOD: subscribe(handler) and unsubscribe(handler)\n\n    void onPayment(PaymentEvent event) { /* ... */ }\n}\n\n// Function.apply declares no checked exceptions: wrap at the boundary\nList<String> statements = paths.stream()\n        .map(path -> {\n            try {\n                return Files.readString(path);\n            } catch (IOException e) {\n                throw new UncheckedIOException(e);\n            }\n        })\n        .toList();\n\n// A bound method reference evaluates its receiver immediately\nString merchantId = lookup.find(request);              // may be null\nSupplier<Integer> lazy = () -> merchantId.length();    // NPE only when get() is called\nSupplier<Integer> eager = merchantId::length;          // NPE right here if null\n\n// Captured locals must be effectively final\nint failed = 0;\n// payments.forEach(p -> { if (p.failed()) failed++; });   // does not compile\nlong failedCount = payments.stream().filter(Payment::failed).count();","note":"The listener bug compiles, runs and leaks: every `stop` leaves the old subscriber in place. Storing the reference in a field gives you one object to add and remove."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does a lambda differ from an anonymous inner class?","a":"A lambda introduces no new scope: `this` is the enclosing instance and it cannot shadow locals. It has no class file of its own; the class is spun at runtime through `invokedynamic`, and non-capturing lambdas usually share one instance. An anonymous class creates a new object on every evaluation, can hold fields, and can implement an interface with several abstract methods."},{"q":"Why must captured local variables be effectively final?","a":"The lambda captures the value, not the variable: the local lives in a stack frame that may be gone when the lambda runs, possibly on another thread, so mutation would silently diverge. An `AtomicInteger` counter works around it but hides shared mutable state; a reduction like `count()` or `sum()` is the real fix."}],"extra":["**Overload ambiguity:** a method overloaded on `Consumer<String>` and `Function<String, String>` makes `f(s -> s.trim())` a compile error, because the lambda fits both; avoid overloading on functional interfaces of the same arity.","**Primitive specializations:** `Function<Integer, Integer>` boxes on every call; `IntUnaryOperator`, `ToLongFunction` and `LongSupplier` avoid that in hot paths.","**Stack traces:** lambda frames appear as `lambda$recentOrders$0`; extract long lambdas into named methods.","**Runnable or Callable:** `executor.submit(() -> repository.save(order))` picks `Callable` because the body returns a value; a void block lambda picks `Runnable`.","**Effectively final:** a local assigned once is effectively final without the keyword; one later assignment breaks every lambda that captures it."],"redFlags":["Calls lambdas anonymous classes with shorter syntax","Cannot name the kinds of method references","Swallows checked exceptions inside lambdas"]},{"id":36,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"How should Optional be used, and where is it a mistake?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`Optional` is a return type that tells callers a result may be absent and makes them handle it with `map`, `orElseThrow` or `orElseGet`. It is a mistake as a field, a parameter, a wrapper around a collection, or anything that can itself be null.","answer":["`Optional<T>` holds zero or one non-null value. Its designers aimed it at method return types where 'no result' is a normal outcome and returning null would invite errors; Spring Data's `findById` is the canonical example. You create one with `Optional.of` (throws on null), `Optional.ofNullable` or `Optional.empty`, and consume it with `map`, `flatMap`, `filter`, `orElse`, `orElseGet`, `orElseThrow`, `ifPresentOrElse`, `or` and `stream`. Writing `isPresent()` followed by `get()` is a null check with extra steps; `orElseThrow()` says what happens when the value is missing.","The common real bug is `orElse` versus `orElseGet`. The argument of `orElse` is evaluated before the call, every time, so `findByIban(iban).orElse(createAccount(iban))` inserts an account even when one exists. `orElseGet` takes a `Supplier` and runs it only when the `Optional` is empty. Use `orElse` for constants, `orElseGet` for anything with cost or side effects, and `orElseThrow(() -> new AccountNotFoundException(id))` when absence is an error that should become a 404. `flatMap` chains lookups that each return `Optional` without ending up with `Optional<Optional<T>>`.","The mistakes are about where it appears. As a field it adds an object per instance, is not `Serializable` and is not a JPA attribute type; keep the field nullable and return `Optional` from the getter. As a parameter it forces every caller to wrap; overload instead. Around a collection it creates two kinds of nothing; return an empty list. And an `Optional`-returning method must never return null, or callers get the exact `NullPointerException` it was meant to prevent. Primitive variants such as `OptionalInt` avoid boxing but have no `map` or `filter`."],"keyPoints":["a return type for results that may be absent","orElse is eager, orElseGet is lazy","not for fields, parameters or collections"],"examples":[{"title":"Idiomatic use in a service: chaining, orElseThrow and orElseGet","lang":"java","code":"public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, UUID> {\n    Optional<Account> findByIban(String iban);         // absence is a normal outcome\n}\n\n@Service\nclass PayoutService {\n\n    BigDecimal availableBalance(UUID accountId) {\n        return accounts.findById(accountId)\n                .filter(Account::isActive)\n                .map(Account::balance)\n                .orElseThrow(() -> new AccountNotFoundException(accountId));  // becomes a 404\n    }\n\n    String payoutCurrency(Merchant merchant) {\n        return merchant.settlementAccountId()          // Optional<UUID>\n                .flatMap(accounts::findById)           // Optional<Account>, not nested\n                .map(Account::currency)\n                .orElse(\"SEK\");                        // constant: orElse is fine\n    }\n\n    Account accountFor(String iban) {\n        // BAD: createAccount runs, and inserts a row, on EVERY call\n        // return accounts.findByIban(iban).orElse(createAccount(iban));\n\n        // GOOD: the supplier runs only when the Optional is empty\n        return accounts.findByIban(iban).orElseGet(() -> createAccount(iban));\n    }\n}","note":"The commented line passes every test that only checks the returned account; it shows up later as duplicate rows or a unique constraint violation."},{"title":"Where Optional is a mistake, and what to write instead","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: Optional field; not Serializable, not a JPA attribute type, one more object\n// private Optional<String> phoneNumber;\n\n// GOOD: nullable field, Optional at the accessor\n@Entity\nclass Customer {\n    @Column(name = \"phone_number\")\n    private String phoneNumber;\n\n    public Optional<String> phoneNumber() {\n        return Optional.ofNullable(phoneNumber);\n    }\n}\n\n// BAD: Optional parameter, every caller must wrap\nList<Order> search(String customerId, Optional<OrderStatus> status);\n// GOOD: overloads\nList<Order> search(String customerId);\nList<Order> search(String customerId, OrderStatus status);\n\n// BAD: two kinds of \"nothing\"\nOptional<List<Order>> openOrders(String customerId);\n// GOOD: an empty list already means none\nList<Order> openOrders(String customerId);\n\n// BAD: a null check with extra steps\nif (order.isPresent()) { ship(order.get()); }\n// GOOD\norder.ifPresentOrElse(this::ship, () -> log.warn(\"Order {} not found\", orderId));\n\n// BAD: promises Optional, returns null\nOptional<Refund> findRefund(UUID paymentId) { return null; }","note":"Each GOOD version keeps `Optional` at the boundary where a caller decides what absence means, and plain types everywhere else."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Should service methods and controllers return Optional?","a":"Repositories and lookup methods yes, when absence is a normal outcome the caller should decide on. When absence is an error for that use case, throw a domain exception such as `OrderNotFoundException` and map it to 404 in a `@ControllerAdvice`. In a controller, `ResponseEntity.of(optional)` returns 200 with the body or 404 when empty."},{"q":"Can Optional model a PATCH field that may be absent, null or set?","a":"No: that needs three states (leave unchanged, clear, set) and `Optional` has two, so people fall back to a null `Optional` as the third state, the exact misuse to avoid. Use a tri-state wrapper such as `JsonNullable` from the OpenAPI tooling, or apply a JSON Merge Patch document."}],"extra":["**Streams of Optionals:** `.flatMap(Optional::stream)` keeps the present values, replacing `filter(Optional::isPresent).map(Optional::get)`.","**of versus ofNullable:** `Optional.of(null)` throws immediately, which is useful as an assertion; use `ofNullable` for legacy APIs that may return null.","**Value-based class:** do not compare `Optional`s with `==` or synchronize on them; use `equals`.","**get() versus orElseThrow():** they behave the same, but the no-argument `orElseThrow()` says it can throw; static analysis flags unchecked `get()`.","**Record components:** prefer a nullable component with an `Optional` accessor method, or two record types, over an `Optional` component; the same field rules apply."],"redFlags":["Uses isPresent and get everywhere","Puts Optional in fields and parameters","Passes an expensive call to orElse"]},{"id":37,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"A stream pipeline with `peek(log::info)` and `map` prints nothing at all. What explains this, and when does the work run?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Intermediate operations only describe a pipeline; nothing runs until a terminal operation pulls elements through the fused stages one at a time, short-circuiting operations can stop early, and a stream can be consumed only once.","answer":["Calling `filter`, `map`, `sorted` or `peek` processes nothing: each call returns a new stage linked to the previous one and captures the lambda. When a terminal operation such as `collect`, `forEach`, `reduce`, `count`, `findFirst` or `anyMatch` runs, the stages are fused into a chain of sinks and each element flows through the whole chain before the next one starts, rather than stage by stage over the full collection. That is why `filter` then `map` over a million elements does not allocate an intermediate list.","Short-circuiting operations exploit this: `findFirst`, `findAny`, `anyMatch`, `allMatch`, `noneMatch`, `limit` and `takeWhile` stop pulling from the source as soon as the answer is known, which also makes infinite sources like `Stream.iterate` usable. Stateful operations such as `sorted` and `distinct` behave differently: `sorted` must see every element before it emits the first one, which removes most of the short-circuit benefit.","Streams are single-use: once a terminal operation has run, or once another stage has been chained from it, reusing the stream throws `IllegalStateException` (stream has already been operated upon or closed). To traverse twice, keep the source collection or a `Supplier<Stream<T>>`. The practical consequences: side effects placed in `peek` or `map` may run fewer times than you expect or never, and a pipeline without a terminal operation silently does nothing."],"keyPoints":["intermediate calls only record stages; no element moves until a terminal call","elements flow one at a time through all stages, not stage by stage","operations like `findFirst` or `limit` stop pulling once satisfied","a consumed stream throws `IllegalStateException` if used again"],"examples":[{"title":"Watching when work actually happens","lang":"java","code":"List<String> paymentIds = List.of(\"p-1\", \"p-2\", \"p-3\", \"p-4\");\n\nStream<Payment> pipeline = paymentIds.stream()\n        .filter(id -> {\n            System.out.println(\"filter \" + id);\n            return !id.equals(\"p-1\");\n        })\n        .map(id -> {\n            System.out.println(\"load \" + id);\n            return paymentRepository.load(id);   // expensive call\n        });\n\nSystem.out.println(\"pipeline built, nothing printed yet\");\n\nOptional<Payment> first = pipeline.findFirst();\n// Output: filter p-1, filter p-2, load p-2   (p-3 and p-4 are never touched)\n\npipeline.count(); // IllegalStateException: stream has already been operated upon or closed","note":"Only one expensive `load` call happens, and the elements are processed vertically (filter p-2 is immediately followed by load p-2). Reusing `pipeline` fails because streams are single-use."},{"title":"Lazy streaming from PostgreSQL with Spring Data","lang":"java","code":"public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {\n    @QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = \"org.hibernate.fetchSize\", value = \"500\"))\n    Stream<Order> streamByStatus(OrderStatus status);\n}\n\n@Service\nclass OrderExportService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n    private final CsvWriter csv;\n\n    OrderExportService(OrderRepository orders, CsvWriter csv) {\n        this.orders = orders;\n        this.csv = csv;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional(readOnly = true)             // the cursor needs an open transaction\n    public void exportPending() {\n        try (Stream<Order> stream = orders.streamByStatus(OrderStatus.PENDING)) {\n            stream.map(OrderCsvRow::from)       // rows are fetched as the terminal op pulls\n                  .forEach(csv::write);         // terminal operation drives the JDBC cursor\n        }                                       // close releases the ResultSet\n    }\n}","note":"Laziness here spans all the way to the database: rows arrive in fetch-size batches as `forEach` pulls them, so a multi-million row export can run in bounded memory, provided the stream is closed, the transaction stays open and each entity is detached after it is written; otherwise the persistence context keeps every streamed entity."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does adding `sorted()` before `findFirst()` change how much of the source is read?","a":"`sorted` is a stateful barrier: it cannot emit its first element until it has seen all of them, so it buffers the entire upstream before anything reaches `findFirst`. Without it, `findFirst` stops traversal right after the first element that survives the earlier stages. If you only need the smallest element, `min(comparator)` does one pass without sorting or buffering."},{"q":"A stream from `Files.lines` is built but never consumed. What is the cost?","a":"No lines are read and none of the lambdas run, but the file was opened when `Files.lines` returned, so the handle stays open until the stream is closed. Under load, leaked handles can exhaust the process's file descriptors. Always wrap I/O-backed streams in try-with-resources, whether or not a terminal operation runs."}],"extra":["**count() may skip the pipeline:** since Java 9, `count()` can compute the size directly from a sized source without executing `map` or `peek`, so `List.of(1, 2, 3).stream().peek(System.out::println).count()` prints nothing.","**PostgreSQL cursors:** the PostgreSQL JDBC driver only streams rows in fetch-size batches when auto-commit is off; otherwise it loads the whole result set into memory, so a lazy `Stream<Order>` can still cause an `OutOfMemoryError` outside a transaction.","**Persistence context growth:** streamed entities stay managed until the transaction ends, so for large exports detach or clear the `EntityManager` periodically, or stream DTO projections instead of entities.","**findFirst versus findAny in parallel:** `findFirst` must respect encounter order and may keep earlier segments working, while `findAny` can return whichever match any thread finds first and is cheaper when order does not matter.","**Lazy sources hold resources:** `Files.lines` and `BufferedReader.lines` read as elements are pulled, so the file stays open until the stream is closed; always use try-with-resources with I/O-backed streams."],"redFlags":["relies on `peek` for required side effects such as saving or auditing","assumes `sorted` is lazy like `filter` and does not buffer its input","forgets to close a stream returned by `Files.lines` or a repository"]},{"id":38,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"How do groupingBy and partitioningBy work with downstream collectors?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`groupingBy` puts elements into buckets by a classifier key and runs a downstream collector on each bucket (`toList()` by default); `partitioningBy` does the same with a predicate and always returns both a `true` and a `false` bucket. The downstream collector (`counting`, `reducing`, `mapping`, `teeing`, another `groupingBy`) turns rows into a report.","answer":["`groupingBy(classifier)` applies the function to each element and adds the element to the bucket for that key, giving a `Map<K, List<T>>`. The two-argument form `groupingBy(classifier, downstream)` replaces the list with any collector applied per bucket, and the three-argument form also takes a map factory such as `TreeMap::new` for sorted keys or `() -> new EnumMap<>(OrderStatus.class)` for enum keys. `partitioningBy(predicate, downstream)` is the boolean special case: the result always contains both `true` and `false`, even when one side is empty, while `groupingBy` only creates keys that actually occur. A status with no orders is simply missing from the map.","Downstream collectors compose like a small query language. `counting()` and `summingLong` aggregate; `mapping(f, toSet())` transforms before collecting; `filtering(p, toList())` filters inside each group, keeping groups whose result is empty, unlike a `filter` before grouping, which drops them; `flatMapping` flattens per group; `maxBy` returns an `Optional` per group, which `collectingAndThen(maxBy(...), Optional::orElseThrow)` unwraps safely because a group is never empty; `teeing` runs two collectors and merges their results, which is how you compute an exact `BigDecimal` average. Nesting `groupingBy` gives multi-level maps such as customer to status to order ids.","In production the questions are correctness and where the work happens. `averagingDouble` suits latencies, not money; use `BigDecimal` reductions or sum minor units with `summingLong`. A null classifier key throws `NullPointerException`, so map missing values to an explicit key such as `UNKNOWN`. And grouping holds every element in memory: if the rows live in the database, a `GROUP BY` query returns ten rows instead of loading a million entities to compute them."],"keyPoints":["classifier picks the bucket, downstream reduces it","partitioningBy always has both keys","aggregate money with BigDecimal, not doubles"],"examples":[{"title":"Total order value per customer, count and exact average per status","lang":"java","code":"enum OrderStatus { CREATED, PAID, SHIPPED, CANCELLED }\n\nrecord Order(UUID id, String customerId, OrderStatus status, BigDecimal total) {}\n\n// Sorted by customer id, values summed exactly\nMap<String, BigDecimal> totalByCustomer = orders.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Order::customerId, TreeMap::new,\n                Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Order::total, BigDecimal::add)));\n// {c1=75.50, c2=100.00}\n\n// EnumMap: compact, iterates in declaration order; statuses with no orders are absent\nMap<OrderStatus, Long> countByStatus = orders.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Order::status,\n                () -> new EnumMap<>(OrderStatus.class), Collectors.counting()));\n// {PAID=2, SHIPPED=1}\n\n// Average order value per status: teeing combines a sum and a count\nMap<OrderStatus, BigDecimal> averageByStatus = orders.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Order::status,\n                Collectors.teeing(\n                        Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Order::total, BigDecimal::add),\n                        Collectors.counting(),\n                        (sum, count) -> sum.divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(count), 2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN))));\n// {PAID=75.00, SHIPPED=25.50}\n\n// BAD for money: binary floating point\n// Collectors.averagingDouble(o -> o.total().doubleValue())","note":"The division can safely use `count` because a group always has at least one element. Pass the map factory whenever iteration order or mutability of the result matters."},{"title":"partitioningBy, filtering versus filter, and unwrapping maxBy","lang":"java","code":"BigDecimal reviewThreshold = new BigDecimal(\"10000\");\n\n// Always both keys, even when nothing needs review\nMap<Boolean, Long> needsReview = orders.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(\n                o -> o.total().compareTo(reviewThreshold) > 0, Collectors.counting()));\n// {false=3, true=0}\nlong flagged = needsReview.get(true);       // safe, never null\n\nBigDecimal large = new BigDecimal(\"80\");\n\n// filter first: customers with no large orders disappear from the map\nMap<String, List<Order>> dropped = orders.stream()\n        .filter(o -> o.total().compareTo(large) > 0)\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Order::customerId));\n// {c2=[...]}\n\n// filtering downstream: every customer stays, some with an empty list\nMap<String, List<Order>> kept = orders.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Order::customerId,\n                Collectors.filtering(o -> o.total().compareTo(large) > 0, Collectors.toList())));\n// {c1=[], c2=[...]}\n\n// Largest payment per merchant: maxBy gives Optional, a group is never empty\nMap<String, Payment> largestByMerchant = payments.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Payment::merchantId,\n                Collectors.collectingAndThen(\n                        Collectors.maxBy(Comparator.comparing(Payment::amount)),\n                        Optional::orElseThrow)));","note":"Whether an empty group should appear is a business decision, for example a report row showing zero for a customer; `filtering` and `filter` give different answers."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you get the top 10 customers by total spend?","a":"Group with `reducing` into a `Map<String, BigDecimal>`, then stream its `entrySet()`, sort by `Map.Entry.comparingByValue(Comparator.reverseOrder())`, `limit(10)`, and collect into a `LinkedHashMap` so the ranking order survives. If the orders are in the database, `GROUP BY customer_id ORDER BY sum(total) DESC LIMIT 10` is far cheaper than loading every order."},{"q":"When is groupingByConcurrent useful?","a":"With a parallel stream where encounter order does not matter: all threads insert into one `ConcurrentHashMap` instead of building per-thread maps and merging them, which can be faster for large inputs. It gives up order within each group, and most backend grouping is too small for parallel streams to pay off at all."}],"extra":["**Insertion order of groups:** `groupingBy(f, LinkedHashMap::new, toList())` keeps groups in order of first appearance, useful when the input is already sorted.","**Missing groups:** to show zero rows for absent statuses, start from `EnumMap` filled with every constant and `putAll` the grouped counts.","**Minor units:** `summingLong(Order::totalCents)` is exact and fast when amounts are stored as cents.","**toMap is not grouping:** `toMap` fails on duplicate keys without a merge function, while `groupingBy` expects many elements per key.","**Result mutability:** without a factory the returned map and lists have no guaranteed type or mutability; wrap with `collectingAndThen(..., Map::copyOf)` for an immutable result."],"redFlags":["Collects to lists and loops to aggregate","Averages money with doubles","Groups millions of rows in memory instead of using GROUP BY"]},{"id":39,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"Someone builds a `List` with `reduce(new ArrayList<>(), ...)`. What would you suggest instead, and when is `reduce` the right tool?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`reduce` folds a stream into a single value by repeatedly applying an associative function, starting from a genuine identity and without mutating anything; `collect` gives each segment a fresh container from a supplier, fills it with an accumulator and joins segments with a combiner, so `Collectors` is what you use for lists, maps and groupings.","answer":["`reduce(identity, accumulator, combiner)` treats values as immutable: each step returns a new value, as in `reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add)` or `reduce(Integer::max)`. The operation must be associative and the identity must be a real neutral element, because a parallel stream is split, each part is reduced separately starting from the identity, and partial results are combined in an unspecified grouping. Subtraction, or a sum that starts at 100 instead of 0, silently produces different results in parallel.","`collect` is mutable reduction: a `supplier` creates a fresh container (one per parallel segment), the `accumulator` adds each element into it in place, and the `combiner` merges containers. That is efficient for building a `List`, `Map`, `StringBuilder` or statistics object, and it is parallel-safe because each thread owns its container. The `Collector` interface packages these functions together with a `finisher` and characteristics such as `CONCURRENT` and `UNORDERED`.","In practice, use `reduce`, or better the primitive specializations like `mapToLong(...).sum()`, for scalar results, and `collect` with `Collectors` (`toList`, `toMap`, `groupingBy`, `partitioningBy`, `joining`, `teeing`) for anything container-shaped. Using `reduce` with an `ArrayList` identity is the classic bug: it mutates one shared object and breaks under parallelism."],"keyPoints":["a fold needs an associative function and a real identity to split safely","a collector creates one container per segment and merges them at the end","reducing into an `ArrayList` shares one identity object across threads","sums, maxima and other immutable results suit `reduce`; collections suit `collect`"],"examples":[{"title":"Scalar reduction versus mutable reduction","lang":"java","code":"// reduce: immutable values, associative operation, true identity\nBigDecimal settledTotal = payments.stream()\n        .filter(Payment::isSettled)\n        .map(Payment::amount)\n        .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);\n\nlong totalMinorUnits = payments.stream()\n        .mapToLong(Payment::amountMinorUnits)   // primitive stream: no boxing\n        .sum();\n\n// BAD: the identity is one shared ArrayList, mutated by every accumulator call\nList<String> idsBad = payments.parallelStream()\n        .reduce(new ArrayList<String>(),\n                (list, p) -> { list.add(p.id()); return list; },\n                (l1, l2) -> { l1.addAll(l2); return l1; });\n\n// GOOD: collect gives each segment its own container\nList<String> ids = payments.parallelStream()\n        .map(Payment::id)\n        .collect(Collectors.toList());\n\n// GOOD: the same thing with an explicit supplier, accumulator and combiner\nArrayList<String> ids2 = payments.parallelStream()\n        .map(Payment::id)\n        .collect(ArrayList::new, ArrayList::add, ArrayList::addAll);","note":"The BAD version may even pass tests sequentially, but in parallel several threads call `add` on the same `ArrayList` and the combiner merges the list with itself, producing duplicates, lost elements or exceptions."},{"title":"groupingBy, toMap and teeing in a settlement report","lang":"java","code":"// groupingBy: many values per key, a downstream collector shapes each value\nMap<String, BigDecimal> totalByMerchant = payments.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(\n                Payment::merchantId,\n                TreeMap::new,                                    // sorted report order\n                Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Payment::amount, BigDecimal::add)));\n\nMap<PaymentStatus, Long> countByStatus = payments.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(\n                Payment::status,\n                () -> new EnumMap<>(PaymentStatus.class),\n                Collectors.counting()));\n\n// toMap: exactly one value per key, duplicates must be resolved explicitly\nMap<String, Payment> latestByOrder = payments.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(\n                Payment::orderId,\n                Function.identity(),\n                (a, b) -> a.createdAt().isAfter(b.createdAt()) ? a : b));\n\n// teeing (Java 12): two reductions in one pass\nrecord Summary(long count, BigDecimal total) { }\nSummary summary = payments.stream().collect(Collectors.teeing(\n        Collectors.counting(),\n        Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Payment::amount, BigDecimal::add),\n        Summary::new));","note":"Each collector owns its intermediate containers, so the same code is correct sequentially and in parallel; the choice between `groupingBy` and `toMap` states whether duplicates per key are expected."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why might `reduce(10, Integer::sum)` give different answers sequentially and in parallel?","a":"Because 10 is not an identity for addition. A sequential stream adds it once, but a parallel stream starts every segment from the identity, so 10 is added once per segment and the total depends on how the source was split. The identity must satisfy `combiner.apply(identity, x)` equals `x` for every `x`; to start from an offset, reduce from 0 and add 10 afterwards."},{"q":"When grouping orders by customer, how do `groupingBy` and `toMap` treat a second order for the same customer?","a":"`groupingBy` expects many values per key, so the second order simply joins that customer's `List`, or feeds a downstream collector such as `counting()` or `summingLong`. `toMap` expects one value per key and throws `IllegalStateException` on the duplicate unless you pass a merge function. Both have null restrictions: `groupingBy` rejects a null classifier result and `toMap` rejects null values."}],"extra":["**Floating point is not associative:** summing `double` values with `reduce` in parallel can give slightly different results from run to run; `DoubleStream.sum()` may use compensated summation, and money should be `BigDecimal` or long minor units anyway.","**Three-argument reduce:** it lets the result type differ from the element type, but a `map` followed by the two-argument `reduce` is usually clearer and just as fast; if you need a container, you want `collect`.","**Downstream collectors:** inside `groupingBy`, `reducing`, `summingLong`, `summarizingLong` and `mapping` compute per-key aggregates in the same pass instead of grouping first and iterating again.","**toList flavors:** `Stream.toList()` (Java 16) returns an unmodifiable list, `Collectors.toList()` makes no mutability guarantee, and `Collectors.toCollection(ArrayList::new)` is the explicit way to get a mutable one.","**groupingByConcurrent:** in parallel streams, merging many per-segment `HashMap`s is expensive; `groupingByConcurrent` accumulates into one `ConcurrentHashMap` when you do not need encounter order."],"redFlags":["uses a non-associative operation such as subtraction in a parallel `reduce`","picks a starting value like 10 for a sum and is surprised when parallel results differ","concatenates strings with `reduce` instead of `Collectors.joining`"]},{"id":40,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"A nightly job building a `Map` with `Collectors.toMap` suddenly fails with `IllegalStateException`. What happened, and what else can bite you?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`Collectors.toMap` throws on duplicate keys unless you pass a merge function, throws on null values, and returns an unspecified map type (a `HashMap` today) with no ordering guarantee, so choose the merge policy and map supplier deliberately.","answer":["The two-argument `toMap(keyMapper, valueMapper)` assumes keys are unique; the first duplicate throws `IllegalStateException` with a duplicate key message (since Java 9 it names both conflicting values). That is a classic production incident: the code works on test data, then a customer with two active addresses or a feed with a repeated SKU crashes a batch job. The three-argument version takes a merge function, and the policy (keep first, keep newest, sum, or fail with a meaningful domain error) is a business decision, not a way to silence the exception.","Null values throw `NullPointerException` in every `toMap` variant, because the implementation rejects them the way `Map.merge` does, so filter first or map missing values to an explicit default. Null keys depend on the target map: `HashMap` accepts one, while `TreeMap` and `ConcurrentHashMap` throw. `toUnmodifiableMap` (Java 10) rejects null keys, null values and duplicates.","The returned map's type, mutability and iteration order are unspecified. In practice you get a `HashMap`, so iteration order is effectively arbitrary and can change as data grows, which surfaces as flaky tests, reshuffled JSON fields and nondeterministic reports. Use the four-argument overload with `LinkedHashMap::new` for insertion order, `TreeMap::new` for sorted keys, or an `EnumMap` supplier for enum keys. And if duplicates are legitimate, you probably wanted `groupingBy`, not `toMap`."],"keyPoints":["a second value for one key aborts collection unless you supply a merge rule","a null value throws `NullPointerException`","the default result is an unspecified mutable map with no order","the four-argument overload picks both the merge rule and the map type"],"examples":[{"title":"Duplicate keys: crash, silent overwrite, deliberate policy","lang":"java","code":"record PriceUpdate(String sku, BigDecimal price, Instant updatedAt) { }\n\nList<PriceUpdate> feed = priceFeed.fetch(); // may contain the same SKU twice\n\n// BAD: throws IllegalStateException on the first repeated SKU\nMap<String, BigDecimal> crashes = feed.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(PriceUpdate::sku, PriceUpdate::price));\n\n// BAD: silently keeps whichever came last in the feed, with no business reason\nMap<String, BigDecimal> lastWins = feed.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(PriceUpdate::sku, PriceUpdate::price, (a, b) -> b));\n\n// GOOD: the newest update wins, first-seen key order preserved for the report\nMap<String, PriceUpdate> latestBySku = feed.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(\n                PriceUpdate::sku,\n                Function.identity(),\n                BinaryOperator.maxBy(Comparator.comparing(PriceUpdate::updatedAt)),\n                LinkedHashMap::new));\n\n// GOOD: duplicates are a data error here, so fail with business context\nMap<String, PriceUpdate> strict = feed.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(PriceUpdate::sku, Function.identity(), (a, b) -> {\n            throw new DuplicateSkuException(a.sku(), a.updatedAt(), b.updatedAt());\n        }));","note":"Both GOOD versions make the duplicate policy explicit and reviewable; which one is right depends on whether duplicates are expected in the feed or indicate an upstream bug."},{"title":"Null values, map types and legitimate duplicates","lang":"java","code":"record Customer(String id, String email, String segment) { }\n\n// BAD: NullPointerException as soon as one customer has no email\nMap<String, String> emailById = customers.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(Customer::id, Customer::email));\n\n// GOOD: decide explicitly what a missing email means\nMap<String, String> knownEmails = customers.stream()\n        .filter(c -> c.email() != null)\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(Customer::id, Customer::email));\n\n// GOOD (Java 10+): unmodifiable, rejects null keys, null values and duplicates\nMap<String, Customer> byId = customers.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableMap(Customer::id, Function.identity()));\n\n// GOOD: many customers per segment is a grouping, not a map\nMap<String, List<Customer>> bySegment = customers.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Customer::segment, TreeMap::new, Collectors.toList()));","note":"The null value failure surprises people because `HashMap` itself accepts null values; the collector is stricter than the map it builds."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Two price updates arrive for one SKU. How should the merge function pick the winner?","a":"Compare on data, not stream position: `BinaryOperator.maxBy(Comparator.comparing(PriceUpdate::updatedAt))` keeps the latest update by its timestamp. `(a, b) -> b` only keeps whichever arrived later in the stream, which may not match time order and means nothing in an unordered or parallel stream. Add a tie-breaker such as a sequence number if timestamps can collide."},{"q":"A report must list customers in the order they were loaded. How do you get that from `toMap`?","a":"Use the four-argument overload with a map supplier: `toMap(keyFn, valueFn, mergeFn, LinkedHashMap::new)`, where the merge function is mandatory and should reflect a real policy. The source must be ordered too, such as a `List` rather than a `HashSet`, because the map can only keep the order it receives. Pass `TreeMap::new` instead if you need keys sorted rather than in load order."}],"extra":["**Measure merges:** when a merge function resolves duplicates, count them with a metric (for example a Micrometer counter) so upstream data quality problems stay visible instead of being silently absorbed.","**Parallel streams:** `toMap` in a parallel stream merges per-thread maps, which is expensive for large maps; `toConcurrentMap` accumulates into one `ConcurrentHashMap` but gives up encounter order, so its merge function must not depend on which element came first.","**Java 21 SequencedMap:** a `LinkedHashMap` result is now a `SequencedMap`, so `firstEntry()`, `lastEntry()` and `reversed()` are available directly when the order carries meaning.","**Index-by-id pattern:** `toMap(Entity::getId, Function.identity())` after a batch `findAllById` is the standard fix for N+1 lookups; ids are unique there, so the two-argument form is right and its exception acts as a useful assertion.","**Mutability is unspecified:** do not rely on being able to `put` into the result of the default `toMap`; if you need a mutable map, pass `HashMap::new` explicitly so the contract lives in the code."],"redFlags":["merges with `(a, b) -> b` and calls it keeping the newest without checking timestamps","assumes the returned map preserves the order of the source list","expects `toMap` to accept a null value the way `HashMap.put` does"]},{"id":41,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"Code inside `map` and `forEach` writes to shared lists and calls services. Why is that fragile, and what would you do instead?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Stream lambdas should be pure: side effects in `map`, `filter` or `forEach` make behavior depend on laziness, short-circuiting and parallel scheduling, cause races on shared state and hide intent; compute results first, then apply side effects explicitly.","answer":["The stream API requires behavioral parameters to be non-interfering and, ideally, stateless. A `map` or `filter` that writes to a field, list, counter or database is no longer a function of its input: whether and how often it runs depends on laziness, short-circuiting (`findFirst` stops early), optimizations (`count()` may skip the pipeline) and, in parallel streams, on which thread handles which element in what order. The code loses referential transparency, so you can no longer read or test the pipeline as a pure transformation.","Under parallel execution, side effects on shared non-thread-safe state are data races: `ArrayList.add` from several threads loses elements, stores nulls or throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`, and a plain counter under-counts. A synchronized collection fixes the crash but serializes the work and still yields a nondeterministic order. `forEach` itself gives no ordering guarantee in parallel, even for an ordered source such as a `List`.","The pattern: transform with pure functions, let a collector build the result, then perform side effects explicitly, either in a terminal `forEach` whose only job is the effect (publishing events, writing rows) or in a plain `for` loop when the logic needs checked exceptions, early exit or readable stack traces. `peek` exists for debugging, not business logic."],"keyPoints":["laziness and short-circuiting decide whether a lambda runs at all","parallel scheduling turns writes to shared state into races","a pure pipeline computes a result that is then acted on in one visible place"],"examples":[{"title":"Collect results instead of mutating shared state","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: side effects inside the pipeline, broken under parallel execution\nList<String> rejected = new ArrayList<>();\nAtomicInteger approvedCount = new AtomicInteger();\napplications.parallelStream()\n        .filter(app -> {\n            boolean ok = scoring.approve(app);\n            if (!ok) rejected.add(app.id());      // race: ArrayList is not thread-safe\n            else approvedCount.incrementAndGet();\n            return ok;\n        })\n        .forEach(notifier::sendApproval);          // order not guaranteed\n\n// GOOD: pure classification, one collector, explicit side effect at the end\nMap<Boolean, List<CreditApplication>> byOutcome = applications.parallelStream()\n        .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(scoring::approve));\n\nList<String> rejectedIds = byOutcome.get(false).stream()\n        .map(CreditApplication::id)\n        .toList();\nint approved = byOutcome.get(true).size();\nbyOutcome.get(true).forEach(notifier::sendApproval); // deliberate, sequential effect","note":"The GOOD version is deterministic in content, safe in parallel, and makes the only real side effect, sending notifications, visible as its own step."},{"title":"Collecting validation errors safely","lang":"java","code":"record ValidationError(String rowId, String field, String message) { }\n\nclass PaymentFileValidator {\n\n    List<ValidationError> validate(PaymentRow row) {\n        List<ValidationError> errors = new ArrayList<>();   // local, confined to this call\n        if (row.amount() == null || row.amount().signum() <= 0) {\n            errors.add(new ValidationError(row.id(), \"amount\", \"must be positive\"));\n        }\n        if (row.iban() == null || row.iban().isBlank()) {\n            errors.add(new ValidationError(row.id(), \"iban\", \"is required\"));\n        }\n        return errors;\n    }\n\n    Map<String, List<ValidationError>> errorsByField(List<PaymentRow> rows) {\n        return rows.parallelStream()\n                .flatMap(row -> validate(row).stream())     // pure: each row maps to its errors\n                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(ValidationError::field));\n    }\n}","note":"Mutating a list that is created and returned inside one call is fine; the problem is only state shared between elements or threads, and here there is none."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Your pipeline must return results and also send one audit event per item. How do you structure it?","a":"Compute first, act second: collect the results with a pure pipeline, then loop over the collected list to send events, or publish them in one batch. That keeps I/O out of the stream, so short-circuiting, retries and parallelism cannot skip or duplicate an event. If the events must be reliable, write them to an outbox table in the same transaction as the results."},{"q":"Why might `forEach` print a sorted list out of order, and how do you control that?","a":"On a parallel stream `forEach` handles elements in whatever order worker threads reach them, even when the source is an ordered `List`. `forEachOrdered` respects encounter order but serializes that final step and gives up much of the speedup. If order matters, collect first and iterate the result, or keep the stream sequential."}],"extra":["**Side effects may not run at all:** since Java 9, `list.stream().peek(log::info).count()` can skip `peek` entirely because the size is known, and short-circuiting means `map` may run for only part of the data, so business logic hidden in those lambdas is unreliable.","**Hidden database calls:** touching a lazy JPA association inside `map` issues queries as a side effect; in a parallel stream those queries run on pool threads sharing a non-thread-safe Hibernate `Session`, which is undefined behavior.","**Stateful lambdas:** deduplicating with `filter(seen::add)` on a shared `HashSet` is both racy and order dependent; use `distinct()` or a collector keyed by id instead.","**Partial failure:** a side effect that throws halfway through a pipeline leaves some rows written and others not, with no clear boundary, whereas collecting first lets you validate everything and then apply effects in one transaction.","**Concurrent collectors when needed:** if you genuinely need to accumulate from parallel threads, use `Collectors.toConcurrentMap` or `groupingByConcurrent`, which are designed for it, rather than hand-rolled synchronized collections."],"redFlags":["assumes `map` runs for every element even when `findFirst` follows","fixes a race by switching to `Collections.synchronizedList` instead of collecting","puts business logic in `peek`"]},{"id":42,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"A teammate adds `.parallelStream()` to a REST handler that calls a pricing service per item. What could go wrong?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"`parallelStream` borrows workers from the single `ForkJoinPool` common pool that the whole JVM shares, so blocking calls, tiny or poorly splittable inputs, order constraints, costly merges and writes to shared state can each slow it down or produce wrong results; measure CPU-heavy work before adopting it.","answer":["Parallel streams split the source with its `Spliterator` and run the pieces on `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()`, which is shared by every parallel stream in the JVM and by `CompletableFuture` async methods called without an executor. Its parallelism defaults to the number of available processors minus one, with the calling thread helping. In a server, several requests running parallel streams at once compete for those few workers, so you get contention and latency spikes instead of speedup, and there is no isolation between features.","Performance suffers when per-element work is tiny or the dataset is small (splitting and task overhead dominate), when the source splits badly (`LinkedList`, `Stream.iterate`, I/O-backed streams), when ordering must be preserved (`forEachOrdered`, `limit`, `findFirst`, `sorted` on ordered streams), when merging is costly (combining many per-thread `HashMap`s in `groupingBy`), or when boxing dominates. Blocking I/O is the worst case: an HTTP or JDBC call inside the stream parks a common-pool worker and starves unrelated work across the whole JVM.","Correctness breaks with shared mutable state (adding to an `ArrayList` from `forEach`), non-associative reductions, code that relies on encounter order in `forEach`, and anything based on thread-locals: MDC trace ids, Spring's `SecurityContextHolder` and the transaction-bound `EntityManager` do not follow work onto pool threads. Use parallel streams only for large, CPU-bound, stateless computations, benchmark first with JMH under realistic concurrent load, and for I/O fan-out prefer virtual threads or a dedicated, bounded executor."],"keyPoints":["one shared pool sized to the cores serves every parallel stream in the JVM","splitting and merging cost more than they save on small inputs","a worker parked on I/O is a core no other stream can use","unsynchronized writes from worker threads lose or corrupt data"],"examples":[{"title":"Blocking fan-out: parallelStream versus virtual threads","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: blocking HTTP calls on the JVM-wide common pool, inside a request thread\npublic List<Quote> quotesBad(List<String> skus) {\n    return skus.parallelStream()\n            .map(pricingClient::fetchQuote)   // parks a common pool worker per call\n            .toList();\n}\n\n// GOOD: virtual threads for blocking I/O, bounded to protect the pricing service\nprivate final Semaphore pricingPermits = new Semaphore(20);\n\npublic List<Quote> quotes(List<String> skus) throws InterruptedException {\n    try (ExecutorService executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n        List<Future<Quote>> futures = skus.stream()\n                .map(sku -> executor.submit(() -> fetchWithPermit(sku)))\n                .toList();\n        List<Quote> result = new ArrayList<>(futures.size());\n        for (Future<Quote> f : futures) {\n            try {\n                result.add(f.get());\n            } catch (ExecutionException e) {\n                throw new PricingUnavailableException(\"Quote lookup failed\", e.getCause());\n            }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n}\n\nprivate Quote fetchWithPermit(String sku) throws InterruptedException {\n    pricingPermits.acquire();\n    try {\n        return pricingClient.fetchQuote(sku);\n    } finally {\n        pricingPermits.release();\n    }\n}","note":"The BAD version competes with every other parallel stream and default `CompletableFuture` in the JVM and gets at most a few workers in a small container. The GOOD version gives each call its own cheap virtual thread and makes the downstream concurrency limit explicit."},{"title":"When a parallel stream helps and when it hurts","lang":"text","code":"Scenario                                       Verdict\nSumming 50M longs from an array                Likely faster: large, CPU-bound, splits evenly\nHashing 10k JSON payloads in a batch job       Maybe: measure with JMH first\nMapping 200 DTOs in a request handler          No: overhead exceeds the work\nCalling a REST API or database per element     No: blocks common pool workers\nReading lines from a socket via BufferedReader No: splits poorly, I/O bound\ngroupingBy into HashMap on 1M elements         Often slower: map merging cost\nforEach adding to a shared ArrayList           Wrong: data race, lost elements\nCode relying on MDC or SecurityContextHolder   Wrong: thread-locals not propagated","note":"Only the first row is a clear win; parallel streams are a tool for bulk CPU work in batch jobs, not a default for request-path code."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does a `LinkedList` or `Stream.iterate` source parallelize poorly compared with an array?","a":"Parallel streams divide work by repeatedly splitting the source's `Spliterator`, and arrays or `ArrayList` split in O(1) into balanced halves of known size. A `LinkedList` must be walked to split, and `Stream.iterate` or an I/O source has unknown size and splits into small batches, so threads spend more time splitting and waiting than computing. `IntStream.range`, arrays and `ArrayList` are the sources that parallelize well."},{"q":"How can slow HTTP calls inside one parallel stream hurt unrelated endpoints?","a":"Each blocked call parks a common-pool worker, and the pool has roughly one worker per core, so a few slow calls can occupy all of them. Every other parallel stream and every `CompletableFuture` using the default async executor then queues behind those calls, and unrelated requests slow down or time out. Run I/O fan-out on virtual threads or a dedicated bounded executor, and cap concurrency toward the downstream service."}],"extra":["**CompletableFuture shares the pool:** `supplyAsync` without an executor uses the same common pool, so parallel streams and async code interfere; and when the common pool parallelism is below 2 (for example a container with 1 or 2 CPUs), `CompletableFuture` falls back to creating a new thread for every task.","**Container CPU limits:** the JVM derives `availableProcessors()` from the container's CPU quota, so a pod with a 2 CPU limit gets a common pool with a single worker; code that looked fast on a 16-core laptop gains nothing in Kubernetes.","**No compensation for blocking:** the common pool only adds threads for blocked workers when the blocking code goes through `ForkJoinPool.managedBlock`, which ordinary JDBC and HTTP clients do not, so blocked workers simply reduce parallelism.","**unordered() can help:** calling `.unordered()` on a parallel pipeline lets operations like `limit` and `distinct` skip encounter-order bookkeeping, which can make them much cheaper when order does not matter.","**Custom pool trick:** running the stream inside `customPool.submit(() -> list.parallelStream()...).get()` makes it use that pool, but this relies on implementation behavior rather than a documented contract, so treat it as a last resort."],"redFlags":["collects into a shared `ArrayList` from `forEach` in a parallel stream","expects `forEach` on a parallel stream to keep list order","uses a `LinkedList` or `Stream.iterate` source and expects a good speedup"]},{"id":43,"category":"Concurrency","question":"Runnable vs Callable, start() vs run(): how is a thread created and what states does it go through?","level":"Basic","tldr":"A `Runnable` returns nothing and cannot throw checked exceptions; a `Callable<V>` returns a value and may throw, so it is submitted to an executor and read through a `Future`. `start()` makes the JVM create a thread that calls `run()`, while calling `run()` yourself is a plain method call on the current thread. A thread then moves through `NEW`, `RUNNABLE`, `BLOCKED`, `WAITING`, `TIMED_WAITING` and `TERMINATED`.","answer":["A thread is the execution mechanism; a task is the work. You pass a `Runnable` to `new Thread(task)` or the Java 21 builders (`Thread.ofPlatform()`, `Thread.ofVirtual()`), or, in service code, submit it to an `ExecutorService`. `Runnable.run()` is `void` and cannot throw checked exceptions; `Callable.call()` returns `V` and declares `throws Exception`. `submit(Callable)` returns a `Future<V>`, `execute(Runnable)` returns nothing. Subclassing `Thread` welds the task to one execution strategy, so implement the task interfaces instead.","`start()` creates the thread (or schedules a virtual thread on a carrier), which then invokes `run()`; `start()` returns immediately. Calling `run()` directly executes the body on the caller's thread without error, which is why the bug survives review. A thread starts only once: a second `start()` throws `IllegalThreadStateException`. Exceptions differ by entry point: with a raw thread or `execute`, an uncaught exception ends the thread and reaches its `UncaughtExceptionHandler`; with `submit`, it is stored in the `Future` and only appears, wrapped in `ExecutionException`, when someone calls `get()`.","`Thread.State` has six values: `NEW`; `RUNNABLE` (running or ready, which includes a platform thread blocked in native socket I/O); `BLOCKED` (waiting to enter a `synchronized` monitor); `WAITING` (`wait()`, `join()` or `LockSupport.park()` without a timeout, which covers `ReentrantLock` contention and an idle `BlockingQueue.take()`); `TIMED_WAITING` (the same with a timeout, plus `sleep`); and `TERMINATED`. This is what makes thread dumps readable: many threads `BLOCKED` on one monitor means lock contention, pool workers `WAITING` in `take()` are idle, and `RUNNABLE` threads in socket reads are waiting on a slow downstream, not burning CPU."],"keyPoints":["Callable returns a value and may throw checked exceptions","start() creates a thread; run() is a plain method call","states: NEW, RUNNABLE, BLOCKED, WAITING, TIMED_WAITING, TERMINATED"],"examples":[{"title":"run() vs start(), and where a task's exception goes","lang":"java","code":"Runnable auditTask = () ->\n        System.out.println(\"audit on \" + Thread.currentThread().getName());\n\nThread worker = new Thread(auditTask, \"audit-worker\");\nworker.run();    // BAD: prints \"audit on main\", no new thread\nworker.start();  // GOOD: prints \"audit on audit-worker\"\nworker.join();\n// worker.start(); // IllegalThreadStateException: a thread starts only once\n\nCallable<BigDecimal> totalTask = () -> orderService.loadTotal(\"order-42\");\n\ntry (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4)) {\n    Future<BigDecimal> total = pool.submit(totalTask);\n    System.out.println(\"total = \" + total.get(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS));\n\n    Future<?> invoice = pool.submit(() -> invoiceService.publish(\"order-42\"));\n    // If publish throws, nothing is logged: the exception waits inside the Future\n    try {\n        invoice.get();\n    } catch (ExecutionException e) {\n        log.error(\"invoice publishing failed\", e.getCause());\n    }\n}","note":"The `run()` mistake is only visible in the thread name. Without the `invoice.get()` call the failure would vanish; with `execute` instead of `submit` it would at least reach the `UncaughtExceptionHandler`."},{"title":"Producing the thread states on purpose","lang":"java","code":"Object ledgerLock = new Object();\n\nThread holder = new Thread(() -> {\n    synchronized (ledgerLock) {\n        sleepQuietly(500);  // TIMED_WAITING while holding the monitor\n    }\n}, \"ledger-holder\");\nThread contender = new Thread(() -> {\n    synchronized (ledgerLock) { }  // BLOCKED until holder releases it\n}, \"ledger-contender\");\nThread reporter = new Thread(() -> joinQuietly(holder), \"report-joiner\"); // WAITING in join()\n\nSystem.out.println(holder.getState());  // NEW\nholder.start();\nThread.sleep(50);\ncontender.start();\nreporter.start();\nThread.sleep(50);\n\nSystem.out.println(holder.getState());     // TIMED_WAITING\nSystem.out.println(contender.getState());  // BLOCKED\nSystem.out.println(reporter.getState());   // WAITING\n\nreporter.join();\ncontender.join();\nSystem.out.println(holder.getState());  // TERMINATED","note":"The sleeps make this timing dependent, so it is a demonstration, not a test. In a `jstack` dump, `BLOCKED` threads show `waiting to lock` and threads parked on `java.util.concurrent` locks show `parking to wait for`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens to an exception thrown inside a pooled task?","a":"With `execute`, it terminates that worker, goes to the `UncaughtExceptionHandler` (by default a stack trace on stderr) and the pool replaces the worker. With `submit`, it is captured in the `Future` and rethrown as `ExecutionException` from `get()`; if nobody calls `get()`, it disappears. Log inside the task, always consume the future, or use `CompletableFuture` with explicit error handling."},{"q":"Is a thread waiting for a ReentrantLock in the BLOCKED state?","a":"No. `BLOCKED` is only for monitor entry: `synchronized`, or reacquiring a monitor after `wait()`. Threads contending on `ReentrantLock` or waiting on a latch or queue park through `LockSupport` and show as `WAITING` or `TIMED_WAITING` with a `parking to wait for` line naming the synchronizer, so look for both patterns when hunting contention."}],"extra":["**Daemon threads:** the JVM exits when only daemon threads remain, without running their `finally` blocks; work that must complete belongs on non-daemon threads with orderly shutdown. `setDaemon` must be called before `start()`.","**Interruption is cooperative:** `interrupt()` sets a flag and wakes `sleep`, `wait` and `join` with `InterruptedException`; the task must check it and stop. `Future.cancel(true)` and `shutdownNow()` rely on this.","**Name your threads:** a `ThreadFactory` or builder that sets names like `order-consumer-3` makes dumps and logs actionable.","**RUNNABLE is not busy:** correlate dump states with per-thread CPU (`top -H` and the thread id in the dump) before concluding a service is CPU bound.","**Raw threads in services:** a `new Thread` per request gives no limit, reuse or shutdown handling; use an executor, or a virtual-thread-per-task executor for blocking I/O."],"redFlags":["Believes calling run() starts a new thread","Does not know submit() hides exceptions inside the Future","Creates raw threads per request in service code"]},{"id":44,"category":"Concurrency","question":"One service returns wrong totals, another freezes with idle CPU. Which is a race, which a deadlock, and what prevents each?","level":"Basic","tldr":"A race condition is wrong results from an unlucky interleaving, a deadlock is a cycle of threads waiting on each other; prevent races with atomicity and immutability, deadlocks with lock ordering, timeouts and small lock scopes.","answer":["A **race condition** means the result depends on thread timing: check-then-act (`if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k, v)`), read-modify-write (`count++`), or reading a half-built object. Each call may even be thread-safe on its own; the bug is that the compound action is not atomic. A **data race** is the narrower JMM term: two unsynchronized accesses to the same variable, at least one a write. Races corrupt data silently: double charges, lost updates, oversold stock.","A **deadlock** is a cycle in the wait-for graph: thread A holds lock 1 and waits for lock 2, while B holds 2 and waits for 1. It needs four conditions at once: mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption and circular wait. The symptom is the opposite of a race: nothing happens, threads sit BLOCKED and throughput drops to zero.","Prevent races by making compound actions atomic (`ConcurrentHashMap.compute`, `AtomicLong.compareAndSet`, a lock, a unique constraint or `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`), by preferring immutable objects, and by confining mutable state to one thread. Prevent deadlocks by breaking the cycle: acquire locks in one global order (for example by account id), use `tryLock` with a timeout and backoff, keep lock scopes small, and never do I/O or call foreign code while holding a lock. Diagnose with thread dumps and `ThreadMXBean.findDeadlockedThreads()`. Adding `Thread.sleep` or more locks fixes neither."],"keyPoints":["a race corrupts results silently because steps from two threads interleave","a deadlock stops progress because each thread holds what another needs","atomic operations, confinement and immutable data remove the interleaving","a single global lock order or timed acquisition breaks the waiting cycle"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: transfer deadlock fixed by lock ordering","lang":"java","code":"public class TransferService {\n\n    // BAD: T1 moves A to B while T2 moves B to A; each holds one monitor and waits for the other\n    public void transferBad(Account from, Account to, long cents) {\n        synchronized (from) {\n            synchronized (to) {\n                from.debit(cents);\n                to.credit(cents);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: always lock in a global order (account id), so no cycle can form\n    public void transfer(Account from, Account to, long cents) {\n        Account first = from.id() < to.id() ? from : to;\n        Account second = first == from ? to : from;\n        synchronized (first) {\n            synchronized (second) {\n                from.debit(cents);\n                to.credit(cents);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The same rule applies in PostgreSQL: lock rows in a consistent order (for example `SELECT ... ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE`), otherwise the database detects the cycle and aborts one transaction with SQLSTATE `40P01`."},{"title":"What the deadlock looks like in a thread dump","lang":"text","code":"$ jcmd 41872 Thread.print\n...\nFound one Java-level deadlock:\n=============================\n\"transfer-worker-1\":\n  waiting to lock monitor 0x00007f2a6c003f80 (object 0x0000000712a4b6e8, a com.acme.bank.Account),\n  which is held by \"transfer-worker-2\"\n\n\"transfer-worker-2\":\n  waiting to lock monitor 0x00007f2a6c006a10 (object 0x0000000712a4b700, a com.acme.bank.Account),\n  which is held by \"transfer-worker-1\"\n\nJava stack information for the threads listed above:\n===================================================\n\"transfer-worker-1\":\n        at com.acme.bank.TransferService.transferBad(TransferService.java:7)\n        - waiting to lock <0x0000000712a4b6e8> (a com.acme.bank.Account)\n        - locked <0x0000000712a4b700> (a com.acme.bank.Account)\n\"transfer-worker-2\":\n        at com.acme.bank.TransferService.transferBad(TransferService.java:7)\n        - waiting to lock <0x0000000712a4b700> (a com.acme.bank.Account)\n        - locked <0x0000000712a4b6e8> (a com.acme.bank.Account)\n\nFound 1 deadlock.","note":"Read it as a wait-for graph: worker 1 waits for the object worker 2 locked and vice versa. The JVM reports cycles over monitors and `java.util.concurrent` locks, but not pool starvation or database waits."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Two transfers lock accounts A then B and B then A. How do you make that deadlock impossible?","a":"Impose one global order: always lock the account with the smaller id first, whatever the transfer direction, so no thread can hold the second lock while waiting for the first. If the ordering key can tie, as with `System.identityHashCode`, take an extra tie-breaking lock first. Alternatively take the first lock and `tryLock` the second with a timeout, releasing both and retrying with jittered backoff on failure."},{"q":"Threads look busy in every thread dump but no work finishes. What would you suspect?","a":"A livelock: threads stay RUNNABLE and keep reacting to each other, for example both releasing and retrying `tryLock` in lockstep, but nobody makes progress. Unlike a deadlock, the JVM will not report it, so compare several dumps taken a few seconds apart and look for the same retry loop. Randomized backoff with jitter breaks the symmetry."}],"extra":["**Thread starvation deadlock:** a task in a bounded pool that submits a subtask to the same pool and blocks on `get()` can hang with no locks at all once every thread is waiting; the JVM deadlock detector will not report it.","**Connection pool deadlock:** a thread holding one Hikari connection that opens a `REQUIRES_NEW` transaction needs a second one; under load every connection is held by a thread waiting for another, visible as threads parked in `HikariPool.getConnection`.","**Database deadlocks:** PostgreSQL checks for lock cycles after `deadlock_timeout` (1s by default) and aborts one transaction; the application should retry the whole transaction, and consistent row ordering removes most occurrences.","**Races across instances:** with several pods, in-JVM locks do nothing; use a unique constraint, optimistic locking with `@Version`, `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` or an idempotency key table.","**Testing races:** they rarely show in unit tests; use stress tests with many iterations and threads, `jcstress` for memory-model level behavior, and review specifically for check-then-act patterns."],"redFlags":["claims a race is fixed by marking the shared field `volatile`","says a deadlocked service shows high CPU","restarts the service to clear a deadlock and never looks for the lock cycle"]},{"id":45,"category":"Concurrency","question":"A worker never sees another thread set its `stop` flag. Does `volatile` fix that, and where does it stop helping?","level":"Basic","tldr":"A `volatile` field guarantees that a write is visible to, and ordered before, any later read of it (happens-before), yet it makes no read-modify-write atomic and excludes no one, so it fits stop flags and values with a single writing thread.","answer":["`volatile` is a Java Memory Model tool, not a lock. A write to a volatile field **happens-before** every subsequent read of that same field, so the reader sees the new value and everything the writer did before writing it. It also restricts reordering: ordinary writes before a volatile write cannot move after it, and ordinary reads after a volatile read cannot move before it. Without it the JIT may keep a field in a register or hoist the read out of a loop, and a thread can spin forever on a stale value. Volatile `long` and `double` accesses are also atomic, which plain 64-bit fields are not guaranteed to be.","What it does not give you is atomicity of compound actions. `count++` is read, add, write: two threads can both read 41 and both write 42, losing an update even though every single access is volatile. There is no mutual exclusion either, so it cannot protect an invariant spanning several fields, such as `balance` and `lastUpdated` changing together.","The sweet spot is a status flag (`running`, `shutdownRequested`), a single writer publishing a value many threads read (a config snapshot reference, a leader flag), and the field in double-checked locking. For counters use `AtomicLong` or `LongAdder`; for multi-field invariants use a lock or an immutable snapshot swapped through one volatile reference. Calling volatile a \"lightweight lock\" is the classic red flag."],"keyPoints":["every read sees the latest write instead of a cached or reordered value","writes made before a volatile write become visible to a thread that later reads it","read, increment and write steps can still interleave and lose updates","safe when one thread writes and the others only read"],"examples":[{"title":"Lost updates with a volatile counter, and the fix","lang":"java","code":"public class PaymentStats {\n\n    // BAD: volatile makes the value visible, not the increment atomic\n    private volatile long processedBad;\n\n    public void onPaymentBad() {\n        processedBad++;              // read, add, write: concurrent increments are lost\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: one atomic read-modify-write\n    private final AtomicLong processed = new AtomicLong();\n\n    public void onPayment() {\n        processed.incrementAndGet();\n    }\n\n    public long processed() {\n        return processed.get();\n    }\n}","note":"Run the BAD version from 16 threads doing a million increments each and the total comes up short. `incrementAndGet` is a single atomic operation that HotSpot compiles to one locked CPU instruction on x86."},{"title":"Correct shutdown flag for a worker loop","lang":"java","code":"public class OutboxRelay implements Runnable {\n\n    private volatile boolean running = true;   // one writer (stop), one reader (the loop)\n    private final OutboxRepository outbox;\n    private final EventPublisher publisher;\n\n    public OutboxRelay(OutboxRepository outbox, EventPublisher publisher) {\n        this.outbox = outbox;\n        this.publisher = publisher;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void run() {\n        while (running && !Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {\n            List<OutboxEvent> batch = outbox.fetchUnpublished(100);\n            batch.forEach(publisher::publish);\n            if (batch.isEmpty()) {\n                LockSupport.parkNanos(Duration.ofMillis(200).toNanos());\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void stop() {\n        running = false;   // seen by the relay thread on its next loop check\n    }\n}","note":"Without `volatile` the JIT may hoist the `running` read out of the loop and the thread never stops. A flag is only checked between iterations, so for loops that block also interrupt the thread."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why can a loop on a plain `boolean` field spin forever even after another thread changes it?","a":"Without a happens-before edge, the JIT may treat the field as loop-invariant, read it once and hoist it out of the loop, effectively turning `while (!stop)` into `while (true)`. Nothing in the memory model forces the reading thread to ever observe the other thread's write. Declaring the field `volatile`, or reading it under a lock or through an `AtomicBoolean`, rules out that optimization."},{"q":"Two threads call `hits++` on a `volatile int`. Why do counts still go missing, and what would you use?","a":"`hits++` is a read, an add and a write, and two threads can both read 41 and both write 42, losing one increment. `volatile` makes each individual read and write visible but does nothing to stop another thread acting between them. Use `AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet()`, a `LongAdder` for hot counters, or a `synchronized` block."}],"extra":["**Arrays and references:** a `volatile` array or object reference only makes the reference volatile; writes to elements or fields are plain. Use `AtomicLongArray`, `AtomicReference` or a `VarHandle` for per-element semantics.","**Piggybacking publication:** because a volatile write publishes all earlier writes, assigning a fully built immutable object to a volatile field is a safe way to hot-reload configuration or routing tables.","**VarHandle access modes:** since Java 9, `VarHandle` offers weaker modes such as `setRelease`/`getAcquire` and `getOpaque`; they are for library authors, and application code should stay with volatile or the atomic classes.","**Double-checked locking:** the lazily initialized field must be volatile, otherwise another thread can see a non-null reference to a partially constructed object; the holder class idiom avoids the question entirely.","**Cost model:** on x86 a volatile read is about as cheap as a normal load, while a volatile write needs a store-load fence; heavy write contention on the same cache line is what actually hurts."],"redFlags":["thinks that without `volatile` the other thread will always see the change eventually","puts `volatile` on a reference and assumes the object's fields become thread-safe"]},{"id":46,"category":"Concurrency","question":"A team replaced every `synchronized` block with `ReentrantLock` to gain speed. Was that worthwhile, and when is the explicit lock justified?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Both give reentrant mutual exclusion with the same memory visibility; use `synchronized` by default for simplicity and automatic release, and `ReentrantLock` when you need tryLock, timeouts, interruptible waits, fairness or several conditions.","answer":["Both are reentrant mutexes with the same memory semantics: releasing the lock happens-before the next acquisition, so state written inside the critical section is visible to the next owner. `synchronized` is block-structured: the monitor is released automatically when the block exits, including on exceptions, and the JIT can optimize it (lock elision through escape analysis, lock coarsening). Thread dumps show monitor owners directly.","`ReentrantLock` adds capabilities: `tryLock()` and `tryLock(timeout)` for bounded waiting, `lockInterruptibly()` so a waiting thread can be cancelled, an optional fairness policy, several `Condition` objects per lock (separate `notFull` and `notEmpty` queues in a buffer), and introspection such as `getQueueLength()`. The price is manual release: `unlock()` must sit in a `finally` block, or one exception leaves the lock held forever and every later caller hangs.","A sensible default is `synchronized` for short critical sections and `ReentrantLock` when you need one of those features. Java 21 adds a reason: a virtual thread that blocks inside `synchronized` pins its carrier thread, while `ReentrantLock` lets it unmount; JDK 24 (JEP 491) removed that limitation. `synchronized` is not obsolete, and neither lock rescues a design that holds locks across I/O. For read-heavy data consider `ReentrantReadWriteLock` or `StampedLock`."],"keyPoints":["both give exclusive, reentrant access and the same happens-before guarantees","the intrinsic monitor releases itself even when an exception escapes","an explicit lock earns its place with timed, polled or interruptible acquisition, or several conditions","raw speed is rarely a reason to switch on modern JVMs"],"examples":[{"title":"Forgetting finally: the lock leak","lang":"java","code":"public class AccountLedger {\n\n    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();\n    private long balanceCents;\n\n    // BAD: if applyFee throws, the lock is never released and every later caller hangs\n    public void debitBad(long amountCents) {\n        lock.lock();\n        balanceCents -= amountCents;\n        applyFee();\n        lock.unlock();\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: lock() just before try, unlock() in finally\n    public void debit(long amountCents) {\n        lock.lock();\n        try {\n            balanceCents -= amountCents;\n            applyFee();\n        } finally {\n            lock.unlock();\n        }\n    }\n\n    private void applyFee() {\n        // may throw, for example when the fee schedule is missing\n    }\n}","note":"Call `lock()` outside the `try`: if it were inside and failed, the `finally` would call `unlock()` on a lock you do not hold and throw `IllegalMonitorStateException`. With `synchronized` this whole class of bug cannot happen."},{"title":"tryLock to coalesce a cache refresh","lang":"java","code":"public class FxRateCache {\n\n    private final ReentrantLock refreshLock = new ReentrantLock();\n    private final FxRateClient client;\n    private volatile Map<String, BigDecimal> rates = Map.of();\n\n    public FxRateCache(FxRateClient client) {\n        this.client = client;\n    }\n\n    // Only one thread refreshes; the others keep serving the current snapshot\n    public void refreshIfIdle() {\n        if (!refreshLock.tryLock()) {\n            return;                                   // a refresh is already running\n        }\n        try {\n            rates = Map.copyOf(client.fetchLatest());\n        } finally {\n            refreshLock.unlock();\n        }\n    }\n\n    public BigDecimal rate(String pair) {\n        return rates.get(pair);\n    }\n}","note":"`synchronized` cannot express \"skip if busy\"; with it, every caller would queue behind the slow HTTP refresh. Readers never take the lock because the map is immutable and published through a volatile field."}],"followUps":[{"q":"A refresh job and request threads share a cache. How could `tryLock` keep requests from piling up?","a":"Let the thread that wins `tryLock()` refresh the cache while every other thread skips the refresh and serves the current value, so a slow refresh never blocks request threads. On a request path you can also bound waiting with `tryLock(100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)` and return a retryable error on failure. Only call `unlock()` in the branch where the acquire succeeded."},{"q":"Why might `new ReentrantLock(true)` lower throughput under contention?","a":"A fair lock hands the lock to the longest-waiting thread, so a running thread cannot barge in when the lock becomes free. Nearly every handoff therefore wakes a parked thread and pays a context switch, and the Javadoc warns that fair locks are often much slower under contention. It buys freedom from starvation and steadier tail latency, which is only worth it when starvation is a measured problem."}],"extra":["**Biased locking is gone:** it was deprecated in JDK 15 and removed in JDK 18, so old benchmarks comparing uncontended `synchronized` with `ReentrantLock` no longer apply; measure on your JDK.","**Untimed tryLock barges:** `tryLock()` without a timeout ignores the fairness setting and takes a free lock even if threads are queued; `tryLock(0, TimeUnit.SECONDS)` honors fairness.","**Diagnostics:** in a thread dump, monitors appear as `waiting to lock <0x...>`, while `ReentrantLock` waiters appear as `parking to wait for` a `ReentrantLock$NonfairSync`; `jstack -l <pid>` also lists the ownable synchronizers each thread holds.","**Conditions need loops:** `Condition.await()`, like `Object.wait()`, can wake spuriously, so always wait in a `while (!predicate)` loop; separate conditions avoid waking every waiter with `notifyAll`.","**Private lock objects:** synchronizing on `this`, a `String` literal or a boxed `Integer` lets unrelated code contend on or deadlock your monitor; lock on a private final object instead."],"redFlags":["calls `lock()` inside the `try` block, so a failed acquire still runs `unlock()`","assumes a fair lock is always the safer default"]},{"id":47,"category":"Concurrency","question":"sleep() vs wait(): what is the difference, and why must wait() be called in a loop?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`Thread.sleep()` pauses the current thread and keeps every lock it holds; `Object.wait()` requires owning that object's monitor, releases it, and suspends until notified, interrupted or timed out. `wait()` goes in a `while` loop that rechecks the condition, because wakeups can be spurious, stolen or meant for another condition.","answer":["`sleep` is a static method on `Thread`: the current thread stops for at least the given time, keeps its locks, and only an interrupt wakes it early. `wait` is an instance method on `Object` for coordination: the caller must own the monitor (be inside `synchronized (obj)`) or it gets `IllegalMonitorStateException`. `wait()` atomically releases the monitor and parks the thread in the object's wait set; when woken, it must reacquire the monitor before returning, competing like any other thread. Use `sleep` for a delay and `wait` to wait for state another thread will change.","The `while` loop (the guarded block idiom) covers several cases. The specification allows spurious wakeups: `wait()` may return with no notification. Between the `notify` and reacquiring the monitor, another thread can take the lock and consume the state (a stolen wakeup). With `notifyAll` every waiter wakes, but only one may find the condition still true. And producers and consumers often share one monitor, so a wakeup may concern the other condition. With `if`, the thread proceeds on a false assumption. Checking under the lock before the first `wait()` also prevents a lost wakeup, where a notify sent earlier is missed forever.","`notify()` wakes one arbitrary waiter and is safe only when all waiters wait for the same condition and any of them can progress; otherwise it may wake a producer when only a consumer can proceed, the signal is lost, and threads hang. `notifyAll()` is the safe default. Today you rarely write this code: `ReentrantLock` with separate `Condition` objects lets `signal()` target the right waiters, and `BlockingQueue`, `CountDownLatch` and `CompletableFuture` encode the loop, signaling and timeouts for you. `Condition.await()` can also wake spuriously, so it still goes in a loop."],"keyPoints":["sleep keeps locks; wait releases the monitor it was called on","wait requires owning the monitor and belongs in a while loop","prefer Condition and BlockingQueue over raw wait/notify"],"examples":[{"title":"A bounded order buffer with wait/notifyAll, and the bug an if creates","lang":"java","code":"public final class OrderBuffer {\n    private final Deque<Order> orders = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    private final int capacity;\n\n    public OrderBuffer(int capacity) { this.capacity = capacity; }\n\n    public synchronized void put(Order order) throws InterruptedException {\n        while (orders.size() == capacity) {  // GOOD: recheck after every wakeup\n            wait();                          // releases this monitor while waiting\n        }\n        orders.addLast(order);\n        notifyAll();  // consumers proceed, producers recheck\n    }\n\n    public synchronized Order take() throws InterruptedException {\n        // BAD: if (orders.isEmpty()) wait();\n        // a spurious or stolen wakeup makes removeFirst() throw NoSuchElementException\n        while (orders.isEmpty()) {\n            wait();\n        }\n        Order next = orders.removeFirst();\n        notifyAll();\n        return next;\n    }\n\n    public synchronized void retryLater(Order order) throws InterruptedException {\n        Thread.sleep(1_000);  // BAD: holds the monitor, the buffer stops for 1 s\n        put(order);\n    }\n}","note":"Producers and consumers share one wait set, so `notify()` could wake the wrong side and lose the signal; `while` plus `notifyAll()` is the correct pair. `retryLater` shows the cost of sleeping under a lock."},{"title":"The same buffer with ReentrantLock and two Conditions","lang":"java","code":"private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();\nprivate final Condition notFull = lock.newCondition();\nprivate final Condition notEmpty = lock.newCondition();\n\npublic boolean offer(Order order, Duration timeout) throws InterruptedException {\n    long nanos = timeout.toNanos();\n    lock.lock();\n    try {\n        while (orders.size() == capacity) {\n            if (nanos <= 0) {\n                return false;  // caller applies backpressure\n            }\n            nanos = notFull.awaitNanos(nanos); // remaining time survives spurious wakeups\n        }\n        orders.addLast(order);\n        notEmpty.signal();  // only consumers wait on notEmpty\n        return true;\n    } finally {\n        lock.unlock();\n    }\n}\n\npublic Order take() throws InterruptedException {\n    lock.lock();\n    try {\n        while (orders.isEmpty()) {\n            notEmpty.await();\n        }\n        Order next = orders.removeFirst();\n        notFull.signal();\n        return next;\n    } finally {\n        lock.unlock();\n    }\n}","note":"Separate conditions make a single `signal()` safe because every waiter on `notEmpty` is a consumer that can progress. This is essentially `ArrayBlockingQueue`, which is what real code should use."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens if you call Thread.sleep() inside a synchronized block?","a":"The thread keeps the monitor for the whole sleep, so every other thread needing it goes `BLOCKED`; a retry backoff written this way serializes the service. Wait for conditions with `wait()` or `Condition.await()`, which release the lock, and do delays outside the lock or on a `ScheduledExecutorService`."},{"q":"How should InterruptedException from sleep() or wait() be handled?","a":"Propagate it, or restore the flag with `Thread.currentThread().interrupt()` and stop promptly. An empty catch clears the interrupt status, so `shutdownNow()`, `Future.cancel(true)` and graceful shutdown can no longer stop the thread. An interrupted `wait()` reacquires the monitor before throwing."}],"extra":["**Spurious wakeups are in the contract:** the `Object.wait` and `Condition` javadocs allow returning without a notification; they are rare, but the same loop also handles stolen and mismatched wakeups, which are common.","**Wrong monitor:** calling `wait` or `notify` on an object other than the one you synchronized on throws `IllegalMonitorStateException` at run time, not at compile time.","**Polling with sleep:** `while (!done) Thread.sleep(100)` adds latency and needs `done` to be volatile; a `CountDownLatch` or `CompletableFuture` wakes the waiter exactly when state changes. In tests, Awaitility replaces fixed sleeps.","**Timed waits:** `wait(millis)` returns without telling you whether it timed out; recompute the remaining time in the loop, or use `Condition.awaitNanos`, which returns it.","**Virtual threads:** on Java 21, a virtual thread blocked in `synchronized` or `Object.wait()` pins its carrier; JEP 491 (Java 24) removed that pinning for monitors."],"redFlags":["Says sleep() and wait() both release the lock","Uses if instead of while around wait()","Uses notify() without explaining when a single wakeup is safe"]},{"id":48,"category":"Concurrency","question":"How would you implement a producer/consumer with a BlockingQueue?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Producers `put` items into a bounded `BlockingQueue` and consumers `take` them; the queue does all locking and signaling, and its capacity creates backpressure when consumers fall behind. Shutdown needs a protocol: one poison pill per consumer after all producers finish, or a stop flag with timed `poll` and a final drain.","answer":["A `BlockingQueue` offers four styles of operation: `add`/`remove` throw when full or empty, `offer`/`poll` return immediately with false or null, `put`/`take` block, and `offer(e, timeout)`/`poll(timeout)` block up to a limit. `ArrayBlockingQueue` is a fixed array with one lock; `LinkedBlockingQueue` has separate put and take locks, allocates a node per element and defaults to `Integer.MAX_VALUE` capacity.","Capacity is the real design decision. An unbounded queue never pushes back, so when consumers are slower (a slow database, an outage) the backlog grows until the heap runs out. A bounded queue turns that into backpressure: `put` slows producers to the consumers' pace, and a short timed `offer` lets a request fail fast with a 503. Size it from tolerable delay: 10,000 items at 2,000 per second is five seconds.","Stopping is what people forget. With poison pills, once producers finish, one sentinel per consumer is enqueued; each consumer exits on its pill, after the real items ahead of it. With several producers, something (a `CountDownLatch`) must know they are all done before pills are sent, and the pill cannot be `null` because blocking queues reject nulls. Services often use a volatile running flag with timed `poll` and a final drain instead; `shutdownNow()` abandons queued items, and a crash loses them all."],"keyPoints":["put/take block, timed offer/poll let callers give up","a bounded queue is what creates backpressure","plan shutdown: one poison pill per consumer, or stop flag plus drain"],"examples":[{"title":"Multi-producer payment export with poison pills","lang":"java","code":"private static final PaymentRecord POISON = new PaymentRecord(null, null, null);\nprivate final BlockingQueue<PaymentRecord> queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1_000);\n\npublic void export(List<Path> files, int consumers) throws InterruptedException {\n    CountDownLatch producersDone = new CountDownLatch(files.size());\n    try (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(files.size() + consumers)) {\n        for (Path file : files) {\n            pool.submit(() -> {\n                try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(file)) {\n                    String line;\n                    while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {\n                        queue.put(PaymentRecord.parse(line));  // blocks while the queue is full\n                    }\n                } catch (IOException e) {\n                    log.error(\"cannot read {}\", file, e);  // not hidden in the Future\n                } finally {\n                    producersDone.countDown();  // failed producers count too\n                }\n                return null;\n            });\n        }\n        for (int i = 0; i < consumers; i++) {\n            pool.submit(() -> {\n                for (PaymentRecord r = queue.take(); r != POISON; r = queue.take()) {\n                    try {\n                        ledgerClient.export(r);\n                    } catch (RuntimeException e) {  // one bad record must not kill the consumer\n                        log.warn(\"export failed for payment {}\", r.paymentId(), e);\n                    }\n                }\n                return null;\n            });\n        }\n        producersDone.await();\n        for (int i = 0; i < consumers; i++) {\n            queue.put(POISON);  // exactly one pill per consumer\n        }\n    } // close() waits until every consumer has drained the queue and exited\n}","note":"Pills go in only after every producer finishes, so they sit behind all real records; the consumer compares by identity. Returning `null` makes the lambdas `Callable`, so `put` and `take` may throw `InterruptedException`."},{"title":"Request threads with backpressure, a batching writer and a draining stop","lang":"java","code":"@Component\npublic class AuditEventBuffer {\n    private final BlockingQueue<AuditEvent> queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(10_000);\n    private volatile boolean running = true;\n    private Thread writer;\n\n    @PostConstruct\n    void start() { writer = Thread.ofPlatform().name(\"audit-writer\").start(this::writeLoop); }\n\n    // false: the controller answers 503 with Retry-After\n    public boolean tryAccept(AuditEvent event) throws InterruptedException {\n        return running && queue.offer(event, 50, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);\n    }\n\n    private void writeLoop() {\n        List<AuditEvent> batch = new ArrayList<>(500);\n        while (running || !queue.isEmpty()) {  // after shutdown, drain the rest\n            try {\n                AuditEvent first = queue.poll(200, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);\n                if (first == null) continue;\n                batch.add(first);\n                queue.drainTo(batch, 499);  // one multi-row insert per batch\n                repository.insertAll(batch);\n            } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n                return;\n            } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n                log.error(\"dropped {} audit events\", batch.size(), e);\n            } finally {\n                batch.clear();\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    @PreDestroy\n    void shutdown() {\n        running = false;  // stop accepting, let the writer drain\n        joinQuietly(writer, Duration.ofSeconds(20));\n    }\n}","note":"The bounded queue plus a 50 ms `offer` is the backpressure: overloaded callers get a fast 503, not heap growth. Keep the drain within the pod's termination grace period."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why not just use an unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue?","a":"It never blocks producers, so a slow consumer becomes heap growth and eventually `OutOfMemoryError`. The same trap hides in `Executors.newFixedThreadPool`, whose work queue is an unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue`. Pass an explicit capacity and decide what producers do when it is full."},{"q":"When would you replace the in-memory queue with Kafka or RabbitMQ?","a":"When items must survive a crash or deploy, when producers and consumers run in different processes, or when you need replay and dead-lettering. In-memory queues suit work that can be lost or rebuilt."}],"extra":["**No nulls:** every `BlockingQueue` rejects `null`, because `poll` uses it to mean empty, so the pill must be a real sentinel.","**drainTo for batching:** after one blocking `poll`, `drainTo(batch, max)` grabs whatever else is waiting without blocking, turning many inserts into one batch.","**Monitor the depth:** export `size()` as a gauge; a queue pinned near capacity means consumers are the bottleneck.","**Handoff queues:** `SynchronousQueue` makes the producer wait until a consumer takes the item: backpressure with zero buffering.","**Virtual threads:** blocking in `put` and `take` is cheap there, but capacity still provides the backpressure."],"redFlags":["Uses an unbounded queue and calls it backpressure","Sends one poison pill for several consumers, or sends pills before producers finish","Lets one failing item kill the consumer thread"]},{"id":49,"category":"Concurrency","question":"One pool resizes images, another calls a partner API. What size suits each, and what really limits the second?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Compute-heavy work gains nothing past roughly one thread per core; work that mostly waits can use more threads, scaled by how long each task blocks relative to how long it computes, but the dependency's own capacity sets the true limit, so cap the queue and confirm the numbers with a load test.","answer":["For **CPU-bound** work (scoring, encryption, transforming large payloads), more threads than cores only add context switches and cache misses, so size near `Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors()`, perhaps plus one. In a container this value follows the CPU quota, and with Kubernetes CPU limits too many busy threads cause CFS throttling and latency spikes.","For **I/O-bound** work the formula from Java Concurrency in Practice is `threads = cores * targetUtilization * (1 + wait / compute)`: a task that computes for 5 ms and waits 95 ms on HTTP can keep about 20 threads per core busy. Little's law gives the same answer from the load side: required concurrency equals throughput times latency, so 400 requests per second at 100 ms needs about 40 in flight.","Formulas give an upper bound; downstream capacity sets the real one. If the pool calls PostgreSQL through a Hikari pool of 20 connections, 200 threads just queue on `getConnection`. The same applies to HTTP client connection limits and a partner's rate limit. Use a bounded queue with an explicit rejection policy, separate pools per dependency (bulkheads) so one slow partner cannot starve everything, and then measure queue wait, active threads, p99 latency and downstream saturation in a load test. Tune from data, not a big round number."],"keyPoints":["extra threads on CPU work only add context switches and memory","a thread that mostly waits frees its core, so waiting work tolerates more threads","the partner's rate limit or connection pool caps useful concurrency","a load test with latency percentiles settles the final number"],"examples":[{"title":"CPU-bound and I/O-bound executors sized differently","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass ExecutorConfig {\n\n    // CPU-bound: in-memory risk scoring\n    @Bean(destroyMethod = \"shutdown\")\n    ExecutorService scoringExecutor() {\n        int cores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();   // follows the container CPU quota\n        return new ThreadPoolExecutor(cores, cores, 0L, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS,\n                new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(cores * 4),\n                new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());\n    }\n\n    // I/O-bound: credit bureau calls, about 5 ms CPU and 95 ms waiting each\n    // formula: cores * (1 + 95 / 5), then capped by the HTTP client's connection limit\n    @Bean(destroyMethod = \"shutdown\")\n    ExecutorService bureauExecutor(@Value(\"${bureau.max-connections:20}\") int maxConnections) {\n        int cores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();\n        int threads = Math.min(cores * (1 + 95 / 5), maxConnections);\n        return new ThreadPoolExecutor(threads, threads, 60L, TimeUnit.SECONDS,\n                new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(100),\n                new ThreadPoolExecutor.AbortPolicy());\n    }\n}","note":"The I/O pool is capped by what the downstream accepts, not by the formula. Both queues are bounded, so saturation shows up as rejections you can count and map to 503, not as silent latency."},{"title":"Worked sizing for one dependency","lang":"text","code":"Sizing the credit bureau pool (4 vCPU pods, 6 replicas)\n\nMeasured per call:   compute ~4 ms, wait ~96 ms at p50, p99 latency 300 ms\nFormula bound:       4 cores * (1 + 96/4) = 100 threads per pod\nLittle's law:        peak 300 calls/s over 6 pods = 50 calls/s per pod\n                     50/s * 0.1 s = 5 in flight at p50, 50/s * 0.3 s = 15 at p99\nDownstream limit:    contract allows 120 concurrent calls in total = 20 per pod\n\nDecision:            core = max = 20 threads, ArrayBlockingQueue(100), AbortPolicy to 503 + Retry-After\n                     HTTP client max connections per route = 20\n                     alert on queue wait p99 > 50 ms and on any rejections","note":"The formula says 100, the load needs about 15, the partner allows 20: the smallest meaningful limit wins. Revisit when latency or traffic changes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does Little's Law help estimate the threads an I/O-bound pool needs?","a":"Concurrency equals throughput times latency: to sustain 200 requests per second against a dependency that takes 250 ms, you need about 50 calls in flight. That is the thread count the pool needs, plus headroom for latency spikes. If the dependency only accepts 20 concurrent calls, the formula shows the target throughput is unreachable, and more threads would only queue."},{"q":"The executor has 200 threads but Hikari allows 20 connections. What do you see in production?","a":"Most threads block in `getConnection()` until a connection frees up or Hikari's `connectionTimeout` (30 seconds by default) expires, and then fail with `SQLTransientConnectionException`. Throughput stays at what 20 connections can deliver while latency, memory use and timeout errors climb. Size the executor to the pool, or gate database work with a semaphore."}],"extra":["**Container awareness:** since JDK 10 the JVM reads cgroup CPU limits, so `availableProcessors()` in a pod limited to 2 CPUs returns 2; `-XX:ActiveProcessorCount` overrides it when the detection is wrong for your workload.","**Common pool:** `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()` defaults to parallelism of cores minus one and is shared by parallel streams and default `CompletableFuture` async methods, so never run blocking I/O there.","**Database side:** the HikariCP pool sizing guidance starts around `(core_count * 2) + effective_spindle_count` connections per database server, and PostgreSQL `max_connections` is shared by every replica, so pool size times pod count must fit.","**Virtual threads change the question:** for blocking I/O on Java 21 you can use one virtual thread per task and cap concurrency with a `Semaphore` sized to the downstream limit instead of tuning a pool size.","**Bulkheads:** giving each downstream its own bounded executor (or a Resilience4j bulkhead) means a slow dependency exhausts only its own threads, not the servlet container's request threads."],"redFlags":["sizes the pool from request rate alone, ignoring how long each task takes","runs CPU-heavy and blocking tasks in the same pool","applies a formula once and never checks the result under real traffic"]},{"id":50,"category":"Concurrency","question":"During a spike, a `ThreadPoolExecutor` on an unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue` never exceeds its core size. Why, and what would you configure instead?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"ThreadPoolExecutor fills core threads, then the queue, then grows to max, then rejects; choose a bounded queue and a deliberate rejection policy so overload becomes visible backpressure, not growing latency and memory.","answer":["On `execute()` the executor follows a fixed order: fewer than `corePoolSize` threads running, start a thread; otherwise offer the task to the queue; if the queue refuses because it is full, start a thread up to `maximumPoolSize`; if that is reached too, call the `RejectedExecutionHandler`. So the queue type decides how core and max interact. `SynchronousQueue` hands off directly and grows threads at once (with an unlimited max, as in `newCachedThreadPool`, that means unbounded threads). An unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue`, as in `newFixedThreadPool`, never fills, so max is ignored. A bounded `ArrayBlockingQueue` gives you both limits.","Unbounded queues hide overload: latency grows first, then heap, and callers time out while their tasks still wait. Capping the queue and choosing on purpose how excess work is refused is what turns overload into backpressure. `AbortPolicy` (the default) throws `RejectedExecutionException`, which you map to 503 or a retry. `CallerRunsPolicy` runs the task on the submitting thread, which slows the producer naturally. `DiscardPolicy` and `DiscardOldestPolicy` drop work silently; they are only acceptable for genuinely lossy work, with a metric on every drop.","Also name the threads, set `keepAliveTime` for burst threads, and check Spring defaults: a plain `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor` and Spring Boot's auto-configured `applicationTaskExecutor` both have an unbounded queue capacity unless you set it."],"keyPoints":["below core size a task gets a new thread, then tasks queue, then extra threads start, then rejection begins","extra threads are only created when the queue refuses a task","a queue with a cap turns overload into an explicit, countable signal","running rejected work on the submitting thread throttles intake"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: bounded, observable executor","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass PaymentCallbackExecutorConfig {\n\n    // BAD: Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8) uses an unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue,\n    // so a slow acquirer grows the backlog, latency and heap with no signal\n\n    // GOOD: bounded queue, explicit rejection that is counted\n    @Bean(destroyMethod = \"shutdown\")\n    ThreadPoolExecutor paymentCallbackExecutor(MeterRegistry registry) {\n        Counter rejected = registry.counter(\"payment.callback.rejected\");\n\n        RejectedExecutionHandler rejectAndCount = (task, executor) -> {\n            rejected.increment();\n            throw new RejectedExecutionException(\"payment callback pool saturated\");\n        };\n\n        ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(\n                8,                                  // core threads\n                16,                                 // max, only used once the queue is full\n                30, TimeUnit.SECONDS,               // idle burst threads retire after 30s\n                new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(500),\n                Thread.ofPlatform().name(\"payment-callback-\", 0).factory(),\n                rejectAndCount);\n\n        new ExecutorServiceMetrics(executor, \"payment.callback\", List.of()).bindTo(registry);\n        return executor;\n    }\n}","note":"Micrometer's `ExecutorServiceMetrics` exposes pool size, active threads, queued tasks and remaining queue capacity; the JDK does not count rejections, so the handler does."},{"title":"Bounding Spring Boot's default async executor","lang":"yaml","code":"# Auto-configured applicationTaskExecutor, used by @Async\nspring:\n  task:\n    execution:\n      thread-name-prefix: async-\n      pool:\n        core-size: 8\n        max-size: 16\n        queue-capacity: 500        # default is unbounded, which makes max-size meaningless\n        keep-alive: 30s\n      shutdown:\n        await-termination: true\n        await-termination-period: 20s","note":"With no completions, tasks 9 to 508 queue, tasks 509 to 516 start burst threads, and task 517 is rejected with Spring's `TaskRejectedException`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does `SynchronousQueue` change about how the executor grows?","a":"A `SynchronousQueue` has no capacity, so every task is handed straight to a thread; when none is free, the executor creates a new thread up to `maximumPoolSize` and rejects beyond that. That is how `Executors.newCachedThreadPool()` works, with an effectively unlimited maximum, which can create thousands of threads under a spike. Use it with a firm maximum and a rejection policy if you want direct handoff without queueing."},{"q":"Which executor signals would you put on a dashboard to catch saturation early?","a":"Active threads against the maximum, queue depth and remaining capacity, and a rejection counter you increment in the handler, since the JDK does not count rejections. Add a timer for queue wait separately from execution time, because rising queue wait is the earliest saturation sign. Alert on sustained queue growth, any rejection and p99 queue wait, and read them next to downstream pool metrics such as pending Hikari connections."}],"extra":["**CallerRuns during shutdown:** `CallerRunsPolicy` silently discards the task if the executor is already shut down, so do not rely on it to guarantee execution during a rolling restart.","**Exceptions vanish with submit:** a task passed to `submit()` stores its exception in the returned `Future`, so if nobody calls `get()` the failure is invisible; `execute()` reaches the uncaught exception handler. Override `afterExecute` or wrap tasks to log failures.","**Prestart and core timeout:** `prestartAllCoreThreads()` avoids a cold start on the first burst, and `allowCoreThreadTimeOut(true)` lets a rarely used pool shrink to zero threads.","**Queue wait is the early warning:** time from submit to start rises before errors do; Micrometer's `ExecutorServiceMetrics.monitor(...)` wrapper records idle (queued) time and execution time as timers.","**Graceful shutdown:** call `shutdown()`, then `awaitTermination` with a deadline shorter than the pod's termination grace period, then `shutdownNow()`, so in-flight callbacks finish during a Kubernetes rolling update."],"redFlags":["sets a large `maximumPoolSize` and expects it to matter with an unbounded queue","chooses `CallerRunsPolicy` for work submitted from an event loop thread"]},{"id":51,"category":"Concurrency","question":"You are reviewing a PR that fans out calls with `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync`. Which problems would you look for first?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Common mistakes: blocking work on the common pool, missing timeouts and error handling, join mid-pipeline, lost MDC and security context, unbounded fan-out; fix with explicit bounded executors, composed stages and deliberate failure handling.","answer":["`supplyAsync(fn)` without an executor runs on `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()`, sized to cores minus one and shared by parallel streams across the whole JVM. Put blocking HTTP or JDBC calls there and a few slow requests stall every other async user. Pass a dedicated, bounded executor per dependency, or on Java 21 a virtual-thread executor with a semaphore as the concurrency limit.","Failures are easy to lose. An exception completes the future exceptionally, wrapped in `CompletionException`; if no stage calls `exceptionally`, `handle` or `whenComplete` and nobody joins, it simply vanishes. There is no default timeout either: add `orTimeout` or `completeOnTimeout` (Java 9+) plus client-level timeouts, because completing the future does not stop the underlying call. Calling `join()` or `get()` inside a stage blocks a pool thread and can starve the pool; compose with `thenCompose`, `thenCombine` and `allOf`, and block once, at the edge, with a timeout. Nesting futures with `thenApply` instead of `thenCompose` is a common red flag.","Context does not follow the task: MDC, `SecurityContextHolder`, tracing spans and transactions are ThreadLocal-based, so logs lose the correlation id and a `@Transactional` boundary does not extend into async stages. Propagate with a `TaskDecorator` or Micrometer context propagation. Finally, one future per item for 10,000 orders floods the executor and the downstream service; batch or cap in-flight work."],"keyPoints":["without an executor argument, blocking calls land on the shared common pool","every external call needs a timeout and a defined fallback or failure path","calling `join()` mid-pipeline turns async code back into blocked threads","logging MDC and security context do not follow work onto other threads by default"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: credit decision pipeline","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: common pool, blocking HTTP, no timeout, nested futures, failures lost\nCompletableFuture<CompletableFuture<Decision>> bad =\n        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> bureauClient.fetchReport(customerId))\n                .thenApply(report -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> scorer.score(report)));\n\n// GOOD: explicit executors, flat composition, timeout and a deliberate fallback\nCompletableFuture<Decision> decision =\n        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> bureauClient.fetchReport(customerId), bureauExecutor)\n                .orTimeout(800, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)\n                .thenCompose(report ->\n                        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> scorer.score(report), scoringExecutor))\n                .exceptionally(ex -> {\n                    log.warn(\"Credit decision degraded for customer {}\", customerId, ex);\n                    return Decision.manualReview(customerId);\n                });","note":"`orTimeout` only fails the future; the bureau client still needs its own read timeout so the socket and thread are actually released. The fallback is a business decision (manual review), not a silent default."},{"title":"Bounded fan-out with MDC propagation","lang":"java","code":"@Bean\nThreadPoolTaskExecutor pricingExecutor() {\n    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();\n    executor.setCorePoolSize(16);\n    executor.setMaxPoolSize(16);\n    executor.setQueueCapacity(200);\n    executor.setThreadNamePrefix(\"pricing-\");\n    executor.setTaskDecorator(task -> {\n        Map<String, String> mdc = MDC.getCopyOfContextMap();     // captured on the caller thread\n        return () -> {\n            if (mdc != null) MDC.setContextMap(mdc);\n            try { task.run(); } finally { MDC.clear(); }\n        };\n    });\n    return executor;\n}\n\nCompletableFuture<List<Price>> priceAll(List<String> skus) {\n    List<CompletableFuture<Price>> futures = skus.stream()\n            .map(sku -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> pricingClient.price(sku), pricingExecutor)\n                    .completeOnTimeout(Price.unavailable(sku), 300, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)\n                    .exceptionally(ex -> Price.unavailable(sku)))\n            .toList();\n    return CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(CompletableFuture[]::new))\n            .thenApply(done -> futures.stream().map(CompletableFuture::join).toList());\n}","note":"The `join()` calls run only after `allOf` completed, so they never block. If a request brings more SKUs than the 16 threads and 200 queue slots can absorb, `supplyAsync` throws `TaskRejectedException` immediately: chunk large lists instead of fanning out without limit."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does `cancel(true)` on a `CompletableFuture` often leave the work running?","a":"A `CompletableFuture` is not tied to the thread computing it, so `cancel(true)` only completes it exceptionally with `CancellationException`; the `mayInterruptIfRunning` flag has no effect. The supplier keeps running and holds its socket or connection until it finishes. To actually stop work, submit to an `ExecutorService` and cancel that `Future`, or rely on client timeouts and structured concurrency."},{"q":"Loading a customer and then fetching their credit report: which combinator chains those two async calls, and why?","a":"`thenCompose`, because the second call depends on the first result and itself returns a future. `thenApply` would produce `CompletableFuture<CompletableFuture<Report>>`, which tempts someone to call `join()` inside the stage. For two independent calls, start both and combine them with `thenCombine` or `allOf` instead."}],"extra":["**Which thread runs a stage:** `thenApply` runs on whichever thread completes the previous stage (or the caller if it is already complete), while `thenApplyAsync` without an executor jumps to the common pool; pass your executor to the `*Async` variants too.","**cancel does not interrupt:** `CompletableFuture.cancel(true)` just completes the future with `CancellationException`; the `mayInterruptIfRunning` flag has no effect, so the running HTTP or SQL call continues.","**Unwrap before matching:** inside `handle` and `exceptionally` the throwable is usually a `CompletionException`; check `getCause()` before mapping to domain exceptions or HTTP status codes.","**Java 19+ helpers:** `Future.resultNow()`, `exceptionNow()` and `state()` inspect already completed futures after `allOf` without checked exceptions or blocking.","**Consider plain blocking code:** with virtual threads, straightforward blocking calls in `newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` are often clearer than long future chains; `StructuredTaskScope` adds sibling cancellation but is still a preview API in Java 21."],"redFlags":["believes `cancel(true)` interrupts the thread running the task","starts one future per item of an unbounded list with no concurrency cap","hides failures with `exceptionally(e -> null)` and passes nulls downstream"]},{"id":52,"category":"Concurrency","question":"After moving a service to virtual threads, throughput stays flat under load. What did they fix, and what not?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Virtual threads make blocking thread-per-request code scale to huge concurrency cheaply, but they add no CPU capacity, do not raise database or partner limits, and still suffer from pinning and heavy ThreadLocal use.","answer":["Platform threads map one to one to OS threads, reserve a large stack and are expensive to create, so servers cap them at a few hundred and waste them while waiting on I/O. Virtual threads (final in Java 21, JEP 444) are scheduled by the JVM onto a small pool of carrier threads. When a virtual thread blocks on a socket, lock or sleep, it unmounts and its stack is parked on the heap, freeing the carrier. You can create millions, so plain blocking code (JDBC, `RestClient`) handles high concurrency without reactive frameworks. In Spring Boot 3.2+, `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` switches Tomcat and `@Async` to them.","They do not make anything faster per request. CPU-bound work still needs cores, and more threads only add contention. Downstream limits stay: a Hikari pool of 20 connections still allows 20 concurrent queries, and 10,000 virtual threads will queue on it. Since you should not pool virtual threads, limit concurrency explicitly with a `Semaphore` or the connection pool.","Two gotchas remain. **Pinning:** on JDK 21, blocking inside `synchronized` or a native call keeps the carrier occupied, and with few carriers the application can stall (JDK 24 fixed the `synchronized` case). **ThreadLocal:** every virtual thread gets its own copy, so ThreadLocal caches of expensive objects multiply memory; pass context explicitly or use scoped values."],"keyPoints":["blocking a virtual thread unmounts it, so idle waits no longer hold an OS thread","compute-heavy work still competes for the same cores","databases and partners still cap how many calls run at once","pinned carriers and large per-thread caches erode the gains"],"examples":[{"title":"Thread per task with an explicit downstream limit","lang":"java","code":"public class BureauBatchFetcher {\n\n    private final Semaphore bureauPermits = new Semaphore(50);   // partner allows 50 concurrent calls\n    private final BureauClient bureauClient;\n\n    public BureauBatchFetcher(BureauClient bureauClient) {\n        this.bureauClient = bureauClient;\n    }\n\n    public List<CreditReport> fetchAll(List<String> customerIds)\n            throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {\n        try (ExecutorService executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n            List<Future<CreditReport>> futures = customerIds.stream()\n                    .map(id -> executor.submit(() -> fetchWithPermit(id)))\n                    .toList();\n            List<CreditReport> reports = new ArrayList<>();\n            for (Future<CreditReport> future : futures) {\n                reports.add(future.get());\n            }\n            return reports;\n        }\n    }\n\n    private CreditReport fetchWithPermit(String customerId) throws InterruptedException {\n        bureauPermits.acquire();\n        try {\n            return bureauClient.fetch(customerId);   // blocking HTTP: the virtual thread unmounts\n        } finally {\n            bureauPermits.release();\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Ten thousand ids create ten thousand cheap virtual threads, but only 50 talk to the bureau at once. `ExecutorService` is `AutoCloseable` since Java 19, and `close()` waits for submitted tasks."},{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: pinning on JDK 21","lang":"java","code":"public class BureauTokenProvider {\n\n    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();\n    private final AuthClient authClient;\n    private AccessToken token;\n\n    public BureauTokenProvider(AuthClient authClient) {\n        this.authClient = authClient;\n    }\n\n    // BAD on JDK 21 to 23: an HTTP call inside synchronized pins the carrier thread\n    public synchronized AccessToken currentTokenPinned() {\n        if (token == null || token.expiresSoon()) {\n            token = authClient.fetchToken();\n        }\n        return token;\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: ReentrantLock lets the virtual thread unmount while it waits\n    public AccessToken currentToken() {\n        lock.lock();\n        try {\n            if (token == null || token.expiresSoon()) {\n                token = authClient.fetchToken();\n            }\n            return token;\n        } finally {\n            lock.unlock();\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Find pinning with the JFR event `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned`, or on JDK 21 with `-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full`. In real code pick one of the two locks for the `token` field, never both."}],"followUps":[{"q":"On JDK 21, what happens when a virtual thread blocks inside a `synchronized` method, and how do you fix it?","a":"It is pinned: it cannot unmount, so its carrier platform thread stays blocked as well. With enough pinned threads every carrier is stuck and throughput collapses, which `-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads` or the JFR `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` event reveals. Replace the monitor with a `ReentrantLock` around the blocking section, or move to JDK 24 or later, where JEP 491 removed pinning for `synchronized`."},{"q":"With 10,000 virtual threads and a 20-connection Hikari pool, how many queries run at once?","a":"Twenty. The rest wait in `getConnection()` until a connection returns or `connectionTimeout` expires, so the pool acts as the semaphore for database work. Size it from what the database can execute in parallel, keep transactions short, and use a modest timeout so waiters fail fast instead of piling up."}],"extra":["**Carrier pool:** the default scheduler is a `ForkJoinPool` whose parallelism equals the number of available processors, tunable with the system property `jdk.virtualThreadScheduler.parallelism`.","**Throughput, not latency:** by Little's law virtual threads raise how many requests can be in flight; a single request's latency is unchanged, so a benchmark at low load shows no difference.","**Do not pool them:** creating a virtual thread is cheap, so use `Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` and express limits with semaphores; a fixed pool of virtual threads recreates the old problem.","**Scoped values:** `ScopedValue` is a preview API in Java 21 and final in JDK 25; it is an immutable, bounded alternative to ThreadLocal for request context such as tenant or principal.","**Library readiness:** older JDBC drivers and client libraries guarded socket I/O with `synchronized`, so upgrade dependencies and check for pinning under load before switching production traffic."],"redFlags":["puts virtual threads in a fixed-size pool","removes all concurrency limits because threads are now cheap","keeps long `synchronized` sections around I/O on JDK 21 without knowing about pinning"]},{"id":53,"category":"Concurrency","question":"What is the Java Memory Model's happens-before relation, and why does it matter?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Happens-before is the partial order the Java Memory Model uses to define visibility: if A happens-before B, B sees A's effects and everything before A. Conflicting accesses not ordered by it form a data race, and only data-race-free programs are guaranteed to behave like a simple interleaving of statements.","answer":["The JIT and CPUs reorder and cache memory operations: values sit in registers, writes wait in store buffers. One thread never notices, but another can observe writes late, out of order or never. The Java Memory Model does not describe hardware; it defines which writes a read may see, and its tool is happens-before. If A happens-before B, A's effects and everything that happened-before A are visible to B. It is not wall-clock order: an earlier write with no happens-before edge to your read may legitimately stay invisible.","The edges come from a short list. Program order: each action in a thread happens-before every later action in it. Monitor: an unlock happens-before every subsequent lock of the same monitor. Volatile: a write to a volatile field happens-before every subsequent read of that field. Thread start: `t.start()` happens-before every action in `t`. Termination: everything `t` did happens-before another thread returns from `t.join()`. The relation is transitive, which is how publication works: fill a map, write a volatile reference, and a reader that sees the reference also sees the contents. Final fields add a separate guarantee: if `this` does not escape the constructor, any thread that obtains the reference sees the final fields initialized.","It matters because the failures are silent. A data race is two accesses to one variable, at least one a write, with no happens-before between them. Data-race-free programs behave sequentially consistently; racy ones can see stale values, a reference to an object whose fields look unset, or a loop that never notices a flag because the JIT hoisted the read. Such code passes tests and fails under load, after JIT compilation, or on ARM servers. In practice you chain edges that `java.util.concurrent` documents: executor submission before task execution, task completion before `Future.get()` returns, insertion into a concurrent collection before retrieval."],"keyPoints":["happens-before defines visibility and ordering, not wall-clock time","rules: program order, monitor, volatile, start, join, final fields","no data races means sequentially consistent behavior"],"examples":[{"title":"A racy rate cache, and the happens-before chain that fixes it","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: two plain fields, no happens-before between the refresher and readers\nclass ExchangeRates {\n    private Map<String, BigDecimal> rates = new HashMap<>();\n    private boolean loaded;\n\n    void refresh() {  // scheduler thread\n        Map<String, BigDecimal> fresh = new HashMap<>();\n        provider.fetchAll().forEach(r -> fresh.put(r.currency(), r.rate()));\n        rates = fresh;\n        loaded = true;  // may become visible before rates\n    }\n\n    BigDecimal rate(String currency) {               // request threads\n        return loaded ? rates.get(currency) : null;  // old map, or new map missing entries\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: one volatile reference to an immutable snapshot\nclass ExchangeRates {\n    private volatile Map<String, BigDecimal> rates = Map.of();\n\n    void refresh() {\n        Map<String, BigDecimal> fresh = new HashMap<>();\n        provider.fetchAll().forEach(r -> fresh.put(r.currency(), r.rate()));\n        rates = Map.copyOf(fresh);  // (1) puts happen-before this write: program order\n    }                               // (2) volatile write happens-before later volatile reads\n\n    BigDecimal rate(String currency) {\n        return rates.get(currency);  // (3) volatile read, then get(): transitively sees every put\n    }\n}","note":"The fix is not two volatile fields but one reference, so readers never see a mix of old and new. The numbered comments are the proof to say aloud."},{"title":"Edges from start, join and executors, and how this-escape voids final","lang":"java","code":"// Start and join rules: a plain ArrayList is safe, one thread touches it at a time\nList<Payment> parsed = new ArrayList<>();\nThread importer = new Thread(() -> parsed.addAll(parser.parse(statementFile)));\nimporter.start();  // everything before start() is visible inside importer\nimporter.join();   // everything importer did is visible after join() returns\nreconciler.reconcile(parsed);\n\n// Executor rules: submit happens-before the task, the task happens-before get() returns\nFuture<List<Payment>> pending = executor.submit(() -> parser.parse(statementFile));\nreconciler.reconcile(pending.get());\n\n// Final field semantics hold only if 'this' does not escape the constructor\nclass SettlementListener {\n    private final Map<String, SettlementHandler> handlers;\n\n    SettlementListener(EventBus bus, Map<String, SettlementHandler> handlers) {\n        bus.register(this);  // BAD: onEvent can run before handlers is assigned\n        this.handlers = Map.copyOf(handlers);\n    }\n\n    void onEvent(SettlementEvent event) {\n        handlers.get(event.currency()).handle(event);  // NullPointerException on handlers\n    }\n}\n// FIX: assign every field first and register from a factory method after construction","note":"The first two blocks need no `volatile` or locks: `start`, `join`, `submit` and `Future.get` each create an edge. `final` only protects readers that obtain the reference after the constructor completes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Is double-checked locking safe in Java?","a":"Only with a `volatile` field. Without it, the reference write can become visible before the constructor's writes, so a thread passing the unsynchronized first check may use a half-initialized object. For static singletons the holder class idiom is simpler, because class initialization is safely published."},{"q":"Does synchronized only provide mutual exclusion?","a":"No, it also provides visibility: an unlock happens-before the next lock of the same monitor, so writes in one critical section are visible to the next thread entering it. A reader that skips the lock, or locks another object, has no edge and may see stale values."}],"extra":["**DRF-SC:** a program without data races behaves as if its statements were simply interleaved; the hard corners of the JMM only matter for racy code.","**x86 is forgiving:** x86 does not reorder stores with other stores, so some racy code works there and fails on ARM servers; the JIT can still break it on x86.","**long and double tearing:** the JMM allows non-volatile `long` and `double` writes to be split in two, so a racing reader may combine halves of two values; `volatile` makes them atomic.","**VarHandle modes:** `VarHandle` offers acquire/release and opaque modes weaker than volatile; they are for library internals, and application code should stick to volatile, locks and `java.util.concurrent`.","**Testing races:** unit tests rarely expose them; OpenJDK's jcstress harness runs small concurrent cases millions of times and reports which outcomes occur."],"redFlags":["Describes happens-before as real-time order","Believes a volatile field makes `count++` safe without a lock or an atomic class","Argues racy code is fine because tests pass on their machine"]},{"id":54,"category":"Concurrency","question":"How does ThreadLocal work, and how does it leak in thread pools or misbehave with virtual threads?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Each `Thread` holds a map from `ThreadLocal` keys to values, giving every thread a private copy without locking. Pooled threads live as long as the application, so values not removed in `finally` leak memory and, worse, leak one request's context into the next; on virtual threads per-thread caches stop paying off, and `ScopedValue` is the bounded, immutable alternative for context.","answer":["Values do not live in the `ThreadLocal` object: every `Thread` has a `ThreadLocalMap`, and `get()` looks in the current thread's map with the `ThreadLocal` as key. Keys are weak references but values are strong, so a value outlives its unreachable key until the map happens to purge stale entries. The intended uses are per-request context carried implicitly down the call stack (security principal, tenant, transaction, MDC) and per-thread caches of objects that are not thread-safe, classically `SimpleDateFormat`.","A pool worker runs thousands of tasks, so whatever one task leaves behind is there for the next. The correctness failure is worse: a tenant id, user id or MDC `orderId` that was never cleared shows up in another user's request, in logs blaming the wrong customer, or in a query scoped to the wrong tenant. The memory failure: large values stay referenced per thread forever, and in an app server the value's class pins the web app's class loader after redeploy. The fix: set, `try`, `remove()` in `finally`, owned by one place such as a filter or task decorator.","Virtual threads support `ThreadLocal`, but the economics change. They are meant to be created per task, not pooled, so the cross-request leak mostly disappears, while per-thread caching backfires: a cache once built for each of 200 pooled threads is now built for each of a million virtual threads. `InheritableThreadLocal` copies values into a child thread when it is created, which is wrong for pools whose workers are created once and reused. `ScopedValue` is the modern alternative for context: a binding lasts exactly for a `run` or `call`, is immutable, vanishes when the scope ends, and is inherited cheaply by structured concurrency subtasks. It is final since Java 25 (JEP 506); on Java 21 it is a preview API that needs `--enable-preview`."],"keyPoints":["values live in each Thread's ThreadLocalMap, keyed by the ThreadLocal","pooled threads keep values: always remove() in finally","avoid per-thread caches on virtual threads; prefer ScopedValue for context"],"examples":[{"title":"Tenant context that leaks between pooled requests, and the filter that fixes it","lang":"java","code":"public final class TenantContext {\n    private static final ThreadLocal<String> TENANT = new ThreadLocal<>();\n\n    public static void set(String tenantId) { TENANT.set(tenantId); }\n    public static String current() { return TENANT.get(); }\n    public static void clear() { TENANT.remove(); }\n}\n\n// BAD: in an interceptor's preHandle, with no matching cleanup\n//   TenantContext.set(request.getHeader(\"X-Tenant-Id\"));\n// later code on this Tomcat worker outside the interceptor (an excluded path, a filter) still sees the previous tenant\n\n// GOOD: one filter owns the full lifecycle of the context\n@Component\nclass TenantFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {\n    @Override\n    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response,\n                                    FilterChain chain) throws ServletException, IOException {\n        String tenantId = tenantResolver.resolve(request);  // reject unknown tenants here\n        TenantContext.set(tenantId);\n        MDC.put(\"tenantId\", tenantId);\n        try {\n            chain.doFilter(request, response);\n        } finally {\n            MDC.remove(\"tenantId\");\n            TenantContext.clear();\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The leak is invisible in tests that run one request per thread, and a tenant bleeding into the next request is a data-isolation incident, not just a memory problem."},{"title":"Carrying context across a pool hop, and the ScopedValue alternative","lang":"java","code":"// Spring: copy MDC and tenant from the submitting thread into the worker, then clean up\n@Bean\nThreadPoolTaskExecutor reportExecutor() {\n    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();\n    executor.setCorePoolSize(8);\n    executor.setTaskDecorator(task -> {\n        Map<String, String> mdc = MDC.getCopyOfContextMap();\n        String tenantId = TenantContext.current();\n        return () -> {\n            if (mdc != null) MDC.setContextMap(mdc);\n            TenantContext.set(tenantId);\n            try {\n                task.run();\n            } finally {\n                MDC.clear();\n                TenantContext.clear();\n            }\n        };\n    });\n    return executor;\n}\n\n// ScopedValue: bound for a scope, immutable, gone when the scope ends\nprivate static final ScopedValue<String> TENANT = ScopedValue.newInstance();\n\nvoid handle(HttpServletRequest request) {\n    ScopedValue.where(TENANT, tenantResolver.resolve(request))\n               .run(() -> orderService.listOrders());  // anything below can call TENANT.get()\n}\n\nList<Order> listOrders() {\n    return orderRepository.findByTenant(TENANT.get());  // throws if called outside a binding\n}","note":"The decorator captures context on the submitting thread and restores and clears it on the worker; without it, async work has no tenant or a stale one. With `ScopedValue` there is no `remove` to forget."}],"followUps":[{"q":"If ThreadLocalMap keys are weak references, why do ThreadLocals still leak?","a":"Only the key is weak; the value is strongly referenced from the thread's map until a later `get`, `set` or `remove` happens to purge the stale entry, which may never happen on a quiet pool thread. And the usual `static final` ThreadLocal is never collected at all, so its values live as long as the thread unless you call `remove()`."},{"q":"Why is InheritableThreadLocal a poor fit for executors?","a":"It copies the parent's values once, when a child thread is constructed. Pool workers are created by whichever request first needs them and then reused, so tasks see a long-gone request's context, or none. Use explicit propagation (a `TaskDecorator`, Micrometer context propagation) or `ScopedValue` with structured concurrency."}],"extra":["**MDC is a ThreadLocal:** Logback's MDC is stored per thread, so a missing `MDC.clear()` writes another request's `traceId` or `orderId` into logs.","**Framework context:** Spring's `SecurityContextHolder`, `RequestContextHolder` and transaction synchronization are ThreadLocal based by default, so `@Async` methods and `CompletableFuture` callbacks do not see the caller's user or transaction.","**Class loader leaks:** a ThreadLocal value whose class came from a web app keeps that app's class loader reachable through container threads after undeploy; Tomcat warns about this.","**withInitial:** the supplier runs on a thread's first `get()`, and again after `remove()`.","**Reactive code:** a Reactor pipeline hops across threads, so use the Reactor `Context` with Micrometer context propagation instead."],"redFlags":["Thinks values are stored inside the ThreadLocal object","Sets context without remove() in finally","Recommends InheritableThreadLocal to propagate context into a thread pool"]},{"id":55,"category":"Concurrency","question":"CountDownLatch vs CyclicBarrier vs Semaphore: which coordination problem does each solve?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"`CountDownLatch` lets threads wait until N events have happened, once; `CyclicBarrier` makes a fixed group of threads wait for each other at a meeting point, repeatedly; `Semaphore` limits how many threads use a resource at the same time. A latch counts events, a barrier counts arriving parties, and a semaphore hands out permits.","answer":["`CountDownLatch` is a one-shot gate: it starts at N, any thread calls `countDown()`, and threads in `await()` proceed when the count reaches zero; it cannot be reset. Counting threads do not wait, and one thread may count down several times. Typical uses: wait for N parallel tasks, or block startup until components are ready. `CyclicBarrier` is for a fixed number of parties that each do their own part and then wait for all the others; when the last one arrives, an optional barrier action runs once and the barrier resets for the next round. It fits phased batch work where nobody may start phase two until everyone finishes phase one.","`Semaphore` is a permit counter. `acquire()` takes a permit or blocks, `release()` returns one, so with N permits at most N threads are inside. Unlike a lock it has no owner: any thread may release, so a missed or doubled `release()` silently changes your limit. It is the tool for bulkheading a scarce resource: capping concurrent calls to a partner API, limiting memory-heavy report generation, or limiting in-flight calls per downstream on virtual threads, where no pool size does that job anymore. Use `tryAcquire(timeout)` in request paths so callers fail fast when the resource is saturated.","Failure behavior is where they really differ. If a task that should count down a latch throws before `countDown()`, waiters block forever, so count down in `finally` and always use `await(timeout)` in production. A barrier is all or nothing: if a party is interrupted or times out, or the barrier action throws, every waiter gets `BrokenBarrierException`; that is good for fail-fast phases, but one slow party stalls everyone. A semaphore leaks permits if `release()` is not in `finally`, and capacity shrinks until the service stalls. In new code, `CompletableFuture.allOf` or `invokeAll` often replaces a latch, and `Phaser` handles a changing number of parties."],"keyPoints":["latch: wait for N events, one shot","barrier: N parties wait for each other, reusable, breaks as a group","semaphore: at most N concurrent holders, release in finally"],"examples":[{"title":"Latch for a parallel warm-up, semaphore as a bulkhead for a partner API","lang":"java","code":"// CountDownLatch: block readiness until every cache region has loaded\npublic boolean warmUp(List<CacheRegion> regions) throws InterruptedException {\n    CountDownLatch loaded = new CountDownLatch(regions.size());\n    for (CacheRegion region : regions) {\n        executor.execute(() -> {\n            try {\n                region.load();\n            } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n                log.error(\"warm-up failed for {}\", region.name(), e);\n            } finally {\n                loaded.countDown();  // in finally, or one failure blocks readiness forever\n            }\n        });\n    }\n    return loaded.await(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);  // false: report not ready, do not hang\n}\n\n// Semaphore: at most 10 concurrent calls to a partner limited to 10 parallel requests\nprivate final Semaphore fxPermits = new Semaphore(10);\n\npublic FxQuote quote(String currencyPair) throws InterruptedException {\n    if (!fxPermits.tryAcquire(200, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {\n        throw new PartnerBusyException(\"fx provider saturated\");  // maps to 503 upstream\n    }\n    try {\n        return fxClient.quote(currencyPair);\n    } finally {\n        fxPermits.release();  // only after a successful acquire\n    }\n}","note":"The `try` starts after `tryAcquire` succeeds; releasing a permit you never acquired silently raises the limit. With virtual threads this semaphore, not the thread count, is what protects the partner."},{"title":"CyclicBarrier for phased settlement across partitions","lang":"java","code":"int partitions = 4;\nCyclicBarrier phaseDone = new CyclicBarrier(partitions,\n        () -> log.info(\"phase complete for all {} partitions\", partitions));  // runs once per round\n\nfor (int p = 0; p < partitions; p++) {\n    int partition = p;\n    executor.submit(() -> {\n        try {\n            settlement.loadTransactions(partition);\n            phaseDone.await(5, TimeUnit.MINUTES);  // nobody nets until everyone has loaded\n\n            settlement.computeNetPositions(partition);\n            phaseDone.await(5, TimeUnit.MINUTES);  // barrier reset automatically: second round\n\n            settlement.postToLedger(partition);\n        } catch (BrokenBarrierException | TimeoutException e) {\n            log.error(\"partition {} aborted: another partition failed\", partition);\n            settlement.rollback(partition);\n        } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n            phaseDone.reset();  // break the barrier now: others stop waiting\n            settlement.rollback(partition);\n            throw e;\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n        }\n        return null;\n    });\n}","note":"A failure before the last `await` stops every partition before anything is posted: `reset()` or a timeout breaks the barrier and the others get `BrokenBarrierException`. The pool needs at least four threads, or waiting parties starve the one that never ran."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How is a Semaphore with one permit different from a lock?","a":"A lock has an owner: only the holder may unlock, and a `ReentrantLock` owner can reacquire it. A binary semaphore has no owner and is not reentrant, so the same thread calling `acquire()` twice deadlocks itself, and any thread may `release()`. Use a lock for mutual exclusion and a semaphore for counting access to a resource or signaling between threads."},{"q":"When would you use Phaser instead?","a":"When the number of parties changes or phases are open ended: parties `register()` and `arriveAndDeregister()` dynamically, and one `Phaser` covers many phases, effectively a latch and barrier combined. For a fixed group and fixed phases, `CyclicBarrier` is simpler to read."}],"extra":["**Starting gun for tests:** a `CountDownLatch(1)` that all workers await, released once all have started, makes race tests far more likely to collide.","**Fairness:** `new Semaphore(n, true)` grants permits in FIFO order and prevents starvation, at some throughput cost; the default is non-fair, and `tryAcquire()` without a timeout barges even on a fair semaphore.","**Single JVM only:** all three coordinate threads in one process; with several pods, a 10-permit semaphore per pod allows 10 times the pod count, so partner limits need a gateway or distributed rate limiter.","**Resilience4j:** its semaphore bulkhead is this pattern plus metrics and configuration.","**Barrier deadlock:** parties waiting at a barrier occupy their threads, so running more parties than pool threads means the last parties never start and everyone waits until the timeout."],"redFlags":["Treats CountDownLatch as resettable or confuses it with CyclicBarrier","Calls countDown() or release() outside finally","Uses await() without a timeout in production code"]},{"id":56,"category":"Concurrency","question":"A config object is built on one thread and read by request threads. What guarantees they never see it half-initialized?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Immutable objects cannot be corrupted by concurrent writes, so they need no locking, and safe publication (final fields, volatile, locks or concurrent collections) guarantees other threads see them fully initialized.","answer":["Most concurrency bugs involve shared **mutable** state. An immutable object (all fields `final`, no setters, no leaked mutable internals, no `this` escape) has exactly one state, so any number of threads can share, cache and use it as a map key without synchronization. Updates become \"build a new object and swap the reference\", which turns a multi-field invariant into a single reference write.","Visibility is the other half. Finishing a constructor does not by itself make an object's state visible to other threads: store a reference to a mutable object in a plain field and another thread may see the reference but stale or default field values, because writes can be reordered. **Safe publication** creates the happens-before edge: initialize in a static initializer, store the reference in a `volatile` field or `AtomicReference`, store it in a `final` field of a properly constructed object, publish under a lock that readers also take, or hand it over through a concurrent collection or `BlockingQueue`.","`final` fields get special treatment in the JMM: if `this` does not escape during construction, any thread that sees the reference sees the final fields, and objects reachable through them, as initialized. Records give you final fields for free but are shallow: a `List` component still needs `List.copyOf`. In practice: immutable value objects and config snapshots, published through a volatile reference, with defensive copies at the boundaries."],"keyPoints":["an object nobody can change needs no locks for its readers","values assigned to final fields in the constructor are visible to any thread that sees the reference","publishing through a volatile field, a lock or a concurrent collection gives the same guarantee","without one of these, another thread may see default field values"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: publishing FX rates to request threads","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: mutable object published through a plain field\nclass MutableFxRates {\n    Map<String, BigDecimal> rates = new HashMap<>();\n}\n\nclass FxRatesHolderBad {\n    MutableFxRates current = new MutableFxRates();     // no volatile, no lock\n\n    void reload(Map<String, BigDecimal> fresh) {\n        MutableFxRates next = new MutableFxRates();\n        next.rates.putAll(fresh);                       // readers may see the reference before these writes\n        current = next;\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: immutable snapshot published through a volatile reference\nrecord FxRates(Map<String, BigDecimal> rates, Instant loadedAt) {\n    FxRates {\n        rates = Map.copyOf(rates);                      // defensive, unmodifiable copy\n    }\n}\n\nclass FxRatesHolder {\n    private volatile FxRates current = new FxRates(Map.of(), Instant.EPOCH);\n\n    void reload(Map<String, BigDecimal> fresh) {\n        current = new FxRates(fresh, Instant.now());    // one swap of a complete snapshot\n    }\n\n    BigDecimal rate(String currencyPair) {\n        return current.rates().get(currencyPair);\n    }\n}","note":"In the BAD version a reader can observe the new holder with a partially filled or even null map. In the GOOD version readers never lock and always see one consistent snapshot, rates and timestamp together."},{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: this escaping from a constructor","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: this escapes before construction finishes\nclass SettlementListener implements Consumer<SettlementEvent> {\n    private final Map<String, SettlementEvent> pending;\n\n    SettlementListener(EventBus bus) {\n        bus.subscribe(this);                         // another thread may call accept() right now\n        this.pending = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();    // still null as seen by that thread\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void accept(SettlementEvent event) {\n        pending.put(event.paymentId(), event);       // NullPointerException in the race window\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: finish construction first, then publish from a factory method\nfinal class SafeSettlementListener implements Consumer<SettlementEvent> {\n    private final Map<String, SettlementEvent> pending = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    private SafeSettlementListener() { }\n\n    static SafeSettlementListener subscribeTo(EventBus bus) {\n        SafeSettlementListener listener = new SafeSettlementListener();\n        bus.subscribe(listener);\n        return listener;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void accept(SettlementEvent event) {\n        pending.put(event.paymentId(), event);\n    }\n}","note":"`EventBus` stands for any in-house event dispatcher. The final field guarantee only holds for objects whose `this` did not escape, so the private constructor plus factory is the robust fix."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is double-checked locking broken without `volatile` on the instance field?","a":"Without `volatile`, the write of the reference can become visible before the writes made by the constructor, so a second thread passing the unsynchronized first check may see a non-null instance with default field values. `volatile` adds the happens-before edge that orders construction before publication. The initialization-on-demand holder idiom avoids the issue entirely by letting class initialization do the locking."},{"q":"How could a constructor that registers `this` as a listener expose a broken object?","a":"The event source can call the listener from another thread before the constructor finishes, so that thread may see unset fields, and the final-field guarantee no longer applies. Overridable methods called from a constructor are a similar trap, since the subclass override runs before subclass fields are set. Register from a static factory after construction, or in a separate `start()` step such as Spring's `@PostConstruct`."}],"extra":["**Unmodifiable view vs copy:** `Collections.unmodifiableList(list)` is a read-only view that still changes when the backing list changes; `List.copyOf` and `Map.copyOf` create real immutable copies and reject nulls.","**Records are shallow:** a record with a `List`, array or `Date` component is only as immutable as that component; normalize in the compact constructor and prefer `java.time` types over `Date`.","**Effectively immutable objects:** an object never modified after publication, such as a fully built `HashMap` handed to readers through a volatile field, is safe to read concurrently as long as the publication itself is safe.","**this-escape lint:** JDK 21 added `javac -Xlint:this-escape`, which warns when a constructor can leak `this` to subclass-overridable code before the subclass is initialized.","**Copy-on-write for read-mostly data:** an immutable snapshot in an `AtomicReference` updated with `updateAndGet`, or a `CopyOnWriteArrayList`, suits routing tables, feature flags and listener lists where reads vastly outnumber writes."],"redFlags":["writes double-checked locking without `volatile`","assumes writes become visible to other threads just because time has passed","starts a thread from the constructor that reads the object's fields"]},{"id":57,"category":"Concurrency","question":"A request counter on `AtomicLong` burns CPU under heavy contention. Would `LongAdder` help, and what would you lose by switching?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"For a hot, write-heavy metric choose `LongAdder`: it stripes updates across cells to avoid CAS contention, but `sum()` is not an atomic snapshot, so exact values and conditional updates stay on `AtomicLong`.","answer":["`AtomicLong` holds one value updated with atomic instructions (`incrementAndGet`, `compareAndSet`, `updateAndGet`). Every update targets the same cache line, so with many cores hammering it the line bounces between CPUs and CAS retries pile up; throughput flattens or even drops as you add threads. In exchange you get an exact value at every moment and conditional updates.","`LongAdder` (built on `Striped64`) keeps a `base` value plus a lazily created array of padded cells. Uncontended updates go to `base`; once a CAS on it fails, threads are hashed to separate cells, so increments rarely collide. `sum()` adds base and cells without locking, so it is **not an atomic snapshot**: concurrent increments may or may not be included, and there is no `compareAndSet`. It also costs more memory per counter.","When a counter is hammered by many threads (requests per endpoint, bytes processed, cache hits) and only read now and then by a metrics scraper, `LongAdder` is the right choice. For sequence numbers, rate-limit budgets, or anything where you check a value and then act on it, use `AtomicLong`, a lock or the database. Using `LongAdder` for an account balance is the red flag. Under low contention the difference is negligible, so change existing code only when a profiler or JMH benchmark shows the counter is a hotspot."],"keyPoints":["contended compare-and-swap retries on one variable waste CPU","striped cells let each thread bump its own slot, summed on read","a read can miss increments that are still in flight","anything needing a check before the update stays with `AtomicLong` or a lock"],"examples":[{"title":"Per-endpoint request metric with LongAdder","lang":"java","code":"@Component\npublic class EndpointMetrics {\n\n    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, LongAdder> requests = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    // Hot path: called on every request from hundreds of threads\n    public void record(String endpoint) {\n        requests.computeIfAbsent(endpoint, k -> new LongAdder()).increment();\n    }\n\n    // Cold path: called by the metrics scraper, an approximate total is fine\n    public Map<String, Long> snapshot() {\n        Map<String, Long> result = new TreeMap<>();\n        requests.forEach((endpoint, adder) -> result.put(endpoint, adder.sum()));\n        return result;\n    }\n}","note":"`computeIfAbsent` makes counter creation atomic, and the increment itself rarely contends. The snapshot may miss increments that are in flight, which is irrelevant for a metric scraped every few seconds."},{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: an invariant needs CAS, not an adder","lang":"java","code":"public class CreditLine {\n\n    private final long limitCents;\n\n    public CreditLine(long limitCents) {\n        this.limitCents = limitCents;\n    }\n\n    // BAD: sum() is not a snapshot and check-then-add is not atomic, so two threads can overdraw\n    private final LongAdder usedBad = new LongAdder();\n\n    public boolean reserveBad(long amountCents) {\n        if (usedBad.sum() + amountCents <= limitCents) {\n            usedBad.add(amountCents);\n            return true;\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: CAS loop enforces used <= limit atomically\n    private final AtomicLong used = new AtomicLong();\n\n    public boolean reserve(long amountCents) {\n        while (true) {\n            long current = used.get();\n            long next = current + amountCents;\n            if (next > limitCents) {\n                return false;\n            }\n            if (used.compareAndSet(current, next)) {\n                return true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"In a real system the authoritative credit line lives in the database behind a row lock or version column; an in-memory CAS like this fits a per-instance budget or rate limiter."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does `LongAdder` cost compared with `AtomicLong` when contention is low?","a":"With no contention it behaves much like `AtomicLong`, updating a single base field with CAS. Cells are allocated only after CAS failures, and each is padded to avoid false sharing, so a heavily contended adder can use far more memory than one `long`. For thousands of rarely updated counters, such as one per customer, plain `AtomicLong` is usually the better trade."},{"q":"Why must an overdraft check never rely on `LongAdder.sum()`?","a":"`sum()` can miss updates still in flight and there is no `compareAndSet`, so a check followed by an add lets two threads both see enough funds and both withdraw. A balance needs the read and the conditional write as one atomic step, such as an `AtomicLong.compareAndSet` loop, a lock, or better a conditional database update like `UPDATE ... WHERE balance >= ?`. Metrics tolerate slightly stale reads because increments commute; money does not."}],"extra":["**Same trick inside the JDK:** `ConcurrentHashMap` counts its size with a base count plus counter cells adapted from `LongAdder`, which is why `size()` is only an estimate while updates are in flight.","**False sharing and memory:** each cell is annotated `@Contended` so it sits on its own cache line; that padding is why an adder costs much more memory than an `AtomicLong`, which matters if you create millions of them.","**LongAccumulator:** generalizes striping to any associative, commutative function, for example `new LongAccumulator(Long::max, Long.MIN_VALUE)` to track peak latency without contention.","**sumThenReset caveat:** it is only exact when no updates race with it, so for interval metrics under load prefer cumulative counters and let Prometheus compute rates.","**Per JVM only:** both classes are in-process; across pods the metric is aggregated by the monitoring system, and a business counter that must survive restarts or be exact belongs in the database."],"redFlags":["assumes `LongAdder` is always faster, even for rarely updated values","reads `sum()` and then calls `reset()`, expecting no increments to slip between them"]},{"id":58,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"A JVM with a 1.5 GB heap uses 2 GB of RAM. Which areas explain that gap, and what lives in each?","level":"Basic","tldr":"The heap holds objects, each thread has its own stack of frames, metaspace holds class metadata, and direct and other native memory live outside the heap, so a JVM's real footprint is well beyond `-Xmx`.","answer":["The **heap** holds every object and array and is the only area the garbage collector manages; it is sized by `-Xmx` or, in containers, `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage`. Each platform **thread** has its own **stack** (`-Xss`, 1 MB by default on 64-bit Linux) of frames with local variables, operand stacks and return addresses; references in those frames point into the heap and are GC roots. Deep recursion ends in `StackOverflowError`, too many threads in `OutOfMemoryError: unable to create native thread`.","**Metaspace** holds class metadata: class structures, method bytecode, constant pools, annotations. It is native memory, unbounded by default, and grows with loaded classes, so heavy proxy generation, scripting engines or classloader leaks on redeploy show up as `OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace`. **Direct memory** is native memory allocated through `ByteBuffer.allocateDirect`, used by NIO, Netty, database drivers and compression libraries so the OS can read and write without copying through the heap.","The JVM also uses native memory for the JIT code cache, GC data structures (card tables, remembered sets, mark bitmaps), symbols, and whatever JNI libraries allocate. The production consequence: a healthy heap graph says little about the process, because container limits apply to total resident memory. Leave headroom (heap at roughly 50 to 75 percent of the container limit, depending on thread count and native usage) and use Native Memory Tracking to see where the rest goes."],"keyPoints":["objects and arrays live in the garbage-collected heap, bounded by `-Xmx`","every thread gets its own stack, so a large thread count adds noticeable native memory","loaded classes occupy metaspace, which grows with frameworks, generated proxies and redeploys","off-heap allocations by NIO buffers, Netty and native libraries are invisible to heap sizing"],"examples":[{"title":"Seeing all JVM memory areas with Native Memory Tracking","lang":"bash","code":"# Start with Native Memory Tracking (small overhead; enable when diagnosing)\njava -XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary \\\n     -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=70 \\\n     -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize=256m \\\n     -jar payments.jar &\nPID=$!\n\n# Committed memory per area: heap, class metadata, threads, code cache, GC, other\njcmd \"$PID\" VM.native_memory summary\n\n# Find growth: take a baseline, wait under load, then diff\njcmd \"$PID\" VM.native_memory baseline\nsleep 600\njcmd \"$PID\" VM.native_memory summary.diff\n\n# Compare with what the kernel (and the OOM killer) sees, in KB\nps -o rss= -p \"$PID\"\n\n# In Kubernetes a native OOM is a container kill, not a Java error\nkubectl get pod payments-7d9f -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.reason}'","note":"If RSS grows while heap and NMT totals stay flat, suspect memory NMT cannot see, such as allocations inside native libraries or malloc fragmentation."},{"title":"Memory areas, what sizes them and how each one fails","lang":"text","code":"Area            Holds                                  Sized by                           Typical failure\nHeap            objects and arrays                     -Xmx, -XX:MaxRAMPercentage         OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space\nThread stacks   frames, locals, operand stacks         -Xss per platform thread           StackOverflowError, unable to create native thread\nMetaspace       class metadata, bytecode               -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize (unbounded)   OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace\nCode cache      JIT compiled code                      -XX:ReservedCodeCacheSize          JIT stops compiling, CodeCache is full warning\nDirect memory   NIO direct ByteBuffers                 -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize            OutOfMemoryError: Direct buffer memory\nOther native    GC structures, JNI and library malloc  not capped by the JVM              container OOMKilled, exit code 137","note":"Only the first row is \"the heap\"; the rest is why RSS is always larger than `-Xmx`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you measure where the non-heap memory is going?","a":"Start the JVM with `-XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary` and run `jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary`, which splits committed memory into heap, class, thread, code, GC and internal categories; take a `baseline` and later a `summary.diff` to see what grows. Compare the total with the process RSS reported by the OS, because a large gap usually points to allocations NMT cannot see, such as native libraries calling `malloc` directly. NMT adds some overhead, so enable it deliberately rather than everywhere."},{"q":"Metaspace grows after every redeploy in the same JVM. What is the likely cause?","a":"Old classloaders are still reachable, so none of their classes can be unloaded; a lingering thread, a `ThreadLocal` value, a JDBC driver registration or a static cache is usually pinning them. Metaspace is unbounded by default, so the process keeps growing until the container is killed, or until `-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize` is reached and the JVM throws `OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace`. A heap dump that shows several instances of the same application classloader confirms it."}],"extra":["**Virtual threads:** An unmounted virtual thread's stack lives on the heap as stack chunk objects, so a million virtual threads cost heap rather than a million native stacks, and `-Xss` does not size them.","**Compressed class space:** With compressed class pointers, class structures live in a separate compressed class space inside metaspace, capped by `-XX:CompressedClassSpaceSize` (1 GB by default), which can run out before metaspace as a whole.","**Container defaults:** Without explicit flags the JVM typically sets max heap to 25 percent of the container memory limit, which wastes memory in small pods; most teams set `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` explicitly.","**glibc arenas:** Many threads allocating natively can fragment glibc malloc arenas and inflate RSS; a small `MALLOC_ARENA_MAX` or an alternative allocator such as jemalloc is a known mitigation.","**Code cache:** If the code cache fills, the JVM stops JIT compiling and throughput degrades quietly; a `CodeCache is full` warning in the logs is the only obvious symptom."],"redFlags":["sets the container memory limit equal to `-Xmx` with no headroom","says objects created in a method live on that thread's stack","has never compared process RSS with heap usage when diagnosing memory"]},{"id":59,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"StackOverflowError vs OutOfMemoryError: what causes each, and what kinds of OutOfMemoryError exist?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`StackOverflowError` means one thread's call stack is full, almost always from unbounded or very deep recursion; `OutOfMemoryError` means the JVM could not satisfy an allocation in some memory area. The message names the area (Java heap, Metaspace, direct buffer memory, native threads), and each has a different cause and fix.","answer":["Both extend `VirtualMachineError`, so they are errors, not exceptions. Each thread has its own stack of frames, sized by `-Xss` (commonly 1 MB on x86-64 Linux), and `StackOverflowError` is thrown when a call needs a frame and that stack is full. The cause is nearly always recursion without a working base case, or deep recursion over unbounded input: a recursive tree or JSON walk on a hostile payload, a cycle in an object graph that `toString`, `equals` or a serializer follows forever, or two mappers calling each other. Only the failing thread is affected, the stack unwinds, and the repeating frames in the trace point at the cycle.","`OutOfMemoryError` is thrown when an allocation fails, and the message names the area. `Java heap space`: live objects do not fit even after a full GC, because of a leak, a result set or file loaded at once, or a heap that is too small. `GC overhead limit exceeded`: the JVM spends nearly all its time collecting and recovers almost nothing. `Metaspace`: too many classes, typically runaway proxy generation or class loader leaks on redeploy. `Direct buffer memory`: off-heap NIO and Netty buffers exceeded `-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize`. `unable to create native thread`: the OS refused another thread. `Requested array size exceeds VM limit`: one array too large to allocate.","In production they call for different responses. A stack overflow is a code bug: iterate with an explicit stack or add a depth limit, cap input nesting (Jackson's `StreamReadConstraints` does this), and break object graph cycles; raising `-Xss` hides it and costs memory per thread. An OOM leaves the JVM unreliable: it can hit any thread, mid-update or holding a lock, so catching it is almost never right. Run with `-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError` and `-XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError` to get evidence and a clean restart. In containers, the kernel OOM killer can also kill the process (exit code 137) with no Java error at all, when heap plus native memory exceeds the limit."],"keyPoints":["StackOverflowError: one thread's stack is full, usually recursion","OutOfMemoryError: an allocation failed; the message names the area","do not catch OOM: dump the heap, exit and restart"],"examples":[{"title":"Recursion that overflows on bad data, and an iterative replacement","lang":"java","code":"record Category(String id, String parentId) {}\n\n// BAD: stack depth equals hierarchy depth; a cycle in the data recurses forever\nList<String> breadcrumb(String categoryId) {\n    Category category = categories.get(categoryId);\n    if (category.parentId() == null) {\n        return new ArrayList<>(List.of(category.id()));\n    }\n    List<String> path = breadcrumb(category.parentId());\n    path.add(category.id());\n    return path;  // a parent pointing back to a child: StackOverflowError\n}\n\n// GOOD: constant stack depth, explicit cycle and depth protection\nList<String> breadcrumbIterative(String categoryId) {\n    Deque<String> path = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    Set<String> seen = new HashSet<>();\n    for (String current = categoryId; current != null; current = categories.get(current).parentId()) {\n        if (!seen.add(current) || seen.size() > 64) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"cycle or excessive depth at category \" + current);\n        }\n        path.addFirst(current);\n    }\n    return List.copyOf(path);\n}","note":"Bad data, not bad luck, is the usual trigger: one corrupted parent pointer turns a working endpoint into a `StackOverflowError`. The iterative version fails with a message naming the broken record."},{"title":"JVM flags so an OOM leaves evidence and a clean restart","lang":"bash","code":"# OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space            -> leak, huge result set, heap too small\n# OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace                  -> class generation or class loader leak\n# OutOfMemoryError: Direct buffer memory       -> NIO/Netty buffers not released, limit too low\n# OutOfMemoryError: unable to create native thread -> thread leak, pids or memory limit\n\nJAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS=\"\\\n -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=70 \\\n -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize=256m \\\n -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize=256m \\\n -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError \\\n -XX:HeapDumpPath=/dumps \\\n -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError\"\n\n# Exit code 137 and no Java error: the kernel OOM killer, not the JVM.\n# Heap + metaspace + thread stacks + direct memory + code cache exceeded the limit.\nkubectl get pod payments-7d9f \\\n  -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.reason}'\n# OOMKilled","note":"Leave headroom below the container limit, because the heap is only part of the process. Put `/dumps` on a volume large enough for a dump about the size of the heap, or the evidence dies with the pod."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can you catch OutOfMemoryError and continue?","a":"Technically yes, since it is a `Throwable`, but it can surface in any thread, including one holding a lock or halfway through updating shared state, so the application may be corrupt afterwards. The narrow exception is one isolated huge allocation, such as decoding an oversized upload, and validating size up front is better even then. The production stance is dump, exit, restart, then fix from the dump."},{"q":"Why can a JVM fail with unable to create native thread while the heap is mostly empty?","a":"Thread stacks are native memory, not heap, and the OS or container also caps the number of threads or processes. The usual root cause is a thread leak, such as an executor created per request and never shut down; a thread dump with thousands of similar threads confirms it."}],"extra":["**Not exceptions:** `catch (Exception e)` does not catch either error, while `catch (Throwable t)` does, which is one reason broad `Throwable` catches are dangerous.","**Native memory tracking:** `-XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary` plus `jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary` breaks down non-heap usage when the container dies but the heap looks fine.","**Threads cost native memory:** each platform thread reserves its own stack outside the heap, so thousands of threads are a common hidden cause of container OOM kills.","**JPA and JSON cycles:** Jackson or `toString` walking a bidirectional JPA association is a classic `StackOverflowError`; return DTOs, or use `@JsonManagedReference` and `@JsonBackReference`, and keep associations out of `toString`.","**Compressed class space:** with compressed class pointers, class metadata also has its own space, so `OutOfMemoryError: Compressed class space` has the same causes as Metaspace."],"redFlags":["Treats StackOverflowError as a heap problem, or fixes it by raising -Xss","Thinks OutOfMemoryError always means the heap is too small","Catches OutOfMemoryError and lets the service carry on"]},{"id":60,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"The heap's floor after each full GC rises slowly for days. How can a garbage-collected Java service still leak memory?","level":"Basic","tldr":"GC reclaims only unreachable objects, so anything still strongly referenced (unbounded caches, static maps, forgotten listeners, uncleared ThreadLocals, pinned classloaders) is a leak the collector is not allowed to fix.","answer":["Garbage collection answers one question: is this object reachable from a GC root (live thread stacks, static fields, JNI references, held monitors)? If yes, it stays. A Java memory leak is therefore a logical leak: objects the program no longer needs but still references. The collector works correctly; the reference graph is wrong.","The usual suspects are static or singleton maps used as caches with no size limit or expiry; listeners and callbacks registered on long-lived objects and never removed; `ThreadLocal` values on pooled threads that are never cleared; collections that only grow, such as an in-memory dedup set or a Hibernate persistence context in a long batch; lambdas and inner classes capturing a large outer object; and classloader leaks, where one reference from a JVM-wide place pins an old application classloader and all its classes in metaspace after each redeploy.","The symptom is a sawtooth whose floor keeps rising: occupancy after each old or full collection grows until GC runs back to back and you hit `OutOfMemoryError`. Raising `-Xmx` only delays the crash and makes each dump bigger. Prove retention instead: take a heap dump, or two some time apart, find the dominators and their path to GC roots, and fix the owning reference with bounds, expiry, explicit cleanup, or weak references where the semantics allow."],"keyPoints":["the collector frees only what no live reference chain can reach","one forgotten reference from a long-lived root keeps a whole object graph alive","usual culprits are unbounded maps, listeners never deregistered and `ThreadLocal` values on pooled threads","the signature is a rising post-GC baseline, not high usage alone"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: unbounded static cache vs bounded cache with expiry","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: static map as a cache, one entry per pair and day forever, never evicted\npublic class StaticRateCache {\n    private static final Map<String, RateSnapshot> CACHE = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    private final RateProvider provider;\n\n    public StaticRateCache(RateProvider provider) { this.provider = provider; }\n\n    public RateSnapshot get(String pair, LocalDate day) {\n        return CACHE.computeIfAbsent(pair + \":\" + day, k -> provider.fetch(pair, day));\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: bounded size, expiry and stats you can export as metrics\n@Component\npublic class RateCache {\n    private final RateProvider provider;\n    private final Cache<String, RateSnapshot> cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()\n            .maximumSize(50_000)\n            .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofHours(6))\n            .recordStats()\n            .build();\n\n    public RateCache(RateProvider provider) { this.provider = provider; }\n\n    public RateSnapshot get(String pair, LocalDate day) {\n        return cache.get(pair + \":\" + day, k -> provider.fetch(pair, day));\n    }\n}","note":"Every cache needs an answer to \"when does an entry leave?\"; if the answer is never, it is a leak with a delay."},{"title":"ThreadLocal on pooled request threads: always remove in finally","lang":"java","code":"public final class TenantContext {\n    private static final ThreadLocal<Tenant> CURRENT = new ThreadLocal<>();\n    public static void set(Tenant tenant) { CURRENT.set(tenant); }\n    public static Tenant get() { return CURRENT.get(); }\n    public static void clear() { CURRENT.remove(); }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass TenantFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {\n    @Override\n    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)\n            throws ServletException, IOException {\n        TenantContext.set(Tenant.fromHeader(req.getHeader(\"X-Tenant-Id\")));\n        // BAD: calling chain.doFilter(req, res) without cleanup leaves the tenant on the pooled thread\n        try {\n            chain.doFilter(req, res);\n        } finally {\n            TenantContext.clear();   // GOOD: removed even when the request throws\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Without `remove()`, the value stays on a Tomcat worker thread for the life of the application, retaining memory and possibly exposing one tenant's context to the next request."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you build a cache that cannot grow without limit?","a":"Use a bounded cache such as Caffeine with `maximumSize` or `maximumWeight` plus `expireAfterWrite`, so entries are evicted by size and age instead of living forever in a `HashMap`. Export its hit rate and eviction metrics to confirm the bound fits the workload. Weak or soft references are a poor substitute, because soft references make eviction depend on GC pressure, which produces unpredictable latency."},{"q":"Why can a forgotten event listener keep a large object graph alive?","a":"The publisher holds a strong reference to each registered listener, and the listener often references its enclosing component, its caches and the data it was built for. If the component is discarded without unregistering, everything it reaches stays live for as long as the publisher does, which is often the whole application lifetime. Pair every registration with a deregistration in a lifecycle hook, such as `@PreDestroy` or a `close()` method."}],"extra":["**ThreadLocalMap internals:** Entries have weak keys (the `ThreadLocal`) but strong values, so a value can outlive its `ThreadLocal` until stale entries happen to be expunged; on a pooled thread that may be never, and a value whose class came from an application classloader pins that loader too.","**Hibernate batch leak:** A long `@Transactional` batch keeps every loaded or saved entity in the persistence context; `flush()` and `clear()` every N rows (aligned with `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size`) or use a `StatelessSession`.","**WeakHashMap trap:** `WeakHashMap` holds keys weakly but values strongly, so a value that references its own key is never collected; soft reference caches make GC behavior unpredictable, so prefer an explicit bounded cache.","**Leaks outside the heap:** Retention also shows up in metaspace (classloaders, generated classes), direct memory (unreleased Netty buffers, which `ResourceLeakDetector` can report) and threads (executors created per request and never shut down).","**Detection:** Alert on old generation occupancy after GC and on GC time percentage, not raw heap usage, and run with `-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError` and `-XX:HeapDumpPath` pointing at a volume that survives the pod restart."],"redFlags":["treats rising heap between collections as proof of a leak","proposes `System.gc()` calls or scheduled restarts as the fix","cannot name a single concrete retention path, such as a static field"]},{"id":61,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"Two services are slow: one has busy cores, the other has idle cores and long response times. How do you treat each?","level":"Basic","tldr":"CPU-bound systems show hot runnable threads and saturated cores, fixed by doing less work or adding compute; I/O-bound systems show waiting threads and downstream latency, fixed with timeouts, batching, caching and bounded concurrency.","answer":["**CPU-bound**: CPU is near its limit (or the container is throttled), thread dumps show many threads RUNNABLE in application frames, a CPU profile has clear hot spots, and throughput plateaus while latency climbs with load. Adding threads beyond the core count only adds context switches and cache misses. The remedies are to do less work (better algorithms and data structures, less serialization, logging and allocation, caching computed results) and then to scale out or up. Bounded pools sized near the core count suit CPU work.","**I/O-bound**: CPU is moderate or low while latency is high; threads wait on sockets, JDBC calls, `HikariPool.getConnection()` or HTTP clients; a wall-clock profile shows time in I/O; and your latency tracks a dependency's latency or saturation. Here more concurrency can help, and virtual threads make waiting cheap, but only up to what the dependency can absorb; beyond that you just move the queue downstream and slow everyone down.","I/O remedies target the dependency relationship: timeouts and deadlines on every call, connection pooling and reuse, batching and fewer round trips (N+1 queries are the classic), caching, bulkheads and circuit breakers, and fixing the dependency itself, such as a missing index or an undersized downstream service. Latency is rarely purely an application issue, so use distributed tracing to see where the time actually goes before optimizing code."],"keyPoints":["saturated cores with RUNNABLE threads point to computation, so cut work or add compute","idle cores with parked or socket-waiting threads point to waiting on something external","more threads help waiting work only up to what the downstream system can absorb","for waiting-bound paths, timeouts, batching, caching and fixing the slow dependency pay off most"],"examples":[{"title":"Different concurrency tools for CPU work and for I/O fan-out","lang":"java","code":"// CPU-bound: a bounded pool near the core count; more threads only add context switches\nprivate final ExecutorService scoringPool =\n        Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());\n\n// I/O-bound: virtual threads make waiting cheap, a semaphore protects the dependency\nprivate final Semaphore bureauPermits = new Semaphore(50);   // what the credit bureau can take\n\npublic List<CreditReport> fetchReports(List<Applicant> applicants) throws InterruptedException {\n    try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n        List<Future<CreditReport>> futures = applicants.stream()\n                .map(a -> executor.submit(() -> fetchLimited(a)))\n                .toList();\n        List<CreditReport> reports = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (Future<CreditReport> f : futures) {\n            try {\n                reports.add(f.get(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS));\n            } catch (TimeoutException e) {\n                f.cancel(true);                          // interrupt the stuck call, do not hang\n                reports.add(CreditReport.unavailable());\n            } catch (ExecutionException e) {\n                reports.add(CreditReport.unavailable());\n            }\n        }\n        return reports;\n    }\n}\n\nprivate CreditReport fetchLimited(Applicant a) throws InterruptedException {\n    bureauPermits.acquire();\n    try {\n        return bureauClient.fetch(a.personalNumber());   // client has its own connect and read timeouts\n    } finally {\n        bureauPermits.release();\n    }\n}","note":"The I/O path gets cheap concurrency, a cap matched to the downstream capacity, and timeouts that degrade instead of blocking; the CPU path gets no more threads than cores."},{"title":"Telling the two apart from signals you already have","lang":"text","code":"Signal                    CPU-bound                              I/O-bound\nProcess CPU               near 100% of cores or of the limit     low to moderate\nThread dumps              RUNNABLE in app code, same frames      WAITING or TIMED_WAITING, socket reads, getConnection\nCPU profile               clear hot methods                      little to see\nWall-clock profile        same as CPU profile                    time in socket read, JDBC, pool acquire\nLatency vs load           rises once cores saturate              tracks downstream latency and pool waits\nPool metrics              normal                                 pending above 0, acquire time up\nAdding threads            makes it worse                         helps until the dependency saturates\nMain fix                  less work, better code, more cores     timeouts, batching, caching, fix the dependency","note":"Use the CPU profile and the wall-clock profile together: a method that is hot in wall-clock but absent in CPU mode is waiting, not computing."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Would virtual threads help either service?","a":"They help the waiting service when its throughput is capped by the number of platform threads blocked on I/O, because a blocked virtual thread releases its carrier thread and they are cheap to create by the thousand. They do nothing for the CPU-bound service, which is limited by cores, and they do not make the downstream dependency any faster, so you still need connection limits and timeouts. Watch for pinning, for example blocking inside `synchronized` blocks on JDK versions before 24, which ties up carrier threads."},{"q":"How many worker threads would you give each of the two services, and how would you check the number?","a":"For CPU-bound tasks, start near the number of available cores (`Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors()`, which respects container limits), since more threads only add context switching. For I/O-bound tasks, a common estimate is cores times (1 + wait time / compute time), capped by what the downstream system and its connection pool can handle. Validate either choice with load tests and queue metrics rather than trusting the formula."}],"extra":["**Socket reads look RUNNABLE:** A platform thread blocked in a socket read usually shows as RUNNABLE in a native frame (for example `sun.nio.ch.Net.poll` or `socketRead0`), so counting states misleads; read the top frames.","**Throttling masquerades as waiting:** A container hitting its CPU quota can show moderate average CPU but long stalls; check `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total` before blaming a dependency.","**Virtual thread pinning:** On Java 21, blocking inside `synchronized` pins the carrier thread, which can starve I/O-bound virtual threads; JDK 24 (JEP 491) removed most of that pinning, and JFR's `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` event reveals it on 21.","**USE method:** For each resource check utilization, saturation and errors (CPU run queue, pool pending count, disk queue); saturation metrics show where the queue actually is.","**Queueing reality:** Near full utilization, queueing makes latency climb steeply, so keep critical resources, CPU or connections, well below 100 percent on latency-sensitive paths."],"redFlags":["suggests virtual threads or reactive code as a fix for CPU-heavy work","raises downstream concurrency without checking that the dependency can take it","judges the bottleneck from fleet-wide average CPU instead of per-instance and per-thread data"]},{"id":62,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"How does generational garbage collection work, and how do G1, ZGC and Parallel GC differ?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Most objects die young, so new objects go into a young generation that is collected often by copying out the few survivors, and long-lived objects are promoted to an old generation collected less often. Parallel GC maximizes throughput with stop-the-world pauses, G1 meets a pause-time goal with region-based incremental collection, and ZGC works almost entirely concurrently for sub-millisecond pauses.","answer":["Most backend allocations are short-lived: request DTOs, JSON buffers, iterators, temporary strings. Collectors exploit this by splitting the heap. Threads allocate in the young generation (eden), each in its own thread-local allocation buffer, so allocation is a lock-free pointer bump. When eden fills, a minor collection copies the live objects to a survivor space and frees the rest of eden at once, so its cost depends on survivors, not on the amount of garbage. Objects that survive several collections are promoted to the old generation. Write barriers record references from old to young objects, so a minor collection need not scan the whole old generation.","Parallel GC uses all cores for stop-the-world young and full collections: the best throughput and lowest overhead, but pauses grow with the live set, which suits batch work. G1 divides the heap into equal regions (young, old or humongous), collects young regions in short stop-the-world copying pauses, marks the old generation concurrently, then runs mixed collections that also evacuate the old regions with the most garbage, sizing each pause to meet `-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis` (200 ms by default). ZGC marks and relocates concurrently using colored pointers and barriers, so pauses stay below a millisecond even on very large heaps. The price is CPU for barriers and concurrent threads, plus memory headroom: if allocation outruns collection, threads stall. Generational ZGC arrived in Java 21 behind `-XX:+ZGenerational` and is now the only ZGC mode, which cut its overhead considerably.","Choose from the goal, then measure. For latency-sensitive APIs with moderate heaps, G1 is the default on server-class machines and usually needs only a correct heap size. When GC pauses dominate p99 latency, or heaps reach tens of gigabytes, use ZGC. For batch jobs where pauses do not matter, Parallel GC does the same work with less CPU. In small containers (under two CPUs or about 1.8 GB), current LTS JVMs do not treat the machine as server-class and fall back to Serial GC, so set the collector explicitly. Size the heap first, reduce allocation in hot paths second, and only then tune collector flags, verifying with GC logs and latency percentiles under realistic load."],"keyPoints":["most objects die young: copy survivors, free eden in bulk","Parallel: throughput; G1: pause goal; ZGC: concurrent, tiny pauses","choose by latency vs throughput goal, verify with GC logs"],"examples":[{"title":"Choosing collectors per workload","lang":"bash","code":"# Latency-sensitive REST API, 4 GB container: G1\njava -XX:+UseG1GC -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=70 -XX:MaxGCPauseMillis=100 \\\n     -Xlog:gc*:file=/logs/gc.log:time,uptime:filecount=5,filesize=20m \\\n     -jar orders-api.jar\n\n# Large heap and strict p99 (risk scoring, big in-memory caches): ZGC\n# On Java 21 also pass -XX:+ZGenerational; on Java 25 ZGC is always generational\njava -XX:+UseZGC -Xmx32g -Xlog:gc:file=/logs/gc.log:time,uptime \\\n     -jar risk-scoring.jar\n\n# Nightly settlement batch: throughput first, pauses irrelevant\njava -XX:+UseParallelGC -Xmx8g -jar settlement-batch.jar\n\n# Check which collector and heap size the JVM actually picked in the container\njava -XX:+PrintFlagsFinal -version | grep -E 'Use(G1|Z|Parallel|Serial)GC |MaxHeapSize'","note":"The last command catches the classic surprise: a pod with one CPU or a small memory limit running Serial GC. Check what the JVM chose instead of assuming."},{"title":"Allocation rate in a hot path drives young GC frequency","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: per tick, a String copy of the payload, a List of boxed Longs and an eager log string\npublic void onPriceTick(byte[] payload) throws IOException {\n    String json = new String(payload, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\n    PriceTick tick = objectMapper.readValue(json, PriceTick.class);\n    List<Long> levels = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (long level : tick.levels()) {\n        levels.add(level);  // one Long object per level\n    }\n    log.debug(\"tick \" + tick.symbol() + \" levels \" + levels);  // built even when debug is off\n    book.update(tick.symbol(), levels);\n}\n\n// GOOD: same behavior, far fewer short-lived objects\npublic void onPriceTick(byte[] payload) throws IOException {\n    PriceTick tick = tickReader.readValue(payload);  // reused ObjectReader, parses bytes directly\n    log.debug(\"tick {} levels {}\", tick.symbol(), tick.levels().length);\n    book.update(tick.symbol(), tick.levels());  // primitive long[] passed through\n}","note":"At 50,000 ticks per second these objects all die young, which is cheap per object but fills eden fast and means more frequent young pauses. Confirm with JFR allocation profiling before optimizing; most code does not need this."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is a humongous object in G1, and why can it cause trouble?","a":"An object of at least half a region, such as a large byte array, is allocated directly into contiguous humongous regions that belong to the old generation. Many of them waste space and fragment the heap, and can force extra collections or a full GC when no contiguous regions are free. Stream instead of building huge arrays, or raise `-XX:G1HeapRegionSize`."},{"q":"If ZGC pauses are so short, why not use it everywhere?","a":"Its concurrent work and barriers cost CPU and some throughput, and it needs headroom so collection keeps up with allocation; on small, CPU-limited containers that can hurt more than G1's pauses. Batch jobs finish sooner on Parallel GC. Choose ZGC when pause time is the measured problem."}],"extra":["**Full GC in G1:** a stop-the-world compaction of the whole heap means concurrent marking and mixed collections could not keep up; resize the heap or reduce allocation rather than tightening the pause goal.","**Allocation stalls:** ZGC's version of a long pause is threads waiting for memory because concurrent collection fell behind; GC logs report it, and the fix is headroom or less allocation.","**Pauses are not the whole cost:** time-to-safepoint and GC threads competing for a container's CPU quota add latency that pause times alone do not show.","**Other collectors:** Shenandoah, available in many OpenJDK builds, is another concurrent low-pause collector; Serial suits tiny heaps; Epsilon never collects and is only for testing.","**Metrics:** Micrometer's `jvm.gc.pause` and `jvm.gc.memory.allocated` show pause distribution and allocation rate; graph them next to request latency to see whether GC actually correlates."],"redFlags":["Says GC cost grows with the amount of garbage rather than live objects","Tunes collector flags before sizing the heap or reading GC logs","Claims ZGC is always better without mentioning CPU and headroom"]},{"id":63,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"p99 latency spikes line up with GC log entries. How do you find out what is driving the pauses?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Start from GC logs, not flags: study the pause distribution and causes, allocation rate, live set, promotion and humongous allocations, correlate them with traffic, and fix allocation or retention before tuning the collector.","answer":["First establish facts. Enable unified GC logging (`-Xlog:gc*` to a rotating file) and look at the distribution of pauses, p99 and max rather than the average, because one 3 second full GC hidden among thousands of 20 ms young pauses is what users feel. For each long pause identify type and cause: young evacuation, mixed collection, remark, evacuation failure, humongous allocation, metadata threshold, or a full GC, which with G1 means concurrent collection lost the race.","Then look at the drivers. **Allocation rate** sets young collection frequency; **live set** (occupancy after a full or mixed cycle) sets marking and copying cost and tells you whether the heap is simply too small; **promotion rate** shows objects surviving long enough to reach old regions; **humongous objects** (at least half a G1 region, often large `byte[]` or `char[]` from JSON, files or exports) go straight to old regions and fragment the heap. Correlate with traffic, deploys, batch jobs and schedules, and rule out non-GC causes: slow time to safepoint, container CPU throttling of GC threads, and swapping.","Fix causes before tuning: stream large results instead of loading them, bound caches, avoid per-request copies of big payloads, fix leaks. Only then size the heap for the live set plus headroom, adjust `MaxGCPauseMillis`, or change collectors, for example generational ZGC on Java 21 for low latency on large heaps. Change one thing at a time and compare pause percentiles under the same load."],"keyPoints":["unified GC logging (`-Xlog:gc*`) reveals each pause's cause, duration and phase","high churn means frequent young pauses, while a big retained set means costly old-generation work","large array allocations in G1 and premature promotion are frequent culprits","reducing what the code allocates or keeps usually beats flipping collector settings"],"examples":[{"title":"Turning on the right logs and finding the long pauses","lang":"bash","code":"# Unified GC and safepoint logging, rotated, with wall clock and uptime\nJAVA_OPTS=\"-Xlog:gc*,safepoint:file=/logs/gc.log:time,uptime,level,tags:filecount=10,filesize=50m\"\n\n# The 20 longest pauses and their kinds\ngrep -E \"Pause (Young|Full|Remark|Cleanup)\" /logs/gc.log | awk '{print $NF, $0}' | sort -n | tail -20\n\n# Red flags with G1: full collections and humongous allocations\ngrep -c \"Pause Full\" /logs/gc.log\ngrep \"Humongous\" /logs/gc.log | tail -5\n\n# Live view: old generation occupancy and GC counts every second\njstat -gcutil \"$PID\" 1000\n\n# Allocation by stack trace tells you what to change in the code\njcmd \"$PID\" JFR.start settings=profile duration=5m filename=/logs/gc-investigation.jfr","note":"Analyzers such as GCeasy, GCViewer or JDK Mission Control turn the same log into pause histograms, allocation and promotion rates."},{"title":"Reading G1 pause lines (illustrative)","lang":"text","code":"[2026-09-20T10:15:02.118+0000] GC(4211) Pause Young (Normal) (G1 Evacuation Pause) 3890M->1204M(6144M) 38.412ms\n[2026-09-20T10:15:07.903+0000] GC(4212) Pause Young (Concurrent Start) (G1 Humongous Allocation) 2510M->1650M(6144M) 61.020ms\n[2026-09-20T10:15:31.440+0000] GC(4219) Pause Young (Normal) (G1 Evacuation Pause) 5980M->5870M(6144M) 412.771ms\n[2026-09-20T10:15:33.002+0000] GC(4220) Pause Full (G1 Compaction Pause) 6120M->4810M(6144M) 2874.330ms\n\n4211: normal young pause, heap drops to about 1.2 GB: healthy.\n4212: a humongous allocation started concurrent marking: look for large arrays or JSON buffers.\n4219: almost nothing freed (5980M to 5870M) and a long pause: the live set is near the heap size.\n4220: 2.9 s stop the world compaction, still 4.8 GB live afterwards: retention or undersized heap, not a flag problem.","note":"The before and after numbers are the key: memory that does not drop after a collection is live set, and no collector flag makes live data cheaper to keep."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does an evacuation failure (To-space exhausted) in a G1 log tell you?","a":"G1 ran out of free regions to copy surviving objects into during a pause, so it had to handle the failure in place, which makes that pause much longer and often precedes a full GC. It usually means the heap is too full: the live set is close to the heap size, a burst of allocation or promotion outran concurrent marking, or large objects fragmented the free space. Reduce the live set or the allocation spikes first, then give the heap more headroom or reserve more free space with `-XX:G1ReservePercent`."},{"q":"When would switching to ZGC or Shenandoah be the right move?","a":"When the service has a strict tail latency target and measurements show the remaining pauses come from work G1 does while the application is stopped, typically evacuating a large heap. ZGC and Shenandoah do almost all of that work concurrently, keeping pauses short regardless of heap size (ZGC typically stays under a millisecond), at the cost of some throughput and extra CPU and memory headroom. They do not fix a leak or an excessive allocation rate; with too little headroom a concurrent collector stalls allocating threads instead of pausing them."}],"extra":["**G1 defaults:** The pause target `MaxGCPauseMillis` defaults to 200 ms, and G1 resizes the young generation to meet it; the humongous threshold is half a region, and region size is derived from heap size unless set with `-XX:G1HeapRegionSize`.","**Time to safepoint:** A pause can be long before GC even starts if a thread takes long to reach a safepoint; safepoint logging reports reaching time separately from the operation time.","**Container throttling:** GC worker threads share the CFS CPU quota with application threads, so a pause can stretch across throttled periods; check `nr_throttled` in the cgroup `cpu.stat` and keep GC thread counts consistent with the CPU limit.","**ZGC evolution:** Generational ZGC arrived in JDK 21 behind `-XX:+UseZGC -XX:+ZGenerational`, became the default ZGC mode in JDK 23 and the only mode in JDK 24, with sub-millisecond pauses in exchange for more CPU and memory headroom.","**Explicit GC:** Calls to `System.gc()` from libraries show up with that cause in the log and trigger full collections; `-XX:+ExplicitGCInvokesConcurrent` turns them into concurrent cycles with G1, or remove the call."],"redFlags":["assumes every long stop is GC without checking time-to-safepoint or other safepoint operations","ignores host effects such as container CPU throttling or swapping, which stretch pauses","treats a full GC in a G1 log as routine background work rather than a warning sign"]},{"id":64,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"A pod sits at full CPU while heap usage looks flat. Walk through your first ten minutes of diagnosis.","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Find which container and which threads are burning the cores, match the hottest native threads to their Java stacks, sample them several times or record a profile, then look for runaway loops, contention, heavy serialization or regex, GC and traffic shifts, all before any restart.","answer":["First confirm the symptom at the right level. Is it this JVM or a sidecar, user or system CPU, the whole node or a container at its CPU limit? Throttling shows as latency and throttled periods in the cgroup `cpu.stat`, not necessarily as 100 percent on the node. Then ask what changed: a deploy, a traffic spike, a new tenant or client, a retry storm from a failing dependency.","Next find the hot threads. `top -H -p <pid>` lists threads by CPU; convert the TID to hex and find the matching `nid` in a thread dump. Take three to five dumps a few seconds apart and, where possible, record 30 to 60 seconds with async-profiler or JFR for a flame graph. If the hot threads are GC workers, the root cause is memory pressure, not code. Capture this evidence before restarting, because a restart clears both the symptom and the clues; if you must mitigate, drain the pod first.","Typical findings: tight or infinite loops (retry without backoff, spin waits, a corrupted non thread-safe `HashMap`), catastrophic regex backtracking on user input, heavy JSON serialization or logging of large payloads, lock contention with spinning, crypto or compression on a hot path, and simply more traffic or a costly new request pattern. Mitigate first (scale out, rate limit, disable a feature flag, roll back), then fix the code with the profile as proof."],"keyPoints":["first confirm whether the JVM, a sidecar or CPU throttling explains the reading","per-thread CPU from `top -H`, plus thread dumps taken seconds apart, ties hot native threads to Java code","a sampling profiler or JFR recording ranks the methods burning the cycles","capture evidence before restarting, since a restart erases the state you need"],"examples":[{"title":"From 100 percent CPU to the guilty Java thread","lang":"bash","code":"# 1. Which process, and is the container being throttled? (cgroup v2)\ntop -b -n 1 | head -15\ncat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat            # nr_throttled, throttled_usec\n\n# 2. Hot threads inside the JVM\nPID=$(pgrep -f checkout-service.jar)\ntop -H -b -n 1 -p \"$PID\" | head -20    # note the hottest TIDs, for example 12107\n\n# 3. Map the Linux TID to the Java thread: nid is the TID in hex\nprintf 'nid=0x%x\\n' 12107              # prints nid=0x2f4b\njcmd \"$PID\" Thread.print > /tmp/td-1.txt\ngrep -A 20 'nid=0x2f4b' /tmp/td-1.txt\n\n# 4. Repeat to see what stays hot\nfor i in 2 3 4 5; do sleep 5; jcmd \"$PID\" Thread.print > /tmp/td-$i.txt; done\n\n# 5. Profile instead of guessing: 60 s CPU flame graph\nasprof -d 60 -e cpu -f /tmp/cpu.html \"$PID\"\n\n# 6. Is it GC? Back-to-back collections and a full old generation\njstat -gcutil \"$PID\" 1000 10","note":"Steps 2 and 3 connect the OS view to the Java view; step 5 gives statistical evidence that survives a restart."},{"title":"A classic CPU burner: catastrophic regex backtracking","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: nested quantifiers backtrack exponentially on input that almost matches\nprivate static final Pattern REFERENCE = Pattern.compile(\"^([A-Za-z0-9]+\\\\s?){1,64}$\");   // bounded repeat; Java 9+ already memoizes the unbounded * form\n\nboolean isValidReference(String input) {\n    return REFERENCE.matcher(input).matches();   // 40 letters then \"!\" can pin a core for a long time\n}\n\n// GOOD: unambiguous pattern and a length limit before matching\nprivate static final Pattern REFERENCE_SAFE = Pattern.compile(\"^[A-Za-z0-9]+(?: [A-Za-z0-9]+)*$\");\nprivate static final int MAX_REFERENCE_LENGTH = 140;\n\nboolean isValidReferenceSafe(String input) {\n    return input.length() <= MAX_REFERENCE_LENGTH\n            && REFERENCE_SAFE.matcher(input).matches();\n}","note":"In the BAD pattern a run of letters can be split between iterations in exponentially many ways; in the GOOD one each repetition must start with a space, so there is only one way to match."}],"followUps":[{"q":"The profile shows most time inside `java.util.regex`. What do you look for?","a":"Catastrophic backtracking: a pattern with nested or overlapping quantifiers, such as a bounded `(a+){1,64}` (Java 9+ already defuses the unbounded `(a+)+`), that explores exponentially many paths on certain inputs, often long strings that almost match. Find the pattern and the triggering input from the stack and logs, then rewrite the pattern to be unambiguous, bound the input length, or use possessive quantifiers or atomic groups that prevent backtracking. Also make sure hot patterns are compiled once into a `static final Pattern` rather than on every call through `String.matches`."},{"q":"How would you tell real CPU saturation from container CPU throttling?","a":"Check the cgroup statistics: `cpu.stat` shows `nr_throttled` and `throttled_usec` on cgroup v2 (`throttled_time` on v1), and container metrics such as `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total` expose the same data. Throttling means the pod used up its CPU quota within a scheduling period, which hurts latency even when average usage looks moderate, especially for JVMs whose GC and JIT threads burn the quota quickly. If throttling is high, raise or remove the limit or reduce parallel threads; if not, the work itself is the problem."}],"extra":["**Thread names in top:** Recent JDKs set native thread names on Linux, so `top -H` often shows truncated names such as `GC Thread#0` or `C2 CompilerThre` directly, which speeds up triage.","**JIT can be the hot thread:** Right after a deploy, C2 compiler threads can dominate CPU for a while; that is warm-up, not a bug, and it fades as compilation completes.","**Container CPU sizing:** `availableProcessors()` follows the container CPU limit and sizes GC threads, the common `ForkJoinPool` and many library pools, so a pod limited to 1 CPU on a 64 core node behaves very differently from your laptop.","**HashMap under concurrency:** Before Java 8, concurrent resizing could create a cycle and an infinite loop; Java 8 changed resizing, but unsynchronized concurrent writes still corrupt the map, so use `ConcurrentHashMap`.","**Keep the pod for diagnosis:** In Kubernetes, remove the pod from the Service by changing a label or failing readiness, so traffic moves away but the process stays alive for dumps and profiling."],"redFlags":["scales out or raises the CPU limit before knowing what consumes it","confuses container CPU throttling with the application genuinely being busy","takes one thread dump and treats whatever is RUNNABLE as the culprit"]},{"id":65,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"Memory creeps up, requests hang, and CPU climbs after a release. Which diagnostic artifact would you capture for each symptom?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A heap dump shows what retains memory, a thread dump shows what every thread is doing or waiting for right now, and a CPU or allocation profile shows where time and allocations go.","answer":["A **heap dump** is a snapshot of every object on the heap with its references. It answers memory questions: leaks, unexpected retention, a bloated cache, duplicated strings. Tools such as Eclipse MAT compute the dominator tree and retained sizes, so you see which few objects keep most of the heap alive and the path from GC roots to them. It is expensive: it pauses the JVM, the file is roughly the size of the used heap, and it contains sensitive data.","A **thread dump** shows each thread's state (RUNNABLE, BLOCKED, WAITING, TIMED_WAITING), its stack, the monitors it holds and the lock it waits for, plus detected deadlocks. It is cheap and answers hangs, deadlocks, lock contention, pool exhaustion (200 threads parked in `HikariPool.getConnection`) and, through repetition, which code is hot. One dump is a single instant; take several a few seconds apart to tell a stuck thread from a busy one.","A **CPU profile** samples stacks over time and aggregates them, usually as a flame graph, to show which methods consume CPU. Allocation profiling shows who allocates the most bytes, which explains GC pressure, and lock or wall-clock modes show waiting. JFR (low overhead, production safe) and async-profiler are the standard tools. Match the artifact to the symptom: memory growth, heap dump; hang or latency, thread dumps; high CPU or GC churn, profiler."],"keyPoints":["a heap dump is a snapshot of every live object and what keeps each one reachable","a thread dump captures each thread's stack, state and the locks it holds or waits for at one instant","a sampling profile aggregates many stack samples over a window to show where time or allocations accumulate","dumps freeze one moment, while profiles summarize behavior across many samples"],"examples":[{"title":"Collecting each artifact with JDK tools and async-profiler","lang":"bash","code":"PID=$(pgrep -f orders-service.jar)\n\n# Heap dump of live objects (triggers a full GC); large and full of PII, handle with care\njcmd \"$PID\" GC.heap_dump /dumps/orders-$(date +%s).hprof\n\n# Quick class histogram without writing a full dump\njcmd \"$PID\" GC.class_histogram | head -30\n\n# Thread dumps: several, a few seconds apart, including java.util.concurrent locks\nfor i in 1 2 3 4 5; do\n  jcmd \"$PID\" Thread.print -l > /dumps/threads-$i.txt\n  sleep 5\ndone\n\n# Virtual threads are not listed by Thread.print; dump them to a file (JDK 21)\njcmd \"$PID\" Thread.dump_to_file -format=json /dumps/vthreads.json\n\n# CPU and allocation profile: 60 s JFR recording with the profile settings\njcmd \"$PID\" JFR.start name=incident settings=profile duration=60s filename=/dumps/incident.jfr\n\n# Or an async-profiler flame graph (modes: cpu, alloc, lock, wall)\nasprof -d 30 -e cpu -f /dumps/cpu-flame.html \"$PID\"","note":"Write dumps to a volume that outlives the pod, and remove the pod from load balancing before a heap dump on a large heap."},{"title":"Reading a thread dump: a lock owner doing slow work","lang":"text","code":"\"http-nio-8080-exec-17\" #58 daemon prio=5 os_prio=0 cpu=10234.55ms elapsed=3605.12s nid=0x2f4b waiting for monitor entry\n   java.lang.Thread.State: BLOCKED (on object monitor)\n\tat com.acme.pricing.RateCache.refresh(RateCache.java:88)\n\t- waiting to lock <0x00000006c1a2b3c8> (a com.acme.pricing.RateCache)\n\tat com.acme.pricing.PricingService.quote(PricingService.java:41)\n\n\"http-nio-8080-exec-3\" #44 daemon prio=5 os_prio=0 cpu=98541.20ms elapsed=3605.40s nid=0x2f3d runnable\n   java.lang.Thread.State: RUNNABLE\n\tat java.util.regex.Pattern$Loop.match(Pattern.java:4852)\n\tat java.util.regex.Matcher.matches(Matcher.java:729)\n\tat com.acme.pricing.RateCache.parseFeed(RateCache.java:131)\n\tat com.acme.pricing.RateCache.refresh(RateCache.java:95)\n\t- locked <0x00000006c1a2b3c8> (a com.acme.pricing.RateCache)","note":"Many threads BLOCKED on the same address plus one RUNNABLE owner with high `cpu=` points to a slow critical section (here regex parsing under a lock), not a deadlock."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you capture these artifacts safely from a pod in production?","a":"Use `jcmd` inside the container: `jcmd <pid> Thread.print` for a thread dump, `jcmd <pid> GC.heap_dump /tmp/app.hprof` for a heap dump, and `jcmd <pid> JFR.start` with a duration for a recording. Write to a volume with enough space, because a heap dump is roughly the size of the live heap and a full ephemeral disk can get the pod evicted. Take the pod out of load balancing first if a long dump pause would fail health checks, and set `-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError` with a path so a crash captures one automatically."},{"q":"Why is JFR often preferred over a profiler attached ad hoc?","a":"JFR is built into the JDK, is designed to run continuously in production with low overhead (typically around 1 percent or less with the default settings), and records CPU samples, allocations, locks, GC and I/O events in one file. Because it can run all the time with a rolling buffer, you can dump the last few minutes after an incident instead of trying to reproduce it. JDK Mission Control or the `jfr` command-line tool then reads the recording."}],"extra":["**Heap dump cost:** Dumping a 16 GB heap can pause the application for many seconds and fill a disk; take it from a pod drained of traffic, and store dumps encrypted with restricted access.","**Safepoint bias:** Thread dumps and old sampling profilers observe threads only at safepoints, which skews hot spots; JFR method sampling and async-profiler avoid that bias, so their flame graphs are more trustworthy.","**Allocation profiles often beat heap dumps:** `asprof -e alloc` or JFR's `jdk.ObjectAllocationSample` events explain GC pressure directly, because a high allocation rate is a flow problem that a snapshot of retained objects cannot show.","**Continuous profiling:** Running JFR or async-profiler continuously (for example through Pyroscope or a vendor agent) means the profile from the incident window already exists instead of needing a reproduction.","**Class histogram as a first step:** `jcmd <pid> GC.class_histogram` is fast and small; two histograms minutes apart often reveal a growing class before you commit to a full heap dump."],"redFlags":["takes a heap dump of a large production heap without warning that the JVM pauses while it is written","reads every WAITING pool thread in a thread dump as stuck","copies heap dumps around freely, forgetting they contain customer data and secrets"]},{"id":66,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"How does class loading work, and what is the difference between ClassNotFoundException and NoClassDefFoundError?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Classes are loaded on demand by a hierarchy of loaders (bootstrap, platform, application) that ask their parent first, then linked and initialized on first active use. `ClassNotFoundException` is a checked exception from a lookup by name that finds nothing; `NoClassDefFoundError` is an error when a class the code was compiled against cannot be loaded at run time, including after its static initializer failed.","answer":["A class goes through loading (finding the bytes, creating the `Class`), linking (verification, default values for static fields, resolving references) and initialization (static initializers). Initialization runs lazily on first active use, such as creating an instance or calling a static method, exactly once and under a lock. The bootstrap loader loads core modules such as `java.base`, the platform loader further JDK modules such as `java.sql`, and the application loader your class path or module path. Each loader asks its parent first and searches itself only if the parent fails, so you cannot shadow `java.lang.String`. A class's identity is its name plus its defining loader: one class loaded by two loaders gives two incompatible types.","`ClassNotFoundException` is checked and comes from a lookup by name: `Class.forName`, `ClassLoader.loadClass`, or a framework loading drivers, plugins or serialized types. `NoClassDefFoundError` is an `Error` raised when the JVM resolves a reference that was valid at compile time and the class is now missing: an excluded dependency, a badly built fat jar, or conflicting library versions. Its other common cause: when a static initializer throws, the first access fails with `ExceptionInInitializerError` and the class is marked erroneous, so every later access fails with `NoClassDefFoundError: Could not initialize class`, and the root cause is in that first failure.","Application servers create a loader per application, and Tomcat's web app loader searches the application before its parent (except for Java SE classes), so apps can use different library versions, at the risk of `ClassCastException` between two same-named classes. A class loader leak happens on redeploy when anything outside the app still references one of its classes: a thread it started, a `ThreadLocal` value on a container thread, a JDBC driver registered with `DriverManager`. The old loader and all its classes stay reachable, and after a few redeploys Metaspace fills. A Spring Boot fat jar runs one application per JVM, which avoids this."],"keyPoints":["loading, linking, initialization; initialization runs once, lazily","bootstrap, platform and application loaders, parent first","CNFE: lookup by name failed; NCDFE: compiled-against class missing or failed init"],"examples":[{"title":"A failing static initializer: ExceptionInInitializerError, then NoClassDefFoundError","lang":"java","code":"final class FeeSchedule {\n    // Runs during class initialization; NullPointerException if the variable is unset\n    static final BigDecimal CARD_FEE_PERCENT = new BigDecimal(System.getenv(\"CARD_FEE_PERCENT\"));\n\n    static BigDecimal feeFor(BigDecimal amount) {\n        return amount.multiply(CARD_FEE_PERCENT).movePointLeft(2);\n    }\n}\n\nclass FeeDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= 2; attempt++) {\n            try {\n                System.out.println(FeeSchedule.feeFor(new BigDecimal(\"250.00\")));\n            } catch (Throwable t) {\n                System.out.println(attempt + \": \" + t);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n// 1: java.lang.ExceptionInInitializerError\n// 2: java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError: Could not initialize class FeeSchedule\n// FIX: read configuration into a bean (@ConfigurationProperties) and fail at startup\n//      with a clear message, not inside a static initializer.","note":"In a server the first failure may have been logged hours earlier on another request, so the error in front of you is the misleading second one; log full stack traces with causes."},{"title":"Loading by name, and finding which loader and jar a class came from","lang":"java","code":"// ClassNotFoundException: lookup by name, checked, the caller decides what to do\ntry {\n    Class.forName(\"org.postgresql.Driver\");\n} catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {\n    throw new IllegalStateException(\"PostgreSQL driver missing from the runtime class path\", e);\n}\n\n// NoSuchMethodError or NoClassDefFoundError after an upgrade: which jar won?\nClass<?> mapper = ObjectMapper.class;\nSystem.out.println(mapper.getClassLoader());\nSystem.out.println(mapper.getProtectionDomain().getCodeSource().getLocation());  // the actual jar\n\nSystem.out.println(String.class.getClassLoader());               // null: the bootstrap loader\nSystem.out.println(java.sql.Connection.class.getClassLoader());  // the platform loader\n\n// Same class name, two loaders, two different types\nClassLoader pluginA = new URLClassLoader(new URL[] {pluginJar}, null);\nClassLoader pluginB = new URLClassLoader(new URL[] {pluginJar}, null);\nClass<?> a = pluginA.loadClass(\"com.acme.payments.FeeRule\");\nClass<?> b = pluginB.loadClass(\"com.acme.payments.FeeRule\");\nSystem.out.println(a == b);  // false: identity is name plus defining loader","note":"The code source settles version conflicts fast; `mvn dependency:tree` or `gradle dependencyInsight` then shows who pulled in the wrong version."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is NoSuchMethodError, and how is it related?","a":"Another linkage error: the class was found but lacks a method the caller was compiled against, almost always because a different library version won dependency resolution. Align versions with a BOM and check the code source of the class actually loaded."},{"q":"Why does a JDBC driver or a thread started by a web app cause a class loader leak?","a":"`DriverManager` belongs to the JDK and keeps a static list of registered drivers, so a driver loaded by the web app stays referenced after undeploy, and with it the app's loader and every class it defined. A thread the app started references app classes and its context class loader. Deregister drivers and stop executors on shutdown; Tomcat warns about both."}],"extra":["**Context class loader:** `ServiceLoader` and many serialization frameworks load classes through the thread context class loader, so a wrong context loader on a pool thread causes puzzling `ClassNotFoundException`s.","**forName initializes:** `Class.forName(name)` runs static initializers, `ClassLoader.loadClass(name)` only loads, and `Class.forName(name, false, loader)` loads without initializing.","**Constants are inlined:** `static final` primitive and string constants are copied into callers at compile time, so reading them does not initialize the class, and changing them requires recompiling callers.","**Class data sharing:** CDS archives store preparsed class metadata to speed up startup without changing loading semantics.","**Module path:** reflecting into a package a module does not open throws `InaccessibleObjectException`."],"redFlags":["Treats ClassNotFoundException and NoClassDefFoundError as the same thing","Does not know a failed static initializer leads to NoClassDefFoundError","Cannot explain parent delegation or why class identity includes the loader"]},{"id":67,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"Six replicas each run 200 Tomcat threads against one PostgreSQL primary. How would you size each HikariCP pool?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Size the pool from database capacity and connection hold time (Little's law), not request threads; too small queues requests, too large overloads PostgreSQL, so watch acquire time, pending threads and DB saturation.","answer":["A connection pool is a concurrency limit on the database. The number of connections you need is throughput times the time each connection is held (Little's law): 1,000 transactions per second at 10 ms each keep about 10 connections busy on average. Hold time is the whole transaction, not only the SQL, so an HTTP call inside `@Transactional`, open session in view, or slow result processing multiplies the pool you need.","Too small a pool makes requests wait in `getConnection()`: latency rises, request threads pile up, and after `connectionTimeout` requests fail. Too large a pool adds no capacity, because PostgreSQL has a fixed number of cores and disks and each connection is a backend process with its own memory. Past the database's sweet spot, more concurrent queries mean more context switching, lock and buffer contention, and higher latency for everyone. Remember the multiplier: 30 pods with 20 connections each is 600 connections against a `max_connections` that defaults to 100.","So size from the database backwards: decide how many concurrent active queries it handles well, often a small multiple of its cores, split that across instances, and let the application queue briefly in the pool with a short timeout instead of queueing inside the database. Verify with metrics: acquire time, pending threads, active versus max on the application side, and CPU, I/O, locks and `pg_stat_activity` on the database side. Use PgBouncer in transaction mode when many instances must share a limited number of server connections."],"keyPoints":["the database's useful parallelism, not the web thread count, sets the total connection budget","per-replica pool size times replica count must stay under that budget, including during rolling deploys","required connections roughly equal throughput times how long each transaction holds a connection","pending threads and acquire latency show whether the pool, not the database, is the bottleneck"],"examples":[{"title":"Sizing a HikariCP pool with arithmetic, not guesswork","lang":"yaml","code":"# Sizing sketch for one service\n#   peak 600 transactions/s per pod, p95 connection hold time 15 ms\n#   busy connections = 600 * 0.015 = 9, plus headroom for bursts: 16\n#   8 pods * 16 = 128 connections, below PostgreSQL max_connections=200 with room for admin and migrations\nspring:\n  datasource:\n    hikari:\n      maximum-pool-size: 16\n      minimum-idle: 16                  # fixed size pool avoids churn under load\n      connection-timeout: 2000          # ms; fail fast instead of the 30 s default\n      max-lifetime: 1500000             # ms; below any proxy or firewall idle cutoff\n      leak-detection-threshold: 20000   # ms; logs a stack trace for connections held too long\n  jpa:\n    open-in-view: false                 # do not hold a connection for the whole web request\nmanagement:\n  metrics:\n    distribution:\n      percentiles:\n        hikaricp.connections.acquire: [0.5, 0.95, 0.99]","note":"Recalculate when pod count changes: autoscaling from 8 to 20 pods silently multiplies database connections by 2.5."},{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: transaction duration drives pool demand","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: a DB connection stays checked out while waiting for a remote service\n@Transactional\npublic void approveLoan(UUID applicationId) {\n    LoanApplication app = applications.findById(applicationId).orElseThrow();\n    FraudVerdict verdict = fraudClient.check(app);   // 300 ms HTTP call holding a connection\n    app.apply(verdict);\n}\n\n// GOOD: remote I/O outside the transaction, a short transaction for the write\npublic void approveLoan(UUID applicationId) {\n    LoanApplication snapshot = applications.findById(applicationId).orElseThrow();\n    FraudVerdict verdict = fraudClient.check(snapshot);            // no connection held here\n    tx.executeWithoutResult(status -> {\n        LoanApplication app = applications.findById(applicationId).orElseThrow();\n        app.apply(verdict);   // @Version on the entity detects concurrent changes\n    });\n}","note":"At 50 approvals per second, the BAD version needs about 15 connections just for waiting on HTTP; the GOOD version needs one or two. `tx` is a `TransactionTemplate`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Autoscaling doubles your pod count during a spike. What happens at the database?","a":"Every new pod opens its own pool, so total connections can double right as load peaks, and PostgreSQL may reach `max_connections` and refuse new ones, or slow down from per-connection memory and contention. Size the per-pod maximum so that the highest replica count times the pool size still fits the database's budget. When many pods or services share one database, a server-side pooler such as PgBouncer lets many client connections share a smaller number of real ones."},{"q":"Why can a smaller pool give higher throughput than a larger one?","a":"A database server has a limited number of cores and disks, so beyond a point extra concurrent queries only add context switching, lock contention and cache thrashing, and every query gets slower. A small pool keeps the database near its efficient concurrency and queues the excess in the application, where waiting is cheap. HikariCP's pool sizing guidance starts from roughly twice the core count plus the number of effective spindles and then adjusts by measurement."}],"extra":["**HikariCP's sizing advice:** Its pool sizing guide starts from roughly `(core_count * 2) + effective_spindle_count` on the database server and argues that a small pool with a queue in front beats a large one.","**Virtual threads move the limit:** With virtual threads, request concurrency is no longer capped by a thread pool, so the connection pool becomes the real limit; add a bulkhead or semaphore and a short acquire timeout so thousands of virtual threads do not queue for 30 seconds.","**Separate pools for separate workloads:** Give batch jobs and reports their own small pool or a read replica, so a long export cannot starve checkout; this is the bulkhead pattern applied to connections.","**Idle in transaction:** Sessions in state `idle in transaction` in `pg_stat_activity` reveal code holding transactions open while doing other work; `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` is a useful safety net.","**Pool deadlock:** A request that needs a second connection while holding one (for example a `REQUIRES_NEW` inner transaction) can deadlock the pool under load when every thread holds one and waits for another; avoid the pattern or size for it explicitly."],"redFlags":["forgets that every replica and every autoscaled pod opens its own pool","raises the pool size to fix slow queries, making the database slower for everyone","sets a long acquire timeout, so requests pile up instead of failing fast"]},{"id":68,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"A quick timing loop says the new parser is 40x faster. Why distrust it, and how would you measure properly?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Java code begins in the interpreter and is compiled speculatively as it gets hot, so a hand-rolled timing loop often measures warm-up, or code the JIT deleted or precomputed, rather than your logic; use JMH with warm-up iterations, several forks and representative inputs.","answer":["HotSpot runs code in the interpreter first, profiles it, compiles hot methods with C1 and then C2 using that profile (tiered compilation), inlines aggressively and optimizes speculatively, deoptimizing when an assumption breaks, for example when a new subclass is loaded or a call site becomes megamorphic. Performance therefore changes over the first seconds or minutes and depends on the history of the run. A loop timed with `System.currentTimeMillis()` mixes interpreter time, compilation, on-stack replacement and GC.","The optimizer can also delete the work you meant to measure. If a result is never used, **dead-code elimination** removes the computation; if the inputs are constants, **constant folding** computes the answer at compile time; loop unrolling and hoisting can make a single operation disappear from the loop. The result is an impressively small number that means nothing.","**JMH** exists to control this: warm-up iterations until steady state, measurement iterations, several forks (fresh JVMs) to expose run-to-run variance, returned values or `Blackhole` to defeat dead-code elimination, non-final `@State` fields to prevent constant folding, and profilers like `-prof gc` for allocation. Even then use realistic data sizes and distributions, and confirm conclusions with production-like load tests, since a microbenchmark answers only a narrow question. In production, the same warm-up explains slow first requests after every deploy."],"keyPoints":["early iterations run interpreted or lightly compiled, so they reflect startup, not steady state","the optimizer can remove or precompute work whose result is never used","a harness like JMH controls warm-up, consumes results and isolates runs in separate JVMs","inputs and data sizes should resemble production, or branch and type profiles mislead"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: a naive benchmark that measures the wrong thing","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: one JVM, one loop, wall clock time, constant input, result discarded\npublic static void main(String[] args) {\n    FeeCalculator calculator = new FeeCalculator();\n    Order order = new Order(\"ORD-1\", new BigDecimal(\"1250.00\"), \"SEK\");   // constant input\n\n    long start = System.currentTimeMillis();\n    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {\n        calculator.calculate(order);   // result unused: the JIT may remove the work\n    }\n    System.out.println(\"took \" + (System.currentTimeMillis() - start) + \" ms\");\n}","note":"This mixes interpreted and compiled execution, may time an empty loop, and a single run cannot show variance."},{"title":"GOOD: the same question asked with JMH","lang":"java","code":"@BenchmarkMode(Mode.AverageTime)\n@OutputTimeUnit(TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS)\n@Warmup(iterations = 5, time = 1)\n@Measurement(iterations = 10, time = 1)\n@Fork(3)\n@State(Scope.Benchmark)\npublic class FeeCalculatorBenchmark {\n\n    private FeeCalculator calculator;\n    private List<Order> orders;   // non-final state: nothing to constant fold\n    private int next;\n\n    @Setup(Level.Trial)\n    public void setUp() {\n        calculator = new FeeCalculator();\n        orders = SampleOrders.realisticMix(10_000, 42L);   // amounts, currencies, discounts like production\n    }\n\n    @Benchmark\n    public BigDecimal calculateFee() {\n        Order order = orders.get(next);\n        next = (next + 1) % orders.size();\n        return calculator.calculate(order);   // returned value is consumed by JMH: no dead-code elimination\n    }\n}","note":"Run with `java -jar target/benchmarks.jar FeeCalculator -prof gc` to see allocation per operation next to the time, and compare implementations only when the difference exceeds the reported error."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Your service is slow for the first minute after every deployment. What is happening and what can you do?","a":"The JIT has not yet compiled the hot paths, so early requests run interpreted or at lower tiers, and class loading and cache filling add to the delay. Common mitigations are a warm-up phase that replays representative traffic before the readiness probe passes, Class Data Sharing (AppCDS) to cut class loading time, and enough replicas that a rolling deploy never sends full load to cold pods. Newer approaches such as CRaC snapshots or the ahead-of-time cache from Project Leyden go further, but need explicit setup and a supporting JDK."},{"q":"Which JMH modes and settings would you choose for a latency-sensitive method?","a":"Use `Mode.SampleTime`, which reports percentiles, rather than only average time or throughput, with `@OutputTimeUnit` set to microseconds or nanoseconds. Give it enough `@Warmup` and `@Measurement` iterations to reach steady state, several forks, and `@Setup` at trial or iteration level so preparation stays outside the measured code. Add the GC profiler (`-prof gc`) too, because allocation rate often explains why one variant is slower under real load."}],"extra":["**Tiered levels:** Code moves from level 0 (interpreter) to level 3 (C1 with profiling) to level 4 (C2); `-XX:+PrintCompilation` shows these transitions and deoptimizations marked `made not entrant` while a benchmark runs.","**Faster warm-up in production:** Send warm-up traffic before marking a pod ready, and look at AppCDS, CRaC and the Project Leyden AOT cache (class loading and linking in JDK 24, method profiles in JDK 25) to shorten time to peak performance.","**Coordinated omission:** Load generators that wait for a slow response before sending the next request hide latency outliers; use a constant arrival rate (wrk2, Gatling open model, k6 arrival-rate executors) and report percentiles.","**Pick the right mode:** `Mode.Throughput`, `Mode.AverageTime` and `Mode.SampleTime` answer different questions; `SampleTime` reports percentiles, which matter more than averages for latency-sensitive code.","**Trust the error bars:** JMH prints a confidence interval per result; a difference smaller than the error is noise, not an improvement."],"redFlags":["benchmarks with constant inputs the compiler can fold away","draws production conclusions from a laptop run with a different JDK, GC or core count","assumes a microbenchmark speedup translates into a proportional end-to-end gain"]},{"id":69,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"How do you implement a thread-safe Singleton in Java, and which approach would you pick?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Let the JVM's class initialization do the locking: a `static final` field, the lazy holder idiom, or an enum with one constant are all thread-safe without explicit synchronization. Double-checked locking also works but only with a `volatile` field. In an application with a DI container, a singleton-scoped bean replaces all of them.","answer":["The JLS guarantees that a class is initialized exactly once, under a lock held by the JVM, and that its static initializers' writes are visible to every thread that uses the class afterwards. So `private static final RateTable INSTANCE = new RateTable();` is thread-safe, and lazy enough, because initialization happens on first use of the class. The holder idiom moves the field into a private static nested class, so the instance is created on the first `getInstance()` call and reads afterwards cost nothing. An enum with a single `INSTANCE` constant gets the same guarantee, and also survives serialization and blocks reflective construction, which is why Effective Java calls it the best approach when it fits.","Double-checked locking checks the field, synchronizes, checks again and creates. The field must be `volatile`: without it, the write of the reference can become visible before the writes made in the constructor, so another thread can see a non-null, half-constructed object. Copying the field into a local means the fast path reads the volatile once. A `synchronized` static `getInstance()` is simpler and also correct, but every call takes the lock. Both fit mainly lazily initialized instance fields, where the holder idiom does not apply.","In production, hand-rolled singletons cause their own failures. If the constructor throws during class initialization, the first caller gets `ExceptionInInitializerError` and every later caller `NoClassDefFoundError: Could not initialize class`, so the class is dead until a restart. 'One instance' really means one per class loader. Static access hides dependencies and makes tests share state. That is why, in Spring code, the answer is a singleton-scoped bean: the container creates one instance per application context, injects it through constructors, and tests replace it. I would pick a bean in Spring, and an enum or holder idiom only in code that has no container."],"keyPoints":["class initialization is the free lock","double-checked locking needs volatile","prefer a DI singleton bean"],"examples":[{"title":"Holder idiom, enum and double-checked locking","lang":"java","code":"// Holder idiom: created on the first getInstance() call, no locking afterwards\npublic final class CountryRules {\n    private CountryRules() { }\n    private static final class Holder {\n        static final CountryRules INSTANCE = new CountryRules();\n    }\n    public static CountryRules getInstance() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }\n}\n\n// Enum: serialization-safe and reflection-proof\npublic enum CurrencyRegistry {\n    INSTANCE;\n    private final Map<String, Currency> byCode = Currency.getAvailableCurrencies().stream()\n            .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableMap(Currency::getCurrencyCode, c -> c));\n    public Currency require(String code) {\n        Currency currency = byCode.get(code);\n        if (currency == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Unknown currency \" + code);\n        return currency;\n    }\n}\n\n// Double-checked locking: volatile is mandatory\npublic final class ExchangeRateClient {\n    private static volatile ExchangeRateClient instance;\n    private ExchangeRateClient() { }\n    public static ExchangeRateClient getInstance() {\n        ExchangeRateClient local = instance;            // one volatile read on the fast path\n        if (local == null) {\n            synchronized (ExchangeRateClient.class) {\n                local = instance;\n                if (local == null) {\n                    instance = local = new ExchangeRateClient();\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return local;\n    }\n}","note":"Only double-checked locking needs explicit synchronization; the others rely on class initialization. Removing its `volatile` compiles and usually passes tests, which is what makes it dangerous."},{"title":"What replaces them: a container-managed singleton you can swap in tests","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: hidden static dependency, state shared across tests, cannot be replaced\nclass RefundServiceStatic {\n    BigDecimal refundInSek(Refund refund) {\n        return ExchangeRateClient.getInstance().convert(refund.amount(), \"SEK\");\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: one instance per application context, created and closed by the container\n@Configuration\nclass ExchangeRateConfig {\n    @Bean\n    ExchangeRateClient exchangeRateClient(ExchangeRateProperties props) {\n        return new ExchangeRateClient(props.baseUrl(), props.connectTimeout());\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass RefundService {\n    private final ExchangeRateClient rates;\n\n    RefundService(ExchangeRateClient rates) {         // explicit dependency\n        this.rates = rates;\n    }\n\n    BigDecimal refundInSek(Refund refund) {\n        return rates.convert(refund.amount(), \"SEK\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Unit test: new RefundService(new FixedRateClient(new BigDecimal(\"11.42\")))\n@SpringBootTest\nclass RefundServiceIT {\n    @MockitoBean ExchangeRateClient rates;\n}","note":"The bean is still a single shared instance, so it must be thread-safe, but nothing reaches for it statically and every test decides what it gets."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why exactly does double-checked locking need volatile?","a":"Without a happens-before edge, another thread can see the stored reference before the constructor's field writes, skip the lock because the field is non-null, and use an object with default field values. A `volatile` write publishes the reference only after everything written before it, and the reader's volatile read sees all of it."},{"q":"How can a classic singleton end up with two instances?","a":"Deserialization creates a new object unless the class defines `readResolve` returning the instance; reflection can call a private constructor after `setAccessible(true)`; and each class loader that loads the class gets its own static field. An enum handles the first two: the JVM forbids creating enum instances reflectively and serializes enums by name."}],"extra":["**Static init failure:** an exception in a static initializer turns into `ExceptionInInitializerError` once, then `NoClassDefFoundError` for every later use; avoid I/O and remote calls in singleton constructors.","**Enum limits:** an enum cannot extend a class and is created at class initialization, so it does not fit instances that need runtime configuration.","**Spring singleton scope:** it means one instance per container and bean definition, not per JVM; two contexts in the same JVM have two instances.","**Thread-safety of the instance:** making creation safe says nothing about the object's methods; a singleton with mutable fields still needs immutable state, concurrent collections or locks.","**Testing static singletons:** mutated state leaks between tests, so results depend on execution order."],"redFlags":["Writes double-checked locking without volatile","Synchronizes every getInstance call without knowing alternatives","Uses static singletons in a Spring codebase"]},{"id":70,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"A teammate calls `PricingCache.getInstance()` everywhere and argues Spring beans are singletons too. What is the real difference?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Classic Singleton is global state reached through a static accessor, which hides dependencies and breaks test isolation; a Spring singleton bean is fine because it is injected explicitly, but it must be stateless or thread-safe.","answer":["The problem with the GoF Singleton is not having one instance; it is how callers get it. `TaxRates.getInstance()` is a global variable: any class can reach it without declaring the dependency, tests cannot substitute it, state leaks between tests that share a JVM, and initialization order and lifecycle (close, reload, reconfigure) are hard-coded. If it holds mutable state, every thread shares it too.","A container-managed singleton removes most of that. Spring creates one instance per application context and consumers receive it through constructor injection, so the dependency is visible in the signature, replaceable in tests with a stub or `@MockitoBean`, and its startup and shutdown are managed by the container. That is why singleton is the default scope and why it is the right choice for services, repositories and clients.","What the container does not give you is thread safety. A singleton bean is called concurrently by every request thread, so it must be stateless (only final references to thread-safe collaborators and configuration) or protect its state with immutable snapshots, concurrent data structures or proper synchronization. Request data belongs in method parameters and local variables, or in a request-scoped bean, never in a field of a singleton."],"keyPoints":["a static accessor lets any class reach the instance, so its dependencies never show up in constructors","tests cannot swap or reset the shared instance, so state leaks from one test case into the next","a container-scoped bean is still one shared object, but consumers receive it through injection","one object serving many threads must hold no per-call fields unless access is synchronized"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: request state in a singleton bean","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: request-specific state stored in fields of a singleton bean\n@Service\npublic class StatefulCreditLimitService {\n    private Customer currentCustomer;        // shared by every request thread\n    private BigDecimal requestedAmount;\n\n    public Decision decide(Customer customer, BigDecimal amount) {\n        this.currentCustomer = customer;     // thread A writes\n        this.requestedAmount = amount;\n        return evaluate();                   // thread B may have overwritten both fields\n    }\n\n    private Decision evaluate() {\n        return requestedAmount.compareTo(currentCustomer.limit()) <= 0\n                ? Decision.approve(currentCustomer.id())\n                : Decision.reject(currentCustomer.id());\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: stateless singleton, request data flows through parameters and locals\n@Service\npublic class CreditLimitService {\n    private final LimitRepository limits;    // thread-safe collaborator, assigned once\n\n    public CreditLimitService(LimitRepository limits) {\n        this.limits = limits;\n    }\n\n    public Decision decide(Customer customer, BigDecimal amount) {\n        BigDecimal limit = limits.limitFor(customer.id());\n        return amount.compareTo(limit) <= 0 ? Decision.approve(customer.id()) : Decision.reject(customer.id());\n    }\n}","note":"The BAD version passes every single-threaded test and fails only under concurrent load, where customer A can receive a decision computed with customer B's data."},{"title":"Hidden dependency vs explicit dependency","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: static accessor hides the dependency and cannot be replaced in a test\npublic class InvoiceService {\n    public Invoice issue(Order order) {\n        BigDecimal vat = TaxRates.getInstance().rateFor(order.country());\n        return Invoice.of(order, vat);\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: same single instance at runtime, but declared and injectable\n@Service\npublic class InvoiceService {\n    private final TaxRates taxRates;\n\n    public InvoiceService(TaxRates taxRates) {\n        this.taxRates = taxRates;\n    }\n\n    public Invoice issue(Order order) {\n        return Invoice.of(order, taxRates.rateFor(order.country()));\n    }\n}","note":"Both versions have exactly one `TaxRates` at runtime; only the second lets a test pass a fixed rate table and makes the dependency visible in the constructor."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you migrate a legacy `getInstance()` class toward injection without a big-bang rewrite?","a":"Extract an interface from the class and register the existing object as a Spring bean through a `@Bean` method that returns `getInstance()`, so there is still exactly one instance. Inject that interface through constructors in new and touched code, while untouched legacy callers keep using the static accessor for a while. Once the last caller is migrated, remove the accessor and the private constructor, and tests can pass in a fake implementation directly."},{"q":"Is there any case where a hand-written static singleton is still reasonable?","a":"Yes, when the object is immutable and has no dependencies and no I/O, such as a stateless comparator, a constant lookup table or `Collections.emptyList()`. An `enum` with a single constant is the idiomatic way to write one, because the JVM guarantees one instance and handles serialization. As soon as the object needs configuration, a clock, a database or a mock in tests, it should become an injected bean instead."}],"extra":["**Per context, not per JVM:** Spring singleton means one per `ApplicationContext`; a test suite with several cached contexts has several instances, and in Kubernetes every pod has its own, so an in-memory counter, lock or rate limiter in a singleton is not cluster-wide.","**Scope mismatch:** Injecting a prototype or request-scoped bean into a singleton captures one instance forever; use `ObjectProvider<T>`, a `@Lookup` method, or a scoped proxy (`@RequestScope` creates one by default).","**True JVM-wide singleton:** Outside a container, an `enum` with one constant or the holder class idiom gives lazy, thread-safe initialization without double-checked locking; still expose it through an interface and inject it where you can.","**Legitimate shared state:** Caches, rate limiters and metrics are valid state in singletons; use `ConcurrentHashMap`, `LongAdder`, `AtomicReference` holding immutable snapshots, or a bounded Caffeine cache, and document the thread-safety contract.","**ThreadLocal is not the fix:** Moving the field into a `ThreadLocal` hides the design problem and leaks across pooled threads when not cleared; prefer parameters, or `ScopedValue` on recent JDKs (preview in 21, final in 25)."],"redFlags":["suggests double-checked locking as the fix, as if the problem were lazy initialization","cannot explain how a test would substitute a fake for a `getInstance()` dependency","never mentions that static state survives across tests in the same JVM, making results order-dependent"]},{"id":71,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"A pricing `switch` grew to fifteen cases, each needing its own dependencies. When would you move to Strategy, and when keep it?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Use Strategy when the variation is real, growing and needs independent tests, dependencies or runtime selection; keep a switch (ideally an exhaustive Java 21 pattern switch) for a small, stable, closed set of branches.","answer":["A switch keeps every variant in one method. That is easy to read with three stable branches and painful once each branch grows its own rules, collaborators and tests. **Strategy** puts each algorithm behind one interface (`CreditPolicy.evaluate(app)`), so every variant is a separate class with its own dependencies, unit tests and owner, and the caller selects one at runtime by key, configuration or feature flag.","The justification is change pressure, not elegance. Strategy pays off when variants are added regularly (new segments, markets, providers), when variants need different dependencies (one policy calls a credit bureau, another reads internal limits), when you must switch at runtime or run an A/B test, or when different teams own different variants. For a stable two or three way branch with no dependencies it is overkill: a switch is more honest, and the extra indirection only hides the logic.","In Spring the strategies are beans, injected as `List<CreditPolicy>` and indexed by an explicit key each strategy declares. Build that index at startup and fail fast on duplicate or missing keys, so a misconfiguration breaks the deploy instead of silently routing a customer to the wrong policy. Avoid selection that depends on bean registration order or on the first strategy whose `supports()` returns true.","For a closed, compiler-checked set, Java 21 gives a middle ground: a sealed interface plus an exhaustive pattern switch has no `default`, and adding a variant becomes a compile error everywhere it is not handled."],"keyPoints":["each strategy can be tested and given collaborators in isolation","selection can happen at runtime from data such as segment or market","a small closed set of cases reads better as an exhaustive pattern `switch`","sealed types let the compiler flag a missing case"],"examples":[{"title":"Credit policy per segment: Strategy with a fail-fast registry","lang":"java","code":"public enum Segment { RETAIL, SME, CORPORATE }\n\npublic record CreditApplication(String customerId, Segment segment, BigDecimal amount, int score) {}\npublic record CreditDecision(boolean approved, String policy, String reason) {}\n\npublic interface CreditPolicy {\n    Segment segment();\n    CreditDecision evaluate(CreditApplication app);\n}\n\n@Component\nclass SmeCreditPolicy implements CreditPolicy {\n    public Segment segment() { return Segment.SME; }\n    public CreditDecision evaluate(CreditApplication app) {\n        boolean ok = app.score() >= 620 && app.amount().compareTo(new BigDecimal(\"500000\")) <= 0;\n        return new CreditDecision(ok, \"SME_V3\", ok ? \"within SME limits\" : \"score or amount out of range\");\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class CreditPolicyRegistry {\n    private final Map<Segment, CreditPolicy> policies;\n\n    public CreditPolicyRegistry(List<CreditPolicy> all) {\n        this.policies = all.stream().collect(Collectors.toMap(CreditPolicy::segment, p -> p,\n                (a, b) -> { throw new IllegalStateException(\"Duplicate policy for \" + a.segment()); },\n                () -> new EnumMap<>(Segment.class)));\n        EnumSet<Segment> missing = EnumSet.complementOf(EnumSet.copyOf(policies.keySet()));\n        if (!missing.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException(\"No credit policy for \" + missing);\n    }\n\n    public CreditDecision decide(CreditApplication app) {\n        return policies.get(app.segment()).evaluate(app);\n    }\n}","note":"Selection is an explicit key lookup, not list order, and the constructor fails at startup on duplicates or gaps. `EnumSet.copyOf` throws on an empty collection, which is also a useful failure when no policies are registered."},{"title":"When a switch is the better tool: sealed types and an exhaustive switch","lang":"java","code":"public sealed interface FeeRule permits FlatFee, PercentageFee, TieredFee {}\npublic record FlatFee(BigDecimal amount) implements FeeRule {}\npublic record PercentageFee(BigDecimal rate) implements FeeRule {}\npublic record TieredFee(BigDecimal threshold, BigDecimal below, BigDecimal above) implements FeeRule {}\n\npublic final class Fees {\n    public static BigDecimal fee(FeeRule rule, BigDecimal amount) {\n        return switch (rule) {   // no default: a new FeeRule type is a compile error here\n            case FlatFee f -> f.amount();\n            case PercentageFee p -> amount.multiply(p.rate());\n            case TieredFee t -> amount.compareTo(t.threshold()) <= 0 ? t.below() : t.above();\n        };\n    }\n}","note":"Small, stable, dependency-free variants read better as data plus one exhaustive switch. Reach for Strategy when each branch needs its own collaborators or is added by different teams."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you look up the right strategy for a customer segment without a chain of `if` checks?","a":"Give each strategy a `segment()` method, inject `List<CreditPolicy>` and build an `EnumMap<Segment, CreditPolicy>` at startup, failing on duplicates and on segments with no policy. The service resolves the segment and does one map lookup. If the key depends on more than segment, such as country or experiment cohort, compute it in a small resolver so policies stay unaware of how they were chosen."},{"q":"Why is relying on Spring's `List` injection order risky for strategies?","a":"Bean order depends on registration and on `@Order` annotations spread across classes, so a refactor or a new bean can silently change which strategy wins in a first-match loop. Select by a unique key instead and verify uniqueness at startup. When order genuinely matters, as in a rule chain, declare it in one place and pin it with a test."}],"extra":["**Expression problem:** Sealed types with pattern switches make adding operations cheap and adding variants expensive; Strategy is the opposite. Pick based on which axis changes more often in your domain.","**Enum strategy:** For small algorithms without dependencies, an enum with a per-constant method body (or a field holding a lambda) is a lightweight Strategy with exhaustiveness and fast `EnumMap` lookups.","**Map injection pitfall:** Spring can inject `Map<String, CreditPolicy>` keyed by bean name, but that ties routing to class or bean names that someone will rename; an explicit `segment()` key is safer and self-documenting.","**Not every family of classes is Strategy:** When several rules must all run in sequence (hard rules, then scoring, then affordability), you are in Chain of Responsibility territory; declare the order in one place instead of spreading `@Order` across classes.","**Observability and audit:** Record the selected strategy key and version on every decision (`policy=SME_V3`) in logs, metrics tags and the stored decision; in regulated credit flows you must be able to explain which rules ran."],"redFlags":["moves logic into strategies but still picks them with copies of the old `switch` across services","never checks at startup that every segment has exactly one strategy"]},{"id":72,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"You need one provider client, a matching family of provider components, and requests with many optional fields. Which creational pattern fits each?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Factory Method lets an implementation decide which single product to create, Abstract Factory creates a family of related products that must match, and Builder assembles one complex object step by step and validates it.","answer":["**Factory Method** defines a creation method whose override decides the concrete product: `createWriter()` in a CSV exporter returns a CSV writer, in a PDF exporter a PDF writer. The caller depends only on the product interface and polymorphism picks the class. In everyday Java the term is also used loosely for static factories such as `List.of()` or `Money.of(amount, currency)`, which add names, caching and the freedom to return a subtype.","**Abstract Factory** is one level up: an interface that creates several related objects that must be used together. A payment provider kit returning a matching API client, webhook signature verifier and error translator is an Abstract Factory; pairing one provider's verifier with another provider's client is exactly the bug it prevents. The cost is that adding a new product type to the family changes every concrete factory.","**Builder** solves a different problem: constructing one object with many parameters, several optional, and invariants across them. It replaces telescoping constructors, makes call sites readable, and should end in a `build()` that validates and returns an immutable object. A builder that produces a mutable JPA entity with no validation, or lets callers skip mandatory fields, just becomes a convenient way to create invalid state.","Rule of thumb: one product whose type varies, Factory Method; consistent families, Abstract Factory; one product with complex configuration, Builder. Spring DI replaces many hand-written factories, but the patterns still matter when the choice depends on runtime data."],"keyPoints":["a factory method defers the choice of one concrete product to a subclass or implementation","an abstract factory guarantees that related objects come from the same family","a builder collects optional settings and validates once at `build()`"],"examples":[{"title":"Builder with mandatory fields up front and validation in build()","lang":"java","code":"public final class PaymentRequest {\n    private final String orderId;\n    private final BigDecimal amount;\n    private final Currency currency;\n    private final String description;      // optional\n    private final Duration captureDelay;   // optional\n\n    private PaymentRequest(Builder b) {\n        this.orderId = b.orderId; this.amount = b.amount; this.currency = b.currency;\n        this.description = b.description; this.captureDelay = b.captureDelay;\n    }\n    public static Builder builder(String orderId, BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {\n        return new Builder(orderId, amount, currency);\n    }\n\n    public static final class Builder {\n        private final String orderId;\n        private final BigDecimal amount;\n        private final Currency currency;\n        private String description = \"\";\n        private Duration captureDelay = Duration.ZERO;\n\n        private Builder(String orderId, BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {\n            this.orderId = Objects.requireNonNull(orderId);\n            this.amount = Objects.requireNonNull(amount);\n            this.currency = Objects.requireNonNull(currency);\n        }\n        public Builder description(String d) { this.description = d; return this; }\n        public Builder captureDelay(Duration d) { this.captureDelay = d; return this; }\n        public PaymentRequest build() {\n            if (amount.signum() <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"amount must be positive\");\n            if (captureDelay.compareTo(Duration.ofDays(7)) > 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"capture delay too long\");\n            return new PaymentRequest(this);\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Mandatory fields are constructor arguments of the builder, so forgetting them does not compile; cross-field rules live in `build()`, and the product is immutable once built."},{"title":"Abstract Factory for a provider family, Factory Method for one product","lang":"java","code":"// Abstract Factory: every provider supplies a matching family of objects\npublic interface PaymentProviderKit {\n    PaymentClient client();\n    WebhookVerifier webhookVerifier();\n    ErrorTranslator errorTranslator();\n}\n\n// Factory Method: the subclass decides which writer the template method uses\npublic abstract class SettlementExporter {\n    public final byte[] export(SettlementReport report) {\n        ReportWriter writer = createWriter();\n        return writer.write(report);\n    }\n    protected abstract ReportWriter createWriter();\n}\n\npublic final class CsvSettlementExporter extends SettlementExporter {\n    @Override\n    protected ReportWriter createWriter() { return new CsvReportWriter(';'); }\n}","note":"The kit guarantees that a provider's client, verifier and translator are never mixed with another provider's; the exporter shows the classic polymorphic Factory Method inside a template method."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Each payment provider needs a client, a webhook verifier and an error translator that must match. How do you guarantee they are never mixed?","a":"Give each provider one Abstract Factory, such as a `PaymentProviderKit` with `client()`, `webhookVerifier()` and `errorTranslator()`, and resolve the kit once by provider key. Code then asks the kit for every component, so one provider's verifier can never be paired with another provider's client. If a provider only needed a client, a plain Strategy behind a common interface would be enough."},{"q":"When are a few named static factories on a record simpler than a builder?","a":"When there are few parameters or a handful of known combinations, such as `Refund.full(payment)` and `Refund.partial(payment, amount)`: the names document intent and the compact constructor validates. With many optional fields you end up with a factory explosion or positional nulls that are easy to swap. A builder can still delegate to the record's canonical constructor, so validation stays in one place."}],"extra":["**Static factory vs GoF Factory Method:** Effective Java static factories (`Optional.of`, `Duration.ofSeconds`) are named constructors, while GoF Factory Method relies on overriding; interviewers sometimes probe whether you know the difference.","**Spring's factories:** `@Bean` methods and `FactoryBean<T>` are container-level factory methods, and `ObjectProvider<T>` gives lazy or optional creation without coupling business code to `ApplicationContext`.","**Lombok caveat:** `@Builder` generates no validation, so unset fields silently become null or zero; put checks in the constructor the builder calls, or write `build()` yourself, and avoid builders on JPA entities whose invariants belong in domain methods.","**Step builder:** When several fields are mandatory, a staged builder (each step returns a different interface) makes the required order and presence a compile-time guarantee, at the cost of more boilerplate.","**Immutability payoff:** A validated immutable product is safe to share across threads, cache and use as a map key, which is a concrete reason to prefer Builder plus final fields over setters."],"redFlags":["calls any static method that returns an object an Abstract Factory","adds a builder to a two-field value type"]},{"id":73,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"Which design patterns does Spring itself use, and where?","level":"Basic","tldr":"The container is a factory managing singleton and prototype beans, `@Transactional` and other AOP features are proxies, `JdbcTemplate` is template method with callbacks, application events are observer, and `DispatcherServlet` is a front controller built on adapters and strategies. Knowing where each sits explains most surprises.","answer":["The container is a factory: `BeanFactory` and `ApplicationContext` create, wire and cache objects, `@Bean` methods are factory methods, and a `FactoryBean<T>` is a bean that produces another object, which is how Spring Data turns repository interfaces into implementations. Scopes are container-managed singleton and prototype: one instance per context and bean definition, or a new one per lookup. Proxies wrap beans after creation: a `BeanPostProcessor` swaps the target for a JDK dynamic proxy or CGLIB subclass that applies `@Transactional`, `@Async` or `@Cacheable` around each call.","Template method fixes the algorithm and lets you supply the varying steps: `JdbcTemplate` gets the connection, prepares the statement, iterates rows, translates `SQLException` into `DataAccessException` and releases resources, while your `RowMapper` maps one row; `TransactionTemplate` and `RestTemplate` work the same way, via callbacks, not subclassing. Observer is `ApplicationEventPublisher` with `@EventListener` and `@TransactionalEventListener`. In Spring MVC, `DispatcherServlet` is a front controller: every request enters there, a `HandlerMapping` finds the handler, a `HandlerAdapter` adapts that kind of handler to one invocation contract, and `HttpMessageConverter`s and `ViewResolver`s are strategies chosen per request.","Production surprises come from these patterns. Self-invocation bypasses the proxy, so an internal call to a `@Transactional` method gets no transaction. A prototype bean injected into a singleton is created once. A synchronous listener runs in the publisher's thread and transaction, so its exception rolls back the publisher. And the container gives your own code strategy for free: inject a `Map<String, PaymentProvider>` and new providers are just new beans."],"keyPoints":["container is a factory managing scopes","proxies implement AOP features","DispatcherServlet with adapters and strategies"],"examples":[{"title":"Patterns you touch in ordinary service code","lang":"java","code":"public interface PaymentProvider {                     // strategy\n    ChargeResult charge(Payment payment);\n}\n\n@Component(\"card\") class CardProvider implements PaymentProvider { /* ... */ }\n\n@Service\nclass PaymentService {\n    private final Map<String, PaymentProvider> providers;     // strategy beans by name\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;           // observer: subject side\n    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;                          // template method\n\n    PaymentService(Map<String, PaymentProvider> providers,\n                   ApplicationEventPublisher events, JdbcTemplate jdbc) {\n        this.providers = providers;\n        this.events = events;\n        this.jdbc = jdbc;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional                                     // proxy: begins and commits around the call\n    public ChargeResult pay(Payment payment) {\n        ChargeResult result = providers.get(payment.method()).charge(payment);\n        events.publishEvent(new PaymentCaptured(payment.id(), result.reference()));\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    List<PaymentRow> recent(String merchantId) {\n        // JdbcTemplate owns connection, statement, iteration and cleanup; you map one row\n        return jdbc.query(\"select id, amount from payments where merchant_id = ? limit 50\",\n                (rs, rowNum) -> new PaymentRow(rs.getObject(\"id\", UUID.class), rs.getBigDecimal(\"amount\")),\n                merchantId);\n    }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass ReceiptMailer {                                  // observer: listener side\n    @TransactionalEventListener                        // runs AFTER_COMMIT by default\n    void on(PaymentCaptured event) { /* send receipt */ }\n}","note":"A plain `@EventListener` would send the receipt before the commit, even if the payment rolls back."},{"title":"FactoryBean, @Configuration proxying and prototype scope","lang":"java","code":"// Factory: the bean named \"signingKey\" is the product, \"&signingKey\" is the factory itself\n@Component(\"signingKey\")\nclass SigningKeyFactoryBean implements FactoryBean<PrivateKey> {\n    @Override public PrivateKey getObject() throws Exception { return loadFromVault(); }\n    @Override public Class<?> getObjectType() { return PrivateKey.class; }\n}\n\n// @Configuration classes are CGLIB proxies (proxyBeanMethods = true by default)\n@Configuration\nclass ClientConfig {\n    @Bean ConnectionPool pool() { return new ConnectionPool(20); }\n    @Bean LedgerClient ledger() { return new LedgerClient(pool()); }   // the pool bean\n    @Bean AuditClient audit()   { return new AuditClient(pool()); }    // the same pool bean\n}\n// With @Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false) each pool() call creates a new pool\n\n// Prototype injected directly into a singleton is created only once\n@Component @Scope(\"prototype\")\nclass ReconciliationRun { /* mutable per-run state */ }\n\n@Service\nclass ReconciliationService {\n    private final ObjectProvider<ReconciliationRun> runs;\n\n    ReconciliationService(ObjectProvider<ReconciliationRun> runs) {\n        this.runs = runs;\n    }\n\n    void reconcile(LocalDate day) {\n        ReconciliationRun run = runs.getObject();      // a new instance per call\n    }\n}","note":"Each behavior follows from a pattern: the factory is itself a bean, the configuration proxy intercepts factory method calls, and prototype means new per lookup, not per use."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When does Spring use a JDK dynamic proxy and when CGLIB?","a":"A JDK dynamic proxy implements the bean's interfaces, so it intercepts only interface methods and cannot be injected as the concrete class. CGLIB generates a subclass, so it needs no interface but cannot intercept `final` or private methods. Plain Spring AOP uses JDK proxies when interfaces exist; Spring Boot defaults to class-based proxies."},{"q":"Is RestTemplate still the template to use for HTTP calls?","a":"It works but is in maintenance mode. New synchronous code should use `RestClient`, a fluent client built on the same message converters, or `@HttpExchange` interface clients; `WebClient` remains the reactive option."}],"extra":["**Chain of responsibility:** Spring Security's filter chain and MVC `HandlerInterceptor`s pass requests through ordered handlers that can stop the chain.","**Builder:** `RestClient.builder()` and `UriComponentsBuilder` build objects with many options.","**Exception translation:** `SQLExceptionTranslator` maps vendor error codes to subtypes such as `DuplicateKeyException`.","**Strategy hooks:** `PlatformTransactionManager`, `TaskExecutor` and `PasswordEncoder` are swappable; Boot auto-configures the first two unless you define a bean, and Spring Security falls back to a `DelegatingPasswordEncoder` when no `PasswordEncoder` bean exists.","**Async events:** an `@Async` listener runs outside the publisher's transaction and loses the event if the JVM stops first; use an outbox for events that must not be lost."],"redFlags":["Names patterns without saying where they appear","Thinks singleton scope means one per JVM","Does not know @Transactional is a proxy"]},{"id":74,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"Your loan service calls a credit bureau SDK with dozens of classes. What role should Adapter and Facade each play there?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Adapter translates a vendor's interface, data and errors into your domain port so vendor types never leak; Facade simplifies a complex subsystem behind a few calls; real integrations often combine both.","answer":["An **Adapter** exists because two interfaces disagree. The domain defines a port in its own language, for example `CreditBureau.fetchReport(PersonalNumber)` returning a `CreditReport`, and the adapter implements it by calling the vendor API and translating everything: request shapes, field names, units and scales, enums, date formats, and vendor error codes into domain exceptions or result types. Vendor DTOs, SDK classes and HTTP status codes stop at the adapter package.","A **Facade** exists because a subsystem is complicated, not because it is incompatible. If the vendor needs a token exchange, a session, three calls and a polling loop to produce one report, a facade exposes `fetchReport()` and hides the choreography. It simplifies, but on its own it may still speak the vendor's language.","For a third-party integration you almost always need the adapter, because it is what protects the domain (the ports and adapters, or hexagonal, architecture). You add a facade when the vendor workflow is complex, and it usually sits inside or behind the adapter, so both are used together. Keep business rules out of the adapter but make its translation thorough, and put per-vendor timeouts, retries and circuit breakers at this boundary, since that is where you know which vendor failures are transient."],"keyPoints":["the adapter implements an interface the domain owns and converts vendor requests, responses and failures","the facade hides a multi-step vendor workflow behind a small number of calls","vendor classes stay inside the integration module, so swapping vendors touches one place","one integration class often plays both roles at once"],"examples":[{"title":"Adapter implementing a domain port and translating payloads and errors","lang":"java","code":"// Domain port: domain language only, no vendor types\npublic interface CreditBureau {\n    CreditReport fetchReport(PersonalNumber subject);\n}\n\n// Adapter: translates the vendor API, payload and errors into the port\n@Component\nclass AcmeBureauAdapter implements CreditBureau {\n    private final RestClient http;\n\n    AcmeBureauAdapter(RestClient.Builder builder, AcmeProperties props) {\n        this.http = builder.baseUrl(props.baseUrl()).build();\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public CreditReport fetchReport(PersonalNumber subject) {\n        try {\n            AcmeReportDto dto = http.get()\n                    .uri(\"/v2/reports/{id}\", subject.value())\n                    .retrieve()\n                    .body(AcmeReportDto.class);\n            // Vendor risk index 0 to 1000 (high is bad); domain score 0 to 100 (high is good)\n            int score = 100 - dto.riskIndex() / 10;\n            boolean remarks = dto.remarks() != null && !dto.remarks().isEmpty();\n            return new CreditReport(score, remarks);\n        } catch (HttpClientErrorException.NotFound e) {\n            throw new SubjectNotFoundException(subject);\n        } catch (HttpClientErrorException.TooManyRequests | HttpServerErrorException | ResourceAccessException e) {\n            throw new CreditBureauUnavailableException(\"Acme bureau unavailable\", e);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Vendor DTO stays package-private inside the adapter package\nrecord AcmeReportDto(int riskIndex, List<String> remarks, String vendorStatus) {}","note":"The domain sees only `CreditReport`, `SubjectNotFoundException` and `CreditBureauUnavailableException`; the scale inversion and the status code meanings are written once, here, and tested."},{"title":"Testing the adapter against a fake HTTP server with vendor payloads","lang":"java","code":"class AcmeBureauAdapterTest {\n    private final RestClient.Builder builder = RestClient.builder();\n    private final MockRestServiceServer server = MockRestServiceServer.bindTo(builder).build();\n    private final AcmeBureauAdapter adapter = new AcmeBureauAdapter(builder, new AcmeProperties(\"https://acme.test\"));\n    private final PersonalNumber subject = new PersonalNumber(\"190001010000\");\n\n    @Test\n    void mapsVendorReportToDomainReport() {\n        server.expect(requestTo(\"https://acme.test/v2/reports/190001010000\"))\n              .andRespond(withSuccess(\"\"\"\n                      {\"riskIndex\": 200, \"remarks\": [\"LATE_PAYMENT\"], \"vendorStatus\": \"OK\"}\n                      \"\"\", MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON));\n\n        CreditReport report = adapter.fetchReport(subject);\n\n        assertThat(report.score()).isEqualTo(80);\n        assertThat(report.hasPaymentRemarks()).isTrue();\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void translatesVendorOutageToDomainException() {\n        server.expect(requestTo(\"https://acme.test/v2/reports/190001010000\"))\n              .andRespond(withStatus(HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE));\n\n        assertThatThrownBy(() -> adapter.fetchReport(subject))\n                .isInstanceOf(CreditBureauUnavailableException.class);\n    }\n}","note":"`MockRestServiceServer.bindTo(RestClient.Builder)` (Spring Framework 6.1 and later) exercises real HTTP mapping and JSON binding without a network; feed it recorded vendor responses, including error bodies."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Which module should own the port interface, and why does that matter?","a":"The domain or application module owns it, written in domain terms such as `CreditReportPort.fetchReport(applicantId)`, and the integration module depends on it, not the other way around. That is dependency inversion: the core compiles without the vendor SDK on its classpath, so changing vendors or SDK versions recompiles only the adapter. If the interface is shaped like the vendor's API, the abstraction leaks and you gain little."},{"q":"The vendor ships a new major API version. How does this design limit the blast radius?","a":"Only the adapter changes: you write a new implementation of the same port against the new API, or update the mappings in the existing one, while the domain keeps its types and errors. You can run both adapters side by side behind a feature flag or routing rule and compare results before switching traffic. Had domain code used vendor DTOs directly, the same upgrade would ripple through every service that touched them."}],"extra":["**Anti-corruption layer:** In DDD terms, adapters plus translators around a vendor or legacy model form an anti-corruption layer, a useful name when justifying the extra mapping code to a team.","**Explicit semantic mapping:** MapStruct is fine for plain field copies, but semantic translations (score scale inversions, money in minor units, status meanings) deserve explicit, unit-tested code.","**Enforce the boundary:** An ArchUnit rule such as \"no class outside `..adapter.acme..` depends on `..acme..` types\" keeps vendor DTOs from creeping into the domain over time.","**Resilience at the edge:** Per-vendor timeouts, Resilience4j circuit breakers and bulkheads, and metrics tagged by vendor belong in the adapter, so the domain only sees available or unavailable semantics.","**Logging hygiene:** Log vendor request IDs and correlation IDs in the adapter, and mask personal data such as personal identity numbers before logging vendor payloads."],"redFlags":["claims a facade is required for every third-party library, even one with a small, clean API","puts retry and timeout handling in domain services instead of at the integration boundary","cannot say which side of the boundary owns the port interface"]},{"id":75,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"Checkout routes each payment to one of several providers. How would you structure that code, and which SOLID ideas does it reflect?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Put each provider behind a `PaymentProvider` Strategy interface, let Spring DI create and register them, and route through a registry; new providers are new classes (OCP) and the core depends only on the abstraction (DIP).","answer":["The core is **Strategy**: a `PaymentProvider` interface (`id()`, `supports(method, currency)`, `authorize`, `capture`, `refund`) with one implementation per provider. `PaymentService` never knows which concrete provider it calls; a routing policy chooses by merchant, country, currency, method, cost or provider health, and a registry returns that strategy. Each implementation is also an adapter that translates the vendor API and errors into the domain's `PaymentResult`.","Instantiation is **Factory/DI**: Spring creates each provider bean with its own configuration and HTTP client, and injects `List<PaymentProvider>` into the service, which indexes them by id and fails fast on duplicates. When instances depend on runtime data, such as per-merchant credentials, a dedicated factory builds and caches them.","SOLID follows. **OCP**: a new provider is a new class plus configuration; the service and the existing providers are untouched. **DIP**: the high-level payment flow depends on the `PaymentProvider` abstraction, and vendor SDKs sit behind implementations of it. **SRP** separates routing, vendor translation and payment state; **LSP** requires every provider to honor the same contract (idempotency keys, amounts in minor units, identical failure semantics); **ISP** suggests optional capabilities like `RefundCapable` instead of throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`.","Extensibility and testability are the payoff: the service is tested with a fake provider, each provider against a mocked HTTP server, routing rules as pure functions. In production add idempotency keys, per-provider timeouts and circuit breakers, and fail over to another provider only when the first call definitely did not charge."],"keyPoints":["each provider sits behind one shared interface and is chosen at runtime by a registry","the container collects every implementation, so onboarding a provider means adding a class, not editing a switch","core payment logic depends only on the interface, which reflects the open/closed and dependency inversion principles","fake providers make the service unit-testable without network calls"],"examples":[{"title":"Strategy interface, one provider, and a registry-based service","lang":"java","code":"public interface PaymentProvider {\n    String id();                                         // \"adyen\", \"stripe\", a value, not an enum\n    boolean supports(PaymentMethod method, Currency currency);\n    PaymentResult authorize(AuthorizeRequest request);   // request carries an idempotency key\n}\n\n// Registered by AdyenConfig only when the provider is enabled\nclass AdyenPaymentProvider implements PaymentProvider {\n    private final AdyenClient client;\n    private final AdyenProperties props;\n\n    AdyenPaymentProvider(AdyenClient client, AdyenProperties props) { this.client = client; this.props = props; }\n\n    public String id() { return \"adyen\"; }\n    public boolean supports(PaymentMethod method, Currency currency) {\n        return props.currencies().contains(currency.getCurrencyCode());\n    }\n    public PaymentResult authorize(AuthorizeRequest request) {\n        return AdyenMapper.toResult(client.authorize(AdyenMapper.toVendor(request, props.merchantAccount())));\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class PaymentService {\n    private final Map<String, PaymentProvider> providers;\n    private final RoutingPolicy routing;\n\n    public PaymentService(List<PaymentProvider> all, RoutingPolicy routing) {\n        // toUnmodifiableMap throws on duplicate ids: fail at startup, not at checkout\n        this.providers = all.stream().collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableMap(PaymentProvider::id, p -> p));\n        this.routing = routing;\n    }\n\n    public PaymentResult authorize(AuthorizeRequest request) {\n        String providerId = routing.choose(request, providers.values());\n        return providers.get(providerId).authorize(request);\n    }\n}","note":"`PaymentService` compiles against the interface only; adding a provider never edits it. `RoutingPolicy` is a separate, pure component that can be unit tested with tables of cases."},{"title":"Provider-specific, validated configuration and conditional registration","lang":"java","code":"@Validated\n@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = \"payments.providers.adyen\")\npublic record AdyenProperties(\n        @NotBlank String baseUrl,\n        @NotBlank String merchantAccount,\n        @NotEmpty Set<String> currencies,\n        @NotNull Duration timeout,\n        @NotBlank String apiKey) {}              // from a Kubernetes secret or Vault, never from git\n\n@Configuration\n@EnableConfigurationProperties(AdyenProperties.class)\n@ConditionalOnProperty(prefix = \"payments.providers.adyen\", name = \"enabled\", havingValue = \"true\")\nclass AdyenConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    AdyenClient adyenClient(RestClient.Builder builder, AdyenProperties props) {\n        var requestFactory = new SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory();\n        requestFactory.setConnectTimeout((int) props.timeout().toMillis());\n        requestFactory.setReadTimeout((int) props.timeout().toMillis());\n        RestClient http = builder.baseUrl(props.baseUrl())\n                .requestFactory(requestFactory)\n                .defaultHeader(\"X-API-Key\", props.apiKey())\n                .build();\n        return new AdyenClient(http);\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    PaymentProvider adyenPaymentProvider(AdyenClient client, AdyenProperties props) {\n        return new AdyenPaymentProvider(client, props);\n    }\n}","note":"A missing merchant account fails startup through Bean Validation, and switching `payments.providers.adyen.enabled` adds or removes the provider without touching any other class. `AdyenClient` is your own thin wrapper, not a vendor SDK type."}],"followUps":[{"q":"A provider times out and you do not know whether the charge went through. What do you do?","a":"Treat the outcome as unknown, not failed: keep the payment in a pending state and never retry blindly with a different provider, which could charge the customer twice. Send every request with an idempotency key so a retry to the same provider returns the original result, and resolve the status by querying the provider or waiting for its webhook. A reconciliation job should settle anything still pending after a deadline."},{"q":"How would you fail over to a second provider when the primary is down?","a":"Give each provider its own circuit breaker and let the routing layer pick the next eligible strategy when the primary's breaker is open or it fails in a way that proves no money moved. Failover only applies to attempts known not to have reached the provider, such as a refused connection or an open breaker; a timeout after sending is an unknown outcome that must be resolved first. Record which provider handled each payment, because refunds and disputes must go back to that same provider."}],"extra":["**Idempotency is part of the contract:** Every provider call carries a stable idempotency key derived from the payment attempt, so a retry after a timeout cannot double charge; LSP means every provider must honor it the same way.","**Failover is not retry:** After a timeout you do not know whether the first provider charged, so query its status or wait for the webhook before routing the same payment elsewhere.","**Webhooks are the other half:** Each provider also needs a signature verifier and an event mapper for asynchronous notifications, which is where an Abstract Factory style provider kit starts to pay off.","**Money representation:** Use `BigDecimal` or minor units with an ISO currency at the port, and let each adapter convert to its vendor format; rounding and currency exponent differences (JPY has none) are classic provider bugs.","**Per-provider observability:** Tag latency, error rate and decline rate by provider and response code; routing on health or cost is only as good as these metrics."],"redFlags":["models provider ids as an enum, so every new provider still edits shared code","lets provider SDK types and error codes appear in the core payment service","shares one HTTP client, timeout and circuit breaker across all providers"]},{"id":76,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"Spring wraps a bean for `@Transactional`; you wrap a client to add logging. Which is a proxy, which a decorator, and why?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Each implements the interface of the object it wraps, yet a Decorator always forwards the call and layers extra behavior on top, whereas a Proxy stands in for the real object to govern whether, when and where it is reached: security, lazy loading, remote access, caching or lifecycle.","answer":["Structurally they are identical: a class implements `ExchangeRateClient` and holds another `ExchangeRateClient`. The difference is intent. A **Decorator** adds responsibilities, is usually composed explicitly by the client or configuration, can be stacked (metrics around retry around the HTTP client), and always forwards the call, enriching it before or after. `BufferedInputStream` around a `FileInputStream` is the textbook case.","A **Proxy** controls access to a subject that callers treat as the real thing, and it may decide not to call the target at all. A protection proxy rejects unauthorized calls, a caching proxy answers from a cache, a virtual proxy delays creating an expensive object (Hibernate lazy associations), a remote proxy hides the network (a gRPC stub or a Feign client). The client usually does not know the proxy exists because a framework created it.","In Spring, `@Transactional`, `@Cacheable`, `@PreAuthorize` and `@Async` are implemented with AOP proxies (JDK dynamic proxies for interfaces, CGLIB subclasses otherwise). The practical consequence you should mention: a call from one method to another inside the same bean bypasses the proxy, so the annotation silently does nothing. And not every Spring proxy adds behavior: a scoped proxy only routes each call to the right target instance, which is why calling every Spring proxy a decorator is wrong."],"keyPoints":["a decorator always calls through and enriches the call or its result","a proxy may refuse, delay, cache or relocate the call entirely","the class structure is nearly identical, so intent is what separates them"],"examples":[{"title":"Decorators: explicit, stackable, always delegating","lang":"java","code":"public interface ExchangeRateClient {\n    BigDecimal rate(Currency from, Currency to);\n}\n\n// Decorator: adds retry for transient failures, always calls the delegate\npublic final class RetryingExchangeRateClient implements ExchangeRateClient {\n    private final ExchangeRateClient delegate;\n    private final RetryTemplate retry;\n\n    public RetryingExchangeRateClient(ExchangeRateClient delegate, RetryTemplate retry) {\n        this.delegate = delegate;\n        this.retry = retry;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public BigDecimal rate(Currency from, Currency to) {\n        return retry.execute(ctx -> delegate.rate(from, to));\n    }\n}\n\n@Configuration\nclass FxClientConfig {\n    @Bean\n    ExchangeRateClient exchangeRateClient(HttpExchangeRateClient http, RetryTemplate retry, MeterRegistry registry) {\n        Timer timer = Timer.builder(\"fx.rate.latency\").register(registry);\n        ExchangeRateClient retrying = new RetryingExchangeRateClient(http, retry);\n        // Decorator as a lambda: metrics(retry(http)), order is visible in one place\n        return (from, to) -> timer.record(() -> retrying.rate(from, to));\n    }\n}","note":"Order is a design decision: the timer here measures total latency including retries. Retrying is fine for a read-only rate lookup; a payment charge would need an idempotency key first."},{"title":"Proxies: protection and caching, by hand and via Spring","lang":"java","code":"// Protection proxy: may refuse to call the real subject at all\npublic final class AuthorizingAccountQueries implements AccountQueries {\n    private final AccountQueries target;\n    private final AccessPolicy access;\n\n    public AuthorizingAccountQueries(AccountQueries target, AccessPolicy access) {\n        this.target = target;\n        this.access = access;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public AccountStatement statement(AccountId id, YearMonth month) {\n        if (!access.canRead(CurrentUser.get(), id)) {\n            throw new AccessDeniedException(\"No access to account \" + id);\n        }\n        return target.statement(id, month);\n    }\n}\n\n// Same concerns declared through Spring's AOP proxies\n@Service\nclass StatementService implements AccountQueries {\n    @Override\n    @PreAuthorize(\"@accessPolicy.canRead(authentication, #id)\")\n    @Cacheable(cacheNames = \"statements\", key = \"#id + ':' + #month\")\n    public AccountStatement statement(AccountId id, YearMonth month) {\n        return ledger.buildStatement(id, month);   // skipped entirely on a cache hit\n    }\n}","note":"A cache hit never reaches the target, which is the defining proxy behavior. Authorization must be evaluated before the cache, otherwise a cached statement can be served to a caller who may not see it."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does calling a `@Transactional` method from another method in the same class skip the transaction?","a":"Spring applies the transaction in a proxy around the bean, and `this.method()` calls the target directly without passing through that proxy. The interceptor never runs, so no transaction begins and no rollback rules apply. Move the method to another bean, inject the bean's own proxy, or use `TransactionTemplate` for programmatic control."},{"q":"You stack a caching wrapper and an authorization check on the same service. Which must run first, and why?","a":"Authorization must run first, so the access check happens before the cache lookup; otherwise a cache hit returns data the caller was never allowed to see. With Spring annotations, verify that the security interceptor wraps the caching one, for example through advisor order. Both are proxies by intent: one controls access, the other can answer without reaching the target."}],"extra":["**Self-invocation:** `this.otherMethod()` inside a proxied bean skips `@Transactional`, `@Cacheable` and `@Async`; fix it by moving the method to another bean, and remember AspectJ weaving removes the limitation entirely.","**CGLIB limits:** Spring Boot proxies classes with CGLIB by default (`spring.aop.proxy-target-class=true`), which cannot intercept `final` or `private` methods, a frequent reason annotations silently do nothing.","**Wrapper order changes semantics:** Retry outside a circuit breaker counts failures differently than retry inside it, and a cache outside authorization leaks data; make the order explicit with advisor order (`@EnableCaching(order = ...)`), `@Order`, or manual composition.","**Runtime building blocks:** `java.lang.reflect.Proxy` with an `InvocationHandler` backs interface-based proxies, and Hibernate lazy entities are generated subclasses, which is why `getClass()` on a lazy reference may not return the entity class and why `equals` should not rely on it.","**Classic examples:** Decorators include `BufferedReader`, `Collections.synchronizedList` and `HttpServletRequestWrapper`; proxies include `@Lazy` injection points, request-scoped proxies, Feign clients and gRPC stubs."],"redFlags":["assumes a self-invoked `@Transactional` method still gets a transaction","says the difference is that proxies are generated at runtime and decorators are hand-written"]},{"id":77,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"An email listener's exception just rolled back a customer's order. What other hazards come with Observer-style listeners like this?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Observer cuts the compile-time link between publisher and listeners but hides the runtime one: unseen subscribers, leaked registrations, undefined order, listeners triggering each other and one exception halting the rest; going asynchronous adds lost, duplicate, reordered and not-yet-committed events.","answer":["Subscribe and notify look trivial, but the pattern moves coupling from code you can read to runtime wiring you cannot. The publisher no longer knows who reacts, so a change to `OrderPlaced` can break a listener three modules away, and following a business flow means hunting for every subscriber. Listeners registered on long-lived subjects and never removed leak memory, and lambdas that capture request or session objects keep those alive too.","In-process delivery is usually synchronous: listeners run on the publisher's thread, inside its transaction, one after another. That brings ordering uncertainty (Spring orders listeners only through `@Order`), latency coupling (a slow email listener slows checkout), failure propagation (one exception stops the remaining listeners and can roll back the publisher's transaction), and recursive updates, where a listener changes state that fires the same event again.","Going asynchronous fixes latency and isolation but brings distributed systems problems: events lost on crash, delivered twice on retry, processed out of order, or seen before the state change is committed. A production design therefore decides per listener: critical reactions go through a transactional outbox or a broker with at-least-once delivery and idempotent consumers, best effort reactions use an after-commit listener, and every failure path has metrics, retries and a dead letter destination."],"keyPoints":["the publisher cannot see who reacts, so behavior is spread across unseen classes","listeners that are never unregistered keep their owners alive","a synchronous listener shares the publisher's thread, transaction and failures","async delivery needs idempotent handlers and a plan for retries and dead letters"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: side effects of a domain event and the transaction boundary","lang":"java","code":"public record OrderPlaced(UUID orderId, String customerEmail) {}\n\n@Service\nclass OrderService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;\n\n    OrderService(OrderRepository orders, ApplicationEventPublisher events) {\n        this.orders = orders;\n        this.events = events;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public Order place(PlaceOrder cmd) {\n        Order order = orders.save(Order.from(cmd));\n        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlaced(order.getId(), cmd.email()));\n        return order;\n    }\n}\n\n// BAD: runs in the publisher's thread and transaction; if the mail server throws,\n// the order rolls back, and if the order rolls back later, the email was already sent\n@Component\nclass EmailOnOrderPlaced {\n    @EventListener\n    void on(OrderPlaced e) { mailer.sendConfirmation(e.customerEmail(), e.orderId()); }\n}\n\n// GOOD: runs only after a successful commit, on another thread, isolated from checkout\n@Component\nclass EmailAfterCommit {\n    @Async\n    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)\n    void on(OrderPlaced e) { mailer.sendConfirmation(e.customerEmail(), e.orderId()); }\n}","note":"The GOOD version is still best effort: a crash right after commit loses the email. If the reaction must happen, persist it with the order (outbox or Spring Modulith's event publication registry) and process it with retries."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What changes when the same events move from Spring's `ApplicationEventPublisher` to a Kafka topic?","a":"Events become durable and replayable and can be consumed by other services at their own pace, but ordering holds only within a partition and delivery is typically at least once, so consumers must be idempotent. Writing to the database and publishing to Kafka are two separate writes that cannot commit atomically, which is why teams use a transactional outbox. You trade operational cost and eventual consistency for decoupling in time and deployment."},{"q":"How would you keep a failing email listener from rolling back the order?","a":"Run it after commit with `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)`, ideally asynchronously, so its exception cannot affect the committed order. Because an after-commit listener cannot undo anything and may be lost on a crash, persist the intent as an outbox row or job when the email must be delivered. Add metrics and alerting so failures in isolated listeners are not silent."}],"extra":["**Silent drops:** `@TransactionalEventListener` defaults to `AFTER_COMMIT` and ignores events published outside a transaction unless `fallbackExecution = true`, which surprises teams when a non-transactional code path starts publishing.","**Spring 6.1 rule:** A `@TransactionalEventListener` method that is also `@Transactional` must use `REQUIRES_NEW` or `NOT_SUPPORTED`, otherwise the context fails at startup, because the original transaction has already completed when the listener runs.","**In-process outbox:** Spring Modulith's event publication registry persists each publication for transactional listeners in the same transaction and marks it complete on success, so incomplete events can be republished after a restart.","**Error routing:** Spring's `SimpleApplicationEventMulticaster` accepts an `ErrorHandler`; without one, a synchronous listener exception propagates to the publisher, and with `@Async` it goes to the `AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler`, which by default only logs.","**Recursion guard:** Publish facts (`OrderPlaced`), not commands disguised as events, keep handlers idempotent, and avoid listeners that mutate the aggregate that emitted the event; a cycle shows up as `StackOverflowError` when synchronous and as an event storm when asynchronous."],"redFlags":["lets a side-effect listener run inside the publisher's transaction without deciding whether it may fail it","assumes listeners always run in registration order","treats an in-memory event as if it were durable"]},{"id":78,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"Two objects are expensive: a heavily configured template and a database connection. Would you clone or pool each, and why?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Prototype copies an already initialized object to avoid repeating expensive setup; Object Pool lends a bounded set of scarce, costly resources like connections or threads; pooling ordinary objects on a modern JVM usually hurts.","answer":["First decide what is actually expensive. If it is computing the object's state (parsing a large rules or template file, building a pricing grid), use **Prototype**: build one fully initialized instance and create new ones by copying it, then customize the copy. If the object is immutable you do not need copies at all; share the one instance. Prototype costs memory for the template and forces a careful deep versus shallow copy decision.","If the expensive part is acquiring a limited external resource (TCP, TLS and authentication for a database connection, an OS thread, a native buffer), use an **Object Pool**: keep a bounded set of live instances, lend them out and take them back. The pool doubles as a concurrency limit that protects the resource. Its costs are lifecycle work: validating stale connections, detecting leaks, resetting state between borrowers, and waiting when empty. A pool is not a cache: a cache shares values by key, while a pool lends exclusive use of interchangeable resources.","The trade-off is memory held versus creation cost saved. For ordinary Java objects pooling is usually an anti-pattern: TLAB allocation is a pointer bump, young GC cost is proportional to survivors rather than garbage, and escape analysis can remove allocations entirely. A pool keeps objects alive into the old generation, adds synchronization, and invites bugs when state is not reset. Pool only what is scarce: JDBC connections (HikariCP), threads (`ExecutorService`), HTTP connections, and sometimes large direct buffers (Netty's pooled allocator)."],"keyPoints":["cloning pays the setup cost once and then copies the prepared state, so shallow versus deep copies matter","pooling caps how many costly external resources exist and lends them out under contention","a pool trades steady memory use and lifecycle complexity for avoided creation work","plain heap objects are cheap to allocate on the JVM, so pooling them rarely pays"],"examples":[{"title":"Prototype: copy a parsed template instead of parsing again","lang":"java","code":"// FeeLine is an immutable record, so copying the list shallowly is a safe copy\npublic record FeeLine(String code, BigDecimal amount) {}\n\npublic final class LoanOfferTemplate {\n    private final Map<String, String> clauses;   // parsed from a large legal document\n    private final List<FeeLine> fees;\n\n    private LoanOfferTemplate(Map<String, String> clauses, List<FeeLine> fees) {\n        this.clauses = clauses;\n        this.fees = fees;\n    }\n\n    // Expensive: read and parse once at startup\n    public static LoanOfferTemplate load(Path source) throws IOException {\n        Map<String, String> clauses = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n        for (String line : Files.readAllLines(source)) {\n            int eq = line.indexOf('=');\n            if (eq > 0) clauses.put(line.substring(0, eq).trim(), line.substring(eq + 1).trim());\n        }\n        return new LoanOfferTemplate(clauses, new ArrayList<>());\n    }\n\n    // Prototype: a cheap, independent copy of the initialized state\n    public LoanOfferTemplate copy() {\n        return new LoanOfferTemplate(new LinkedHashMap<>(clauses), new ArrayList<>(fees));\n    }\n\n    public void putClause(String key, String text) { clauses.put(key, text); }\n    public void addFee(FeeLine fee) { fees.add(fee); }\n}","note":"Each customer offer starts from `template.copy()` and is customized without touching the shared template. Collections are copied; their elements are immutable, so a deeper copy is unnecessary."},{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: pool only what is scarce","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: pooling cheap, short-lived objects\nclass QuotePool {\n    private final ConcurrentLinkedQueue<MutableQuote> free = new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>();\n\n    MutableQuote borrow() {\n        MutableQuote q = free.poll();\n        return q != null ? q : new MutableQuote();\n    }\n\n    void release(MutableQuote q) {\n        q.reset();        // forget this once and one customer's data leaks into the next quote\n        free.offer(q);    // pooled objects survive GCs and get promoted to the old generation\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: just allocate; TLAB allocation is a pointer bump and young garbage is nearly free\nQuote quote(Order order) {\n    return new Quote(order.id(), pricing.total(order), order.currency());\n}\n\n// GOOD: pool what is scarce and slow to establish, bounded and with timeouts\nDataSource ordersDataSource() {\n    HikariConfig cfg = new HikariConfig();\n    cfg.setJdbcUrl(\"jdbc:postgresql://orders-db:5432/orders\");\n    cfg.setMaximumPoolSize(20);         // sized to what PostgreSQL can serve, not to request threads\n    cfg.setConnectionTimeout(2_000);    // fail fast instead of waiting for the 30 s default\n    cfg.setMaxLifetime(1_500_000);      // below any server, proxy or firewall idle cutoff\n    return new HikariDataSource(cfg);\n}","note":"The pool is justified by the cost and scarcity of the resource behind the object, not by the object itself."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What goes wrong if a prototype is copied shallowly?","a":"The copy and the original share every referenced object, so mutating a nested list or map through one silently changes the other, and concurrent users of different copies can race on that shared state. That is why `Object.clone()` is risky: it copies shallowly by default and is awkward to combine with final fields and inheritance. Prefer a copy constructor or a builder seeded from the prototype that deep-copies the mutable parts, or make the prototype's internals immutable so sharing them is safe."},{"q":"How would you notice that a pool is leaking borrowed objects?","a":"The pool's active count climbs and never returns to its baseline, while borrowers start waiting and eventually time out even though load is flat. Good pools expose this directly: HikariCP has `leakDetectionThreshold`, which logs the stack trace of code that held a connection too long, and Apache Commons Pool 2 can abandon and log objects that are not returned in time. The fix is almost always returning the object in a `finally` block or with try-with-resources."}],"extra":["**Avoid Cloneable:** `Object.clone()` is shallow, bypasses constructors and final field initialization logic, and `Cloneable` declares no method; implement Prototype with a copy constructor or a `copy()` method instead.","**Spring prototype scope is different:** `@Scope(\"prototype\")` asks the container for a brand new bean on every lookup rather than cloning a template, and Spring does not call destroy callbacks on prototype beans.","**Do not pool virtual threads:** They are cheap and meant to be one per task; limit concurrency toward a scarce resource with a `Semaphore` or the connection pool itself.","**Pool metrics:** Active, idle and pending counts plus acquire time and timeouts (`hikaricp.connections.pending`, `hikaricp.connections.acquire`) show whether the pool or the resource behind it is the bottleneck.","**Escape analysis:** C2 can scalar replace objects that do not escape a method, so short-lived value objects in hot loops may never be heap allocated, an optimization a pool would defeat."],"redFlags":["relies on `Object.clone()` without considering mutable fields shared by a shallow copy","forgets that a pooled object must be reset before reuse, leaking state between borrowers","assumes a bigger pool always means better throughput"]},{"id":79,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"What are inversion of control and dependency injection, and what does the ApplicationContext do?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Inversion of control means a class stops creating or looking up its collaborators; something outside builds the object graph and hands each object what it needs, and dependency injection, usually through the constructor, is how Spring does it. The `ApplicationContext` is that container: it reads bean definitions, creates and wires beans, applies post-processors such as proxying, and adds environment, events and resource loading on top of `BeanFactory`.","answer":["Without IoC, an `OrderService` calls `new JdbcOrderRepository(dataSource)` and `new StripeClient(apiKey)` itself, so it decides which implementation it gets, how it is configured and how long it lives. Inversion of control moves those decisions out: the class declares what it needs, normally as constructor parameters, and the container (or a unit test) supplies it. Dependency injection is the form of IoC Spring uses; the other form, looking collaborators up with `context.getBean(...)`, is a service locator that hides dependencies. The payoff: the same class runs with a real repository in production and a fake in a test.","`ApplicationContext` extends `BeanFactory` with what an application needs: an `Environment` with profiles and property sources, event publishing, resource loading, `MessageSource`, and automatic detection of post-processor beans. On `refresh()` it collects bean definitions (component scanning, `@Configuration` classes, `@Bean` methods), lets factory post-processors modify them, registers bean post-processors, then eagerly creates every non-lazy singleton, resolving dependencies by type and letting post-processors wrap beans in proxies for `@Transactional`, `@Async` or caching. Finally it starts auto-startup `SmartLifecycle` beans and publishes `ContextRefreshedEvent`. In Spring Boot, `SpringApplication.run` picks the context type (servlet, reactive or plain) and the embedded server starts during that refresh.","Eager creation is a feature: a missing bean, an ambiguous type, a bad property or a dependency cycle fails the deployment at startup, not on the first request at 3 a.m. It is also why startup time grows with the bean count, and why `spring.main.lazy-initialization=true` (or `@Lazy`) trades faster startup for wiring errors and slow first requests at runtime; keep it for local development. In production, keep the context boring: one composition root, constructor injection, no `getBean` in domain code, and configuration validated at startup so a wrong property stops the rollout, not the traffic."],"keyPoints":["the container, not the class, creates and wires collaborators","dependency injection is the mechanism Spring uses for IoC","the context adds environment, events and post-processing, and creates singletons eagerly"],"examples":[{"title":"The same service wired by a test and by the container","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class OrderService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n    private final PaymentGateway payments;\n\n    public OrderService(OrderRepository orders, PaymentGateway payments) {\n        this.orders = orders;\n        this.payments = payments;\n    }\n\n    public OrderId place(PlaceOrder command) {\n        Order order = orders.save(Order.from(command));\n        payments.authorize(order.id(), order.total());\n        return order.id();\n    }\n}\n\n// Unit test: you are the container\nvar service = new OrderService(new InMemoryOrderRepository(), new FakePaymentGateway());\n\n// Plain Spring without Boot: the container builds the same graph\n@Configuration\n@ComponentScan(\"com.acme.orders\")\nclass OrdersConfig {\n    @Bean\n    PaymentGateway paymentGateway(Environment env) {\n        return new StripePaymentGateway(env.getRequiredProperty(\"payments.stripe.api-key\"));\n    }\n}\n\ntry (var context = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(OrdersConfig.class)) {\n    OrderService orderService = context.getBean(OrderService.class); // composition root only\n    orderService.place(command);\n}","note":"`getBean` appears once, at the entry point, never inside `OrderService`. Closing the context (here through try-with-resources) runs destroy callbacks such as closing connection pools."},{"title":"The context is more than a factory: event publishing","lang":"java","code":"public record OrderPlaced(OrderId orderId, Money total) {}\n\n@Service\nclass CheckoutService {\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events; // the context itself, no @Bean needed\n\n    CheckoutService(ApplicationEventPublisher events) {\n        this.events = events;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void complete(Order order) {\n        order.markPlaced();\n        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlaced(order.id(), order.total()));\n    }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass ReceiptMailer {\n    // Delivered only if the publishing transaction commits\n    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)\n    void on(OrderPlaced event) {\n        // send the receipt\n    }\n}","note":"A plain `@EventListener` runs synchronously inside the publisher's transaction; `@TransactionalEventListener` waits for the commit, so a rolled back order sends no receipt."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is the difference between BeanFactory and ApplicationContext?","a":"`BeanFactory` is the core container: bean definitions, creation, dependency resolution and scopes. `ApplicationContext` builds on it with eager singleton creation, automatic registration of post-processors, events, the `Environment`, resource loading and `MessageSource`. Applications use an `ApplicationContext`; a bare `BeanFactory` would not even process `@Autowired` unless you registered the post-processor yourself."},{"q":"Why is calling getBean from business code a problem?","a":"It turns injection back into lookup: dependencies vanish from the constructor, the class cannot be built in a test without a context, and a missing bean fails at call time instead of at startup. The entry point and framework integration code are the legitimate exceptions, and even there an injected `ObjectProvider` is usually cleaner."}],"extra":["**Resolvable dependencies:** `ApplicationContext`, `ApplicationEventPublisher`, `ResourceLoader` and `Environment` can be injected without declaring them, because the context registers itself and its environment for injection.","**Context hierarchy:** a child context sees its parent's beans but not the reverse. Classic Spring MVC used a root context plus a `DispatcherServlet` child; Spring Boot builds one context by default.","**Test context caching:** test classes with identical configuration share one cached context; each distinct `@MockitoBean` set, profile or property override builds another, so standardize test base classes.","**Not everything is a bean:** the container manages services, repositories and clients; entities, DTOs and value objects are created with `new`.","**Events are not messaging:** listeners run in the same JVM and a listener exception propagates to the publisher by default; an in-process event is lost on a crash, so cross-service notifications still need an outbox."],"redFlags":["Describes IoC as just \"Spring creates objects\" without saying who decides the wiring","Treats context.getBean in services as normal practice","Cannot explain what happens at startup or why wiring errors surface there"]},{"id":80,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"In code review you see `@Autowired private` fields on a new service. What would you ask the author to change, and why?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Constructor injection makes dependencies explicit, `final` and required at construction, so beans are never half-built, are testable with plain `new`, and oversized constructors expose design problems early.","answer":["Constructor injection declares every collaborator as a constructor parameter assigned to a `final` field. The object cannot be created, by Spring or by a test calling `new`, without its required dependencies, so it never exists half-initialized and its collaborators cannot be swapped later. Since Spring 4.3 a class with a single constructor needs no `@Autowired`, and a missing bean fails the context at startup with a clear message instead of an NPE on the first request.","Field injection hides dependencies: they are private fields set by reflection after construction, invisible in the class's API. The fields cannot be `final`, unit tests need a Spring context or reflection tricks such as `@InjectMocks` or `ReflectionTestUtils`, and the class accumulates collaborators silently because one more annotated field costs nothing visually. Outside the container the object is simply broken.","The trade-offs are small. Constructors get longer (Lombok `@RequiredArgsConstructor` or just accepting the lines solves that), and circular dependencies fail fast at startup, which is a design signal rather than a nuisance. Setter injection is still fine for genuinely optional or reconfigurable collaborators, and `ObjectProvider<T>` covers optional or lazy lookup. The production payoff is fail-fast wiring, immutable thread-safe singletons, and plain JUnit tests that run in milliseconds."],"keyPoints":["constructor parameters document every collaborator on the class's public surface","fields marked `final` and assigned once mean the object is complete before anyone can use it","a unit test can build the class with `new` and fakes, without a Spring context or reflection","an overlong parameter list surfaces a design smell early"],"examples":[{"title":"Field injection vs constructor injection","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: hidden, mutable, untestable without Spring\n@Service\npublic class PaymentService {\n    @Autowired private PaymentGateway gateway;\n    @Autowired private LedgerRepository ledger;\n    @Autowired private FraudClient fraud;      // grew silently over time\n}\n\n// GOOD: explicit and final; no @Autowired needed for a single constructor\n@Service\npublic class PaymentService {\n    private final PaymentGateway gateway;\n    private final LedgerRepository ledger;\n\n    public PaymentService(PaymentGateway gateway, LedgerRepository ledger) {\n        this.gateway = gateway;\n        this.ledger = ledger;\n    }\n\n    public PaymentResult pay(PaymentCommand cmd) {\n        PaymentResult result = gateway.charge(cmd.amount(), cmd.cardToken());\n        ledger.record(cmd.accountId(), result);\n        return result;\n    }\n}","note":"The good version documents its needs in the constructor signature, cannot be created without them, and keeps its collaborators immutable for the lifetime of the singleton."},{"title":"Unit test without a Spring context","lang":"java","code":"class PaymentServiceTest {\n    private static final UUID ACCOUNT_ID = UUID.randomUUID();\n\n    @Test\n    void recordsApprovedChargeInLedger() {\n        var gateway = mock(PaymentGateway.class);\n        var ledger = mock(LedgerRepository.class);\n        var approved = PaymentResult.approved(\"tx-42\");\n        when(gateway.charge(any(), any())).thenReturn(approved);\n\n        var service = new PaymentService(gateway, ledger);   // plain constructor call\n        service.pay(new PaymentCommand(ACCOUNT_ID, new BigDecimal(\"99.00\"), \"tok_visa\"));\n\n        verify(ledger).record(ACCOUNT_ID, approved);\n    }\n}","note":"No `@SpringBootTest`, no reflection: the compiler forces the test to supply every dependency, and adding a ninth one breaks every test loudly."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Two beans need each other through their constructors and startup fails. What is the right response?","a":"Treat the failure as useful feedback: a cycle usually means responsibilities are split in the wrong place. Extract the shared logic into a third bean both depend on, invert one direction with an event or callback interface, or merge the two if they are really one concept. Workarounds such as `@Lazy` on a constructor parameter make startup succeed but keep the tangled design. Re-enabling `spring.main.allow-circular-references` does not help here, because it only resolves field and setter cycles, never constructor ones; Spring Boot has rejected circular references by default since 2.6."},{"q":"Are there cases where setter injection is the better choice?","a":"Yes, for genuinely optional dependencies that have a sensible default, and for collaborators that infrastructure code must be able to reconfigure. For ordinary application services, required dependencies belong in the constructor, and an optional one can still be injected there as `ObjectProvider<T>`. Classes that need a no-argument constructor, such as JPA entities, should not receive injected beans at all."}],"extra":["**Circular references:** Spring Boot 2.6+ sets `spring.main.allow-circular-references=false` by default; with constructor injection a cycle fails at startup, which usually points to a missing abstraction or a side effect that should be an event.","**Multiple constructors:** if a class has several constructors, annotate the one Spring should use with `@Autowired`; otherwise Spring falls back to a no-arg constructor and fails if there is none.","**Safe publication:** `final` fields get initialization-safety guarantees from the Java Memory Model, a real bonus for singletons read concurrently by hundreds of request threads.","**Configuration classes:** in `@Configuration` classes prefer parameters on `@Bean` methods over `@Autowired` fields; each bean then declares exactly what it needs, and it works with `proxyBeanMethods = false`.","**Optional dependencies:** instead of `@Autowired(required = false)` on a field, inject `ObjectProvider<T>` and call `getIfAvailable()`, which keeps the dependency visible and supports lazy resolution."],"redFlags":["hides a circular dependency by switching to field injection instead of fixing the design","believes a single constructor still needs `@Autowired` to be used","cannot explain how a test would supply the dependencies"]},{"id":81,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"Users see each other's data after a release. How could the default Spring bean scope cause it, and what other scopes exist?","level":"Basic","tldr":"The default scope creates a single instance for each application context, and every request thread uses it concurrently, so it must hold no per-call state or be made thread-safe on purpose; data for one request belongs in parameters, local variables or a request-scoped bean.","answer":["Spring's scopes are `singleton` (default: one instance per bean definition per `ApplicationContext`), `prototype` (a new instance per injection or lookup, with no destroy callbacks), and the web scopes `request`, `session`, `application` and `websocket`. Singleton is not the GoF singleton: it is per context, so two contexts in one JVM, every pod in a cluster and every test context each have their own instance. Never use a singleton field for cluster-wide state or coordination.","A singleton is not automatically thread-safe. Spring creates it once, every Tomcat or virtual thread calls it concurrently, and no synchronization is added. The rule is that singleton services are stateless: fields hold only `final` injected collaborators and immutable configuration, while request data travels as method parameters and local variables on each thread's stack. Genuinely shared mutable state (a cache, a counter, a rate limiter) must use thread-safe structures such as `ConcurrentHashMap`, `LongAdder`, Caffeine or Micrometer meters, never plain fields.","Scope mismatches are the other trap. Injecting a prototype or request-scoped bean into a singleton captures one instance at startup unless you go through a scoped proxy (`@RequestScope` proxies by default), `ObjectProvider<T>` or `@Lookup`. Request scope is handy for per-request context such as tenant or correlation id, but explicit parameters are usually clearer and easier to test."],"keyPoints":["the default scope gives every thread the same object, so its instance fields are shared memory","per-call data should travel as method arguments or local variables, never as bean fields","prototype, request and session scopes change lifetime, but injecting them into a singleton needs a scoped proxy or `ObjectProvider`","the single instance is per container, so each JVM and each context has its own copy"],"examples":[{"title":"Mutable request state in a singleton","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: one instance serves every thread; requests overwrite each other's fields\n@Service\npublic class CreditDecisionService {\n    private Applicant applicant;                 // shared mutable state\n    private BigDecimal requestedAmount;\n\n    public Decision decide(Applicant a, BigDecimal amount) {\n        this.applicant = a;\n        this.requestedAmount = amount;\n        return evaluate();                       // may see another thread's applicant\n    }\n\n    private Decision evaluate() {\n        return applicant.score() >= 620 ? Decision.approve(requestedAmount) : Decision.decline();\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: stateless; request data lives on the caller's stack\n@Service\npublic class CreditDecisionService {\n    private final ScoringModel model;            // immutable collaborator\n\n    public CreditDecisionService(ScoringModel model) { this.model = model; }\n\n    public Decision decide(Applicant applicant, BigDecimal amount) {\n        int score = model.score(applicant);\n        return score >= 620 ? Decision.approve(amount) : Decision.decline();\n    }\n}","note":"The bad version passes every single-threaded unit test and fails only under concurrent load, by approving or declining the wrong applicant."},{"title":"Using narrower scopes from a singleton","lang":"java","code":"@Component\n@RequestScope                                    // proxied by default, safe to inject\npublic class RequestContext {\n    private String tenantId;\n    public String tenantId() { return tenantId; }\n    public void tenantId(String id) { this.tenantId = id; }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class StatementService {\n    private final RequestContext ctx;                     // really a scoped proxy\n    private final ObjectProvider<PdfRenderer> renderers;  // PdfRenderer is @Scope(\"prototype\")\n\n    public StatementService(RequestContext ctx, ObjectProvider<PdfRenderer> renderers) {\n        this.ctx = ctx;\n        this.renderers = renderers;\n    }\n\n    public byte[] render(Statement statement) {\n        PdfRenderer renderer = renderers.getObject();     // fresh instance per call\n        return renderer.render(statement, ctx.tenantId());\n    }\n}","note":"Injecting `PdfRenderer` directly would give the singleton one renderer forever; the provider and the scoped proxy resolve the right instance on each call."}],"followUps":[{"q":"You inject a prototype-scoped bean into a singleton. How many instances do you get?","a":"Just one per injection point, created when the singleton is built, because injection happens only once; the prototype scope does not make Spring create a fresh object on each method call. To get a new instance per use, inject `ObjectProvider<T>` and call `getObject()`, use a `@Lookup` method, or put the prototype behind a scoped proxy. Often a plain `new` or a small factory method is clearer than a prototype bean."},{"q":"How do request-scoped beans work when injected into a singleton controller?","a":"Spring injects a proxy instead of the real object, and each method call on the proxy looks up the instance bound to the current request, creating it on first use and discarding it when the request ends. Annotating the class with `@RequestScope` sets this up, because it defaults to a class-based scoped proxy. Calling the proxy outside a request, for example from an `@Async` task or a scheduler, fails because no request is bound to that thread."}],"extra":["**Virtual threads:** with `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` (Boot 3.2+) many more requests hit the same singletons at once, so latent races surface faster; the stateless rule matters more, not less.","**Eager creation:** singletons are instantiated at startup by default, so wiring errors fail the deployment; `@Lazy` or `spring.main.lazy-initialization=true` trade that safety for faster startup.","**Prototype lifecycle:** Spring does not call `@PreDestroy` on prototype beans; whoever obtains the instance owns its cleanup.","**ThreadLocal leaks:** stashing request data in a `ThreadLocal` inside a singleton leaks between requests on pooled threads unless it is cleared in `finally`; Java 21 previews `ScopedValue` for bounded, immutable per-call context.","**Enforce it:** treat any non-final instance field in a `@Service`, `@Component` or `@Controller` as a review finding; an ArchUnit rule can check it in CI."],"redFlags":["switches the bean to prototype scope and expects every request to get a fresh instance","synchronizes every method as the fix, ignoring the throughput cost","moves request data into a `ThreadLocal` without clearing it in a `finally` block"]},{"id":82,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"What does @SpringBootApplication do, and what is a starter?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`@SpringBootApplication` combines `@SpringBootConfiguration` (a `@Configuration` that tests can find), `@EnableAutoConfiguration` and `@ComponentScan` rooted at the main class's package. A starter is a dependency descriptor that pulls in a tested set of libraries for one capability, with versions managed by the Spring Boot BOM, and the classes it brings are what make the matching auto-configuration activate.","answer":["`@SpringBootApplication` is a meta-annotation for three things. `@SpringBootConfiguration` marks the class as configuration and is what `@SpringBootTest` and test slices search for, walking upward through the packages. `@EnableAutoConfiguration` imports the auto-configuration classes every jar on the classpath declares, each guarded by conditions, and registers the main class's package as the default base for Spring Data repositories and JPA entity scanning. `@ComponentScan` scans that package and everything below it, with filters that keep auto-configuration classes out.","Package placement is the practical consequence. With the main class in a root package such as `com.acme.orders`, everything below it is found. In a sub-package, siblings are silently skipped; the symptom is \"required a bean of type ... that could not be found\", or `Not a managed type` for entities. Patching that with `scanBasePackages` fixes components but not repositories or entities, which then also need `@EnableJpaRepositories` and `@EntityScan`, so moving the class is the better fix. Never use the default package: scanning would read every class in every jar.","A starter such as `spring-boot-starter-web` contains little or no code: it is a POM depending on Spring MVC, Jackson, embedded Tomcat, logging and Boot's auto-configuration. Versions come from the `spring-boot-dependencies` BOM, inherited through `spring-boot-starter-parent` or imported, so an upgrade moves dozens of libraries to a combination that was tested together. Auto-configuration reacts to the classpath: `@ConditionalOnClass` sees Tomcat and Spring MVC, and `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` backs off when you define your own bean. So dependencies are configuration: the security starter locks every endpoint, and the JPA starter without a database URL fails startup."],"keyPoints":["@SpringBootApplication is configuration plus auto-configuration plus component scan","the main class's package is the root for scanning, repositories and entities","starters are curated dependency sets whose classes trigger auto-configuration"],"examples":[{"title":"What the annotation expands to, and why package placement matters","lang":"java","code":"// Simplified definition from Spring Boot\n@SpringBootConfiguration\n@EnableAutoConfiguration\n@ComponentScan(excludeFilters = {\n        @Filter(type = FilterType.CUSTOM, classes = TypeExcludeFilter.class),\n        @Filter(type = FilterType.CUSTOM, classes = AutoConfigurationExcludeFilter.class) })\npublic @interface SpringBootApplication { /* exclude, scanBasePackages, ... */ }\n\n// com/acme/orders/OrdersApplication.java      root package\n// com/acme/orders/api/OrderController.java    scanned\n// com/acme/orders/domain/Order.java           entity, found\n// com/acme/shared/MoneyFormatter.java         NOT scanned: sibling of the root\n\npackage com.acme.orders;\n\n@SpringBootApplication\npublic class OrdersApplication {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SpringApplication.run(OrdersApplication.class, args);\n    }\n}\n\n// BAD: components found, repositories and entities in com.acme.shared still not\n// @SpringBootApplication(scanBasePackages = {\"com.acme.orders\", \"com.acme.shared\"})\n\n// GOOD: move the code under com.acme.orders, or import its configuration\n// @Import(SharedFormattingConfig.class)","note":"Tests under `src/test/java/com/acme/orders` find `OrdersApplication` automatically because `@SpringBootTest` searches upward from the test's own package."},{"title":"Starters and the BOM in a build that keeps a company parent","lang":"text","code":"<properties>\n  <spring-boot.version>3.5.6</spring-boot.version>\n</properties>\n\n<dependencyManagement>\n  <dependencies>\n    <dependency>\n      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n      <artifactId>spring-boot-dependencies</artifactId>\n      <version>${spring-boot.version}</version>\n      <type>pom</type>\n      <scope>import</scope>\n    </dependency>\n  </dependencies>\n</dependencyManagement>\n\n<dependencies>\n  <!-- no versions: the BOM decides -->\n  <dependency>\n    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>        <!-- MVC, Jackson, Tomcat -->\n  </dependency>\n  <dependency>\n    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId> <!-- not part of web -->\n  </dependency>\n  <dependency>\n    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>   <!-- Hibernate, HikariCP -->\n  </dependency>\n</dependencies>","note":"Importing the BOM aligns versions without replacing your corporate parent, but you lose the Boot parent's plugin defaults (such as compiling with `-parameters`) and must configure them yourself."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does a test fail with \"Unable to find a @SpringBootConfiguration\"?","a":"`@SpringBootTest` and slices such as `@WebMvcTest` search upward from the test's package for a class annotated with `@SpringBootConfiguration`. If the test is not inside the main class's package, the search finds nothing. Mirror the main packages under `src/test/java`, or pass `classes = ...`."},{"q":"When would you exclude an auto-configuration, and how?","a":"When a dependency you cannot remove triggers configuration you do not want, such as a `DataSource` in a service with no database. Use `exclude` on the annotation or the `spring.autoconfigure.exclude` property, which can vary per environment. First check whether defining your own bean already makes it back off; excluding also removes beans other auto-configurations may rely on."}],"extra":["**Parent vs BOM overrides:** with `spring-boot-starter-parent` you override one managed version through a property such as `postgresql.version`; with an imported BOM those properties do nothing, and the override goes in `dependencyManagement` before the Boot BOM.","**Validation is separate:** the web starter does not include Bean Validation, so `@Valid` silently does nothing until `spring-boot-starter-validation` is on the classpath.","**Custom starters:** a company starter is usually an autoconfigure module with `@AutoConfiguration` classes plus a starter POM named `acme-spring-boot-starter`; the `spring-boot-starter-*` prefix is reserved for Spring Boot.","**proxyBeanMethods:** `proxyBeanMethods = false` skips the CGLIB subclass of a configuration class; it is safe when `@Bean` methods never call each other, and Boot's own auto-configurations use it.","**Boot 4 modules:** Spring Boot 4 split auto-configuration into smaller per-technology modules with matching starters, so upgrades can require added or renamed starters."],"redFlags":["Thinks @SpringBootApplication only starts the embedded server","Fixes a misplaced main class with scanBasePackages and then fights missing repositories","Pins explicit versions on every Boot-managed dependency"]},{"id":83,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"You need the AWS `S3Client` and your own `PricingService` as Spring beans. Which registration style suits each, and why?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Use `@Component` stereotypes for classes you own and want discovered by scanning; use `@Bean` factory methods when you need explicit construction: third-party types, several configured instances of one type, or conditional setup.","answer":["`@Component` (and `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Controller`, `@Configuration`) marks your own class for classpath scanning. Spring finds it under the `@ComponentScan` base packages (in Boot, the package of the `@SpringBootApplication` class and below), picks the constructor and wires its parameters. It is the right default for application services, adapters and controllers: one class, one bean, trivial construction.","`@Bean` is a factory method in a `@Configuration` class where you write the construction yourself. Use it for types you cannot annotate (`ObjectMapper`, `RestClient`, `DataSource`, `KafkaTemplate`, a vendor SDK client), for several instances of the same type with different settings distinguished by name and `@Qualifier`, for conditional or environment-specific wiring (`@Profile`, `@ConditionalOnProperty`), and whenever construction needs builders, properties or lifecycle methods.","The trade-off is implicit versus explicit. Scanning is convenient but should stay bounded to your application root: scanning broad packages drags in library or test classes, slows startup and produces surprising beans. `@Bean` is explicit and reviewable but more code. Shared libraries should not ask consumers to scan their packages; they should ship auto-configuration whose `@Bean` methods are guarded by conditions."],"keyPoints":["annotating a class you own lets component scanning find it with no extra wiring","library classes cannot be annotated, so a factory method in a `@Configuration` class builds them","factory methods are the natural home for builders, conditions and several named instances of one type","limiting scanning to your own packages keeps startup fast and predictable"],"examples":[{"title":"Two differently configured HTTP clients","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)\n@EnableConfigurationProperties(ClientsProperties.class)\nclass HttpClientsConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    RestClient creditBureauClient(RestClient.Builder builder, ClientsProperties props) {\n        return builder                                   // prototype: a fresh builder per method\n            .baseUrl(props.creditBureau().baseUrl())\n            .requestFactory(timeouts(Duration.ofMillis(500), Duration.ofSeconds(3)))\n            .defaultHeader(\"X-Api-Key\", props.creditBureau().apiKey())\n            .build();\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    RestClient paymentGatewayClient(RestClient.Builder builder, ClientsProperties props) {\n        return builder\n            .baseUrl(props.paymentGateway().baseUrl())\n            .requestFactory(timeouts(Duration.ofMillis(300), Duration.ofSeconds(10)))\n            .build();\n    }\n\n    private static ClientHttpRequestFactory timeouts(Duration connect, Duration read) {\n        var factory = new SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory();\n        factory.setConnectTimeout(connect);\n        factory.setReadTimeout(read);\n        return factory;\n    }\n}","note":"`RestClient` is a third-party type needed twice with different settings, the textbook case for `@Bean`; the bean names come from the method names."},{"title":"Owned class as @Component consuming qualified beans","lang":"java","code":"@Component\npublic class CreditBureauAdapter {\n    private final RestClient client;\n\n    public CreditBureauAdapter(@Qualifier(\"creditBureauClient\") RestClient client) {\n        this.client = client;\n    }\n\n    public CreditReport fetch(String nationalId) {\n        return client.get()\n            .uri(\"/reports/{id}\", nationalId)\n            .retrieve()\n            .body(CreditReport.class);\n    }\n}","note":"Your own adapter is simply scanned; the `@Qualifier` picks one of the two `RestClient` beans, and without it startup fails with a no-unique-bean error."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What changes if a `@Bean` method lives in a class without `@Configuration`?","a":"The class is processed in lite mode: its bean methods still register beans, but the class is not CGLIB-enhanced, so calling one `@Bean` method from another is a plain Java call that creates a second instance instead of returning the container's singleton. The same applies to `@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)`, which Spring Boot uses for its own auto-configuration to cut startup cost. In both cases, pass dependencies as method parameters rather than calling sibling bean methods."},{"q":"How do you choose between two beans of the same type at an injection point?","a":"Mark the usual choice with `@Primary` so unqualified injection gets it, and use `@Qualifier` with a bean name, or a custom qualifier annotation, where the other one is needed. Injecting `Map<String, T>` or `List<T>` gives you all of them, keyed by bean name in the map case, which suits strategy-style lookups. If no hint exists and the parameter name matches no bean name, startup fails with `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException`."}],"extra":["**Full vs lite mode:** `@Configuration` classes are CGLIB-proxied so calling one `@Bean` method from another returns the singleton; `proxyBeanMethods = false` (what Boot's own auto-configurations use) skips that proxy for faster startup, so inject beans as method parameters instead of calling methods.","**Destroy inference:** a `@Bean` whose type has a public `close()` or `shutdown()` method gets it called on context shutdown automatically; set `destroyMethod = \"\"` for objects you do not own.","**Declared return type:** type matching and conditions such as `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` look at the declared return type before instantiation, so return the most specific type rather than a vague interface.","**No silent overrides:** Spring Boot disables bean definition overriding by default, so two beans with the same name fail startup instead of one silently replacing the other.","**Static post-processor beans:** declare `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` beans as `static` `@Bean` methods so they do not force early instantiation of their configuration class."],"redFlags":["writes `@Bean` methods in a plain class and expects calls between them to return the shared instance","annotates a JPA entity or DTO with `@Component`","cannot explain how a `@Bean` method receives its dependencies through its parameters"]},{"id":84,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"Two beans implement the same interface. How does Spring choose which one to inject?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Spring resolves by type, narrows the candidates with any `@Qualifier`, then prefers a `@Primary` bean, and can fall back to matching the parameter name against bean names. If nothing singles out one bean, startup fails with `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException`; when you actually want every implementation, inject a `List` or `Map` of them, and use `ObjectProvider` for optional or lazy access.","answer":["Injection by type finds every bean assignable to the requested type. One match is injected; zero fails with `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException` unless the dependency is optional; more than one triggers disambiguation. `@Qualifier` filters the candidates, either by bean name (`@Qualifier(\"stripeGateway\")`) or, better, by a custom qualifier annotation such as `@Recurring` that survives class renames and documents intent. Among what remains, a `@Primary` bean wins. Spring can also match the parameter name to a bean name, but that wiring changes silently when someone renames a variable. If nothing decides, the context fails with \"expected single matching bean but found 2\".","Often the right answer is not to choose. Injecting `List<PaymentProvider>` gives you every implementation, sorted by `@Order` or `Ordered`, and `Map<String, PaymentProvider>` gives them keyed by bean name. That is the Strategy pattern without a hand-written factory: each provider says what it supports and a router picks at runtime. Key your own map by a domain value (`provider.method()`) rather than bean names, and fail at startup on duplicates. `ObjectProvider<T>` is the lazy, optional form: `getIfAvailable()` for an optional collaborator, `getIfUnique()` to use a bean only when exactly one exists, `orderedStream()` to iterate. It resolves on every call, so a singleton can also obtain prototype beans through it.","In production the ambiguity usually arrives through auto-configuration. Define a second `DataSource` for reporting and Boot's auto-configured one backs off (`@ConditionalOnMissingBean`), so you now own both, and every unqualified `DataSource` injection point (including JPA's own) fails with `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` unless one is `@Primary`. The pattern that scales is a primary default plus qualified exceptions. Prefer qualifier annotations over string names, and treat a growing number of qualified injection points as a sign that a router or registry belongs there instead."],"keyPoints":["resolution is by type, narrowed by qualifiers, then @Primary","inject a List or Map to get every implementation","unresolved ambiguity fails at startup with NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException"],"examples":[{"title":"A primary default plus a qualified special case","lang":"java","code":"public interface PaymentGateway {\n    AuthorizationResult authorize(PaymentRequest request);\n}\n\n@Qualifier\n@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)\n@Target({ElementType.TYPE, ElementType.METHOD, ElementType.PARAMETER, ElementType.FIELD})\npublic @interface Recurring {}\n\n@Component\n@Primary                       // used wherever no qualifier is given\nclass AdyenGateway implements PaymentGateway { /* ... */ }\n\n@Component\n@Recurring                     // used only where @Recurring is requested\nclass StripeSubscriptionsGateway implements PaymentGateway { /* ... */ }\n\n@Service\nclass CheckoutService {\n    CheckoutService(PaymentGateway gateway) { /* AdyenGateway */ }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass RenewalService {\n    RenewalService(@Recurring PaymentGateway gateway) { /* StripeSubscriptionsGateway */ }\n}\n\n// Without @Primary, CheckoutService fails at startup:\n// NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException: No qualifying bean of type\n// 'com.acme.payments.PaymentGateway' available: expected single matching bean\n// but found 2: adyenGateway,stripeSubscriptionsGateway","note":"The qualifier filters before `@Primary` is considered, so `RenewalService` gets Stripe even though Adyen is primary."},{"title":"A registry of all implementations and an optional collaborator","lang":"java","code":"public interface PaymentProvider {\n    PaymentMethod method();                     // CARD, SWISH, INVOICE\n    PaymentResult pay(PaymentRequest request);\n}\n\n@Service\nclass PaymentRouter {\n    private final Map<PaymentMethod, PaymentProvider> providers;\n    private final ObjectProvider<FraudScreening> fraudScreening;\n\n    PaymentRouter(List<PaymentProvider> all, ObjectProvider<FraudScreening> fraudScreening) {\n        // Key by a domain value, not by bean name; duplicates fail at startup\n        this.providers = all.stream().collect(Collectors.toMap(\n                PaymentProvider::method,\n                Function.identity(),\n                (a, b) -> { throw new IllegalStateException(\"Two providers for \" + a.method()); },\n                () -> new EnumMap<>(PaymentMethod.class)));\n        this.fraudScreening = fraudScreening;\n    }\n\n    PaymentResult pay(PaymentRequest request) {\n        fraudScreening.ifAvailable(screening -> screening.check(request)); // optional bean\n        PaymentProvider provider = providers.get(request.method());\n        if (provider == null) {\n            throw new UnsupportedPaymentMethodException(request.method());\n        }\n        return provider.pay(request);\n    }\n}","note":"Adding a provider means adding a bean. `ObjectProvider` resolves at call time, so the same code runs where no `FraudScreening` bean exists."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Does @Qualifier or @Primary win when both apply?","a":"The qualifier. Qualifiers filter the candidate set first, so an injection point with a qualifier only ever sees matching beans, and `@Primary` only breaks ties among the beans that remain. That is what makes \"primary default, qualified exceptions\" a safe pattern."},{"q":"How do generics take part in resolution?","a":"Spring treats generic type arguments as an implicit qualifier. With beans of type `EventHandler<OrderPlaced>` and `EventHandler<OrderCancelled>`, injecting `EventHandler<OrderPlaced>` is unambiguous, and `List<EventHandler<OrderPlaced>>` collects only the matching ones. Declare `@Bean` methods with the most specific generic return type, because before creation Spring only knows the declared type."}],"extra":["**Zero or one:** an `Optional<FraudScreening>` parameter or `ObjectProvider` expresses an optional bean in the constructor; `required = false` does the same only for fields and setters.","**Ordering:** `@Order` sorts an injected `List` and `orderedStream()`; it does not control singleton startup order.","**@Fallback:** Spring Framework 6.2 added `@Fallback`, the inverse of `@Primary`: such a bean is injected only when no other candidate exists.","**String qualifiers are fragile:** the default bean name is the decapitalized class name, so `@Qualifier(\"adyenGateway\")` breaks on a class rename and refactoring tools cannot see it.","**Parameter names need -parameters:** name-based matching reads constructor parameter names from bytecode, which requires compiling with `-parameters`; Boot's build plugins set it, and Spring 6.1 removed the old debug-info fallback."],"redFlags":["Marks a bean @Primary everywhere instead of qualifying the special case","Relies on bean or parameter names for wiring without realizing it","Writes a switch-based factory instead of injecting a List or Map of implementations"]},{"id":85,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"A `@Transactional` method throws `IOException` after two inserts, and both rows are still committed. Why, and what controls that behavior?","level":"Basic","tldr":"By default Spring rolls back only when a `RuntimeException` or `Error` escapes the transactional method; checked exceptions commit unless you add rollback rules, and an exception caught inside the method triggers no rollback unless it already crossed an inner `@Transactional` proxy, which marks the whole transaction rollback-only.","answer":["The rule comes from the EJB tradition: unchecked exceptions (`RuntimeException` and subclasses, plus `Error`) signal an unexpected failure and roll back, while checked exceptions are treated as business outcomes and the transaction commits. So a method that throws a checked `TransferRejectedException` after it already debited an account commits that partial debit. Fix it with `rollbackFor`, by making the exception unchecked, or on Spring 6.2+ globally with `@EnableTransactionManagement(rollbackOn = RollbackOn.ALL_EXCEPTIONS)`. `noRollbackFor` does the opposite, and when several rules match, the most specific one wins.","The interceptor can only react to exceptions that leave the proxied method. If you catch and log inside the transactional method, the proxy sees a normal return and commits whatever was written before the failure. If you must handle the exception locally, rethrow it (wrapped if needed) or call `TransactionAspectSupport.currentTransactionStatus().setRollbackOnly()` explicitly.","The subtle production case is a joined transaction: an inner `REQUIRED` method throws a runtime exception, the proxy marks the shared transaction rollback-only, and the outer method catches it and carries on. The outer commit then rolls back anyway and throws `UnexpectedRollbackException`. Exception handling and transaction boundaries are one design decision, not two."],"keyPoints":["unchecked exceptions and errors that leave the method mark the transaction for rollback","checked exceptions are treated as expected outcomes and commit unless `rollbackFor` says otherwise","only exceptions that cross the proxy count; anything swallowed inside never reaches Spring","calling `setRollbackOnly()` on the current status rolls back without throwing"],"examples":[{"title":"Checked and swallowed exceptions commit partial work","lang":"java","code":"public class TransferRejectedException extends Exception {\n    public TransferRejectedException(String reason) { super(reason); }\n}\n\n// BAD: both failure paths COMMIT the debit\n@Transactional\npublic void transfer(UUID from, UUID to, BigDecimal amount) throws TransferRejectedException {\n    accounts.debit(from, amount);\n    if (accounts.isBlocked(to)) {\n        throw new TransferRejectedException(\"target blocked\");  // checked: commit\n    }\n    try {\n        accounts.credit(to, amount);\n    } catch (DataAccessException e) {\n        log.error(\"credit failed\", e);                          // swallowed: commit\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: explicit rule, nothing swallowed\n@Transactional(rollbackFor = TransferRejectedException.class)\npublic void transfer(UUID from, UUID to, BigDecimal amount) throws TransferRejectedException {\n    if (accounts.isBlocked(to)) {\n        throw new TransferRejectedException(\"target blocked\");\n    }\n    accounts.debit(from, amount);\n    accounts.credit(to, amount);            // runtime exceptions propagate and roll back\n}","note":"Money moved out of one account and never arrived in the other: the classic result of assuming every exception rolls back."},{"title":"Global rule and local handling with rollback","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\n@EnableTransactionManagement(rollbackOn = RollbackOn.ALL_EXCEPTIONS)   // Spring 6.2+\nclass TransactionConfig {\n}\n\n@Service\nclass ReversalService {\n    private final LedgerService ledger;\n    ReversalService(LedgerService ledger) { this.ledger = ledger; }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public ReversalResult reverse(ReversalCommand cmd) {\n        try {\n            return ledger.reverse(cmd);\n        } catch (LedgerPeriodClosedException e) {\n            // return a business result, but make sure nothing written so far commits\n            TransactionAspectSupport.currentTransactionStatus().setRollbackOnly();\n            return ReversalResult.rejected(e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"`setRollbackOnly()` lets you return a normal response while still discarding the transaction's writes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you make one specific checked exception roll back, or one runtime exception commit?","a":"Declare it on the annotation: `@Transactional(rollbackFor = PaymentDeclinedException.class)` rolls back for that checked type and its subclasses, and `noRollbackFor = DuplicateRequestException.class` lets a runtime exception propagate while still committing. Rules match the thrown type and its supertypes, and the most specific matching rule wins. A team convention, such as making business failures that must roll back unchecked, is better than scattering rules over every method."},{"q":"A test annotated `@Transactional` passes, but the same code fails in production. How can that happen?","a":"Spring's test support rolls back the test-managed transaction at the end and the code under test joins it, so Hibernate may never flush, constraint violations that surface at flush or commit are missed, and `AFTER_COMMIT` listeners never fire. Lazy loading can also succeed in the test only because the persistence context stays open for the whole test method. Call `flush()` in such tests, or run key paths without a surrounding test transaction, to see production behavior."}],"extra":["**Persistence exceptions:** once Hibernate throws (constraint violation, optimistic lock failure), the JPA transaction is marked rollback-only and the `EntityManager` should be considered unusable; you cannot catch it and keep working in the same persistence context.","**Exception translation:** `@Repository` beans get JDBC and Hibernate exceptions translated to Spring's unchecked `DataAccessException` hierarchy, so they roll back by default; `DataIntegrityViolationException` is the one you typically map to HTTP 409.","**Kotlin:** Kotlin has no checked exceptions, so a Java-checked exception thrown from Kotlin code commits silently; Spring's own documentation recommends `RollbackOn.ALL_EXCEPTIONS` for Kotlin applications.","**Reacting to rollbacks:** `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_ROLLBACK)` lets you record metrics or trigger compensation without catching exceptions inside the transaction.","**Programmatic transactions:** with `TransactionTemplate`, a runtime exception thrown from the callback rolls back, and `status.setRollbackOnly()` rolls back while still returning a value."],"redFlags":["adds `rollbackFor = Exception.class` everywhere without deciding which failures should still commit","expects an exception thrown on another thread, such as in an `@Async` task, to roll back the caller","assumes the database rolls back by itself whenever the Java method fails"]},{"id":86,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"How do you handle exceptions globally in a Spring REST API?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Put one `@RestControllerAdvice` with `@ExceptionHandler` methods in charge of mapping exceptions to statuses, and return `ProblemDetail` so every error is an RFC 9457 `application/problem+json` body. Extend `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler` so Spring MVC's own exceptions use the same format, and keep a catch-all that logs the stack trace server-side but returns only a generic message.","answer":["When a handler method throws, `DispatcherServlet` asks its exception resolvers in order. `ExceptionHandlerExceptionResolver` looks for an `@ExceptionHandler` in the controller, then in `@ControllerAdvice` beans; next come `@ResponseStatus` handling and the defaults for Spring MVC's own exceptions. Within one advice the closest match in the exception hierarchy wins, but across several advices the first one in `@Order` that can handle the exception wins, even if a later advice has a more specific handler. An advice can be scoped with `basePackages` or `assignableTypes`.","`ProblemDetail` is Spring's model of RFC 9457 (which replaced RFC 7807): `type` (a URI naming the problem class), `title`, `status`, `detail`, `instance`, plus extension members added with `setProperty`, such as an error code, a trace id or a list of field errors. Returning it from an `@ExceptionHandler` sets the status and the `application/problem+json` content type. Extending `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler` gives you `ProblemDetail` handlers for all Spring MVC exceptions; override the ones to enrich, typically `handleMethodArgumentNotValid`. In Spring Boot, `spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true` registers such an advice if you have not written your own.","Never leak internals: the catch-all logs the exception with the trace id and returns a generic `500` whose `detail` says nothing about SQL, class names or stack frames, and Boot's `server.error.include-stacktrace` and `include-message` stay at their default of `never`. Log expected client errors (validation, not found, conflict) at `INFO` or `WARN` without a stack trace and unexpected ones once at `ERROR`, so alerts mean something. The advice only sees exceptions thrown inside `DispatcherServlet`; failures in servlet filters, including Spring Security's authentication and access-denied paths, end up at Boot's `/error` endpoint or the security handlers, so make those emit the same shape."],"keyPoints":["one @RestControllerAdvice maps exceptions to statuses and bodies","problem details give clients an RFC 9457 problem+json contract","log details server-side, return generic messages for unexpected errors"],"examples":[{"title":"A central advice producing ProblemDetail","lang":"java","code":"@RestControllerAdvice\nclass ApiExceptionHandler extends ResponseEntityExceptionHandler {\n\n    @ExceptionHandler(OrderNotFoundException.class)\n    ProblemDetail orderNotFound(OrderNotFoundException ex) {\n        ProblemDetail problem = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(\n                HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, \"Order \" + ex.orderId() + \" does not exist\");\n        problem.setType(URI.create(\"https://api.acme.com/problems/order-not-found\"));\n        problem.setProperty(\"code\", \"ORDER_NOT_FOUND\");\n        return problem;\n    }\n\n    @Override // @Valid @RequestBody failures: 400 with one entry per field\n    protected ResponseEntity<Object> handleMethodArgumentNotValid(\n            MethodArgumentNotValidException ex, HttpHeaders headers,\n            HttpStatusCode status, WebRequest request) {\n        ProblemDetail problem = ex.getBody();\n        problem.setDetail(\"Request has invalid fields\");\n        problem.setProperty(\"errors\", ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors().stream()\n                .map(e -> Map.of(\"field\", e.getField(), \"message\", e.getDefaultMessage()))\n                .toList());\n        return handleExceptionInternal(ex, problem, headers, status, request);\n    }\n\n    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class) // last resort: log everything, reveal nothing\n    ProblemDetail unexpected(Exception ex) {\n        logger.error(\"Unhandled exception\", ex);       // inherited logger\n        ProblemDetail problem = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(\n                HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR, \"Unexpected error\");\n        problem.setProperty(\"traceId\", MDC.get(\"traceId\"));\n        return problem;\n    }\n}","note":"The `Exception` handler does not swallow Spring's own exceptions: the inherited handlers are more specific matches in the same advice, so they keep their 4xx statuses."},{"title":"What clients receive","lang":"http","code":"HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request\nContent-Type: application/problem+json\n\n{\n  \"type\": \"about:blank\",\n  \"title\": \"Bad Request\",\n  \"status\": 400,\n  \"detail\": \"Request has invalid fields\",\n  \"instance\": \"/orders\",\n  \"errors\": [\n    { \"field\": \"customerId\", \"message\": \"must not be blank\" },\n    { \"field\": \"lines\", \"message\": \"must not be empty\" }\n  ]\n}\n\nHTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error\nContent-Type: application/problem+json\n\n{\n  \"type\": \"about:blank\",\n  \"title\": \"Internal Server Error\",\n  \"status\": 500,\n  \"detail\": \"Unexpected error\",\n  \"instance\": \"/orders\",\n  \"traceId\": \"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\"\n}","note":"The 500 body carries a trace id and nothing about the cause; the stack trace lives in the log line with the same id. Keep `type` URIs and `code` values stable, because clients branch on them."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does an exception thrown in a filter not reach your @RestControllerAdvice?","a":"Advice is applied by `DispatcherServlet`'s exception resolvers, and filters run outside the servlet. A filter exception goes to the servlet container, which forwards to Boot's `/error` endpoint. Handle it in the filter, delegate to the `HandlerExceptionResolver` bean, or give Spring Security an `AuthenticationEntryPoint` and `AccessDeniedHandler` that write a `ProblemDetail`."},{"q":"@ResponseStatus on the exception or a central handler: which is better?","a":"`@ResponseStatus` on a domain exception is quick but couples the domain to HTTP and controls neither body nor logging. For an API with a published error contract, keep domain exceptions free of web annotations and let one advice decide status, body and log level."}],"extra":["**ErrorResponseException:** web-layer exceptions can extend `ErrorResponseException` to carry their own `ProblemDetail`; keep domain exceptions free of HTTP.","**Message codes:** `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler` resolves `detail` and `title` through `MessageSource` with codes such as `problemDetail.` plus the exception class name, so wording can change without code.","**Malformed JSON:** `HttpMessageNotReadableException` messages contain parser details and type names; the inherited handler returns a generic detail, so do not override it by echoing `ex.getMessage()`.","**Committed responses:** once a streamed response has started, a later exception cannot change the status; the client sees a truncated 200.","**Test the contract:** `@WebMvcTest` cases asserting status, content type and `code` for each mapped exception keep the error contract from drifting."],"redFlags":["Wraps every controller method in try/catch","Returns ex.getMessage() or a stack trace to the client","Does not know that filter and security exceptions bypass the advice"]},{"id":87,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"How does Bean Validation work with @Valid and @Validated in Spring?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`@Valid` (Jakarta) tells Spring to validate an argument, or to cascade into a nested object, against its constraint annotations; `@Validated` (Spring) does the same but accepts validation groups, and on a class it turns on method validation through an AOP proxy. A failed `@Valid @RequestBody` raises `MethodArgumentNotValidException`; proxy-based method validation raises `ConstraintViolationException`.","answer":["Constraints such as `@NotBlank`, `@Positive` and `@Size` live on fields or record components, and Hibernate Validator evaluates them. In a controller, `@Valid @RequestBody CreateOrderRequest request` makes Spring validate right after deserialization; a failure becomes `MethodArgumentNotValidException`, whose `BindingResult` holds every field error, and the default response is `400`. Validation does not cascade by itself: a nested object or the elements of a list are only checked if marked `@Valid` (for collections, `List<@Valid OrderLine>`). `@Validated(OnCreate.class)` on the parameter selects groups, so one DTO can require `id` to be null on create and present on update.","Validation is not only for controllers. Annotate a service with `@Validated` and constrain its parameters and return values directly; Boot's `MethodValidationPostProcessor` wraps the bean in a proxy that validates every call and throws `ConstraintViolationException`. Self-invocation skips the proxy, as with `@Transactional`. Spring Framework 6.1 also added built-in method validation to Spring MVC: when constraints sit directly on controller parameters, such as `@PathVariable @Min(1) long id`, and the controller is not `@Validated`, failures arrive as `HandlerMethodValidationException`, which then also covers a `@Valid` body in that method. The exception you get depends on version and setup, so handle all three and test it.","Custom constraints cover what the built-ins cannot: an annotation with `@Constraint(validatedBy = ...)` and the required `message`, `groups` and `payload` attributes, plus a `ConstraintValidator`. Spring-created validators can inject beans, but keep them to format and shape: a uniqueness check (\"email not taken\") in a validator is racy and costs a query per request, and belongs to a unique constraint in the database. Cross-field rules such as an end date after a start date go in a class-level constraint. Typical production failures: an unhandled `ConstraintViolationException` turning bad input into a `500`, and error bodies echoing rejected personal data."],"keyPoints":["@Valid triggers and cascades validation, @Validated adds groups and method validation","body validation and method validation raise different exceptions","validators check shape; the database enforces uniqueness"],"examples":[{"title":"Groups, cascading and controller validation","lang":"java","code":"public interface OnCreate extends Default {}   // also runs constraints without a group\npublic interface OnUpdate extends Default {}\n\npublic record OrderRequest(\n        @Null(groups = OnCreate.class) @NotNull(groups = OnUpdate.class) UUID id,\n        @NotBlank String customerId,\n        @NotEmpty List<@Valid OrderLineRequest> lines) {}   // cascade into every line\n\npublic record OrderLineRequest(@NotBlank String sku, @Positive int quantity) {}\n\n@RestController\n@RequestMapping(\"/orders\")\nclass OrderController {\n    private final OrderService orders;\n\n    OrderController(OrderService orders) {\n        this.orders = orders;\n    }\n\n    @PostMapping\n    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)\n    OrderResponse create(@Validated(OnCreate.class) @RequestBody OrderRequest request) {\n        return orders.create(request);\n    }\n\n    @PutMapping(\"/{id}\")\n    OrderResponse update(@PathVariable UUID id,\n                         @Validated(OnUpdate.class) @RequestBody OrderRequest request) {\n        return orders.update(id, request);\n    }\n}","note":"Without `extends Default`, validating with `OnCreate` would skip `@NotBlank` and every constraint in `OrderLineRequest`, because cascaded objects are validated with the same group."},{"title":"A custom constraint and service method validation","lang":"java","code":"@Documented\n@Constraint(validatedBy = IbanValidator.class)\n@Target({ElementType.FIELD, ElementType.PARAMETER})\n@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)\npublic @interface ValidIban {\n    String message() default \"must be a valid IBAN\";\n    Class<?>[] groups() default {};\n    Class<? extends Payload>[] payload() default {};\n}\n\npublic class IbanValidator implements ConstraintValidator<ValidIban, String> {\n    @Override\n    public boolean isValid(String value, ConstraintValidatorContext context) {\n        if (value == null) {\n            return true;                  // null is @NotNull's job\n        }\n        return IbanChecksum.isValid(value.replace(\" \", \"\"));\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\n@Validated                                // proxy validates calls from other beans\npublic class PayoutService {\n    public PayoutId schedule(@NotNull @Valid PayoutCommand command,\n                             @ValidIban String destinationIban) {\n        // ...\n    }\n}\n// Invalid input: jakarta.validation.ConstraintViolationException, a 500 unless handled","note":"Returning `true` for null keeps the constraint composable with `@NotNull`. A call from inside `PayoutService` bypasses the proxy."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you turn these exceptions into one consistent 400 response?","a":"Handle each in your `@RestControllerAdvice` and build the same `errors` list: field errors from `getBindingResult()` for `MethodArgumentNotValidException`, `getPropertyPath()` for `ConstraintViolationException`, and the per-parameter results of `HandlerMethodValidationException`. Map a service-level violation to 400 only when its input came straight from the request; otherwise it is a bug and a 500 is honest."},{"q":"Why do groups sometimes skip constraints you expected to run?","a":"A constraint without `groups` belongs to `Default`, and `@Validated(OnCreate.class)` checks only `OnCreate` constraints. Objects cascaded with `@Valid` are validated with the same group, so their default constraints are skipped too. Make group interfaces extend `jakarta.validation.groups.Default`, or list `Default.class` explicitly, and test both paths."}],"extra":["**Missing dependency:** without `spring-boot-starter-validation`, `@Valid` is silently ignored; one test posting an invalid body proves validation is on.","**Configuration properties:** a `@ConfigurationProperties` class annotated with `@Validated` is validated at startup, so a missing or out-of-range setting stops the deployment.","**Messages and PII:** custom messages that interpolate `${validatedValue}` copy user input into responses and logs; keep IBANs and emails out of error bodies.","**Programmatic validation:** inject `jakarta.validation.Validator` and call `validate(...)` where annotations cannot reach, such as consumed Kafka messages.","**Fail-fast:** Hibernate Validator reports every violation by default, which API clients want; fail-fast mode suits internal hot paths."],"redFlags":["Thinks @Valid covers nested objects or list elements without marking them","Lets ConstraintViolationException surface as a 500","Relies on a validator query as the only guard for uniqueness"]},{"id":88,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"One jar runs in dev, staging and production. How do you feed it environment settings and keep its credentials safe?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Bind configuration into validated, typed `@ConfigurationProperties` that fail fast at startup, supply environment differences from outside the artifact, and keep secrets in a secret manager or Kubernetes Secret, never in Git.","answer":["Build one artifact and promote it unchanged through environments; everything that differs comes from outside it: environment variables, mounted files, config imports or a config server. Spring Boot's property source order (command-line arguments, environment variables with relaxed binding such as `SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL`, profile-specific files, then `application.yaml`) makes this natural. Use profiles for environment wiring, not for business logic.","Make configuration typed and validated. Group related settings into one `@ConfigurationProperties` record per concern, annotate it with `@Validated` and Bean Validation constraints, and let the context refuse to start when a required URL or credential is missing. No silent defaults for anything environment-specific: a default production URL or an empty fallback password turns a deployment mistake into a data incident. Scattered `@Value` strings are untyped, unvalidated and hard to discover.","Secrets never go into Git, Dockerfiles, Helm values or `application-prod.yaml`. Keep them in a secret manager (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault) or Kubernetes Secrets, ideally synced by the External Secrets Operator or a CSI driver, and mount them as files read through `spring.config.import=configtree:`. Files beat environment variables, which leak into child processes, crash dumps and debug output. A Kubernetes Secret is only base64 encoded, so enable etcd encryption and restrict RBAC on secrets. Add secret scanning (gitleaks, push protection) in CI; a leaked secret must be rotated, not just deleted from history."],"keyPoints":["build once and inject differences at deploy time through environment variables, mounted files or a config service","bind settings into typed, validated classes so a bad value stops startup instead of a later request","credentials come from a vault or orchestrator secret and never appear in the repository or image","required values should have no fallback, so a missing one is loud"],"examples":[{"title":"Typed, validated properties that fail fast","lang":"java","code":"@Validated\n@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = \"credit.bureau\")\npublic record CreditBureauProperties(\n        @NotNull URI baseUrl,\n        @NotBlank String apiKey,\n        @NotNull Duration connectTimeout,\n        @NotNull Duration readTimeout,\n        @DefaultValue(\"3\") @Min(1) @Max(10) int maxAttempts) {\n\n    @AssertTrue(message = \"readTimeout must be greater than connectTimeout\")\n    public boolean isTimeoutOrderValid() {\n        return readTimeout == null || connectTimeout == null\n            || readTimeout.compareTo(connectTimeout) > 0;\n    }\n}\n\n@SpringBootApplication\n@ConfigurationPropertiesScan\npublic class CreditServiceApplication {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SpringApplication.run(CreditServiceApplication.class, args);\n    }\n}\n// Missing api-key: APPLICATION FAILED TO START, Property: credit.bureau.apiKey, Reason: must not be blank","note":"Durations parse from values like `500ms` or `2s`, and a broken deployment is rejected before it takes traffic instead of failing on the first credit check."},{"title":"Committed config vs mounted secret","lang":"yaml","code":"# File 1: application.yaml (committed): structure and non-secret settings only\nspring:\n  config:\n    import: \"optional:configtree:/etc/secrets/\"\ncredit:\n  bureau:\n    base-url: ${CREDIT_BUREAU_URL}     # no default: startup fails if unset\n    connect-timeout: 500ms\n    read-timeout: 2s\n    # credit.bureau.api-key arrives as the file /etc/secrets/credit.bureau.api-key\n---\n# File 2: pod spec excerpt; the Secret is synced from Vault by External Secrets Operator\ncontainers:\n  - name: credit-service\n    env:\n      - name: CREDIT_BUREAU_URL\n        value: https://api.bureau.example.com\n    volumeMounts:\n      - name: app-secrets\n        mountPath: /etc/secrets\n        readOnly: true\nvolumes:\n  - name: app-secrets\n    secret:\n      secretName: credit-service-secrets","note":"With `configtree:` each file name becomes a property name, so the Secret key `credit.bureau.api-key` binds straight into the record without appearing in any committed file."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Kubernetes Secrets are only base64-encoded. How do you actually protect them?","a":"Turn on encryption at rest for Secrets in etcd, ideally through a KMS provider, and limit who can `get` or `list` them with RBAC scoped to namespaces and service accounts. Keep the source of truth in a dedicated store such as Vault or a cloud secret manager and sync values in with the External Secrets Operator or the Secrets Store CSI driver, instead of committing Secret manifests to Git. Mounting secrets as files rather than environment variables also keeps them out of crash reports and debug output that print the environment."},{"q":"How do you stop a secret from ending up in logs or actuator output?","a":"Never log raw configuration objects, and give types that carry secrets a `toString()` that masks them; records print every component by default, so override it there. In Spring Boot 3 the actuator `env` and `configprops` endpoints mask values by default, so keep `management.endpoint.env.show-values` at `NEVER` or `WHEN_AUTHORIZED` and expose those endpoints only internally. As a safety net, add a check in CI or the log pipeline that flags patterns resembling keys and tokens."}],"extra":["**Actuator masking:** since Boot 3.0 `/actuator/env` and `/actuator/configprops` mask all values by default; keep it that way and never expose those endpoints publicly.","**Configuration metadata:** adding `spring-boot-configuration-processor` generates metadata so IDEs autocomplete and document your custom properties and flag unknown keys.","**Rotation and mounts:** Kubernetes eventually refreshes mounted Secret files, but not `subPath` mounts or environment variables; either way Spring binds configuration at startup, so rotation needs a restart or an explicit refresh mechanism.","**Dynamic credentials:** Vault's database secrets engine issues short-lived, per-instance database users with leases, which removes long-lived shared passwords entirely.","**Cross-field validation:** an `@AssertTrue` method on the properties record (as in the example) catches inconsistent combinations such as a read timeout shorter than the connect timeout at startup."],"redFlags":["builds a separate artifact for each environment","logs the full configuration at startup, passwords included","treats the base64 encoding of a Kubernetes Secret as encryption"]},{"id":89,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"What does Spring Boot Actuator give you, and what should you not expose?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Actuator adds operational endpoints under `/actuator`: health with liveness and readiness groups, Micrometer metrics with Prometheus or OTLP export, info, loggers, and diagnostics such as `env`, `beans`, `threaddump` and `heapdump`. Expose an explicit list, never `*`, and keep diagnostics off the public network: a heap dump contains every secret and token in memory.","answer":["Each endpoint has an id and is reachable only when access to it is allowed and it is exposed over HTTP or JMX. Over HTTP only `health` is exposed by default; `management.endpoints.web.exposure.include` lists the rest. `health` aggregates `HealthIndicator`s (database, disk space, Redis, broker) into one status, and with probes enabled (automatic on Kubernetes) Boot adds `/actuator/health/liveness` and `/actuator/health/readiness` groups backed by the application's availability state. `metrics` and `prometheus` come from Micrometer, which instruments HTTP requests, JVM memory and GC, HikariCP pools and executors, and your own meters go on the same `MeterRegistry`.","Probe groups are where teams hurt themselves. Liveness asks \"is this JVM broken beyond repair\", so it must not include the database: if Postgres hiccups, Kubernetes restarts every pod at once and a dependency blip becomes a full outage with a cold-start storm. Readiness asks \"should this pod get traffic now\"; adding a shared dependency there pulls all pods out of the load balancer together, which is rarely better than failing fast. Boot's defaults (`livenessState` and `readinessState` only) are the right start, and the app flips readiness itself during startup and graceful shutdown. Use `show-details: when-authorized` so component details stay private.","Some endpoints are dangerous on a reachable port. `heapdump` hands out passwords, tokens and customer data in clear; `env` lists property sources and `configprops` the bound `@ConfigurationProperties` values (Boot 3 masks values by default, keep it that way); `beans`, `mappings` and `conditions` map your internals for an attacker; `loggers` accepts POSTs that change log levels. The production pattern: an explicit include list (`health`, `info`, `prometheus`), the management server on a separate `management.server.port` that the ingress does not route, Spring Security on actuator paths, and diagnostics only for operators or through a port-forward. Recent Boot versions also set an `access` level per endpoint (`none`, `read-only`, `unrestricted`)."],"keyPoints":["health groups for probes, metrics through Micrometer","expose an explicit list over HTTP, never everything","heapdump, env and beans belong behind auth or off the network"],"examples":[{"title":"Production management configuration","lang":"yaml","code":"management:\n  server:\n    port: 8081                        # not routed by the public ingress\n  endpoints:\n    web:\n      exposure:\n        include: health,info,prometheus\n  endpoint:\n    health:\n      probes:\n        enabled: true                 # /actuator/health/liveness and /readiness\n        add-additional-paths: true    # also /livez and /readyz on the main port\n      show-details: when-authorized\n      group:\n        readiness:\n          include: readinessState     # no shared dependencies here\n    env:\n      show-values: never\n  metrics:\n    tags:\n      application: payments-api       # common tag on every meter\n    distribution:\n      percentiles-histogram:\n        http.server.requests: true    # buckets for latency SLOs","note":"Point the kubelet at `/livez` and `/readyz` on the main port: a healthy management port can hide a broken main connector."},{"title":"Securing the endpoints and adding a business metric","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass ActuatorSecurityConfig {\n    @Bean\n    @Order(1)\n    SecurityFilterChain actuatorChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        http.securityMatcher(EndpointRequest.toAnyEndpoint())\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\n                .requestMatchers(EndpointRequest.to(\"health\", \"info\")).permitAll()\n                .requestMatchers(EndpointRequest.to(\"prometheus\")).hasRole(\"METRICS\")\n                .anyRequest().hasRole(\"OPS\"))       // env, loggers, heapdump, ...\n            .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());\n        return http.build();\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass PayoutService {\n    private final PayoutProvider provider;\n    private final MeterRegistry registry;\n    private final Timer providerLatency;\n\n    PayoutService(PayoutProvider provider, MeterRegistry registry) {\n        this.provider = provider;\n        this.registry = registry;\n        this.providerLatency = Timer.builder(\"payouts.provider.latency\").register(registry);\n    }\n\n    PayoutResult execute(Payout payout) {\n        PayoutResult result = providerLatency.record(() -> provider.send(payout));\n        if (result.rejected()) {\n            // tag values from a small enum, never ids or free text\n            registry.counter(\"payouts.rejected\", \"reason\", result.reason().name()).increment();\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n}","note":"Health stays public so probes need no credentials (details are hidden), Prometheus authenticates, and everything else needs an operator role."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Should readiness check the database?","a":"Usually not by default. If all replicas share the database, a failing readiness check removes every pod together, and clients get connection errors instead of fast 503s from your app. Include a dependency in readiness only when it is per pod (a sidecar, a cache that must load first) and the pod is useless without it; never include it in liveness."},{"q":"How do @Timed and the Observation API relate to Actuator metrics?","a":"Micrometer is the instrumentation library; Actuator auto-configures its registry and exporters. `@Timed` on arbitrary methods needs a `TimedAspect` bean. The Observation API (`@Observed` with an `ObservedAspect`, or `Observation` in code) records one observation that yields both a timer and a trace span, and is how Spring's own HTTP instrumentation works since Boot 3."}],"extra":["**Info endpoint:** publish build and git metadata (the Boot plugin's `build-info` goal plus a git properties plugin) so \"which version is running\" has an answer during an incident; it is not exposed by default.","**Custom health indicators:** keep them fast with a timeout and put them in a separate group for dashboards, not in the probe groups.","**Loggers at runtime:** a POST to `/actuator/loggers/com.acme.payments` with a `configuredLevel` switches to DEBUG without a restart; useful in incidents, and exactly why it needs an operator role.","**Histogram cost:** percentile histograms add dozens of bucket series per timer and tag combination; enable them where you have SLOs, not globally.","**Startup endpoint:** with `BufferingApplicationStartup` configured, the `startup` endpoint shows how long each bean took to create, which finds slow initialization."],"redFlags":["Sets the exposure include list to * in production","Puts the database in the liveness probe","Does not know heapdump leaks secrets"]},{"id":90,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"A public method calls `this.saveAudit()`, which is annotated `@Transactional`, yet no transaction starts. Why, and how do proxies explain `@Async` too?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Spring wraps the bean in a proxy whose interceptors start transactions or hand work to an executor, but only calls entering through the proxy are advised, so `this.method()` silently skips `@Transactional` and `@Async`.","answer":["Annotations do not change bytecode. At startup a `BeanPostProcessor` (the auto-proxy creator) sees `@Transactional` or `@Async` and replaces the bean with a proxy, and that proxy is what gets injected everywhere. An external call hits the proxy, runs an interceptor chain, then delegates to the target. `TransactionInterceptor` obtains a transaction from the `PlatformTransactionManager`, binds the connection to the current thread, invokes the method, then commits or rolls back. `AsyncExecutionInterceptor` submits the invocation to a `TaskExecutor` and returns immediately with `void` or a `CompletableFuture`.","Self-invocation: once inside the target, `this.otherMethod()` is a plain Java call on the raw object, not on the proxy, so no advice runs. A `REQUIRES_NEW` audit method called internally just joins the outer transaction, and an internal `@Async` call runs synchronously on the caller's thread. There is no error, only wrong behavior.","Proxy type matters. JDK dynamic proxies implement the bean's interfaces, so only interface methods are intercepted and injecting the concrete class fails. CGLIB proxies subclass the target (Spring Boot's default), so `final` classes cannot be proxied and `final` or `private` methods are silently not intercepted. Since Spring 6.0, `protected` and package-private methods can be transactional with class-based proxies, while interface proxies still need public interface methods. The proxy is also not in play during `@PostConstruct`, so do not rely on transactions there."],"keyPoints":["other beans receive a wrapper object whose interceptors run before and after the real method","a call from inside the same object goes straight to the target and skips every interceptor","private, final or static methods cannot be intercepted by the default proxies","an interface-based JDK proxy and a CGLIB subclass differ in what can be injected and intercepted"],"examples":[{"title":"Self-invocation bug and the structural fix","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: internal call bypasses the proxy; the audit joins (and dies with) the outer transaction\n@Service\npublic class OrderService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n    private final AuditRepository audit;\n    // constructor omitted\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void placeOrder(Order order) {\n        orders.save(order);\n        this.writeAudit(order.getId(), \"PLACED\");   // plain call on the target\n    }\n\n    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)\n    public void writeAudit(UUID orderId, String event) {\n        audit.save(new AuditEntry(orderId, event));\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: the advised method lives in another bean, so the call crosses a proxy\n@Service\npublic class AuditService {\n    private final AuditRepository audit;\n    public AuditService(AuditRepository audit) { this.audit = audit; }\n\n    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)\n    public void write(UUID orderId, String event) {\n        audit.save(new AuditEntry(orderId, event));\n    }\n}\n// OrderService injects AuditService and calls auditService.write(...): new transaction","note":"Both versions compile and look identical in review; only the second actually runs the audit in its own transaction."},{"title":"Proving what was proxied","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass ProxyCheck implements ApplicationRunner {\n    private final OrderService orderService;\n\n    ProxyCheck(OrderService orderService) { this.orderService = orderService; }\n\n    @Override\n    public void run(ApplicationArguments args) {\n        System.out.println(orderService.getClass());               // OrderService$$SpringCGLIB$$0\n        System.out.println(AopUtils.isAopProxy(orderService));     // true\n        System.out.println(AopUtils.isCglibProxy(orderService));   // true\n    }\n}\n// application.properties:\n// logging.level.org.springframework.transaction.interceptor=TRACE\n// logs \"Getting transaction for [...]\" only for calls that went through the proxy","note":"When a transaction seems to be ignored, TRACE logging on the transaction interceptor shows immediately whether the call was intercepted at all."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you prove in a test that a method really runs inside a transaction?","a":"Assert it from inside the code path: `TransactionSynchronizationManager.isActualTransactionActive()` returns true only when a transaction is bound to the current thread. In an integration test you can also verify behavior, for example that a failure after the first write leaves no rows behind. A plain unit test that creates the class with `new` proves nothing here, because there is no proxy; the advice exists only on the bean Spring creates."},{"q":"What does `@Async` return to the caller, and what happens to an exception thrown inside it?","a":"The caller gets control back immediately; a `void` method returns nothing, and a method declared to return `CompletableFuture<T>` hands back a future that completes with the result or the exception. An exception from a `void` `@Async` method never reaches the caller and goes to the `AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler`, which by default only logs it. The work also runs on an executor thread, so it does not join the caller's transaction or see its `ThreadLocal` context."}],"extra":["**Advice ordering:** when a method carries `@Transactional` plus a retry or custom aspect, the order decides nesting; retry must wrap the transaction so every attempt gets a fresh transaction and persistence context.","**Async context loss:** `@Async` runs on another thread, so the caller's transaction, `SecurityContext` and MDC do not follow; use a `TaskDecorator` for MDC and security context and give the async method its own `@Transactional`.","**Async exceptions:** exceptions from `void` `@Async` methods never reach the caller; they go to the `AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler`, so return a `CompletableFuture` when the caller needs the outcome.","**AspectJ mode:** `@EnableTransactionManagement(mode = AdviceMode.ASPECTJ)` with compile-time or load-time weaving advises internal calls and private methods, at the cost of build or agent complexity.","**Spring 6 naming:** CGLIB proxy classes are now named `...$$SpringCGLIB$$0`, which is a quick way to spot a proxy in a stack trace or debugger."],"redFlags":["puts `@Transactional` on a private method and expects it to apply","assumes an `@Async` method called from the same class still runs on another thread","forgets that `@Async` does nothing until async support is enabled with `@EnableAsync`"]},{"id":91,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"A batch import must skip bad rows but keep good ones. Which propagation fits, and how do `REQUIRED`, `NESTED` and `REQUIRES_NEW` differ?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"`REQUIRED` joins the current transaction or starts one, `REQUIRES_NEW` suspends it and runs an independent physical transaction on a second connection, and `NESTED` stays in the same transaction using a savepoint for partial rollback.","answer":["`REQUIRED` is the default: if a transaction is active the method joins it, otherwise it starts one. Joined methods share one physical transaction and one connection, so an unchecked exception escaping an inner method marks the whole transaction rollback-only, even if the outer method catches it; the outer commit then fails with `UnexpectedRollbackException`. Isolation, timeout and read-only settings on a joining method are ignored.","`REQUIRES_NEW` suspends the outer transaction and starts an independent physical transaction on a second pooled connection. It commits or rolls back on its own: its commit survives an outer rollback, and its failure does not doom the outer transaction if caught. The costs are real: two connections per thread (pool deadlock when many threads each wait for their second connection), no visibility of the outer transaction's uncommitted rows, and a self-deadlock if it tries to lock a row the suspended outer transaction already holds.","`NESTED` is one physical transaction with a JDBC savepoint: an inner failure rolls back to the savepoint and the outer can continue, but an outer rollback undoes everything, inner work included. It needs savepoint support: it works with `DataSourceTransactionManager` and `JdbcTransactionManager`, while `JpaTransactionManager` disallows it by default because rolling back a savepoint does not roll back entity state in the persistence context.","Use `REQUIRED` almost always, `REQUIRES_NEW` for work that must outlive a failure (audit of attempts, idempotency markers), and `NESTED` for best-effort sub-steps in JDBC code."],"keyPoints":["the default `REQUIRED` reuses the caller's transaction, so one inner failure dooms the whole unit","with `REQUIRES_NEW` the caller's transaction is parked and a second connection is opened, which can drain a small pool","a `NESTED` call sets a savepoint, so it can undo its own part while still committing with the outer transaction","whether savepoints work at all depends on the transaction manager in use"],"examples":[{"title":"Audit of attempts with REQUIRES_NEW","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class LoanApprovalService {\n    private final LoanRepository loans;\n    private final AuditLog auditLog;              // separate bean, so propagation applies\n\n    public LoanApprovalService(LoanRepository loans, AuditLog auditLog) {\n        this.loans = loans;\n        this.auditLog = auditLog;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional                                // REQUIRED\n    public void approve(UUID loanId, String officer) {\n        auditLog.record(loanId, officer, \"APPROVAL_ATTEMPTED\");   // committed immediately\n        Loan loan = loans.findById(loanId).orElseThrow();\n        loan.approve(officer);                    // may throw LimitExceededException\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class AuditLog {\n    private final AuditRepository repo;\n    public AuditLog(AuditRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }\n\n    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)   // own connection, own commit\n    public void record(UUID loanId, String actor, String event) {\n        repo.save(new AuditEntry(loanId, actor, event, Instant.now()));\n    }\n}","note":"The audit row survives if `approve` rolls back, which is why it records an attempt rather than an approval; while `record` runs, the thread holds two connections."},{"title":"Propagation cheat sheet","lang":"text","code":"Propagation   | Outer tx exists          | Connections | Inner failure            | Later outer rollback\nREQUIRED      | join it                  | 1           | whole tx rollback-only   | undoes inner work\nREQUIRES_NEW  | suspend, start new tx    | 2           | only inner rolls back    | inner already committed\nNESTED        | savepoint in same tx     | 1           | roll back to savepoint   | undoes inner work\n(no outer tx) | all three simply start a new physical transaction","note":"The last column is the one interviewers probe: only `REQUIRES_NEW` produces work that outlives an outer rollback."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Under load, methods using `REQUIRES_NEW` start timing out on connection acquisition. Why?","a":"Each thread already holds one connection for the suspended outer transaction and then asks the pool for a second one. If enough threads do this at once, every connection is held by an outer transaction waiting for an inner one, and the pool deadlocks until acquire timeouts fire. Size the pool for that pattern, use it less often, or move the independent work outside the outer transaction."},{"q":"How would you implement skip-bad-rows with `NESTED`, and what is the catch with JPA?","a":"Call a `NESTED` method for each row from the outer import transaction and catch the failure for a bad row: Spring rolls back to that row's savepoint and the outer transaction continues with the rest. With JPA, a savepoint rollback does not reset the persistence context, so entities touched in the failed attempt may still be flushed later, and `JpaTransactionManager` only allows nesting when `nestedTransactionAllowed` is enabled. With JPA it is often simpler to validate rows up front or to commit small chunks with `REQUIRES_NEW`."}],"extra":["**Pool deadlock math:** with a pool of 10 and 10 concurrent requests that each hold an outer connection and wait for a `REQUIRES_NEW` connection, everyone blocks until HikariCP's `connectionTimeout` (30 s by default); size the pool for the worst-case connections per request.","**Other propagations:** `MANDATORY` fails without an active transaction (good for domain methods that must never run alone), `NOT_SUPPORTED` suspends around slow non-database work, and `SUPPORTS` and `NEVER` complete the set.","**Proxy caveat:** propagation is applied by the proxy, so a `REQUIRES_NEW` method called through `this` silently joins the outer transaction.","**Strict participation:** setting `validateExistingTransaction` to `true` on the transaction manager makes a joining method with an incompatible isolation level or read-only flag fail instead of being silently ignored."],"redFlags":["expects a `REQUIRES_NEW` method to see rows the outer transaction has not committed yet","ignores that the suspended outer transaction keeps its locks, so the inner one can wait on them forever","believes an inner `REQUIRED` method can commit on its own before its caller finishes"]},{"id":92,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"A use case writes two tables, calls a remote API and publishes an event. Where should the transaction start and end?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Put the transaction on the application service method that implements one use case, keep it short and database-only, and move remote calls and message publishing outside it using separate transactions or an outbox.","answer":["The natural boundary is the application service method that implements one use case, such as `approveLoan` or `capturePayment`: everything that must be atomic commits together, and nothing else is inside. Not the controller, which would hold the transaction through validation, serialization and web concerns, and not only the repository, where each call commits separately and you lose atomicity across steps. Ideally one transaction changes one aggregate; consistency across aggregates goes through events.","Keep transactions short. A transaction pins a pooled connection (HikariCP's default pool is only 10), holds row locks until commit, and in PostgreSQL keeps an old snapshot alive that blocks vacuum cleanup. Load, decide and write inside; do expensive computation, file parsing and waiting outside.","Never make remote calls inside a database transaction. An HTTP call to a payment provider or credit bureau can take seconds or time out while the connection and locks are held; under load the pool drains and unrelated endpoints fail. It also cannot be rolled back: if the provider charges the card and your commit then fails, the two systems disagree. Split the flow: transaction one records intent (`PENDING` plus an idempotency key), the remote call runs without a transaction, transaction two records the result, and a reconciliation job repairs crashes in between.","Use `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` for query use cases, set `timeout` on risky ones, and route messages through an outbox rather than sending them from inside the transaction."],"keyPoints":["one use case in the application layer maps to one transaction","only database work belongs inside, so connections and locks are released quickly","network calls happen before or after the commit, with state recorded so failures can resume","events are written alongside the data and published once the commit succeeds"],"examples":[{"title":"Remote call inside vs outside the transaction","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: connection and row locks held while waiting on the payment provider\n@Transactional\npublic void capture(UUID orderId) {\n    Order order = orders.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();\n    order.markPaymentPending();\n    PaymentResult result = paymentClient.charge(order.total(), order.idempotencyKey()); // 50 ms to 30 s\n    order.applyPayment(result);\n}\n\n// GOOD: two short transactions (TransactionTemplate tx) around the remote call\npublic void capture(UUID orderId) {\n    ChargeRequest request = tx.execute(status -> {\n        Order order = orders.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();\n        order.markPaymentPending();\n        return new ChargeRequest(order.total(), order.idempotencyKey());\n    });\n\n    PaymentResult result = paymentClient.charge(request.amount(), request.idempotencyKey());\n\n    tx.executeWithoutResult(status ->\n        orders.findById(orderId).orElseThrow().applyPayment(result));\n}","note":"The idempotency key makes a retried charge safe, and the `PENDING` state lets a reconciliation job find orders whose second transaction never ran."},{"title":"PostgreSQL guardrails and finding long transactions","lang":"sql","code":"-- Safety nets for the application role\nALTER ROLE payments_app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = '30s';\nALTER ROLE payments_app SET statement_timeout = '5s';\n\n-- Longest running transactions right now\nSELECT pid, usename, state,\n       now() - xact_start AS tx_age,\n       left(query, 80)    AS last_query\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY tx_age DESC\nLIMIT 10;","note":"An `idle in transaction` session with a large `tx_age` is the classic signature of application code doing remote work or waiting while a transaction is open."}],"followUps":[{"q":"The remote call succeeds but the following commit fails. How do you keep the systems consistent?","a":"Assume it will happen and design for it: record the intent and an idempotency key in a first short transaction, make the remote call, then record the outcome in a second one. If the second commit fails, a retry or a reconciliation job can safely repeat the call because the remote side recognizes the key, or it can compensate by cancelling the remote action. The result is a small local saga rather than one transaction pretending to cover two systems."},{"q":"Should a method that only queries also be transactional?","a":"Often yes, with `@Transactional(readOnly = true)`: it gives several queries one consistent connection and persistence context, lets Hibernate skip dirty checking and flushing, and lets a routing data source send the work to a replica. A single simple query does not strictly need it, since Spring Data repository reads already run in their own read-only transaction. Keep it short for the same reasons as writes, because it still holds a connection and, in PostgreSQL, a snapshot while open."}],"extra":["**readOnly:** `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` makes Hibernate skip flushing and dirty checking and passes a read-only hint to the driver; it is also the usual hook for routing queries to a replica through an `AbstractRoutingDataSource`.","**Open Session in View:** Spring Boot enables `spring.jpa.open-in-view` by default and logs a warning; it keeps the persistence context open for the whole request, so disable it to make the service method the real boundary.","**Timeouts as guardrails:** `@Transactional(timeout = 5)` plus PostgreSQL `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` and `statement_timeout` stop one bad code path from pinning connections forever.","**Isolation is part of the boundary:** PostgreSQL defaults to READ COMMITTED; if you choose REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE the whole use case must be retryable, because commits can fail with SQLSTATE `40001`.","**Retry wraps the transaction:** retries for optimistic lock or serialization failures belong outside the transactional method so each attempt starts with a fresh transaction and persistence context."],"redFlags":["wraps an entire batch job or scheduler loop in a single transaction","assumes a rollback also undoes an email sent or an HTTP call made inside it","relies on transactional repository methods alone, so a service calling several of them gets separate commits"]},{"id":93,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"Walk through the Spring bean lifecycle from definition to destruction.","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A bean starts as a `BeanDefinition` that factory post-processors may change; it is then instantiated, injected, given its `Aware` callbacks and initialized (`@PostConstruct`, `afterPropertiesSet`, init method) between the `BeanPostProcessor` before and after hooks, and the after hook is where AOP proxies are created. On shutdown `SmartLifecycle` beans stop first, then singletons get their destroy callbacks; prototypes never do.","answer":["Before any object exists, Spring builds `BeanDefinition`s (class, scope, constructor arguments, lifecycle methods, flags) from component scanning, `@Bean` methods and auto-configuration. `BeanFactoryPostProcessor`s may then change definitions; `ConfigurationClassPostProcessor`, which parses `@Configuration` classes, is one. Next all `BeanPostProcessor`s are created, very early: one that injects ordinary beans drags them into early creation, and Spring logs that they are \"not eligible for getting processed by all BeanPostProcessors\", meaning no proxies on them. Declare post-processor `@Bean` methods `static`.","For each singleton the container instantiates it (constructor injection happens here), populates fields and setters, and calls `BeanNameAware` and `BeanFactoryAware`. Then each post-processor's `postProcessBeforeInitialization` runs, which is where `ApplicationContextAware` and `@PostConstruct` are invoked, followed by `afterPropertiesSet` and any `initMethod`. Last comes `postProcessAfterInitialization`, where the auto-proxy creator wraps the bean for `@Transactional`, `@Cacheable` and aspects, so other beans receive the proxy. Once all singletons exist, `SmartInitializingSingleton` callbacks run, `SmartLifecycle.start()` is called in ascending phase order, and Boot publishes `ApplicationReadyEvent` after the runners.","This order explains classic bugs. `@PostConstruct` runs on the raw object, so calling your own `@Transactional` method there runs without a transaction; startup work that needs proxies belongs in an `ApplicationReadyEvent` listener or a runner, and `@PostConstruct` should never block on remote calls. Consumers and pollers belong in `SmartLifecycle`. On close, Spring publishes `ContextClosedEvent`, stops lifecycle beans in reverse phase order (graceful web server shutdown is one), then destroys singletons in reverse dependency order: `@PreDestroy`, `DisposableBean.destroy`, then the destroy method, which `@Bean` infers from a public `close` or `shutdown`. Prototypes are handed over and forgotten; their destroy callbacks never run."],"keyPoints":["definitions and post-processors come before any bean exists","proxies are created after initialization, so @PostConstruct sees the raw object","use SmartLifecycle for running components; prototypes are never destroyed"],"examples":[{"title":"Callback order on one bean","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass LedgerClient implements BeanNameAware, InitializingBean, DisposableBean {\n    private final LedgerProperties properties;\n    private Clock clock;\n\n    LedgerClient(LedgerProperties properties) {   // 1. instantiation, constructor injection\n        this.properties = properties;\n    }\n\n    @Autowired\n    void setClock(Clock clock) { this.clock = clock; } // 2. population: setters, fields\n\n    @Override\n    public void setBeanName(String name) { }      // 3. Aware callbacks\n\n    @PostConstruct\n    void validate() {                              // 4. before-initialization post-processing\n        properties.requireValid();                 //    raw object: no proxy, no transaction\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void afterPropertiesSet() { }           // 5. InitializingBean, then initMethod\n\n    // 6. after-initialization post-processing: AOP proxies wrap the bean here,\n    //    and every injection point receives the proxy\n\n    @PreDestroy\n    void flush() { }                               // 7. on close: @PreDestroy\n\n    @Override\n    public void destroy() { }                      // 8. DisposableBean, then destroyMethod\n}","note":"Real beans should pick one style, usually `@PostConstruct`, which keeps Spring interfaces out of the class."},{"title":"A message consumer as SmartLifecycle instead of @PostConstruct","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass PaymentEventsConsumer implements SmartLifecycle {\n    private final PaymentEventHandler handler;   // a proxied @Transactional bean\n    private volatile boolean running;\n    private ExecutorService worker;\n\n    PaymentEventsConsumer(PaymentEventHandler handler) {\n        this.handler = handler;\n    }\n\n    // BAD: starting the loop in @PostConstruct consumes before the context is ready\n\n    @Override\n    public void start() {                        // after every singleton is wired\n        worker = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();\n        running = true;\n        worker.submit(this::poll);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void stop() {                         // before any singleton is destroyed\n        running = false;\n        worker.close();                          // waits for the poll loop to finish\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean isRunning() { return running; } // default phase: starts last, stops first\n\n    private void poll() {\n        while (running) {\n            handler.handleNextBatch(Duration.ofSeconds(1));\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"`stop()` runs before the connection pool is destroyed, so in-flight batches finish against live resources."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does a @Transactional method called from @PostConstruct run without a transaction?","a":"`@PostConstruct` runs during before-initialization processing on the raw object, before the auto-proxy creator wraps it, and the call is a self-invocation anyway. It runs without a transaction, so lazy loading or atomicity fails. Use an `ApplicationReadyEvent` listener that calls the proxied bean, or a `TransactionTemplate`."},{"q":"BeanFactoryPostProcessor vs BeanPostProcessor?","a":"A `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` works on definitions before regular beans exist: it can change properties or scopes and register new definitions. A `BeanPostProcessor` works on each instance around initialization and may return a wrapper instead. `@Configuration` parsing is a factory post-processor; `@Autowired`, `@PostConstruct` and AOP proxying are bean post-processors."}],"extra":["**Lazy and scoped beans:** request, session and lazy beans run the same steps on first use, so their wiring errors appear at runtime.","**Destruction order:** dependents are destroyed before their dependencies, so collaborators are still alive in `@PreDestroy`; `@DependsOn` adds an order injection cannot express.","**Prototype cleanup:** Spring does not track prototype instances, so a prototype holding a connection or thread must be closed by whoever obtained it.","**Shutdown needs a signal:** Boot's shutdown hook closes the context on SIGTERM; `kill -9` or an OOM kill runs no callbacks, so `@PreDestroy` guarantees nothing.","**Phase timeouts:** each lifecycle phase gets `spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase` (30 seconds by default); keep the total below the pod's grace period."],"redFlags":["Thinks @PostConstruct runs on the proxy or inside a transaction","Starts consumers or threads in a constructor or @PostConstruct","Expects Spring to destroy prototype beans"]},{"id":94,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"Adding the JDBC starter and a driver gives you a working `DataSource` without any code. What is Spring Boot doing?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Boot loads a list of `@AutoConfiguration` classes after your own configuration and applies each one only if its conditions (classpath, existing beans, properties) match, so defining your own bean makes the default back off.","answer":["`@SpringBootApplication` includes `@EnableAutoConfiguration`, which imports `AutoConfigurationImportSelector`. It reads candidate class names from `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports` in every jar on the classpath (Boot 3 no longer uses `spring.factories` for this), removes exclusions and registers the rest as ordinary configuration classes. Because it is a deferred import selector, auto-configuration is processed after your own `@Configuration` classes and scanned components.","Each auto-configuration is guarded by conditions. `@ConditionalOnClass` activates it only if a library is on the classpath (Hikari, Jackson, Kafka client), `@ConditionalOnProperty` checks a property, `@ConditionalOnWebApplication` checks the application type, and `@ConditionalOnBean` or `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` inspect the bean registry. Settings come from `@ConfigurationProperties` classes such as `DataSourceProperties` bound to `spring.datasource.*`, and ordering between auto-configurations uses `@AutoConfiguration(before = ..., after = ...)`.","Back-off is the key design idea: most auto-configured beans are `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`, so declaring your own `DataSource` or `ObjectMapper` replaces the default with no extra ceremony. Prefer customizer beans such as `JsonMapperBuilderCustomizer` (`Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilderCustomizer` on Boot 3) or `RestClientCustomizer` when you only want to tweak the default, and exclude an auto-configuration explicitly with `spring.autoconfigure.exclude` when you want it gone.","It is not magic, it is inspectable: run with `--debug` to print the condition evaluation report, or query `/actuator/conditions`, to see which configurations matched and why the others did not."],"keyPoints":["candidate configuration classes are listed in `AutoConfiguration.imports` files inside the jars","each one is guarded by conditions on classes present, existing beans and property values","they are processed after your own configuration, so a bean you declare takes precedence","the conditions evaluation report (`--debug` or the actuator `conditions` endpoint) shows what matched and why"],"examples":[{"title":"A company starter's auto-configuration","lang":"java","code":"@AutoConfiguration(after = JacksonAutoConfiguration.class)\n@ConditionalOnClass(AuditClient.class)\n@ConditionalOnProperty(prefix = \"acme.audit\", name = \"enabled\", havingValue = \"true\", matchIfMissing = true)\n@EnableConfigurationProperties(AuditProperties.class)\npublic class AuditAutoConfiguration {\n\n    @Bean\n    @ConditionalOnMissingBean          // backs off if the application defines its own AuditClient\n    AuditClient auditClient(AuditProperties props, ObjectMapper objectMapper) {\n        return new HttpAuditClient(props.endpoint(), props.timeout(), objectMapper);\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    @ConditionalOnMissingBean\n    AuditEventPublisher auditEventPublisher(AuditClient client) {\n        return new AuditEventPublisher(client);\n    }\n}","note":"This is exactly the pattern Boot's own modules use: guard on the classpath, allow a property switch, and let user beans win through `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`."},{"title":"Registering and debugging auto-configuration","lang":"bash","code":"# Registration file inside the starter jar (one class name per line)\ncat src/main/resources/META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports\ncom.acme.audit.autoconfigure.AuditAutoConfiguration\n\n# Why did an auto-configuration apply or not?\njava -jar credit-service.jar --debug | grep -A3 AuditAutoConfiguration\ncurl -s localhost:8080/actuator/conditions | jq '.contexts[].negativeMatches | keys'\n\n# Switch one off explicitly\njava -jar credit-service.jar \\\n  --spring.autoconfigure.exclude=com.acme.audit.autoconfigure.AuditAutoConfiguration","note":"The conditions report lists positive and negative matches with the exact condition that decided each one, which settles most 'why is this bean here' questions."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you write your own auto-configuration for a shared company library?","a":"Create a class annotated `@AutoConfiguration`, guard its beans with conditions such as `@ConditionalOnClass` and `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`, bind settings with `@ConfigurationProperties`, and list the class in `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports`. Ship it in its own module, plus an optional starter that pulls in the dependencies. Test it with `ApplicationContextRunner`, asserting which beans exist under different classpaths and properties."},{"q":"An auto-configured bean is wrong for your app. What are your options, from least to most invasive?","a":"First try properties, since most auto-configurations expose their settings under `spring.*` keys. Next, many offer customizer interfaces, such as `JsonMapperBuilderCustomizer` (`Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilderCustomizer` before Boot 4), that adjust the default without replacing it. If that is not enough, declare your own bean of that type so the default is skipped, and as a last resort exclude the whole auto-configuration with the `exclude` attribute of `@SpringBootApplication` or the `spring.autoconfigure.exclude` property."}],"extra":["**Registration file:** since Boot 2.7 auto-configurations are listed in `AutoConfiguration.imports`, and Boot 3.0 dropped support for registering them through `spring.factories`; an old starter that was never migrated silently does nothing on Boot 3.","**Cheap filtering:** an annotation processor writes `META-INF/spring-autoconfigure-metadata.properties`, letting Boot discard candidates whose `@ConditionalOnClass` fails without loading their classes, which keeps startup fast with hundreds of candidates.","**Testing starters:** `ApplicationContextRunner` with `AutoConfigurations.of(...)` and `withPropertyValues(...)` tests each condition branch in milliseconds without starting a server.","**Native images:** with Spring AOT and GraalVM, conditions are evaluated at build time, so classpath and profile-driven conditions are frozen into the binary.","**Replacement pitfalls:** replacing an auto-configured `ObjectMapper` also drops everything Boot did to it (registered modules, `spring.jackson.*` properties), which is why customizers are usually the better lever."],"redFlags":["still describes `spring.factories` as the registration mechanism for Boot 3 auto-configuration","excludes auto-configurations by trial and error instead of reading the conditions report","assumes auto-configuration overrides beans the application has already defined"]},{"id":95,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"How does Spring MVC handle a request: DispatcherServlet, filters, interceptors and aspects?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A request passes the servlet filter chain (security, tracing, logging) before `DispatcherServlet` picks a handler and its interceptors, runs `preHandle`, invokes the controller through a `HandlerAdapter` (argument resolution, message conversion, validation), then runs `postHandle`, exception resolution and `afterCompletion`. Aspects are separate: they wrap beans through proxies and know nothing about HTTP.","answer":["The servlet container (Tomcat by default) assigns a thread and runs the `Filter` chain. Filters are servlet-level: they see every request, including unmapped ones, and can wrap the request and response; Spring Security's `FilterChainProxy` is one filter hosting its own internal chain. At the end sits `DispatcherServlet`. It asks its `HandlerMapping`s for a handler; `RequestMappingHandlerMapping` matches `@RequestMapping` methods and returns a `HandlerExecutionChain` with the matching interceptors. A `HandlerAdapter` invokes it: argument resolvers build parameters (`@RequestBody` through `HttpMessageConverter`s, `@Valid`), the method runs, and return value handlers write the result.","Interceptors live inside the dispatcher and know the selected handler, so `preHandle` can read annotations on the `HandlerMethod` or stop the request by returning false. `postHandle` runs after the handler, but for `@ResponseBody` and `ResponseEntity` methods the body is already written and committed, so it cannot add headers; use a `ResponseBodyAdvice` or a filter. An exception skips `postHandle` and goes through the `HandlerExceptionResolver`s (`@ExceptionHandler` advice); `afterCompletion` always runs, which makes it the cleanup hook. Aspects are not in this pipeline: `@Transactional`, `@PreAuthorize` or a custom `@Around` apply because the bean is a proxy, and behave the same when a Kafka listener calls it.","Choose the layer by what the concern needs to know. Filters for what applies regardless of handler: authentication, CORS, correlation ids, request logging, rate limiting. Interceptors when the decision depends on the selected controller method, such as an annotation-driven audit or idempotency-key check. Aspects for business-level cross-cutting behavior that is not about HTTP. Two production traps: the chain runs on one request thread, so `ThreadLocal`s such as the MDC must be cleared in `finally` or the next request on that pooled thread inherits them; and exceptions thrown in filters never reach `@ControllerAdvice`."],"keyPoints":["filters wrap everything at the servlet level, before DispatcherServlet","interceptors see the selected handler; postHandle is too late for @ResponseBody headers","aspects wrap beans through proxies and are not web-specific"],"examples":[{"title":"A filter for every request, an interceptor for annotated handlers","lang":"java","code":"@Component\n@Order(Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE)        // before Spring Security, so its logs carry the id\nclass CorrelationIdFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {\n    @Override\n    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response,\n                                    FilterChain chain) throws ServletException, IOException {\n        String id = Optional.ofNullable(request.getHeader(\"X-Correlation-Id\"))\n                .orElseGet(() -> UUID.randomUUID().toString());\n        MDC.put(\"correlationId\", id);\n        response.setHeader(\"X-Correlation-Id\", id);   // before the body is written\n        try {\n            chain.doFilter(request, response);\n        } finally {\n            MDC.remove(\"correlationId\");              // pooled threads are reused\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass IdempotencyKeyInterceptor implements HandlerInterceptor {\n    @Override\n    public boolean preHandle(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response,\n                             Object handler) {\n        if (handler instanceof HandlerMethod method\n                && method.hasMethodAnnotation(RequiresIdempotencyKey.class)\n                && request.getHeader(\"Idempotency-Key\") == null) {\n            response.setStatus(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST.value());\n            return false;                              // the controller never runs\n        }\n        return true;\n    }\n}","note":"The filter runs even for 404s; only the interceptor sees which controller method was selected. Register it with `WebMvcConfigurer.addInterceptors` and path patterns."},{"title":"The order of everything on one request","lang":"text","code":"container thread\n  Filter chain\n    CorrelationIdFilter            MDC\n    ServerHttpObservationFilter    metrics, trace span\n    Spring Security                authentication, CSRF, CORS\n    DispatcherServlet\n      HandlerMapping               handler + interceptors\n      preHandle                    false stops here\n      HandlerAdapter\n        argument resolvers         @RequestBody, @Valid\n        controller (proxy?)        @PreAuthorize, aspects\n          service proxy            @Transactional, @Cacheable\n        return value handlers      body written, committed\n      postHandle                   skipped on exception\n      exception resolvers          @RestControllerAdvice\n      afterCompletion              always runs\n  filters unwind                   finally blocks run","note":"It all runs on one thread unless the controller returns an async type, so `ThreadLocal` state set in a filter is visible in the service and must be cleared on the way out."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why should authorization not live in a HandlerInterceptor?","a":"Interceptor path patterns can drift from how controllers actually match requests, and interceptors do not protect anything outside Spring MVC. The Spring documentation recommends Spring Security instead: its filter chain runs before dispatch and method security covers the service layer. Interceptors suit request-shape checks, not the security boundary."},{"q":"How do you add a header to every JSON response?","a":"Set it in a filter before calling `chain.doFilter`, which covers every response, or use a `ResponseBodyAdvice`, which runs just before message conversion for `@ResponseBody` methods. Setting it in `postHandle` fails silently because the response is already committed."}],"extra":["**Double registration:** a `Filter` declared as `@Component` is registered for all URLs automatically; adding the same bean to the security chain with `addFilterBefore` runs it twice, so disable one with a `FilterRegistrationBean`.","**Security filter order:** Spring Security's filter is registered at order -100 by default, so a filter that needs the authenticated user must run after it.","**Body caching:** the request body can be read once; `ContentCachingRequestWrapper` caches only what the controller read, so log after `chain.doFilter`.","**Async return types:** with `CompletableFuture` or `DeferredResult` the response completes on another thread, and MDC values do not follow automatically.","**Virtual threads:** with `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` each request gets a new, unpooled virtual thread; the pipeline is unchanged."],"redFlags":["Cannot say whether filters or interceptors run first","Uses postHandle to modify @ResponseBody responses","Expects @ControllerAdvice to catch filter exceptions"]},{"id":96,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"How does Spring handle circular dependencies, and why should you remove them instead?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Spring can resolve a cycle between singletons only when at least one side uses field or setter injection, by handing out an early reference to the half-built bean. Constructor-injection cycles cannot be resolved, and Spring Boot 2.6 and later rejects all circular references by default (`spring.main.allow-circular-references`). Treat a cycle as a design defect and refactor it; `@Lazy` only hides it.","answer":["Suppose `OrderService` needs `PaymentService` and `PaymentService` needs `OrderService`. With field injection, Spring instantiates `OrderService` through its no-arg constructor, registers a factory for an early reference to it, then populates fields; creating `PaymentService` asks for `OrderService`, receives the early reference to the incomplete object, finishes, and `OrderService` then completes. With constructor injection there is no half-built object to hand out: neither constructor can run first, and creation fails with `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException` (\"Is there an unresolvable circular reference?\"). That asymmetry favors constructor injection: cycles cannot hide.","Since Spring Boot 2.6, circular references are prohibited by default: startup fails with a diagram of the cycle and offers `spring.main.allow-circular-references=true` only as a last resort. That property re-enables early references for field and setter injection; constructor cycles still fail. Early references carry a trap: the object handed out early must be the final one. The auto-proxy creator behind `@Transactional` copes by creating the proxy early, but the post-processor for `@Async` wraps only after initialization, and Spring fails with \"has been injected into other beans in its raw version as part of a circular reference, but has eventually been wrapped\". Even when it works, the other bean may call the early one before its `@PostConstruct` has run.","`@Lazy` on one constructor parameter breaks the cycle mechanically: Spring injects a lazy-resolution proxy and resolves the real bean on first call. Startup succeeds, but the cycle remains and failures move to the first request; `ObjectProvider` does the same explicitly. Real fixes start from why the cycle exists: extract shared logic into a third bean, merge classes that are one responsibility, add an orchestrator that calls both, or invert one direction with a domain event. Then keep cycles out with an ArchUnit rule or Spring Modulith's verification, because bean cycles almost always start as package cycles."],"keyPoints":["field and setter cycles resolve through early references; constructor cycles cannot","since Boot 2.6 circular references fail startup by default","@Lazy hides a cycle; refactoring removes it"],"examples":[{"title":"A callback cycle and how Boot reports it","lang":"java","code":"@Service\nclass OrderService {\n    private final PaymentService payments;\n\n    OrderService(PaymentService payments) { this.payments = payments; }\n\n    void checkout(Order order) { payments.capture(order); }\n\n    void markPaid(OrderId id) { /* update status */ }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass PaymentService {\n    private final PaymentProvider provider;\n    private final OrderService orders;          // BAD: calls back into its caller\n\n    PaymentService(PaymentProvider provider, OrderService orders) {\n        this.provider = provider;\n        this.orders = orders;\n    }\n\n    void capture(Order order) {\n        provider.capture(order.paymentReference());\n        orders.markPaid(order.id());\n    }\n}\n\n// APPLICATION FAILED TO START\n// The dependencies of some of the beans in the application context form a cycle:\n// ┌─────┐\n// |  orderService defined in file [.../OrderService.class]\n// ↑     ↓\n// |  paymentService defined in file [.../PaymentService.class]\n// └─────┘","note":"With field injection and the property set to true this would start, with `PaymentService` holding an `OrderService` that was unfinished when injected."},{"title":"Removing the back edge with a domain event","lang":"java","code":"public record PaymentCaptured(OrderId orderId, String providerReference) {}\n\n@Service\nclass PaymentService {                         // no longer knows OrderService\n    private final PaymentProvider provider;\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;\n\n    PaymentService(PaymentProvider provider, ApplicationEventPublisher events) {\n        this.provider = provider;\n        this.events = events;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void capture(OrderId orderId, String paymentReference) {\n        String reference = provider.capture(paymentReference);\n        events.publishEvent(new PaymentCaptured(orderId, reference));\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass OrderService {\n    private final PaymentService payments;     // the only direction left\n\n    OrderService(PaymentService payments) { this.payments = payments; }\n\n    void checkout(Order order) {\n        payments.capture(order.id(), order.paymentReference());\n    }\n\n    @EventListener                             // synchronous, same transaction as before\n    void on(PaymentCaptured event) {\n        markPaid(event.orderId());\n    }\n\n    void markPaid(OrderId id) { /* update status */ }\n}","note":"Behavior is unchanged because a plain `@EventListener` runs synchronously inside the publisher's transaction. `capture` now takes an id and a reference instead of `Order`, so payments no longer depends on the orders model either."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does @Async on a bean in a cycle fail startup when @Transactional does not?","a":"Both wrap the bean in a proxy, but the auto-proxy creator behind `@Transactional` implements the early-reference hook, so the other bean receives the proxy from the start. `@Async` is applied by its own post-processor only after initialization, so the other bean already holds the raw object and Spring refuses to continue with two different instances. Removing the cycle fixes it; `@Lazy` on the injection point is the workaround."},{"q":"How do you keep cycles out of a large codebase?","a":"Boot's failure report names every bean in the cycle. For prevention, an ArchUnit rule such as `slices().matching(\"com.acme.(*)..\").should().beFreeOfCycles()` fails the build on package cycles, and Spring Modulith's `ApplicationModules.verify()` does the same for module boundaries."}],"extra":["**Prototype cycles:** Spring never resolves circular references between prototype beans, because there is no cached instance to hand out early.","**The flag is global:** `allow-circular-references=true` re-enables every cycle, including the ones added next month; treat it as migration debt with an owner, not configuration.","**Lazy proxies defer failures:** a `@Lazy` dependency that cannot be resolved fails on its first method call, so a broken configuration passes deployment.","**Domain smell:** two services calling each other usually means one responsibility is split across both.","**Events have costs:** an event removes the dependency but also the explicit control flow; keep synchronous events inside one module and use an outbox when the reaction must survive a crash."],"redFlags":["Fixes every cycle with @Lazy or allow-circular-references","Thinks Spring can resolve constructor injection cycles","Cannot explain early references or why the Boot default changed"]},{"id":97,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"Spring MVC vs WebFlux: when is the reactive stack worth it?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Spring MVC runs one thread per request with blocking I/O; WebFlux runs a few event-loop threads with non-blocking I/O and Reactor types, which pays off for streaming, long-lived connections and backpressure when every dependency is non-blocking. Since Java 21, MVC on virtual threads gives most of the concurrency benefit for ordinary request/response services with plain code and stack traces, so WebFlux needs a specific reason.","answer":["Spring MVC sits on the Servlet API: each request holds a thread until the response is done, and a blocking JDBC or HTTP call parks it. With platform threads concurrency is capped at the pool size (Tomcat's default is 200), so a slow dependency exhausts threads. WebFlux sits on Reactive Streams: Reactor Netty runs about one event-loop thread per core, handlers return `Mono` or `Flux`, and I/O completion resumes the pipeline, so one thread serves thousands of in-flight requests. Backpressure lets a subscriber request only what it can handle (`request(n)`), which matters when streaming large results or Server-Sent Events to slow clients.","The price is that the whole path must be non-blocking. One blocking call on an event-loop thread (JDBC, a blocking SDK, `Thread.sleep`, a lock held during I/O) stalls every connection that thread serves, so you need R2DBC instead of JPA, `WebClient` instead of `RestTemplate`, and reactive drivers for Redis, Mongo and Kafka; leftovers must be wrapped with `subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic())`. Debugging costs more: stack traces show Reactor operators, not your call chain, so you add `checkpoint()` or the debug agent; state MVC keeps in `ThreadLocal`s (MDC, security context, transactions) must travel in Reactor's `Context`; and a publisher nobody subscribes to never runs.","Virtual threads change the default answer. With `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true`, Tomcat runs each request on a virtual thread, and a blocking call unmounts it from its carrier, so thousands of concurrent blocking requests cost little and plain JDBC, JPA and `RestClient` code scales for I/O-bound work. They give no backpressure, do nothing for CPU-bound work, and move the bottleneck to downstream pools. Choose WebFlux for gateways, SSE or WebSocket fan-out and all-reactive streaming pipelines; choose MVC with virtual threads for typical CRUD and orchestration services."],"keyPoints":["the reactive stack trades simple code for high concurrency and backpressure","one blocking call on the event loop stalls every request on that thread","virtual threads let MVC scale blocking I/O for most services"],"examples":[{"title":"WebFlux: blocking inside the pipeline vs isolated and composed","lang":"java","code":"@RestController\nclass AccountController {\n    private final AccountRepository accounts;   // Spring Data R2DBC: Mono and Flux\n    private final WebClient fx;\n    private final LegacyRiskClient risk;        // blocking SDK, no reactive version\n\n    // BAD: risk.score blocks a Netty event-loop thread\n    @GetMapping(\"/v0/accounts/{id}\")\n    Mono<AccountView> bad(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n        return accounts.findById(id)\n                .map(a -> new AccountView(a, null, risk.score(a.id())));\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: calls run concurrently; the blocking one moves to boundedElastic\n    @GetMapping(\"/accounts/{id}\")\n    Mono<AccountView> get(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n        return accounts.findById(id)\n                .switchIfEmpty(Mono.error(new AccountNotFoundException(id)))\n                .flatMap(account -> Mono.zip(\n                        fx.get().uri(\"/rates/{ccy}\", account.currency())\n                                .retrieve().bodyToMono(FxRate.class),\n                        Mono.fromCallable(() -> risk.score(account.id()))\n                                .subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic()))\n                        .map(t -> new AccountView(account, t.getT1(), t.getT2())))\n                .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2));\n    }\n}","note":"Nothing runs until the framework subscribes to the returned `Mono`. Without `subscribeOn`, the blocking call silently goes back onto the event loop."},{"title":"The same endpoint on MVC with virtual threads","lang":"java","code":"// application.properties: spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true\n\n@RestController\nclass AccountController {\n    private final AccountRepository accounts;   // Spring Data JPA, blocking\n    private final RestClient fx;                // configure connect and read timeouts\n    private final LegacyRiskClient risk;\n    private final Semaphore riskBulkhead = new Semaphore(50); // what the SDK tolerates\n\n    // constructor omitted\n\n    @GetMapping(\"/accounts/{id}\")\n    AccountView get(@PathVariable UUID id) throws InterruptedException {\n        Account account = accounts.findById(id)\n                .orElseThrow(() -> new AccountNotFoundException(id));\n        FxRate rate = fx.get().uri(\"/rates/{ccy}\", account.currency())\n                .retrieve().body(FxRate.class);   // parks the virtual thread only\n        if (!riskBulkhead.tryAcquire(500, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {\n            throw new RiskServiceBusyException();\n        }\n        try {\n            return new AccountView(account, rate, risk.score(account.id()));\n        } finally {\n            riskBulkhead.release();\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The calls run sequentially; to overlap them, submit both to `Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` (structured concurrency is still a preview in Java 25). The semaphore replaces the throttling a bounded thread pool used to provide."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens if you call block() inside a WebFlux handler?","a":"On a Reactor Netty event-loop thread, Reactor detects it and throws `IllegalStateException` saying blocking is not supported in that thread. Other blocking calls are not detected and simply stall the loop; BlockHound in tests catches them."},{"q":"Can you mix the stacks, for example WebClient inside an MVC app?","a":"Yes. `WebClient` works in MVC, and MVC controllers can return `Mono`. But `RestClient` gives the same fluent API without Reactor, and with virtual threads there is little reason to pull Reactor into a servlet app for HTTP calls. Mixing the other way, blocking libraries inside WebFlux, is where the trouble starts."}],"extra":["**Pinning:** on Java 21, blocking inside `synchronized` pins a virtual thread to its carrier and can starve the carrier pool; JDK 24 fixed this, and JFR's `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` event finds remaining cases.","**ThreadLocal caches:** per-thread caches assume few long-lived threads; with a virtual thread per request they never hit and memory grows with concurrency.","**Pools set the ceiling:** with virtual threads, thousands of requests wait on 20 database connections; size the pool for the database, keep `connectionTimeout` short and bound concurrency upstream.","**Relational data in WebFlux:** Spring Data R2DBC has no lazy loading or relationship mapping, so a JPA-heavy domain does not port.","**Testing cost:** reactive code needs `StepVerifier` and virtual time for delays; the team should know them before choosing WebFlux."],"redFlags":["Claims WebFlux makes every service faster","Calls JDBC or blocking SDKs inside reactive operators","Does not mention virtual threads as the alternative for blocking I/O"]},{"id":98,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"An endpoint listing 50 orders and their line items runs 51 SQL statements. What is happening, and how would you fix it?","level":"Basic","tldr":"N+1 means a single query for the parents followed by a separate query for each parent once a lazy association is accessed; solve it per use case by fetching joined data, using an entity graph, selecting into DTOs or enabling batch fetching, and verify by counting statements.","answer":["You run one query for the parents, say 50 orders, then iterate and touch a lazy association such as `order.getCustomer().getName()` or `order.getLines()`. Each access initializes a proxy or collection with its own SELECT, so the page costs 51 round trips or more. Each query is fast alone, so the problem hides in development with 5 rows and explodes in production with 500, adding latency, connection pool pressure and database CPU. EAGER mappings cause it too, because JPQL loads eager associations with secondary selects.","Fixes, chosen per use case: `JOIN FETCH` or an `@EntityGraph` loads the association in the same statement; a DTO projection (`select new ...` or a Spring Data interface or record projection) selects only the needed columns and skips entities altogether, the best option for read-only APIs; batch fetching (`@BatchSize` or `hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size`) initializes many parents' associations with one `IN` query, a good global safety net; and `@Fetch(FetchMode.SUBSELECT)` loads the collections of all parents from the previous query in one statement.","Measure, do not guess. Log SQL in development, enable Hibernate statistics, and add integration tests that assert the number of statements per use case. In production, the signature is a burst of identical statements in APM traces or a huge `calls` count relative to rows in `pg_stat_statements`."],"keyPoints":["lazy loading inside a loop turns one logical read into a statement per row","the cost grows with page size and network round trips, so it hides in tests with tiny data","the remedy is a fetch plan chosen per use case, not a global mapping change","asserting statement counts in tests catches regressions early"],"examples":[{"title":"N+1 loop vs single DTO query","lang":"java","code":"public record OrderView(UUID id, String customerName, Long lineCount) {}\n\n// BAD: 1 query for orders, then 1 per order for lines and 1 per distinct customer\n@Transactional(readOnly = true)\npublic List<OrderView> recentOrders() {\n    return orders.findTop50ByOrderByCreatedAtDesc().stream()\n        .map(o -> new OrderView(o.getId(), o.getCustomer().getName(), (long) o.getLines().size()))\n        .toList();\n}\n\n// GOOD: one statement, only the columns the screen needs\npublic interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, UUID> {\n    @Query(\"\"\"\n        select new com.acme.orders.api.OrderView(o.id, c.name, count(l))\n        from Order o join o.customer c left join o.lines l\n        group by o.id, c.name, o.createdAt\n        order by o.createdAt desc\n        \"\"\")\n    List<OrderView> findRecentViews(Pageable page);   // PageRequest.of(0, 50)\n}","note":"The projection pages parents safely because it returns one row per order, and it never creates entities, snapshots or proxies."},{"title":"Guarding the query count in a test","lang":"java","code":"@SpringBootTest(properties = \"spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.generate_statistics=true\")\nclass OrderQueryCountTest {\n\n    @Autowired OrderService orderService;\n    @Autowired EntityManagerFactory emf;\n\n    @Test\n    void recentOrdersUsesASingleStatement() {\n        Statistics stats = emf.unwrap(SessionFactory.class).getStatistics();\n        stats.clear();\n\n        orderService.recentOrders();\n\n        assertThat(stats.getPrepareStatementCount()).isEqualTo(1);\n    }\n}","note":"Seed the test data with several parents and children; with one parent an N+1 is invisible because N is 1."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does batch fetching reduce the number of queries without a join?","a":"With `@BatchSize(size = 50)` on the association or `hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size`, Hibernate collects the ids of several uninitialized proxies or collections and loads them with a single `IN (...)` query when the first one is accessed. Fifty orders then need about two queries instead of fifty-one, and parent pagination keeps working because the parent query is untouched. It is a good default safety net, though it still issues more than one statement."},{"q":"What goes wrong if you fetch join two collections in one query?","a":"The result has one row per combination of children, so an order with 10 lines and 5 payments returns 50 rows, and the data transferred grows multiplicatively. If both collections are mapped as `List` without an order column (bags), Hibernate refuses with `MultipleBagFetchException`; switching to `Set` silences the error but keeps the Cartesian product. Fetch one collection per query, and load the second with batch fetching or a separate query."}],"extra":["**Hibernate 6 deduplication:** join fetching a collection no longer needs `distinct` in HQL; Hibernate 6 deduplicates the root entities automatically.","**Cartesian products:** fetching two collections in one query multiplies rows (orders x lines x payments); fetch one collection per query and let batch fetching or a second query load the other.","**Hidden triggers:** `toString`, `equals`/`hashCode` and Jackson serialization that touch lazy associations cause N+1 just like explicit loops, so keep them off associations.","**Bind logging:** in Hibernate 6 `org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG` logs statements and `org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind=TRACE` logs bind values; never leave bind logging on in production because it writes customer data to logs."],"redFlags":["only notices the problem from slow production endpoints, never from SQL logs or tests","fetch-joins two collections at once and gets a Cartesian product or `MultipleBagFetchException`","treats the second-level cache as the cure for N+1"]},{"id":99,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"JPA vs Hibernate vs Spring Data JPA: what is each, and how do they fit together?","level":"Basic","tldr":"JPA (Jakarta Persistence) is a specification: mapping annotations, the `EntityManager` API, JPQL and the persistence context rules. Hibernate is the most widely used implementation of it, with a native API and extra features on top. Spring Data JPA generates repository implementations from interfaces and delegates every call to the `EntityManager`, so Hibernate still does the real work.","answer":["Jakarta Persistence (`jakarta.persistence.*`, still called JPA) defines entity mapping (`@Entity`, `@OneToMany`), the `EntityManager` interface, JPQL, the Criteria API and the persistence context semantics. A specification does nothing at runtime; a provider implements it. Hibernate ORM is the dominant provider (EclipseLink is the other well-known one), and it adds a native API: its `Session` extends `EntityManager`, HQL is a superset of JPQL, and it offers features the spec lacks, such as `@BatchSize`, `@NaturalId`, filters, multi-tenancy and `StatelessSession`.","Spring Data JPA is not an ORM. You declare `interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, UUID>`, and at startup Spring creates a proxy backed by `SimpleJpaRepository`, which calls `persist`, `merge`, `find` and JPQL queries on the `EntityManager`. Derived methods such as `findByStatusAndCreatedAtAfter` are parsed into JPQL, `@Query` takes JPQL or native SQL, and the module adds paging, projections, specifications and auditing. Spring ORM is the glue: `JpaTransactionManager` binds an `EntityManager` to each transaction, and the injected `EntityManager` is a shared proxy that routes to the current one.","Knowing the layers tells you where to look. Dirty checking, flush timing, lazy loading, N+1 queries and caching are Hibernate behavior even behind a one-line repository method, so performance work means reading Hibernate's SQL and statistics. Provider portability is mostly theoretical; teams use Hibernate features freely. The real design decision is when to drop below the repository: a custom fragment using the `EntityManager`, `unwrap(Session.class)` for Hibernate-only features, or `JdbcClient` or jOOQ for reporting and bulk SQL where entity mapping adds cost without value."],"keyPoints":["JPA is the specification, Hibernate the implementation that does the work","Spring Data JPA generates repositories that delegate to the EntityManager","SQL, flushing, lazy loading and caching behavior come from Hibernate"],"examples":[{"title":"The same data access at each layer","lang":"java","code":"// 1. JPA: the standard API, portable across providers\n@Repository\nclass OrderQueries {\n\n    @PersistenceContext\n    private EntityManager em;   // shared proxy bound to the current transaction\n\n    List<Order> findOpen(UUID customerId) {\n        return em.createQuery(\"\"\"\n                select o from Order o\n                where o.customer.id = :customerId and o.status = :status\n                \"\"\", Order.class)\n            .setParameter(\"customerId\", customerId)\n            .setParameter(\"status\", OrderStatus.OPEN)\n            .getResultList();\n    }\n\n    // 2. Hibernate: unwrap the native API for features JPA does not define\n    Optional<Order> findByOrderNumber(String orderNumber) {\n        return em.unwrap(Session.class)\n            .bySimpleNaturalId(Order.class)      // needs @NaturalId on orderNumber\n            .loadOptional(orderNumber);\n    }\n}\n\n// 3. Spring Data JPA: you write the interface, Spring generates the implementation\npublic interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, UUID> {\n\n    // derived query: the method name is parsed into JPQL at startup\n    List<Order> findByCustomerIdAndStatus(UUID customerId, OrderStatus status);\n}","note":"All three paths end in the same Hibernate session. Inside one transaction the repository and the hand-written query share a persistence context, so they return the same managed instances."},{"title":"One repository call, layer by layer (simplified)","lang":"text","code":"orderRepository.findById(orderId)\n  +- JDK proxy for OrderRepository      Spring Data: generated at startup\n  +- repository interceptors            Spring: readOnly transaction declared on\n  |                                     SimpleJpaRepository, exception translation\n  +- SimpleJpaRepository.findById       Spring Data: default CRUD implementation\n  +- shared EntityManager proxy         Spring ORM: this transaction's EntityManager\n  +- Session.find                       Hibernate: the provider does the real work\n  |    1. persistence context (first-level cache)\n  |    2. second-level cache, if enabled for Order\n  |    3. SQL for the dialect, row mapped to a managed Order\n  +- HikariCP                           pooled JDBC connection\n  +- PostgreSQL JDBC driver             wire protocol to the database","note":"Spring owns proxies, transactions and exception translation; SQL, caching, flushing and lazy loading all happen in Hibernate, so that is where performance questions get answered."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can you use Spring Data JPA without Hibernate?","a":"Yes. It codes against the JPA API, so EclipseLink works, but Spring Boot auto-configures only Hibernate: you set up the `EntityManagerFactory` with `EclipseLinkJpaVendorAdapter` yourself, and Hibernate-specific annotations or HQL in the codebase stop working. Teams that want less ORM usually choose Spring Data JDBC instead, a separate project with no JPA underneath: no persistence context, no lazy loading, no dirty checking, and aggregates saved as a whole."},{"q":"When would you go below the repository abstraction?","a":"When a derived method or `@Query` stops being readable, add a custom fragment (an `OrderRepositoryCustom` interface plus an `OrderRepositoryCustomImpl` class) that uses the `EntityManager` or the Criteria API for dynamic filters. Unwrap the Hibernate `Session` for natural id loading, filters or a `StatelessSession` in batch jobs. For reporting, bulk updates and SQL with window functions or CTEs, `JdbcClient` or jOOQ is simpler than forcing it through entities."}],"extra":["**Jakarta rename:** since Spring Boot 3 and Hibernate 6 the imports are `jakarta.persistence.*`; a leftover `javax.persistence` annotation is simply not recognized by the provider.","**Hibernate 7 cleanup:** Hibernate 7, used by Spring Boot 4, removed `Session.save`, `update`, `saveOrUpdate` and `delete` in favor of the JPA operations `persist`, `merge` and `remove`.","**Repository transactions:** `SimpleJpaRepository` is annotated `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` and its write methods override it, so repository calls made outside a service transaction each run and commit on their own.","**Who owns a setting:** `spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.*` goes to Hibernate unchanged, `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto` is a Boot shortcut for `hibernate.hbm2ddl.auto`, and `spring.jpa.open-in-view` controls a Spring MVC interceptor.","**HQL is not JPQL:** HQL accepts syntax JPQL does not, so a query using Hibernate-only features ties the code to Hibernate even when it runs through `EntityManager.createQuery`."],"redFlags":["Calls Spring Data JPA an ORM or a replacement for Hibernate","cannot say which layer generates SQL, caches entities or runs dirty checking","assumes repository methods bypass the persistence context and its flush rules"]},{"id":100,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"A teammate fixed a `LazyInitializationException` by marking every association `FetchType.EAGER`. What problems will that cause, and what would you do instead?","level":"Basic","tldr":"EAGER is a static, global decision that loads associations for every use case, causing over-fetching, large joins and hidden N+1; keep associations LAZY and pick a fetch plan per use case inside the transaction.","answer":["`FetchType.EAGER` is fixed in the mapping, so it applies to every query and every use case that touches the entity, whether or not it needs the association. A list screen that shows 50 order numbers ends up loading customers, addresses and lines. Eager collections produce wide joins and cartesian products (two eager `List` collections even fail with `MultipleBagFetchException`), and JPQL queries do not join eager associations automatically, so Hibernate loads them with extra SELECTs afterwards: EAGER often creates N+1 rather than preventing it. You also cannot switch it back to lazy for one query.","JPA defaults make this worse: `@ManyToOne` and `@OneToOne` are EAGER by default, while collections are LAZY. The production-safe rule is to declare every association `fetch = FetchType.LAZY` explicitly and decide what to load per use case.","LAZY is not free either: it requires deliberate transaction and fetch design. Data must be loaded inside the transaction, and each use case declares its fetch plan with `JOIN FETCH`, an `@EntityGraph`, a DTO projection, or batch fetching as a safety net. `LazyInitializationException` is a useful signal that a use case has no fetch plan, not a reason to switch to EAGER, rely on Open Session in View, or enable `hibernate.enable_lazy_load_no_trans`, which opens a temporary session and connection for every lazy load."],"keyPoints":["an eager mapping applies to every query that touches the entity, whether or not the data is needed","queries written in JPQL load eager associations through extra selects afterward, so N+1 simply moves","to-one associations are already eager by default, a frequent hidden cost","lazy mappings plus a per-query fetch plan inside the transaction give each use case exactly what it reads"],"examples":[{"title":"Lazy mapping with per-use-case fetch plans","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\npublic class LoanApplication {\n    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;\n\n    // BAD would be plain @ManyToOne: EAGER by default, loaded by every query\n    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, optional = false)\n    private Customer customer;\n\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"application\")          // LAZY by default\n    private Set<Document> documents = new HashSet<>();\n\n    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)\n    private ApplicationStatus status;\n}\n\npublic interface LoanApplicationRepository extends JpaRepository<LoanApplication, Long> {\n\n    // Review screen: customer and documents in one statement\n    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {\"customer\", \"documents\"})\n    Optional<LoanApplication> findWithDetailsById(Long id);\n\n    // Underwriting batch: only the customer\n    @Query(\"select a from LoanApplication a join fetch a.customer where a.status = :status\")\n    List<LoanApplication> findWithCustomerByStatus(@Param(\"status\") ApplicationStatus status);\n}","note":"The mapping says nothing about what to load; each repository method states the plan its use case needs, so a list screen never pays for the review screen's graph."},{"title":"Safety net configuration","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  jpa:\n    open-in-view: false              # lazy loading only inside service transactions\n    properties:\n      hibernate:\n        default_batch_fetch_size: 32 # remaining lazy loads use IN lists, not one query per row\n        # never: enable_lazy_load_no_trans: true","note":"Disabling OSIV surfaces missing fetch plans as exceptions in tests, and batch fetching limits the damage of any plan you missed."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does `hibernate.enable_lazy_load_no_trans` do, and why avoid it?","a":"It lets Hibernate open a temporary session and connection for each lazy load that happens after the original session closed, so the exception disappears. Every such access becomes a separate query in its own short transaction, outside the consistency of the original read, which hides N+1 problems and can mix data from different points in time. It treats the symptom; load what the use case needs inside the transaction instead."},{"q":"How do you catch accidental eager fetching or N+1 before production?","a":"Count statements in tests: Hibernate statistics (`hibernate.generate_statistics`) or a datasource proxy library can assert that a use case runs a fixed number of queries. Log SQL with bind parameters during development and review the statements each endpoint issues. In production, query counts per request in traces and call counts in `pg_stat_statements` show which statements explode."}],"extra":["**Batch fetching:** `hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size` initializes many proxies or collections of the same association per query using an IN list, turning N+1 into roughly N divided by the batch size plus one.","**Inverse one-to-one:** a `@OneToOne(mappedBy = ...)` side cannot be truly lazy without bytecode enhancement, because Hibernate must query to decide between null and a proxy; prefer `@MapsId` or mapping it only from the foreign key side.","**fetchgraph vs loadgraph:** a fetch graph treats attributes outside the graph as lazy while a load graph keeps their mapped fetch type; Spring Data's `@EntityGraph` defaults to the FETCH type.","**Measure the plan:** assert statement counts in integration tests (Hibernate statistics or datasource-proxy) so a fetch plan regression fails the build rather than production latency.","**Skip entities for reads:** for read-only screens a DTO projection sidesteps the lazy vs eager question entirely and selects only the columns shown."],"redFlags":["does not know that `@ManyToOne` defaults to eager loading","believes eager loading always means a single SQL join","silences lazy loading errors by enabling `hibernate.enable_lazy_load_no_trans`"]},{"id":101,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"What are the four JPA lifecycle states, and what happens to an entity loaded in one request and edited in the next?","level":"Basic","tldr":"A transient object is unknown to the persistence context; a managed entity is watched and its changes are flushed automatically; a detached one is no longer watched; a removed one is scheduled for deletion; `merge` writes detached state onto a managed copy and returns that copy.","answer":["A **transient** entity is a plain `new` object with no persistence context; `persist()` makes it managed and schedules an INSERT. A **managed** entity lives in the persistence context, which is both an identity map (one instance per id per context) and a unit of work: Hibernate keeps a snapshot of its loaded state and at flush compares the current state with it, issuing UPDATEs automatically. Inside a transaction, changing a managed entity needs no `save()` call.","A **detached** entity was managed but its context ended: the transaction finished and the context closed, `clear()` or `detach()` was called, or the object went to a client and came back. It keeps its id, but changes are not tracked, and touching an uninitialized lazy association throws `LazyInitializationException`. A **removed** entity is still associated with the persistence context, though `contains()` now returns false, and is scheduled for DELETE at flush.","`merge(detached)` finds or loads the managed instance with the same id (a SELECT if it is not in the context yet), copies the detached state onto it and returns that managed instance; the argument stays detached. Transient input produces a new managed copy and an INSERT. Spring Data's `save()` calls `persist` when `isNew()` is true and `merge` otherwise, which matters for entities with application-assigned ids: they look not new, so every save costs an extra SELECT. Implementing `Persistable` or adding a wrapper-typed `@Version` field (null means new) fixes that."],"keyPoints":["the persistence context only watches objects it loaded or was asked to persist","changes to a watched object reach the database at flush with no explicit call","once the context closes, the object keeps its data but nothing records further edits","reattaching works by copying data into the context's own instance, which is the one you must keep using"],"examples":[{"title":"State transitions in one service","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class CustomerService {\n    private final EntityManager em;\n\n    public CustomerService(EntityManager em) { this.em = em; }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public UUID register(String email) {\n        Customer c = new Customer(email);           // transient\n        em.persist(c);                              // managed: INSERT at flush\n        return c.getId();\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void changeEmail(UUID id, String email) {\n        Customer c = em.find(Customer.class, id);   // managed\n        c.changeEmail(email);                       // no save(): UPDATE at commit\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public Customer verify(Customer fromClient) {   // detached: has id, not tracked\n        Customer managed = em.merge(fromClient);    // SELECT, copy state, return managed copy\n        fromClient.markVerified();                  // BAD: ignored, still detached\n        managed.markVerified();                     // GOOD: tracked and flushed\n        return managed;                             // managed != fromClient\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void delete(UUID id) {\n        em.remove(em.find(Customer.class, id));     // removed: DELETE at flush\n    }\n}","note":"The key line is the BAD one: changes to the object you passed into `merge` are silently lost, because only the returned instance is managed."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does `persist` on a detached entity fail while `merge` works?","a":"`persist` is meant for new objects, so when it receives an instance that already has an id and a row but is not managed, Hibernate typically rejects it with an error such as `detached entity passed to persist`. `merge` instead finds or loads the managed instance for that id and copies the detached state onto it. Spring Data's `save()` chooses between the two using `isNew()`, which is why entities with assigned ids may need a `@Version` field or `Persistable` to be classified correctly."},{"q":"When an entity is removed, when is the row actually deleted?","a":"`remove()` only moves the managed entity to the removed state; the `DELETE` statement runs at the next flush, usually at commit. Until then the row still exists for other transactions, while inside the same persistence context a `find` for that id returns null. Cascaded removals are marked at the same time, so one `remove()` can produce many deletes when the context flushes."}],"extra":["**Identity guarantee:** within one persistence context two `find` calls for the same id return the same Java instance, which gives application-level repeatable reads for entities.","**getReference:** `em.getReference(Customer.class, id)` returns an uninitialized proxy without a SELECT, ideal for setting a foreign key association when you only have the id.","**clear() drops pending work:** `clear()` detaches everything including unflushed changes, so always `flush()` before `clear()` in batch code.","**Hibernate 6 API:** the legacy `Session.save`, `update` and `saveOrUpdate` methods are deprecated in Hibernate 6 and removed in Hibernate 7; use `persist` and `merge`, which follow the JPA state model.","**Web updates:** instead of merging a client-built entity (which can null out fields the client never sent), carry the id and version in a DTO, load the managed entity, check the version and apply the change through domain methods."],"redFlags":["calls `persist` on a detached entity and is surprised when it throws","thinks `remove` deletes the row immediately rather than at flush","confuses the Java `transient` keyword with the transient entity state"]},{"id":102,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"How do you map a bidirectional @OneToMany correctly: owning side, mappedBy and helper methods?","level":"Basic","tldr":"The child's `@ManyToOne` with `@JoinColumn` owns the association because it maps the foreign key; the parent's `@OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\")` is the inverse side, which Hibernate never writes. Keep both sides in sync with helper methods on the parent, make the `@ManyToOne` lazy, and do not expose the mutable collection.","answer":["In the database only the child table holds the relationship: `order_lines.order_id`. JPA mirrors that. The child's `@ManyToOne @JoinColumn(name = \"order_id\")` maps the foreign key, so it is the owning side, and Hibernate decides what to write into `order_id` from it alone. The parent's `@OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\")` is the inverse side: `mappedBy` names the field on the child that owns the association and declares the collection a read view of the same foreign key. Without `mappedBy`, Hibernate sees two independent relationships and maps the collection through an extra join table.","Because only the owning side is written, memory and database can disagree. Adding a line to `order.getLines()` without setting `line.order` inserts it with a null `order_id` (a NOT NULL violation or an orphan row). Setting only `line.order` stores the link, but an already loaded collection lacks the line, so the rest of the transaction works on stale state. Helper methods on the aggregate root fix both: `addLine` sets both sides, `removeLine` clears both, and the getter returns an unmodifiable view so callers cannot bypass them.","Production details: initialize the collection at the field, and declare `@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)` because to-one associations default to EAGER. Bidirectional links make Lombok's `toString` and `equals`, and Jackson, recurse until the stack overflows; keep associations out of them. Above all, the collection is optional: map it only when the parent manages a bounded set of children as part of its aggregate (order lines). For unbounded children (an account's ledger entries), keep only the `@ManyToOne` and query the children with paging."],"keyPoints":["the @ManyToOne side with the foreign key owns the association","mappedBy marks the collection as the inverse side Hibernate does not write","helper methods keep both sides in sync; skip the collection for unbounded children"],"examples":[{"title":"Owning side, inverse side and helper methods","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\n@Table(name = \"orders\")\npublic class Order {\n    @Id @GeneratedValue\n    private UUID id;\n\n    // inverse side: mappedBy names the owning field on OrderLine\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)\n    private List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    public void addLine(OrderLine line) {\n        lines.add(line);\n        line.setOrder(this);            // owning side: this is what writes order_id\n    }\n\n    public void removeLine(OrderLine line) {\n        lines.remove(line);\n        line.setOrder(null);            // orphanRemoval deletes the row at flush\n    }\n\n    public List<OrderLine> getLines() {\n        return Collections.unmodifiableList(lines);   // callers must use the helpers\n    }\n}\n\n@Entity\n@Table(name = \"order_lines\")\npublic class OrderLine {\n    @Id @GeneratedValue\n    private UUID id;\n\n    // owning side: maps the foreign key column\n    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)\n    @JoinColumn(name = \"order_id\", nullable = false)\n    private Order order;\n\n    private String sku;\n    private int quantity;\n\n    void setOrder(Order order) { this.order = order; }   // only Order calls it\n}","note":"`mappedBy` names the Java field `order`, not the column, which appears only in the owning side's `@JoinColumn`. Cascade and orphan removal sit on the inverse side because they describe the parent's lifecycle, not who writes the key."},{"title":"Three mapping mistakes and the SQL they produce","lang":"java","code":"// MISTAKE 1: only the inverse side is set (possible if getLines() exposes the raw list)\nOrder order = orderRepository.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();\norder.getLines().add(new OrderLine(\"SKU-1042\", 2));   // line.order stays null\n// flush: insert into order_lines (order_id, quantity, sku, id) values (null, 2, 'SKU-1042', ?)\n// result: NOT NULL violation, or an orphan row if the column allows null\n\n// MISTAKE 2: both sides mapped, no mappedBy\n@OneToMany\nprivate List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>();\n// Hibernate sees two relationships: OrderLine.order writes order_id, and this\n// collection expects its own join table. ddl-auto=validate fails on the missing\n// table; ddl-auto=update quietly creates it.\n\n// MISTAKE 3: unidirectional @OneToMany with @JoinColumn, no field on the child\n@OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)\n@JoinColumn(name = \"order_id\")\nprivate List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>();\n// insert into order_lines (quantity, sku, id) values (?, ?, ?)\n// update order_lines set order_id = ? where id = ?     (an extra UPDATE per line)","note":"Mistakes 2 and 3 compile and pass simple tests; only the SQL log (`org.hibernate.SQL`) or a statement-count test reveals them."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why not map every parent-child relationship as a bidirectional @OneToMany?","a":"A collection is a liability as well as a convenience: it can be loaded by accident, it takes part in dirty checking, and one `getEntries()` on an account with two million ledger entries takes the service down. Map it only when the parent owns a bounded set of children and changes them together, as with order lines. Otherwise a `@ManyToOne` on the child plus a paged query is simpler and cheaper."},{"q":"List or Set for the collection, and what is MultipleBagFetchException?","a":"A `List` without `@OrderColumn` is a bag to Hibernate: unordered and allowing duplicates. Fetching two bags in one query is rejected with `MultipleBagFetchException`, because the cartesian product of rows cannot be split back into two bags reliably. Switching to `Set` silences the exception but keeps the cartesian product; the real fix is one fetched collection per query. A `Set` also needs an `equals` and `hashCode` that stay stable when the generated id is assigned."}],"extra":["**Typos fail at startup:** `mappedBy` must name an existing field on the target entity, so a misspelling stops the `EntityManagerFactory` from building.","**Attach by reference:** to insert a child for an existing parent without loading it, set the `@ManyToOne` to `getReferenceById(orderId)`, a proxy carrying only the id, which is enough for the foreign key.","**Many-to-many:** one side owns the join table and the other uses `mappedBy`; once the link needs its own columns, map the join table as an entity with two `@ManyToOne` associations.","**One-to-one:** the owning side is the entity whose table holds the foreign key; `@MapsId` lets the child share the parent's primary key.","**Serialization loops:** `@JsonManagedReference` and `@JsonBackReference` stop Jackson's infinite recursion, but serializing entities still triggers lazy loading; DTOs remove both problems."],"redFlags":["Puts mappedBy on the @ManyToOne side or cannot say which side writes the foreign key","sets only the collection side and expects the link to be stored","maps unbounded child sets as collections and returns them from APIs"]},{"id":103,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"persist vs merge, and save vs saveAndFlush in Spring Data JPA: what is the difference?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`persist` makes a new instance managed and schedules its INSERT; `merge` copies the state of a detached or new object onto a managed instance, loading the row first if needed, and returns that managed copy. Spring Data's `save` calls `persist` when it decides the entity is new and `merge` otherwise, and `saveAndFlush` also flushes, which sends SQL to the database but does not commit.","answer":["`em.persist(payment)` makes that same transient object managed; the INSERT runs at flush, or immediately with an IDENTITY id, and a detached argument fails with a 'detached entity passed to persist' error. `em.merge(order)` never manages its argument: it finds the id in the persistence context or SELECTs the row, copies the argument's state onto that managed instance and returns it; without an id it creates a new managed copy to insert. The argument stays detached, so later changes to it are ignored.","`SimpleJpaRepository.save` is essentially `if (isNew(entity)) { em.persist(entity); return entity; } return em.merge(entity);`. By default `isNew` checks a wrapper-typed `@Version` field if there is one (null means new), otherwise the id (null, or 0 for a primitive). With ids assigned in Java (a `UUID` set in the constructor, an id from an upstream system) the id is never null, so `save` calls `merge`, which SELECTs to see whether the row exists before the INSERT: two statements per new row and an extra round trip per row in bulk imports. Fix it with `Persistable<ID>` and an explicit `isNew()`, a `@Version Long` field, or a direct `persist`.","`saveAndFlush` is `save` plus `flush()`: pending INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE statements run now, inside the open transaction, so constraint violations and generated values appear at a known line. Flush is not commit: the rows hold locks, no other transaction can see them, and a later failure still rolls them back. Use it to surface a unique violation as a 409 (the transaction is then rollback-only and must end), or before plain JDBC in the same transaction that must see the rows. Calling `save` on an entity loaded in the current transaction is redundant: dirty checking writes it at flush anyway."],"keyPoints":["persist makes the same object managed; merge returns a managed copy","save picks persist or merge via isNew; assigned ids cost an extra SELECT","saveAndFlush sends SQL now but commits nothing"],"examples":[{"title":"Assigned UUIDs turn save into SELECT plus INSERT; Persistable fixes it","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: id assigned in Java and no @Version, so isNew() is false and save() merges\n@Entity\npublic class Payment {\n    @Id\n    private UUID id = UUID.randomUUID();\n    private BigDecimal amount;\n}\n\npaymentRepository.save(new Payment(new BigDecimal(\"49.90\")));\n// select ... from payment where id = ?        (merge checks whether the row exists)\n// insert into payment (amount, id) values (?, ?)\n\n// GOOD: tell Spring Data explicitly when the entity is new\n@Entity\npublic class Payment implements Persistable<UUID> {\n    @Id\n    private UUID id = UUID.randomUUID();\n    private BigDecimal amount;\n\n    @Transient\n    private boolean isNew = true;\n\n    @Override public UUID getId() { return id; }\n    @Override public boolean isNew() { return isNew; }\n\n    @PostPersist\n    @PostLoad\n    void markNotNew() { this.isNew = false; }\n}\n// save(new Payment(...)) now calls persist: one INSERT at flush, batchable","note":"`@PostLoad` matters as much as `@PostPersist`: without it, a loaded and modified payment would report itself as new and `save` would try to insert it again."},{"title":"save, flush and commit, and why merge's return value matters","lang":"java","code":"@Transactional\npublic OrderView place(PlaceOrder cmd) {\n    Order order = orders.save(new Order(cmd.customerId(), cmd.orderNumber()));\n    // new entity: persist, same instance returned, INSERT deferred to flush\n    try {\n        orders.flush();   // INSERT runs now: the unique order_number is checked here\n    } catch (DataIntegrityViolationException e) {\n        throw new DuplicateOrderException(cmd.orderNumber(), e);  // tx is rollback-only\n    }\n    order.confirm();      // managed: dirty checking writes it, no save() needed\n    return OrderView.from(order);\n}   // COMMIT: only now can other transactions see the order\n\n@Transactional\npublic OrderView updateNote(Order detachedOrder, String note) {\n    Order managed = orders.save(detachedOrder);  // merge: SELECT, copy, managed copy\n    detachedOrder.setNote(note);                 // BAD: the argument is not tracked\n    managed.setNote(note);                       // GOOD: change the returned instance\n    return OrderView.from(managed);\n}","note":"The flush moves the error to a line you control; it does not make the order visible to anyone else. Visibility and durability come only with the commit when the method returns."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Does calling save() on an entity you just loaded in the same transaction do anything?","a":"Almost nothing: the entity is managed, `isNew` is false, and `merge` finds the instance in the persistence context and returns it unchanged. The UPDATE comes from dirty checking at flush either way. The call matters only when the entity is detached, for example loaded in one transaction and modified after it ended; then `save` is what writes it, through a SELECT and a copy."},{"q":"When is an explicit flush worth it, and when does it hurt?","a":"It is worth it to surface constraint errors at a known point, before plain JDBC or `JdbcClient` calls in the same transaction that must see pending changes (Hibernate auto-flushes before its own queries, not yours), and together with `clear()` every few hundred rows in batch jobs to bound memory. It hurts in loops: `saveAndFlush` per entity sends statements one at a time and defeats JDBC batching. Flush also releases no locks and makes nothing visible; only commit does."}],"extra":["**merge of a new object:** merging an object without an id inserts a copy and leaves the argument's id null, so merging that argument again inserts a second row.","**Hibernate 6.6 merge change:** merging an object whose `@GeneratedValue` id was set by hand but has no row now fails with an optimistic locking error (`ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException` in Spring) instead of inserting, which broke imports and tests that relied on `save` to insert such entities.","**Use the return value:** always continue with what `save` returns; after a merge that is the managed copy, the one whose `@Version` Hibernate increments at flush, and tests asserting on the argument pass with persist and fail with merge.","**@Version as the new-entity signal:** a wrapper `@Version Long version` makes `isNew` check the version instead of the id, fixing assigned-id inserts and adding optimistic locking in one change; do not initialize it to 0, or every entity looks existing.","**saveAll is a loop:** `saveAll` calls `save` per element, so with assigned ids and no `Persistable` every element pays the merge SELECT."],"redFlags":["Says saveAndFlush commits the transaction","cannot explain why an entity with an assigned id triggers a SELECT before its INSERT","treats persist and merge as interchangeable ways to write a row"]},{"id":104,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"Removing a line from `order.getLines()` deleted its row in the database. Which mapping setting does that, and how do JPA cascades relate?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Cascade propagates entity manager operations such as persist, merge and remove from a parent to its associated entities; `orphanRemoval` also deletes a child when it is disconnected from its owning parent's association.","answer":["Cascading is about lifecycle ownership. `CascadeType.PERSIST`, `MERGE`, `REMOVE`, `REFRESH` and `DETACH` (or `ALL`) make an operation on the parent apply to associated entities, so persisting an `Order` also persists its new `OrderLine`s. It is not a database `ON DELETE CASCADE`: Hibernate performs it on loaded entities. Use it only from an aggregate root to children that live and die with it.","`orphanRemoval = true` goes further: when a child is removed from the parent's collection, or a one-to-one reference is set to null, Hibernate deletes the child row at flush even though the parent still exists. That is exactly right for composition (order lines, documents of a loan application) and wrong for anything shared or reassignable.","Misused cascades delete data. `CascadeType.ALL` or `REMOVE` on a `@ManyToOne` means deleting one order deletes its customer; on a `@ManyToMany` it deletes shared tags or roles still used elsewhere. Cascade REMOVE also loads every child and deletes them one by one, which is slow for large collections, and JPQL bulk deletes bypass cascades entirely.","A sound default: `@OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)` on true compositions, no cascade on `@ManyToOne` or `@ManyToMany`, and helper methods that keep both sides of the association in sync."],"keyPoints":["cascading forwards operations such as persist, merge and remove from a parent to the entities it references","orphan removal deletes a child as soon as it is taken out of its parent's collection or reference","both belong on parent-to-child links where the child has no life of its own","cascading remove toward shared or many-to-one targets can delete data that other rows still need"],"examples":[{"title":"Aggregate root owning its lines","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\n@Table(name = \"orders\")\npublic class Order {\n    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;\n\n    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)              // GOOD: no cascade toward a shared entity\n    private Customer customer;\n\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)\n    private List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    public void addLine(Product product, int quantity) {\n        lines.add(new OrderLine(this, product, quantity));   // sets the owning side\n    }\n\n    public void removeLine(OrderLine line) {\n        lines.remove(line);                         // orphanRemoval: DELETE at flush\n        line.detachFromOrder();                     // keep both sides consistent\n    }\n\n    public void replaceLines(List<OrderLine> newLines) {\n        lines.clear();                              // GOOD: mutate the managed collection\n        newLines.forEach(l -> { l.attachTo(this); lines.add(l); });\n        // BAD: this.lines = newLines;  fails at flush with orphanRemoval\n    }\n}","note":"`OrderLine` owns the foreign key through its `@ManyToOne order` field; `Order` owns the lifecycle through cascade and orphanRemoval."},{"title":"Cascades that delete the wrong data","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\npublic class LoanApplication {\n    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;\n\n    // BAD: removing an application also removes the shared customer\n    @ManyToOne(cascade = CascadeType.ALL)\n    private Customer customer;\n\n    // BAD: removing an application deletes Tag rows other applications still use\n    @ManyToMany(cascade = CascadeType.REMOVE)\n    private Set<Tag> tags = new HashSet<>();\n\n    // GOOD: owned children, removed with the application or when detached from it\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"application\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)\n    private List<Document> documents = new ArrayList<>();\n}","note":"Ask of each cascade: if the parent disappears, should this row disappear too? Only the documents pass that test."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why might you prefer a database `ON DELETE CASCADE` over a JPA cascade for large child tables?","a":"A JPA remove cascade loads every child into the persistence context and issues one `DELETE` per row, which is slow and memory hungry for thousands of children. A foreign key with `ON DELETE CASCADE` lets the database remove them in one statement, and Hibernate can be told about it with `@OnDelete(action = OnDeleteAction.CASCADE)`. The trade-off is that entity lifecycle callbacks and listeners do not run for rows the database deletes, and children already loaded in memory may be stale."},{"q":"What does `CascadeType.MERGE` do when a client sends an order with its lines?","a":"Merging the order also merges every line in the graph: changed lines are copied onto their managed versions, and lines without ids become new managed instances that will be inserted. Lines missing from the incoming list are deleted only if orphan removal is enabled. Merging a whole client-supplied graph is convenient but risky, because the client can change fields or children it should not touch; applying explicit DTO changes to loaded entities is safer."}],"extra":["**Bulk operations skip everything:** JPQL or Criteria bulk `delete` and `update` bypass cascades, orphanRemoval, lifecycle callbacks and the persistence context, so children must be handled explicitly or by the database.","**Large child sets:** `@OnDelete(action = OnDeleteAction.CASCADE)` on the child's `@ManyToOne` together with `ON DELETE CASCADE` in the schema lets PostgreSQL delete children in one statement instead of loading and deleting them one by one.","**Replace vs mutate:** with orphanRemoval, assigning a new collection instance instead of mutating the managed one fails with 'A collection with cascade=\"all-delete-orphan\" was no longer referenced by the owning entity instance'.","**Unidirectional @OneToMany:** without `mappedBy`, Hibernate uses a join table, or with `@JoinColumn` inserts children and then issues extra UPDATEs to set the foreign key; the bidirectional mapping with the `@ManyToOne` owning side is the efficient one.","**MERGE cascade on client graphs:** cascading `merge` from a client-built graph can overwrite children or, with orphanRemoval, delete the ones the client simply omitted from the payload."],"redFlags":["puts `CascadeType.REMOVE` on a `@ManyToOne` that points at a shared parent","expects JPA cascades to create `ON DELETE CASCADE` constraints in the schema","reassigns a child to a different parent while orphan removal is on and is surprised by the outcome"]},{"id":105,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"A controller returns JPA entities straight to JSON. What risks does that create, and what would you return instead?","level":"Basic","tldr":"DTO projections fetch only the columns a use case needs, avoid lazy-loading and serialization traps, keep persistence internals private, and give clients a stable contract that does not change every time the schema does.","answer":["A DTO projection selects exactly what the endpoint returns: a JPQL constructor expression, a Spring Data interface or record projection, or plain SQL through `JdbcClient`. No entities are loaded, so there are no snapshots, no dirty checking, no accidental UPDATEs, fewer columns over the wire, and often index-only scans. For read-heavy APIs it is the single biggest ORM performance win.","Returning entities couples the API to the persistence model. Jackson walks the object graph: uninitialized lazy proxies either throw `LazyInitializationException` or, with Open Session in View, fire N+1 queries during serialization; bidirectional associations recurse infinitely; and Hibernate proxy internals leak into the JSON. Internal fields such as password hashes, risk scores, `version` or soft-delete flags are exposed unless someone remembers `@JsonIgnore`. On the input side, binding a request body to an entity is a mass-assignment hole: a client can set `creditLimit` or `status`.","DTOs make the API a deliberate contract. You can rename columns, split tables or change associations without breaking clients, version the API independently, and review exactly what leaves the service. The cost is mapping code, which records and MapStruct keep small. For writes, accept request DTOs, map them to commands, and let entity methods enforce invariants."],"keyPoints":["a projection selects just the columns the response needs, cutting I/O and memory","serializing entities can trigger lazy loads, infinite recursion or leaks of internal fields","a separate response type lets the schema evolve without breaking clients","read-only DTOs skip dirty checking and snapshots entirely"],"examples":[{"title":"Entity as API vs query straight into a record","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: the entity is the API contract; lazy graph, internals and version leak into JSON\n@GetMapping(\"/customers/{id}\")\npublic Customer get(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n    return customers.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n}\n\n// GOOD: an API record filled directly by the query\npublic record CustomerSummary(UUID id, String name, String segment, Long openApplications) {}\n\npublic interface CustomerRepository extends JpaRepository<Customer, UUID> {\n    @Query(\"\"\"\n        select new com.acme.credit.api.CustomerSummary(c.id, c.name, c.segment, count(a))\n        from Customer c left join c.applications a on a.status = :status\n        where c.id = :id\n        group by c.id, c.name, c.segment\n        \"\"\")\n    Optional<CustomerSummary> findSummary(@Param(\"id\") UUID id,\n                                          @Param(\"status\") ApplicationStatus status);\n}\n\n@GetMapping(\"/customers/{id}\")\npublic CustomerSummary get(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n    return customers.findSummary(id, ApplicationStatus.OPEN).orElseThrow(CustomerNotFoundException::new);\n}","note":"The good endpoint issues one narrow query, cannot trigger lazy loading during serialization, and exposes only the four fields it declares."},{"title":"Write side: request DTO instead of entity binding","lang":"java","code":"public record UpdateContactRequest(@NotBlank @Email String email, @NotBlank String phone) {}\npublic record ContactResponse(UUID id, String email, String phone) {}\n\n@PutMapping(\"/customers/{id}/contact\")\npublic ContactResponse updateContact(@PathVariable UUID id,\n                                     @Valid @RequestBody UpdateContactRequest req) {\n    return customerService.updateContact(id, req.email(), req.phone());\n}\n\n// in CustomerService\n@Transactional\npublic ContactResponse updateContact(UUID id, String email, String phone) {\n    Customer c = customers.findById(id).orElseThrow(CustomerNotFoundException::new);\n    c.changeContact(email, phone);          // entity enforces invariants; creditLimit is unreachable\n    return new ContactResponse(c.getId(), c.getEmail(), c.getPhone());   // mapped inside the tx\n}","note":"The request type physically cannot carry fields the client must not change, which closes the mass-assignment hole by construction."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What projection options does Spring Data JPA offer?","a":"Interface projections declare getters for the needed properties: closed ones select only those columns, while open ones that use `@Value` SpEL expressions load the whole entity. Class or record projections use a JPQL constructor expression, such as `select new com.example.OrderSummary(o.id, o.total) from Order o`, or a derived query that returns the record type. Dynamic projections take a `Class<T>` parameter so one repository method can return different shapes."},{"q":"When is returning an entity acceptable?","a":"In small internal tools or prototypes where the same team owns the API and the schema and changes them together, and the entity has no lazy associations or sensitive fields. Even then, the moment an external client depends on the shape, a dedicated response type pays for itself. Inside the service layer, working with entities is normal; what matters is what leaves the process."}],"extra":["**Closed vs open projections:** Spring Data closed interface projections select only the declared columns, but open projections using `@Value` SpEL load the full entity and lose the benefit.","**Index-only scans:** a narrow DTO query can be served entirely from a covering index in PostgreSQL (`CREATE INDEX ... INCLUDE (...)`), which an entity load selecting every column never can.","**Error contracts too:** return `ProblemDetail` (RFC 9457) bodies instead of exception or entity dumps, so error payloads are as stable as success payloads.","**Contract tests:** with explicit DTOs, JSON snapshot or consumer-driven contract tests (Spring Cloud Contract, Pact) catch accidental API changes that an entity refactor would otherwise ship silently.","**Security review:** DTOs turn field exposure into a visible code change: a new sensitive column added to an entity never reaches clients unless someone adds it to a DTO."],"redFlags":["piles `@JsonIgnore` and `@JsonManagedReference` onto entities until serialization stops failing","loads full entities and maps them to DTOs for large read-only lists, missing the query-level gain","exposes fields like password hashes or internal flags simply because the entity has them"]},{"id":106,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"What causes LazyInitializationException, and is Open Session in View the right fix?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"It is thrown when code touches an uninitialized lazy proxy or collection after the persistence context that loaded it has closed, typically in a controller, serializer, async task or cache. Open Session in View hides it by keeping the context open for the whole request, at the cost of queries outside the transaction and longer connection use; turn it off and load what each use case needs with fetch joins, entity graphs or DTO projections.","answer":["A lazy `@ManyToOne` is a generated proxy holding only the id; a lazy collection is a Hibernate wrapper such as `PersistentBag`. Both reference the session that created them, and on first real access (a getter other than the id, `size()`, iteration) they ask it to run a SELECT. If that session is closed or the entity was detached, Hibernate throws `LazyInitializationException`. With OSIV off, Spring closes the persistence context when the outermost `@Transactional` method returns, so the exception surfaces in whatever touches the entity next.","Typical triggers: returning entities from controllers so Jackson walks associations after the service returned, mapping to DTOs in the controller, logging `toString` after the transaction and handing entities to `@Async` methods or caches. Common bad fixes: switching associations to EAGER (global over-fetching), `hibernate.enable_lazy_load_no_trans=true` (a temporary session and connection per lazy access), and calling getters in the service just to force loading, one query each.","Spring Boot enables OSIV by default in servlet web apps (`spring.jpa.open-in-view=true`) and logs a startup warning unless you set the property explicitly. The interceptor opens the `EntityManager` when the request starts and closes it after the response is written, so lazy loads in controllers and serializers work, but they run outside the service transaction, one statement at a time, as invisible N+1, and once a query has run the connection typically stays checked out until the response is done. Set `spring.jpa.open-in-view=false` and give each failing use case an explicit fetch plan inside the transaction: `join fetch`, `@EntityGraph` on the repository method, or a DTO projection that never creates proxies."],"keyPoints":["lazy proxies need the persistence context that created them","OSIV hides the exception by stretching that context over the whole request","turn OSIV off and give each use case an explicit fetch plan inside the transaction"],"examples":[{"title":"Reproducing the exception and fixing it with a fetch plan","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: the transaction ends when findOrder returns; Jackson touches lazy fields later\n@GetMapping(\"/orders/{id}\")\nOrder get(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n    return orderService.findOrder(id);   // serializing customer or lines throws\n}                                        // LazyInitializationException (OSIV off)\n\n@Transactional(readOnly = true)\npublic Order findOrder(UUID id) {\n    return orders.findById(id).orElseThrow();        // customer and lines are proxies\n}\n\n// GOOD: the controller returns orderService.getOrder(id), a DTO built in the transaction\n@Transactional(readOnly = true)\npublic OrderResponse getOrder(UUID id) {\n    Order order = orders.findWithCustomerAndLinesById(id)\n        .orElseThrow(() -> new OrderNotFoundException(id));\n    return OrderResponse.from(order);                // reads only initialized state\n}\n\ninterface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, UUID> {\n    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {\"customer\", \"lines\"})\n    Optional<Order> findWithCustomerAndLinesById(UUID id);\n}","note":"With OSIV on, the bad version works and quietly issues extra queries during serialization; with OSIV off, that hidden cost becomes an error tests can find."},{"title":"Entities crossing thread and cache boundaries","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: the entity crosses into another thread; its session belongs to the caller\n@Async\npublic void sendInvoice(Order order) {\n    pdfRenderer.render(order.getLines());   // LazyInitializationException, or worse,\n}                                           // concurrent use of the caller's session\n\n// GOOD: pass the id and load what this task needs in its own transaction\n@Async\n@Transactional(readOnly = true)\npublic void sendInvoice(UUID orderId) {\n    Order order = orders.findWithLinesById(orderId).orElseThrow();\n    pdfRenderer.render(order.getLines());\n}\n\n// BAD: cached entities are detached; a later getAddresses() call throws\n@Cacheable(\"customers\")\n@Transactional(readOnly = true)\npublic Customer findCustomer(UUID id) {\n    return customers.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n}\n\n// GOOD: cache an immutable DTO built inside the transaction\n@Cacheable(\"customer-summaries\")\n@Transactional(readOnly = true)\npublic CustomerSummary findSummary(UUID id) {\n    return customers.findSummaryById(id);   // record or interface projection\n}","note":"OSIV never covered these paths: async tasks, scheduled jobs, message listeners and cache reads run outside any web request."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is hibernate.enable_lazy_load_no_trans worse than OSIV?","a":"Every lazy initialization outside a session opens a temporary session, borrows a connection, runs one SELECT and closes it again, so rendering 50 orders can mean 50 or more pool checkouts. Each load runs in its own implicit transaction, so the pieces can reflect different points in time. It removes the exception without any fetch planning; OSIV at least keeps one persistence context per request."},{"q":"How do you turn OSIV off in a large existing application without breaking production?","a":"Set `spring.jpa.open-in-view=false` in a branch, run integration and contract tests with realistic data, and exercise the main endpoints in staging; every `LazyInitializationException` marks an endpoint whose fetch plan lived in the web layer. Fix each by moving entity to DTO mapping into the transactional service with a fetch join, entity graph or projection. `hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size` is a useful safety net for lazy loads that remain inside transactions."}],"extra":["**Tests that hide it:** `@DataJpaTest` and any `@Transactional` test keep the persistence context open for the whole test, so lazy loading that fails in production passes; test mapping through the service's public API without a test transaction.","**Self-invocation:** calling a `@Transactional` method from the same class skips the proxy, so there is no transaction and the entity the repository returns is already detached.","**Check without loading:** `Hibernate.isInitialized(order.getLines())` checks whether data is loaded without triggering a query, useful in mappers that map an association only when fetched.","**Hibernate.initialize:** forcing initialization inside the transaction works and is explicit, but costs one SELECT per call; fetching in the original query is cheaper.","**Silence the warning deliberately:** setting `spring.jpa.open-in-view` explicitly, even to true, removes Boot's startup warning, so make the setting a recorded decision, not an accident."],"redFlags":["Fixes it by switching associations to EAGER or enabling enable_lazy_load_no_trans","cannot explain what a lazy proxy needs in order to initialize","keeps OSIV on without knowing it runs queries outside the service transaction"]},{"id":107,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"Two agents edit the same customer record at once. How can JPA detect or prevent the conflict, and which would you pick?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Optimistic locking holds no locks and detects conflicts at write time via a `@Version` check, so it needs retries; pessimistic locking takes row locks up front, risking waits, deadlocks and lower throughput.","answer":["Optimistic locking adds a `@Version` column. Every UPDATE becomes `update ... set version = ? where id = ? and version = ?`; if another transaction committed first, zero rows match and Hibernate throws `OptimisticLockException` (Spring translates it to `ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException`). No locks are held between read and write, so it scales when conflicts are rare and even works across requests: return the version as an ETag and require `If-Match` on updates.","The price is conflict handling. Retry the whole use case in a new transaction (re-read, re-apply, re-validate) a bounded number of times, or return 409 Conflict when the collision is a real human edit conflict. Use `OPTIMISTIC_FORCE_INCREMENT` to bump an aggregate root's version when only a child changed.","Pessimistic locking (`LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE`, a `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` style lock in PostgreSQL) locks rows at read time until commit. Conflicting transactions wait instead of failing, which is right for hot rows with frequent conflicts, but waiting transactions hold connections, throughput drops, and deadlocks appear when two transactions lock rows in different orders. Always pair it with a lock timeout or `NOWAIT`, lock in a consistent order, and keep the transaction short.","Default to optimistic; reach for pessimistic when contention is high or retries are expensive, and consider an atomic conditional UPDATE, which often beats both for balances and counters."],"keyPoints":["a version column checked in the `UPDATE ... WHERE` clause catches concurrent edits without holding locks","the losing writer gets an exception and must reload, merge or retry","row locks taken at read time block other writers until commit","low-contention, user-facing edits favor versions; hot rows with frequent conflicts may justify locks"],"examples":[{"title":"Optimistic locking with bounded retries","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\npublic class CreditLimit {\n    @Id private UUID accountId;\n    private BigDecimal available;\n    @Version private long version;               // checked and bumped on every UPDATE\n\n    public void reserve(BigDecimal amount) {\n        if (available.compareTo(amount) < 0) throw new LimitExceededException(accountId);\n        available = available.subtract(amount);\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class LimitService {\n    private final TransactionTemplate tx;\n    private final CreditLimitRepository limits;\n\n    public LimitService(TransactionTemplate tx, CreditLimitRepository limits) {\n        this.tx = tx;\n        this.limits = limits;\n    }\n\n    public void reserve(UUID accountId, BigDecimal amount) {\n        for (int attempt = 1; ; attempt++) {\n            try {                                // each attempt: new transaction, fresh read\n                tx.executeWithoutResult(s -> limits.findById(accountId).orElseThrow().reserve(amount));\n                return;\n            } catch (OptimisticLockingFailureException e) {\n                if (attempt == 3) throw e;       // surface as 409 after bounded retries\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The retry loop sits outside the transaction, so every attempt re-reads the current balance and re-validates the business rule instead of replaying stale data."},{"title":"Pessimistic lock vs atomic conditional update","lang":"java","code":"public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, UUID> {\n\n    // Pessimistic: row lock until commit; timeout \"0\" means NOWAIT (fail instead of queueing)\n    @Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)\n    @QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = \"jakarta.persistence.lock.timeout\", value = \"0\"))\n    @Query(\"select a from Account a where a.id = :id\")\n    Optional<Account> findByIdForUpdate(@Param(\"id\") UUID id);\n\n    // Often better: one atomic statement, no read, no lock wait window\n    @Modifying\n    @Query(\"update Account a set a.balance = a.balance - :amount where a.id = :id and a.balance >= :amount\")\n    int debitIfSufficient(@Param(\"id\") UUID id, @Param(\"amount\") BigDecimal amount);\n}","note":"A return value of 0 from `debitIfSufficient` means insufficient funds; note that bulk JPQL updates bypass `@Version` and the persistence context."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does the version check protect an edit that spans two HTTP requests?","a":"Within the second request the entity is reloaded with the current version, so the database check alone cannot tell that the user edited stale data. The client must send back the version it originally read, in the payload or as an `If-Match` ETag, and the server compares it with the current version before applying the change. On a mismatch, return `409 Conflict` or `412 Precondition Failed` so the client can reload and reapply the edit."},{"q":"What should a retry after `OptimisticLockException` look like?","a":"Retry the whole unit of work in a new transaction, reloading the entity so the decision is made on current data; resending the failed `UPDATE` with the old object would just fail again or overwrite the other change. Bound the attempts, add a little jitter, and retry automatically only when the operation can be recomputed safely, such as incrementing a counter. For user edits, surface the conflict instead and let the person decide."}],"extra":["**SKIP LOCKED queues:** `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` (lock timeout hint `-2` in Hibernate) lets several workers claim different rows from a job or outbox table without blocking each other.","**Deadlock detection:** PostgreSQL checks for deadlocks after `deadlock_timeout` (1 s by default) and aborts one transaction with SQLSTATE `40P01`, which your code should treat as retryable.","**Bulk updates and versions:** JPQL bulk `update` statements do not increment `@Version` unless you set it yourself (HQL also offers `update versioned`), so mixing bulk updates with optimistic entities needs care.","**Version type:** prefer a numeric `@Version` over a timestamp; timestamps can collide within clock resolution and depend on clock sync across nodes.","**Compare the right version:** across HTTP requests the check only protects you if you compare the version the client saw (from `If-Match` or the DTO); loading fresh and copying fields without that check reintroduces lost updates."],"redFlags":["holds a database lock while a human edits a form","omits the version from the API, so stale client edits silently overwrite newer data","believes `@Version` makes the second writer wait instead of failing"]},{"id":108,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"First-level vs second-level cache in Hibernate: what does each cache and when does it help?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"The first-level cache is the persistence context: one per `EntityManager` (in Spring, per transaction), always on, one instance per id. The second-level cache is optional, shared by every session of the `SessionFactory`, and stores state for entities you mark cacheable; it helps read-mostly data loaded by id, and query results need the separate query cache.","answer":["The first-level cache is not a tuning feature; it is the persistence context. Two `em.find(Account.class, id)` calls in one transaction return the same object and run one SELECT, because the context maps each id to one managed instance, the basis of dirty checking. It cannot be disabled and lives as long as the `EntityManager`. It serves lookups by id (`find`, `getReference`, association loading); a JPQL query always hits the database, but returned rows whose ids are already managed resolve to the existing instances, so in-memory state wins over newer column values until `refresh`.","The second-level (L2) cache belongs to the `SessionFactory` and is shared across transactions and threads. It is off until you configure a provider (JCache with Ehcache 3 or Caffeine, or Infinispan) and mark entities `@Cacheable`, usually with Hibernate's `@Cache(usage = ...)` to pick a concurrency strategy. It stores disassembled state, column values rather than objects, so each session builds its own instance. On `find` or association loading Hibernate checks the persistence context, then L2, then the database. Strategies: `READ_ONLY` for immutable data, `NONSTRICT_READ_WRITE` for rarely updated data that tolerates brief staleness, `READ_WRITE` with soft locks, `TRANSACTIONAL` with JTA.","L2 pays off for read-mostly entities loaded by id or through to-one associations far more often than they change: countries, currencies, product catalog, tenant settings. It hurts for write-heavy entities (puts and invalidations, few hits), data read mostly through queries, data that other services or scripts write behind Hibernate's back, and mutable data in local caches on several pods. A Spring `@Cacheable` method returning DTOs, or Redis with explicit invalidation, is often simpler."],"keyPoints":["first level: per persistence context, always on, identity and by-id reuse","second level: shared across sessions, opt-in per entity, stores dehydrated state","L2 suits read-mostly data loaded by id; queries need the separate query cache"],"examples":[{"title":"The persistence context: identity, by-id reuse and stale state","lang":"java","code":"@Transactional\npublic void showPersistenceContext(UUID accountId) {\n    Account first = em.find(Account.class, accountId);    // SELECT ... where id = ?\n    Account again = em.find(Account.class, accountId);    // no SQL: served by the context\n    System.out.println(first == again);                   // true: one instance per id\n\n    // a JDBC write in the same transaction bypasses Hibernate\n    jdbcClient.sql(\"update account set status = 'FROZEN' where id = :id\")\n              .param(\"id\", accountId)\n              .update();\n\n    Account viaQuery = em.createQuery(\n            \"select a from Account a where a.id = :id\", Account.class)\n        .setParameter(\"id\", accountId)\n        .getSingleResult();              // SQL runs, but the row resolves to the\n                                         // already managed instance\n    System.out.println(viaQuery.getStatus());             // ACTIVE, not FROZEN\n\n    em.refresh(first);                                    // explicit reload\n    System.out.println(first.getStatus());                // FROZEN\n}","note":"This staleness lasts only until the transaction ends; a shared second-level cache stretches it across transactions and pods, which is why it needs a strategy and a single owner of every write."},{"title":"A good and a poor second-level cache candidate","lang":"java","code":"// spring.jpa.properties: hibernate.cache.region.factory_class=jcache,\n// hibernate.javax.cache.provider=org.ehcache.jsr107.EhcacheCachingProvider\n\n// GOOD candidate: reference data, read constantly by id and through @ManyToOne\n@Entity\n@Cacheable\n@org.hibernate.annotations.Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_ONLY)\n@Immutable\npublic class Country {\n    @Id\n    private String isoCode;          // \"SE\", \"DE\"\n    private String name;\n    private String currencyCode;\n}\n\n@Entity\npublic class Merchant {\n    @Id @GeneratedValue\n    private UUID id;\n\n    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)\n    private Country country;         // initialized from L2, not a SELECT per merchant\n}\n\n// POOR candidate: updated on every transfer and mostly read through queries,\n// so the region churns with puts and invalidations and rarely serves a hit\n@Entity\n@Cacheable\n@org.hibernate.annotations.Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_WRITE)\npublic class Account {\n    @Id\n    private UUID id;\n    private BigDecimal balance;\n    }","note":"By default only entities marked cacheable are cached, so configuring the provider alone changes nothing."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Six pods each run a local second-level cache. What goes wrong?","a":"Each pod has its own region contents, and a write through pod A updates or invalidates only A's copy, so the other five serve old state until eviction or expiry. For `READ_ONLY` reference data this does not matter. For mutable entities you need a clustered provider that propagates invalidations (Infinispan in a clustered mode), a short expiry that bounds staleness the business accepts, or no L2 cache for that entity."},{"q":"How does the query cache work, and why is it often left off?","a":"It is enabled with `hibernate.cache.use_query_cache=true` and opted into per query with the `org.hibernate.cacheable` hint, and it stores results keyed by query text and parameter values. Hibernate keeps an update timestamp per table and treats a cached result as stale once any table it reads has been written. On frequently written tables the hit rate is near zero while the bookkeeping still costs, so enable it only for specific queries over rarely changing tables."}],"extra":["**Bypass per query:** the standard hints `jakarta.persistence.cache.retrieveMode` (`BYPASS`) and `jakarta.persistence.cache.storeMode` (`REFRESH`) let one read skip or refresh L2, for example a balance check that must see current data.","**Collection caches:** caching a `@OneToMany` stores only the child ids; if the child entity is not cached too, each hit loads the children one SELECT at a time, an N+1 caused by the cache.","**Natural id cache:** `@NaturalIdCache` caches the natural id to primary key resolution, making `bySimpleNaturalId` lookups by order number or IBAN cheap.","**Batch jobs:** a job loading a million rows in one transaction keeps them all managed, with snapshots; `flush()` and `clear()` every N items, or use a `StatelessSession`, which has no first-level cache.","**Measure per region:** with `hibernate.generate_statistics` on, compare hits, misses and puts per region; many puts and few hits means pure overhead."],"redFlags":["Thinks the first-level cache is shared between requests or can be switched off","expects the second-level cache to speed up JPQL queries on its own","caches frequently updated entities locally on many pods without considering staleness"]},{"id":109,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"A method only loads a customer and trims its name for display, yet the SQL log shows an `UPDATE`. Why?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Hibernate snapshots each managed entity on load and, at flush, issues UPDATEs for any difference from the snapshot, so any mutation of a managed entity inside a transaction gets written.","answer":["When an entity is loaded into the persistence context, Hibernate stores a copy of its loaded state. At flush (commit, before overlapping queries, or an explicit `flush()`), it compares every managed entity's current property values with its snapshot and schedules an UPDATE for each dirty entity, by default setting all columns. No `save()` is needed; calling `repository.save()` on a managed entity changes nothing. The cost grows with the number of managed entities and properties, and snapshots roughly double the memory of a large context.","Surprise updates come from treating entities as ordinary mutable objects inside a transaction. A service masks a phone number for a response, a mapper calls a setter, validation fills in a default, or a temporary calculation writes into a field, and all of it is persisted at commit. Broad boundaries amplify this: class-level `@Transactional` on a read-heavy service, or Open Session in View keeping entities managed through the web layer. Mutable types cause phantom updates too: a `Date` mutated in place, a JSON attribute, or an `AttributeConverter` that does not round-trip identically looks dirty on every flush, bumping `@Version` and producing optimistic lock failures nobody can explain.","Mitigations: use `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` for reads (no snapshots, no flush), return DTO projections instead of entities, keep transactions narrow, expose intention-revealing domain methods instead of setters, mark reference data `@Immutable`, and watch for UPDATE statements on GET endpoints."],"keyPoints":["on load, Hibernate keeps a copy of each managed entity's state","at flush it compares current field values with that copy and writes every difference","a setter call on a managed object inside a transaction is enough; no save is needed","read-only transactions, DTO projections or detaching prevent accidental writes"],"examples":[{"title":"Presentation logic writing to the database","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: formatting mutates a managed entity; the commit issues an UPDATE on a GET\n@Transactional\npublic CustomerResponse getCustomer(UUID id) {\n    Customer c = customers.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n    c.setPhone(PhoneFormat.mask(c.getPhone()));   // masked number now stored in the DB\n    c.setName(c.getName().trim());                 // silent data change\n    return CustomerResponse.from(c);\n}\n\n// GOOD: read-only transaction, formatting applied to the DTO only\n@Transactional(readOnly = true)\npublic CustomerResponse getCustomer(UUID id) {\n    Customer c = customers.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n    return new CustomerResponse(c.getId(), c.getName().trim(), PhoneFormat.mask(c.getPhone()));\n}","note":"No `save()` appears anywhere in the bad version, yet it permanently overwrites the customer's phone number with the masked value."},{"title":"What the SQL log shows for the bad version","lang":"sql","code":"-- GET /customers/42 with logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG\nselect c1_0.id,c1_0.name,c1_0.phone,c1_0.version from customer c1_0 where c1_0.id=?\nupdate customer set name=?,phone=?,version=? where id=? and version=?","note":"An UPDATE in the log of a read endpoint is the fastest way to spot unintended dirty checking in production code."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why can loading thousands of entities in one transaction get slow even without any changes?","a":"Each managed entity has a snapshot copy, so memory roughly doubles, and every flush walks all of them comparing fields, which costs CPU in proportion to the context size. Use a read-only transaction or Hibernate's read-only query hint so no snapshots are kept, select DTOs for reporting, or process large sets in chunks with `flush()` and `clear()`. Bytecode-enhanced dirty tracking can also replace the full comparison with per-field change flags."},{"q":"How would you make an entity safe from accidental modification?","a":"Mark a reference or reporting entity with Hibernate's `@Immutable`, which skips dirty checking and ignores changes to it. For individual columns, `@Column(updatable = false)` keeps a field out of `UPDATE` statements. More broadly, avoid public setters on entities, expose intention-revealing methods, and run code that only reads in `@Transactional(readOnly = true)`."}],"extra":["**readOnly internals:** for `readOnly = true` transactions Spring's `HibernateJpaDialect` sets `FlushMode.MANUAL` and makes the session default to read-only, so entities are loaded without snapshots and never flushed.","**@DynamicUpdate:** makes Hibernate write only the changed columns, useful for wide tables, at the cost of generating SQL per update instead of reusing one cached statement.","**Bytecode enhancement:** Hibernate's enhancement plugin with dirty tracking lets entities record their own changes, avoiding the full snapshot comparison in very large persistence contexts.","**Selective read-only:** `session.setReadOnly(entity, true)` or the query hint `org.hibernate.readOnly` loads specific entities without snapshots inside an otherwise writable transaction.","**Symptoms to watch:** `@PreUpdate` callbacks and `@LastModifiedDate` fire for these surprise updates too, so `updated_at` changing on rows nobody edited is a strong hint."],"redFlags":["blames a database trigger or another service before looking for setter calls in the transaction","does not know that a converter or a mutable type with a weak `equals` can make an untouched field look changed","assumes the comparison is free regardless of how many entities are loaded"]},{"id":110,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"Importing a million rows through Spring Data `saveAll` takes an hour. Which Hibernate settings and id strategy would you look at first?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Enable JDBC batching with ordered inserts so many rows share one round trip, use pooled SEQUENCE or application-generated ids because IDENTITY forces immediate inserts, and flush and clear periodically to bound memory.","answer":["Without batching, persisting 100,000 rows means 100,000 network round trips. Setting `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size` (for example 50) makes Hibernate group identical INSERT or UPDATE statements into JDBC batches sent in one round trip each. Add `hibernate.order_inserts=true` and `hibernate.order_updates=true` so parent and child statements are grouped per table instead of alternating and breaking batches, and on PostgreSQL set `reWriteBatchedInserts=true` on the JDBC URL so the driver rewrites a batch into multi-row INSERTs.","The identifier strategy decides whether batching happens at all. With `IDENTITY`, the id exists only after the row is inserted, but Hibernate needs it at `persist()` time, so it executes each INSERT immediately and silently disables insert batching for that entity. `SEQUENCE` with a pooled optimizer (`allocationSize` 50, matching the sequence's `INCREMENT BY`) fetches a block of ids per database call, so inserts can be deferred and batched. Application-generated UUIDs also work; prefer time-ordered UUIDv7 over random v4 for B-tree locality.","Memory is the other limit. Every persisted entity stays managed, with its snapshot, until the transaction ends, so a naive loop over a million rows grows the heap and makes every flush slower. Call `flush()` and `clear()` every batch-size entities and split huge imports into chunked transactions. Spring Data `saveAll()` does neither, and with assigned ids it calls `merge`, adding a SELECT per row. For very large loads, `JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate` or PostgreSQL `COPY` beat the ORM."],"keyPoints":["setting `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size` together with `hibernate.order_inserts` sends rows in far fewer round trips","database-generated identity keys force each insert to run on its own","a pooled sequence lets Hibernate assign ids in memory and keep batching","flushing and clearing after every batch stops the persistence context from growing without bound"],"examples":[{"title":"Sequence ids and flush/clear in chunks","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\npublic class LedgerEntry {\n    @Id\n    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = \"ledger_entry_seq\")\n    @SequenceGenerator(name = \"ledger_entry_seq\", sequenceName = \"ledger_entry_seq\", allocationSize = 50)\n    private Long id;            // BAD alternative: GenerationType.IDENTITY disables insert batching\n    private UUID accountId;\n    private BigDecimal amount;\n    // constructors and factory omitted\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class LedgerImportService {\n    private static final int BATCH_SIZE = 50;       // same as hibernate.jdbc.batch_size\n    private final EntityManager em;\n\n    public LedgerImportService(EntityManager em) { this.em = em; }\n\n    @Transactional                                  // caller passes chunks of a few thousand rows\n    public void importChunk(List<LedgerRow> rows) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < rows.size(); i++) {\n            em.persist(LedgerEntry.from(rows.get(i)));\n            if ((i + 1) % BATCH_SIZE == 0) {\n                em.flush();                         // send one JDBC batch\n                em.clear();                         // drop managed entities and snapshots\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The database sequence must be created with `INCREMENT BY 50` to match `allocationSize`, otherwise ids collide or Hibernate reports a mismatch."},{"title":"Batching configuration for PostgreSQL","lang":"properties","code":"spring.datasource.url=jdbc:postgresql://db:5432/ledger?reWriteBatchedInserts=true\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.order_inserts=true\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.order_updates=true\n# Verify: session metrics report how many JDBC batches were executed\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.generate_statistics=true","note":"Enable statistics in a test or staging run to confirm batches are actually executed, then turn it off again in production."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does a sequence with `allocationSize = 50` reduce database round trips?","a":"Hibernate's pooled optimizer fetches one sequence value, treats it as the boundary of a block of 50 ids and hands those ids out in memory, so it calls `nextval` once per 50 inserts instead of once per row. The database sequence's `INCREMENT BY` must match the allocation size, or ids can collide or schema validation fails. Gaps in ids after restarts are normal and harmless."},{"q":"When would you bypass JPA entirely for a bulk load?","a":"When the rows need no entity behavior, such as lifecycle callbacks, cascades or dirty checking, and volume dominates. `JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate` with the PostgreSQL driver's `reWriteBatchedInserts=true`, or `COPY` through the driver's `CopyManager`, is typically much faster than the ORM. You give up the persistence context, so validate the data first and keep already loaded entities out of the same transaction, or clear them, to avoid stale state."}],"extra":["**Hibernate 6 sequence defaults:** Hibernate 6 uses one sequence per entity named after it (suffix `_seq`) instead of a shared `hibernate_sequence`, and the JPA default `allocationSize` is 50, so schema migrations must create sequences with a matching increment.","**Logs can mislead:** `org.hibernate.SQL` prints every statement even when they are sent as one batch, so verify batching with Hibernate statistics or a datasource-proxy listener rather than by counting log lines.","**Versioned entities:** batched UPDATEs of `@Version` entities are supported (`hibernate.jdbc.batch_versioned_data` is enabled by default), so optimistic locking does not rule out batching.","**Chunked transactions:** commit every few thousand rows (Spring Batch chunk-oriented steps do exactly this) so a late failure does not roll back a million-row import and a long transaction does not hold back vacuum.","**COPY for large loads:** PostgreSQL `COPY ... FROM STDIN` through PgJDBC's `CopyManager` is usually far faster than batched INSERTs for millions of rows, at the cost of bypassing entity logic entirely."],"redFlags":["never confirms in SQL logs or statistics that batching actually happens","keeps a sequence allocation size of 1, adding a round trip for every id","adds parallel threads before removing the per-row round trips"]},{"id":111,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"What does ACID mean, concretely, for a backend service?","level":"Basic","tldr":"For a transfer of 100 SEK between two accounts: atomicity means both balance changes and the ledger entry commit together or not at all, consistency means the declared constraints still hold afterwards, isolation means concurrent transfers do not corrupt each other, within the limits of the isolation level, and durability means a committed transfer survives a crash. Each covers one database transaction, nothing outside it.","answer":["**Atomicity**: debiting account A, crediting account B and inserting the ledger row form one unit; if the credit fails or the pod dies halfway, the database discards the debit too. **Consistency** in ACID means the transaction takes the database from one state that satisfies its declared rules to another: `CHECK (balance >= 0)`, foreign keys, `UNIQUE` and `NOT NULL`. A violation aborts the transaction. A rule that lives only in Java, such as 'debits equal credits', is your job, not ACID's.","**Isolation** is a spectrum, not a yes or no. Only Serializable promises that concurrent transactions behave as if they ran one at a time; Read Committed (PostgreSQL's default) and Repeatable Read allow specific anomalies in exchange for less blocking and fewer aborts. Under Read Committed, two transfers that read A's balance, check it in Java and write back the result can both succeed and overdraw it. The usual fix is a guarded atomic `UPDATE` whose affected row count is checked, or a stricter level with retries. **Durability** means that once `COMMIT` returns, the change survives a crash: PostgreSQL writes the commit to its write-ahead log (WAL) and fsyncs it before acknowledging.","In production the boundaries matter most. Durability is only as good as the storage and settings: `synchronous_commit = off` acknowledges before the WAL flush, so a crash can lose the last moments of acknowledged commits; `fsync = off` risks corruption; and with asynchronous replication, failing over to a lagging replica loses acknowledged commits. Atomicity stops at the database: a Kafka publish or payment provider call inside a `@Transactional` method is not rolled back with it, which is what the transactional outbox and idempotency keys exist for."],"keyPoints":["atomic: all statements of the transfer commit or none do","consistency means declared constraints; isolation is a spectrum of levels","durability rests on WAL fsync and replication, and ends at the database boundary"],"examples":[{"title":"A transfer that leans on all four guarantees","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE account (\n    id      uuid PRIMARY KEY,\n    balance numeric(19,2) NOT NULL CHECK (balance >= 0)       -- consistency: declared rule\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE ledger_entry (\n    transfer_id uuid NOT NULL,\n    account_id  uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES account (id),\n    amount      numeric(19,2) NOT NULL,                       -- negative = debit\n    PRIMARY KEY (transfer_id, account_id)                     -- a replayed transfer fails\n);\n\nBEGIN;                                                        -- READ COMMITTED by default\n\n-- isolation: waits for a concurrent update, then re-checks the guard on the new row\nUPDATE account SET balance = balance - 100.00\n WHERE id = :from_account AND balance >= 100.00;\n-- 0 rows updated: insufficient funds, so the application rolls back\n\nUPDATE account SET balance = balance + 100.00 WHERE id = :to_account;\n\nINSERT INTO ledger_entry (transfer_id, account_id, amount) VALUES\n  (:transfer_id, :from_account, -100.00),\n  (:transfer_id, :to_account,    100.00);\n\nCOMMIT;  -- durability: returns only after the WAL record is flushed to disk\n         -- atomicity: an error or crash before this point leaves no trace of any of it","note":"The `CHECK` constraint is a backstop; the guarded `UPDATE` is the business check, and its zero-row result becomes a clean domain error."},{"title":"Where the ACID boundary ends in a Spring service","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class TransferService {\n\n    private final JdbcClient jdbc;\n    private final PaymentEvents events;   // publishes to Kafka; constructor omitted\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void transfer(UUID transferId, UUID from, UUID to, BigDecimal amount) {\n        int debited = jdbc.sql(\"\"\"\n                update account set balance = balance - :amount\n                where id = :id and balance >= :amount\n                \"\"\")\n            .param(\"amount\", amount).param(\"id\", from)\n            .update();\n        if (debited == 0) {\n            throw new InsufficientFundsException(from);   // unchecked: rolls back\n        }\n        jdbc.sql(\"update account set balance = balance + :amount where id = :id\")\n            .param(\"amount\", amount).param(\"id\", to)\n            .update();\n        insertLedgerEntries(transferId, from, to, amount);\n\n        // BAD: outside the transaction; if the commit fails, consumers saw a\n        // transfer that never happened\n        events.publishTransferCompleted(transferId);\n        // GOOD: insert an outbox row here and let a relay publish after commit\n    }\n}","note":"Everything on the same connection is atomic; the Kafka call is not. The unique `transferId` also makes a retry after an unknown commit outcome safe."}],"followUps":[{"q":"The connection drops right after the service sends COMMIT. Did the transfer happen?","a":"The client cannot know: the commit may have completed with the acknowledgment lost, or never reached the server. The database itself stays atomic, so the transfer is either fully applied or absent, but a blind retry could apply it twice. Give each transfer a client-generated id with a unique constraint, so a retry either inserts or hits the constraint and returns the stored outcome."},{"q":"Would you run transfers under Serializable?","a":"Usually not as a blanket setting. Read Committed with guarded atomic updates, locking rows in a consistent order (by account id) to avoid deadlocks, protects single-row invariants cheaply. Serializable is worth it when an invariant spans rows you do not lock, such as a limit across all of a customer's accounts; PostgreSQL then aborts conflicting transactions with SQLSTATE `40001`, so the whole transaction must be retried."}],"extra":["**Two meanings of consistency:** the C in ACID is about constraints within one database; the C in CAP is about replicas returning the latest write.","**Defaults differ:** PostgreSQL defaults to Read Committed and MySQL InnoDB to Repeatable Read, and PostgreSQL treats Read Uncommitted as Read Committed, so it never shows dirty reads.","**Durability per transaction:** `SET LOCAL synchronous_commit = off` relaxes durability for one low-value transaction, such as an analytics event, while transfers keep full durability.","**Deferred constraints:** `DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED` moves a unique or foreign key check to `COMMIT`, allowing temporary violations such as swapping two unique positions.","**Rollback-only after errors:** in PostgreSQL any failed statement aborts the transaction, and later statements fail until rollback; use a savepoint to continue after an expected error."],"redFlags":["Defines consistency vaguely as data being correct or confuses it with CAP consistency","assumes every transaction is fully isolated by default","thinks a Kafka publish or HTTP call inside the transaction is rolled back with it"]},{"id":112,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"Why does an index make a lookup faster, and what would you tell a teammate who wants one on every column?","level":"Basic","tldr":"An index is a separate sorted structure (a B-tree by default in PostgreSQL) that lets the planner reach matching rows without scanning the whole table, paid for with storage, slower writes and ongoing maintenance.","answer":["A B-tree index keeps key values sorted with pointers (TIDs) to heap tuples, so a lookup costs a few page reads plus one heap fetch per match instead of reading every page. Because it is ordered, the same index can also serve `ORDER BY ... LIMIT` without a sort, support merge joins, enforce uniqueness and, when it holds every referenced column, answer the query with an index-only scan.","The costs are real. Each index takes disk and buffer cache, and every `INSERT`, `DELETE` and non-HOT `UPDATE` must also modify every index on the table: write amplification, extra WAL and extra replication traffic. Indexes bloat, need vacuum and sometimes `REINDEX CONCURRENTLY`, and adding one to a live table requires `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` to avoid blocking writes.","Whether an index helps depends on selectivity and query shape. Reading 30% of a table through random heap access is slower than a sequential scan, so a plain index on a low-cardinality `status` column is usually ignored; a partial index on the rare value is what helps. The predicate must also match the index: a function on the column, a leading wildcard `LIKE`, an implicit cast or a filter on a non-leading composite column defeats it.","In production I add indexes from evidence (`pg_stat_statements`, `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`), prefer one composite index that serves several queries over many single-column ones, and drop indexes that `pg_stat_user_indexes` shows are never scanned."],"keyPoints":["a sorted structure lets the planner jump to matching entries instead of reading the whole table","every insert, update and delete must also maintain each index, adding write I/O and WAL volume","indexes occupy disk and memory and compete with table data for cache","the benefit depends on how selective the filter is and whether the query can use the index's order"],"examples":[{"title":"Index from a real query, not one per column","lang":"sql","code":"-- Hot query: a customer's most recent orders\nSELECT id, status, total_amount, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = $1\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nLIMIT 20;\n\n-- BAD: single-column indexes on everything\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_status ON orders (status);        -- 4 distinct values, planner ignores it\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_total  ON orders (total_amount);  -- no query filters on it, pure write cost\n\n-- GOOD: one composite index matching filter and sort, built without blocking writes\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_customer_created\n    ON orders (customer_id, created_at DESC);\n\n-- GOOD: partial index for a skewed predicate (only a few rows are PENDING)\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_pending_created\n    ON orders (created_at)\n    WHERE status = 'PENDING';\n\n-- Case-insensitive lookups need an expression index\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_customers_email_lower\n    ON customers (lower(email));","note":"The composite index serves both the filter and the `ORDER BY ... LIMIT`, so the query stops after 20 index entries. The partial index stays tiny because it only contains the rows the job actually looks for."},{"title":"Find indexes that cost writes but never serve reads","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT s.relname                                   AS table_name,\n       s.indexrelname                              AS index_name,\n       s.idx_scan,\n       pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(s.indexrelid)) AS index_size\nFROM pg_stat_user_indexes s\nJOIN pg_index i ON i.indexrelid = s.indexrelid\nWHERE s.idx_scan = 0\n  AND NOT i.indisunique        -- unique indexes enforce constraints even if never scanned\n  AND NOT i.indisprimary\nORDER BY pg_relation_size(s.indexrelid) DESC;","note":"Statistics are per server and reset on `pg_stat_reset()`, so check replicas and the time window before dropping anything. Drop with `DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you add an index to a large production table without blocking writes?","a":"Use `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`, which builds the index without a lock that blocks inserts, updates and deletes, at the cost of a slower build that scans the table twice. It cannot run inside a transaction block, so the migration must be marked non-transactional, which both Flyway and Liquibase support. If it fails midway it leaves an `INVALID` index that you must drop and create again."},{"q":"How do you find indexes that cost more than they give?","a":"Check `pg_stat_user_indexes` for indexes whose `idx_scan` stays near zero over a representative period, remembering that statistics can be reset and that replicas keep their own counts. Look for duplicates and for indexes made redundant by a composite index with the same leading columns. Before dropping one, confirm it does not back a unique constraint, then remove it with `DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY`."}],"extra":["**HOT updates:** PostgreSQL skips index maintenance when an `UPDATE` changes no indexed column and the new tuple fits on the same page; indexing a frequently changed column such as `updated_at` disables that and multiplies write cost. A `fillfactor` below 100 leaves room for HOT updates.","**Index-only scans need the visibility map:** even with a covering index, PostgreSQL visits the heap for pages not marked all-visible, which shows up as high `Heap Fetches` in `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on poorly vacuumed tables.","**Other index types:** GIN for `jsonb`, arrays and full-text search; BRIN for huge append-only tables whose physical order follows a column such as `created_at` (tiny but coarse); GiST for ranges, geometry and exclusion constraints.","**Concurrent builds can fail halfway:** `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` cannot run inside a transaction block, and if it fails it leaves an `INVALID` index that still costs writes; find it via `pg_index.indisvalid` and drop it before retrying.","**Foreign keys are not indexed automatically:** PostgreSQL indexes the referenced primary key but not the referencing column, so deleting a parent row or joining on `order_lines.order_id` scans the child table unless you add that index yourself."],"redFlags":["builds an index on a large live table without `CONCURRENTLY`, blocking writes for the duration","expects an index on a low-cardinality column such as `status` to speed up every query","never checks `pg_stat_user_indexes` for indexes nobody uses"]},{"id":113,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"INNER, LEFT, RIGHT and FULL OUTER joins: what does each return?","level":"Basic","tldr":"An INNER JOIN returns only row pairs that satisfy the `ON` condition; a LEFT JOIN also keeps left rows without a match, filling the right columns with NULL; a RIGHT JOIN does the same for the right table; and a FULL OUTER JOIN keeps unmatched rows from both sides. Joins multiply rows on one-to-many relationships, and a `WHERE` condition on the right table of a LEFT JOIN quietly turns it into an inner join.","answer":["Think of a join as producing every pair of rows from both tables, then keeping the pairs where `ON` is true; that is the INNER JOIN. Outer joins add back rows that found no partner: LEFT keeps every row of the left table, with NULLs in the right table's columns when nothing matched; RIGHT keeps every row of the right table; FULL OUTER keeps both. RIGHT JOIN is rare in practice because swapping the table order turns it into the more readable LEFT JOIN. Joins are about cardinality too: joining 3 customers to 10 orders returns up to 10 rows, not 3, because each customer appears once per matching order.","The classic bug: 'list every customer with their orders from the last 30 days, including customers with none'. Writing `LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE o.created_at > now() - interval '30 days'` loses the customers without recent orders. For them `o.created_at` is NULL, the comparison yields NULL, and `WHERE` keeps only rows where it is true, so the LEFT JOIN now behaves like an INNER JOIN. Conditions on the optional side belong in `ON`, which decides what counts as a match; `WHERE` filters the joined result. For inner joins the two placements are equivalent, which is why the bug survives code review.","The opposite question, 'customers with no orders', is an anti-join: `LEFT JOIN ... WHERE o.id IS NULL`, or more readably `WHERE NOT EXISTS (select 1 from orders o where o.customer_id = c.id)`, which PostgreSQL plans as an anti-join as well. Avoid `NOT IN (select customer_id from orders)`: one NULL in the subquery makes the condition unknown for every row and the query returns nothing. In production, joins also explain inflated numbers: joining orders to both their lines and their payments multiplies rows, so `sum(p.amount)` counts each payment once per line; aggregate each child table separately, then join the results."],"keyPoints":["inner keeps matches; outer joins add unmatched rows padded with NULLs","a WHERE filter on the right table of a LEFT JOIN makes it an inner join","anti-joins with NOT EXISTS, never NOT IN over nullable columns"],"examples":[{"title":"Each join type on the same data","lang":"sql","code":"-- customers: (1, 'Anna'), (2, 'Ben'), (3, 'Cara')\n-- orders:    (101, customer 1), (102, customer 1), (103, customer 2), (104, guest: NULL)\n\nSELECT c.name, o.id AS order_id\nFROM customers c\nINNER JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id;\n-- Anna 101 | Anna 102 | Ben 103                     (Cara and the guest order disappear)\n\nSELECT c.name, o.id AS order_id\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id;\n-- Anna 101 | Anna 102 | Ben 103 | Cara NULL         (every customer, Anna twice)\n\nSELECT c.name, o.id AS order_id\nFROM customers c\nRIGHT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id;\n-- Anna 101 | Anna 102 | Ben 103 | NULL 104          (every order, guest order kept)\n\nSELECT c.name, o.id AS order_id\nFROM customers c\nFULL OUTER JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id;\n-- Anna 101 | Anna 102 | Ben 103 | Cara NULL | NULL 104\n\n-- NULL never equals NULL: a guest order would not match a customer\n-- whose id were NULL either; use IS NOT DISTINCT FROM if that is intended","note":"Anna appears twice in every result: a join returns one row per matching pair, so counting customers after a join needs `count(DISTINCT c.id)` or a query that does not join at all."},{"title":"The LEFT JOIN filter trap and two anti-joins","lang":"sql","code":"-- BAD: meant to list all customers with their recent orders.\n-- For Cara, o.created_at is NULL, the comparison is NULL, and WHERE drops her.\nSELECT c.name, o.id\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE o.created_at > now() - interval '30 days';\n\n-- GOOD: the condition decides what counts as a match, so it belongs in ON\nSELECT c.name, o.id\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o\n       ON o.customer_id = c.id\n      AND o.created_at > now() - interval '30 days';\n\n-- Anti-join: customers with no orders at all\nSELECT c.id, c.name\nFROM customers c\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id);\n\n-- Equivalent, and planned the same way by PostgreSQL\nSELECT c.id, c.name\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE o.id IS NULL;            -- test the right table's NOT NULL key\n\n-- BAD: the guest order has customer_id NULL, so NOT IN returns zero rows\nSELECT c.id FROM customers c\nWHERE c.id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders);","note":"In the `IS NULL` anti-join, test a column that can only be NULL when there is no match, such as the primary key; testing a nullable column would also return customers whose orders merely have that column empty."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why can a join make a SUM come out too high?","a":"Joining a parent to two child tables multiplies rows: an order with 3 lines and 2 payments yields 6 rows, so `sum(p.amount)` counts each payment 3 times and `sum(l.quantity)` each line twice. `DISTINCT` inside the aggregate is not a fix, because two payments of the same amount would then count once. Aggregate each child in its own subquery or CTE grouped by order id and join those results, or use correlated subqueries per value."},{"q":"How does this carry over to JPQL and join fetch?","a":"In JPQL, `join` is an inner join, so `select o from Order o join fetch o.lines` silently drops orders that have no lines; use `left join fetch` when the association is optional. A `where` condition on a left-joined association has the same effect as in SQL, so put it in the join's `on` clause (`with` in older HQL). Path expressions such as `o.customer.name` in `where` also create inner joins, which can remove rows with a null customer."}],"extra":["**Reconciliation with FULL OUTER JOIN:** joining the internal ledger to a provider's settlement report on the payment reference and keeping rows where either side is NULL lists exactly the breaks: missing settlements and unknown payments.","**Semi-join with EXISTS:** to filter customers that have at least one order without duplicating them, `WHERE EXISTS (...)` returns each customer once, while an inner join returns one row per order.","**Avoid NATURAL JOIN:** it joins on every column with the same name, so adding `created_at` to both tables silently changes the join condition; use explicit `ON` or `USING`.","**CROSS JOIN:** a join with no condition returns every combination; it is useful with `generate_series` to build calendars, and a missing `ON` predicate in old comma syntax creates one by accident.","**Indexes for joins:** PostgreSQL does not index foreign key columns automatically, so `orders.customer_id` needs its own index for nested loop joins and for deletes on the parent table."],"redFlags":["Thinks a LEFT JOIN returns exactly one row per left row","filters the right table of a LEFT JOIN in WHERE and expects unmatched rows to remain","uses NOT IN against a nullable subquery for anti-joins"]},{"id":114,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"How do you find the N-th highest salary per department?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Rank rows within each department with a window function partitioned by department and ordered by salary descending, then filter the rank in an outer query. `DENSE_RANK` gives the N-th distinct salary value, `RANK` leaves gaps after ties, and `ROW_NUMBER` picks one arbitrary row among ties, so first agree with the interviewer what N-th means when salaries tie.","answer":["Clarify the question before writing SQL: with salaries 90k, 90k, 80k, is the second highest 90k (the second person) or 80k (the second distinct value)? Window functions give you either. `DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY department_id ORDER BY salary DESC)` numbers distinct values 1, 1, 2, so rank 2 is 80k. `RANK()` numbers 1, 1, 3, leaving a gap, so a department where two people share the top salary has no rank 2 at all. `ROW_NUMBER()` numbers 1, 2, 3 and breaks ties arbitrarily, so the result can change between runs unless you add a tiebreaker such as `id`. For 'N-th highest salary' the usual intent is the N-th distinct value: `DENSE_RANK`.","Window functions are computed after `WHERE`, `GROUP BY` and `HAVING`, so you cannot filter on them in the same query's `WHERE`; wrap the ranking in a CTE or subquery and filter the rank outside (PostgreSQL has no `QUALIFY` clause). The pre-window alternative is a correlated subquery: keep an employee when exactly N-1 distinct salaries in the same department are higher. It is portable and readable but re-counts per row, so it degrades quadratically on large departments. Two edge cases come up: PostgreSQL sorts NULLs first in descending order, so a NULL salary ranks as the highest unless you exclude it or write `NULLS LAST`; and departments with fewer than N distinct salaries return no row, which may need to be shown explicitly.","On a large table the window version reads and sorts every employee; an index on `(department_id, salary DESC)` removes the sort but still visits all rows. When there are few departments and N is small, a `LATERAL` subquery per department with `ORDER BY salary DESC OFFSET n - 1 LIMIT 1` walks only the top of the index for each department, reading a handful of entries instead of the whole table."],"keyPoints":["DENSE_RANK for the N-th distinct value; RANK leaves gaps; ROW_NUMBER is arbitrary on ties","filter the rank in an outer query: windows run after WHERE","handle NULL salaries and departments with fewer than N values"],"examples":[{"title":"RANK, DENSE_RANK and ROW_NUMBER on tied salaries","lang":"sql","code":"-- employees in the payments department (id 10)\n-- (1, 'Lina', 95000), (2, 'Omar', 95000), (3, 'Sara', 88000), (4, 'Jonas', 80000)\n\nSELECT name,\n       salary,\n       ROW_NUMBER() OVER w AS row_number,\n       RANK()       OVER w AS rank,\n       DENSE_RANK() OVER w AS dense_rank\nFROM employees\nWHERE department_id = 10\nWINDOW w AS (PARTITION BY department_id ORDER BY salary DESC);\n\n--  name  | salary | row_number | rank | dense_rank\n-- -------+--------+------------+------+-----------\n--  Lina  |  95000 |          1 |    1 |          1\n--  Omar  |  95000 |          2 |    1 |          1     (row_number 1 or 2: arbitrary)\n--  Sara  |  88000 |          3 |    3 |          2\n--  Jonas |  80000 |          4 |    4 |          3\n\n-- \"Second highest salary\" means:\n--   DENSE_RANK = 2  -> Sara, 88000  (second distinct value: usually what is asked)\n--   RANK = 2        -> no row       (the tie at the top skips rank 2)\n--   ROW_NUMBER = 2  -> Lina or Omar, 95000, varying between runs","note":"The named `WINDOW` clause avoids repeating the same `OVER (...)` three times; all three functions see the same partition and ordering, and only their tie handling differs."},{"title":"The answer, the correlated subquery and the index-friendly version","lang":"sql","code":"-- 1. Window function: the N-th distinct salary per department (:n = 2)\nWITH ranked AS (\n    SELECT department_id, id, name, salary,\n           DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY department_id\n                              ORDER BY salary DESC) AS salary_rank\n    FROM employees\n    WHERE salary IS NOT NULL          -- PostgreSQL sorts NULLs first in DESC order\n)\nSELECT department_id, id, name, salary\nFROM ranked\nWHERE salary_rank = :n;               -- tied employees are all returned\n\n-- 2. Correlated subquery: exactly n - 1 distinct higher salaries in the department\nSELECT e.department_id, e.id, e.name, e.salary\nFROM employees e\nWHERE e.salary IS NOT NULL\n  AND :n - 1 = (SELECT count(DISTINCT e2.salary)\n                FROM employees e2\n                WHERE e2.department_id = e.department_id\n                  AND e2.salary > e.salary);\n\n-- 3. LATERAL with an index on (department_id, salary DESC):\n--    reads only the top of each department's index range\nSELECT d.id AS department_id, top.salary\nFROM departments d\nLEFT JOIN LATERAL (\n    SELECT DISTINCT e.salary\n    FROM employees e\n    WHERE e.department_id = d.id AND e.salary IS NOT NULL\n    ORDER BY e.salary DESC\n    OFFSET :n - 1 LIMIT 1\n) top ON true;                        -- departments with fewer values show NULL","note":"Version 3 returns one salary value per department, NULL where there are too few; join back to `employees` for names."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What changes if you need the top 3 earners per department instead?","a":"Filter `salary_rank <= 3` instead of `= 3`, and decide what ties mean: `DENSE_RANK` returns everyone at the top three salary values, possibly many people, while `ROW_NUMBER` with a tiebreaker returns exactly three rows per department. `RANK <= 3` sits between them, returning more than three only when a tie spans the cutoff. The same pattern serves 'latest order per customer' with `ROW_NUMBER() ... ORDER BY created_at DESC` and rank 1."},{"q":"Could you use NTH_VALUE instead?","a":"Carefully. `NTH_VALUE(salary, 2) OVER (PARTITION BY department_id ORDER BY salary DESC)` uses the default frame, which ends at the current row, so it returns NULL for rows before the second one; you must widen the frame with `ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING`. It also counts rows, not distinct values, so with a tie at the top it returns the top salary again, like `ROW_NUMBER`."}],"extra":["**Single N-th across the company:** `SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary DESC NULLS LAST OFFSET 1 LIMIT 1` is the simple version; it does not generalize to per department without a window or `LATERAL`.","**Empty result vs NULL:** a scalar subquery that finds no N-th salary yields NULL, while the CTE version yields no row; decide which one your API returns.","**Windows do not reduce rows:** unlike `GROUP BY`, a window function keeps every input row and adds a column, which is why you can rank and still select names and ids.","**Top N from the index:** with an index on `(department_id, salary DESC)` and a filter on one department, `ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT n` stops after n index entries without sorting.","**ORM reach:** derived Spring Data methods cannot express per-group ranking; use a native query with an interface or record projection, or `JdbcClient`, with N as a bind parameter, rather than ranking in Java."],"redFlags":["Uses ROW_NUMBER without asking what N-th means when salaries tie","puts the window function directly in WHERE","ignores NULL salaries and departments with fewer than N distinct salaries"]},{"id":115,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"WHERE vs HAVING, and in what logical order is a SELECT evaluated?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`WHERE` filters individual rows before grouping and cannot use aggregates; `HAVING` filters groups after aggregation and can. A SELECT is defined as if evaluated in the order FROM and JOIN, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT (window functions here), DISTINCT, ORDER BY, LIMIT, which is why a SELECT alias is unknown in WHERE but usable in ORDER BY.","answer":["The clauses are written in one order and logically evaluated in another. **FROM and JOIN** build the input rows; **WHERE** discards rows; **GROUP BY** collapses the survivors into groups; **HAVING** discards groups using aggregates such as `count(*)` or `sum(amount)`; **SELECT** computes the output expressions, including window functions; **DISTINCT** removes duplicate output rows; **ORDER BY** sorts; **LIMIT and OFFSET** cut the result. Each step sees only what earlier steps produced. `WHERE` runs before any aggregate exists, so `WHERE count(*) > 5` is an error, and `HAVING` runs after rows are grouped, so it can only refer to grouped columns and aggregates.","The order explains the familiar errors. A column alias defined in `SELECT` does not exist yet when `WHERE` runs, so `WHERE vat > 100` fails with 'column vat does not exist'; repeat the expression or compute it in a subquery or CTE. `ORDER BY` runs after `SELECT`, so it can use the alias. PostgreSQL also accepts output column names in `GROUP BY` as an extension, but not in `WHERE` or `HAVING`, while MySQL accepts them in `HAVING`, so portable SQL repeats the expression. Window functions are computed in the `SELECT` step, after `HAVING`, which is why filtering on `ROW_NUMBER()` needs an outer query. And `LIMIT` without `ORDER BY` returns whichever rows the plan produces first.","This is the logical order that defines the result, not the physical execution order. The planner may push predicates into scans and joins, reorder joins, read an index in sorted order so `ORDER BY ... LIMIT 20` stops after 20 rows, and PostgreSQL even moves a `HAVING` condition without aggregates into `WHERE`. For performance and readability, filter rows in `WHERE` whenever the condition does not need an aggregate: fewer rows reach the grouping step and indexes can be used. In Spring Data this is where derived query methods stop: grouping with `HAVING` needs `@Query` with a projection."],"keyPoints":["WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups with aggregates","logical order: FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, DISTINCT, ORDER BY, LIMIT","SELECT aliases are unknown in WHERE but visible in ORDER BY"],"examples":[{"title":"WHERE and HAVING in one report, numbered by logical step","lang":"sql","code":"-- Merchants with more than 50 failed card payments in the last 24 hours\nSELECT p.merchant_id,                                   -- 5. compute output columns\n       m.name,\n       count(*)                                 AS failed_count,\n       count(*) FILTER (WHERE p.amount > 1000)  AS large_failed_count,\n       sum(p.amount)                            AS failed_amount\nFROM payments p                                         -- 1. build input rows\nJOIN merchants m ON m.id = p.merchant_id\nWHERE p.status = 'FAILED'                               -- 2. filter rows (index usable)\n  AND p.method = 'CARD'\n  AND p.created_at >= now() - interval '24 hours'\nGROUP BY p.merchant_id, m.name                          -- 3. one group per merchant\nHAVING count(*) > 50                                    -- 4. filter groups by aggregate\nORDER BY failed_count DESC                              -- 6. sort: alias allowed here\nLIMIT 20;                                               -- 7. cut the result\n\n-- BAD: a row filter in HAVING; PostgreSQL's planner moves it into WHERE, but write it there yourself\nSELECT p.merchant_id, p.status, count(*) AS payment_count\nFROM payments p\nGROUP BY p.merchant_id, p.status\nHAVING p.status = 'FAILED' AND count(*) > 50;","note":"`FILTER (WHERE ...)` is a per-aggregate condition evaluated during grouping, a cleaner PostgreSQL alternative to `sum(CASE WHEN ... END)` that lets one query count several subsets."},{"title":"Alias and ordering errors explained by the evaluation order","lang":"sql","code":"-- ERROR: column \"vat\" does not exist (WHERE runs before SELECT defines it)\nSELECT id, amount * 0.25 AS vat\nFROM invoices\nWHERE vat > 100;\n\n-- FIX 1: repeat the expression\nSELECT id, amount * 0.25 AS vat FROM invoices WHERE amount * 0.25 > 100;\n\n-- FIX 2: define it in a subquery or CTE, then filter on it\nSELECT * FROM (\n    SELECT id, amount * 0.25 AS vat FROM invoices\n) t\nWHERE t.vat > 100;\n\n-- OK: ORDER BY runs after SELECT, so the alias exists\nSELECT id, amount * 0.25 AS vat FROM invoices ORDER BY vat DESC;\n\n-- ERROR in PostgreSQL: column \"failed_count\" does not exist\nSELECT merchant_id, count(*) AS failed_count\nFROM payments GROUP BY merchant_id HAVING failed_count > 50;\n-- FIX: HAVING count(*) > 50\n\n-- ERROR: for SELECT DISTINCT, ORDER BY expressions must appear in select list\nSELECT DISTINCT customer_id FROM orders ORDER BY created_at DESC;\n-- after DISTINCT there is no single created_at per customer_id to sort by","note":"Each error names a value that does not exist yet, or no longer exists, at that step of the logical order."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Does the database actually execute the clauses in that order?","a":"No. The logical order defines what the result must be; the planner may execute anything that produces the same result. It pushes `WHERE` predicates into index scans and below joins, reorders joins, uses an index's sort order to skip a sort, and stops early when `LIMIT` is satisfied. `EXPLAIN` shows the physical plan, which often looks nothing like the clause order."},{"q":"What does SELECT count(*) return for an empty table, with and without GROUP BY?","a":"Without `GROUP BY`, an aggregate query treats all input as a single group, so `SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE false` returns one row with 0 and `sum(amount)` returns one row with NULL. With `GROUP BY customer_id` there are no groups, so the query returns no rows at all. Java code that expects exactly one row, or a non-null sum, should account for both, for example with `coalesce(sum(amount), 0)`."}],"extra":["**HAVING without GROUP BY:** it is legal and treats the whole result as one group, as in `SELECT count(*) FROM orders HAVING count(*) > 1000`, which returns one row or none.","**Functional dependency:** PostgreSQL lets you select any column of a table when you group by that table's primary key, so `GROUP BY c.id` allows `c.name` without listing it.","**Counting rows vs values:** `count(*)` counts rows, `count(column)` skips NULLs and `count(DISTINCT column)` counts distinct non-null values; mixing them up after an outer join counts unmatched rows as 1 instead of 0.","**Window functions run after HAVING:** a window function can use aggregates, as in `rank() OVER (ORDER BY sum(amount) DESC)`, but it cannot appear in `WHERE`, `GROUP BY` or `HAVING`.","**Stable pagination:** `ORDER BY created_at LIMIT 20` is nondeterministic when timestamps tie; add a unique tiebreaker such as `id` so pages neither repeat nor skip rows."],"redFlags":["Uses HAVING for plain row filters or tries to use aggregates in WHERE","believes clauses run in the order they are written","expects LIMIT without ORDER BY to return the same rows every time"]},{"id":116,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"A feed API slows down on deep pages and shows duplicates as new items arrive. How would you redesign its pagination?","level":"Basic","tldr":"OFFSET reads and discards every skipped row and shifts when data changes; keyset pagination seeks from the last seen unique sort key through an index, so every page costs the same and stays stable.","answer":["`ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50 OFFSET 200000` makes the database produce 200,050 rows and throw 200,000 away, so page cost grows linearly with depth and deep pages become the slowest queries in the system. It is also inconsistent: an insert or delete between two requests shifts every later page, so users see duplicates or miss rows. Its advantages are simplicity and jumping to an arbitrary page number.","Keyset (seek, cursor) pagination remembers the sort key of the last row returned and asks for rows after it: `WHERE (created_at, id) < ($2, $3) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT 50`. With an index on `(merchant_id, created_at DESC, id DESC)` each page is an index seek plus 50 entries, whatever the depth, and concurrent inserts do not shift pages you have not read yet.","It requires a total, stable order: a unique tiebreaker (the primary key) in both `ORDER BY` and the cursor, and sort columns that do not change while a client is paging. The cursor should be opaque to clients, and each supported sort order needs its own index. You lose random page access and cheap page counts.","I use offset only for small admin lists with a capped maximum offset, and keyset for public APIs, infinite scroll, exports and batch jobs. Spring Data 3.1+ supports keyset scrolling natively with `ScrollPosition` and `Window`."],"keyPoints":["the database must still read and throw away every row before the requested offset","inserts between requests shift offsets, so rows repeat or go missing","a seek predicate on the last seen sort key uses the index and costs the same on every page","the trade-off is losing random jumps to arbitrary page numbers"],"examples":[{"title":"Deep OFFSET versus keyset seek","lang":"sql","code":"-- BAD: scans and discards 200000 rows; pages shift when orders are inserted\nSELECT id, customer_id, total_amount, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE merchant_id = $1\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50 OFFSET 200000;\n\n-- Index for the keyset query; id makes the order total and unique\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_merchant_created_id\n    ON orders (merchant_id, created_at DESC, id DESC);\n\n-- GOOD: first page\nSELECT id, customer_id, total_amount, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE merchant_id = $1\nORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n\n-- GOOD: next page; $2 and $3 are created_at and id of the last row already returned\nSELECT id, customer_id, total_amount, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE merchant_id = $1\n  AND (created_at, id) < ($2, $3)\nORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\nLIMIT 50;","note":"The row-value comparison `(created_at, id) < ($2, $3)` becomes a single index range condition because both columns sort in the same direction, so page 4,000 costs the same as page 1."},{"title":"Keyset scrolling with Spring Data 3.1+","lang":"java","code":"public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {\n    Window<Order> findFirst50ByMerchantIdOrderByCreatedAtDescIdDesc(long merchantId, ScrollPosition position);\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class OrderExportService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n\n    public OrderExportService(OrderRepository orders) {\n        this.orders = orders;\n    }\n\n    // Walks every order of a merchant page by page without OFFSET\n    public void export(long merchantId, Consumer<Order> sink) {\n        WindowIterator<Order> it = WindowIterator\n                .of(position -> orders.findFirst50ByMerchantIdOrderByCreatedAtDescIdDesc(merchantId, position))\n                .startingAt(ScrollPosition.keyset());\n        while (it.hasNext()) {\n            sink.accept(it.next());\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Spring Data derives the `(created_at, id)` seek predicate from the sort. For a REST API, serialize the keys of `window.positionAt(last)` into an opaque cursor and rebuild it with `ScrollPosition.forward(keys)`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you encode the cursor returned to clients?","a":"Serialize the last row's sort key values, for example timestamp and id, together with the sort direction, then base64url encode the result and sign it with an HMAC if tampering matters. Clients treat it as opaque and send it back as `?after=...`. Adding a version to the format lets you change the sort or schema later without breaking clients that still hold old cursors."},{"q":"Users still want to jump straight to page 40. What can you offer?","a":"Keep keyset for next and previous navigation and offer coarse jumps through filters instead, such as a date picker that seeks to a timestamp. If numbered pages are a hard requirement, allow offsets only up to a small cap and keep them on an index-backed order. Showing an approximate total from planner statistics, rather than an exact `COUNT(*)`, also avoids an expensive scan."}],"extra":["**Mixed sort directions:** a row-value comparison only maps to one index range when all columns sort the same way; for `created_at DESC, id ASC` you need `created_at < $2 OR (created_at = $2 AND id > $3)`, which is less index friendly, so keep directions aligned.","**Counting is expensive too:** `COUNT(*)` for \"page X of Y\" is a full scan in PostgreSQL because MVCC keeps no stored row count; fetch `LIMIT n + 1` to know if there is a next page, or show an estimate from `pg_class.reltuples`.","**Cursor hygiene:** encode the sort values as opaque base64, bind the cursor to the filter and sort it was issued for, and sign it (HMAC) if clients must not craft or tamper with cursors.","**Batch jobs and backfills:** walking a big table with `WHERE id > :last ORDER BY id LIMIT 1000` keeps every batch fast and lets the job resume after a crash, while offset batching gets slower with each batch.","**Backward navigation:** a \"previous page\" is the same query with the comparison and sort flipped, then the result reversed in the application."],"redFlags":["exposes raw column values in the cursor instead of an opaque token clients cannot tamper with","promises total page counts that need a full `COUNT(*)` on every request","forgets an index that matches the sort order exactly, so keyset queries still sort the table"]},{"id":117,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"A dashboard query joins seven tables, and someone proposes a wide summary table. What would convince you to denormalize?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Normalize by default so each fact lives in one place and constraints protect integrity; denormalize only for a measured read problem, with one declared source of truth and an explicit synchronization and reconciliation plan.","answer":["Normalization stores each fact once: an update touches one row, and foreign keys, unique and check constraints keep the data valid, so update anomalies cannot happen. Systems of record such as orders, payments and accounts start normalized, and with the right indexes PostgreSQL joins are cheap at OLTP scale; \"joins are slow\" usually means a missing index.","Denormalization is an optimization for a proven read problem: a hot endpoint whose `EXPLAIN` shows expensive joins or aggregates, such as an account balance summed from millions of ledger entries, or a list page needing counts and names from four tables. The forms range from a stored aggregate column, to a copied field, a materialized view, a CQRS read model, or a search index.","It is never free. Every copy needs a declared source of truth and a synchronization plan: update it in the same transaction (strongly consistent, but more write work and possible hot-row contention), refresh it periodically (bounded staleness), or propagate it asynchronously through an outbox or CDC (eventual consistency, rebuildable). Add a reconciliation job that detects drift, because copies do drift.","Distinguish a snapshot from a copy: an order's unit price and shipping address are facts about the order at purchase time, not redundancy, and must not follow later changes. I decide field by field based on read to write ratio, staleness tolerance and who owns the truth."],"keyPoints":["a normalized schema stores each fact once, so constraints and updates stay simple","a copy is justified only by a measured read problem that indexes or query changes cannot fix","every duplicated value needs one owner, an update path and a way to detect drift","materialized views or read models are often safer than duplicating columns in core tables"],"examples":[{"title":"Stored balance with a sync plan and drift detection","lang":"sql","code":"-- Source of truth: append-only ledger\nCREATE TABLE ledger_entries (\n    id          bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n    account_id  bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES accounts (id),\n    amount      numeric(19, 4) NOT NULL,\n    created_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\n-- Denormalized read field on the account row\nALTER TABLE accounts ADD COLUMN balance numeric(19, 4) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;\n\n-- Sync plan: both writes in one transaction, so they cannot diverge\nBEGIN;\nINSERT INTO ledger_entries (account_id, amount) VALUES (42, -250.00);\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 250.00 WHERE id = 42;\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- Reconciliation: accounts whose stored balance differs from the ledger\nSELECT a.id, a.balance, COALESCE(SUM(l.amount), 0) AS ledger_sum\nFROM accounts a\nLEFT JOIN ledger_entries l ON l.account_id = a.id\nGROUP BY a.id, a.balance\nHAVING a.balance <> COALESCE(SUM(l.amount), 0);","note":"The ledger stays the truth and the balance is a cache of it; the reconciliation query is the safety net that proves they still agree."},{"title":"Materialized view for a reporting read path","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW merchant_daily_sales AS\nSELECT merchant_id,\n       date_trunc('day', created_at) AS sales_day,\n       count(*)                      AS order_count,\n       sum(total_amount)             AS revenue\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'PAID'\nGROUP BY merchant_id, date_trunc('day', created_at);\n\n-- A unique index is required for a concurrent refresh\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ON merchant_daily_sales (merchant_id, sales_day);\n\n-- Scheduled every few minutes; readers are not blocked during the refresh\nREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY merchant_daily_sales;","note":"Staleness is bounded by the refresh interval and the view can always be rebuilt from `orders`, which makes it a low-risk form of denormalization for dashboards."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When would a materialized view be enough instead of a hand-maintained summary table?","a":"When the summary can be slightly stale and recomputing it is affordable, for example a dashboard that refreshes every few minutes. PostgreSQL's `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY` rebuilds it without blocking readers, but it needs a unique index on the view and recomputes the whole query each time. If you need near real-time numbers or the full query is too expensive, maintain a summary table incrementally from events or triggers instead."},{"q":"How would you detect that a denormalized copy has drifted from its source?","a":"Run a scheduled reconciliation query that compares the copy with values computed from the source, such as counts or sums per customer, and alert on any difference. Track the lag of the pipeline that updates it, for example consumer lag on the event topic. When drift is found, rebuild the affected rows from the source rather than patching them by hand, so the source remains the only authority."}],"extra":["**Generated columns:** for values derived from the same row, `GENERATED ALWAYS AS (...) STORED` columns let PostgreSQL maintain the copy, with no application sync code to get wrong.","**Triggers versus application code:** triggers keep a copy consistent no matter who writes (other services, manual SQL), but they are hidden logic that is harder to test and reason about; application updates are explicit but must share the transaction.","**Hot-row contention:** a denormalized counter such as `merchants.order_count` updated by every insert serializes all writers on one row lock; aggregate periodically or spread the counter over several rows.","**jsonb is not an escape hatch:** a `jsonb` column with a GIN index suits genuinely variable attributes, but moving relational facts into JSON loses constraints and type checks.","**Erasure cost:** every copy of personal data multiplies GDPR erasure and retention work, so personal fields are a strong argument for keeping a single source."],"redFlags":["denormalizes on intuition before running `EXPLAIN` on the slow query","updates copies from application code in several places with no reconciliation job","assumes a read model matches its source the instant data changes"]},{"id":118,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"Walk through the classic concurrency anomalies, from reading uncommitted data to overwriting a concurrent write. How does each one show up?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Dirty reads see uncommitted data, non-repeatable reads see a row change between two reads, phantoms see the matching set change, and lost updates silently overwrite a concurrent write; isolation levels and locking prevent different subsets.","answer":["Concrete scenarios: a **dirty read** sees another transaction's uncommitted debit that later rolls back. A **non-repeatable read** reads a balance of 100, another transaction commits 50, and a second read in the same transaction returns 50. A **phantom** counts a customer's open orders as 3 for a credit check while a concurrent insert makes it 4. A **lost update** has two transactions both read 100, each subtract its own amount in application code and write back, and the second commit erases the first.","Mapping to PostgreSQL: MVCC never allows dirty reads, so Read Uncommitted behaves as Read Committed. At the default Read Committed level, each statement works from a new snapshot, which means non-repeatable reads, phantom rows and lost updates can all occur. Repeatable Read is snapshot isolation: no non-repeatable reads or phantoms, and a concurrent update of the same row fails with SQLSTATE `40001`, but write skew across different rows remains. Serializable (SSI) also detects write skew. Other engines differ; MySQL InnoDB Repeatable Read, for example, does not detect lost updates on plain reads.","Lost updates are the one teams hit most, and they need more than a level name: an atomic conditional `UPDATE`, a pessimistic `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, optimistic versioning with a `version` column, or a higher isolation level with a retry loop. I pick atomic SQL when the change fits one statement, optimistic locking for low contention or long user think time, and row locks for hot rows with short transactions."],"keyPoints":["each anomaly describes what one transaction can observe or overwrite while another is in flight","stronger isolation levels rule out more anomalies at the cost of retries or blocking","even at the weakest requested level, PostgreSQL never exposes uncommitted data","read-modify-write cycles in application code are the usual source of lost updates"],"examples":[{"title":"Lost update on a balance, and two fixes","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: read-modify-write at Read Committed; two concurrent debits both read 100\n@Transactional\npublic void debit(long accountId, BigDecimal amount) {\n    Account acc = accountRepository.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();\n    acc.setBalance(acc.getBalance().subtract(amount)); // last commit wins, one debit vanishes\n}\n\n// GOOD (1): atomic conditional update; the row lock serializes writers\npublic interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, Long> {\n    @Modifying\n    @Query(\"\"\"\n        update Account a set a.balance = a.balance + :delta\n        where a.id = :id and a.balance + :delta >= 0\n        \"\"\")\n    int applyDelta(@Param(\"id\") long id, @Param(\"delta\") BigDecimal delta); // 0 rows: insufficient funds\n}\n\n// GOOD (2): optimistic locking; Hibernate adds \"where version = ?\" to the UPDATE\n@Entity\npublic class Account {\n    @Id private Long id;\n    private BigDecimal balance;\n    @Version private long version; // conflict throws OptimisticLockException, retry or return 409\n}","note":"Option 1 needs no read at all and is the simplest correct fix. Option 2 keeps the read-modify-write style but turns a silent overwrite into a detectable conflict."},{"title":"Anomalies by isolation level in PostgreSQL","lang":"text","code":"Anomaly                  Read Committed   Repeatable Read (PG)       Serializable (PG)\nDirty read               prevented        prevented                  prevented\nNon-repeatable read      possible         prevented                  prevented\nPhantom read             possible         prevented (snapshot)       prevented\nLost update (R-M-W)      possible         detected, SQLSTATE 40001   detected, SQLSTATE 40001\nWrite skew               possible         possible                   detected, SQLSTATE 40001","note":"The SQL standard allows phantoms at Repeatable Read, but PostgreSQL's snapshot prevents them. Every 40001 means the application must retry the whole transaction."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is write skew, and why doesn't snapshot isolation prevent it?","a":"Two transactions read an overlapping set of rows, each checks an invariant that holds in its snapshot, and each updates a different row, so neither write conflicts with the other yet together they break the rule; the classic case is two on-call doctors both going off duty. Snapshot isolation only detects conflicts when two transactions write the same row, so PostgreSQL's Repeatable Read allows it. Serializable detects the dangerous read and write pattern and aborts one transaction, or you can lock the rows you read with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`."},{"q":"How does PostgreSQL's Repeatable Read differ from the SQL standard's description?","a":"The standard only requires that rows already read do not change, and it permits phantoms at that level. PostgreSQL implements it as snapshot isolation, so the whole transaction sees one snapshot and phantoms do not appear either. In exchange, updating a row that a concurrent transaction changed and committed fails with a serialization error, which the application has to retry."}],"extra":["**Write skew:** two on-call doctors each check that someone else is on call and both go off call; they update different rows, so no row conflict occurs. Only Serializable, or explicitly locking the checked rows with `FOR UPDATE`, prevents it.","**Retries are mandatory above Read Committed:** at Repeatable Read or Serializable, catch SQLSTATE `40001` (and `40P01` for deadlocks) on the root `SQLException` and rerun the entire transaction with bounded attempts; Spring surfaces these as `ConcurrencyFailureException` subtypes.","**Serializable read-only optimization:** declaring a transaction `SERIALIZABLE READ ONLY DEFERRABLE` lets PostgreSQL wait for a safe snapshot, after which the read can never fail with a serialization error, which suits long consistent reports.","**JPA versioning scope:** `@Version` only protects writes that go through the entity; bulk JPQL or native `UPDATE` statements bypass it unless you increment the version column yourself."],"redFlags":["assumes every database implements a given isolation level the same way","treats Serializable as merely slower, ignoring that the application must retry serialization failures","mixes up a phantom with a non-repeatable read"]},{"id":119,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"A query filters by `tenant_id` and a `created_at` range, then sorts by `created_at`. How would you order the columns in its index?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A B-tree on (a, b, c) is sorted by a, then b, then c, so it only seeks on a leftmost prefix; put equality columns first, then the range or sort column, based on real queries.","answer":["A composite B-tree is sorted lexicographically, like a phone book ordered by last name and then first name. It can seek efficiently for `a = ?`, `a = ? AND b = ?`, and `a = ? AND b = ? AND c > ?`, because each prefix narrows the scan to one contiguous range. `WHERE b = ?` alone cannot seek, because matching `b` values are scattered across every `a` group.","Equality columns usually go before range and sort columns. Once the scan hits a range condition (`created_at >= ?`), the columns after it no longer narrow the range; they are only checked row by row inside it. So for `merchant_id = ? AND status = ? AND created_at >= ? ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50`, the right index is `(merchant_id, status, created_at DESC)`: the two equalities pin a single range, entries come out already sorted, and the scan stops after 50 rows.","\"Most selective column first\" is a myth when all predicates are equalities; the seek is equally tight in any order. What matters more is reuse: pick the order whose prefixes serve the most real queries, since `(merchant_id, status, created_at)` also covers `merchant_id = ?` and makes a separate index on `merchant_id` redundant.","So I design from the workload: take the top queries from `pg_stat_statements`, group them by access pattern, and verify with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`. A wrong order shows up as an extra `Sort` node or far more buffers read than rows returned."],"keyPoints":["the index is sorted by its first column, then by the next within each value, and so on","lookups can seek efficiently only on a leading run of columns","columns compared with `=` go first, with the range or `ORDER BY` column after them","the right order comes from the queries you actually run, and one index may not serve them all"],"examples":[{"title":"Equality first, then range and sort","lang":"sql","code":"-- Query: recent pending orders of one merchant, newest first\nSELECT id, total_amount, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE merchant_id = $1\n  AND status = 'PENDING'\n  AND created_at >= $2\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n\n-- BAD: range column first; merchant_id and status are checked across the whole date range\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_created_merchant_status\n    ON orders (created_at, merchant_id, status);\n\n-- GOOD: equalities pin one contiguous range that is already in ORDER BY order\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_merchant_status_created\n    ON orders (merchant_id, status, created_at DESC);\n\n-- The GOOD index also serves, through its leftmost prefix:\n--   WHERE merchant_id = $1\n--   WHERE merchant_id = $1 AND status = $2\n-- It cannot seek for:\n--   WHERE status = 'PENDING'      (leading column missing)\n--   WHERE created_at >= $2        (leading columns missing)","note":"With the BAD index, `EXPLAIN` may still list all three predicates under `Index Cond`, yet the scan walks every entry since `$2` for all merchants. Compare `Buffers` between the two plans, not just the index name."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can an index on `(tenant_id, created_at)` also serve `ORDER BY created_at DESC`?","a":"Yes, when the query also fixes `tenant_id` with an equality, because within one tenant the entries are already sorted by `created_at`, and a B-tree can be scanned backward for `DESC`. PostgreSQL can then return rows in order and stop early at the `LIMIT`, with no sort step. Without the tenant filter the rows are not globally ordered by `created_at`, so the planner needs a sort or a different index."},{"q":"How do you check that a composite index is used the way you intended?","a":"Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the real query and check that the index scan touches roughly as many rows and buffers as it returns; a large gap, a `Filter` line with many rows removed, or an extra `Sort` node means the column order does not fit the query. Test with realistic parameter values, since a small tenant and a huge one can get different plans."}],"extra":["**Skip scan:** PostgreSQL 18 added B-tree skip scan, which can use `(a, b)` for `WHERE b = ?` when `a` has few distinct values by probing each `a` group; it is a fallback, not a reason to ignore column order.","**Sort direction matters:** `ORDER BY a ASC, b DESC` needs an index declared `(a ASC, b DESC)` or its exact reverse; a plain `(a, b)` forces an explicit sort for mixed directions.","**Index Cond is not proof of efficiency:** conditions on later columns can appear as `Index Cond` even when the scan range is set only by the leading range column, so judge by rows and buffers touched.","**Redundant indexes:** `(a)` is redundant next to `(a, b)` unless it enforces uniqueness or is much smaller for a very hot query; each redundant index is pure write amplification.","**Same rule elsewhere:** MongoDB documents it as ESR (Equality, Sort, Range) for compound indexes, and MySQL InnoDB follows the same leftmost-prefix behavior, so the reasoning transfers across engines."],"redFlags":["puts the most selective column first as a fixed rule, regardless of how queries filter","creates separate single-column indexes and expects them to act like one composite index","places a range column ahead of an equality column and cannot explain the extra rows read"]},{"id":120,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"A query that took 20 ms yesterday takes 4 seconds today. What would you look for in its `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` output?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`, read the tree from the inside out, and compare estimated with actual rows at every node; large mismatches reveal the misestimates that make the planner choose bad scans, joins and sorts.","answer":["`EXPLAIN` shows the planner's chosen tree with estimates: `cost=startup..total`, `rows` and `width`. `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` executes the statement and adds `actual time`, actual `rows` and `loops`, and `BUFFERS` adds shared hits versus reads, which separates cache work from disk I/O. Child nodes feed their parents, so read it inside out, and remember that actual time and rows are per loop: multiply by `loops`.","At each node I check the scan type (Seq, Index, Index Only with `Heap Fetches`, Bitmap with `Recheck`), the join algorithm (Nested Loop for small outer inputs with an indexed inner side, Hash Join for large unsorted sets, Merge Join for sorted inputs), sorts that spill (`Sort Method: external merge`), high `Rows Removed by Filter`, and where the time and buffers really go.","The most important signal is estimated versus actual rows. The planner costs every alternative from cardinality estimates, so if it expects 12 rows and gets 480,000 it picks a nested loop that runs an index lookup 480,000 times. Typical causes are stale statistics after a bulk load, correlated columns the planner treats as independent, skew, expressions without statistics, and generic plans for prepared statements. Fixes are `ANALYZE`, a higher per-column statistics target, `CREATE STATISTICS` for correlated columns, expression indexes, or rewriting the query.","Plans depend on data volume and cache state, so capture real ones from production with `auto_explain` instead of trusting a laptop. Seeing an index in the plan proves nothing on its own."],"keyPoints":["the plan is a tree whose nodes feed their parents, so reading starts from the innermost nodes","a big gap between the planner's row guess and the real row count usually explains a bad choice","per-loop timings multiply by the loop count, which makes nested loops over misestimated inputs expensive","adding `BUFFERS` shows how many pages were found in shared buffers and how many had to be read"],"examples":[{"title":"A cardinality misestimate causing a slow nested loop","lang":"text","code":"EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT o.id, c.email\nFROM orders o JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.status = 'PENDING' AND o.region = 'EU';\n\nNested Loop  (cost=0.87..152.40 rows=12 width=48) (actual time=0.041..4210.337 rows=480112 loops=1)\n  Buffers: shared hit=1920455 read=38211\n  ->  Index Scan using idx_orders_status on orders o  (cost=0.43..48.10 rows=12 width=16) (actual time=0.020..610.114 rows=480112 loops=1)\n        Index Cond: (status = 'PENDING'::text)\n        Filter: (region = 'EU'::text)\n        Rows Removed by Filter: 91230\n  ->  Index Scan using customers_pkey on customers c  (cost=0.43..8.45 rows=1 width=40) (actual time=0.006..0.006 rows=1 loops=480112)\n        Index Cond: (id = o.customer_id)\nPlanning Time: 0.412 ms\nExecution Time: 4298.905 ms","note":"Estimated 12 rows, actual 480,112: the inner index scan runs 480,112 times (`loops`). With correct estimates the planner would hash join the two tables instead."},{"title":"Fixing the estimate and explaining writes safely","lang":"sql","code":"-- Refresh statistics after a bulk load\nANALYZE orders;\n\n-- Teach the planner that status and region are correlated (PostgreSQL 10+, mcv since 12)\nCREATE STATISTICS orders_status_region (dependencies, mcv)\n    ON status, region FROM orders;\nANALYZE orders;\n\n-- Finer histogram for a skewed column\nALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN status SET STATISTICS 1000;\nANALYZE orders;\n\n-- EXPLAIN ANALYZE really executes the statement: wrap writes in a rolled back transaction\nBEGIN;\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nUPDATE orders SET status = 'EXPIRED'\nWHERE status = 'PENDING' AND created_at < '2026-01-01';\nROLLBACK;","note":"Extended statistics fix estimates for predicates on correlated columns without changing the query. The `BEGIN ... ROLLBACK` wrapper is essential because `ANALYZE` runs the `UPDATE` for real."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Estimated rows say 10, actual rows say 200,000. What are the usual causes and fixes?","a":"Stale or too coarse statistics are the first suspect: run `ANALYZE`, or raise the column's statistics target with `ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN ... SET STATISTICS`. Correlated columns are another cause, because the planner assumes predicates are independent; `CREATE STATISTICS` with dependencies or MCV lists teaches it the relationship. Functions or expressions on columns also hide statistics, which an expression index fixes because it gets statistics of its own."},{"q":"How do you capture the plan of a slow query that only misbehaves in production?","a":"Enable the `auto_explain` module with `auto_explain.log_min_duration` set to a threshold, and optionally `log_analyze`, so PostgreSQL logs the plan of every slow execution with the real parameters. `pg_stat_statements` tells you which normalized queries consume the most time, so you know where to look. Because `log_analyze` adds timing overhead to every query, sample with `auto_explain.sample_rate` or turn off `log_timing` on busy systems."}],"extra":["**auto_explain in production:** set `auto_explain.log_min_duration` to log plans of slow statements, and use `auto_explain.log_analyze` with `auto_explain.sample_rate` because per-node timing adds overhead.","**Generic plans:** pgJDBC switches to a server-side prepared statement after `prepareThreshold` executions (default 5), and PostgreSQL may then pick a generic plan that ignores the actual parameter value; `plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan` helps queries with heavily skewed parameters.","**Spilling sorts and hashes:** `Sort Method: external merge  Disk: ...` or a hash with multiple batches means `work_mem` was too small; raise it per session or query, since it applies per operation per backend and a global increase can exhaust memory.","**Parallel and JIT noise:** compare `Workers Planned` with `Workers Launched`, and check `JIT` timing at the bottom; on short OLTP queries JIT compilation can cost more than the query, which is why many teams run with `jit = off`.","**Startup cost matters for LIMIT:** the first number in `cost=startup..total` is the cost before the first row; plans for `LIMIT` queries favor low startup cost, which is why an ordered index scan can beat a cheaper-looking sort."],"redFlags":["runs `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on an `UPDATE` or `DELETE` in production without wrapping it in a transaction that is rolled back","reads per-loop timings as totals and misses where the time really goes","tests the plan with parameter values unlike production ones and trusts the result"]},{"id":121,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"Clustered vs secondary indexes, and what is a covering index?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"InnoDB stores rows inside the primary key B-tree, so secondary indexes carry the primary key and a lookup through them descends twice; PostgreSQL keeps rows in an unordered heap that every index points into. A covering index holds every column a query reads, so the engine skips the table, which in PostgreSQL also requires pages marked all-visible.","answer":["In InnoDB the table is the clustered index: a B+tree keyed by the primary key whose leaf pages hold the full rows, so a primary key lookup is one descent and a key range reads adjacent pages. Secondary index entries store the indexed columns plus the primary key, not a row address, so a query through `idx_customer_created` collects primary keys and then descends the clustered index once per row. The primary key is copied into every secondary index, so a wide key inflates all of them, and changing a primary key value moves the row.","PostgreSQL stores rows in a heap, wherever there is free space, and every index, the primary key included, is a separate B-tree pointing to a tuple id (`ctid`). Any index lookup is a descent plus a heap page read, whatever the primary key looks like. Because MVCC writes a new row version on every `UPDATE`, each version normally needs new entries in all indexes, unless it is a HOT update (no indexed column changed and the new version fits on the same page). InnoDB favors primary key access and pays with double lookups; PostgreSQL treats all indexes alike and pays with heap visits and write amplification on updates.","A covering index contains every column the query references. In InnoDB the primary key is already in each secondary entry, so `(customer_id, created_at)` covers `SELECT id, created_at ... WHERE customer_id = ?`; MySQL has no `INCLUDE`, so extra columns must join the key. PostgreSQL 11 added `INCLUDE` for non-key leaf columns, enabling index-only scans, but index entries carry no visibility data: the heap is skipped only for pages `VACUUM` has marked all-visible, and `Heap Fetches` in `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` shows how often that failed. Covering indexes cost size and write work, so reserve them for a few hot queries."],"keyPoints":["InnoDB rows live in the primary key B-tree; secondary entries carry the primary key","PostgreSQL indexes point into a heap; index-only scans depend on the visibility map","covering indexes trade size and write cost for skipping row lookups"],"examples":[{"title":"InnoDB: double lookup vs a query covered by the implicit primary key","lang":"sql","code":"-- MySQL InnoDB: the table is the primary key B+tree\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n    id           BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n    customer_id  BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL,\n    created_at   DATETIME(6)     NOT NULL,\n    status       VARCHAR(20)     NOT NULL,\n    total_amount DECIMAL(12,2)   NOT NULL,\n    PRIMARY KEY (id),\n    KEY idx_customer_created (customer_id, created_at)  -- entry: (customer_id, created_at, id)\n) ENGINE = InnoDB;\n\n-- Not covered: 20 ids from the secondary index, then 20 clustered index descents\nEXPLAIN SELECT status, total_amount\nFROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42\nORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;\n\n-- Covered for free: id rides along in every secondary entry (Extra: Using index)\nEXPLAIN SELECT id, created_at\nFROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42\nORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;\n\n-- No INCLUDE in MySQL: to cover the first query, payload columns join the key\nALTER TABLE orders\n    ADD KEY idx_customer_created_cover (customer_id, created_at, status, total_amount);","note":"The second query needs no row lookups because InnoDB secondary entries always carry the primary key. The same property is why a `CHAR(36)` primary key makes every secondary index on the table larger."},{"title":"PostgreSQL: INCLUDE columns, index-only scans and heap fetches","lang":"sql","code":"-- Key columns serve WHERE and ORDER BY; INCLUDE columns sit only in leaf pages\nCREATE INDEX orders_customer_created_idx\n    ON orders (customer_id, created_at DESC)\n    INCLUDE (status, total_amount);\n\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT created_at, status, total_amount\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = 42\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nLIMIT 20;\n-- Index Only Scan using orders_customer_created_idx on orders\n--   Index Cond: (customer_id = 42)\n--   Heap Fetches: 20    (pages not all-visible, so the heap was read anyway)\n\nVACUUM (ANALYZE) orders;   -- sets visibility map bits for unchanged pages\n-- Same query again: Heap Fetches: 0\n\n-- status is now indexed, so status updates can no longer be HOT updates\nSELECT n_tup_upd, n_tup_hot_upd\nFROM pg_stat_user_tables\nWHERE relname = 'orders';","note":"An index-only scan is a plan choice, not a guarantee: `Heap Fetches` shows how often visibility forced a heap read. Putting `status` in `INCLUDE` also means every status change writes a new entry to every index."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is a HOT update, and how should it influence which columns you index?","a":"A heap-only tuple update happens when an `UPDATE` changes no indexed column (key or `INCLUDE`) and the new version fits on the same heap page: PostgreSQL chains it from the old version and writes no index entries. Indexing a frequently changing column such as `status` or `updated_at` disables HOT, so every update writes to every index and creates more WAL and vacuum work. A lower `fillfactor` leaves room for same-page updates; compare `n_tup_hot_upd` with `n_tup_upd` to see how often it works."},{"q":"Can PostgreSQL keep a table physically ordered like InnoDB?","a":"Not continuously. `CLUSTER orders USING orders_customer_created_idx` rewrites the table once in index order under an `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock (`pg_repack` does it online), but later writes go wherever there is free space and the order decays. Append-mostly tables stay correlated with insertion time, which is why a small BRIN index works well on `created_at` in event tables."}],"extra":["**No primary key in InnoDB:** InnoDB then clusters on the first `UNIQUE` index whose columns are all `NOT NULL`, or on a hidden 6-byte row id; declare a compact primary key so you control row order and secondary index size.","**Key width:** an InnoDB `CHAR(36)` UUID key is copied into every secondary index, while a PostgreSQL index entry points with a fixed 6-byte `ctid` whatever the primary key type.","**Unique with INCLUDE:** in `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ON users (email) INCLUDE (status)` only `email` is checked for uniqueness, and the `INCLUDE` columns are not search keys and cannot provide ordering.","**Insert-only tables:** since PostgreSQL 13 autovacuum also triggers on inserts, so append-only tables get their visibility map set; heavily updated tables may need tighter autovacuum settings before index-only scans pay off.","**Other engines:** SQL Server makes the primary key clustered by default but lets you cluster on another index and supports `INCLUDE`; Oracle uses heap tables by default with index-organized tables as the clustered option."],"redFlags":["Assumes every database stores rows in primary key order","believes a PostgreSQL index-only scan never touches the table","adds every selected column to an index without weighing write cost"]},{"id":122,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"Your logs show occasional `deadlock detected` errors during transfers between accounts. What is going on, and how should the code react?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A deadlock is a lock-wait cycle between transactions; the database aborts one victim, so prevent cycles with consistent lock order and short transactions, and retry the victim idempotently with a bound.","answer":["Transaction 1 locks account A and then needs B, while transaction 2 locks B and then needs A; neither can proceed. PostgreSQL runs deadlock detection after a lock wait exceeds `deadlock_timeout` (1 second by default), aborts one transaction with SQLSTATE `40P01`, and lets the other continue. Typical sources are transfers in opposite directions, batch updates touching the same rows in different orders, and `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` without a deterministic order.","Prevention matters more than handling. Acquire locks in one global order (sort the ids, lock the lower id first, or lock all rows up front with `ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE`), keep transactions short with no remote calls inside, touch as few rows as possible, and set `lock_timeout` so waits are bounded.","A deadlock is not corruption: the victim is rolled back completely and the database stays consistent. The application should retry the whole transaction, not the failed statement, with a small attempt limit, exponential backoff and jitter, and only if the unit of work is idempotent. The retry must sit outside the `@Transactional` boundary so each attempt gets a fresh transaction and re-reads state.","Operationally, track `pg_stat_database.deadlocks` and enable `log_lock_waits`. A steady deadlock rate is a design bug in lock ordering, not something to hide behind retries."],"keyPoints":["two transactions each hold a lock the other needs, so neither can progress","the database spots the cycle and aborts one participant so the other can finish","acquiring rows in a fixed order, such as ascending id, removes the cycle","the aborted unit of work can be retried a bounded number of times as a whole"],"examples":[{"title":"The cycle, and the ordered-locking fix","lang":"sql","code":"-- Session 1: transfer from account 1 to account 2\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;   -- holds row 1\n-- Session 2: transfer from account 2 to account 1\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 50 WHERE id = 2;    -- holds row 2\n-- Session 1\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;   -- waits for session 2\n-- Session 2\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 50 WHERE id = 1;    -- cycle\n-- ERROR: deadlock detected (SQLSTATE 40P01); session 2 is rolled back\n\n-- GOOD: lock every involved row in a deterministic order before changing anything\nBEGIN;\nSELECT id, balance\nFROM accounts\nWHERE id IN (1, 2)\nORDER BY id\nFOR UPDATE;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;","note":"PostgreSQL applies `ORDER BY` before the locking clause, so rows are locked in id order and two opposite transfers queue instead of forming a cycle."},{"title":"Bounded retry outside the transaction, idempotent unit of work","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class TransferFacade {\n    private final TransferService transferService;\n\n    public TransferFacade(TransferService transferService) {\n        this.transferService = transferService;\n    }\n\n    // Each attempt calls a @Transactional method, so it runs in a brand new transaction.\n    // CannotAcquireLockException (deadlock loser, lock timeout) extends PessimisticLockingFailureException.\n    @Retryable(retryFor = PessimisticLockingFailureException.class,\n               maxAttempts = 3,\n               backoff = @Backoff(delay = 50, multiplier = 2, random = true))\n    public TransferResult transfer(TransferCommand cmd) {\n        return transferService.execute(cmd);\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass TransferService {\n    // execute() is @Transactional: it inserts into transfers (transfer_id is the primary key),\n    // then locks both accounts ordered by id and applies the debit and credit.\n    // A replayed transfer_id returns the stored result instead of moving money twice.\n}","note":"Spring Retry (`@EnableRetry`, now archived; on Spring Framework 7 use the built-in `@Retryable` enabled by `@EnableResilientMethods`) retries only transient lock failures, three times with jittered exponential backoff. Putting `@Retryable` and `@Transactional` on different beans guarantees a fresh transaction per attempt."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you find which statements are deadlocking?","a":"PostgreSQL writes a detailed message to its server log for each deadlock, listing the processes involved, the locks they waited for and, with default settings, the statements they were running. Correlate those with application traces to find the code paths, and query `pg_locks` joined with `pg_stat_activity` while a lock wait is live. The usual finding is two code paths that touch the same rows or tables in a different order."},{"q":"Why does sorting the account ids before locking prevent the transfer deadlock?","a":"A deadlock needs a cycle, where the first transaction waits for the second while the second waits for the first. If every transfer locks the lower id first, two transfers between the same accounts both start with the same row, so the second one simply waits for the first to finish instead of grabbing the other row. Locking both rows with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` and `ORDER BY id`, or updating them in id order, enforces that."}],"extra":["**deadlock_timeout is not a transaction timeout:** it is how long a lock wait lasts before PostgreSQL checks for a cycle, so every deadlock costs at least that much latency; with `log_lock_waits = on` any wait longer than it is logged with the blocking process.","**Bound waits explicitly:** `lock_timeout` fails a blocked statement with SQLSTATE `55P03` and `statement_timeout` caps total runtime, so a blocked request frees its pooled connection instead of pinning it.","**Batch updates:** `UPDATE ... WHERE id = ANY(?)` locks rows in whatever order the plan visits them; sort ids in the application and lock through an ordered `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, or process fixed ordered chunks.","**Server log has the story:** PostgreSQL logs both process ids, the lock types and the statements involved in the deadlock, which is usually enough to identify the conflicting code paths.","**Hot rows are a different problem:** a single contended counter row causes lock queueing rather than deadlocks; the fix there is redesign (append-only ledger rows, sharded counters), not ordering."],"redFlags":["catches the error and retries only the last statement inside the already aborted transaction","raises the lock timeout, hoping deadlocks will resolve by waiting longer","never reads the database log, which names the statements involved"]},{"id":123,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"A client retries `POST /payments` after a timeout and two payments appear. How do you stop that at the database level?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Attach an idempotency key or natural business key to every create request, enforce it with a unique index, and write the row in one atomic `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT` statement, so simultaneous duplicates end up as a single row rather than racing a lookup followed by an insert.","answer":["Retries are normal: client timeouts, at-least-once message delivery, double clicks. The naive `if (!exists) insert` races, because two requests can both see no row and both insert. Only the database can arbitrate that cheaply: a unique constraint on the key makes a second row impossible no matter how many application instances run.","The key is either a natural business key (`merchant_id, merchant_order_ref`, a provider's payment id, a message id) or a client-supplied `Idempotency-Key`, scoped per client or tenant. Insert atomically with `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (...) DO NOTHING RETURNING id`: a returned row means you created it, no row means it already exists, so load it and return the original result. Catching the unique violation (SQLSTATE `23505`, `DataIntegrityViolationException` in Spring) also works, but in PostgreSQL an error aborts the surrounding transaction, so you need a savepoint or a fresh transaction.","For APIs, store the key together with a hash of the request and the response you returned, written in the same local transaction as the business row so both commit or neither does. Replays then return the identical response, a concurrent in-flight duplicate can get `409`, and old keys are purged after the documented retry window.","Side effects (events, emails) belong in an outbox table written in that same transaction, so they happen exactly once per created row."],"keyPoints":["the client sends the same key on every retry, so the server can recognize a repeat","a unique index makes the database itself reject the second row, even under concurrency","an `ON CONFLICT` clause turns the duplicate into a no-op or a fetch of the original, in one statement","a lookup before insert leaves a window where two requests both see nothing"],"examples":[{"title":"Unique key plus one atomic insert","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE payments (\n    id               uuid PRIMARY KEY,\n    merchant_id      bigint         NOT NULL,\n    idempotency_key  text           NOT NULL,\n    request_hash     text           NOT NULL,\n    amount           numeric(19, 4) NOT NULL,\n    currency         char(3)        NOT NULL,\n    status           text           NOT NULL,\n    created_at       timestamptz    NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    CONSTRAINT uq_payments_idempotency UNIQUE (merchant_id, idempotency_key)\n);\n\n-- BAD: check-then-insert; two concurrent requests both see no row and both insert\nSELECT id FROM payments WHERE merchant_id = $1 AND idempotency_key = $2;\n\n-- GOOD: one statement; the unique index arbitrates the race\nINSERT INTO payments (id, merchant_id, idempotency_key, request_hash, amount, currency, status)\nVALUES ($3, $1, $2, $4, $5, $6, 'PENDING')\nON CONFLICT (merchant_id, idempotency_key) DO NOTHING\nRETURNING id;\n-- No row returned: duplicate. Load the existing row and compare request_hash.","note":"If the first insert has not committed yet, the second one waits on the unique index and then does nothing (or inserts, if the first rolled back), so concurrency is resolved inside the database."},{"title":"Service method: create, replay or reject","lang":"java","code":"@Transactional\npublic PaymentResult create(long merchantId, String idempotencyKey, CreatePayment cmd) {\n    String hash = RequestHashes.sha256(cmd);              // hash of the canonical request body\n    UUID newId = UUID.randomUUID();\n    Optional<UUID> inserted = jdbc.sql(\"\"\"\n            INSERT INTO payments (id, merchant_id, idempotency_key, request_hash, amount, currency, status)\n            VALUES (:id, :merchantId, :key, :hash, :amount, :currency, 'PENDING')\n            ON CONFLICT (merchant_id, idempotency_key) DO NOTHING\n            RETURNING id\n            \"\"\")\n        .param(\"id\", newId).param(\"merchantId\", merchantId).param(\"key\", idempotencyKey)\n        .param(\"hash\", hash).param(\"amount\", cmd.amount()).param(\"currency\", cmd.currency())\n        .query((rs, rowNum) -> rs.getObject(\"id\", UUID.class))\n        .optional();\n\n    if (inserted.isPresent()) {\n        outbox.append(new PaymentCreated(newId, merchantId, cmd.amount())); // same transaction\n        return PaymentResult.created(newId);\n    }\n    Payment existing = payments.findByMerchantIdAndIdempotencyKey(merchantId, idempotencyKey);\n    if (!existing.requestHash().equals(hash)) {\n        throw new IdempotencyKeyReusedException(idempotencyKey);           // mapped to 422\n    }\n    return PaymentResult.replayed(existing.id());\n}","note":"`JdbcClient` (Spring 6.1+) keeps the atomic upsert explicit. The three outcomes are created, identical replay, and key reuse with a different payload, which is rejected."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you return the original response when a duplicate request arrives?","a":"Store the outcome with the key, either the created resource id from which you rebuild the response or the serialized response itself, in the same transaction that creates the row. On a conflict, look up that record and return it with the original status code, so the client cannot tell a replay from the first success. Note that `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING RETURNING id` returns no row for a duplicate, so follow it with a select by key."},{"q":"Two identical requests arrive within milliseconds. What does each one see?","a":"Both try to insert the same key; PostgreSQL lets the first insert proceed and makes the second wait on the unique index until the first transaction commits or rolls back. If the first commits, the second hits the conflict and takes its duplicate path; if it rolls back, the second insert succeeds. When the first request is still busy with slow work, the second should get `409 Conflict` or a retry hint rather than wait for the whole duration."}],"extra":["**JPA save() with assigned ids:** `repository.save()` on an entity with a pre-set id calls `merge`, which does a `SELECT` then an `INSERT`, a hidden check-then-insert; implement `Persistable#isNew` or use an explicit insert so the constraint handles duplicates cleanly.","**Repeatable Read caveat:** at Repeatable Read, `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` against a row committed after your snapshot raises a serialization failure, and a follow-up `SELECT` would not see that row anyway; the create-or-load pattern is simplest at Read Committed.","**Key retention:** keep keys at least as long as clients may retry (payment APIs commonly honor keys for about 24 hours) and purge them with a cleanup job or daily partitions that you drop.","**Consumers too:** for Kafka or queue consumers, the same pattern is an inbox table with primary key `(consumer_name, message_id)` inserted in the transaction that applies the effect.","**Upsert is not idempotent create:** `ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` implements create-or-update; a retried request can overwrite newer state, so use it only when last-write-wins is the intended semantics."],"redFlags":["generates the idempotency key on the server, so a client retry arrives with a new one","catches the unique violation but returns an error instead of the original result","keeps idempotency keys forever with no expiry or retention plan"]},{"id":124,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"A nightly job keeps one PostgreSQL transaction open for three hours. What damage can that do to the rest of the system?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A long transaction holds row locks, an old MVCC snapshot and a pooled connection for its entire duration, so it blocks writers and DDL, stops vacuum from removing dead rows, raises deadlock risk and starves the pool.","answer":["**Locks:** row locks from `UPDATE` or `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` are held until commit, so other writers queue behind them. Even a plain `SELECT` holds a table-level lock, so a migration's `ALTER TABLE` waits for the long transaction, and every later query on that table then queues behind the waiting DDL: a small migration becomes an outage.","**Snapshots:** PostgreSQL keeps old row versions as long as any snapshot might need them. A transaction open for an hour holds back the xmin horizon, so vacuum cannot remove dead tuples in any table of the database; tables and indexes bloat and queries slow down. Sessions left `idle in transaction` by application bugs are the classic cause.","**Connections:** the transaction pins a pooled connection. A `@Transactional` method that calls a payment API for two seconds holds a HikariCP connection that whole time, so a pool of 10 supports five such requests per second before requests start waiting and timing out. Longer lock hold times also raise deadlock probability, and a failure late in a long transaction throws away a lot of work.","Keep transactions to local database work: no remote calls, no user think time, large jobs chunked with a commit per chunk. Back that with `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout`, `statement_timeout`, `lock_timeout` on migrations, and alerts on transaction age."],"keyPoints":["row locks taken early stay held, so other writers and schema changes queue behind them","an old snapshot holds back the xmin horizon, so vacuum cannot remove dead rows and tables bloat","the connection stays checked out of the pool for the full duration","the bigger the transaction, the more work a single failure throws away"],"examples":[{"title":"Remote call inside versus outside the transaction","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: the connection, row locks and snapshot are held during a slow HTTP call\n@Transactional\npublic void placeOrder(PlaceOrder cmd) {\n    Order order = orderRepository.save(Order.pending(cmd));\n    inventoryRepository.reserve(cmd.items());                                // row locks taken\n    PaymentResponse res = paymentClient.charge(cmd.paymentToken(), order.total()); // 2 s remote call\n    order.markPaid(res.reference());\n}\n\n// GOOD: two short local transactions around the remote call (tx is a TransactionTemplate)\npublic void placeOrder(PlaceOrder cmd) {\n    Order order = tx.execute(status -> {\n        Order o = orderRepository.save(Order.pending(cmd));\n        inventoryRepository.reserve(cmd.items());\n        return o;\n    });                                                                      // committed, locks released\n    PaymentResponse res = paymentClient.charge(cmd.paymentToken(), order.total());\n    tx.executeWithoutResult(status ->\n            orderRepository.markPaid(order.getId(), res.reference()));       // conditional on PENDING\n}","note":"The GOOD version holds a connection only for milliseconds. It now needs a state machine for failures between the steps, which the follow-up covers."},{"title":"Find long transactions and set guard rails","lang":"sql","code":"-- Oldest open transactions and what they last ran\nSELECT pid, usename, application_name, state,\n       now() - xact_start AS xact_age,\n       left(query, 80)    AS last_query\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY xact_start\nLIMIT 10;\n\n-- Guard rails for the application role\nALTER ROLE order_service SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = '30s';\nALTER ROLE order_service SET statement_timeout = '5s';\n\n-- Migrations: fail fast instead of queueing every query behind the DDL\nSET lock_timeout = '3s';\nALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN sales_channel text;","note":"`state = 'idle in transaction'` with a large `xact_age` is the smoking gun. A migration tool should retry the `lock_timeout` failure a few times rather than wait indefinitely."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you find long-running or idle-in-transaction sessions in PostgreSQL?","a":"Query `pg_stat_activity` for rows with an old `xact_start`, ordered by `xact_start` so the oldest come first, and check `state` for `idle in transaction`, which means a client opened a transaction and stopped sending work. Alert on the age of the oldest transaction and set `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` so abandoned sessions are terminated automatically. Setting `statement_timeout` and `lock_timeout` for migrations keeps a single statement from running or waiting indefinitely."},{"q":"How would you rewrite a large batch update so it does not hold one huge transaction?","a":"Process it in chunks of a few thousand rows, each in its own short transaction, selecting the next chunk by key range or with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` if several workers share the load. Make each chunk idempotent and record progress, so a failure resumes where it stopped instead of starting over. A brief pause between chunks gives vacuum and replication time to keep up."}],"extra":["**Open Session in View:** Spring Boot enables `spring.jpa.open-in-view` by default and logs a warning about it; it keeps the persistence context open for the whole web request, so lazy loading can run queries outside your transactional boundary. Set it to `false` in services.","**Other horizon holders:** abandoned replication slots, forgotten prepared transactions (`pg_prepared_xacts`) and replicas with `hot_standby_feedback = on` running long queries hold back vacuum exactly like a long transaction; check `age(backend_xmin)` and slot `xmin` values.","**Pool-side detection:** HikariCP's `leakDetectionThreshold` logs a stack trace when a connection is held longer than the threshold, which points straight at the offending code path.","**Chunked batch work:** process large updates in keyset-ordered chunks of a few thousand rows with a commit per chunk, so locks and snapshots stay short and a failure resumes from the last chunk."],"redFlags":["thinks a long transaction that only reads is harmless","does not know that a migration waiting for a lock blocks every later query on that table","has no `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` or alert on transaction age"]},{"id":125,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"UUID vs auto-increment primary keys: what are the trade-offs?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Auto-increment `bigint` keys are compact and always insert at the right edge of the index, but only the database can assign them and they reveal order and volume. Random UUIDv4 values can be generated anywhere and are unguessable but scatter inserts across the index; time-ordered UUIDv7 keeps app-side generation with most of the locality, at 16 bytes and with a visible timestamp.","answer":["A sequence, `IDENTITY` or `AUTO_INCREMENT` column hands out increasing `bigint` values: 8 bytes, cheap joins and foreign keys, and every new key is the largest so far, so inserts hit the rightmost B-tree leaf, which stays in memory, and pages fill completely. The cost is that the database is the only authority: the application learns the id after the insert, data from two databases cannot be merged without collisions, and in Hibernate an `IDENTITY` id forces an immediate `INSERT` on `persist` and disables insert batching, while a pooled `SEQUENCE` keeps it. Use `bigint`, never `int`, because changing a key type later rewrites the table and every foreign key.","A random UUIDv4 sends consecutive inserts to unrelated leaf pages. While the index fits in memory that is mostly harmless; once it outgrows the buffer pool, inserts read random pages from disk, pages split and stay partly empty, and PostgreSQL logs more full-page images to WAL after each checkpoint. InnoDB suffers more because the clustered index is the table itself and the 16-byte key is copied into every secondary index. UUIDv7 (RFC 9562) starts with a 48-bit millisecond timestamp, so recent values sort together and inserts return to the right edge. PostgreSQL 18 has `uuidv7()`; elsewhere generate it in the application, and store the native `uuid` type or `BINARY(16)`, never text.","Exposure matters as much as speed. `/orders/1043` reveals business volume and invites enumeration; UUIDs make guessing impractical but are not access control, so every lookup still checks ownership. UUIDv7 reveals creation time, v4 reveals nothing. App-side generation pays off when the id must exist before the row: idempotency keys, outbox events and logs before commit, or aggregates created on several shards. A common compromise is an internal `bigint` key for joins plus a unique `uuid` public id in the API. Decide early: the key type spreads into every referencing table and client."],"keyPoints":["Sequential bigint keys are compact and append at the right edge of the index","random UUIDv4 hurts index locality; time-ordered UUIDv7 restores most of it","sequential ids leak volume, but no id format replaces authorization checks"],"examples":[{"title":"Application-generated UUIDv7 ids with Spring Data JPA","lang":"java","code":"public final class UuidV7 {\n\n    private static final SecureRandom RANDOM = new SecureRandom();\n\n    private UuidV7() {}\n\n    // RFC 9562: 48-bit Unix millis | version 7 | 12 random bits | variant | 62 random bits\n    public static UUID next() {\n        long msb = (System.currentTimeMillis() << 16) | 0x7000L | (RANDOM.nextInt() & 0x0FFFL);\n        long lsb = (RANDOM.nextLong() & 0x3FFF_FFFF_FFFF_FFFFL) | 0x8000_0000_0000_0000L;\n        return new UUID(msb, lsb);\n    }\n}\n\n@Entity\n@Table(name = \"payments\")\npublic class Payment {\n\n    @Id\n    private UUID id;\n\n    @Version\n    private Long version;        // null until persisted, so Spring Data treats the entity as new\n\n    private String accountId;\n    private BigDecimal amount;\n\n    protected Payment() {}       // for JPA\n\n    public Payment(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {\n        this.id = UuidV7.next(); // known before the INSERT: usable in events, logs and responses\n        this.accountId = accountId;\n        this.amount = amount;\n    }\n}","note":"Without the `@Version` field (or `Persistable`), `save()` sees a non-null id, calls `merge` and runs a `SELECT` before every `INSERT`. In production prefer a maintained UUIDv7 library or the database function."},{"title":"Database-generated UUIDv7 and the bigint plus public UUID hybrid","lang":"sql","code":"-- PostgreSQL 18: time-ordered UUID generated by the database\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n    id          uuid        PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuidv7(),\n    customer_id bigint      NOT NULL,\n    created_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\n-- Hybrid: compact internal key for joins, opaque id for the API\nCREATE TABLE accounts (\n    id         bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n    public_id  uuid   NOT NULL DEFAULT gen_random_uuid() UNIQUE,\n    owner_name text   NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE ledger_entries (\n    id         bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n    account_id bigint        NOT NULL REFERENCES accounts (id),  -- 8-byte foreign key\n    amount     numeric(19,4) NOT NULL,\n    booked_at  timestamptz   NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\n-- GET /accounts/{publicId}/entries: resolve the public id once, join on bigint\nSELECT e.amount, e.booked_at\nFROM accounts a\nJOIN ledger_entries e ON e.account_id = a.id\nWHERE a.public_id = $1\nORDER BY e.booked_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;","note":"The public id costs one extra unique index, but joins and foreign keys stay at 8 bytes, and a v4 public id reveals neither volume nor creation time. On PostgreSQL before 18, generate v7 values in the application."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can you rely on ids increasing in commit order, for example to poll for new rows?","a":"No. A sequence value is taken when the insert runs, not when it commits, so a transaction holding id 1001 can commit after a poller using `WHERE id > :lastSeen` already saw 1002, and row 1001 is skipped forever. UUIDv7 from several nodes adds clock skew. Use logical replication or CDC, or poll an outbox with a safety margin and idempotent consumers."},{"q":"Do UUIDs protect against enumeration attacks?","a":"They make blind guessing impractical, since `UUID.randomUUID()` draws 122 bits from `SecureRandom`, but not against someone who got another customer's id from a shared link or a log. The real control is an authorization check on every lookup, such as `findByIdAndOwnerId`. Never use an id as a secret like a reset token."}],"extra":["**Gaps are normal:** sequences never roll back and Hibernate's pooled optimizer reserves blocks of ids (`allocationSize` 50 by default), so rollbacks and restarts leave gaps; gapless invoice numbers need a separate serialized counter.","**MySQL storage:** keep UUIDs in `BINARY(16)` via `UUID_TO_BIN`; its swap flag reorders the time fields of MySQL's own v1 values, while v7 values are already time-ordered.","**Snowflake-style ids:** 64-bit ids built from timestamp, worker id and counter give ordering and app-side generation in 8 bytes, but need unique worker ids and care when clocks move backwards.","**Hot ranges:** time-ordered keys send every new row to the newest key range, ideal for one B-tree but a hot spot under range-based sharding; shard on a hash or on another column such as the tenant id.","**Size adds up:** 16 versus 8 bytes repeats in the primary key, every foreign key, every index containing them and, in InnoDB, every secondary index, so less of each index fits in memory."],"redFlags":["Stores UUID keys as `varchar(36)`","calls UUIDs slow without distinguishing random v4 from time-ordered v7","treats unguessable ids as a substitute for authorization"]},{"id":126,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"Your team shifts read traffic onto replicas to offload the primary. What anomalies will users see, and how do you contain them?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Replicas apply the primary's changes asynchronously, so reads can be stale: users may not see their own writes and data can appear to go backwards across replicas; route critical reads to the primary or enforce read-your-writes.","answer":["PostgreSQL streaming replication ships WAL to replicas, asynchronously by default. Replay lag is usually milliseconds, but it grows to seconds or minutes under heavy writes, bulk jobs, network trouble, or when long queries on the replica delay replay. Even synchronous replication with `synchronous_commit = on` only waits for the WAL to be flushed on the standby, not replayed; only `remote_apply` makes the commit visible there before the client is acknowledged.","The resulting anomalies are **read-after-write** failures (create an order, get redirected, the GET hits a lagging replica and returns 404), **non-monotonic reads** (successive requests hit replicas with different lag, so a row appears and then disappears), and **stale decisions** (checking a balance or an idempotency key on a replica and then acting on it).","Critical reads go to the primary: anything inside a writing transaction, balances before a debit, uniqueness and idempotency checks. For user-facing flows, add session guarantees: pin a user to the primary for a short window after a write, or record the commit LSN and use a replica only once it has replayed that LSN. Pinning a session to one replica gives monotonic reads.","Operationally, monitor replay lag, remove replicas above a threshold from the read pool, and decide per endpoint whether staleness is acceptable: reports and catalogs yes, money and permissions no."],"keyPoints":["asynchronous apply means every replica trails the primary by some lag","a user can miss their own fresh write right after saving","reads spread across replicas can appear to move backward in time","send sensitive reads to the primary or track a per-session position"],"examples":[{"title":"Routing read-only transactions, with a read-your-writes fence","lang":"java","code":"public class ReadWriteRoutingDataSource extends AbstractRoutingDataSource {\n    @Override\n    protected Object determineCurrentLookupKey() {\n        boolean readOnly = TransactionSynchronizationManager.isCurrentTransactionReadOnly();\n        boolean recentWrite = WriteFence.userWroteWithin(Duration.ofSeconds(5)); // own helper, per session\n        return (readOnly && !recentWrite) ? \"replica\" : \"primary\";\n    }\n}\n\n@Configuration\nclass DataSourceConfig {\n    @Bean\n    @Primary\n    DataSource dataSource(@Qualifier(\"primaryDs\") DataSource primary,\n                          @Qualifier(\"replicaDs\") DataSource replica) {\n        var routing = new ReadWriteRoutingDataSource();\n        routing.setTargetDataSources(Map.<Object, Object>of(\"primary\", primary, \"replica\", replica));\n        routing.setDefaultTargetDataSource(primary);\n        routing.afterPropertiesSet();\n        // Lazy proxy: the physical connection is chosen after @Transactional(readOnly) is known\n        return new LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy(routing);\n    }\n}","note":"Without `LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy`, the connection is fetched before the read-only flag is set and every query goes to the primary. The write fence keeps a user on the primary right after they changed something."},{"title":"LSN-based read-your-writes and lag monitoring","lang":"sql","code":"-- On the primary, right after the write commits: remember the WAL position in the session\nSELECT pg_current_wal_lsn() AS commit_lsn;           -- e.g. 3/5A0C2F10\n\n-- On the replica, before serving that user's next read\nSELECT pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() >= '3/5A0C2F10'::pg_lsn AS caught_up;\n-- caught_up = false: read from the primary, or wait briefly and check again\n\n-- Lag per replica, measured on the primary\nSELECT application_name, client_addr, write_lag, flush_lag, replay_lag\nFROM pg_stat_replication;\n\n-- On a replica: time since the last replayed transaction\nSELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS replay_delay;","note":"The LSN check is precise, unlike a fixed time window. `replay_delay` also grows when the primary is simply idle, so alert on LSN distance or `replay_lag` instead."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you detect replica lag before users report it?","a":"On PostgreSQL, query `pg_stat_replication` on the primary, whose `write_lag`, `flush_lag` and `replay_lag` columns show how far each standby trails, or compare `now()` with `pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp()` on the replica. Alert when lag exceeds what your read paths tolerate, and let the routing layer pull a replica out of the pool above a threshold. The timestamp method overstates lag on an idle primary, because nothing new is being replayed."},{"q":"Why might a comment appear and then vanish when the user refreshes?","a":"Consecutive requests landed on different replicas: the first had already replayed the insert and the second had not, so the user saw time move backward. That breaks monotonic reads, which users notice even when a few seconds of lag is otherwise acceptable. Pin a session to one replica, or remember the highest WAL position the session has observed and serve it only from replicas that have replayed past that point, falling back to the primary otherwise."}],"extra":["**Built-in routing:** since Spring Framework 6.1.2, `LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy#setReadOnlyDataSource` sends `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` work to a replica without a custom `AbstractRoutingDataSource`.","**Replica query conflicts:** a long query on a hot standby can be cancelled with \"conflict with recovery\" or delay replay, depending on `max_standby_streaming_delay`; `hot_standby_feedback = on` avoids cancellations but causes bloat on the primary.","**Failover loses data too:** promoting an asynchronous replica can drop the last acknowledged transactions, so the replica set has a nonzero recovery point objective unless you use synchronous replication.","**Caches inherit lag:** repopulating a cache from a lagging replica right after an invalidation re-caches stale data for a full TTL, so cache refills after writes should read from the primary."],"redFlags":["treats replica lag as a rare edge case that needs no design","proposes adding more replicas as the fix for stale reads","never considers reads jumping between replicas with different lag"]},{"id":127,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"Partitioning vs sharding: when do you need each, and what do you give up?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Partitioning splits a large table into child tables inside one database: joins and transactions keep working, queries prune partitions and retention becomes dropping one. Sharding spreads data across separate nodes by a shard key to scale writes and storage past one machine, at the cost of cross-shard queries, transactions, global constraints and simple operations.","answer":["Partitioning stores one logical table as many physical partitions chosen by a partition key: `RANGE` by time is typical, `LIST` by region, `HASH` to spread rows. Everything stays on one server with one transaction manager. The planner prunes partitions whose bounds cannot match the `WHERE` clause, so last week's payments touch one partition and its smaller indexes. The biggest win is often lifecycle: removing a month is dropping a table, not a 100-million-row `DELETE` that bloats the table, floods WAL and lags replicas.","Partitioning adds no CPU, memory or write capacity. Queries without the partition key visit every partition, unique constraints must include the partition key, and future partitions must exist before data arrives. It suits event tables queried by recent ranges. Sharding comes after one primary is exhausted despite indexing, vertical scaling, caching, replicas (which scale only reads) and archiving: each shard is an independent database owning a subset of keys, so write capacity and storage grow with nodes.","The shard key decides everything: high cardinality, even spread, present in nearly every query and aligned with transactions. `customer_id` works when a customer's orders and payments live together, keeping most requests on one shard. Queries without the key fan out to every shard and wait for the slowest, joins and global sorting move into the application, cross-shard transactions need two-phase commit or a saga, and global uniqueness needs a lookup table. Plan rebalancing up front: `hash % nodeCount` remaps almost every key when a node is added. The two combine: shard by tenant, partition by month within each shard."],"keyPoints":["Partitioning splits a table within one database; sharding splits data across nodes","partitioning buys pruning and cheap retention, not more capacity","sharding scales writes but makes cross-shard queries, transactions and constraints hard"],"examples":[{"title":"PostgreSQL range partitioning: pruning and retention by dropping partitions","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE payment_events (\n    id          uuid          NOT NULL,\n    account_id  bigint        NOT NULL,\n    event_type  text          NOT NULL,\n    created_at  timestamptz   NOT NULL,\n    PRIMARY KEY (id, created_at)          -- must include the partition key\n) PARTITION BY RANGE (created_at);\n\nCREATE TABLE payment_events_2026_10 PARTITION OF payment_events\n    FOR VALUES FROM ('2026-10-01') TO ('2026-11-01');\n\n-- Defined on the parent, created on every partition\nCREATE INDEX ON payment_events (account_id, created_at);\n\n-- Pruned: the plan shows only payment_events_2026_10\nEXPLAIN SELECT event_type FROM payment_events\nWHERE account_id = 7781\n  AND created_at >= '2026-10-20' AND created_at < '2026-10-27';\n\n-- Not pruned: no partition key in the filter\nEXPLAIN SELECT * FROM payment_events WHERE event_type = 'CHARGEBACK';\n\n-- Retention: metadata work instead of a huge DELETE\nALTER TABLE payment_events DETACH PARTITION payment_events_2024_10 CONCURRENTLY;\nDROP TABLE payment_events_2024_10;","note":"`DETACH PARTITION ... CONCURRENTLY` (PostgreSQL 14+) avoids blocking queries on the parent but cannot run inside a transaction block. Create next month's partition ahead of time (a scheduled job or `pg_partman`), or its inserts fail."},{"title":"Application shard routing with fixed logical buckets and scatter-gather","lang":"java","code":"// 1024 fixed logical buckets; rebalancing only edits the bucket-to-node map\npublic final class ShardRouter {\n\n    private static final int BUCKETS = 1024;\n    private static final String FAILED_SQL = \"SELECT id, customer_id, created_at FROM orders\"\n        + \" WHERE status = 'FAILED' ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT ?\";\n\n    private final Map<String, JdbcTemplate> nodes;   // node name -> connection pool\n    private volatile String[] bucketToNode;           // from a routing table\n\n    // Explicit, stable hash: never change it after go-live\n    static int bucketOf(UUID customerId) {\n        long bits = customerId.getMostSignificantBits() ^ customerId.getLeastSignificantBits();\n        return Math.floorMod(Long.hashCode(bits), BUCKETS);\n    }\n\n    public JdbcTemplate forCustomer(UUID customerId) {   // the common, single-shard path\n        return nodes.get(bucketToNode[bucketOf(customerId)]);\n    }\n\n    // Scatter-gather: top N from every shard, merge, cut to N\n    public List<OrderSummary> latestFailedOrders(int limit) {\n        List<Future<List<OrderSummary>>> parts;\n        try (var pool = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n            parts = nodes.values().stream()\n                .map(db -> pool.submit(() -> db.query(FAILED_SQL, OrderSummary.MAPPER, limit)))\n                .toList();\n        }                                           // close() waits for the slowest\n        return parts.stream()\n            .flatMap(part -> part.resultNow().stream())\n            .sorted(Comparator.comparing(OrderSummary::createdAt).reversed())\n            .limit(limit)\n            .toList();\n    }\n}","note":"Because the bucket function never changes, rebalancing moves whole buckets. A correct global top N needs `limit` rows from every shard, so scatter-gather cost grows with shard count."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you move data between shards without downtime?","a":"Pre-split into many logical buckets so a move reassigns a bucket instead of rehashing keys. Copy the bucket's snapshot to the target, stream changes with CDC until it catches up, briefly block writes for that bucket, flip the routing entry, then delete the source copy. Vitess and Citus automate this."},{"q":"Users are sharded by user id. How do you keep emails unique?","a":"A unique index per shard only guarantees uniqueness within that shard. Keep a global lookup table from email to user id with a unique constraint, claim the email there before creating the user, and release it if creation fails. The same table routes logins, which arrive with an email, not a user id."}],"extra":["**No global indexes:** a PostgreSQL index on a partitioned table is a set of per-partition indexes, so `UNIQUE (id)` alone is rejected; MySQL has the same rule for unique keys.","**Default partition:** a `DEFAULT` partition catches unmatched rows, but attaching a partition then scans it for conflicts, and `DETACH ... CONCURRENTLY` is not allowed while it exists.","**Granularity:** match partitions to retention and query ranges (daily for high-volume events, monthly for most tables); thousands of partitions slow planning.","**Distributed SQL:** Citus, Vitess, CockroachDB and Spanner automate routing and rebalancing; cross-shard work still pays coordination latency.","**Functional split first:** moving a domain such as the catalog to its own database often relieves a primary before any table needs sharding."],"redFlags":["Uses partitioning and sharding as synonyms","proposes sharding before indexing, vertical scaling, replicas and partitioning","picks a shard key without checking the main queries and transactions"]},{"id":128,"category":"NoSQL / MongoDB","question":"What are the main families of NoSQL databases, and what is each good for?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Key-value stores serve lookups by exact key, document stores hold aggregates read and written as a whole, wide-column stores absorb huge partitioned write volumes, and graph databases traverse relationships. Time series databases and search engines are specialized stores usually fed from a system of record; choose by access pattern, consistency needs and scale.","answer":["NoSQL is an umbrella for non-relational stores that give up some relational features (joins, ad hoc queries, multi-row transactions, a fixed schema) to optimize one access pattern or to scale out. Key-value stores keep an opaque value behind a key with fast get and put: Redis in memory with rich data structures, DynamoDB as a managed store with a partition key and optional sort key. They fit sessions, caches, rate-limit counters, idempotency keys and carts; other access paths need a secondary index or another store.","Document stores such as MongoDB hold JSON-like documents whose fields can be queried and indexed, so a whole aggregate, an order with its lines and address, is read in one call and updated atomically: catalogs with varying attributes, profiles, content. Wide-column stores such as Cassandra and ScyllaDB group rows into partitions by a partition key, sorted by clustering columns. They are built for very high write throughput, multi-datacenter replication and linear scale, but you design one table per query and accept duplication: inboxes, feeds, IoT readings, event history.","Graph databases such as Neo4j store relationships as first-class data, so a traversal costs in proportion to the neighborhood visited, not the table size: fraud rings sharing cards or devices, recommendations, permission graphs. Time series databases (TimescaleDB, InfluxDB, Prometheus for metrics) optimize appends, time-range queries, downsampling and retention, and search engines (Elasticsearch, OpenSearch) provide full-text relevance and facets. Most backends pair a relational source of truth with a cache and a search index; every extra store adds synchronization and operational cost, so add one only for an access pattern the primary serves badly."],"keyPoints":["Key-value for exact keys, document for aggregates, wide-column for partitioned writes, graph for relationships","time series and search engines are specialized stores fed from a source of truth","choose by access pattern, consistency and scale, not schema flexibility alone"],"examples":[{"title":"One order domain shaped for each family","lang":"text","code":"# Key-value (Redis, DynamoDB): opaque value, read and written by exact key\nsession:7f3c9a          -> {\"userId\":\"u-1842\",\"roles\":[\"CUSTOMER\"]}   TTL 30m\nidem:payment:5d1e7c     -> \"payment-88213\"                            TTL 24h\n\n# Document (MongoDB): the whole aggregate, any indexed field is queryable\norders: { _id: \"o-1001\", customerId: \"u-1842\", status: \"PAID\",\n          lines: [ { sku: \"KB-201\", qty: 1, unitPrice: 79.00 } ],\n          shipping: { city: \"Lund\", country: \"SE\" } }\n\n# Wide-column (Cassandra): partition key picks the nodes, clustering key sorts rows\norders_by_customer\n  partition u-1842 | 2026-10-29 10:15 | o-1001 | PAID    | 79.00\n                   | 2026-10-12 08:02 | o-0977 | SHIPPED | 129.00\n  partition u-2210 | 2026-10-30 07:41 | o-1012 | PENDING | 45.50\n\n# Graph (Neo4j): relationships are data you can traverse\n(:Customer {id:'u-1842'})-[:PLACED]->(:Order {id:'o-1001'})\n(:Customer {id:'u-1842'})-[:PAID_WITH]->(:Card {fp:'c9f1'})<-[:PAID_WITH]-(:Customer {id:'u-3307'})\n\n# Search (Elasticsearch): inverted index, rebuilt from the source of truth if needed\nGET /products/_search { \"query\": { \"match\": { \"name\": \"wireless keyboard\" } } }","note":"The same data takes a different shape in each family, and the shape decides which questions are cheap. The graph line answers which customers share a card in one hop, a self-join per hop in SQL."},{"title":"Cassandra: one table per query, partition key in every read","lang":"sql","code":"-- CQL: the partition key decides which replicas own the data\nCREATE TABLE payment_events_by_account (\n    account_id  uuid,\n    day         date,              -- bucketing keeps partitions bounded\n    event_time  timestamp,\n    event_id    timeuuid,\n    event_type  text,\n    amount      decimal,\n    PRIMARY KEY ((account_id, day), event_time, event_id)\n) WITH CLUSTERING ORDER BY (event_time DESC, event_id DESC)\n  AND default_time_to_live = 7776000;   -- 90 days\n\n-- GOOD: one partition, rows already sorted newest first\nSELECT event_type, amount, event_time\nFROM payment_events_by_account\nWHERE account_id = 5b1c7e8a-3f2d-4c1b-9a6e-2d7f0c8b1e44\n  AND day = '2026-10-29'\nLIMIT 50;\n\n-- BAD: no partition key; rejected unless you add ALLOW FILTERING (cluster-wide scan)\nSELECT * FROM payment_events_by_account WHERE event_type = 'REFUND';\n\n-- A new query pattern means a new table (refunds_by_day), written alongside this one","note":"Cassandra modeling starts from queries, not entities, and duplicates data across tables to serve them. Without the `day` bucket an active account's partition grows without bound, which hurts reads, repair and compaction."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why not just use PostgreSQL JSONB instead of a document database?","a":"Often you should: `jsonb` with GIN or expression indexes gives flexible attributes next to transactions, joins and constraints in a database you already run. A document database pays off when documents are the main model, you need built-in sharding for write scale, or you rely on update operators for nested arrays and on change streams."},{"q":"When would you pick Cassandra over MongoDB?","a":"For write-heavy, append-mostly data read by a known key (events, messages, sensor readings) that must scale linearly and run active-active across datacenters, since Cassandra is leaderless with tunable consistency such as `LOCAL_QUORUM`. You give up ad hoc queries, joins and cheap conditional updates (lightweight transactions are slow), and deletes and TTLs leave tombstones. MongoDB suits rich queries over aggregates."}],"extra":["**Not one category:** consistency, query power and scaling differ more between NoSQL stores than between NoSQL and SQL; DynamoDB is key-value with document features, and Redis offers lists, sets and sorted sets, not only strings.","**Search is derived data:** Elasticsearch and OpenSearch make writes searchable after a refresh (about one second by default); feed them through CDC or an outbox and keep the ability to rebuild the index.","**Graph or recursive SQL:** a recursive CTE handles shallow hierarchies such as category trees; a graph database earns its place for variable-depth traversals such as accounts within three hops of a flagged device.","**Consistency is a setting:** Cassandra picks it per request, DynamoDB reads are eventually consistent unless you request strongly consistent reads (not available on global secondary indexes), and MongoDB uses read and write concerns.","**Time series in PostgreSQL:** TimescaleDB is a PostgreSQL extension, so time-partitioned tables, compression and retention policies come with ordinary SQL and joins."],"redFlags":["Picks NoSQL as faster or schemaless without naming the access pattern","treats all NoSQL databases as interchangeable","uses a cache or search index as the system of record"]},{"id":129,"category":"NoSQL / MongoDB","question":"Designing a MongoDB schema, how do you decide whether related data lives inside the parent document or in its own collection?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Embed data that is owned by the parent, read with it and bounded in size; reference data that is shared, large, unbounded or updated independently, and decide from the real query and update patterns.","answer":["**Embed** what is always read together and belongs to one parent: order lines inside the order, a customer's few addresses, a small status history. You get one read, an atomic single-document update and good locality, with no `$lookup`. The cost is duplication when the embedded data is really shared, and document growth.","**Reference** what is shared by many parents (a product used by thousands of orders), large (long descriptions, media metadata), unbounded (all orders of a customer, reviews of a popular product, event histories), or read and updated independently (a price that changes daily should not require rewriting historical orders). The cost is extra queries or `$lookup` and no referential integrity enforced by the database.","**Size and query patterns decide.** A BSON document is capped at 16 MB, and performance degrades long before that: large documents waste bandwidth and cache, and every update rewrites the whole document. Unbounded arrays are the classic anti-pattern. A useful guide: one-to-few, embed; one-to-many, an array of ids in the parent; one-to-very-many, the child stores the parent id. The hybrid **extended reference** embeds the id plus the few fields you display.","I list the hot operations with their frequency and atomicity needs, then check how often the embedded data changes. Neither embedding nor referencing is a default."],"keyPoints":["nest what the parent owns, loads with it and keeps small","keep apart anything many parents share, that grows without limit or changes on its own","growing arrays push documents toward the 16 MB cap and slow every update","let the real read and write paths settle each relationship"],"examples":[{"title":"Order with embedded lines and extended references","lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"_id\": { \"$oid\": \"66f1c2a9e4b0a1b2c3d4e5f6\" },\n  \"orderNumber\": \"ORD-2026-000481\",\n  \"customer\": {\n    \"customerId\": { \"$oid\": \"66a0f1d2e4b0a1b2c3d4e001\" },\n    \"name\": \"Sara Lind\",\n    \"email\": \"sara.lind@example.com\"\n  },\n  \"status\": \"PAID\",\n  \"lines\": [\n    { \"productId\": \"sku-48213\", \"name\": \"Trail Running Shoe X2\",\n      \"unitPrice\": { \"$numberDecimal\": \"129.00\" }, \"qty\": 1 },\n    { \"productId\": \"sku-10077\", \"name\": \"Merino Socks\",\n      \"unitPrice\": { \"$numberDecimal\": \"15.00\" }, \"qty\": 3 }\n  ],\n  \"shippingAddress\": { \"street\": \"Storgatan 12\", \"city\": \"Stockholm\", \"postalCode\": \"111 51\", \"country\": \"SE\" },\n  \"total\": { \"$numberDecimal\": \"174.00\" },\n  \"createdAt\": { \"$date\": \"2026-09-20T10:15:00Z\" }\n}","note":"Lines are embedded (owned and bounded); product and customer are referenced by id with a snapshot of the fields the order must keep as they were at purchase time."},{"title":"Embedded versus referenced in Spring Data MongoDB","lang":"java","code":"// Embedded value objects: owned, bounded, always read with the order\nrecord OrderLine(String productId, String name, BigDecimal unitPrice, int qty) {}\nrecord CustomerSnapshot(String customerId, String name, String email) {}\n\n@Document(\"orders\")\npublic class Order {\n    @Id private String id;\n    private CustomerSnapshot customer;   // extended reference: id plus fields shown on the order\n    private List<OrderLine> lines;       // bounded by checkout rules, for example max 100 lines\n    private String status;\n    @Field(targetType = FieldType.DECIMAL128) private BigDecimal total;\n}\n\n// Referenced: unbounded and read independently, so the child points to the parent\n@Document(\"reviews\")\n@CompoundIndex(def = \"{ 'productId': 1, 'createdAt': -1 }\")\nclass Review {\n    @Id private String id;\n    private String productId;            // never an ever-growing array inside Product\n    private int rating;\n    private String text;\n    private Instant createdAt;\n}","note":"Reviews of a popular product would grow without limit, so they live in their own collection with an index on `productId`; the product keeps only a computed rating summary."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When does embedding only the latest few comments make sense?","a":"When the post page shows a handful of recent comments on every view but the full thread is long and unbounded. Keep all comments in their own collection and maintain a capped array of the newest ones on the post with `$push` using `$each` and `$slice: -10`. That gives a one-read page render while the post stays small; the cost is two writes per new comment, so wrap them in a transaction or tolerate the preview being briefly behind."},{"q":"How does the 16 MB document limit shape these decisions?","a":"A document that grows without bound eventually fails writes at 16 MB, but it hurts long before that: every read transfers the whole document, updates touch a large record, and big documents take more of the WiredTiger cache. Treat any array that grows with time or usage as a signal to move it out or to use the bucket pattern, which groups items into fixed-size documents. Design for the worst-case size, not the average."}],"extra":["**Updates rewrite the document:** changing one array element still ends up persisted as a new version of the whole document, so large, frequently updated documents cost write throughput and cache even far below 16 MB.","**Bucket pattern:** for histories and time series, group children into bounded bucket documents (per device per hour, or per 100 events) to get embedding benefits without unbounded growth; time series collections automate this.","**Outlier pattern:** embed the first N reviews for typical products and move overflow into a separate collection flagged with `hasOverflow: true`, so a few viral products do not dictate the model for all.","**$lookup needs an index:** `$lookup` runs a lookup per input document against the foreign field, so it needs an index there; it works with sharded collections since MongoDB 5.1, but frequent `$lookup` on a hot path suggests you should embed.","**Atomicity boundary:** only single-document writes are atomic without a transaction, so data that must change together (order status and totals) belongs in the same document."],"redFlags":["nests an unbounded list such as every comment ever posted","copies shared data everywhere with no plan to keep copies in sync","treats separate collections as free, ignoring the extra round trip or `$lookup`"]},{"id":130,"category":"NoSQL / MongoDB","question":"A team ports a normalized SQL schema table by table into MongoDB collections. What goes wrong, and how should they model instead?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Relational modeling normalizes entities first and lets queries join them later; MongoDB modeling starts from the application's access patterns and shapes documents around aggregates, trading controlled duplication for single-document reads and atomic writes.","answer":["In a relational design you model entities and relationships to third normal form, largely independent of the queries, and rely on joins, constraints and the optimizer to serve whatever queries come later. MongoDB modeling is **query-first**: list the operations, their frequency, latency targets and consistency needs, then **design around those access patterns** so each hot operation touches one document or one index range, with **fewer joins** because `$lookup` is comparatively expensive.","**Document-oriented design** means the document is the aggregate and the consistency boundary: what changes together lives together, because single-document writes are atomic. Things with an independent lifecycle become separate documents linked by id. **Denormalization** is deliberate: copy read-mostly fields (product name into an order line, author name into a post) and decide how each copy is maintained, for example a change stream consumer, a background job or accepting staleness.","The rest of the toolkit differs too: the schema lives in the application plus `$jsonSchema` validators, documents carry a `schemaVersion` and migrate lazily, compound indexes follow the ESR rule, arrays get multikey indexes, and the shard key is part of the model from day one.","The common failure is porting a relational schema one table per collection and joining in the application, which gives you the weaknesses of both. The opposite failure is one giant document that grows forever."],"keyPoints":["start from the queries the application runs, not from the entities","shape each document around a unit of data that changes together","accept controlled duplication to serve a screen in one read","single-document writes are atomic, replacing many joins and transactions"],"examples":[{"title":"Query-first worksheet for an order service","lang":"text","code":"Operation                           Frequency  Latency    Shape it implies\nGet order by id with lines          high       < 20 ms    one document, lines embedded\nList a customer's recent orders     high       < 50 ms    index { customerId: 1, createdAt: -1 }, projection\nUpdate order status                 medium     < 20 ms    atomic update of one document\nShow product name and price on it   high       n/a        copy name and price into each line (snapshot)\nRename a product                    rare       async      do not propagate to past orders\nDaily sales per product             low        minutes    aggregation pipeline or warehouse export","note":"The collections, embedded fields and indexes are read off this table. A relational design would start from the entity diagram instead."},{"title":"Model and repository derived from the access patterns","lang":"java","code":"@Document(\"orders\")\n@CompoundIndex(name = \"customer_recent\", def = \"{ 'customerId': 1, 'createdAt': -1 }\")\npublic class Order {\n    @Id private String id;\n    private String customerId;\n    private int schemaVersion = 2;            // readers handle v1 and v2, migration is lazy\n    private String status;\n    private List<OrderLine> lines;            // embedded: part of the aggregate\n    private BigDecimal total;                 // computed pattern: stored, not summed on every read\n    private Instant createdAt;\n}\n\npublic interface OrderRepository extends MongoRepository<Order, String> {\n    // Served by the compound index; the projection keeps list pages small\n    @Query(value = \"{ 'customerId': ?0 }\",\n           fields = \"{ 'status': 1, 'total': 1, 'createdAt': 1 }\",\n           sort = \"{ 'createdAt': -1 }\")\n    List<Order> findRecentSummaries(String customerId, Pageable page);\n}\n\n// Status change is one atomic single-document update, no transaction required\nmongoTemplate.updateFirst(\n        Query.query(Criteria.where(\"_id\").is(orderId).and(\"status\").is(\"PENDING\")),\n        new Update().set(\"status\", \"PAID\").set(\"paidAt\", Instant.now()),\n        Order.class);","note":"Every hot operation maps to one document or one index range. The conditional filter on `status` makes the transition safe under concurrency without locks."}],"followUps":[{"q":"A value copied into many documents changes. How do you deal with that?","a":"First decide whether the copy is a snapshot, like the price on a past order, which should never change, or a display copy that must follow the source. For display copies, update every holder with `updateMany` filtered on the source ID, often asynchronously from a change stream consumer, and accept a short window where some documents show the old value. If the value changes often or appears in a huge number of documents, a reference is cheaper than duplication."},{"q":"What document layout suits a high-volume stream of sensor readings?","a":"Avoid one document per reading at high volume. Group readings into buckets, for example one document per sensor per hour, or use a time series collection (MongoDB 5.0 and later) with a `timeField` and a `metaField` such as the sensor ID, which buckets and compresses automatically. Add a TTL or archiving policy for old data, and keep a small per-sensor document if the latest value is read constantly."}],"extra":["**ESR rule:** MongoDB's guideline for compound indexes is Equality fields first, then Sort fields, then Range fields, the same reasoning as composite index order in SQL.","**Schema versioning pattern:** store a `schemaVersion`, make readers accept old and new shapes, and upgrade documents on write or with a background job instead of a big-bang migration.","**Many-to-many:** model it as an array of ids on the side you query from (for example `categoryIds` on products with a multikey index), and only introduce a separate linking collection when the relationship has its own attributes or is unbounded.","**Validation levels:** a `$jsonSchema` validator with `validationLevel: \"moderate\"` checks inserts and updates to documents that already conform, so legacy documents can coexist while new writes are enforced."],"redFlags":["maps every table to a collection and rebuilds joins with `$lookup`","duplicates data without saying how the copies get updated","assumes MongoDB has no transactions, or wraps every write in one"]},{"id":131,"category":"NoSQL / MongoDB","question":"A startup wants MongoDB as its main datastore. What would make you agree, and what would you give up?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Choose MongoDB when data is naturally aggregate-shaped, read and written as whole documents, has an evolving schema and needs horizontal write scaling; in return accept weaker joins, reporting and cross-document constraints.","answer":["MongoDB fits when the **document model** matches the domain: a product with category-specific attributes, a user profile with nested preferences, an order with its lines. One document is one aggregate, so the hot path is one read and one atomic write. A **flexible schema** lets heterogeneous or fast-evolving attributes live together without a migration per field (you still enforce shape with `$jsonSchema` validators), and **sharding** scales writes and data volume horizontally, with replica sets for automatic failover. That combination supports **high write throughput** for ingestion, catalogs and content.","The trade-offs I accept: data is **denormalized**, so duplicated fields need a sync strategy; **joins are limited** (`$lookup` exists but is slower and there are no foreign keys); multi-document transactions exist but cost more and should be the exception; ad hoc **reporting** and analytics across entities are harder and usually move to a warehouse; and the shard key is a near-permanent decision.","A relational database is the better fit when relationships are many-to-many and queried in many directions, when integrity spans entities (ledgers, inventory, bookings), and when ad hoc SQL matters. PostgreSQL `jsonb` also covers \"some flexible attributes\", so flexibility alone does not justify MongoDB.","So I **choose by use case**: known aggregate-oriented access patterns and scale needs point to MongoDB, while cross-entity invariants point to SQL. MongoDB is not faster by default; performance comes from modeling and indexes, and many systems use both."],"keyPoints":["fits aggregates that are loaded and saved as one document","documents can evolve shape without a migration for every change","sharding gives a built-in path to scale writes out","the price is weak joins, ad hoc reporting and rules spanning documents"],"examples":[{"title":"A document that plays to MongoDB's strengths","lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"_id\": \"sku-48213\",\n  \"name\": \"Trail Running Shoe X2\",\n  \"category\": \"footwear\",\n  \"price\": { \"amount\": { \"$numberDecimal\": \"129.00\" }, \"currency\": \"EUR\" },\n  \"attributes\": {\n    \"dropMm\": 6,\n    \"waterproof\": true,\n    \"surface\": [\"trail\", \"gravel\"]\n  },\n  \"variants\": [\n    { \"sku\": \"sku-48213-41-blk\", \"size\": 41, \"color\": \"black\" },\n    { \"sku\": \"sku-48213-42-blu\", \"size\": 42, \"color\": \"blue\" }\n  ],\n  \"categoryIds\": [\"running\", \"outdoor\"],\n  \"schemaVersion\": 3,\n  \"updatedAt\": { \"$date\": \"2026-09-20T10:15:00Z\" }\n}","note":"A laptop in the same collection would carry completely different `attributes`; the page needs the whole product in one read, and there are no cross-product invariants to enforce."},{"title":"Deciding by use case","lang":"text","code":"Use case                          Better fit         Main reason\nProduct catalog, CMS content      MongoDB            heterogeneous attributes, whole-document reads\nUser profiles and preferences     MongoDB            one aggregate per user, key lookups\nEvent or IoT ingestion at scale   MongoDB            sharded writes, time series collections\nPayments ledger, accounting       PostgreSQL         multi-row invariants, constraints, audit SQL\nInventory with reservations       PostgreSQL         concurrent decrements across entities\nAd hoc reporting, analytics       SQL or warehouse   joins and aggregations across entities","note":"Mixed systems are common: PostgreSQL for the transactional core, MongoDB for catalog, content or read models fed from events."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you keep MongoDB documents consistent without a fixed schema?","a":"Attach a `$jsonSchema` validator to the collection and choose `validationLevel` and `validationAction`, for example `moderate` and `warn` while migrating, then `strict` and `error`. Store a schema version field in each document so code can read old shapes and upgrade them lazily on write. Map documents to typed classes in the application, with Spring Data MongoDB for instance, so most drift is caught in code review rather than in production."},{"q":"Reporting needs to join MongoDB orders with customers and payments. How would you serve that?","a":"Avoid running heavy aggregation pipelines against the primary that serves live traffic. For moderate needs, read from secondaries or Atlas analytics nodes with a suitable read preference; for real cross-domain reporting, stream changes through change streams or a CDC connector into a warehouse where joins are cheap. Accept that reports run slightly behind and label them with an as-of time."}],"extra":["**Transactions exist but are not free:** multi-document ACID transactions arrived in 4.0 (replica sets) and 4.2 (sharded clusters), with a default 60 second lifetime limit, and cross-shard transactions pay two-phase commit costs; needing them everywhere signals the wrong model or the wrong database.","**Write and read concern:** durability is tunable per operation; since MongoDB 5.0 the default write concern is `majority`, and `readConcern: \"majority\"` avoids reading data that a failover could roll back, while `w: 1` is faster but can lose acknowledged writes.","**Shard key pitfalls:** a monotonically increasing key such as an ObjectId or timestamp sends every insert to one shard; prefer a high-cardinality, evenly distributed key aligned with the main query, since queries without it become scatter-gather across all shards.","**Schema still exists:** \"schemaless\" means the schema lives in the application, so use `$jsonSchema` validation and a `schemaVersion` field to evolve documents safely."],"redFlags":["picks MongoDB mainly to avoid writing migrations","claims multi-document transactions make it equal to SQL for every workload","never asks how the data will be queried or reported on"]},{"id":132,"category":"NoSQL / MongoDB","question":"How do indexes and multi-document transactions work in MongoDB, and when should you avoid relying on transactions?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Order compound index fields by equality, sort, range (ESR), check with `explain()`, and know that indexing an array creates a multikey index. Multi-document transactions need a replica set or sharded cluster, abort after 60 seconds by default and cost throughput, so model invariants inside one document and keep transactions the exception.","answer":["Without a usable index a query is a `COLLSCAN`. For compound indexes the ESR guideline orders fields as Equality, Sort, Range: equality fields narrow the scan to a contiguous key range, the sort field next returns documents already ordered, and range fields last keep that order; put the range first and you get a blocking in-memory `SORT`. In `explain(\"executionStats\")` look for `IXSCAN`, no `SORT`, and examined counts close to `nReturned`. Indexing an array makes the index multikey, one entry per element, so large arrays inflate index size and write cost; a compound index allows at most one array field per document.","Single-document writes are always atomic, nested arrays included. Multi-document transactions add snapshot isolation and all-or-nothing commits across documents and collections, but only on a replica set or sharded cluster; a standalone `mongod` rejects them. They abort after `transactionLifetimeLimitSeconds` (60 by default), and a write to a document another operation changed after the snapshot aborts with a `WriteConflict` labeled `TransientTransactionError`, so the whole transaction must run again. The driver's `withTransaction` retries that; Spring's `MongoTransactionManager` does not.","Transactions cost throughput: snapshots pin old versions in the WiredTiger cache, conflicts force retries, and cross-shard commits add two-phase coordination. The usual answer is modeling: an order that embeds its lines and status changes them in one atomic update, and a filter such as `{ _id, status: \"PENDING\" }` or `{ balance: { $gte: amount } }` enforces invariants without a transaction. Keep transactions for rare multi-document invariants, such as a wallet transfer or a state change plus its outbox document, and keep them short (MongoDB suggests at most about 1,000 modified documents). If most writes need one, consider a relational database."],"keyPoints":["Compound indexes follow equality, sort, range; verify with explain","transactions need a replica set, time out at 60 seconds by default and must be retried","model invariants inside one document so transactions stay rare"],"examples":[{"title":"ESR index design and reading explain output in mongosh","lang":"text","code":"// Query: one customer's paid orders over 100, newest first\n// BAD: range before sort, so the index cannot deliver the order (in-memory SORT)\ndb.orders.createIndex({ customerId: 1, status: 1, total: 1, createdAt: -1 })\n\n// GOOD (ESR): equality, then sort, then range\ndb.orders.createIndex({ customerId: 1, status: 1, createdAt: -1, total: 1 })\n\ndb.orders.find({ customerId: \"c-1842\", status: \"PAID\", total: { $gte: 100 } })\n         .sort({ createdAt: -1 }).limit(20)\n         .explain(\"executionStats\")\n// winningPlan: LIMIT -> FETCH -> IXSCAN { customerId: 1, status: 1, createdAt: -1, total: 1 }\n// nReturned: 20, totalKeysExamined: 27, totalDocsExamined: 20   (no SORT stage)\n\n// Multikey: one index entry per array element\ndb.products.createIndex({ tags: 1, price: 1 })    // fine: one array field\ndb.products.createIndex({ tags: 1, colors: 1 })   // fails if a document has both as arrays","note":"A few extra keys examined is the price of putting the range last; `total` is still checked on index keys before any fetch. A `SORT` stage or `totalDocsExamined` far above `nReturned` means a poor fit."},{"title":"Atomic single-document update first, a retried transaction only when needed","lang":"java","code":"// Usually enough: one document, one conditional update, no transaction\npublic boolean markPaid(String orderId) {\n    UpdateResult result = orders.updateOne(\n        and(eq(\"_id\", orderId), eq(\"status\", \"PENDING\")),\n        combine(set(\"status\", \"PAID\"), currentDate(\"paidAt\")));\n    return result.getModifiedCount() == 1;   // false: already paid or cancelled\n}\n\n// Genuinely multi-document: debit, credit and a transfer record together\npublic void transfer(String transferId, String fromId, String toId, long cents) {\n    TransactionOptions options = TransactionOptions.builder()\n        .readConcern(ReadConcern.SNAPSHOT).writeConcern(WriteConcern.MAJORITY).build();\n    try (ClientSession session = mongoClient.startSession()) {\n        // Retries on TransientTransactionError and UnknownTransactionCommitResult\n        session.withTransaction(() -> {\n            UpdateResult debit = wallets.updateOne(session,\n                and(eq(\"_id\", fromId), gte(\"balanceCents\", cents)),\n                inc(\"balanceCents\", -cents));\n            if (debit.getModifiedCount() == 0) throw new InsufficientFundsException(fromId);\n            wallets.updateOne(session, eq(\"_id\", toId), inc(\"balanceCents\", cents));\n            transfers.insertOne(session, new Document(\"_id\", transferId)   // replay: duplicate key\n                .append(\"from\", fromId).append(\"to\", toId).append(\"cents\", cents));\n            return null;\n        }, options);\n    }\n}","note":"The body may run several times, so it must not call HTTP services or send Kafka messages. With `transferId` as `_id`, a client replay hits a duplicate key and aborts instead of moving money twice."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens when two transactions update the same document at the same time?","a":"The second writer gets a `WriteConflict` and its transaction aborts with the `TransientTransactionError` label, so it must run again. A hot document such as a global counter turns this into a retry storm. Use a single-document `$inc` outside a transaction or split the counter into shards summed on read."},{"q":"How do you find slow or badly indexed queries in production?","a":"The profiler or slow query log records operations over `slowms` (100 ms by default) with plan summaries and keys and documents examined. Re-run suspects with `explain(\"executionStats\")` and look for `COLLSCAN`, `SORT` stages and high examined-to-returned ratios. The `$indexStats` aggregation stage finds unused indexes that still slow every write."}],"extra":["**Tests:** transactions fail on a standalone `mongod`, so run a single-node replica set; the Testcontainers `MongoDBContainer` starts one.","**Spring:** `@Transactional` works with MongoDB only after you register a `MongoTransactionManager` bean, which Spring Boot does not create for you.","**Commit write concern:** commit with `w: \"majority\"`; a transaction acknowledged with `w: 1` can be rolled back if the primary fails before replicating it.","**Partial and TTL indexes:** `partialFilterExpression` indexes only matching documents (such as `status: \"PENDING\"`); TTL indexes delete in a background pass about every 60 seconds, so expiry is not exact.","**Unique indexes when sharded:** a unique index on a sharded collection must be prefixed by the shard key, so other unique fields need a separate collection."],"redFlags":["Wraps every write in a multi-document transaction by habit","orders compound index fields by query text instead of equality, sort, range","does not know transactions can abort and must be retried"]},{"id":133,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"Why is Redis fast, and which data structure would you use for common backend problems?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Redis keeps data in memory and executes commands one at a time on a main thread whose event loop multiplexes thousands of connections, so there are no locks and each command is atomic. Its real value is the data structures: strings with `INCR`, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, streams and HyperLogLog each turn a common backend problem into one server-side command.","answer":["Redis holds the dataset in RAM, so most commands finish in microseconds. One main thread runs an event loop over `epoll` or `kqueue`, reading requests from many sockets and executing commands one after another: no locks, no contention, and every command is atomic. The network round trip usually dominates latency, so pipelining matters more than server CPU. Redis 6 added optional I/O threads for socket reads, writes and parsing, but command execution stays single-threaded; snapshots and AOF rewrites run in a forked child.","The sharp edge: one slow command stalls every client. `KEYS *`, `HGETALL` on a hash with millions of fields, `DEL` of a huge key or a long Lua script can block the server for hundreds of milliseconds and time out callers and health checks. Iterate with `SCAN` and `HSCAN`, free memory with `UNLINK`, keep values small, and find offenders with `SLOWLOG GET`. One instance executes on about one core, so throughput scales by sharding with Redis Cluster, not a bigger machine.","Pick the structure that makes the operation one command. Strings hold cached values, `INCR` counters and `SET NX EX` idempotency keys. Hashes model objects whose fields change separately, like a cart. Lists give simple queues (`LPUSH` with `BRPOP`, or `LMOVE` into a processing list) and capped recent items. Sets track uniqueness, such as one coupon redemption per user. Sorted sets order by score: leaderboards, sliding-window rate limits with timestamps as scores, delayed jobs by due time. Streams are an append-only log with consumer groups and acknowledgements. HyperLogLog counts distinct visitors in at most 12 KB with about 0.81% error."],"keyPoints":["In-memory data and single-threaded command execution make each command atomic","one O(N) command on a big key blocks every client","match the problem to a structure so it becomes one server-side command"],"examples":[{"title":"One structure per backend problem in redis-cli","lang":"bash","code":"# Strings: atomic counters and idempotency keys\nINCR orders:created:2026-10-30                       # no read-modify-write race\nSET idem:payment:5d1e7c \"payment-88213\" NX EX 86400  # OK first time, (nil) for a replay\n\n# Hashes: an object whose fields change individually\nHSET cart:u-1842 KB-201 1 MS-330 2\nHINCRBY cart:u-1842 MS-330 1                         # (integer) 3\n\n# Lists: capped \"recently viewed\"\nLPUSH recent:viewed:u-1842 KB-201\nLTRIM recent:viewed:u-1842 0 19                      # keep the last 20\n\n# Sets: uniqueness\nSADD promo:redeemed:BLACKFRIDAY u-1842               # (integer) 0 if already redeemed\n\n# Sorted sets: leaderboard\nZINCRBY leaderboard:2026-10 250 seller-17\nZRANGE leaderboard:2026-10 0 9 REV WITHSCORES        # top 10 in O(log N + 10)\n\n# Streams: log with consumer groups and acks (group made once with XGROUP CREATE)\nXADD payments:events MAXLEN ~ 1000000 * type CAPTURED paymentId p-88213\nXREADGROUP GROUP ledger worker-1 COUNT 10 BLOCK 5000 STREAMS payments:events >\n\n# HyperLogLog: approximate distinct count in at most 12 KB\nPFADD visitors:2026-10-30 u-1842 u-2210 u-3307\nPFCOUNT visitors:2026-10-30","note":"Each line is one atomic command, so concurrent clients cannot interleave halfway through. The same logic as `GET`, modify in Java, then `SET` loses updates under concurrency."},{"title":"Sliding-window rate limiter on a sorted set, made atomic with Lua","lang":"java","code":"@Component\npublic class SlidingWindowRateLimiter {\n\n    // Atomic: no other command runs between ZREMRANGEBYSCORE, ZCARD and ZADD\n    private static final RedisScript<Long> SCRIPT = RedisScript.of(\"\"\"\n        local key, window, limit = KEYS[1], tonumber(ARGV[1]), tonumber(ARGV[2])\n        local t = redis.call('TIME')                  -- one clock for all pods\n        local now = t[1] * 1000 + math.floor(t[2] / 1000)\n        redis.call('ZREMRANGEBYSCORE', key, '-inf', now - window)\n        if redis.call('ZCARD', key) >= limit then\n            return 0\n        end\n        redis.call('ZADD', key, now, ARGV[3])\n        redis.call('PEXPIRE', key, window)\n        return 1\n        \"\"\", Long.class);\n\n    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;\n\n    public SlidingWindowRateLimiter(StringRedisTemplate redis) {\n        this.redis = redis;\n    }\n\n    public boolean tryAcquire(String apiKey, int limit, Duration window) {\n        Long allowed = redis.execute(SCRIPT, List.of(\"rate:\" + apiKey),\n            String.valueOf(window.toMillis()), String.valueOf(limit), UUID.randomUUID().toString());\n        return Long.valueOf(1L).equals(allowed);\n    }\n}","note":"Each request needs a unique member, or two calls in the same millisecond collapse into one entry. Memory grows with the limit per key, the price of an exact window compared with a fixed-window `INCR` counter."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is KEYS dangerous in production, and what do you use instead?","a":"`KEYS pattern` walks the whole keyspace in one command, so with millions of keys it blocks the command thread and every client stalls; health checks can time out and trigger a failover. `SCAN` with a cursor does the work incrementally, but may return a key twice and sees no consistent snapshot. Better still, maintain an index, such as a set of a user's session keys, and block `KEYS` with ACLs."},{"q":"If command execution is single-threaded, how do you scale Redis?","a":"First cut round trips with pipelining and multi-key commands. Replicas add read capacity but serve slightly stale data. For write throughput and memory, Redis Cluster spreads 16,384 hash slots over several primaries; multi-key commands must stay in one slot, which hash tags such as `{order:42}` guarantee. A single hot key still lives on one shard, so put a local cache in front or split the key."}],"extra":["**Atomic per command, not per sequence:** two commands from Java can interleave with other clients; use a combined command, `MULTI`/`EXEC` with `WATCH` for check-and-set (no rollback on errors), or a Lua script or function.","**Pipelining is not a transaction:** it sends many commands in one round trip, but other clients' commands can run in between.","**Compact encodings:** small hashes, sets and sorted sets use listpack or intset encodings below configurable thresholds, so grouping fields in a hash often saves a lot of memory over one key per field.","**Pub/Sub vs streams:** Pub/Sub drops messages when no subscriber is connected; streams keep entries, track pending messages per consumer group and let `XAUTOCLAIM` take over messages from dead consumers.","**Big and hot keys:** `redis-cli --bigkeys` and `--hotkeys` (the latter needs an LFU eviction policy) find keys that dominate memory or traffic; in a cluster both pin load on one shard."],"redFlags":["Credits the speed to memory alone and ignores single-threaded execution","runs `KEYS` or unbounded O(N) commands in production","does read-modify-write in the application instead of atomic commands"]},{"id":134,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"Your service reads product prices through Redis with cache-aside. Walk through reads and writes, and show where stale prices creep in.","level":"Basic","tldr":"Cache-aside reads the cache, falls back to the database on a miss and populates the cache; writes update the database and then delete the key, which is simple and resilient but always leaves stale windows.","answer":["Read path: `GET` the key; on a hit return it; on a miss load from the database, `SET` it with a TTL and return. Write path: update the database, which stays the source of truth, then invalidate the key. I prefer `DEL` over writing the new value into the cache: two concurrent writers can commit to the database in one order and update the cache in the other, leaving the older value cached, while a delete is idempotent and order-insensitive. The cache only holds data that is actually requested, and if Redis is down the application can still read the database.","The database and the cache are never updated atomically, so stale windows remain. Between the commit and the `DEL`, readers see the old value. If the `DEL` fails (network error, crash after commit), the stale entry lives until its TTL, which is why every entry needs a TTL as the upper bound on staleness. A slow reader can also write an old value back after the invalidation. And invalidation must happen after commit: deleting inside the transaction lets a reader repopulate from the pre-commit state.","Mitigations scale with how much staleness hurts: always set a TTL, delete after commit, add a delayed second delete, store a version and refuse to overwrite with an older one, or drive invalidation from CDC (Debezium reading the WAL) so every committed change, from any writer, evicts reliably. For data where staleness is unacceptable, such as balances or limits, read the database for the decision."],"keyPoints":["the application owns all cache logic; Redis knows nothing about the database","a miss loads from the database and stores the value with a TTL","a write commits to the database first, then deletes the key","the TTL caps how long a stale value can survive"],"examples":[{"title":"Cache-aside with after-commit invalidation","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class ProductCatalog {\n    private static final Duration TTL = Duration.ofMinutes(10);\n    private final RedisTemplate<String, ProductView> redis;\n    private final ProductRepository products;\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;\n    // constructor omitted\n\n    public ProductView get(String sku) {\n        String key = \"product:v1:\" + sku;\n        ProductView cached = redis.opsForValue().get(key);\n        if (cached != null) return cached;                       // hit\n        ProductView fresh = products.findView(sku).orElseThrow();\n        redis.opsForValue().set(key, fresh, TTL);                // TTL bounds staleness\n        return fresh;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void changePrice(String sku, BigDecimal price) {\n        products.updatePrice(sku, price);                        // database is the source of truth\n        events.publishEvent(new ProductChanged(sku));\n    }\n\n    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)\n    void onProductChanged(ProductChanged e) {\n        redis.delete(\"product:v1:\" + e.sku());                   // delete, do not overwrite\n    }\n}","note":"The eviction runs only after the transaction commits, so no reader can repopulate the key from pre-commit data. If the delete fails, the TTL still caps how long the stale value survives."},{"title":"The race that re-caches stale data","lang":"text","code":"t1  Reader A: GET product:v1:42             miss\nt2  Reader A: SELECT price                  returns 100 (old)\nt3  Reader A: long GC pause\nt4  Writer B: UPDATE price = 120; COMMIT\nt5  Writer B: DEL product:v1:42             nothing to delete yet\nt6  Reader A: SET product:v1:42 = 100       stale value cached until the TTL expires","note":"Invalidation happened correctly, yet the cache ends up stale because the reader's write lands after the delete."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why delete the key on a write instead of setting the new value?","a":"Two concurrent writers can commit to the database in one order and update the cache in the other, which leaves the older value cached until the TTL expires. Deleting is idempotent and order-insensitive, and the next reader loads whatever is committed. It also avoids building entries for data nobody reads. The trade-off is one extra miss after each write, which matters only for very hot keys."},{"q":"What happens when Redis restarts empty under full traffic?","a":"Every read misses at once, so database load jumps from the normal miss rate to the full read rate, which can be ten or a hundred times higher. Protect the database with a concurrency limit on cache fills and single-flight per key, prewarm the hottest keys before shifting traffic, and add jitter to TTLs so the refilled keys do not all expire together later."}],"extra":["**Negative caching:** cache \"not found\" results with a short TTL (or check a Bloom filter first) so repeated lookups of nonexistent ids do not all fall through to the database.","**Versioned key prefixes:** putting a version in the key (`product:v1:`) lets a deploy that changes the cached DTO shape switch to `v2` instead of failing to deserialize old entries or flushing the whole cache.","**Spring Cache and transactions:** `@CacheEvict` runs when the method returns, which is still inside the transaction; `RedisCacheManager.builder(factory).transactionAware()` defers puts and evictions until after commit.","**Local single-flight:** `@Cacheable(sync = true)` makes concurrent misses for the same key on one instance wait for a single load, which helps against stampedes but is not a distributed lock."],"redFlags":["updates the cache before the database transaction commits","claims deleting the key removes every chance of stale reads","sets no TTL, so a wrong entry can live forever"]},{"id":135,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"Cache-aside, read-through, write-through and write-behind: how do they differ?","level":"Basic","tldr":"They differ in who loads the cache on a miss and when writes reach the database. Cache-aside and read-through load lazily, by the application or by the cache's own loader; write-through updates database and cache before acknowledging; write-behind acknowledges once the cache has the write and flushes later, trading durability for write speed.","answer":["In cache-aside the application owns both stores: read the cache, load from the database on a miss and populate, and on writes update the database and delete the key. The cache is optional, so an outage degrades to database load; the costs are cache code at every call site and stale windows. Read-through moves the loader into the cache layer (Caffeine `LoadingCache`, a JCache `CacheLoader`, a Hazelcast `MapLoader`), so callers see one API and concurrent misses for a key share one load; over Redis, which has no loader, it is a wrapper in your code.","Write-through sends each write to the database and the cache before acknowledging, so a read right after a write is fresh and hot keys stay warm. The price is the latency of both stores, cached data nobody reads, and no atomicity across two systems: if the commit succeeds and the cache update fails they diverge until expiry, and two concurrent writers can update the cache in the opposite order to their commits. Write the database first and touch the cache only after commit.","Write-behind, or write-back, acknowledges as soon as the cache holds the write and flushes to the database later in batches: the lowest write latency, burst absorption, and a thousand increments of one row become one `UPDATE`. But unflushed writes are lost if the cache node dies, database readers see lagging data, and a constraint violation surfaces after the client heard success. Use it for view counters, last-seen timestamps and telemetry, never for orders or balances. Most services combine cache-aside or read-through with invalidation on write, and use write-through only where read-after-write freshness matters."],"keyPoints":["Cache-aside and read-through load lazily; they differ in who owns the loader","write-through keeps reads fresh but cannot make two stores atomic","write-behind is fast and batched but can lose unflushed writes"],"examples":[{"title":"Read-through with Caffeine and write-through after commit","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class ProductCatalog {\n\n    private final ProductRepository products;\n    private final LoadingCache<Long, ProductView> cache;\n\n    public ProductCatalog(ProductRepository products) {\n        this.products = products;\n        this.cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()   // read-through: the cache owns the loader\n            .maximumSize(50_000)\n            .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(10))   // safety net for missed updates\n            .build(id -> products.findViewById(id).orElse(null));   // null is not stored\n    }\n\n    public ProductView get(long id) {\n        return cache.get(id);   // concurrent misses for one id share a single load\n    }\n\n    // Write-through: database first, cache updated once the commit succeeded\n    @Transactional\n    public ProductView changePrice(long id, BigDecimal newPrice) {\n        Product product = products.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n        product.changePrice(newPrice);\n        ProductView view = ProductView.from(product);\n        TransactionSynchronizationManager.registerSynchronization(new TransactionSynchronization() {\n            @Override\n            public void afterCommit() {\n                cache.put(id, view);   // never publish a value that can still roll back\n            }\n        });\n        return view;\n    }\n}","note":"This local cache only sees writes on this instance; other pods rely on the TTL or an invalidation message. Two concurrent price changes can also commit in one order and `put` in the other."},{"title":"Write-behind view counters: Redis absorbs writes, a batched upsert flushes them","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass ProductViewCounter {\n\n    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;\n    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;\n\n    ProductViewCounter(StringRedisTemplate redis, JdbcTemplate jdbc) {\n        this.redis = redis;\n        this.jdbc = jdbc;\n    }\n\n    void recordView(long productId) {   // acknowledged without touching the database\n        redis.opsForHash().increment(\"views:pending\", Long.toString(productId), 1);\n    }\n\n    @Scheduled(fixedDelay = 10_000)   // on one instance only, for example with ShedLock\n    void flush() {\n        if (Boolean.FALSE.equals(redis.hasKey(\"views:flushing\"))) {   // leftover batch first\n            if (Boolean.FALSE.equals(redis.hasKey(\"views:pending\"))) return;\n            redis.rename(\"views:pending\", \"views:flushing\");   // new views start a fresh hash\n        }\n        Map<Object, Object> batch = redis.opsForHash().entries(\"views:flushing\");\n        jdbc.batchUpdate(\"\"\"\n            INSERT INTO product_stats (product_id, views) VALUES (?, ?)\n            ON CONFLICT (product_id) DO UPDATE SET views = product_stats.views + EXCLUDED.views\n            \"\"\", batch.entrySet(), 500, (ps, e) -> {\n                ps.setLong(1, Long.parseLong((String) e.getKey()));\n                ps.setLong(2, Long.parseLong((String) e.getValue()));\n            });\n        redis.delete(\"views:flushing\");   // crash before this line: batch counted twice\n    }\n}","note":"Two failure windows remain by design: views in Redis vanish if Redis loses data, and a crash between upsert and delete double counts a batch. Fine for view counts, wrong for money."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why do most teams delete the cache key on write instead of updating it?","a":"Deletes are idempotent and order-independent: two concurrent writers that both delete leave an empty key, while two `SET` calls can land in the opposite order to their commits and keep the older value until the TTL. The cost is a miss on the next read, so hot keys need single-flight loading."},{"q":"How would you make write-behind lose nothing?","a":"Not with a cache alone: Redis with `appendfsync everysec` can lose about a second of writes, and failover to an asynchronous replica can lose more. If the data must survive, append each change to a durable log such as Kafka with `acks=all` and apply it idempotently; that is a projection, not a cache."}],"extra":["**Write-around:** writes go straight to the database and only reads fill the cache; cache-aside with delete-on-write is effectively this.","**Refresh-ahead:** Caffeine `refreshAfterWrite` reloads an accessed entry in the background while serving the old value, so hot keys never expire under load.","**Commit before cache:** never update or invalidate a cache inside a transaction that can still roll back; use an after-commit hook or `@TransactionalEventListener`, whose default phase is `AFTER_COMMIT`.","**CDC invalidation:** Debezium reading the PostgreSQL WAL or MySQL binlog can invalidate keys for writes from every path, batch jobs and manual SQL included.","**Local caches are per pod:** a write on one pod leaves the others stale; use short TTLs or broadcast invalidations."],"redFlags":["Proposes write-behind for orders or payments without the loss window","assumes write-through keeps cache and database atomically consistent","cannot say who loads the cache on a miss in each pattern"]},{"id":136,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"Redis caches sessions, product data and feature flags. How would you set expiry for each, and which `maxmemory-policy` would you pick?","level":"Basic","tldr":"TTL is a per-data-type freshness decision (staleness tolerance, change rate, traffic), eviction is what Redis does when memory is full; choose the policy from the workload and watch hit ratio, memory and eviction rate.","answer":["Expiration and eviction are different mechanisms. A TTL (`SET ... EX`, `EXPIRE`) removes a key when its time is up, lazily on access plus an active background sampling cycle, and it expresses freshness. Eviction only starts when `maxmemory` is reached, and `maxmemory-policy` chooses victims regardless of how fresh they are; it expresses capacity.","The TTL follows the freshness tolerance and traffic of each data type: feature flags or FX rates seconds, a product catalog with explicit invalidation 30 minutes or more, sessions a sliding TTL equal to the idle timeout. Shorter TTLs mean fresher data but more misses and database load; longer TTLs raise the hit ratio but also staleness and memory use. With reliable invalidation the TTL is only a safety net and can be long; rarely read keys should get short TTLs so they do not occupy memory. Add jitter either way.","Eviction depends on the workload. `allkeys-lru` suits a general cache with recency-driven access, `allkeys-lfu` a skewed, stable popularity distribution, and `volatile-*` policies evict only keys with a TTL when cache entries share an instance with data that must stay. `noeviction`, the default, rejects writes when memory is full, which is right for locks, queues and primary data. Separate instances for cache and durable data are cleaner than mixing them.","Then monitor hit ratio (`keyspace_hits` against misses, and per key prefix in the application), used memory against `maxmemory`, the rate of `evicted_keys` and `expired_keys`, and fragmentation."],"keyPoints":["expiry is a per-data freshness choice, while eviction is a response to memory pressure","derive each TTL from how stale data may be and how fast it changes","`allkeys-lru` or `allkeys-lfu` suit a pure cache; `volatile-*` evicts only keys with a TTL","watch hit ratio, `evicted_keys` and used memory to see when settings are wrong"],"examples":[{"title":"Per-cache TTLs in Spring Boot","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass CacheTtlConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    RedisCacheManagerBuilderCustomizer cacheTtls() {\n        return builder -> builder\n            .withCacheConfiguration(\"featureFlags\",          // must react quickly to changes\n                RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig().entryTtl(Duration.ofSeconds(30)))\n            .withCacheConfiguration(\"fxRates\",               // provider updates every minute\n                RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig().entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(1)))\n            .withCacheConfiguration(\"productViews\",          // invalidated on change, TTL is a safety net\n                RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig().entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(30)))\n            .withCacheConfiguration(\"merchantProfiles\",      // rarely changes, moderate traffic\n                RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig().entryTtl(Duration.ofHours(6)));\n    }\n}","note":"One TTL for everything is a red flag; each cache name documents its own freshness contract next to the reason for it."},{"title":"Configure eviction and read the signals","lang":"bash","code":"# Cache-only instance: cap memory and evict by access frequency\nredis-cli CONFIG SET maxmemory 6gb\nredis-cli CONFIG SET maxmemory-policy allkeys-lfu\n\n# Hit ratio inputs and churn counters\nredis-cli INFO stats | grep -E 'keyspace_hits|keyspace_misses|evicted_keys|expired_keys'\n\n# Memory pressure and fragmentation\nredis-cli INFO memory | grep -E 'used_memory_human|maxmemory_human|maxmemory_policy|mem_fragmentation_ratio'\n\n# Hot keys (needs an LFU policy) and the biggest keys per type\nredis-cli --hotkeys\nredis-cli --bigkeys\n\n# Remaining TTL and LFU access counter for one key\nredis-cli TTL product:v1:sku-48213\nredis-cli OBJECT FREQ product:v1:sku-48213","note":"Persist the settings in `redis.conf` or your managed service's parameter group; `CONFIG SET` changes are lost on restart unless followed by `CONFIG REWRITE`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does Redis actually remove keys whose TTL has passed?","a":"Two ways: lazily, when a client touches an expired key, and actively, through a background cycle that samples keys with an expiry several times per second (driven by `hz`) and deletes the expired ones, repeating while many samples are expired. Expired keys that nobody reads can therefore occupy memory for a while. Eviction is a separate mechanism that starts only when `maxmemory` is reached."},{"q":"When would `volatile-lru` be preferable to `allkeys-lru`?","a":"When one instance mixes cache entries, which have TTLs, with keys that must never be evicted, such as configuration or counters without TTLs, because volatile policies only evict keys that have an expiry. The catch is that if no key has a TTL, a volatile policy behaves like `noeviction` and writes start failing. Separate instances for cache and persistent data are usually cleaner than relying on this distinction."}],"extra":["**The default is noeviction:** a cache deployed without a policy starts failing writes with OOM errors when full; a Redis that holds locks, queues or sessions must never use an `allkeys-*` policy.","**Approximate algorithms:** Redis LRU and LFU sample `maxmemory-samples` keys (default 5) instead of tracking a global order, and LFU uses a small logarithmic counter that decays over time (`lfu-log-factor`, `lfu-decay-time`).","**volatile-* trap:** with `volatile-lru`, keys without a TTL are never evicted, so if most keys lack one, Redis behaves like `noeviction` and returns OOM errors.","**SET clears the TTL:** overwriting a key with `SET` removes its expiry unless you pass `EX` again or `KEEPTTL` (Redis 6.0+), a common source of keys that silently live forever.","**Sliding versus absolute TTL:** sliding expiry (`GETEX key EX 1800`, Redis 6.2+) fits sessions, but on shared cached data it means a hot key is never refreshed; cached data should use absolute TTLs."],"redFlags":["leaves the default `noeviction` on a cache, so writes fail when memory fills","picks a TTL with no reasoning about staleness or traffic","thinks keys vanish from memory the instant their TTL passes"]},{"id":137,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"A hot product key expires mid-sale and database CPU hits 100%. What happened, and what keeps it from happening again?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A stampede happens when a hot key expires or the cache is cold and many requests miss at once and hammer the database; prevent it with TTL jitter, single-flight or per-key locks, stale-while-revalidate and prewarming.","answer":["A hot key serving 5,000 requests per second expires, and in the same millisecond thousands of requests miss and run the same expensive query. The database saturates, latency climbs, requests time out and clients retry, so the recomputation that would fix the problem never completes. The same happens when many keys were written together with the same TTL, after a cache restart or flush, or when a deploy changes the key format.","The tools, usually combined: **TTL jitter** (for example plus or minus 10%) spreads expirations of keys written together. **Single-flight** collapses concurrent misses for one key into one load per instance (Caffeine `LoadingCache`, `@Cacheable(sync = true)`). A **per-key distributed lock** (`SET lock:key token NX PX 5000`) lets one instance recompute across the cluster. **Stale-while-revalidate** stores a soft expiry inside the value with a longer physical TTL, serves the stale value and refreshes once in the background. **Prewarming** loads known hot keys before traffic arrives.","Locks need failure handling: always an expiry, so a crashed holder does not block the key forever; waiters with a short deadline that then serve stale data or fail fast; and a bounded fallback when Redis itself is unavailable. A global lock or unbounded waiting just moves the outage.","Also protect the source directly with a concurrency limit on loaders, and alert when the miss rate spikes in step with expirations."],"keyPoints":["one expiry turns into thousands of identical database queries at once","randomize expiry so keys loaded together do not expire together","let one caller rebuild a key while others wait or get the old value","warm hot keys before traffic arrives, for example after a deploy"],"examples":[{"title":"Soft TTL, per-key lock and serve-stale","lang":"java","code":"public ProductView get(String sku) {\n    String key = \"product:\" + sku;\n    CachedValue<ProductView> cv = cache.get(key);                  // value plus softExpiresAt\n    if (cv != null && cv.softExpiresAt().isAfter(Instant.now())) {\n        return cv.value();                                         // fresh hit\n    }\n    String lockKey = \"lock:\" + key;\n    String token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();\n    Boolean acquired = redis.opsForValue()\n            .setIfAbsent(lockKey, token, Duration.ofSeconds(5));  // SET NX PX, always with expiry\n    if (Boolean.TRUE.equals(acquired)) {\n        try {\n            ProductView fresh = products.findView(sku).orElseThrow();\n            Duration soft = Duration.ofSeconds(240 + ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(60)); // jitter\n            cache.put(key, fresh, soft, soft.plusMinutes(10));     // physical TTL outlives soft TTL\n            return fresh;\n        } finally {\n            releaseIfOwner(lockKey, token);                        // compare-and-delete in Lua\n        }\n    }\n    if (cv != null) return cv.value();                             // another node refreshes: serve stale\n    return loadWithConcurrencyLimit(sku);                          // cold miss: semaphore-bounded DB load\n}","note":"Only the lock winner hits the database; everyone else gets the stale value immediately. The cold-miss path is still bounded, so a lost lock or a Redis failure cannot become a stampede."},{"title":"In-process single-flight and refresh-ahead with Caffeine","lang":"java","code":"LoadingCache<String, ProductView> local = Caffeine.newBuilder()\n        .maximumSize(50_000)\n        .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(10))\n        .refreshAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(4))   // old value served while one async reload runs\n        .build(sku -> products.findView(sku).orElse(null));\n\n// Concurrent get() calls for the same missing key share a single load\nProductView view = local.get(\"sku-48213\");","note":"`refreshAfterWrite` is stale-while-revalidate for a local cache, and the loading cache gives single-flight per key per JVM for free."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does single-flight work inside one JVM, and what does it leave uncovered?","a":"Concurrent misses for the same key share one in-flight load, typically a `CompletableFuture` stored with `computeIfAbsent` in a `ConcurrentHashMap` and removed when it completes; Caffeine's `AsyncLoadingCache` gives you this out of the box. It deduplicates only within one instance, so 40 pods can still send 40 loads for the same key. That is usually acceptable; if not, combine it with serving the old value during refresh or a short distributed lock."},{"q":"What is probabilistic early expiration?","a":"Each read may decide to refresh a key before it expires, with a probability that rises as expiry approaches and with how long the value takes to recompute, as in the XFetch approach. Under heavy traffic one request almost always refreshes slightly early, so the key never lapses under load and no locks are needed. It requires storing the recompute time and expiry alongside the value."}],"extra":["**Probabilistic early expiration:** with XFetch, each reader recomputes early with a probability that rises as expiry approaches and scales with recompute cost, so usually one request refreshes before expiry and no lock is needed.","**Hot keys also overload a shard:** one very hot key lands on a single Redis node; a short-TTL local near-cache in front of Redis, or copies of the key under several suffixes, spreads that load.","**Cold starts are self-inflicted:** `FLUSHALL` during an incident or a changed key prefix in a deploy creates a fleet-wide cold cache, so roll out new key versions gradually and prewarm the top keys from access logs.","**Signature in metrics:** a stampede looks like a sawtooth of miss rate and database QPS aligned with the TTL; track misses per key prefix, loader concurrency and loader latency."],"redFlags":["blames the database and proposes only scaling it up","adds a per-key lock with no timeout, so a crashed holder blocks everyone","forgets that a cold cache after a restart causes the same surge"]},{"id":138,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"Two workers both believe they hold the same Redis lock. How can that happen, and what limits the damage?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Treat a Redis lock as a time-limited lease: a GC pause, a network split or a failover can leave two clients each believing they hold it, so release only after checking your own token, and protect critical writes with fencing numbers that the resource itself validates.","answer":["The minimum correct pattern is `SET lock:settlement 9b1d4e7a NX PX 30000`, where the value is a random per-owner token: one atomic command that acquires only if absent and always sets an expiry. `SETNX` followed by `EXPIRE` is two commands, and a crash between them leaves a lock that never expires. Release must be compare-and-delete in a Lua script (delete only if the value is still my token); a plain `DEL` can remove a lock another client acquired after mine expired.","Even done right, a lease can have **multiple owners**. The holder can pause (GC, VM freeze, a slow database call) past the lease; the lock expires, another client acquires it, and the first one resumes and writes, still believing it owns the lock. Watchdog renewal shrinks the window but a paused or partitioned process cannot renew or notice. Replication is asynchronous, so a failover can promote a replica that never saw the lock. Redlock (majority of independent masters) addresses failover but still relies on timing assumptions.","Where correctness matters, add **fencing**: every acquisition returns a monotonically increasing token, every write to the protected resource carries it, and the resource rejects tokens older than the highest it has seen. A stale owner then fails harmlessly, but the resource has to take part in the check.","So I use Redis locks for **efficiency** (one node runs a job, avoid duplicate recomputation), where an occasional double run is tolerable. For money, stock or uniqueness, the database enforces correctness with constraints, row locks or conditional updates."],"keyPoints":["the lock is a lease with a timer, not real ownership","a stalled holder can wake after expiry and keep writing","release with a script that deletes only if the stored token matches","the protected resource must reject writes carrying stale fencing numbers"],"examples":[{"title":"SETNX without expiry versus token plus compare-and-delete","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: two commands (a crash between them leaves the lock forever) and an unconditional delete\nredis.opsForValue().setIfAbsent(\"lock:settlement\", \"1\");\nredis.expire(\"lock:settlement\", Duration.ofSeconds(30));\nrunSettlement();\nredis.delete(\"lock:settlement\");                      // may delete a lock another node now holds\n\n// GOOD: atomic SET NX PX with an owner token, release only if we still own it\nprivate static final RedisScript<Long> RELEASE = RedisScript.of(\"\"\"\n        if redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then\n          return redis.call('DEL', KEYS[1])\n        end\n        return 0\n        \"\"\", Long.class);\n\nString token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();\nBoolean acquired = redis.opsForValue()\n        .setIfAbsent(\"lock:settlement\", token, Duration.ofSeconds(30));\nif (Boolean.TRUE.equals(acquired)) {\n    try {\n        runSettlement();                              // must finish well within the lease\n    } finally {\n        redis.execute(RELEASE, List.of(\"lock:settlement\"), token);\n    }\n}","note":"The GOOD version fixes the two classic bugs, a lock without expiry and deleting someone else's lock, but it still cannot stop a paused holder from acting after its lease expired."},{"title":"Fencing enforced by the resource","lang":"sql","code":"-- Each lease comes with a monotonically increasing fencing token,\n-- ideally from a linearizable source such as a database sequence.\nCREATE SEQUENCE settlement_fencing_seq;\n\nCREATE TABLE settlement_state (\n    merchant_id    bigint PRIMARY KEY,\n    last_token     bigint      NOT NULL,\n    settled_until  timestamptz NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- Every write carries the holder's token; an older token is rejected\nUPDATE settlement_state\nSET settled_until = $2,\n    last_token    = $3\nWHERE merchant_id = $1\n  AND last_token < $3;\n-- 0 rows updated: this holder's lease is stale, abort without side effects","note":"Client A with token 33 pauses, client B gets 34 and writes; when A resumes, its write with 33 updates zero rows. Safety no longer depends on timing."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does correct acquire and release look like in Redis?","a":"Acquire with a single `SET lock:resource <random-token> NX PX 30000`, so the key and its expiry are created atomically. Release with a Lua script that deletes the key only if its value still equals your token, so you never remove a lock that expired and was taken by another client. If work can outlast the lease, extend it with a similar compare-and-`PEXPIRE` script, knowing the extension itself can arrive too late after a pause."},{"q":"Why can a Redis failover hand the same lock to two clients?","a":"Replication is asynchronous: the primary can acknowledge the `SET`, crash before the replica receives it, and the promoted replica has no lock key, so the next client acquires it. Redlock spreads the lock over a majority of independent nodes to reduce this, but it still depends on bounded clock drift and pauses, which is why Martin Kleppmann argued it is unsafe for correctness. When correctness matters, rely on fencing or on the database."}],"extra":["**Redisson watchdog:** an `RLock` acquired without an explicit lease time is renewed periodically while the JVM is alive (default watchdog timeout 30 seconds); passing a `leaseTime` disables the watchdog, and neither mode protects against a paused or partitioned holder.","**Fencing source must be consistent:** a Redis `INCR` counter can go backwards after an asynchronous failover, so take fencing tokens from something linearizable such as a PostgreSQL sequence, an etcd revision or a ZooKeeper zxid.","**Redlock debate:** Martin Kleppmann argued Redlock depends on bounded clock drift and pauses and provides no fencing; Salvatore Sanfilippo disputed parts of that. The practical takeaway is the same: fine for efficiency, not a substitute for fencing.","**Scheduled jobs:** ShedLock (`@SchedulerLock(name = \"settlement\", lockAtMostFor = \"PT10M\")`) with a JDBC or Redis provider is the usual Spring answer; `lockAtMostFor` must exceed the worst-case run time and the job should still be idempotent.","**Checking ownership is still racy:** reading the lock value before a write is a time-of-check to time-of-use race; only the resource-side check makes a stale holder harmless."],"redFlags":["assumes Redlock or replication makes the lock safe under any timing","sets a lease far longer than the work and calls the problem solved","never asks whether the guarded resource can check a token"]},{"id":139,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"What are the pitfalls of Spring's @Cacheable in production?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"`@Cacheable` is proxy advice, so self-invocation skips it, and the default key uses all parameters but not the method name. It sets no TTL or size limit, caches nulls by default, can break across deployments through serialization, shares mutable entities, and `@CacheEvict` on a transactional method can run before commit.","answer":["`@Cacheable` works through a proxy that computes a key, returns a hit or calls the method and stores the result, so calls that never cross the proxy, such as `this.findProduct(id)` inside the same bean, are never cached. The default `SimpleKeyGenerator` uses the sole argument as the key, or a `SimpleKey` of all arguments, never the method name. A parameter that changes per call, like a request context, or lacks value-based `equals` and `hashCode` makes keys that never match, and two methods sharing a cache name with equal arguments collide. Name the key in SpEL: `key = \"#sku + ':' + #currency\"`.","Lifetime and size come from the cache manager. With no provider, Spring Boot falls back to a `ConcurrentMapCacheManager` with no expiry or bound, and Redis entries never expire unless you set `entryTtl` or `spring.cache.redis.time-to-live`. Nulls are cached by default, `Optional.empty()` included, so a not-found result sticks after the row is created; add `unless = \"#result == null\"`. Spring Data Redis uses JDK serialization by default, so changing a class breaks old entries, and in a rolling deploy old and new pods read each other's entries. Version the key prefix and treat read errors as misses.","What you cache matters too. A local cache hands the same object to every caller, so one request mutating a cached JPA entity corrupts everyone's copy, and its lazy fields throw `LazyInitializationException` later; cache immutable records or DTOs. `@CacheEvict` runs when the method returns, which on a `@Transactional` method can precede the commit, depending on advisor order or an outer transaction. A concurrent reader then misses, reads the old committed row and caches it again. A transaction-aware cache manager defers puts and evictions until after commit; keep a TTL as a safety net."],"keyPoints":["Proxy-based: self-invocation bypasses it; default keys ignore the method name","TTL, size and null handling come from the cache manager, not the annotation","cache immutable DTOs and evict after commit"],"examples":[{"title":"Common @Cacheable mistakes and their fixes","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class PricingService {\n\n    private final ProductRepository products;   // constructor omitted\n    private final QuoteEngine engine;\n\n    // BAD: caches a mutable JPA entity, and a null for ids that do not exist yet\n    @Cacheable(\"products\")\n    public Optional<Product> findProduct(Long id) {\n        return products.findById(id);\n    }\n\n    // BAD: this.findProduct skips the proxy, so every id goes to the database\n    public List<Product> findProducts(List<Long> ids) {\n        return ids.stream().map(this::findProduct).flatMap(Optional::stream).toList();\n    }\n\n    // BAD: key is SimpleKey(sku, currency, ctx); ctx carries a request id, so nothing hits\n    @Cacheable(\"quotes\")\n    public Quote quote(String sku, String currency, RequestContext ctx) {\n        return engine.quote(sku, currency, ctx);\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: immutable record, explicit key, misses not cached\n    @Cacheable(cacheNames = \"quote-views\", key = \"#sku + ':' + #currency\", unless = \"#result == null\")\n    public QuoteView quoteView(String sku, String currency, RequestContext ctx) {\n        return engine.quoteView(sku, currency);\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: with a transaction-aware cache manager, the eviction waits for the commit\n    @Transactional\n    @CacheEvict(cacheNames = \"quote-views\", allEntries = true)\n    public void changeListPrice(long productId, BigDecimal price) {\n        products.findById(productId).orElseThrow().changeListPrice(price);\n    }\n}","note":"`allEntries = true` is blunt but correct when one price feeds many keys; evict single keys only when the method knows exactly which changed."},{"title":"Redis cache manager with TTLs, versioned keys and after-commit eviction","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\n@EnableCaching\nclass CacheConfig implements CachingConfigurer {\n\n    @Bean\n    RedisCacheManager cacheManager(RedisConnectionFactory connections, RedisSerializer<Object> json) {\n        RedisCacheConfiguration defaults = RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig()\n            .entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(10))          // without this, entries never expire\n            .prefixCacheNameWith(\"v3:\")                // bump on incompatible class changes\n            .serializeValuesWith(SerializationPair.fromSerializer(json));   // not JDK serialization\n        return RedisCacheManager.builder(connections)\n            .cacheDefaults(defaults)\n            .withCacheConfiguration(\"quote-views\", defaults.entryTtl(Duration.ofSeconds(60)))\n            .transactionAware()                        // puts and evictions wait for commit\n            .build();\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public CacheErrorHandler errorHandler() {\n        return new SimpleCacheErrorHandler() {         // the default rethrows everything\n            @Override\n            public void handleCacheGetError(RuntimeException e, Cache cache, Object key) {\n                // Entry written by an older class version: miss, then the put overwrites it\n            }\n        };   // put and evict errors still propagate\n    }\n}","note":"The error handler turns deserialization failures after a deploy into misses instead of 500s; log them in real code. Keep Redis timeouts short, or a slow Redis adds its timeout to every cached call."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you stop many concurrent misses from loading the same key?","a":"`@Cacheable(sync = true)` makes concurrent callers for one key wait for a single invocation, but only within one JVM, so ten pods can still trigger ten loads; it allows one cache name and no `unless`. Across instances use stale-while-revalidate or a short lock around the load."},{"q":"How do you test that caching really works?","a":"Use a Spring context so the proxy exists, replace the dependency behind the cached method with `@MockitoBean`, call twice and verify one invocation, then call the evicting method and verify a reload. Run it against real Redis with Testcontainers, because serialization and TTL problems never show up in an in-memory cache."}],"extra":["**Parameter names in SpEL:** `#sku` needs the `-parameters` compiler flag (Spring Boot's build plugins set it), since Spring Framework 6.1 no longer reads debug information; `#p0` always works.","**condition vs unless:** `condition` is evaluated before the call and can bypass the cache, `unless` after it with access to `#result` (the value inside an `Optional`).","**Disallowing nulls:** with `disableCachingNullValues()` a Redis cache throws on a null result, so you still need `unless`.","**Exceptions are not cached:** a failing method runs again on every request, so shielding a broken dependency is a circuit breaker's job.","**Hit ratio metrics:** Spring Boot exposes caches known at startup as Micrometer `cache.gets` tagged hit or miss; Caffeine needs `recordStats`, Redis `spring.cache.redis.enable-statistics=true`."],"redFlags":["Expects `@Cacheable` to work on calls from inside the same class","caches JPA entities and assumes the annotation sets an expiry","evicts inside a transaction without considering commit order"]},{"id":140,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"Redis goes down at peak traffic. How do you decide, feature by feature, whether to keep serving or refuse requests?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"It depends on what Redis does for each feature: optional caches degrade to the database behind a concurrency limit, while sessions, rate limits, locks and Redis-held primary data each need an explicit fail-open or fail-closed decision.","answer":["Start with an inventory of Redis roles. A **cache** holds derived, rebuildable data, so the feature degrades: bypass to the database, but with protection. **Sessions** hold user state, so users are logged out or cannot log in unless sessions can fall back to another store. **Rate limiters** fail open (allow traffic, perhaps with a local limiter) when availability matters more, and fail closed for abuse and cost protection such as login attempts or OTP sends. **Locks** that only prevent duplicate work can skip a run; locks guarding correctness must fail closed unless the database enforces it anyway. **Primary data** in Redis (queues, carts, leaderboards) is simply unavailable.","The deciding question per feature is the cost of a wrong answer against the cost of no answer. A payment idempotency check stored only in Redis must fail closed (or move to the database); a recommendations widget should just disappear.","Degradation also needs mechanics: short client timeouts so every request does not wait for the default, a circuit breaker so a dead Redis is not retried per request, a concurrency limit on the database fallback, a small local cache for hot keys, and feature flags to shed optional work. Keep optional dependencies out of liveness and readiness probes.","I write the per-feature decision into the runbook and test it by killing Redis in staging, including the recovery, since Redis returns empty and everything misses at once."],"keyPoints":["classify each Redis use: cache, session store, rate limiter, lock, or system of record","a cache can fall back to the database only behind a concurrency cap","rate limiters and locks need an explicit fail-open or fail-closed choice","decide and rehearse these behaviors before the outage, not during it"],"examples":[{"title":"Per-feature outage behavior","lang":"text","code":"Redis role                      On outage                               Why\nProduct and price cache         degrade: DB behind a concurrency limit  cache is derived, DB is the truth\nHTTP sessions (Spring Session)  fail: users must log in again           no other copy of session state\nLogin and OTP rate limiter      fail closed                             abuse, fraud and SMS cost risk\nPartner API quota limiter       fail open with a local token bucket     availability matters more\nScheduler lock (ShedLock)       skip this run, or JDBC lock provider    duplicate batch run is costly\nPayment idempotency keys        fail closed, or keep them in the DB     a double charge is unacceptable\nLeaderboard (primary data)      feature unavailable, 503                nothing to fall back to","note":"Each row is a product decision as much as a technical one, and it belongs in the runbook before the incident, not during it."},{"title":"Cache bypass that cannot stampede the database","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class PriceService {\n    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;\n    private final PriceRepository prices;\n    private final CircuitBreaker redisBreaker;                   // Resilience4j\n    private final Semaphore dbPermits = new Semaphore(20);       // sized from DB capacity per instance\n    // constructor and cachePut (same breaker, errors swallowed) omitted\n\n    public Price get(String sku) {\n        String key = \"price:\" + sku;\n        Optional<String> cached = cacheGet(key);\n        if (cached.isPresent()) return Price.parse(cached.get());\n        if (!dbPermits.tryAcquire()) {                           // shed load instead of queueing\n            throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE, \"price lookup shed\");\n        }\n        try {\n            Price p = prices.find(sku);\n            cachePut(key, p.serialize());                        // best effort, failures only counted\n            return p;\n        } finally {\n            dbPermits.release();\n        }\n    }\n\n    private Optional<String> cacheGet(String key) {\n        try {\n            return Optional.ofNullable(redisBreaker.executeSupplier(() -> redis.opsForValue().get(key)));\n        } catch (Exception e) {                                  // Redis down or breaker open\n            return Optional.empty();\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The breaker makes a dead Redis cost microseconds instead of a timeout per request, and the semaphore caps concurrent database loads whether the miss comes from an outage or a cold key."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Should a Redis-backed rate limiter fail open or closed during an outage?","a":"It depends on what the limit protects. For abuse and cost controls on login, OTP or payment endpoints, fail closed or fall back to a coarse in-memory limiter per instance, roughly the global limit divided by the instance count. For fairness limits on ordinary reads, failing open with that local limiter usually serves users better. Either way, emit a metric and alert, because the system is running without its normal protection."},{"q":"How do you stop slow Redis calls from exhausting request threads?","a":"Give the Redis client short command and connect timeouts, tens of milliseconds for cache reads rather than defaults measured in seconds. Wrap calls in a circuit breaker so that once Redis is clearly down, requests skip it immediately instead of each waiting for a timeout. Treat a cache error like a miss for optional data, and cap the concurrency of fallback work so the database is not the next thing to fall over."}],"extra":["**Timeouts first:** client defaults are usually far too long for a cache; set `spring.data.redis.timeout` and `spring.data.redis.connect-timeout` to a few hundred milliseconds at most, or every request waits the full timeout before the fallback runs.","**Health probes:** the aggregate `/actuator/health` includes the Redis indicator, so a platform probing it marks every pod down during a cache outage; point Kubernetes at `/actuator/health/liveness` and `/actuator/health/readiness`, and add Redis to readiness only if the service truly cannot work without it.","**Local near-cache:** a small Caffeine cache with a short TTL in front of Redis absorbs hot keys during an outage and saves round trips normally, at the price of per-instance staleness.","**Degraded rate limiting:** a local token bucket at roughly the global limit divided by the instance count keeps approximate protection while Redis is gone.","**Recovery is a second incident:** Redis comes back empty, so keep the database limiter in place, prewarm the top keys and use TTL jitter until the hit ratio recovers."],"redFlags":["lets every request fall through to the database with no limit","has no circuit breaker, so each request waits out a Redis timeout","treats sessions as disposable without noting that every user gets logged out"]},{"id":141,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"A team wants to keep shopping carts only in Redis. What can a crash or failover lose under each persistence setting?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"RDB snapshots can lose everything since the last snapshot, AOF with `appendfsync everysec` can lose about a second, and asynchronous replication means a failover can still drop writes that clients saw acknowledged.","answer":["**RDB** writes point-in-time snapshots by forking the process (`BGSAVE`) on a schedule such as `save 3600 1 300 100 60 10000`. Files are compact and restarts fast, which makes RDB good for backups, but a crash loses every write since the last snapshot, potentially minutes. On large datasets the fork itself can stall the server, and copy-on-write can nearly double memory under heavy writes.","**AOF** appends every write command to a log, and `appendfsync` sets the durability: `always` fsyncs on every write (most durable, slowest), `everysec` (the default) can lose about one second on a crash or power loss, and `no` leaves flushing to the OS. The log is compacted by rewrites, and since Redis 7 it is a multi-part AOF with an RDB base plus incremental files. The common setup is AOF `everysec` plus periodic RDB snapshots for backups.","Persistence is per node, and **replication is asynchronous**: the primary acknowledges the client before replicas receive the write. If it fails right then, Sentinel or Cluster promotes a replica without that write, and an acknowledged write is gone. `WAIT` narrows the window by blocking until N replicas acknowledge, `WAITAOF` (7.2+) until the write is fsynced, and `min-replicas-to-write` stops an isolated primary from accepting writes, but none of this turns Redis into a consensus system.","So expect \"durable enough for caches, sessions, rate limits and rebuildable data\", and never treat replicas as backups or Redis as a ledger."],"keyPoints":["snapshots are point-in-time, so a crash drops writes since the last one","`appendfsync everysec` risks about a second; `always` is safer but slow","replication is asynchronous, so a promoted replica may miss acked writes","`WAIT` narrows that gap but does not make Redis strongly consistent"],"examples":[{"title":"Persistence and replication safety settings","lang":"properties","code":"# RDB: snapshot after 3600 s if 1 change, 300 s if 100 changes, 60 s if 10000 changes\nsave 3600 1 300 100 60 10000\n\n# AOF with fsync once per second: a crash loses about the last second\nappendonly yes\nappendfsync everysec\naof-use-rdb-preamble yes\nauto-aof-rewrite-percentage 100\nauto-aof-rewrite-min-size 64mb\n\n# Stop accepting writes when fewer than 1 replica is connected with lag of 10 s or less\nmin-replicas-to-write 1\nmin-replicas-max-lag 10","note":"These are `redis.conf` directives. `min-replicas-to-write` limits how long an isolated primary keeps accepting writes that will be lost, at the cost of rejecting writes when replicas are down."},{"title":"WAIT and WAITAOF on the same connection","lang":"bash","code":"# WAIT applies to writes made on the same connection, so send both in one redis-cli session\nredis-cli <<'EOF'\nSET payment:intent:9f2c AUTHORIZED\nWAIT 1 100\nEOF\n# WAIT returns how many replicas acknowledged within 100 ms (0 means only the primary has it)\n\n# Redis 7.2+: wait until the write is fsynced to the local AOF and to 1 replica's AOF\nredis-cli <<'EOF'\nSET payment:intent:9f2c CAPTURED\nWAITAOF 1 1 100\nEOF\n\n# Persistence and replication health\nredis-cli INFO persistence | grep -E 'rdb_last_bgsave_status|aof_enabled|aof_last_write_status'\nredis-cli INFO replication | grep -E 'role|connected_slaves|master_repl_offset'","note":"A timeout does not roll the write back: the value stays on the primary even if `WAIT` returns 0, so the caller only learns that durability is uncertain."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does the `WAIT` command buy you, and what does it not?","a":"`WAIT numreplicas timeout` blocks the client until its earlier writes are acknowledged by that many replicas, which shrinks the window where a failover loses them. It does not make Redis strongly consistent: replicas acknowledge receipt rather than disk persistence (Redis 7.2 added `WAITAOF` for that), a failover can still promote a replica that missed the write, and on timeout the write is not rolled back. Use it to reduce loss, not to build a ledger."},{"q":"How do RDB and AOF affect how long a restart takes?","a":"Loading an RDB file is fast because it is a compact binary snapshot, while a plain AOF replays every logged command and slows down as the file grows until it is rewritten. With `aof-use-rdb-preamble` enabled, the default in modern versions, a rewritten AOF starts with an RDB snapshot followed by recent commands, so restarts approach RDB speed with AOF's smaller loss window. Large datasets still take noticeable time to load, which matters for failover planning."}],"extra":["**Replicas are not backups:** `FLUSHALL`, a bad deploy deleting keys, or a buggy script replicate to every replica within milliseconds; keep off-box RDB snapshots with retention and test restores.","**Cluster split brain:** during a partition, a primary on the minority side keeps accepting writes until `cluster-node-timeout` expires, and those writes are discarded when it rejoins as a replica.","**Persistence off plus auto-restart:** if a primary without persistence restarts automatically, it comes back empty and its replicas resync to the empty dataset, wiping every copy; the Redis docs explicitly warn against that combination.","**Fork headroom:** leave memory headroom for copy-on-write during `BGSAVE` and AOF rewrites, and disable Transparent Huge Pages, which Redis recommends because they make copy-on-write far more expensive.","**Managed variants differ:** some managed Redis-compatible services add a durable multi-zone transaction log, so read the provider's actual guarantees instead of assuming open-source behavior."],"redFlags":["says turning on AOF means nothing can ever be lost","ignores the window between primary acknowledgement and replication","keeps the only copy of business data in Redis with no way to rebuild it"]},{"id":142,"category":"REST API Design","question":"What makes an API RESTful, and which HTTP methods are safe and which are idempotent?","level":"Basic","tldr":"REST is an architectural style: clients work with resources through representations over a uniform interface, each request is stateless and self-contained, responses state their cacheability, and in Fielding's full definition hypermedia links drive the client (HATEOAS). `GET`, `HEAD` and `OPTIONS` are safe; those plus `PUT` and `DELETE` are idempotent; `POST` and `PATCH` are neither by default.","answer":["Roy Fielding defined REST as constraints, not a URL style: client-server separation, statelessness, cacheability, a layered system (gateways and CDNs can sit in between unnoticed), optional code on demand, and a uniform interface. The uniform interface is the core: resources are identified by URIs (`/orders/8f3c`), clients change them by exchanging representations negotiated through `Accept` and `Content-Type`, and methods, status codes and headers mean the same everywhere. Statelessness means each request carries everything needed to process it, typically a token and the resource id; resource state lives in the database, not conversation state in server memory, so any replica can serve any request.","HATEOAS is the constraint most APIs skip: responses include links to the actions currently allowed (`cancel` only while the order is open), so clients follow links instead of hardcoding URLs and workflow rules. On the Richardson Maturity Model most production APIs are level 2 (resources plus correct verbs and status codes), which is what most interviewers mean by RESTful; claim level 3 only if you ship links, for example with Spring HATEOAS. Hypermedia buys evolvability for clients you do not control.","Safe and idempotent are defined in RFC 9110, and infrastructure depends on them. Safe means the client requests no state change, so crawlers, prefetchers and caches call `GET` freely; a `GET /orders/42/cancel` will eventually be triggered by something that is not a user. Idempotent means N identical requests have the same intended effect on server state as one, which is why clients, proxies and HTTP libraries retry them after a timeout. The response may differ (a second `DELETE` can return 404) and the server may still log the call; the resource state must not differ. `POST` creates or triggers, so a retry can create a second order unless you add an idempotency key, and `PATCH` depends on the patch document."],"keyPoints":["resources, representations and a uniform interface over stateless requests","safe: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS; idempotent: those plus PUT and DELETE","POST and PATCH need explicit design to be retry-safe"],"examples":[{"title":"HTTP method semantics at a glance","lang":"text","code":"Method   Safe  Idempotent  Typical use                         Retry after timeout?\n-------  ----  ----------  ----------------------------------  ------------------------------\nGET      yes   yes         read a resource or collection       yes\nHEAD     yes   yes         headers only (ETag, size, exists?)  yes\nOPTIONS  yes   yes         allowed methods, CORS preflight     yes\nPUT      no    yes         replace or create at a known URI    yes\nDELETE   no    yes         remove a resource                   yes (204 or 404 the 2nd time)\nPOST     no    no          create in a collection, run action  only with an Idempotency-Key\nPATCH    no    no*         partial update                      only if the patch is idempotent\n\n* JSON Merge Patch that sets fields ({\"status\": \"SHIPPED\"}) is idempotent in practice;\n  a JSON Patch \"add\" to an array is not.\n\nSame request sent twice, server state afterwards:\n  PUT    /accounts/7/limits {\"daily\": 5000}   -> limit is 5000, same as once\n  DELETE /payment-methods/91                  -> card 91 is gone, same as once\n  POST   /payments {\"amount\": 100}            -> two payments, different from once","note":"Idempotency is judged by server state after the calls, not by identical responses."},{"title":"Mapping the semantics onto a Spring controller","lang":"java","code":"@RestController\n@RequestMapping(\"/api/orders\")\nclass OrderController {\n\n    private final OrderService orders;\n\n    OrderController(OrderService orders) { this.orders = orders; }\n\n    @GetMapping(\"/{id}\")                    // safe: caching and retries are harmless\n    OrderResponse get(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n        return OrderResponse.from(orders.find(id));\n    }\n\n    @DeleteMapping(\"/{id}\")                 // idempotent: a missing order is still gone\n    ResponseEntity<Void> delete(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n        orders.deleteIfExists(id);\n        return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();\n    }\n\n    @PostMapping                            // not idempotent: deduplicate on a client key\n    ResponseEntity<OrderResponse> create(@RequestHeader(\"Idempotency-Key\") String key,\n                                         @Valid @RequestBody CreateOrder cmd) {\n        Order order = orders.createOnce(key, cmd);   // unique (client_id, idempotency_key)\n        return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create(\"/api/orders/\" + order.id()))\n                .body(OrderResponse.from(order));\n    }\n\n    // BAD: GET /api/orders/{id}/cancel, a crawler or link prefetch cancels orders\n    @PostMapping(\"/{id}/cancellation\")      // GOOD: state change behind a non-safe method\n    OrderResponse cancel(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n        return OrderResponse.from(orders.cancel(id));\n    }\n}","note":"Returning 204 for every `DELETE` and returning 204 then 404 are both idempotent, because the state is the same. Creation is where retries hurt, so the key plus a database uniqueness constraint do the deduplication."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Is a DELETE that returns 404 on the second call still idempotent?","a":"Yes. Idempotency concerns the intended effect on server state, and after one call or two the resource is gone. Both 204 every time and 204 then 404 are acceptable; document the choice and have clients treat a 404 on a retried delete as success."},{"q":"What does stateless mean if the server has a database?","a":"It is about session state between requests, not resource state. The order lives in the database; what must not live on one server is 'this client is on step 3 of checkout' in an in-memory session. Each request carries its credentials and identifiers, so any replica can serve it and no sticky sessions are needed."}],"extra":["**Silent retries:** clients, proxies and meshes retry idempotent requests after connection failures; a `PUT` that appends instead of replacing turns those retries into duplicates.","**Safe is not side-effect free:** a `GET` may write an access log or warm a cache; safe means the client did not request a state change.","**Cacheable methods:** `GET` and `HEAD` responses are cacheable by default; `POST` responses only with explicit freshness and a matching `Content-Location`, which caches rarely support.","**405 vs 404:** when the path exists but the method does not, return 405 with an `Allow` header; Spring MVC does this for you.","**HATEOAS in practice:** Spring HATEOAS renders HAL `_links` with `EntityModel`; worth it for public, long-lived APIs, overhead for one internal consumer."],"redFlags":["Equates REST with JSON over HTTP or with pretty URLs","confuses safe with idempotent, or claims POST is idempotent","puts state changes behind GET"]},{"id":143,"category":"REST API Design","question":"A client sends `PUT /users/42` with only an email field. What should the server do, and when is PATCH the better fit?","level":"Basic","tldr":"PUT replaces the whole representation and is idempotent by definition; PATCH applies a partial change whose idempotency depends on the patch format, so you must define exactly how omitted and null fields behave.","answer":["`PUT` means \"store this representation at this URI\": the body is the complete new state, and any field the client omits is reset to its default or removed. RFC 9110 defines PUT as idempotent, so sending the same PUT twice leaves the resource in the same state, which makes it safe to retry after a timeout. PUT can also create the resource when the client controls the identifier.","`PATCH` (RFC 5789) sends a set of changes, and its semantics come from the patch media type. It is not guaranteed idempotent, but it is not always non-idempotent either: a JSON Merge Patch setting `status` to `SUSPENDED` is idempotent, while a JSON Patch `add` to the end of an array or an \"increment balance\" operation is not. The key design decision is omitted versus null: with Merge Patch an absent field means \"leave unchanged\" and an explicit `null` means \"clear it\", so your request model must be able to tell the two apart.","Use PUT when clients own the full document (settings, configuration) and PATCH for sparse updates of large resources such as a customer profile. Never use PUT as an undocumented partial update: a client that sends only `email` silently wipes `phone`. Pair both with optimistic concurrency (`ETag` plus `If-Match`) so concurrent writers get `412 Precondition Failed` instead of lost updates."],"keyPoints":["a PUT carries the full new state, so missing fields are cleared or rejected","sending the same PUT again always leaves the same resource","a PATCH carries a change description whose format decides retry safety","the API must document what absent fields and explicit nulls mean"],"examples":[{"title":"Same intent, PUT versus JSON Merge Patch","lang":"http","code":"PUT /customers/c-1042 HTTP/1.1\nContent-Type: application/json\nIf-Match: \"v7\"\n\n{ \"name\": \"Sara Lind\", \"email\": \"sara@example.com\", \"phone\": null, \"segment\": \"RETAIL\" }\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nETag: \"v8\"\n\nPATCH /customers/c-1042 HTTP/1.1\nContent-Type: application/merge-patch+json\nIf-Match: \"v8\"\n\n{ \"email\": \"sara.lind@example.com\", \"phone\": null }\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nETag: \"v9\"","note":"The PUT body must be complete; the merge patch touches only `email` and clears `phone`, leaving `name` and `segment` untouched. `If-Match` turns a stale concurrent write into `412` instead of a lost update."},{"title":"Distinguishing absent from null in a Spring merge patch endpoint","lang":"java","code":"@PatchMapping(path = \"/customers/{id}\", consumes = \"application/merge-patch+json\")\npublic ResponseEntity<CustomerDto> patch(@PathVariable UUID id,\n                                         @RequestHeader(\"If-Match\") String ifMatch,\n                                         @RequestBody JsonNode patch) {\n    Customer customer = repository.findById(id).orElseThrow(NotFoundException::new);\n    if (!ifMatch.equals(customer.etag())) {\n        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.PRECONDITION_FAILED).build();\n    }\n    // BAD: binding to a DTO with String fields makes \"absent\" and \"null\" identical\n    // GOOD: has() means the client sent the field, isNull() means clear it\n    if (patch.has(\"email\")) {\n        JsonNode email = patch.get(\"email\");\n        customer.setEmail(email.isNull() ? null : email.asText());\n    }\n    if (patch.has(\"phone\")) {\n        JsonNode phone = patch.get(\"phone\");\n        customer.setPhone(phone.isNull() ? null : phone.asText());\n    }\n    validator.validateMerged(customer);          // validate the result, not the partial body\n    Customer saved = repository.save(customer);\n    return ResponseEntity.ok().eTag(saved.etag()).body(CustomerDto.from(saved));\n}","note":"Binding a patch straight to a plain DTO loses the difference between \"not sent\" and \"set to null\". Reading a `JsonNode` (or using a wrapper such as `JsonNullable`) keeps it, and validation runs on the merged entity."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you prevent two clients' updates from silently overwriting each other?","a":"Use optimistic concurrency with entity tags: `GET` returns an `ETag` that reflects the version, the client sends it back in `If-Match`, and the server applies the change only if the version still matches, otherwise it returns `412 Precondition Failed`. To make clients participate, answer requests that lack the header with `428 Precondition Required`. On the server this maps naturally to a JPA `@Version` column."},{"q":"Can a PUT create a resource?","a":"Yes, when the client legitimately chooses the URI, such as `PUT /buckets/b1/objects/report.pdf`; the server returns `201 Created` for a new resource and `200` or `204` for a replacement. If the server assigns identifiers, creation belongs to `POST` on the collection. Either way, repeating an identical PUT leaves the same final state."}],"extra":["**Merge Patch limits:** RFC 7396 replaces arrays wholesale and cannot store a literal `null` value distinct from removal, so editing one line item in a large array needs JSON Patch or a dedicated sub-resource.","**Validation on partial input:** constraints like `@NotNull` that apply on create cannot be applied to a sparse patch body; apply the patch to the loaded state, then validate the merged result.","**Retry-safe PATCH:** a conditional PATCH with `If-Match` makes even a non-idempotent patch safe to retry, because a replay after success fails with `412` instead of applying twice.","**Discoverability:** advertise supported formats with the `Accept-Patch` header and reject unsupported patch documents with `415 Unsupported Media Type`, as RFC 5789 recommends.","**Client gotcha:** `HttpURLConnection` rejects the `PATCH` method, so a `RestTemplate` on the default `SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory` fails; use a request factory backed by the JDK `HttpClient` or Apache HttpClient."],"redFlags":["says PUT and PATCH are interchangeable verbs for updates","treats a missing field and a null field as the same thing","ignores concurrent edits, with no `If-Match` or version check"]},{"id":144,"category":"REST API Design","question":"Which HTTP status fits invalid input, an edit that clashes with current state, queued processing, and a signed-in user lacking permission?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Pick 400 or 422 for bad input and apply that choice everywhere, return 409 when the resource's present state or version forbids the change, answer long-running requests with 202 and a link to poll, and keep 401 (no valid credentials) separate from 403 (identified but not allowed).","answer":["Validation failures are client errors, never `500`: a `500` pages on-call, burns error budgets and tells clients to retry something that will never succeed. A common convention is `400` for malformed input (unparseable JSON, wrong types) and `422 Unprocessable Content` for well-formed requests that break semantic rules; some teams use `400` for both. Either is fine if it is consistent and documented, with detail carried by the error code.","`409 Conflict` means the request is valid but clashes with current state: a duplicate business key, an illegal transition such as refunding a cancelled order, or a concurrent modification. When the client sent `If-Match`, a stale version is better expressed as `412 Precondition Failed`, and `428 Precondition Required` can force clients to send one.","`202 Accepted` means the work was queued, not completed, and is no promise of success. Return a status resource the client can poll or subscribe to; use `201 Created` with `Location` only when the resource really exists at response time.","`401 Unauthorized` actually means unauthenticated: no token, an expired token or a bad signature, and it must carry `WWW-Authenticate`. `403 Forbidden` means the identity is known but lacks permission. Mixing them breaks clients, because a `401` should trigger a token refresh or login and a `403` should not."],"keyPoints":["bad input is a client error, so 400 or 422, chosen once for the whole API","409 signals that the request clashes with the resource's present state","202 only means accepted; the outcome comes later from a status resource","401 asks for valid credentials, while 403 refuses an identified caller"],"examples":[{"title":"Async credit application: 202 with a status resource","lang":"http","code":"POST /credit-applications HTTP/1.1\nContent-Type: application/json\nIdempotency-Key: 7c9e6679-7425-40de-944b-e07fc1f90ae7\n\n{ \"customerId\": \"c-1042\", \"amount\": 50000, \"currency\": \"SEK\" }\n\nHTTP/1.1 202 Accepted\nLocation: /credit-applications/ca-88121/status\nRetry-After: 2\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{ \"applicationId\": \"ca-88121\", \"status\": \"PENDING\",\n  \"statusUrl\": \"/credit-applications/ca-88121/status\" }\n\nGET /credit-applications/ca-88121/status HTTP/1.1\n\nHTTP/1.1 303 See Other\nLocation: /credit-applications/ca-88121","note":"While scoring runs, the status endpoint returns `200` with `PENDING`; once decided it redirects with `303` to the final resource (returning `200` with a result link is an equally valid style)."},{"title":"Scenario to status code cheat sheet","lang":"text","code":"Scenario                                        Status\nBody is not valid JSON, wrong field type        400 Bad Request\namount <= 0, unsupported currency               422 (or 400, pick one and stay consistent)\nMissing or expired bearer token                 401 + WWW-Authenticate\nValid token, lacks scope payments:write         403 Forbidden\nCancel requested for an already SHIPPED order   409 Conflict\nIf-Match ETag is stale                          412 Precondition Failed\nSame Idempotency-Key still in progress          409 Conflict + Retry-After\nClient exceeded its rate limit                  429 Too Many Requests + Retry-After\nAccepted for async scoring                      202 Accepted + status URL\nCreated synchronously                           201 Created + Location\nService overloaded or in maintenance            503 Service Unavailable + Retry-After","note":"Consistency matters more than the exact choice between 400 and 422; document the table and have every service follow it."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When is `412` more fitting than `409`?","a":"Return `412 Precondition Failed` when a conditional header such as `If-Match` with a stale `ETag` evaluates to false: the client asked for the change only under a condition that no longer holds. Use `409 Conflict` when the request clashes with the resource's current state without any precondition, such as cancelling an order that has already shipped. Both tell the client to re-read before trying again."},{"q":"How do you decide between `400` and `422` for bad input?","a":"A common rule is `400 Bad Request` for requests the server cannot parse, such as malformed JSON or a wrong content type, and `422 Unprocessable Content`, defined in RFC 9110, for well-formed requests that fail validation. Some APIs use `400` for both, which is fine if it is documented. What matters is that clients see the same status for the same kind of failure on every endpoint."}],"extra":["**422 is core HTTP now:** RFC 9110 moved 422 from WebDAV into the core spec as \"Unprocessable Content\", removing the old objection that it was WebDAV-only.","**Spring Security mapping:** the `AuthenticationEntryPoint` produces the `401` (the resource server's `BearerTokenAuthenticationEntryPoint` adds `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer`) and the `AccessDeniedHandler` produces the `403`; customize both so they emit your Problem Details body.","**404 to hide existence:** for another tenant's resource, returning `404` instead of `403` prevents ID enumeration; make that a documented policy, not an accident.","**429 versus 503:** `429` says the caller exceeded its quota, `503` says the server lacks capacity; clients, dashboards and alerts should treat them differently.","**Not 204 for async:** `204 No Content` means done with nothing to return; if work is still running, `202` is the honest code."],"redFlags":["returns 200 with an error flag in the body","returns 202 with no way to learn the final outcome","has no policy on when to hide a resource behind 404 instead of 403"]},{"id":145,"category":"REST API Design","question":"Five teams return errors in five different shapes. Which single error format would you standardize on, and what goes in it?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Return one documented error shape, ideally RFC 9457 Problem Details, with the correct HTTP status, a stable machine code, a safe message, a trace ID, field-level violations and explicit retry guidance.","answer":["Clients should branch on a stable machine code (`INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS`, `ACCOUNT_FROZEN`), never on message text, so the code set is part of the contract and evolves like any other field. The human message must be safe: no stack traces, SQL, class names or internal hostnames, which leak implementation details and help attackers. Include a trace or correlation ID that matches your logs and distributed traces so support can find the failing request in seconds.","For validation failures, return a list of violations with the field path, a code and a message, so a UI can highlight every bad field in one round trip. Add retryability information: the status itself (`429`, `503`), a `Retry-After` header where relevant and optionally an explicit `retryable` flag, because otherwise clients guess and either retry permanent errors or give up on transient ones.","The standard shape is RFC 9457 Problem Details (`application/problem+json`, obsoleting RFC 7807) with `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` and `instance`, plus extension members for your code, trace ID and violations. Spring Boot 3 supports it natively with `ProblemDetail` and `ErrorResponseException`. Never return HTTP 200 with an error flag in the body: it breaks caching, retries, monitoring, alerting and every client library that relies on status codes."],"keyPoints":["use RFC 9457 Problem Details for a standard JSON shape and media type","the HTTP status must match the class of failure","clients branch on a stable code, never on message text","add a trace ID, per-field errors and a hint on whether retrying helps"],"examples":[{"title":"A validation error in Problem Details format","lang":"http","code":"HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Content\nContent-Type: application/problem+json\n\n{\n  \"type\": \"https://api.example.com/problems/validation-failed\",\n  \"title\": \"Request validation failed\",\n  \"status\": 422,\n  \"detail\": \"2 fields are invalid\",\n  \"instance\": \"/payments\",\n  \"code\": \"VALIDATION_FAILED\",\n  \"traceId\": \"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\",\n  \"retryable\": false,\n  \"violations\": [\n    { \"field\": \"amount.value\", \"code\": \"MUST_BE_POSITIVE\", \"message\": \"must be greater than 0\" },\n    { \"field\": \"currency\", \"code\": \"UNSUPPORTED_CURRENCY\", \"message\": \"expected SEK, EUR or USD\" }\n  ]\n}","note":"Standard members give generic tooling something to work with; `code`, `traceId`, `retryable` and `violations` are extension members your clients actually branch on."},{"title":"Central exception handler producing safe ProblemDetail responses","lang":"java","code":"@RestControllerAdvice\nclass ApiExceptionHandler extends ResponseEntityExceptionHandler {   // framework errors keep their 4xx status\n\n    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ApiExceptionHandler.class);\n\n    @ExceptionHandler(InsufficientFundsException.class)\n    ProblemDetail insufficientFunds(InsufficientFundsException ex) {\n        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_CONTENT,   // UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY before Spring 7\n                \"Account balance is too low for this payment\");\n        pd.setType(URI.create(\"https://api.example.com/problems/insufficient-funds\"));\n        pd.setProperty(\"code\", \"INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS\");\n        pd.setProperty(\"retryable\", false);\n        pd.setProperty(\"traceId\", MDC.get(\"traceId\"));\n        return pd;\n    }\n\n    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)\n    ProblemDetail unexpected(Exception ex) {\n        log.error(\"Unhandled error\", ex);                  // GOOD: stack trace goes to logs only\n        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,\n                \"An unexpected error occurred\");           // BAD would be: ex.getMessage()\n        pd.setProperty(\"code\", \"INTERNAL_ERROR\");\n        pd.setProperty(\"traceId\", MDC.get(\"traceId\"));\n        return pd;\n    }\n}","note":"With Micrometer Tracing in Spring Boot 3 the current `traceId` is in the MDC, so the value in the body matches your logs. The catch-all handler never echoes exception messages, which often contain SQL or internal identifiers."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How should per-field validation failures appear in the response?","a":"Use an extension member in the Problem Details body, for example an `errors` array where each entry has a JSON Pointer to the field such as `/items/0/quantity`, a stable code like `MUST_BE_POSITIVE` and a readable detail. Return every violation in one response so clients can show them together, with `400` or `422` according to your convention. RFC 9457 itself uses this kind of array as an example extension."},{"q":"How do you keep internal details out of errors without losing debuggability?","a":"Log the full exception server-side with the trace ID and return only a safe detail plus that trace ID to the client. Map exceptions to responses in one place, for example a `@RestControllerAdvice` that builds Spring's `ProblemDetail`, so no controller leaks a stack trace by accident. Support can then find the exact failure from the ID the customer quotes."}],"extra":["**Framework errors too:** set `spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true` or extend `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler` so errors like `MethodArgumentNotValidException` and `HttpMessageNotReadableException` use the same shape as your domain errors.","**Code catalog:** keep error codes in a documented enum or catalog in your OpenAPI spec; adding a code is additive, but renaming or removing one breaks clients that switch on it.","**Do not leak existence:** for resources the caller must not know about, such as another merchant's payment, return `404` rather than `403` so IDs cannot be enumerated.","**Localization:** keep `code` language-neutral and let clients localize, or honor `Accept-Language` for `title` and `detail`; never translate the machine code.","**Trace propagation:** use W3C `traceparent` end to end so the trace ID in the error body matches what the gateway, every service and your log platform already index."],"redFlags":["expects clients to parse human-readable messages to decide behavior","changes error codes between releases without treating it as breaking","lets each endpoint or framework produce its own error body"]},{"id":146,"category":"REST API Design","question":"Product wants to rename a response field and add three optional ones. Which of these changes justify a `v2`, and why?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Keep shipping additive changes under the current version as long as existing clients still work; cut a new major version only when the contract or its meaning breaks, and pair it with `Deprecation` and `Sunset` headers, a migration guide and tracking of which clients still call the old one.","answer":["Most changes should not need a new version. New optional request fields, new response fields, new endpoints and new optional query parameters are backward compatible, provided clients follow the tolerant reader rule and ignore unknown fields. Versioning every field addition multiplies the code paths you must test, secure and run for years.","A new version is justified when a change breaks existing clients: removing or renaming a field, changing a type or format (amount from integer minor units to decimal string), making an optional field required, tightening validation, changing error codes or changing pagination style. Semantic changes are the most dangerous because the schema stays identical: if `balance` silently switches from booked to available, or `status=ACTIVE` starts including suspended accounts, clients compute wrong numbers with no error at all. Those need a new field or a new version, never an in-place change.","When you do version, pick one scheme and apply it everywhere. A URI prefix like `/v2` is the easiest to route, cache and see in logs; media type or header versioning is purer but harder to debug. Run both versions in parallel over one domain model with version-specific mapping at the edge, announce retirement with `Deprecation` and `Sunset` headers, publish a migration guide, measure traffic per version and per client, and contact the remaining callers before removal."],"keyPoints":["additive, optional changes stay in the current version","removals, renames, type changes and new required inputs break clients","changing what a field means is breaking even when the schema looks identical","a new version ships with a sunset date and per-client usage data"],"examples":[{"title":"Signaling deprecation on the old version","lang":"http","code":"GET /v1/accounts/acc-123 HTTP/1.1\nAccept: application/json\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\nDeprecation: @1767225600\nSunset: Wed, 31 Mar 2027 23:59:59 GMT\nLink: <https://developer.example.com/migrate/accounts-v2>; rel=\"deprecation\"; type=\"text/html\"\n\n{ \"id\": \"acc-123\", \"balance\": 125000, \"currency\": \"SEK\" }","note":"`Deprecation` (RFC 9745, a Unix timestamp here meaning 1 Jan 2026) says since when the endpoint is deprecated, `Sunset` (RFC 8594) says when it stops working, and the `Link` points to the migration guide."},{"title":"Which changes need a new version","lang":"text","code":"Change                                                   Breaking?  Action\nAdd optional response field \"riskScore\"                  No         ship in v1\nAdd optional query parameter includeClosed=true          No         ship in v1\nAdd endpoint GET /accounts/{id}/limits                   No         ship in v1\nAdd value DORMANT to response enum AccountStatus         Depends    only if documented as open enum\nMake request field \"purpose\" required                    Yes        v2, or default it server side\nRename \"balance\" to \"availableBalance\"                   Yes        add new field, deprecate old\nAmount changes from minor units to decimal string        Yes        v2\n\"balance\" changes from booked to available (same type)   Yes        new field; never reuse the name\nTighten validation: max length 100 becomes 50            Yes        v2 or grandfather old clients","note":"Expand and contract (add the new field, migrate consumers, remove the old one later) resolves many breaking changes without a whole new API version."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you rename a response field without cutting a new version?","a":"Expand and contract: add the new field alongside the old one, populate both, and mark the old one `deprecated: true` in the OpenAPI spec. Track which clients still read or send the old field through logs or API analytics, contact them, and remove it only when usage reaches zero or in the next major version. The rename then costs some temporary duplication instead of a migration for every client."},{"q":"URL path versioning or header versioning?","a":"A path segment such as `/v2/orders` is visible, easy to route at the gateway, simple to test with curl and friendly to caches. Header or media type versioning keeps resource URIs stable but is harder to discover, debug and cache, since caches need `Vary` on that header. Either works; consistency across the API and a clear deprecation policy matter more than the mechanism."}],"extra":["**Tolerant reader in Java:** Spring Boot's auto-configured `ObjectMapper` disables `FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES`, but a plain Jackson 2 `new ObjectMapper()` does not (Jackson 3, the default in Spring Boot 4, disables it out of the box), which is a classic way internal clients break on a purely additive change.","**Catch breaks in CI:** run consumer-driven contract tests (Pact or Spring Cloud Contract) and an OpenAPI diff tool such as `oasdiff` in the pipeline so breaking changes fail the build instead of production.","**Events are APIs too:** Kafka message schemas need the same discipline; a schema registry with backward or full compatibility mode blocks producers from publishing breaking Avro or Protobuf changes.","**Internal versus public:** with a handful of known internal consumers you can coordinate a migration in weeks; public APIs need deprecation windows of many months and must assume some clients never migrate."],"redFlags":["assumes clients ignore unknown fields without checking their deserializers","runs old versions forever with no sunset plan","cuts a new version for a rename that a temporary alias could cover"]},{"id":147,"category":"REST API Design","question":"A mobile client times out on `POST /payments` and retries. How does the server make sure the customer is charged once?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"The client sends an `Idempotency-Key`; the server claims it atomically under a unique constraint, stores a request fingerprint and the final response, replays that response on retries and rejects concurrent or mismatched reuse.","answer":["The client generates one key per logical operation (a UUID in the `Idempotency-Key` header) and reuses it on every retry of that operation. The server scopes the key by client or merchant and claims it atomically, for example with `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` against a unique constraint in the same PostgreSQL database as the payment. Keys held only in memory are lost on restart and invisible to other replicas, and a check-then-insert race lets two instances both charge the card.","Next to the key, store a fingerprint of the request (SHA-256 over method, path and canonicalized body), a status such as `IN_PROGRESS` or `COMPLETED`, and, once finished, the response status and body. A retry with a completed key replays the stored response, including deterministic failures like a declined card. A retry whose fingerprint differs is rejected, because the client reused a key for a different request.","Concurrency is the subtle part: if a duplicate arrives while the first request is still in progress, return `409 Conflict` with `Retry-After` instead of executing again. Commit the business write and the key outcome in one local transaction where possible; when an external PSP is involved, pass a derived idempotency key downstream too, because your rollback cannot undo their charge. Expire keys after a documented window, often 24 hours, and run a recovery job that resolves keys stuck in progress by querying the PSP."],"keyPoints":["the client generates one key per logical operation and reuses it on retry","the server claims the key with an insert under a unique constraint","the first final response is saved and returned again for repeats","a request still in progress gets a conflict, not a second execution"],"examples":[{"title":"Idempotency key table and atomic claim in PostgreSQL","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE idempotency_keys (\n    merchant_id    uuid        NOT NULL,\n    idem_key       text        NOT NULL,\n    request_hash   bytea       NOT NULL,\n    status         text        NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('IN_PROGRESS', 'COMPLETED')),\n    response_code  int,\n    response_body  jsonb,\n    payment_id     uuid,\n    created_at     timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    PRIMARY KEY (merchant_id, idem_key)\n);\n\n-- Claim: exactly one concurrent caller gets a row back\nINSERT INTO idempotency_keys (merchant_id, idem_key, request_hash, status)\nVALUES ($1, $2, $3, 'IN_PROGRESS')\nON CONFLICT (merchant_id, idem_key) DO NOTHING\nRETURNING idem_key;\n\n-- Everyone else inspects the existing record\nSELECT request_hash, status, response_code, response_body\nFROM idempotency_keys\nWHERE merchant_id = $1 AND idem_key = $2;","note":"The primary key does the deduplication, not application code, so it holds across replicas and restarts. Scoping by `merchant_id` stops one client's key from colliding with another's."},{"title":"Service flow: replay, mismatch and in-flight handling","lang":"java","code":"@Transactional\npublic ApiResponse createPayment(UUID merchantId, String key, CreatePaymentRequest req) {\n    byte[] hash = Fingerprint.sha256(req);\n    if (!keys.tryClaim(merchantId, key, hash)) {           // INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING\n        IdempotencyRecord existing = keys.find(merchantId, key);\n        if (!Arrays.equals(existing.requestHash(), hash)) {\n            return ApiResponse.problem(422, \"IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED\");\n        }\n        if (existing.status() == KeyStatus.IN_PROGRESS) {\n            return ApiResponse.problem(409, \"REQUEST_IN_PROGRESS\").retryAfterSeconds(1);\n        }\n        return ApiResponse.replay(existing.responseCode(), existing.responseBody());\n    }\n    Payment payment = payments.save(Payment.pending(merchantId, req));\n    ApiResponse response = ApiResponse.created(PaymentDto.from(payment));\n    keys.complete(merchantId, key, response.status(), response.bodyJson(), payment.id());\n    return response;                                        // key, payment and outcome commit together\n}","note":"Because claim and payment share one transaction here, a concurrent duplicate blocks on the unique index until the first commits and then sees `COMPLETED`. When a slow PSP call sits in the middle, commit the claim in its own short transaction first so duplicates see `IN_PROGRESS` and get `409` quickly."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How long should the server remember idempotency keys?","a":"At least as long as clients and intermediaries might retry, commonly 24 hours to a few days; Stripe, for example, may remove keys once they are at least 24 hours old. Document the window, because a key reused after expiry is treated as a new request. Store an expiry timestamp and purge with an indexed delete or by dropping time-based partitions, so the table does not grow forever."},{"q":"The server crashes after the provider charged the card but before saving the response. What now?","a":"The idempotency record is still marked in progress, so a retry must not execute the charge again. Resolve it by asking the provider for the outcome, using the provider idempotency key or merchant reference you sent, then complete the record with the real result and return it. Run the same recovery from a background job for records stuck in progress, so the answer does not depend on the client retrying."}],"extra":["**IETF draft:** `draft-ietf-httpapi-idempotency-key-header` standardizes the `Idempotency-Key` header and suggests `400` for a missing required key, `409` for a request still in flight and `422` for a key reused with a different payload.","**What to store as final:** cache completed outcomes including deterministic `4xx` results, but do not freeze a transient `5xx` or timeout as the answer; mark the key retryable so the next attempt can actually succeed.","**Downstream keys:** derive the PSP idempotency key from your own (for example `merchantId:key:auth`) so a crash between the PSP call and your commit is recovered by safely repeating the PSP call instead of double charging.","**Natural idempotency as a backstop:** add a business uniqueness constraint such as unique `(merchant_id, order_reference)`, which protects you even when a client forgets the header.","**Operations:** purge expired keys with a batched job or time-partitioned table, and alert on keys stuck in `IN_PROGRESS` beyond a threshold, since they indicate crashed requests with unknown outcomes."],"redFlags":["looks for an existing payment and then inserts, with no constraint behind it","lets keys expire before clients have stopped retrying","forgets the downstream payment provider also needs an idempotency key"]},{"id":148,"category":"REST API Design","question":"You expose `GET /transactions` to partners with filters and sorting over millions of rows. How do you keep it fast and safe?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Allowlist filter fields, operators and sort keys, cap page size, add a unique tiebreaker to every ordering, and paginate with opaque keyset cursors instead of deep offsets or a raw query language.","answer":["Treat query parameters as untrusted input mapped onto an explicit allowlist: filter by `status`, `createdFrom`, `createdTo`, sort by `createdAt` or `amount`, each mapped to a known column with a supporting index. Rejecting anything else with `400` prevents SQL injection through `ORDER BY` (column names cannot be bound as parameters), stops clients from filtering on unindexed or sensitive columns, and keeps your schema private so you can refactor it. Exposing raw SQL, JPQL or an unrestricted query language turns every client into a potential full table scan.","Enforce a default and a maximum page size (for example 50 and 200) and clamp or reject larger values; an unbounded `limit=100000` is a denial of service against your own database and heap. Ordering must be total and stable: sort by the requested key plus a unique tiebreaker such as `id`, otherwise rows with equal timestamps shuffle between pages, producing duplicates and gaps.","Prefer keyset pagination with an opaque cursor encoding the last row's sort values and the filter it belongs to. `WHERE (created_at, id) < (?, ?)` with a matching index costs the same on page 1 and page 10,000, while `OFFSET` reads and discards every skipped row and drifts when data changes. Fetch one extra row to compute `hasNext`, and avoid exact totals on large tables."],"keyPoints":["accept only documented fields, operators and sort keys","a hard maximum page size protects the database","every sort ends with a unique column so pages never overlap or skip rows","cursors carry the last seen values instead of a row offset"],"examples":[{"title":"Offset versus keyset pagination in PostgreSQL","lang":"sql","code":"-- BAD: reads and discards 200000 rows; ties on created_at shuffle between pages\nSELECT id, amount, status, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = $1\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nOFFSET 200000 LIMIT 50;\n\n-- GOOD: keyset pagination with a unique tiebreaker, served by one index\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_created_id ON orders (customer_id, created_at, id);\n\nSELECT id, amount, status, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = $1\n  AND (created_at, id) < ($2, $3)        -- last row of previous page, decoded from cursor\nORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\nLIMIT 51;                                 -- 51st row only tells us hasNext","note":"The row comparison `(created_at, id) < ($2, $3)` matches the index order, so PostgreSQL scans the index backward from the cursor position regardless of page depth."},{"title":"Allowlisted sort keys and clamped page size","lang":"java","code":"public record OrderQuery(OrderStatus status, SortKey sort, int limit, Cursor after) {\n\n    private static final int DEFAULT_LIMIT = 50;\n    private static final int MAX_LIMIT = 200;\n\n    public enum SortKey {\n        CREATED_AT(\"createdAt\", \"created_at\"), AMOUNT(\"amount\", \"amount\");\n\n        final String apiName;\n        final String column;                      // hard coded, never taken from the request\n\n        SortKey(String apiName, String column) { this.apiName = apiName; this.column = column; }\n\n        static SortKey parse(String value) {\n            return Arrays.stream(values()).filter(k -> k.apiName.equals(value)).findFirst()\n                    .orElseThrow(() -> new BadRequestException(\"UNSUPPORTED_SORT\", value));\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static OrderQuery from(String status, String sort, Integer limit, String cursor,\n                                  CursorCodec codec) {\n        SortKey key = sort == null ? SortKey.CREATED_AT : SortKey.parse(sort);\n        int size = limit == null ? DEFAULT_LIMIT : Math.clamp(limit, 1, MAX_LIMIT);\n        OrderStatus st = status == null ? null : OrderStatus.parse(status);\n        Cursor after = cursor == null ? null : codec.decodeAndVerify(cursor, key, st); // HMAC plus filter binding\n        return new OrderQuery(st, key, size, after);\n    }\n}","note":"`Math.clamp` is new in Java 21. The cursor is verified against the sort and filter it was issued for, so a client cannot tamper with it or replay it with different filters."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What would you put inside an opaque pagination cursor?","a":"The sort values of the last row returned plus its unique tiebreaker, such as `created_at` and `id`, along with the direction and a hash of the filters so a cursor cannot be reused with different criteria. Serialize it, base64url-encode it and optionally sign it with an HMAC so clients cannot forge positions. The next page then runs `WHERE (created_at, id) < (:createdAt, :id) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT :n`, served by an index on those columns."},{"q":"Why does `OFFSET` pagination degrade on large tables?","a":"The database still reads and discards every skipped row, so page 10,000 costs far more than page 1 even with an index. Offsets are also unstable: rows inserted or deleted between requests shift the window, so clients skip or repeat items. Keyset pagination fixes both, at the cost of not supporting jumps to an arbitrary page number."}],"extra":["**Spring Data defaults:** `spring.data.web.pageable.max-page-size` defaults to 2000, far too high for most public APIs, and a `Pageable` sort parameter accepts any mapped property path, including unindexed or sensitive ones, unless you validate it.","**Cursor hygiene:** sign cursors with an HMAC, bind them to the filter and sort, and version the format so you can change the encoding later without breaking clients holding old cursors.","**Counting is expensive:** under MVCC, `COUNT(*)` on a large filtered PostgreSQL table scans rows; return `hasNext`, or an approximate total from planner statistics when a UI truly needs one.","**Complexity budget:** if filters can be combined, cap the number of conditions and the size of `IN` lists, and set a `statement_timeout` for the API's database role so a pathological query dies instead of saturating the database."],"redFlags":["pastes a sort parameter straight into SQL text","offers filters on columns that no index supports","computes an exact total count on every page of a huge table"]},{"id":149,"category":"REST API Design","question":"How would you implement rate limiting for a public API?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Count requests per client key (API key, user or tenant; IP only for anonymous traffic) with an algorithm that fits the burst behavior you want, usually a token bucket, kept in a shared store such as Redis and updated atomically. Enforce coarse limits at the edge and business-aware limits near the service, and reject with 429, `Retry-After` and quota headers so well-behaved clients can back off.","answer":["Rate limiting protects capacity and fairness: one tenant's script must not starve everyone else, and abuse such as credential stuffing or scraping should be expensive. Start with the key: per API key or OAuth client for partners, per user for authenticated traffic, per tenant for plan quotas, and per IP only for anonymous endpoints such as login, because many users share NAT addresses and attackers rotate IPs. Limits usually differ by plan and by endpoint cost: a search that fans out to three services deserves a lower limit, or a higher cost per request, than a cheap `GET`.","Algorithms trade precision, memory and burst tolerance. A fixed window counter (`INCR` one key per minute) is cheap but allows twice the limit across a window boundary. A sliding window log stores every timestamp, exact but memory hungry; a sliding window counter approximates it by weighting the previous window. A token bucket refills at a steady rate up to a capacity, allowing short bursts while enforcing the average. With several instances the state must live in a shared store and change atomically: a separate read and write races, and `INCR` followed by a separate `EXPIRE` leaves a key without a TTL if the process dies in between, so use one Lua script or a library such as Bucket4j with a Redis backend.","Layer the enforcement. A CDN, WAF or gateway (Spring Cloud Gateway's `RequestRateLimiter` uses a Redis token bucket) absorbs floods before they reach the service; the application enforces limits that need business context such as plan, tenant or endpoint cost. Decide what happens when the store is down: failing open keeps the API up but unprotected, failing closed turns a Redis outage into an API outage, so most public APIs fail open with a local fallback. Reject with 429, `Retry-After` and quota headers, and publish the limits so clients pace themselves instead of retrying in a tight loop."],"keyPoints":["pick the key and the algorithm first; a token bucket allows bounded bursts","shared counters must change atomically, for example in one Redis Lua script","429 with Retry-After and quota headers, coarse limits at the edge"],"examples":[{"title":"Token bucket in Redis, atomic in one Lua script","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass ApiRateLimiter {\n\n    // KEYS[1] bucket; ARGV: capacity, refill per second, cost of this request\n    private static final RedisScript<Long> TOKEN_BUCKET = RedisScript.of(\"\"\"\n        local cap, rate, cost = tonumber(ARGV[1]), tonumber(ARGV[2]), tonumber(ARGV[3])\n        local t = redis.call('TIME')\n        local now = tonumber(t[1]) + tonumber(t[2]) / 1000000\n        local s = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts')\n        local tokens = math.min(cap, (tonumber(s[1]) or cap) + (now - (tonumber(s[2]) or now)) * rate)\n        local allowed = tokens >= cost\n        if allowed then tokens = tokens - cost end\n        redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', tokens, 'ts', now)\n        redis.call('EXPIRE', KEYS[1], math.ceil(cap / rate) + 1)\n        return allowed and math.floor(tokens) or -1\n        \"\"\", Long.class);\n\n    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;\n\n    ApiRateLimiter(StringRedisTemplate redis) { this.redis = redis; }\n\n    /** Remaining tokens, or -1 when the request gets a 429. */\n    long tryConsume(String apiKey, Plan plan, int cost) {\n        try {\n            Long left = redis.execute(TOKEN_BUCKET, List.of(\"rl:\" + apiKey),\n                    String.valueOf(plan.burst()), String.valueOf(plan.perSecond()), String.valueOf(cost));\n            return left == null ? 0 : left;\n        } catch (RedisConnectionFailureException e) {\n            return 0;   // fail open; a local in-memory limiter can backstop this\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"One script is one atomic step, Redis `TIME` gives every app node the same clock, and the TTL removes idle buckets. The `cost` argument lets an export consume 50 tokens and a lookup 1; returning -1 in the catch block would fail closed, which is a product decision."},{"title":"What a well-behaved 429 looks like","lang":"http","code":"GET /api/v1/orders?status=OPEN HTTP/1.1\nHost: api.acme.com\nAuthorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\nRateLimit-Policy: \"standard\";q=100;w=60\nRateLimit: \"standard\";r=3;t=12\n\n### a few requests later\n\nHTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests\nContent-Type: application/problem+json\nRetry-After: 12\nRateLimit-Policy: \"standard\";q=100;w=60\nRateLimit: \"standard\";r=0;t=12\n\n{\n  \"type\": \"https://api.acme.com/problems/rate-limited\",\n  \"title\": \"Too many requests\",\n  \"status\": 429,\n  \"detail\": \"Plan 'standard' allows 100 requests per 60 seconds. Retry in 12 seconds.\"\n}","note":"`RateLimit` and `RateLimit-Policy` come from an IETF httpapi draft that is not yet an RFC, so many APIs send the older `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining` and `X-RateLimit-Reset` instead. `Retry-After` is the header every client library already understands."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Fixed window or token bucket for a partner API?","a":"Token bucket in most cases. It enforces the average rate while allowing a bounded burst (the capacity), which matches real clients such as a batch job that wakes up and sends 20 requests at once. A fixed window is fine for coarse daily or monthly quotas where the boundary effect does not matter, and it is the cheapest to build."},{"q":"How do you rate limit across multiple regions?","a":"A single global Redis adds cross-region latency to every request, so most systems enforce limits per region, splitting the quota between regions, and accept approximate global accuracy. Where exact global quotas matter, such as billing, count asynchronously from usage events and enforce with a short lag."}],"extra":["**429 is not 503:** 429 tells one client it exceeded its allowance; 503 is load shedding, which protects the service regardless of who is calling.","**Concurrency limits:** a per-second limit does not stop one client from holding 500 slow requests open; cap in-flight requests per key to protect threads and pools.","**Login endpoints:** limit per account and per IP: per IP alone misses distributed credential stuffing, per account alone lets an attacker lock out a victim.","**Client behavior:** your own clients should honor `Retry-After` and back off exponentially with jitter, or rejected clients all retry at the same moment.","**Load test the limiter:** one key works fine; 50,000 active keys each running a script per request can overload Redis."],"redFlags":["Keeps counters in local memory on several instances and calls it a global limit","updates Redis with a non-atomic read then write","rejects with 500 or 503 and no Retry-After"]},{"id":150,"category":"REST API Design","question":"How does HTTP caching work with Cache-Control and ETag, and when should an API use it?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"`Cache-Control` tells browsers, proxies and CDNs whether and how long a response may be reused (`max-age`, `s-maxage`, `public`, `private`, `no-cache`, `no-store`); an `ETag` lets a client revalidate with `If-None-Match` and receive a bodiless 304. Use freshness for public, slowly changing reads, validation for data that changes unpredictably, `If-Match` for safe concurrent updates, and `no-store` for sensitive data.","answer":["HTTP caching has two halves. Freshness: `Cache-Control: max-age=300` lets any cache reuse the response for five minutes without asking the server; `s-maxage` overrides it for shared caches such as CDNs; `public` and `private` say whether shared caches may store it at all. Validation: once a stored response is stale, or marked `no-cache`, the cache sends `If-None-Match` with the stored `ETag` (or `If-Modified-Since`), and the server answers 304 Not Modified without a body if nothing changed. The classic mistake: `no-cache` means store but revalidate before every use, while `no-store` means never store.","An `ETag` is an opaque version of a representation; strong ETags promise byte-for-byte identity, weak ones (`W/\"...\"`) semantic equivalence. The cheapest good ETag comes from data you already have, such as the entity's `@Version` column, so the server can answer 304 without building the response; Spring's `ShallowEtagHeaderFilter` hashes the rendered body instead, saving bandwidth but no server work. The same ETag protects updates: the client sends `PUT` with `If-Match: \"7\"` and gets 412 Precondition Failed if the resource has moved on, which is optimistic locking over HTTP; return 428 Precondition Required to make the header mandatory.","Decide per endpoint. Reference data, catalogs and versioned static assets (long `max-age`, `immutable`) benefit most. Per-user data can use `private, no-cache` with an ETag, so the browser revalidates and downloads only on change; balances, tokens and personal data get `no-store`. Behind a CDN, set `s-maxage` deliberately, make the cache key cover what varies the response, never cache `Set-Cookie` or user-specific responses, and plan invalidation (short TTLs, versioned URLs, purge by tag), because a wrong response at the edge reaches everyone until it expires."],"keyPoints":["max-age and s-maxage for freshness; private vs public for who may store","no-cache revalidates, no-store never stores","ETag with If-None-Match gives 304; If-Match gives 412 on concurrent edits"],"examples":[{"title":"Version-column ETag: conditional GET and conditional PUT","lang":"java","code":"@RestController\n@RequestMapping(\"/api/products\")\nclass ProductController {\n\n    private final ProductService products;\n\n    ProductController(ProductService products) { this.products = products; }\n\n    @GetMapping(\"/{id}\")\n    ResponseEntity<ProductResponse> get(@PathVariable long id, WebRequest request) {\n        CacheControl cache = CacheControl.maxAge(Duration.ofMinutes(5)).cachePublic();\n        String etag = \"\\\"\" + products.currentVersion(id) + \"\\\"\";   // SELECT version ... WHERE id = ?\n        if (request.checkNotModified(etag)) {                      // compares with If-None-Match\n            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_MODIFIED).eTag(etag).cacheControl(cache).build();\n        }\n        return ResponseEntity.ok().eTag(etag).cacheControl(cache)\n                .body(ProductResponse.from(products.find(id)));\n    }\n\n    @PutMapping(\"/{id}\")\n    ResponseEntity<ProductResponse> replace(@PathVariable long id,\n                                            @RequestHeader(value = \"If-Match\", required = false) String ifMatch,\n                                            @Valid @RequestBody ProductRequest body) {\n        if (ifMatch == null) {\n            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.PRECONDITION_REQUIRED).build();   // 428\n        }\n        long expected = Long.parseLong(ifMatch.replace(\"\\\"\", \"\"));\n        Product saved = products.replace(id, expected, body);   // stale version -> 412 via @ExceptionHandler\n        return ResponseEntity.ok().eTag(\"\\\"\" + saved.getVersion() + \"\\\"\")\n                .body(ProductResponse.from(saved));\n    }\n}","note":"The GET derives the ETag from the version column before loading the product, so a 304 costs one indexed lookup. The PUT turns the HTTP precondition into the same version check JPA uses for optimistic locking."},{"title":"Revalidation, and a lost update prevented","lang":"http","code":"GET /api/products/42 HTTP/1.1\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nETag: \"7\"\nCache-Control: public, max-age=300\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"id\": 42, \"name\": \"Espresso grinder\", \"priceMinor\": 249900, \"currency\": \"SEK\"}\n\n### six minutes later the cached copy is stale, so the client revalidates\n\nGET /api/products/42 HTTP/1.1\nIf-None-Match: \"7\"\n\nHTTP/1.1 304 Not Modified\nETag: \"7\"\nCache-Control: public, max-age=300\n\n### two editors loaded version 7; the first one saved, the server is now at \"8\"\n\nPUT /api/products/42 HTTP/1.1\nIf-Match: \"7\"\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"name\": \"Espresso grinder\", \"priceMinor\": 229900, \"currency\": \"SEK\"}\n\nHTTP/1.1 412 Precondition Failed","note":"The 304 carries only headers, so the client keeps its body and restarts the freshness lifetime. The second editor's PUT is rejected instead of silently overwriting the first editor's price change."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is the difference between no-cache, no-store and max-age=0?","a":"`no-store` forbids storing anywhere; use it for sensitive data. `no-cache` allows storing but requires revalidation before each reuse, so ETags still save bandwidth. `max-age=0` makes the response stale at once, which usually triggers revalidation, but caches may serve it stale in some cases, such as when disconnected, unless `must-revalidate` is set."},{"q":"Should you put a CDN in front of an authenticated API?","a":"Only for responses identical for every caller, such as catalogs, marked `public` with an `s-maxage`. Per-user responses must be `private` or `no-store`, and verify the CDN never caches on a key that ignores `Authorization` or cookies: serving one user's account to the next caller is a data breach, not a performance bug."}],"extra":["**Spring Security default:** it sends `Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, max-age=0, must-revalidate` unless the app set cache headers itself.","**Authorization and shared caches:** a shared cache must not reuse a response to a request carrying `Authorization` unless the response says `public`, `s-maxage` or `must-revalidate`; `public` on user data removes that protection.","**Vary:** `Vary: Accept-Encoding` is normal; `Vary: Cookie` or `Vary: Authorization` splits the cache per user and usually means the response should not be shared.","**Last-Modified:** its one-second granularity hides two updates within the same second; prefer ETags from a version column.","**stale-while-revalidate:** `max-age=60, stale-while-revalidate=300` lets caches serve slightly stale content while refreshing in the background."],"redFlags":["Thinks no-cache means the response is never stored","marks per-user responses public or caches them at a CDN","hashes the full body for ETags and claims it saves server work"]},{"id":151,"category":"REST API Design","question":"REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL: how do you choose for a given integration?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Choose by consumer and traffic shape: REST with OpenAPI for public and partner APIs, where reach, HTTP caching and tooling matter; gRPC for internal service calls that need strict contracts, low overhead, deadlines and streaming; GraphQL when several front ends need different slices of one data graph and you can afford server-side cost controls. Many systems combine them.","answer":["REST models resources over plain HTTP: every language, browser, curl and API gateway understands it, responses are cacheable by URL, and OpenAPI provides contracts and generated clients. Its weaknesses are chatty or over-fetching payloads for rich screens, and contracts that drift when nobody generates code from the spec. gRPC is RPC over HTTP/2 with Protocol Buffers: services and messages are defined in a `.proto` file, stubs are generated for each language, payloads are compact binary, and calls get deadlines and four modes (unary, server, client and bidirectional streaming). GraphQL exposes one typed schema and lets the client select exactly the fields and nested relations it needs in one request.","The costs decide more than raw speed. gRPC needs gRPC-Web and a proxy for browsers, binary traffic is harder to inspect, and long-lived HTTP/2 connections defeat layer 4 load balancing, which pins all of a client's calls to one pod. GraphQL moves complexity to the server: naive per-field resolvers cause N+1 calls, URL caching does not work for `POST /graphql`, and one nested query can be very expensive, so you need batching (DataLoader, `@BatchMapping` in Spring for GraphQL), depth and complexity limits, and often persisted queries. REST pushes the cost to clients: several round trips, or endpoints built per screen.","A practical rule: partners, webhooks and public APIs get REST with OpenAPI, because anyone can call it and versioning is explicit. Internal high-volume, latency-sensitive or streaming calls between services you own get gRPC, with compatibility checked in CI. A product with several front ends over many services can put GraphQL in a backend-for-frontend that calls REST or gRPC behind it. The hard problems stay the same whatever you pick (timeouts, idempotent retries, backward-compatible evolution), and gRPC and GraphQL report errors outside HTTP status codes, so dashboards built on 5xx rates miss them."],"keyPoints":["REST for reach, caching and public contracts","gRPC for typed, low-overhead internal calls with deadlines and streaming","GraphQL for client-shaped reads, paid for with server-side cost control"],"examples":[{"title":"A gRPC contract that can evolve safely","lang":"text","code":"syntax = \"proto3\";\n\npackage payments.v1;\n\noption java_multiple_files = true;\noption java_package = \"com.acme.payments.grpc.v1\";\n\nservice PaymentService {\n  rpc AuthorizePayment(AuthorizePaymentRequest) returns (AuthorizePaymentResponse);\n  rpc WatchPayment(WatchPaymentRequest) returns (stream PaymentEvent);   // server streaming\n}\n\nmessage AuthorizePaymentRequest {\n  string idempotency_key = 1;\n  string account_id = 2;\n  int64 amount_minor = 3;                  // integer minor units, never double\n  string currency = 4;\n  reserved 5;                              // removed field: its number is never reused\n  reserved \"card_token\";\n  optional string merchant_reference = 6;  // added later; old clients omit it\n}\n\nmessage AuthorizePaymentResponse {\n  enum Status {\n    STATUS_UNSPECIFIED = 0;                // proto3 zero value, no business meaning\n    AUTHORIZED = 1;\n    DECLINED = 2;\n  }\n  string payment_id = 1;\n  Status status = 2;\n}\n\nmessage WatchPaymentRequest { string payment_id = 1; }\nmessage PaymentEvent { string payment_id = 1; AuthorizePaymentResponse.Status status = 2; }","note":"Field numbers, not names, are the wire contract: renaming is harmless for binary clients, reusing a number corrupts data. A check such as `buf breaking` in CI fails the build on incompatible changes."},{"title":"GraphQL in Spring with a batched resolver instead of N+1 calls","lang":"java","code":"// schema.graphqls\n// type Query    { customers(ids: [ID!]!): [Customer!]! }\n// type Customer { id: ID!  name: String!  orders: [Order!]! }\n\n@Controller\nclass CustomerGraphController {\n\n    private final CustomerService customers;\n    private final OrderClient orderClient;      // REST or gRPC client to the order service\n\n    CustomerGraphController(CustomerService customers, OrderClient orderClient) {\n        this.customers = customers;\n        this.orderClient = orderClient;\n    }\n\n    @QueryMapping\n    List<Customer> customers(@Argument List<String> ids) {\n        return customers.findAll(ids);\n    }\n\n    // BAD: @SchemaMapping on Customer.orders calls the order service once per customer\n    @BatchMapping                               // GOOD: one call for all customers in the result\n    Map<Customer, List<Order>> orders(List<Customer> batch) {\n        Map<String, List<Order>> byId =\n                orderClient.findRecentByCustomerIds(batch.stream().map(Customer::id).toList());\n        return batch.stream().collect(Collectors.toMap(c -> c, c -> byId.getOrDefault(c.id(), List.of())));\n    }\n}\n\n// query { customers(ids: [\"c-41\", \"c-42\"]) { name orders { id status } } }","note":"`@BatchMapping` registers a DataLoader, so 50 customers cost one call to the order service. Add depth and complexity limits (graphql-java's `MaxQueryDepthInstrumentation` and `MaxQueryComplexityInstrumentation`) before exposing the schema to untrusted clients."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does gRPC load balancing need special attention?","a":"gRPC multiplexes calls over one long-lived HTTP/2 connection, and a layer 4 balancer balances connections, not requests, so a client's calls all hit one pod and new pods get no traffic. Use client-side `round_robin` over a headless Kubernetes Service, an L7 proxy such as Envoy or a mesh, and a maximum connection age on servers."},{"q":"How do you handle errors and versioning in GraphQL?","a":"Errors usually come back in an `errors` array next to partial `data`, often with HTTP 200, so monitor resolver errors explicitly. Instead of URL versions you add fields, mark old ones `@deprecated(reason: ...)`, and remove them when usage data shows no client requests them, which requires tracking field usage per client."}],"extra":["**Deadlines propagate:** a gRPC deadline (`withDeadlineAfter` on the stub) travels with the call, so downstream services can stop work once the original caller has given up.","**gRPC status codes:** gRPC has its own codes; retry transient ones such as `UNAVAILABLE`, never `INVALID_ARGUMENT` or `NOT_FOUND`.","**GraphQL caching:** every query goes to one URL, so HTTP caches cannot help; persisted queries sent as `GET` with a hash make CDN caching possible.","**Browsers:** browsers cannot speak native gRPC; a web front end needs gRPC-Web through a proxy, or a REST or GraphQL layer.","**Contracts need CI checks:** OpenAPI, `.proto` and GraphQL schemas make breaking changes detectable, not impossible; diff them in the pipeline."],"redFlags":["Picks gRPC or GraphQL because it is faster or newer, without naming the consumer","ignores GraphQL N+1 and query cost, or gRPC load balancing","assumes one protocol must serve the whole system"]},{"id":152,"category":"Security","question":"Your API gateway validates every token. Which security decisions can it own, and which must stay inside each service?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Authentication establishes who the caller is; authorization decides whether that identity may perform this action on this resource. A gateway can authenticate and do coarse checks, but every service must enforce resource and domain authorization.","answer":["**Authentication** verifies credentials or a token and establishes a principal: a user, a service, a tenant. Failure is a 401. **Authorization** is a policy decision over subject, action, resource and context: scopes and roles, but also ownership, tenant, resource state and limits (a merchant may refund only its own captured payments, up to the captured amount). Failure is a 403, or a 404 when you do not want to confirm the resource exists.","At the **edge**, a gateway terminates TLS, validates tokens (signature, `exp`, `iss`, `aud`), rate limits, and can do coarse checks such as requiring the `payments:refund` scope on a route. It cannot do domain authorization because it does not know who owns payment 9f2c or what state it is in. So each **service** must validate the token itself (do not trust an `X-User-Id` header anyone inside the network could forge) and enforce object and domain rules in its own code, ideally in the data access path so a missing check fails closed.","In Spring Boot 3 that means a resource server per service, URL rules for coarse checks, `@PreAuthorize` for action level checks, and ownership built into queries (`findByIdAndMerchantId`). Default deny, log denials for audit, and write negative tests. Broken object level authorization is number one in the OWASP API Security Top 10 (2023) precisely because teams stop at authentication."],"keyPoints":["proving who the caller is says nothing about which records they may touch","the gateway lacks domain data such as ownership or tenant, so it can only do coarse checks","each service enforces object-level rules close to the data","a missing service check turns into a cross-tenant data leak"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: authenticated means allowed; GOOD: scope plus ownership in the query","lang":"java","code":"@RestController\n@RequestMapping(\"/payments\")\nclass PaymentController {\n    private final PaymentRepository payments;\n\n    PaymentController(PaymentRepository payments) {\n        this.payments = payments;\n    }\n\n    // BAD: any authenticated merchant can read any payment by guessing or leaking IDs\n    @GetMapping(\"/legacy/{id}\")\n    PaymentView legacy(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n        return payments.findById(id).map(PaymentView::from).orElseThrow();\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: action checked by scope, object ownership enforced by the query itself\n    @GetMapping(\"/{id}\")\n    @PreAuthorize(\"hasAuthority('SCOPE_payments:read')\")\n    PaymentView get(@PathVariable UUID id, @AuthenticationPrincipal Jwt jwt) {\n        String merchantId = jwt.getClaimAsString(\"merchant_id\");\n        return payments.findByIdAndMerchantId(id, merchantId)\n            .map(PaymentView::from)\n            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND));\n    }\n}","note":"Another merchant's payment ID returns 404, exactly like a nonexistent one, so the endpoint neither leaks data nor confirms which IDs exist."},{"title":"Each service is its own resource server","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\n@EnableMethodSecurity\nclass SecurityConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    SecurityFilterChain api(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        return http\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\n                .requestMatchers(\"/actuator/health/**\").permitAll()\n                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, \"/payments/*/refunds\").hasAuthority(\"SCOPE_payments:refund\")\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\n            .oauth2ResourceServer(rs -> rs.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()))\n            .build();\n    }\n}","note":"The service validates the JWT itself even behind a gateway; URL rules give coarse checks, while domain rules stay in the service and repository code."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How should a service treat identity that the gateway forwards to it?","a":"Prefer having the service validate the original token itself (signature, issuer, audience, expiry), which is cheap with cached JWKS keys. If the gateway forwards identity in headers instead, the service must be reachable only through the gateway (network policy or mTLS) and must reject those headers from any other source, otherwise a caller can simply set `X-User-Id` themselves. For service-to-service calls, propagate the user token or exchange it through OAuth token exchange so the callee still knows who the end user is."}],"extra":["**Confused deputy:** when service A calls B with its own powerful credentials on behalf of a user, B cannot apply the user's permissions; propagate user context via OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange (RFC 8693) or pass a downscoped user token.","**Relationship-based access control:** for complex sharing models (teams, delegated merchant users), Zanzibar-style systems such as OpenFGA or a policy engine like OPA keep rules consistent across services.","**Row-level security:** PostgreSQL RLS policies keyed on a per-transaction tenant setting give a second, database-enforced tenant boundary if application code forgets a filter.","**Cheap negative tests:** `spring-security-test` lets MockMvc requests carry a `jwt()` with another tenant's claims, so every endpoint can assert a 403 or 404 for foreign resources."],"redFlags":["uses the two terms interchangeably","trusts a user or tenant ID taken from the request body over the one in the token","relies on hidden endpoints or unguessable IDs instead of checking ownership"]},{"id":153,"category":"Security","question":"Session-based vs token-based authentication: what are the trade-offs?","level":"Basic","tldr":"With sessions the server keeps authentication state and the browser holds only an opaque session ID cookie: revocation is instant, but instances need a shared store and cookies bring CSRF into scope. With tokens the client sends a bearer token itself; JWTs validate locally and scale across services, but cannot be revoked before expiry without reintroducing state. Browser apps often use a session with a backend, and tokens between services.","answer":["Sessions: after login the server creates a session record (user id, authorities, expiry) and sends a random ID in a cookie marked `HttpOnly`, `Secure` and `SameSite`. Every request looks the session up, so logout, a password change or an admin lock takes effect immediately: delete the record and access ends. The costs are state and browser coupling. Several instances need sticky sessions or a shared store (Spring Session with Redis or JDBC), each request pays for a lookup, and because browsers attach cookies automatically, state-changing requests need CSRF protection.","Tokens: the client sends `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. A JWT carries signed claims, so any service with the issuer's public key validates it without a network call, which suits mobile apps, third-party clients and many services across domains. The price is revocation: a stolen JWT works until `exp`, so access tokens stay short (minutes) and are paired with refresh tokens the authorization server can revoke; a denylist works but brings back a lookup per request. Opaque tokens with introspection (RFC 7662) sit in between: centrally revocable, but each validation calls the authorization server or a cache.","The browser decides most designs. A token in `localStorage` is readable by any injected script, so one XSS leaks a credential usable from anywhere; a token in memory is lost on reload; a token in a cookie brings CSRF back. Current guidance for browser apps therefore favors a backend-for-frontend: the browser holds a hardened session cookie with the BFF, and the BFF keeps the OAuth tokens and calls APIs with them (Spring Cloud Gateway's `TokenRelay` filter does this). Behind it, services stay stateless with short-lived JWTs. So the honest answer is usually both."],"keyPoints":["sessions: server state, instant revocation, shared store and CSRF protection","tokens: stateless validation, hard revocation, short expiry plus refresh","browser apps: session cookie to a BFF, tokens behind it"],"examples":[{"title":"Revoking access: a delete with sessions, shared state again with JWTs","lang":"java","code":"// Spring Session (Redis) with spring.session.redis.repository-type=indexed; the default\n// RedisSessionRepository has no principal index and is not a FindByIndexNameSessionRepository\n@Service\nclass SessionRevocationService {\n\n    private final FindByIndexNameSessionRepository<? extends Session> sessions;\n\n    SessionRevocationService(FindByIndexNameSessionRepository<? extends Session> sessions) {\n        this.sessions = sessions;\n    }\n\n    void revokeAllSessions(String username) {          // password change, locked account\n        sessions.findByPrincipalName(username).keySet().forEach(sessions::deleteById);\n    }\n}\n\n// JWT equivalent: every resource server checks a denylist on every request\n// (combined with the default validators on the JwtDecoder)\nclass RevokedTokenValidator implements OAuth2TokenValidator<Jwt> {\n\n    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;\n\n    RevokedTokenValidator(StringRedisTemplate redis) { this.redis = redis; }\n\n    @Override\n    public OAuth2TokenValidatorResult validate(Jwt jwt) {\n        return Boolean.TRUE.equals(redis.hasKey(\"revoked-jti:\" + jwt.getId()))\n                ? OAuth2TokenValidatorResult.failure(new OAuth2Error(\"invalid_token\", \"Token revoked\", null))\n                : OAuth2TokenValidatorResult.success();\n    }\n}","note":"Each denylist entry must live as long as the token's remaining lifetime and every resource server must consult it: exactly the shared state JWTs were meant to avoid. Short-lived access tokens keep the list small or unnecessary."},{"title":"The same API call, authenticated both ways","lang":"http","code":"### Session based: log in once, the browser keeps an opaque cookie\nPOST /login HTTP/1.1\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n\nusername=maria%40acme.com&password=...\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nSet-Cookie: SESSION=Y2E3ZjQ1MjE...; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax\n\nPOST /api/orders HTTP/1.1\nCookie: SESSION=Y2E3ZjQ1MjE...          (looked up in Redis)\nX-XSRF-TOKEN: 4f1c9a07...               (needed: cookies are sent automatically)\n\n### Token based: a service fetches a short-lived token and attaches it itself\nPOST /oauth2/token HTTP/1.1\nAuthorization: Basic b3JkZXJzLWNsaWVudDpz...\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n\ngrant_type=client_credentials&scope=orders.write\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"access_token\": \"eyJraWQiOiJrMSIsImFsZyI6...\", \"token_type\": \"Bearer\", \"expires_in\": 300}\n\nPOST /api/orders HTTP/1.1\nAuthorization: Bearer eyJraWQiOiJrMSIsImFsZyI6...   (validated locally with the issuer key)","note":"The session ID means nothing offline and dies when the server deletes the session. The bearer token works for anyone holding it until `expires_in` runs out, so it is short-lived and kept away from page scripts."}],"followUps":[{"q":"If JWTs are stateless, how do you log a user out?","a":"You cannot invalidate an issued JWT, so logout revokes the refresh token or session at the authorization server and lets the short access token expire. For an immediate cutoff add a `jti` denylist or a per-user 'issued before' timestamp checked during validation, accepting the extra lookup. Access tokens of about five minutes bound the exposure without one."},{"q":"How do sessions scale horizontally?","a":"Sticky sessions pin users to one instance, which breaks on deploys and skews load; a shared store such as Redis with Spring Session lets any instance serve any request. Keep sessions small (ids and authorities, not cached entities), because they are read on every request."}],"extra":["**Session fixation:** Spring Security changes the session ID at login by default, so a session ID planted by an attacker before login is useless.","**Cookie flags:** `HttpOnly` blocks script access, `Secure` limits the cookie to HTTPS, `SameSite=Lax` keeps it off cross-site POSTs; they reduce theft and CSRF but do not replace a CSRF token.","**Opaque vs JWT:** opaque tokens reveal nothing when logged and revoke centrally at the cost of introspection; many teams use JWTs internally and opaque tokens for third parties.","**Token size:** JWTs with many roles grow to kilobytes on every request; keep claims minimal and resolve fine-grained permissions server-side.","**Stateless chains:** an API accepting only bearer tokens can use `SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS` and disable CSRF; add cookie authentication and CSRF protection must return."],"redFlags":["Calls JWTs more secure than sessions without discussing revocation","stores access tokens in localStorage without mentioning XSS","disables CSRF while still authenticating with cookies"]},{"id":154,"category":"Security","question":"An old system stores passwords as unsalted SHA-256. What would you replace it with, and why?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Store only a slow adaptive hash (Argon2id, or bcrypt) with a unique random salt per user and a tuned work factor, optionally plus a separately stored pepper; never a fast digest or reversible encryption.","answer":["Passwords must be stored with a **slow, adaptive password hashing function**, not encrypted and not hashed with a general purpose digest. SHA-256 and MD5 are designed to be fast, so a GPU rig can test billions of candidates per second against a leaked table; encryption is worse, because whoever obtains the key recovers every password at once. The current first choice is **Argon2id** (memory-hard, so GPU and ASIC cracking gets expensive); **bcrypt** with a cost of at least 10 is still acceptable, and PBKDF2 with a high iteration count when FIPS validated primitives are mandatory.","Every hash gets a **unique random salt** (16 bytes from a CSPRNG), which defeats precomputed rainbow tables and makes identical passwords produce different hashes. The salt is not secret: algorithm, parameters and salt are encoded in the stored string, for example `$argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=2,p=1$<salt>$<hash>`. Tune the **work factor** (memory, iterations, cost) to what your login fleet can afford at peak, and raise it as hardware improves.","A **pepper** is an extra secret applied as an HMAC before hashing, or used to encrypt the resulting hash, and stored outside the database in a KMS, HSM or secrets manager, so a SQL injection or a leaked backup alone is not enough to start cracking. It adds key management and rotation work, so treat it as defense in depth. Never invent your own scheme: in Spring use the `PasswordEncoder` implementations, verify with `matches()` (constant time), and rate limit login, because each attempt is deliberately expensive in CPU and memory."],"keyPoints":["fast digests let attackers test billions of guesses per second on GPUs","a random per-user salt defeats precomputed tables and groups of identical hashes","the memory cost of Argon2id makes parallel cracking expensive; bcrypt is an acceptable fallback","a pepper kept outside the database helps when only the table leaks"],"examples":[{"title":"Bad vs good password storage","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: fast, unsalted digest; a leaked table is cracked at GPU speed\nString sha = HexFormat.of().formatHex(\n        MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\").digest(rawPassword.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8)));\n\n// BAD: reversible; whoever gets the key gets every password\nString encrypted = aesGcm.encrypt(rawPassword);\n\n// GOOD: Argon2id with a fresh random salt; parameters travel with the hash\n// (saltLength, hashLength, parallelism, memoryKiB, iterations)\nPasswordEncoder encoder = new Argon2PasswordEncoder(16, 32, 1, 19 * 1024, 2);\nString stored = encoder.encode(rawPassword);\n// stored looks like $argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=2,p=1$<salt>$<hash>\n\nboolean ok = encoder.matches(candidate, stored); // constant time comparison","note":"The good version never stores anything reversible, and because the parameters are embedded in the string you can raise them later without a schema change. Spring's Argon2 encoder needs BouncyCastle on the classpath."},{"title":"DelegatingPasswordEncoder with transparent rehash on login","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\npublic class PasswordConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {\n        Map<String, PasswordEncoder> encoders = Map.of(\n                \"argon2\", new Argon2PasswordEncoder(16, 32, 1, 19 * 1024, 2),\n                \"bcrypt\", new BCryptPasswordEncoder(12));\n        // New hashes are stored as {argon2}...; old {bcrypt}... hashes still verify\n        return new DelegatingPasswordEncoder(\"argon2\", encoders);\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    UserDetailsPasswordService passwordUpgrader(UserAccountRepository accounts) {\n        // Called by DaoAuthenticationProvider after a successful login\n        // when upgradeEncoding() reports that the stored hash is outdated\n        return (user, newHash) -> {\n            accounts.updatePasswordHash(user.getUsername(), newHash);\n            return User.withUserDetails(user).password(newHash).build();\n        };\n    }\n}","note":"The `{id}` prefix lets old and new algorithms coexist, and the `UserDetailsPasswordService` bean makes Spring Security re-hash with current parameters the next time each user logs in."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does bcrypt's 72-byte input limit mean in practice?","a":"bcrypt only uses the first 72 bytes of input, so longer passphrases, or multi-byte UTF-8 ones that reach 72 bytes quickly, are truncated and extra characters add no strength. Pre-hashing to work around it must be done carefully, encoding the digest (for example as base64) so it contains no NUL bytes. Argon2id has no such limit, which is one reason it is preferred for new systems."},{"q":"Where should a pepper live, and what is its weakness?","a":"Outside the database, typically in a secret manager or an HSM, applied as an HMAC over the password before hashing or as encryption of the stored hash. It helps when an attacker obtains a database dump but not the application secrets. The weakness is operational: rotating it needs a migration plan, since the plaintext is only available at login, and losing it locks every user out."}],"extra":["**OWASP baselines:** Argon2id with at least 19 MiB of memory, 2 iterations and parallelism 1; bcrypt with cost 10 or more; PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 with 600,000 iterations when FIPS 140 compliance forces it.","**Spring defaults:** `Argon2PasswordEncoder.defaultsForSpringSecurity_v5_8()` uses 16 MiB and 2 iterations, slightly below the OWASP minimum, so pass explicit constructor parameters if you want to match the guidance.","**bcrypt 72-byte limit:** bcrypt only uses the first 72 bytes of input, and current Spring Security versions reject longer passwords instead of silently truncating; naive pre-hashing with a raw binary digest can introduce null bytes, so prefer Argon2id for long passphrases.","**User enumeration and DoS:** when the username does not exist, still run a dummy hash so timing does not reveal valid accounts (Spring's `DaoAuthenticationProvider` does this), and put rate limiting, lockout or CAPTCHA in front of login because every attempt burns real memory and CPU.","**Policy side:** NIST SP 800-63B favors length over composition rules, drops forced periodic rotation, and requires screening new passwords against breached-password lists (for example the Have I Been Pwned k-anonymity range API)."],"redFlags":["says salting alone makes a fast hash safe","picks a work factor without measuring it on production hardware"]},{"id":155,"category":"Security","question":"A search endpoint builds SQL from request parameters and echoes the search term back in HTML. How would you harden it?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Stop SQL injection by binding every value and allowlisting identifiers; stop XSS with context-aware output encoding, allowlist sanitizing where HTML is allowed, and CSP; validation helps but is never the primary defense.","answer":["**SQL injection** happens when data is concatenated into query code. Bind values with `PreparedStatement`, `JdbcTemplate` placeholders, named parameters, or JPA parameters and the Criteria API. An ORM is not automatically safe: string-built JPQL or HQL, native queries, and dynamic `ORDER BY` or column names are injectable. Identifiers cannot be bound, so map user input to an allowlist of known columns. Manual escaping is a red flag: it breaks on encodings, backslash rules and dialect differences. Run the application with a least-privilege database role to limit the blast radius.","**XSS** happens when untrusted data reaches the browser as markup or script. The core defense is **output encoding for the context** (HTML body, attribute, JavaScript, URL), which auto-escaping templates such as Thymeleaf `th:text` and React JSX do for you; the bugs come from escape hatches like `th:utext`, `innerHTML` and `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. When users may submit rich text, **sanitize** with an allowlist library such as the OWASP Java HTML Sanitizer, never a homemade regex. Add a **Content Security Policy** without `unsafe-inline` as defense in depth, and `HttpOnly` cookies so a successful XSS cannot read the session.","**Validation** of type, length and format shrinks the attack surface, but perfectly valid data can still be dangerous (a name like O'Brien, a `<` in a comment), so you bind and encode regardless of validation."],"keyPoints":["values go through bind parameters so the driver never parses them as SQL","identifiers such as sort columns come from a fixed allowlist","encode output for the exact context: HTML body, attribute, JavaScript or URL","a content security policy and an HTML sanitizer limit the damage when encoding is missed"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: concatenated value and sort column; GOOD: bound value and allowlisted identifier","lang":"java","code":"@Repository\nclass PaymentSearchRepository {\n    private static final Map<String, String> SORTABLE = Map.of(\n        \"createdAt\", \"created_at\",\n        \"amount\", \"amount\",\n        \"status\", \"status\");\n\n    private final NamedParameterJdbcTemplate jdbc;\n\n    PaymentSearchRepository(NamedParameterJdbcTemplate jdbc) {\n        this.jdbc = jdbc;\n    }\n\n    // BAD: merchantId and sort go straight into the SQL text\n    List<Map<String, Object>> searchUnsafe(String merchantId, String sort) {\n        return jdbc.getJdbcOperations().queryForList(\n            \"SELECT id, amount, status FROM payment WHERE merchant_id = '\" + merchantId\n                + \"' ORDER BY \" + sort);\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: value bound as a parameter, identifier chosen from a fixed allowlist\n    List<Map<String, Object>> search(String merchantId, String sort, boolean desc) {\n        String column = SORTABLE.getOrDefault(sort, \"created_at\");\n        String sql = \"SELECT id, amount, status FROM payment WHERE merchant_id = :merchantId\"\n            + \" ORDER BY \" + column + (desc ? \" DESC\" : \" ASC\") + \" LIMIT 100\";\n        return jdbc.queryForList(sql, Map.of(\"merchantId\", merchantId));\n    }\n}","note":"The only concatenated fragments in the good version come from constants in the code, never from the request."},{"title":"XSS layers: sanitizer for rich text and a CSP header","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass XssDefenses {\n\n    // Merchant descriptions may contain basic formatting and links, nothing else\n    static final PolicyFactory MERCHANT_DESCRIPTION = Sanitizers.FORMATTING.and(Sanitizers.LINKS);\n\n    static String sanitizeDescription(String untrustedHtml) {\n        return MERCHANT_DESCRIPTION.sanitize(untrustedHtml);\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    SecurityFilterChain web(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        return http\n            .headers(h -> h\n                .contentSecurityPolicy(csp -> csp.policyDirectives(\n                    \"default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; \"\n                        + \"base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'\"))\n                .contentTypeOptions(Customizer.withDefaults()))\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().authenticated())\n            .build();\n    }\n}","note":"Encoding in templates is still the first line; the sanitizer covers the one field that must contain HTML, and the CSP limits damage if something slips through."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does a JPQL query built with string concatenation stay vulnerable inside an ORM?","a":"The ORM only binds values you pass as parameters; if you build the query string yourself, for example by concatenating `name` into the text passed to `createQuery` or `createNativeQuery`, the input becomes part of the query before the ORM ever sees it. This is JPQL or HQL injection, and it can still read or alter data. Use named parameters (`:name` with `setParameter`), the Criteria API, or Spring Data's `Sort` built from an allowlist."},{"q":"What does a Content Security Policy add if output is already encoded?","a":"It is a second layer for the day encoding is missed: a policy such as `script-src 'self'` with nonces stops the browser from running injected inline scripts or loading scripts from attacker domains. It can also limit where data goes through `connect-src` and `form-action`, which reduces exfiltration. It does not fix the bug, and a policy with `'unsafe-inline'` gives little protection, so treat it as defense in depth."}],"extra":["**LIKE wildcards:** binding a search term prevents injection, but `%` and `_` in user input still act as wildcards and can force expensive scans; escape them and declare an `ESCAPE` character.","**Second-order injection:** data stored safely can become dangerous when a later job or PL/pgSQL function concatenates it into dynamic SQL; there, use `EXECUTE format('... %I ...', col) USING value` so identifiers are quoted and values bound.","**DOM-based XSS:** the payload may never reach the server (for example `location.hash` written into `innerHTML`), so server encoding cannot help; use `textContent`, safe DOM APIs and Trusted Types.","**CSP rollout:** start with `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` and a report endpoint, then enforce; nonce-based `script-src` with `strict-dynamic` holds up better than long host allowlists.","**Tooling:** SAST rules (Semgrep, Find Security Bugs) that flag string concatenation into query methods catch most injection regressions in code review."],"redFlags":["believes input validation alone stops injection","uses one encoding function everywhere regardless of output context","turns off template auto-escaping to render rich text without a sanitizer"]},{"id":156,"category":"Security","question":"A developer says tightening the CORS policy will protect the app from CSRF. Why is that reasoning wrong?","level":"Basic","tldr":"CSRF tricks the victim's browser into sending an authenticated request with ambient cookies; CORS controls which origins may read cross-origin responses in the browser, so it is not a CSRF defense.","answer":["**CSRF** exploits the fact that browsers attach cookies (and HTTP basic credentials or client certificates) to requests for your domain regardless of which page triggered them. A hidden auto-submitting form on `evil.example` can POST to your payout endpoint with the victim's session cookie. The attacker never sees the response, but the side effect happens. It requires cookie-based (ambient) authentication and a state-changing endpoint.","**CORS** relaxes the same-origin policy: by default scripts cannot read responses from another origin, and the server opts in with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` and related headers. For non-simple requests (JSON bodies, custom headers, PUT or DELETE) the browser sends a preflight `OPTIONS` first. But simple requests, such as a form POST with `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, are sent without any preflight; CORS only hides the response. That is why CORS does not stop CSRF. CORS is also enforced only by browsers: curl ignores it, so it is never server-side access control.","Real **CSRF defenses**: synchronizer or double-submit tokens (on by default in Spring Security for unsafe methods), `SameSite` cookies, checking `Origin` or `Sec-Fetch-Site`, and never changing state on GET. **CORS** should be an explicit allowlist of trusted origins; never reflect arbitrary origins with credentials, and never \"disable CORS\" by allowing everything."],"keyPoints":["the attack works because the browser attaches cookies to requests the user never meant to send","the CORS policy only governs whether page script may read a cross-origin response","a plain form POST reaches the server with no preflight at all","real defenses are CSRF tokens, SameSite cookies and origin checks"],"examples":[{"title":"Cookie-session app: CSRF on, CORS as a strict allowlist","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass WebSecurityConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    SecurityFilterChain web(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        return http\n            .cors(Customizer.withDefaults())       // uses the CorsConfigurationSource bean below\n            .csrf(Customizer.withDefaults())       // on by default; do not disable for cookie auth\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().authenticated())\n            .oauth2Login(Customizer.withDefaults())\n            .build();\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {\n        CorsConfiguration cfg = new CorsConfiguration();\n        cfg.setAllowedOrigins(List.of(\"https://portal.example.com\")); // exact origin, never reflect the request Origin\n        cfg.setAllowedMethods(List.of(\"GET\", \"POST\", \"PUT\", \"DELETE\"));\n        cfg.setAllowedHeaders(List.of(\"Content-Type\", \"X-CSRF-TOKEN\"));\n        cfg.setAllowCredentials(true);\n        cfg.setMaxAge(3600L);\n        UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource source = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();\n        source.registerCorsConfiguration(\"/api/**\", cfg);\n        return source;\n    }\n}","note":"CSRF protection and CORS are configured independently because they solve different problems; the allowlist names exact origins, which is mandatory when credentials are allowed."},{"title":"What a CSRF request looks like on the wire","lang":"http","code":"POST /api/payouts HTTP/1.1\nHost: portal.example.com\nOrigin: https://evil.example\nSec-Fetch-Site: cross-site\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\nCookie: SESSION=5f2c9a17e3b04d8c\n\niban=SE4550000000058398257466&amount=5000","note":"This is a simple request, so no preflight happens and CORS never gets a say; the browser attached the session cookie on its own, and only a CSRF token, `SameSite`, or an `Origin` or `Sec-Fetch-Site` check stops it."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Which cross-site requests skip the preflight, and why does that matter here?","a":"Simple requests do: `GET`, `HEAD` or `POST` with only safelisted headers and a content type of `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, `multipart/form-data` or `text/plain`. The browser sends them without asking first, with whatever cookies `SameSite` allows, and only stops the attacker from reading the response, so a state-changing endpoint has already done its work. That is why such endpoints should reject unexpected content types and rely on a CSRF token or `SameSite` cookies rather than CORS."},{"q":"How does a synchronizer CSRF token work?","a":"The server issues a random token tied to the session and embeds it in pages or exposes it through a readable cookie, and every state-changing request must echo it in a form field or header. A cross-site attacker can make the browser send the session cookie but cannot read the token, because the same-origin policy blocks reading another origin's page or cookies. Spring Security implements this with `CsrfFilter` and a `CsrfTokenRepository`, including a cookie-based one for SPAs."}],"extra":["**Fetch Metadata:** modern browsers send `Sec-Fetch-Site`; rejecting state-changing requests marked `cross-site` is a cheap, strong layer on top of tokens.","**Login CSRF:** an attacker can log the victim into the attacker's account so saved cards or documents land there; login forms need CSRF protection too, which Spring Security applies.","**CORS misconfigurations:** reflecting `Origin` with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, trusting the `null` origin, or loose suffix matching that accepts `example.com.evil.net` turn CORS into a data exfiltration hole.","**Preflight caching:** `Access-Control-Max-Age` cuts OPTIONS traffic, but browsers cap it (Chromium at two hours), so do not expect a longer value to help."],"redFlags":["believes the server blocks disallowed origins, when the browser is what enforces CORS","reflects the request origin in `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` while also allowing credentials","forgets that HTML forms trigger no preflight"]},{"id":157,"category":"Security","question":"A team builds social login by treating an OAuth access token as proof of the user's identity. What does OpenID Connect add?","level":"Basic","tldr":"OAuth 2.0 is delegated authorization: a client gets an access token to call APIs; OpenID Connect adds authentication on top with the `openid` scope, an ID token, UserInfo and standard claims.","answer":["**OAuth 2.0** defines a resource owner, a client, an authorization server and a resource server. The client obtains an **access token** with scopes and uses it to call the resource server on the user's behalf, or on its own behalf with client credentials. The access token is meant for the API, its format is unspecified (opaque or JWT), and it tells the client nothing reliable about who the user is. Using an access token as proof of login is the classic mistake: a token issued to a different client can be substituted.","**OpenID Connect** adds authentication. The client requests `scope=openid`, and the provider returns an **ID token**: a signed JWT addressed to the client (`aud` is the client ID) with `iss`, `sub`, `exp`, `iat`, `nonce` and optionally `auth_time`, `acr` and `amr`. OIDC also standardizes the UserInfo endpoint, standard profile claims, discovery at `/.well-known/openid-configuration`, JWKS and logout.","In practice: user login to a web app is OIDC with the authorization code flow and PKCE; APIs are OAuth resource servers that accept access tokens for their audience and never ID tokens; machine to machine calls use client credentials with no user and no OIDC at all. In Spring Boot that is `oauth2Login()` from the OAuth2 client starter for the app, and the resource server starter for APIs."],"keyPoints":["an access token is meant for the API it targets, not as evidence of who logged in","the ID token is a signed statement to the client about the login event","the `openid` scope, UserInfo and standard claims give a consistent identity layer","the identity layer reuses OAuth flows rather than replacing them"],"examples":[{"title":"Authorization code flow with PKCE and OIDC (query wrapped for readability)","lang":"http","code":"GET /authorize?response_type=code&client_id=merchant-portal\n    &redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fportal.example.com%2Flogin%2Foauth2%2Fcode%2Fidp\n    &scope=openid%20profile%20payments%3Aread&state=af0ifjsldkj&nonce=n0S6WzA2Mj\n    &code_challenge=E9Melhoa2OwvFrEMTJguCHaoeK1t8URWbuGJSstw-cM&code_challenge_method=S256 HTTP/1.1\nHost: idp.example.com\n\nPOST /token HTTP/1.1\nHost: idp.example.com\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n\ngrant_type=authorization_code&code=SplxlOBeZQQYbYS6WxSbIA&client_id=merchant-portal\n&redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fportal.example.com%2Flogin%2Foauth2%2Fcode%2Fidp\n&code_verifier=dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"access_token\": \"eyJhbGciOi...\", \"token_type\": \"Bearer\", \"expires_in\": 900,\n \"refresh_token\": \"8xLOxBtZp8\", \"id_token\": \"eyJraWQiOi...\", \"scope\": \"openid profile payments:read\"}","note":"The `openid` scope is what makes this OIDC: the response carries an `id_token` for the portal to log the user in, and a separate `access_token` for calling the payments API."},{"title":"OIDC login client in Spring Boot","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  security:\n    oauth2:\n      client:\n        registration:\n          idp:\n            client-id: merchant-portal\n            client-secret: ${IDP_CLIENT_SECRET}\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\n            scope: openid, profile, payments:read\n            redirect-uri: \"{baseUrl}/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}\"\n        provider:\n          idp:\n            issuer-uri: https://idp.example.com","note":"With `issuer-uri`, Spring discovers endpoints and keys from the provider's discovery document and validates the ID token's signature, issuer, audience and nonce for you."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why must a client check `aud` and `nonce` in the ID token?","a":"`aud` must contain the client's own ID; otherwise an ID token issued to a different application, perhaps a malicious one the user also signed in to, could be replayed to log in to yours. `nonce` binds the token to the login request the client started, which stops an attacker from injecting an old or stolen ID token into a fresh flow. Together with signature, `iss` and `exp` checks, these make the ID token trustworthy as proof of this specific login."},{"q":"Should a resource server accept ID tokens on its endpoints?","a":"No. An ID token's audience is the client application and it describes a sign-in event; APIs should accept access tokens issued for their own audience with the scopes they require. Accepting ID tokens blurs who the token was meant for and lets any client that received one call the API."}],"extra":["**state, nonce, PKCE:** `state` protects the redirect back to the client against CSRF, `nonce` binds the ID token to the login session to stop replay, and PKCE binds the code to the client instance that started the flow.","**User identity key:** `sub` is unique only per issuer, so key local users by the pair (`iss`, `sub`), never by email, which can change or be reassigned.","**OAuth 2.1 draft:** consolidates current best practice: PKCE for all clients, no implicit or password grants, and exact redirect URI matching.","**Browser apps:** the current recommendation for SPAs is a backend-for-frontend that runs the code flow server-side and keeps tokens out of JavaScript, exposing only an HttpOnly session cookie."],"redFlags":["sends the ID token to resource APIs as a bearer credential","skips checking the ID token's audience and nonce","thinks OIDC is a separate protocol unrelated to OAuth"]},{"id":158,"category":"Security","question":"A database password sits in a repo's `application.yml`. How would you move it to a safer setup that supports rotation?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Keep secrets out of Git, images and logs; store them in a secret manager or encrypted Kubernetes Secrets with least-privilege access and audit, prefer short-lived dynamic credentials, and rotate automatically with apps able to reload.","answer":["**Storage:** a central secret manager (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault) encrypts with KMS keys, applies per-workload policies and audits every read. Kubernetes Secrets are only base64-encoded by default, so enable encryption at rest in etcd with a KMS provider, restrict RBAC tightly, and preferably sync from the external manager with the External Secrets Operator or the Secrets Store CSI driver.","**Access and least privilege:** workloads authenticate by identity instead of a bootstrap secret: IAM roles for service accounts or EKS Pod Identity, GKE Workload Identity, Vault's Kubernetes auth. Each service reads only its own secrets, read-only; humans get break-glass access with audit, not standing access.","**Short-lived credentials and rotation:** prefer credentials that expire on their own: Vault's database secrets engine issues a per-instance DB user with a TTL, and cloud IAM roles replace long-lived access keys. For static secrets, automate rotation with overlap (create new, roll out or reload, then revoke old) and rehearse it, because the day a key leaks is not the day to discover the runbook fails.","**Exposure:** never commit secrets (pre-commit and CI scanning), never bake them into images or ConfigMaps, and keep them out of logs, `/actuator/env` output, crash dumps and `kubectl describe`. File mounts are safer than environment variables, which leak into child processes and diagnostics."],"keyPoints":["the source of truth is a vault or cloud secret store, never the repo or the image","each workload reads only its own credentials, and every access is audited","dynamic credentials that expire on their own shrink the blast radius of a leak","rotation only works if the application can pick up new values without an outage"],"examples":[{"title":"Spring Boot with Vault: Kubernetes auth and dynamic DB credentials","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  application:\n    name: payments\n  config:\n    import: vault://\n  cloud:\n    vault:\n      uri: https://vault.internal:8200\n      authentication: KUBERNETES          # pod's service account token, no static secret\n      kubernetes:\n        role: payments\n      database:\n        enabled: true                     # fills spring.datasource.username and password\n        role: payments-rw                 # Vault creates a DB user with a TTL per instance\n        backend: database","note":"No password exists in Git, the image or the manifest: the pod proves its identity, receives a short-lived DB user, and Vault revokes it when the lease ends."},{"title":"Mounting a synced secret as a read-only file","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels: {app: payments}\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels: {app: payments}\n    spec:\n      serviceAccountName: payments        # workload identity, no cloud keys in the pod\n      containers:\n        - name: app\n          image: registry.example.com/payments:1.42.0\n          volumeMounts:\n            - name: provider-api-key\n              mountPath: /etc/secrets/provider\n              readOnly: true\n      volumes:\n        - name: provider-api-key\n          secret:\n            secretName: payments-provider-api-key   # synced from the secret manager","note":"A mounted Secret volume is refreshed by the kubelet after rotation, while an env var would stay frozen at container start and show up in process dumps."}],"followUps":[{"q":"A credential was committed to Git last month. What do you do now?","a":"Treat it as compromised and rotate or revoke it first, because rewriting history cannot reach existing clones, forks or CI caches. Then review the credential's usage logs since the commit, purge it from history if policy requires (`git filter-repo`), and add secret scanning such as gitleaks or GitHub push protection so it cannot happen again."},{"q":"What makes dynamic database credentials attractive?","a":"A tool such as Vault's database secrets engine creates a unique user per application instance with a short lease and drops it when the lease expires, so there is no long-lived shared password to leak. Every access is traceable to one workload, and revocation is a single lease operation. The cost is that the app must renew leases and the connection pool must cope with credential changes."}],"extra":["**Pod creation implies secret read:** anyone who can create pods in a namespace can mount and read every Secret there, so namespace RBAC for workloads is effectively secret RBAC.","**Leaked means rotated:** once a secret reaches Git history, treat it as compromised and rotate it; rewriting history does not undo clones, forks or CI caches. Tools like gitleaks and GitHub push protection stop most leaks before they land.","**Records leak too:** Spring Boot 3 masks `/actuator/env` and `/actuator/configprops` values by default, but a Java `record` holding a password prints it in its generated `toString()`, so override it or wrap secrets in a type that masks itself.","**Dual-key rotation:** for secrets verified by a peer, such as webhook HMAC keys or partner API keys, accept two active versions during the overlap so rotation needs no coordinated cutover."],"redFlags":["deletes the secret from the latest commit and considers the leak closed","shares one credential across all services and environments","prints resolved configuration at startup, credentials included"]},{"id":159,"category":"Security","question":"You are reviewing a team's JWT setup before launch. Which weaknesses would worry you most?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Typical JWT mistakes are long-lived unrevocable tokens, weak validation of algorithm, signature, issuer, audience and expiry, sensitive data in readable claims, no key rotation or revocation plan, and tokens leaking into logs.","answer":["**Validation mistakes** come first. Letting the token header choose the algorithm enables `alg: none` and algorithm confusion (an RS256 public key reused as an HS256 secret), so pin the expected algorithm. Skipping `aud` lets a token minted for one API be replayed against another; skipping `iss` accepts tokens from the wrong authorization server; ignoring `exp` and `nbf` makes stolen tokens live forever. Decoding the payload in a filter without verifying the signature is the same as no authentication.","**Content mistakes:** a JWS is signed, not encrypted. Anyone holding it can base64url-decode the claims, so no personal data, account numbers or internal details belong there. Keep tokens small because they travel on every request.","**Lifecycle mistakes:** a self-contained token cannot be revoked before `exp` unless you build for it. Use short-lived access tokens (minutes), refresh tokens with rotation and reuse detection, and a revocation path for high-risk cases. Plan **key rotation**: publish keys in a JWKS with `kid`, add the new key before signing with it, and keep old keys until their tokens expire.","**Leakage mistakes:** logging `Authorization` headers, putting tokens in URLs, or storing them in `localStorage` where any XSS can read them. Mask tokens in logs and APM, and prefer a backend-for-frontend with HttpOnly cookies for browser apps."],"keyPoints":["a token valid for days cannot be cut off without extra server-side state","the verifier must pin the algorithm and check issuer, audience and expiry explicitly","claims are only base64url encoded, so anyone holding the token can read them","keys need rotation through JWKS, and tokens must never reach logs"],"examples":[{"title":"Strict JWT validation in Spring Security","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass JwtConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    JwtDecoder jwtDecoder(@Value(\"${auth.issuer}\") String issuer,\n                          @Value(\"${auth.jwk-set-uri}\") String jwkSetUri) {\n        NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = NimbusJwtDecoder.withJwkSetUri(jwkSetUri)\n            .jwsAlgorithm(SignatureAlgorithm.RS256)   // pin the algorithm, never trust the header\n            .build();\n\n        OAuth2TokenValidator<Jwt> audience = new JwtClaimValidator<List<String>>(\n            JwtClaimNames.AUD, aud -> aud != null && aud.contains(\"payments-api\"));\n\n        decoder.setJwtValidator(new DelegatingOAuth2TokenValidator<>(\n            JwtValidators.createDefaultWithIssuer(issuer), // exp, nbf with small skew, iss\n            audience));                                    // reject tokens minted for other APIs\n        return decoder;\n    }\n}","note":"The default validators check time and issuer, but audience must be added explicitly; without it any token from the same issuer is accepted by every API."},{"title":"A reasonable access token payload","lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"iss\": \"https://auth.example.com\",\n  \"sub\": \"usr_8812\",\n  \"aud\": \"payments-api\",\n  \"iat\": 1790000000,\n  \"exp\": 1790000900,\n  \"jti\": \"b1c8f0e2-4a57-4d0c-9d36-51f1a7e0c3aa\",\n  \"scope\": \"payments:read payments:refund\",\n  \"merchant_id\": \"m_1042\"\n}","note":"Fifteen minutes of lifetime, a single audience, a `jti` for revocation lists, and only identifiers and scopes: no email, IBAN or role dumps that anyone holding the token could read."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is trusting the `alg` header in an incoming token dangerous?","a":"If the verifier picks the algorithm from the token itself, an attacker can send `alg: none` and skip the signature, or switch an RS256 token to HS256 and sign it with the public key used as an HMAC secret, which some libraries then accept. The fix is to configure the expected algorithm per key on the server and reject anything else. Spring Security's `NimbusJwtDecoder` works this way, accepting RS256 by default for a JWK set URI rather than trusting the header."},{"q":"How long should an access token live, and what drives that choice?","a":"Usually minutes, commonly 5 to 15, because a stateless token cannot be recalled and its lifetime is the window an attacker keeps after theft. Shorter lifetimes mean more refresh traffic to the authorization server, so the choice balances risk against load and user experience. Highly sensitive operations can add a server-side check, such as a `jti` denylist or token introspection, instead of shrinking lifetimes further."}],"extra":["**Header parameters are attacker input:** never fetch keys from a `jku` or `x5u` URL in the token, and resolve `kid` only against your configured JWKS; injection through `kid` lookups has happened in real libraries.","**Sender-constrained tokens:** DPoP (RFC 9449) or mutual TLS bound tokens (RFC 8705) make a stolen access token useless without the client's private key.","**JWKS caching:** resource servers cache the key set; a decoder should refetch when it sees an unknown `kid`, otherwise a key rotation causes a burst of 401s until the cache expires.","**Permission bloat:** stuffing full permission lists into tokens inflates headers past proxy limits and means permission changes lag until expiry; carry coarse scopes and resolve fine-grained permissions server-side."],"redFlags":["accepts whatever algorithm the token header names, including `none`","cannot explain how a stolen token is stopped before it expires","hardcodes one signing key with no rotation story"]},{"id":160,"category":"Security","question":"How does Spring Security's filter chain authenticate and authorize a request?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"`DelegatingFilterProxy` hands each request to `FilterChainProxy`, which runs the first matching `SecurityFilterChain`. Authentication filters turn credentials into an `Authentication` via the `AuthenticationManager` and store it in the `SecurityContextHolder`; `AuthorizationFilter` applies the URL rules, `ExceptionTranslationFilter` maps failures to 401 or 403, and `@PreAuthorize` adds method-level checks.","answer":["The servlet container only knows filters registered at startup, while security lives in Spring beans. `DelegatingFilterProxy` bridges them: a plain servlet filter that delegates to the `FilterChainProxy` bean. It holds a list of `SecurityFilterChain`s, each a request matcher plus ordered filters, and uses the first that matches, so `@Order` and `securityMatcher` matter: a catch-all chain ordered first swallows everything. It also applies the `StrictHttpFirewall` and clears the context after each request. Chains are `@Bean SecurityFilterChain` methods using the lambda DSL; `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter` was removed in Spring Security 6 and `and()` chaining in 7.","An authentication filter (`UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter`, `BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter` and others) turns credentials into an unauthenticated `Authentication` for the `AuthenticationManager`, usually a `ProviderManager` that asks each `AuthenticationProvider` whether it supports that token type: `DaoAuthenticationProvider` checks a `UserDetailsService` user against the `PasswordEncoder`, `JwtAuthenticationProvider` uses a `JwtDecoder`. The result goes into the `SecurityContextHolder`, a `ThreadLocal` by default; session logins persist it through a `SecurityContextRepository`, which custom login code must call explicitly since 6.0.","`AuthorizationFilter` then evaluates the `authorizeHttpRequests` rules; the first match wins, so specific rules come first and `anyRequest()` closes the list. A denial throws `AccessDeniedException`, which `ExceptionTranslationFilter` translates: anonymous callers go to the `AuthenticationEntryPoint` (401 or a login redirect), authenticated ones to the `AccessDeniedHandler` (403). Method security (`@EnableMethodSecurity`) runs later via AOP proxies, so `@PreAuthorize` can use method arguments for object-level rules such as 'this account belongs to the caller', with the same self-invocation limit as `@Transactional`."],"keyPoints":["first matching SecurityFilterChain wins","AuthenticationManager and providers fill the SecurityContextHolder","AuthorizationFilter decides, ExceptionTranslationFilter maps 401 vs 403"],"examples":[{"title":"Two ordered chains with the lambda DSL","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\n@EnableMethodSecurity  // enables @PreAuthorize\nclass SecurityConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    @Order(1)  // checked first, only for /api/**\n    SecurityFilterChain apiChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        http.securityMatcher(\"/api/**\")\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth  // first match wins\n                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, \"/api/products/**\").permitAll()\n                .requestMatchers(\"/api/admin/**\").hasRole(\"ADMIN\")\n                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, \"/api/orders\").hasAuthority(\"SCOPE_orders.write\")\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\n            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth -> oauth.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()))\n            .sessionManagement(s -> s.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))\n            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable());  // bearer tokens only\n        return http.build();\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    @Order(2)  // no matcher: everything else\n    SecurityFilterChain opsChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth.anyRequest().hasRole(\"OPS\"))\n            .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());\n        return http.build();\n    }\n}","note":"Swap the `@Order` values and startup fails: a chain without `securityMatcher` matches any request, and Spring Security rejects every chain ordered after it. The default JWT converter yields only `SCOPE_` authorities, so `hasRole(\"ADMIN\")` needs a custom `JwtAuthenticationConverter`."},{"title":"Custom filter delegating to the AuthenticationManager","lang":"java","code":"class PartnerApiKeyFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {\n\n    private final AuthenticationManager manager;  // ProviderManager(partnerKeyProvider)\n    private final AuthenticationEntryPoint entryPoint;\n\n    PartnerApiKeyFilter(AuthenticationManager manager, AuthenticationEntryPoint entryPoint) {\n        this.manager = manager;\n        this.entryPoint = entryPoint;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)\n            throws ServletException, IOException {\n        String key = req.getHeader(\"X-Api-Key\");\n        if (key != null) {\n            try {\n                Authentication partner = manager.authenticate(PartnerKeyToken.unauthenticated(key));\n                SecurityContext ctx = SecurityContextHolder.createEmptyContext();\n                ctx.setAuthentication(partner);  // principal plus ROLE_PARTNER\n                SecurityContextHolder.setContext(ctx);\n            } catch (AuthenticationException e) {\n                SecurityContextHolder.clearContext();\n                entryPoint.commence(req, res, e);  // 401, the chain stops\n                return;\n            }\n        }\n        chain.doFilter(req, res);  // no key: later filters decide\n    }\n}","note":"Registered with `http.addFilterBefore(filter, BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter.class)`, not as a `@Component`. It only extracts the credential; the key lookup lives in an `AuthenticationProvider` that supports `PartnerKeyToken`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does a permitAll endpoint return 401 when the client sends an expired token?","a":"`permitAll` only affects `AuthorizationFilter`; authentication filters earlier in the chain still run, so `BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter` rejects the invalid token with 401 before authorization is consulted. Clients should drop the header on public calls, or those paths get their own chain."},{"q":"How does the security context reach @Async methods?","a":"Not by default: `SecurityContextHolder` is thread-local, so another thread sees an empty context. Wrap the executor in `DelegatingSecurityContextAsyncTaskExecutor`, which copies the submitter's context into each task; avoid the inheritable thread-local mode with pools."}],"extra":["**Filters registered twice:** Boot registers every `Filter` bean with the servlet container, so a `@Component` security filter also added with `addFilterBefore` runs twice.","**ignoring() vs permitAll():** `web.ignoring()` skips the chain entirely (no security headers, no context); prefer `permitAll()`.","**Old APIs:** `authorizeRequests` was deprecated during 6.x and removed in 7; `antMatchers` gave way to `requestMatchers` in 6.","**Testing the chain:** `@WithMockUser` or MockMvc's `jwt()` post processor populate the context; import `SecurityConfig` into a `@WebMvcTest`.","**Debugging:** TRACE logging for `org.springframework.security` shows the matched chain and each filter passed."],"redFlags":["Relies on WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter","cannot explain who produces 401 vs 403, or that the first matching rule wins","enforces object-level access only with URL patterns"]},{"id":161,"category":"Security","question":"What happens in a TLS handshake, and when would you use mutual TLS between services?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"TLS 1.3 exchanges ephemeral Diffie-Hellman key shares in the first round trip and derives session keys; the server proves its identity with a certificate chain and a signature over the handshake, checked against the client's trust store and hostname. Mutual TLS adds a client certificate so the server authenticates the calling workload too; use it for service identity, with a mesh or automation rotating short-lived certificates.","answer":["The `ClientHello` carries versions, cipher suites, the server name (SNI), ALPN protocols such as `h2`, and an ephemeral key share such as X25519. The server answers with `ServerHello` and its share; both derive the same secret and, via HKDF, handshake keys, so the rest is encrypted. The server sends its certificate chain, `CertificateVerify` (a transcript signature proving it holds the private key) and `Finished` (a MAC over the handshake); the client verifies and sends `Finished`, and data flows after one round trip. Ephemeral keys give forward secrecy: a leaked server key cannot decrypt recorded traffic.","Validation is where production breaks. The server must send its leaf and intermediates; the client builds a path to a trusted root and checks signatures, dates, key usage, and that the hostname matches a Subject Alternative Name. In Java, `PKIX path building failed` means a missing intermediate or a private CA absent from the JVM truststore, and a 'no subject alternative name' error means the wrong certificate for the host. Fix the chain or truststore, never with a trust-all `TrustManager`.","With mTLS the server also sends `CertificateRequest`; the client answers with its certificate and `CertificateVerify`, and the server validates it against the client CA and maps an identity, typically a SAN such as the SPIFFE ID `spiffe://acme.internal/ns/payments/sa/payments-api`, to a caller. Use it when the caller is a workload: zero-trust networks, bank and partner links. mTLS authenticates but neither authorizes nor carries user context. In Kubernetes a mesh (Istio, Linkerd) terminates mTLS in proxies and issues, rotates and enforces short-lived per-service identities; without one, cert-manager or SPIRE issue certificates and Spring Boot SSL bundles reload them."],"keyPoints":["ephemeral key exchange in one round trip, forward secrecy","certificate chain to a trusted root, SAN must match the host","mTLS proves service identity; a mesh issues and rotates certificates"],"examples":[{"title":"A TLS 1.3 handshake, annotated, with the mTLS additions","lang":"text","code":"orders-service (client)                      ledger.internal.acme.com (server)\n\nClientHello  -------------------------------------->\n  versions: TLS 1.3, 1.2   SNI: ledger.internal.acme.com\n  ALPN: h2, http/1.1       key_share: X25519 ephemeral public key\n                 <--------------------------------------  ServerHello (key_share: server X25519)\n  both: ECDHE shared secret -> HKDF -> handshake keys; the rest is encrypted\n                 <--------------------------------------  EncryptedExtensions (ALPN: h2)\n                 <--------------------------------------  CertificateRequest          [mTLS only]\n                 <--------------------------------------  Certificate: leaf + intermediate\n                 <--------------------------------------  CertificateVerify: signs transcript\n                 <--------------------------------------  Finished: MAC over transcript\nclient checks: chain to trusted root, dates, SAN matches host, signature valid\nCertificate + CertificateVerify   [mTLS only]  ---------->\nFinished  ----------------------------------------------->\n  both: application keys; first HTTP/2 request after one round trip\n  server (mTLS): checks client chain against the client CA, maps SAN to a caller\n\n# Inspect the chain, SANs and expiry the server really sends:\n$ openssl s_client -connect ledger.internal.acme.com:443 -servername ledger.internal.acme.com -showcerts","note":"Identity is proven by `CertificateVerify`, not by the certificate, which is public: only the private key holder can sign the transcript. That is also why a client certificate cannot be replayed like a bearer token."},{"title":"mTLS in Spring Boot with an SSL bundle, or delegated to Istio","lang":"yaml","code":"# Option 1: the service terminates mTLS itself\nspring:\n  ssl:\n    bundle:\n      pem:\n        ledger-server:\n          reload-on-update: true                         # renewed files, no restart\n          keystore:\n            certificate: \"file:/etc/tls/tls.crt\"         # leaf + intermediate\n            private-key: \"file:/etc/tls/tls.key\"\n          truststore:\n            certificate: \"file:/etc/tls/internal-ca.crt\" # CA for client certs\nserver:\n  port: 8443\n  ssl:\n    bundle: ledger-server\n    client-auth: need                                    # no client cert, no handshake\n---\n# Option 2: the mesh does it; the app speaks plain HTTP to its proxy\napiVersion: security.istio.io/v1\nkind: PeerAuthentication\nmetadata: { name: default, namespace: ledger }\nspec: { mtls: { mode: STRICT } }\n---\napiVersion: security.istio.io/v1\nkind: AuthorizationPolicy\nmetadata: { name: ledger-postings, namespace: ledger }\nspec:\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: ledger-api } }\n  action: ALLOW\n  rules:\n    - from: [ { source: { principals: [\"cluster.local/ns/payments/sa/payments-api\"] } } ]\n      to: [ { operation: { methods: [\"POST\"], paths: [\"/internal/postings\"] } } ]","note":"Option 1 leaves issuance, rotation and caller authorization to you. Option 2 moves all three into the platform; the `AuthorizationPolicy` is the authorization step mTLS alone lacks."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is a TLS 1.3 handshake faster than TLS 1.2?","a":"TLS 1.2 needs two round trips because key exchange parameters are negotiated first; TLS 1.3 puts a key share in the `ClientHello`, so one suffices. Resumption can add 0-RTT early data, but early data can be replayed, so accept it only for idempotent requests or not at all."},{"q":"mTLS or JWTs for service-to-service authentication?","a":"They answer different questions. mTLS proves which workload holds the connection; a JWT carries claims such as end user, tenant and scopes across hops. Many platforms use both: mesh mTLS for service identity, a propagated token for user context; RFC 8705 can even bind a token to the client certificate."}],"extra":["**Termination points:** behind a TLS-terminating load balancer traffic is plaintext unless re-encrypted, and forwarded client certificate headers must be stripped from external requests.","**Trust-all is not a fix:** skipping certificate or hostname checks keeps encryption but loses authentication.","**JDK debugging:** `-Djavax.net.debug=ssl:handshake` prints the JVM side of a failing handshake; current JDKs disable TLS 1.0 and 1.1.","**SNI and ALPN:** SNI lets one IP serve many hostnames and travels in clear text; ALPN negotiates `h2`, which gRPC needs.","**Rotation:** rotate a CA by distributing trust in the new CA before issuing from it, and alert on expiry, for example `probe_ssl_earliest_cert_expiry`."],"redFlags":["Thinks TLS encrypts data with the server's public key","fixes handshake errors by trusting all certificates","treats mTLS as authorization or user authentication"]},{"id":162,"category":"Testing","question":"A team's suite is mostly slow browser tests. What would you push down to unit and integration level, and what stays E2E?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Unit tests check isolated logic in milliseconds, integration tests verify real boundaries such as the database, messaging and HTTP adapters, and a few end-to-end tests protect critical journeys; the mix trades confidence against feedback speed.","answer":["**Unit tests** exercise domain logic in isolation: no Spring context, no database, no network. Fee calculation, state transitions, validation and credit rules run in milliseconds, cover edge cases exhaustively with parameterized tests, and pinpoint the failing line. A `@SpringBootTest` is not a unit test, and neither is anything that needs a container.","**Integration tests** verify your code against real infrastructure and framework wiring at a boundary: repositories and SQL against real PostgreSQL via Testcontainers, Flyway migrations, transactions and locking, Kafka serialization, Jackson mapping, HTTP clients against WireMock, security configuration. Slices such as `@DataJpaTest`, `@WebMvcTest` and `@JsonTest` keep them focused and cheaper than a full context. They catch what mocks hide: wrong SQL, constraint violations, lazy loading and transaction boundary bugs.","**End-to-end tests** drive the deployed system through real entry points across services to validate a critical journey such as checkout or refund. They give the most realistic confidence but are slow, need environments and data, fail for reasons unrelated to the change and are hard to debug, so keep a handful for revenue-critical journeys and run them after deployment or as synthetic monitoring.","The balance is confidence per minute of feedback: a wide base of fast unit tests for logic, integration tests at every boundary you own, contract tests between services, and very few E2E tests. Relying mainly on E2E produces slow, flaky pipelines that people learn to ignore."],"keyPoints":["rules and calculations are cheapest to pin down with in-memory tests","adapters for the database, broker and HTTP clients need real infrastructure to be trusted","a handful of journey tests guard the flows that would hurt most if broken","each layer trades feedback speed for realism"],"examples":[{"title":"Unit test vs integration test for the same payment feature","lang":"java","code":"// Unit: pure domain logic, no Spring, runs in milliseconds\nclass RefundPolicyTest {\n\n    private final RefundPolicy policy = new RefundPolicy(Duration.ofDays(30));\n\n    @ParameterizedTest\n    @CsvSource({\"29, true\", \"30, true\", \"31, false\"})\n    void refundAllowedWithinWindow(int daysAgo, boolean expected) {\n        Instant now = Instant.parse(\"2026-03-01T10:00:00Z\");\n        Payment payment = Payment.captured(new BigDecimal(\"120.00\"), now.minus(Duration.ofDays(daysAgo)));\n        assertThat(policy.canRefund(payment, now)).isEqualTo(expected);\n    }\n}\n\n// Integration: real PostgreSQL, real schema from Flyway, real constraints\n@DataJpaTest\n@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace.NONE)\n@Testcontainers\nclass PaymentRepositoryIT {\n\n    @Container\n    @ServiceConnection\n    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>(\"postgres:16-alpine\");\n\n    @Autowired PaymentRepository payments;\n\n    @Test\n    void rejectsDuplicateIdempotencyKey() {\n        payments.saveAndFlush(PaymentEntity.pending(\"idem-123\", new BigDecimal(\"50.00\")));\n        assertThatThrownBy(() -> payments.saveAndFlush(PaymentEntity.pending(\"idem-123\", new BigDecimal(\"50.00\"))))\n            .isInstanceOf(DataIntegrityViolationException.class);\n    }\n}","note":"The unit test covers boundary days exhaustively in microseconds; the integration test proves something only the real database can: that the unique constraint on the idempotency key exists and is translated into Spring's exception hierarchy."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Where does `@SpringBootTest` fit, and what does it cost?","a":"It starts the full application context, so it is an integration test even without external infrastructure, and each distinct configuration (different mocks, properties or profiles) creates a new context that can take seconds to start. Use slices such as `@WebMvcTest` or `@DataJpaTest` for focused boundary tests, keep a few full-context tests for wiring, and keep configuration consistent so Spring's context cache is reused."}],"extra":["**Context caching:** Spring caches application contexts across test classes with identical configuration; each unique combination of properties, profiles or `@MockitoBean` (which replaces `@MockBean`, deprecated since Boot 3.4) creates a new context, so standardize test base classes.","**Component tests:** one service running in-process with real infrastructure containers and stubbed neighbors, tested through its HTTP or messaging API, is often the best value layer for microservices.","**Unit means behavior, not class:** sociable unit tests that use real collaborators inside the same module are less brittle than one test class per production class with everything else mocked.","**Split the build:** Maven Surefire runs `*Test` classes and Failsafe runs `*IT` classes in the `integration-test` phase, so CI can report fast unit feedback first.","**Assert outcomes:** integration tests should assert on observable state (rows, published events, HTTP responses), not on which internal methods were called."],"redFlags":["mocks the database in tests meant to verify SQL","duplicates the same assertions at every level","has no explanation for why the browser tests are flaky"]},{"id":163,"category":"Testing","question":"@Mock, @Spy and @InjectMocks: what does each do, and how do mocks work in Spring Boot tests?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`@Mock` creates a stand-in that records calls and returns empty defaults until stubbed, `@Spy` wraps a real object so real methods run unless stubbed, and `@InjectMocks` builds the class under test with those doubles. In Spring tests, `@MockitoBean` and `@MockitoSpyBean` replace or wrap beans in the context, superseding Spring Boot's `@MockBean` and `@SpyBean`; every distinct set of overrides costs a separately cached context.","answer":["A Mockito mock records every invocation and, until stubbed, returns empty values: `null`, 0, false, empty collections, `Optional.empty()`; Mockito 5's default inline mock maker can mock final classes too. Stub with `when(...).thenReturn(...)` or BDD `given(...).willReturn(...)`, verify with `verify` or `then(...).should()`, capture arguments with an `ArgumentCaptor`. A spy runs real methods unless stubbed, with two traps: Mockito spies a copy of the instance you pass, and `when(spy.method())` calls the real method while stubbing, so use `doReturn(value).when(spy).method()`.","`@InjectMocks` instantiates the class under test, trying the biggest constructor, then setters, then fields, and silently leaves unmatched dependencies null; many teams prefer `new RefundService(gateway, refunds)` in `@BeforeEach`, where a new constructor parameter is a compile error. The annotations need `@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)`, which also enables strict stubs: an unused stub fails with `UnnecessaryStubbingException`, a stub called with other arguments with `PotentialStubbingProblem`, catching setups that no longer match the code. Mock collaborators across boundaries (gateways, publishers); use real value objects and domain logic.","In a Spring test the context creates the beans, so `@InjectMocks` has nothing to inject into. `@MockitoBean` replaces the bean of that type with a mock (creating one if none exists), so every bean depending on it receives the mock; `@MockitoSpyBean` wraps the existing bean. Both come from Spring Framework 6.2 (`org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito`); Boot's `@MockBean` and `@SpyBean` were deprecated in 3.4 and removed in 4.0. Context mocks are reset after each test. The cost is the context cache: overrides are part of the cache key, so classes with different mock sets each start a full context. Declare shared overrides in one base class."],"keyPoints":["mock: defaults until stubbed; spy: real calls unless stubbed","@InjectMocks fails silently","@MockitoBean and @MockitoSpyBean replace @MockBean and @SpyBean"],"examples":[{"title":"Plain Mockito: mocks, a spy and strict stubs","lang":"java","code":"@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)              // processes annotations, strict stubs\nclass RefundServiceTest {\n\n    @Mock PaymentGateway gateway;                // external PSP, never called for real\n    @Mock RefundRepository refunds;\n    @Spy FeePolicy fees = new FeePolicy();       // real fee rules, overridable per test\n    @InjectMocks RefundService service;          // built with the three doubles\n\n    @Captor ArgumentCaptor<Refund> saved;\n\n    @Test\n    void refundsCaptureMinusFee() {\n        given(gateway.refund(\"cap-91\", Money.sek(\"97.00\"))).willReturn(GatewayResult.accepted(\"rf-1\"));\n\n        service.refund(\"cap-91\", Money.sek(\"100.00\"));\n\n        then(refunds).should().save(saved.capture());\n        assertThat(saved.getValue().amount()).isEqualTo(Money.sek(\"97.00\"));   // real 3.00 fee\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void waivesFeeForGoodwillRefunds() {\n        // BAD: when(fees.feeFor(any())) runs the real method while stubbing\n        doReturn(Money.sek(\"0.00\")).when(fees).feeFor(any());\n        given(gateway.refund(\"cap-92\", Money.sek(\"100.00\"))).willReturn(GatewayResult.accepted(\"rf-2\"));\n\n        service.refund(\"cap-92\", Money.sek(\"100.00\"));\n\n        then(gateway).should().refund(\"cap-92\", Money.sek(\"100.00\"));\n    }\n}","note":"If `RefundService` gains a constructor parameter, `@InjectMocks` passes null and the test fails later with a confusing `NullPointerException`. A leftover stub the code never calls fails with `UnnecessaryStubbingException`."},{"title":"Context-level doubles with @MockitoBean and @MockitoSpyBean","lang":"java","code":"@SpringBootTest\nclass CheckoutFlowTest {\n\n    @MockitoBean                          // replaces PaymentGateway everywhere in the context\n    PaymentGateway paymentGateway;\n\n    @MockitoSpyBean                       // wraps the real bean: real behavior, verifiable\n    AuditPublisher auditPublisher;\n\n    @Autowired CheckoutService checkout;  // real bean, wired with the mock and the spy\n\n    @Test\n    void publishesAuditEventWhenAuthorized() {\n        given(paymentGateway.authorize(any())).willReturn(Authorization.approved(\"psp-123\"));\n\n        checkout.placeOrder(new PlaceOrder(\"acct-7\", Money.sek(\"249.00\")));\n\n        then(auditPublisher).should().publish(argThat(e -> e.type().equals(\"PAYMENT_AUTHORIZED\")));\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void declinedPaymentPublishesNothing() {\n        given(paymentGateway.authorize(any())).willReturn(Authorization.declined(\"insufficient_funds\"));\n\n        assertThatThrownBy(() -> checkout.placeOrder(new PlaceOrder(\"acct-7\", Money.sek(\"249.00\"))))\n                .isInstanceOf(PaymentDeclinedException.class);\n        then(auditPublisher).should(never()).publish(any());\n    }\n}\n// Cache key includes {mock PaymentGateway, spy AuditPublisher}: a class\n// mocking only PaymentGateway starts a second full context.","note":"Context mocks are reset after each test, so stubbing in one test does not leak into the next."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does when(spy.get(0)).thenReturn(...) throw on a spied empty list?","a":"Java evaluates `spy.get(0)` before Mockito can intercept anything, so the real `get(0)` runs and throws `IndexOutOfBoundsException`. `doReturn(\"x\").when(spy).get(0)` stubs without invoking the method; the same form is needed for void methods and methods with side effects."},{"q":"Can Mockito mock static methods and final classes?","a":"Yes: with the default inline mock maker final classes mock normally, and `mockStatic(...)` in try-with-resources replaces static methods on the current thread until closed. Treat static mocking as a last resort for legacy code; injecting a `Clock` or wrapping the call in an interface keeps tests simpler."}],"extra":["**Mockito agent on modern JDKs:** the inline mock maker attaches an agent at runtime, which recent JDKs warn about; Mockito recommends a `-javaagent` in the test runner setup.","**Matchers are all or nothing:** if one argument uses a matcher, all must (`eq(\"SEK\")`, `any()`), or Mockito throws `InvalidUseOfMatchersException`.","**verifyNoMoreInteractions sparingly:** it turns every harmless new call into a failure.","**Shared context mocks:** schedulers and listeners in the context can call a `@MockitoBean` mid-test and confuse `verify`.","**Spying the class under test:** stubbing its own methods usually means it does two jobs; extract a collaborator."],"redFlags":["Thinks a spy delegates to the original instance","cannot say what @InjectMocks does when injection fails","uses @MockBean in new code or a unique context mock set per class"]},{"id":164,"category":"Testing","question":"@SpringBootTest vs test slices like @WebMvcTest and @DataJpaTest: when do you use each?","level":"Basic","tldr":"`@SpringBootTest` starts the whole application context, for a few end-to-end flows; slices load one layer: `@WebMvcTest` the MVC infrastructure and chosen controllers, `@DataJpaTest` JPA, repositories and a transactional database. Use slices for fast checks of HTTP mapping and queries, a real database via Testcontainers and `@ServiceConnection`, and few distinct context configurations.","answer":["`@SpringBootTest` finds your `@SpringBootApplication` class and builds the context the application would: every bean, all auto-configuration, real properties. By default it uses a mock servlet environment without a port; add `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` for a `MockMvc`, or `webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT` for a real server. It suits a handful of tests proving the pieces fit, such as a checkout through security, service, database and outbound event, not checking that a missing field returns 400, where it pays for the whole application and fails for unrelated reasons.","Slices apply one layer's auto-configuration and filter component scanning. `@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)` loads MVC infrastructure, that controller, `@ControllerAdvice`, converters, filters and `WebMvcConfigurer` beans, plus Spring Security when present, but no `@Service` or `@Repository`, so collaborators become `@MockitoBean`s; it covers routing, binding, validation, status codes and JSON. `@DataJpaTest` loads the `DataSource`, JPA, repositories, Flyway or Liquibase and `TestEntityManager`, rolls back each test, and covers mappings, queries and constraints. `@JsonTest` and `@RestClientTest` work the same way. Spring Boot 4 moved slices into technology modules with test starters such as `spring-boot-starter-webmvc-test`.","Two production lessons. Test against the database you run: H2 accepts SQL PostgreSQL rejects and lacks its locking and constraint behavior, so use Testcontainers with `@ServiceConnection` (since Boot 3.4 `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase` leaves a container connection alone, no `replace = NONE` needed). And mind the context cache: Spring caches each distinct configuration (classes, profiles, properties, bean overrides) within the JVM, so three configurations mean three startups, while scattered `@MockitoBean` sets, per-class properties and `@DirtiesContext` can push it to sixty. Standardize a few base classes."],"keyPoints":["@SpringBootTest: full context, a few end-to-end flows","@WebMvcTest: HTTP contract; @DataJpaTest: mappings and queries","real database through Testcontainers, few distinct contexts"],"examples":[{"title":"@WebMvcTest for the HTTP contract, @DataJpaTest on real PostgreSQL","lang":"java","code":"@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)           // MVC infrastructure plus this controller\n@Import(SecurityConfig.class)                // the real security rules\nclass OrderControllerTest {\n\n    @Autowired MockMvc mvc;\n    @MockitoBean OrderService orders;        // services are outside the slice\n\n    @Test\n    @WithMockUser(authorities = \"SCOPE_orders.write\")\n    void rejectsOrderWithoutLines() throws Exception {\n        mvc.perform(post(\"/api/orders\").contentType(APPLICATION_JSON)\n                        .content(\"{\\\"accountId\\\": \\\"acct-7\\\", \\\"lines\\\": []}\"))\n           .andExpect(status().isBadRequest());\n        then(orders).shouldHaveNoInteractions();\n    }\n}\n\n@DataJpaTest                                 // JPA, repositories, Flyway; rollback per test\n@Testcontainers\nclass OrderRepositoryTest {\n\n    @Container\n    @ServiceConnection                       // DataSource points at the container\n    static PostgreSQLContainer postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer(\"postgres:17-alpine\");\n\n    @Autowired OrderRepository orders;\n    @Autowired TestEntityManager em;\n\n    @Test\n    void findsOpenOrdersOldestFirst() {\n        Order newer = em.persist(Order.open(\"acct-7\", Instant.parse(\"2026-10-01T10:00:00Z\")));\n        Order older = em.persist(Order.open(\"acct-7\", Instant.parse(\"2026-09-01T10:00:00Z\")));\n        em.flush();\n\n        assertThat(orders.findOpenByAccount(\"acct-7\")).containsExactly(older, newer);\n    }\n}","note":"Neither test starts the broker or the other 200 beans. `PostgreSQLContainer` without a type parameter is Testcontainers 2 (`org.testcontainers.postgresql`); 1.x uses `PostgreSQLContainer<?>` from `org.testcontainers.containers`."},{"title":"Full-context tests sharing one cached context and one container","lang":"java","code":"@SpringBootTest\n@AutoConfigureMockMvc                         // @SpringBootTest alone has no MockMvc\n@Import(TestcontainersConfig.class)\nabstract class IntegrationTestBase {\n\n    @MockitoBean PaymentGateway paymentGateway;   // the one external fake, declared once\n    @Autowired protected MockMvc mvc;\n}\n\n@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)\nclass TestcontainersConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    @ServiceConnection\n    PostgreSQLContainer postgres() {           // lives as long as the cached context\n        return new PostgreSQLContainer(\"postgres:17-alpine\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass CheckoutFlowIT extends IntegrationTestBase {\n\n    @Test\n    @WithMockUser(authorities = \"SCOPE_orders.write\")\n    void placesOrder() throws Exception {\n        given(paymentGateway.authorize(any())).willReturn(Authorization.approved(\"psp-1\"));\n\n        mvc.perform(post(\"/api/orders\").contentType(APPLICATION_JSON).content(Fixtures.ONE_GRINDER))\n           .andExpect(status().isCreated());\n    }\n}\n// RefundFlowIT extends the same base: same cache key, no second startup.","note":"Container beans share the cached context's lifecycle, so every class reusing the context reuses the database. Adding `@MockitoBean FraudClient` to one subclass alone would build a second context."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does a @WebMvcTest fail with 'No qualifying bean of type OrderService'?","a":"The slice excludes `@Service` beans, but the controller's constructor needs one, so provide it as a `@MockitoBean`. Do not widen the slice with `@ComponentScan` or switch to `@SpringBootTest`; that discards the speed and focus the slice exists for."},{"q":"Should @DataJpaTest tests rely on the automatic rollback?","a":"Usually yes: it keeps tests independent. But the test runs in one transaction, so SQL may not reach the database until a flush and commit-time behavior (deferred constraints, after-commit listeners) is never exercised. Flush before asserting; test commit behavior with `@Transactional(propagation = NOT_SUPPORTED)` plus cleanup."}],"extra":["**Cache limit:** Spring keeps up to 32 contexts per JVM by default (`spring.test.context.cache.maxSize`), evicting the least recently used.","**Find the culprit:** DEBUG logging for `org.springframework.test.context.cache` shows cache hits and misses.","**MockMvcTester:** Spring Framework 6.2 added `MockMvcTester` with AssertJ-style assertions.","**MockMvc is not a server:** it calls the `DispatcherServlet` in process; real HTTP needs a `RANDOM_PORT` test.","**Static containers:** a static `@Container` field stops when its class finishes, even if the cached context still uses it; prefer container beans."],"redFlags":["Uses @SpringBootTest for every test, including validation checks","tests repositories against H2 while production runs PostgreSQL","does not know that each distinct mock or property set costs a new context"]},{"id":165,"category":"Testing","question":"Your repository tests pass against H2 but fail in production on a PostgreSQL-specific query. How would Testcontainers have helped?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Testcontainers starts real, disposable dependencies such as PostgreSQL, Kafka or Redis in Docker from test code, so integration tests exercise production-like behavior while staying isolated, reproducible and runnable anywhere Docker is available.","answer":["Mocks and in-memory substitutes lie exactly at the boundary where many bugs live. H2 in PostgreSQL mode differs in SQL dialect, `jsonb`, `ON CONFLICT`, partial indexes, locking, isolation and error codes, so tests pass while production fails. **Testcontainers** starts the real engine (the same major version as production, such as `postgres:16`) in Docker from the test lifecycle, and your real Flyway migrations run against it.","It keeps tests **isolated and reproducible**: every run gets a fresh container on a random port, so there is no shared, dirty dev database, no dependence on another team's data, and the same test behaves identically on a laptop and in CI. The Ryuk sidecar removes containers even if the JVM crashes. Modules exist for PostgreSQL, Kafka, Redis, LocalStack, Keycloak and more, and since Spring Boot 3.1 `@ServiceConnection` wires connection details automatically, without `@DynamicPropertySource` boilerplate.","Trade-offs: CI needs a Docker-compatible runtime, containers add seconds of startup, and images should be cached. Use it for boundary tests (repositories, messaging, caches, HTTP clients), not for pure logic that unit tests cover faster. The production payoff is catching migration errors, constraint violations, and transaction or locking bugs before deploy rather than on the first production run."],"keyPoints":["tests run against the same database engine and version as production","containers are created per run and thrown away, so no state leaks between runs","the setup lives in test code, so CI and laptops behave the same","the cost is Docker on every build agent and some startup time"],"examples":[{"title":"Shared PostgreSQL container with @ServiceConnection","lang":"java","code":"@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)\nclass ContainersConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    @ServiceConnection   // Boot builds the DataSource from the container's host, port and credentials\n    PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres() {\n        return new PostgreSQLContainer<>(\"postgres:16-alpine\");\n    }\n}\n\n@SpringBootTest\n@Import(ContainersConfig.class)   // same config in every IT: one cached context, one container\nclass AccountLedgerIT {\n\n    @Autowired LedgerRepository ledger;\n    @Autowired JdbcTemplate jdbc;\n\n    @BeforeEach\n    void cleanUp() {\n        jdbc.execute(\"TRUNCATE account_balance\");\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void upsertKeepsLatestBalance() {\n        // upsertBalance uses INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (account_id) DO UPDATE, PostgreSQL semantics\n        ledger.upsertBalance(\"acc-42\", new BigDecimal(\"100.00\"));\n        ledger.upsertBalance(\"acc-42\", new BigDecimal(\"75.50\"));\n\n        BigDecimal balance = jdbc.queryForObject(\n            \"SELECT balance FROM account_balance WHERE account_id = ?\", BigDecimal.class, \"acc-42\");\n        assertThat(balance).isEqualByComparingTo(\"75.50\");\n    }\n}","note":"The container is a Spring bean, so its lifecycle follows the cached application context and every test class importing the same configuration reuses it. Tests are isolated by data cleanup, not by new containers."},{"title":"Bad vs good test configuration","lang":"properties","code":"# BAD: src/test/resources/application-test.properties\n# spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb;MODE=PostgreSQL\n# spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop\n# Schema generated from entities, Flyway skipped, PostgreSQL behavior never exercised\n\n# GOOD: no datasource URL at all, @ServiceConnection supplies it\nspring.flyway.enabled=true\nspring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate","note":"With `validate`, Hibernate checks the entity mapping against the schema Flyway built, so a missing migration fails the test instead of production startup."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does Spring Boot's `@ServiceConnection` simplify the setup?","a":"Since Spring Boot 3.1, annotating a container field or bean with `@ServiceConnection` lets Boot derive connection details (JDBC URL, username, password, Kafka bootstrap servers) from the running container, so the `@DynamicPropertySource` boilerplate goes away. Defining containers as beans in a `@TestConfiguration` also lets you reuse them for local development with `SpringApplication.from(...).with(...)`."},{"q":"When is an in-memory fake still the better choice?","a":"For unit tests of domain logic that should not care about storage at all, and for very large suites where millisecond feedback matters more than SQL fidelity. A fake repository is fine there, provided the real adapter is covered separately by container-based tests."}],"extra":["**Dev-time containers:** since Boot 3.1 a test main method such as `SpringApplication.from(OrdersApplication::main).with(ContainersConfig.class).run(args)` runs the app locally against the same containers, replacing hand-maintained docker-compose files for development.","**Wait strategies:** modules ship sensible readiness checks, but a `GenericContainer` needs an explicit `waitingFor(Wait.forHttp(\"/health\"))` or log-based wait; testing before the dependency is ready is a classic flakiness source.","**Pin image tags:** use the production major and minor version, never `latest`, or an upstream release silently changes behavior under your tests.","**Failure injection:** the Toxiproxy module adds latency, bandwidth limits and dropped connections between the app and a real container, which is the honest way to test timeouts and retries.","**CI runtime options:** Testcontainers needs a Docker API (socket mount, Docker-in-Docker or a remote engine such as Testcontainers Cloud); locked-down runners that disable Ryuk must clean up containers another way."],"redFlags":["uses an in-memory database that only emulates the production dialect","pins the container image to `latest`, so tests change without a code change","starts a new container for every test method and then complains about speed"]},{"id":166,"category":"Testing","question":"Every refactor breaks dozens of tests even when behavior is unchanged. What is likely wrong with how they use mocks?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Mock only the boundaries you do not own or cannot run cheaply and assert on observable outcomes; mocking internals, value objects and every call makes tests brittle, painful to refactor and blind to real bugs.","answer":["The common mistakes all couple the test to how the code works instead of what it does. **Mocking implementation details**: every collaborator, including internal helpers of the same module, is mocked, so the test mirrors the code, breaks on every refactor, and still passes when behavior is wrong. **Over-specifying calls**: verifying every interaction, exact argument matching, `verifyNoMoreInteractions` and `inOrder` where order does not matter, so harmless changes fail the build. **Mocking owned value objects**: `Money`, `Order`, records and DTOs are cheap to construct, and their behavior is part of what you should be testing.","Related traps: partial mocks or spies on the class under test; mocks returning mocks (a Law of Demeter smell); `any()` everywhere so wrong arguments pass; static mocks for time and UUIDs instead of injecting `Clock` and an ID generator; mocking third-party types you do not own such as `RestClient` or `KafkaTemplate`; and mocking the repository in a test whose real risk is the SQL.","The better approach: test **observable behavior** through the public API (return values, resulting state, messages sent across real boundaries). Mock or fake only the **external boundaries**: payment provider, email gateway, other services, the clock. Use `verify` only when the interaction is the behavior, such as a capture command sent to the PSP, and match only the arguments that matter. Mockito's strict stubs (the default with `MockitoExtension`) also flag unused stubbing that has gone stale."],"keyPoints":["tests that verify call sequences couple themselves to the implementation","simple domain types should be real objects, not doubles","doubles belong at boundaries the team does not own or cannot run cheaply","asserting on outputs and state lets internals change freely"],"examples":[{"title":"Bad vs good test for capturing a payment","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: mocks value objects and owned pure logic, asserts implementation steps\n@Test\nvoid capture_bad() {\n    Money amount = mock(Money.class);\n    FeeCalculator fees = mock(FeeCalculator.class);\n    when(fees.feeFor(amount)).thenReturn(mock(Money.class));\n    var service = new CaptureService(fees, psp, repository);\n\n    service.capture(paymentId, amount);\n\n    InOrder order = inOrder(fees, psp, repository);\n    order.verify(fees).feeFor(amount);\n    order.verify(repository).findById(paymentId);\n    order.verify(psp).capture(any(), any());\n    verifyNoMoreInteractions(fees, psp, repository);\n}\n\n// GOOD: real domain objects, fake repository, mock only the external PSP\n@Test\nvoid capturesAuthorizedPaymentAndRecordsFee() {\n    var repository = new InMemoryPaymentRepository();\n    repository.save(Payment.authorized(paymentId, Money.of(\"100.00\", \"SEK\")));\n    PaymentProvider psp = mock(PaymentProvider.class);\n    when(psp.capture(paymentId, Money.of(\"100.00\", \"SEK\"))).thenReturn(CaptureResult.approved(\"psp-777\"));\n    var service = new CaptureService(new FeeCalculator(), psp, repository);\n\n    service.capture(paymentId, Money.of(\"100.00\", \"SEK\"));\n\n    Payment saved = repository.findById(paymentId).orElseThrow();\n    assertThat(saved.status()).isEqualTo(PaymentStatus.CAPTURED);\n    assertThat(saved.fee()).isEqualTo(Money.of(\"1.80\", \"SEK\"));\n    assertThat(saved.pspReference()).isEqualTo(\"psp-777\");\n}","note":"The bad test breaks if the service loads the payment before computing the fee, yet never checks the fee value or the final status. The good test survives refactoring and fails only when observable behavior changes."},{"title":"A fake: a small working implementation of a port you own","lang":"java","code":"class InMemoryPaymentRepository implements PaymentRepository {\n\n    private final Map<PaymentId, Payment> store = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    @Override\n    public Payment save(Payment payment) {\n        store.put(payment.id(), payment);\n        return payment;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public Optional<Payment> findById(PaymentId id) {\n        return Optional.ofNullable(store.get(id));\n    }\n}","note":"Written once and reused by many tests, it lets assertions target resulting state instead of recorded calls."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is verifying a stubbed call usually redundant?","a":"If the code under test needs the stubbed value to produce its result, asserting on that result already proves the call happened, so `verify` only adds coupling to the implementation. Verification earns its place for commands with side effects you cannot observe otherwise, such as publishing an event or sending an email through a port."},{"q":"How can a mock hide a real bug?","a":"A mock returns whatever you told it, so if your assumption about the dependency is wrong (the real repository returns an empty `Optional`, throws on duplicates, or the API returns 404 instead of an empty list), the test passes while production fails. Covering the real adapter with integration tests, or running one shared contract suite against both the double and the real implementation, keeps those assumptions honest."}],"extra":["**Don't mock what you don't own:** a guideline from Growing Object-Oriented Software; wrap third-party APIs behind your own port, mock or fake the port in unit tests, and test the adapter against the real thing with WireMock or Testcontainers.","**Spring context cost:** every distinct set of `@MockitoBean` or `@MockBean` fields yields a separate cached application context, so scattering them across tests multiplies startup time.","**Coverage illusion:** a mock-heavy suite can show near 100% line coverage while SQL, serialization and transactions are broken; mutation testing with PIT exposes tests that execute code without asserting on it.","**Strictness is a feature:** `UnnecessaryStubbingException` from strict stubs points to dead setup; reaching for `lenient()` should be rare and explained.","**Stub queries, verify commands:** stub calls that return data and verify only calls with side effects; verifying a call you already stubbed adds brittleness without adding information."],"redFlags":["uses `any()` matchers everywhere, so the test passes with wrong arguments","stubs a method and then verifies the same call, testing the stub","keeps tests green while the real integration is broken because the mock diverged"]},{"id":167,"category":"Testing","question":"Your service is mostly glue between a database and two APIs. Should its test suite still follow the classic pyramid?","level":"Basic","tldr":"The pyramid suggests a wide base of quick, narrow tests, a smaller middle of integration tests and only a thin top of end-to-end tests; what matters is dependable, quick feedback, so a system's architecture and risks may call for another shape.","answer":["The test pyramid (popularized by Mike Cohn) recommends a broad base of fast, focused unit tests, fewer integration or service tests, and very few end-to-end tests. The reasoning: going up the pyramid, tests get slower, costlier to maintain, flakier and less precise. A failing unit test points to a line of code; a failing E2E test points to \"something in the system\".","The shape is a heuristic, not a law, and the percentages are not universal. The distribution should follow where risk and defects actually live. A thin CRUD or orchestration service has most of its bugs in SQL, mappings, serialization and configuration, so a \"honeycomb\" with more component and integration tests gives more confidence per minute than hundreds of unit tests on trivial code. A rule-heavy credit decision engine justifies a very wide unit base, including property-based tests. Microservice architectures shift weight to component and contract tests instead of cross-service E2E.","The goal is **reliable, fast feedback**: a PR suite that runs in minutes, fails only for real problems, and catches the defects that matter. Watch for the ice cream cone (mostly E2E or manual testing) and the hourglass (unit plus E2E with no integration layer). Reshape using data: suite duration, flake rate, and which layer would have caught each escaped production defect."],"keyPoints":["the shape is a heuristic about cost and speed, not a quota","thin logic with heavy integration shifts weight toward real-infrastructure tests","contract and component tests can replace much of the expensive top layer","judge the suite by how quickly and reliably it catches regressions"],"examples":[{"title":"Two services, two justified shapes","lang":"text","code":"payments-gateway: thin orchestration, heavy integration risk\nLayer              Count    Time      Catches\nUnit               ~300     8 s       fee rules, state machine, PSP error classification\nComponent          ~120     90 s      SQL, migrations, Kafka outbox, PSP client via WireMock\nContract           ~40      20 s      breaking changes with order-service and ledger\nE2E (staging)      6        6 min     authorize, capture, refund journeys with PSP sandbox\n\ncredit-decision-engine: rule heavy, few boundaries\nLayer              Count    Time      Catches\nUnit + property    ~4000    25 s      rule combinations, rounding, score boundaries\nIntegration        ~30      40 s      rule repository, cache, API mapping\nE2E                2        3 min     application to decision journey","note":"Neither shape follows a fixed ratio; each puts most of its tests where that service's defects actually come from, while keeping PR feedback in minutes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What are the testing trophy and the testing honeycomb, and when do they fit?","a":"They are alternative shapes that put most weight on integration or component tests: the trophy, popular for frontends, emphasizes integration tests above unit tests, and Spotify's honeycomb argues that microservices should mostly be tested through their real boundaries, with few isolated unit tests and very few integrated tests across services. They fit services whose risk lies in wiring and data rather than complex algorithms. The common lesson is to invest where defects actually occur."}],"extra":["**Other shapes:** the testing trophy (static analysis at the base, integration tests largest) comes from the frontend world, and Spotify's honeycomb puts integration tests of each service at the center for microservices.","**Static analysis is a layer:** the type system, Error Prone and nullness annotations (JSpecify) eliminate whole bug classes at zero runtime cost and belong at the bottom of any shape.","**Mutation testing:** PIT reveals whether a wide unit base actually asserts anything; high line coverage with weak assertions is a pyramid in name only.","**Production as a test layer:** synthetic monitoring, canary analysis and alerting on SLOs catch what no pre-production environment can, and can replace some staging E2E tests.","**Unit of behavior:** sociable unit tests that use real in-module collaborators blur the unit and integration boundary in a healthy way and survive refactoring better."],"redFlags":["writes unit tests full of mocks that only restate the implementation","cannot name what kind of defect each layer is meant to catch","lets the end-to-end suite grow until it takes hours"]},{"id":168,"category":"Testing","question":"What is test-driven development, and when does it pay off?","level":"Basic","tldr":"TDD is a short loop: write one failing test for the next small behavior (red), write the simplest code that passes (green), then clean up code and tests while everything stays green (refactor). It pays off most for domain logic with clear rules and for bug fixes, where the failing test proves the bug and then guards against regressions; it is awkward for exploratory spikes and thin framework glue, and its main benefit is fast design feedback.","answer":["In Kent Beck's cycle you keep a list of behaviors, pick one, and write a test that fails for the right reason: an assertion failure, not a compile error or broken fixture. Then you write just enough code to pass, even naive code, and only then refactor: remove duplication, rename, extract a type, with green tests as the safety net. A loop takes minutes. Each new example forces the code to generalize (triangulation), so the design grows from real cases instead of speculation and there is never more than a few minutes of untested code. Writing all tests after the code, or many tests up front, is not TDD.","Design feedback is the main payoff. A test that needs eight mocks, a static clock and a Spring context to check one fee rule shows the rule is tangled with infrastructure; TDD makes you feel that before the code exists and pushes logic into plain objects with explicit inputs such as a `Clock` or a rate table. Classic (inside-out) TDD uses real collaborators and asserts on results; mockist (outside-in) TDD starts at the API and mocks collaborators that do not exist yet, which defines roles but couples tests to interactions. Either way, test behavior through the public API; tests that mirror private methods break on every refactor.","It pays off where rules are clear and mistakes are expensive: pricing, fees, interest, limits, state machines, parsers and validation. For bug fixes it is the professional default even in teams that do not practice TDD elsewhere: reproduce the report as a failing test, fix it, keep the test as a regression guard. It is awkward when you do not yet know what to build or how an external API behaves (spike, throw the spike away, then test drive the real version), for UI layout, and for wiring a framework does, where an integration test says more. TDD is a tool for the logic that matters, not a ritual for every getter."],"keyPoints":["red, green, refactor in small steps","best for domain rules and bug fixes","hard-to-write tests are design feedback"],"examples":[{"title":"Three TDD cycles for a transfer fee rule","lang":"java","code":"// Cycle 1, red: the simplest rule\n@Test\nvoid domesticTransfersAreFree() {\n    assertThat(fees.feeFor(Transfer.domestic(Money.sek(\"500.00\")))).isEqualTo(Money.sek(\"0.00\"));\n}\n// green: feeFor returns Money.zero(SEK). Naive on purpose.\n\n// Cycle 2, red: a second example forces the real rule (triangulation)\n@Test\nvoid internationalTransfersCostOnePercent() {\n    assertThat(fees.feeFor(Transfer.international(Money.sek(\"5000.00\")))).isEqualTo(Money.sek(\"50.00\"));\n}\n// green: if (t.isInternational()) return t.amount().percent(1); else zero\n\n// Cycle 3, red: an edge from the test list\n@Test\nvoid internationalFeeHasATwentyFiveCrownMinimum() {\n    assertThat(fees.feeFor(Transfer.international(Money.sek(\"1000.00\")))).isEqualTo(Money.sek(\"25.00\"));\n}\n\n// green, then refactor: rate and minimum become data; the tests stay unchanged\nfinal class TransferFees {\n    private final FeeSchedule schedule;      // injected: no static config, no database\n\n    TransferFees(FeeSchedule schedule) { this.schedule = schedule; }\n\n    Money feeFor(Transfer transfer) {\n        if (!transfer.isInternational()) {\n            return Money.zero(transfer.amount().currency());\n        }\n        Money percentage = transfer.amount().percent(schedule.internationalRatePercent());\n        return percentage.max(schedule.internationalMinimum());\n    }\n}","note":"Each test fails before its code exists, and the refactor moves rate and minimum into `FeeSchedule` without touching an assertion. Tests written against behavior are what made that refactor safe."},{"title":"Bug fix test first: reproduce, fix, keep the guard","lang":"java","code":"// Ticket: a 30 day statement period in Stockholm was charged 31 days of\n// interest because it crosses the end of summer time on October 25.\n\n@Test\nvoid interestDaysAreCalendarDaysInTheAccountZone() {\n    ZoneId stockholm = ZoneId.of(\"Europe/Stockholm\");\n    StatementPeriod period = StatementPeriod.of(\n            ZonedDateTime.of(2026, 10, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, stockholm),\n            ZonedDateTime.of(2026, 10, 31, 0, 0, 0, 0, stockholm));\n\n    assertThat(interest.daysCharged(period)).isEqualTo(30);   // red: old code returned 31\n}\n\n// BAD (original): counts 24 hour blocks and rounds up\n//   long hours = Duration.between(period.start(), period.end()).toHours();   // 721\n//   return (long) Math.ceil(hours / 24.0);                                          // 31\n//\n// GOOD (fix): count calendar days in the account's zone\nlong daysCharged(StatementPeriod period) {\n    return ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(period.start().toLocalDate(), period.end().toLocalDate());   // 30\n}\n// The test stays, named after the behavior rather than the ticket number.","note":"If the test passes before the fix, you are reproducing the wrong thing. Edge cases like a time zone change rarely appear in the original tests, which is how the bug shipped."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does TDD work with Spring and a database?","a":"Test drive domain and service logic as plain objects first, where the rules live and the loop takes seconds. Then add slice tests at the edges, a `@WebMvcTest` for the HTTP contract and a `@DataJpaTest` with Testcontainers for queries, written first when the behavior is specified. An outer failing acceptance test that drives inner unit tests (double loop TDD) links the levels."},{"q":"Does TDD guarantee good coverage or good design?","a":"Coverage comes as a side effect, but says nothing about assertion quality; mutation testing with PIT checks whether tests notice a changed condition. Design is not automatic either: TDD gives fast feedback, and you still need judgment in the refactor step."}],"extra":["**Test list:** write behaviors and edge cases down before starting and add new ones as they occur, instead of chasing them; it keeps each cycle small.","**See it fail:** a test that was never red may pass for the wrong reason, such as asserting on a mock or a typo in the expected value.","**Legacy code:** before changing untested code, write characterization tests that pin current behavior, even odd behavior, then refactor and test drive the change.","**Refactor is mandatory:** skipping it yields passing tests and steadily worse code; the design improves in that step.","**Fakes for state:** an in-memory repository fake keeps tests readable and survives refactoring; mocks fit outgoing commands such as sending a payment request."],"redFlags":["Describes writing tests after the code as TDD","tests private methods or implementation details so refactoring breaks tests","is dogmatic in either direction: TDD for everything or never"]},{"id":169,"category":"Testing","question":"A provider team renamed a JSON field and broke three consumers in production. How could contract tests have caught it?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Contract testing verifies that a consumer's expectations of an API or message match what the provider delivers, each side checked independently in its own build, provided contracts are owned, versioned and verified.","answer":["In distributed systems a frequent integration failure is a provider changing an API or event in a way a consumer did not expect: a renamed field, a changed type, a new required field, a different status code. Unit tests cannot catch it because each side mocks its own idea of the other, and full E2E environments catch it late, slowly and flakily. **Contract testing** turns the expectations into an executable artifact: the consumer tests against a stub generated from the contract, and the provider verifies the same contract against its real implementation, each in its own CI build.","With Pact, the consumer test records interactions (given account acc-42 exists, `GET /accounts/acc-42` returns 200 with `id` and `status`) into a pact file published to a broker with the consumer version and branch. Provider CI fetches the relevant pacts, sets up data through provider states and replays them. Before either side deploys, `can-i-deploy` checks that the two versions were verified together. Spring Cloud Contract is the Spring-native alternative that generates provider tests and WireMock stubs from contracts, and message contracts cover Kafka events.","Contracts need **ownership** (who fixes a broken verification), **versioning** tied to what is deployed where, and **provider verification** as a merge gate, otherwise they rot into stale documentation. They check the shape and semantics consumers rely on, not business correctness, performance or each side's own infrastructure, so they complement integration tests of adapters and a few E2E journeys rather than replacing them."],"keyPoints":["each consumer records the requests and fields it actually depends on","the provider's build replays those expectations and fails before release","both sides are checked in their own pipelines, without a shared environment","a broker plus versioning shows which provider versions are safe to deploy"],"examples":[{"title":"Consumer side: Pact test in the payment service","lang":"java","code":"@ExtendWith(PactConsumerTestExt.class)\n@PactTestFor(providerName = \"account-service\")\nclass AccountClientPactTest {\n\n    @Pact(consumer = \"payment-service\")\n    V4Pact activeAccount(PactDslWithProvider builder) {\n        return builder\n            .given(\"account acc-42 exists and is active\")\n            .uponReceiving(\"a request for account acc-42\")\n                .path(\"/accounts/acc-42\")\n                .method(\"GET\")\n            .willRespondWith()\n                .status(200)\n                .headers(Map.of(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\"))\n                .body(new PactDslJsonBody()\n                    .stringValue(\"id\", \"acc-42\")\n                    .stringMatcher(\"status\", \"ACTIVE|BLOCKED\", \"ACTIVE\")\n                    .stringType(\"currency\", \"SEK\"))\n            .toPact(V4Pact.class);\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    @PactTestFor(pactMethod = \"activeAccount\")\n    void readsActiveAccount(MockServer mockServer) {\n        var client = new AccountClient(RestClient.create(mockServer.getUrl()));\n        Account account = client.find(\"acc-42\");\n        assertThat(account.status()).isEqualTo(AccountStatus.ACTIVE);\n    }\n}","note":"The consumer only pins the fields it actually uses and uses type or regex matchers instead of exact values, so the provider stays free to add fields or change unrelated data."},{"title":"Provider side: verifying all consumer pacts from the broker","lang":"java","code":"@Provider(\"account-service\")\n@PactBroker   // broker URL and consumer version selectors come from system properties\n@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)\nclass AccountProviderPactTest {\n\n    @LocalServerPort int port;\n    @Autowired AccountRepository accounts;\n\n    @BeforeEach\n    void target(PactVerificationContext context) {\n        context.setTarget(new HttpTestTarget(\"localhost\", port));\n    }\n\n    @TestTemplate\n    @ExtendWith(PactVerificationInvocationContextProvider.class)\n    void verifyPact(PactVerificationContext context) {\n        context.verifyInteraction();\n    }\n\n    @State(\"account acc-42 exists and is active\")\n    void activeAccount() {\n        accounts.save(new AccountEntity(\"acc-42\", \"ACTIVE\", \"SEK\"));\n    }\n}","note":"Each interaction from every consumer becomes a test against the real provider; provider states are the agreed setup hooks, so the state names are part of the contract too."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does Pact's `can-i-deploy` check add?","a":"The Pact Broker records which consumer and provider versions have been verified against each other and which are deployed to each environment. `can-i-deploy` queries that matrix before a release and fails if the version you are about to deploy has not been verified against the versions already running there. That turns contract testing into a deployment gate rather than only a build check."},{"q":"Do contract tests apply to Kafka events as well?","a":"Yes: Pact supports message contracts, where the consumer states the event shape it expects and the provider verifies that its producer code generates it. With Avro or Protobuf, a schema registry with compatibility rules (backward or full) enforces structural compatibility at publish time, which covers much of the same risk."}],"extra":["**can-i-deploy and environments:** `pact-broker can-i-deploy --pacticipant payment-service --version $GIT_SHA --to-environment production` checks the verification matrix, and `record-deployment` tells the broker what runs where so providers verify against deployed and main-branch consumer versions.","**Loose matching on both sides:** consumers should assert only fields they use, and providers must tolerate unknown fields; Spring Boot's auto-configured `ObjectMapper` already disables `FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES`.","**Event contracts:** for Kafka, combine message pacts or Spring Cloud Contract messaging with schema registry compatibility modes (BACKWARD or FULL for Avro and Protobuf); the registry checks structure, contracts check the semantics consumers rely on.","**Pending pacts:** a new consumer expectation can be marked pending so it reports failures to the provider without breaking its build until it has been verified once, which keeps consumer-driven workflows from blocking provider teams.","**Bi-directional option:** comparing consumer pacts with the provider's OpenAPI specification (offered by PactFlow) helps when the provider team cannot run Pact verification themselves."],"redFlags":["writes contracts that copy the full provider response instead of what the consumer uses","never runs provider verification in CI, so contracts go stale","confuses contract tests with functional tests of the provider's business logic"]},{"id":170,"category":"Testing","question":"A CI test fails about one run in twenty and passes when rerun. How would you track down the cause?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Handle a flaky test like any other defect: quarantine it and track its failure rate, recreate the failure under controlled conditions, locate the source of nondeterminism (clocks, random seeds, test order, threads, leftover data, outside services) and remove that source rather than masking it with retries.","answer":["A flaky test passes and fails on the same code, and the cause is always nondeterminism. **Time**: `now()` calls, time zones, DST, midnight and month end, timeouts too tight for a slow CI runner. **Randomness**: random test data, unseeded generators, iteration order of `HashMap` and `HashSet`. **Ordering**: tests that depend on execution order, or queries without `ORDER BY`. **Concurrency**: asserting before async work finishes, `Thread.sleep` as synchronization, or real races in the code. **Shared state**: static fields, mutated singleton beans in a cached Spring context, shared database rows, fixed ports, leftover files. **External dependencies**: real networks, third-party sandboxes, containers that are not yet ready.","Diagnose with data first: CI history, failure rate, and what differs between passing and failing runs (runner, parallelism, time of day, test order). Then reproduce: run the test in a loop with `@RepeatedTest`, run it alone and inside the full suite to reveal order dependence, randomize order with a logged seed, add parallelism or CPU pressure to expose races, and bisect the suite to find the test that pollutes state.","Fix the root cause: inject `Clock`, seed and log randomness, assert order-independently, replace sleeps with Awaitility polling on an observable condition, give each test its own data, use random ports and proper container wait strategies, and stub external systems. Meanwhile quarantine the test (tagged, excluded from the blocking gate, with an owner and a ticket) so the pipeline stays trusted. Blind retries hide real race conditions that also happen in production and teach the team that red builds are normal."],"keyPoints":["an intermittent failure is reporting real nondeterminism, in the test or in the code","rerun it many times, in random order and under load, to make it fail on demand","usual sources are wall-clock time, unseeded randomness, test order, threads, leftover data and network calls","quarantine keeps the pipeline trustworthy while the fix is in progress"],"examples":[{"title":"Bad vs good: async assertion and wall-clock time","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: races the async listener and depends on the machine clock and zone\n@Test\nvoid publishesInvoiceEvent_flaky() throws Exception {\n    orderService.complete(orderId);\n    Thread.sleep(500);                                   // sometimes too short on CI\n    assertThat(invoiceListener.received()).hasSize(1);\n    assertThat(invoiceRepository.findByOrder(orderId).dueDate())\n        .isEqualTo(LocalDate.now().plusDays(30));        // wrong around midnight or in another zone\n}\n\n// GOOD: fixed Clock injected, bounded polling on the observable outcome\n@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)\nstatic class FixedClockConfig {\n    @Bean\n    @Primary\n    Clock testClock() {\n        // 23:59:30 UTC is already 00:59:30 on Feb 1 in Stockholm: exercises the zone edge\n        return Clock.fixed(Instant.parse(\"2026-01-31T23:59:30Z\"), ZoneId.of(\"Europe/Stockholm\"));\n    }\n}\n\n@Test\nvoid publishesInvoiceEventWithDueDateInThirtyDays() {\n    orderService.complete(orderId);\n\n    await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(10))\n        .untilAsserted(() -> assertThat(invoiceListener.received()).hasSize(1));\n    assertThat(invoiceRepository.findByOrder(orderId).dueDate())\n        .isEqualTo(LocalDate.of(2026, 3, 3));\n}","note":"The service must call `LocalDate.now(clock)`, never the no-argument variant. Awaitility returns as soon as the event arrives, so the good test is both faster on average and robust on a slow runner."},{"title":"Exposing order dependence and shared state","lang":"properties","code":"# src/test/resources/junit-platform.properties\n# Randomize class and method order; the seed is logged, reuse it to reproduce a failure\njunit.jupiter.testclass.order.default=org.junit.jupiter.api.ClassOrderer$Random\njunit.jupiter.testmethod.order.default=org.junit.jupiter.api.MethodOrderer$Random\n# junit.jupiter.execution.order.random.seed=1726823411  (uncomment only to replay a logged order)\n# Run concurrently to surface static state, shared rows and fixed ports\njunit.jupiter.execution.parallel.enabled=true\njunit.jupiter.execution.parallel.mode.default=concurrent","note":"Running in a random but reproducible order turns hidden order dependence into a deterministic failure you can bisect."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you test asynchronous code without sleeps?","a":"Wait for the observable condition instead of a fixed time: Awaitility's `await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(5)).untilAsserted(...)` polls until the assertion passes or fails with a clear timeout. Where possible, inject the executor and use a synchronous one in unit tests, or expose a completion signal such as a `CompletableFuture` or `CountDownLatch`. Tests become faster on average and stable on slow CI machines."},{"q":"How can the order in which tests run cause flakiness?","a":"One test leaves state behind, such as rows in a shared database, a static field, a system property or a cached Spring context with modified beans, and a later test silently depends on it or trips over it. Running the suite in random order (JUnit 5's `MethodOrderer.Random`, or the `junit.jupiter.testmethod.order.default` setting) exposes these couplings. The fix is for each test to set up and clean up its own state."}],"extra":["**Detect, don't hide:** Surefire's `rerunFailingTestsCount` and Gradle's test-retry plugin report tests that pass on rerun as flaky; use that signal to feed a flake dashboard, not as a permanent cover.","**Transactional rollback hides behavior:** `@Transactional` tests roll back, which isolates data but skips commit-time behavior such as deferred constraints, deferred constraint triggers and `@TransactionalEventListener` handlers that run after commit.","**Unordered SQL results:** PostgreSQL guarantees no row order without `ORDER BY`, and the order can change after updates, vacuum or with parallel plans; use `containsExactlyInAnyOrder` or add the `ORDER BY`.","**Time zone in CI:** runners usually run in UTC while developers sit in CET, so set the JVM zone explicitly (`-Duser.timezone`) and periodically run the suite in a non-UTC zone to flush out hidden assumptions.","**Context pollution:** tests that mutate singleton beans or static state leak into Spring's cached context; `@DirtiesContext` fixes it at a large speed cost, so prefer not mutating shared beans at all."],"redFlags":["blames the CI environment without evidence","fixes timing issues by adding `Thread.sleep`","leaves quarantined tests disabled with no owner or deadline"]},{"id":171,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"A six-person team building a new product proposes twelve microservices from day one. What would you push back on?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Microservices are a bad fit when teams are few, boundaries are still unclear, scale is modest or automation and observability are weak; a modular monolith delivers most benefits without distributed failure modes.","answer":["Microservices are primarily an organizational scaling tool: they let many teams deploy independently. With one or two teams you pay for network calls, versioned contracts, distributed tracing, per-service pipelines and on-call rotations without the autonomy payoff. They are also a poor choice while boundaries are unclear, as in a new product still discovering its domain: a wrong boundary inside a monolith is a refactoring, across services it is a coordinated migration of APIs, data and deployments.","Every in-process call turned into a network call gains latency, partial failure, timeouts, retries and the need for idempotency, and every shared transaction becomes a saga with eventual consistency. Without mature CI/CD, centralized logging, tracing and alerting you end up with a distributed monolith: services that must deploy together and fail together. Low scale is another signal: a horizontally scaled monolith behind a load balancer handles far more traffic than most teams expect, and independent scaling only pays off when components have very different resource profiles.","The default I argue for is a modular monolith: one deployable with enforced module boundaries (Spring Modulith or ArchUnit rules), module-owned tables and in-process events. Refactoring stays cheap, and when a real signal appears you extract a module whose boundary is already proven."],"keyPoints":["early boundaries are guesses, and wrong ones are expensive to move between services","every network hop adds latency, partial failure and debugging effort","the number of services must match CI/CD, tracing and on-call maturity","one deployable with strict internal modules keeps most of the benefit"],"examples":[{"title":"Modular monolith with verified boundaries (Spring Modulith)","lang":"java","code":"// One deployable: com.acme.shop.orders, com.acme.shop.payments, com.acme.shop.inventory\n@SpringBootApplication\npublic class ShopApplication {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SpringApplication.run(ShopApplication.class, args);\n    }\n}\n\nclass ModularityTests {\n    @Test\n    void modulesRespectBoundaries() {\n        // Fails the build on cycles or when orders touches payments internals\n        ApplicationModules.of(ShopApplication.class).verify();\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass OrderService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;\n\n    OrderService(OrderRepository orders, ApplicationEventPublisher events) {\n        this.orders = orders;\n        this.events = events;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void place(Order order) {\n        orders.save(order);\n        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlaced(order.id(), order.total())); // no direct call into payments\n    }\n}","note":"Modules talk through events and published interfaces, exactly as services would, but a boundary mistake is still a refactoring inside one repository and one deployment."},{"title":"Decision signals","lang":"text","code":"Signal                               Modular monolith        Microservices\nTeams working on the system          1 to 3                  many, blocking each other\nDomain boundaries                    still moving            stable, proven in code\nScale profile                        uniform, moderate       parts differ by an order of magnitude\nDeployment automation, tracing       partial                 mature, self-service platform\nConsistency needs                    local ACID is valuable  eventual consistency acceptable\nRelease conflicts                    rare                    frequent release train blocking","note":"If most answers fall in the left column, microservices add cost and failure modes without solving a problem you actually have."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you keep a monolith modular enough to split later?","a":"Organize modules by business capability with a small public API each, and keep their internals hidden with package-private classes, JPMS or Spring Modulith. Forbid modules from touching each other's tables and check the dependency rules in CI with ArchUnit. Let modules talk through in-process events where reactions need not be synchronous, so extracting one later mostly means moving those events onto a broker."},{"q":"What is a distributed monolith?","a":"A set of services that must be deployed together, share a database or call each other in long synchronous chains, so they carry the network costs of microservices without independent change. Symptoms are coordinated releases, cascading outages and one schema change touching many repositories. It is usually worse than the monolith it replaced."}],"extra":["**Conway's Law:** service boundaries end up mirroring team communication; splitting a system owned by one team into ten services adds coordination overhead with no organizational benefit.","**Distributed monolith smell:** if features routinely require synchronized releases of several services, or services share database tables, you carry the costs of both architectures and the benefits of neither.","**Multiplied chores:** every service needs its own pipeline, dashboards, alerts, JDK and Spring Boot upgrades, CVE patching, secrets and runbooks; across dozens of services that is a platform team's worth of work.","**Public reversal:** the Amazon Prime Video monitoring team described moving a distributed serverless pipeline back into a single process and reported a large cost reduction, a good reminder that granularity depends on the workload.","**Extraction readiness:** a module with its own tables, communication only via events or a published interface and no cyclic dependencies can be extracted quickly; enforcing that inside the monolith is cheap insurance."],"redFlags":["proposes services before knowing which parts change together","ignores the cost of keeping data consistent across service boundaries","cannot describe what a modular monolith looks like in practice"]},{"id":172,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"Checkout reserves stock, charges a card and emails a confirmation. Which steps would you call synchronously, and which through messaging?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Use synchronous REST or gRPC when the caller needs the answer to proceed; use asynchronous messaging for decoupled reactions, load buffering and long-running workflows, deciding by latency, consistency, ordering and failure requirements.","answer":["Synchronous calls fit queries and commands where the caller cannot continue without the result: authorizing a card, fetching a price, validating an address. They are simple to reason about and debug and give immediate errors, but they couple availability and latency: if Payments is down or slow, Checkout is too, and a chain of calls multiplies tail latency.","Asynchronous messaging (Kafka, RabbitMQ, SQS) fits when the producer does not need the consumer's answer now: sending notifications, updating read models, starting fulfillment, or absorbing spikes by letting consumers work at their own pace. It enables long-running workflows and sagas, but you accept eventual consistency, duplicate delivery, limited ordering (Kafka orders only within a partition, so key by `orderId`), dead letter handling and harder debugging.","Async does not remove coupling, it changes its form. Temporal coupling goes away, but consumers still depend on the event's schema and meaning, and a producer changing `OrderPlaced` can silently break five teams. The deciding questions: does the user wait for this result, can the business tolerate seconds of staleness, what happens if the other side is down for an hour, and does ordering matter? Most real systems mix both, for example a synchronous API that accepts a command and returns `202`, backed by events internally."],"keyPoints":["call directly when the caller cannot continue without the answer","publish a message when others only need to react to what happened","a queue absorbs spikes and lets slow consumers catch up","messaging trades immediacy for retry, ordering and eventual consistency work"],"examples":[{"title":"Choosing per interaction","lang":"text","code":"Use case                                    Style   Why\nAuthorize card at checkout                  sync    user waits; result decides next step\nShow current account balance                sync    read that must be current\nSend order confirmation email               async   caller does not need the result; retry later\nUpdate search index or reporting model      async   eventual consistency is acceptable\nCredit decision calling a bureau (30 s)     async   long running: 202 plus status or notification\nReserve stock, charge, ship (saga)          async   multi-step workflow with compensation\n50k payouts at month end                    async   queue absorbs the burst, consumers scale out","note":"The style is chosen per interaction, not per system; one service typically exposes sync APIs and also publishes and consumes events."},{"title":"Sync call on the critical path, async event for everything else","lang":"java","code":"@Transactional\npublic OrderId checkout(Cart cart) {\n    // sync: we cannot confirm the order without the authorization result\n    AuthorizationResult auth = paymentsClient.post()\n            .uri(\"/authorizations\")\n            .body(new AuthorizeRequest(cart.total(), cart.cardToken(), cart.idempotencyKey()))\n            .retrieve()\n            .body(AuthorizationResult.class);\n    if (!auth.approved()) {\n        throw new PaymentDeclinedException(auth.declineCode());\n    }\n    Order order = orders.save(Order.placed(cart, auth.authorizationId()));\n\n    // async: fulfillment, email and analytics react on their own schedule.\n    // Keyed by order id so all events of one order stay ordered in one partition.\n    // Stored in the outbox table in this same transaction, published by a relay.\n    outbox.append(\"order-events\", order.id().toString(),\n            new OrderPlaced(order.id(), cart.customerId(), cart.total()));\n    return order.id();\n}","note":"Only the authorization is on the synchronous path; downstream reactions are decoupled, so an email outage cannot fail a checkout. In production, make the authorization call before the database transaction starts (for example, call a separate `@Transactional` service that only saves the order and the outbox row), so a slow PSP does not hold a pooled connection."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does a chain of synchronous calls hurt availability?","a":"Availabilities multiply: a request that depends on five services at 99.9% each can be at most about 99.5% available, and latencies add, with tail latency compounding fastest. Each hop also holds threads and connections while waiting downstream. Timeouts, caching, fallbacks and moving non-essential steps to messaging all shorten the chain the user waits on."},{"q":"When would you pick gRPC over REST for a synchronous call?","a":"For internal service-to-service calls with high volume or tight latency, where a protobuf contract, code generation, HTTP/2 multiplexing and streaming pay off. REST with JSON remains the better default for public APIs, browser clients and integrations where readability and tooling matter. gRPC also propagates deadlines natively, which helps keep timeout budgets consistent across hops."}],"extra":["**Availability math:** a request that synchronously depends on five services at 99.9% each is at best about 99.5% available; moving work off the critical path removes it from that product.","**Request and reply over a broker:** possible with reply topics and correlation IDs, but it usually combines sync-style waiting with async complexity; reserve it for long-running or bursty work.","**Backpressure signals:** sync calls push back instantly through timeouts and `429`; with messaging, consumer lag or queue depth is the primary health metric, because a growing backlog is invisible to producers.","**Ordering versus parallelism:** Kafka keys give per-key ordering, but a hot key serializes onto one consumer; competing consumers on a RabbitMQ queue lose ordering unless you use the single active consumer feature.","**Event contracts:** treat event schemas as public APIs with a schema registry and compatibility checks, and include an event ID and version so consumers can deduplicate and evolve."],"redFlags":["chains several synchronous calls and ignores the combined availability","reports success for an async step without showing the user it is pending","picks a style by habit without asking what the caller needs back"]},{"id":173,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"How do service discovery and client-side load balancing work?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Service discovery maps a service name to its current healthy instances, through a registry (Eureka, Consul) or the platform (Kubernetes Services and DNS). With client-side load balancing the caller holds the instance list and picks one per request (Spring Cloud LoadBalancer); with server-side balancing it calls one stable address and a proxy or kube-proxy picks.","answer":["Instances come and go with deploys, autoscaling and failures, so callers cannot hard-code addresses. Discovery has two parts, **registration** and **lookup**. With a registry such as Eureka or Consul, each instance registers on startup and stays listed by heartbeating (Eureka) or by passing health checks run by the Consul agent; callers query the registry and cache the result. On Kubernetes the platform registers for you: it tracks which pods match a Service's selector and pass their readiness probe, publishes them as EndpointSlices, and gives the Service a stable virtual IP and a DNS name such as `inventory-service.shop.svc.cluster.local`.","Balancing happens on the client or the server side. **Client side**: the caller holds the instance list and chooses per request. In Spring, Spring Cloud LoadBalancer (which replaced Netflix Ribbon) resolves a service id in the URL, `http://inventory-service/...`, round robin by default, with optional health-check filtering, zone preference and weighting. It saves a hop and allows per-request decisions, but every client must implement it and its list can be stale. **Server side**: the caller sends to one address and something else picks: an L7 load balancer, a mesh sidecar, or kube-proxy, which spreads **connections**, not requests, across ready pods behind the Service IP.","On Kubernetes, plain Services and DNS are usually enough, and adding Eureka creates a second source of truth that can disagree with the platform; a registry still makes sense on VMs, across clusters or in mixed environments. Whatever the mechanism, the list lags reality: an instance can die between health checks, so callers need timeouts, retries on another instance for idempotent requests, and circuit breaking. Long-lived gRPC connections through a Service are a separate trap: they stick to one pod."],"keyPoints":["a registry or the platform keeps the name to instances mapping","client side picks per request; server side hides instances behind one address","instance lists are always a little stale, so timeouts and retries still matter"],"examples":[{"title":"Client-side balancing with Spring Cloud LoadBalancer and RestClient","lang":"java","code":"// A Eureka or Consul discovery starter provides the DiscoveryClient;\n// spring-cloud-starter-loadbalancer provides the balancing.\n@Configuration\nclass HttpClientsConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    @LoadBalanced                                    // resolves service ids on each request\n    RestClient.Builder loadBalancedRestClientBuilder() {\n        return RestClient.builder();\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    RestClient inventoryClient(@LoadBalanced RestClient.Builder builder) {\n        return builder.clone()                       // the builder bean is a mutable singleton\n                .baseUrl(\"http://inventory-service\") // a service id, not a DNS name\n                .build();\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass StockClient {\n    private final RestClient inventory;\n\n    StockClient(RestClient inventoryClient) { this.inventory = inventoryClient; }\n\n    StockLevel stockFor(String sku) {\n        // per call: take the cached instances of inventory-service, pick one (round robin),\n        // rewrite to e.g. http://10.4.7.21:8080/api/stock/{sku}, then send\n        return inventory.get()\n                .uri(\"/api/stock/{sku}\", sku)\n                .retrieve()\n                .body(StockLevel.class);\n    }\n}","note":"When the discovery source does not check health itself, `spring.cloud.loadbalancer.configurations=health-check` filters instances by their `/actuator/health`. Either way keep client timeouts, because the cached list lags reality."},{"title":"Server-side discovery on Kubernetes, plus a headless Service for gRPC","lang":"yaml","code":"# Callers use http://inventory-service (same namespace) or the full DNS name;\n# kube-proxy spreads connections over ready pods\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: inventory-service\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: inventory\n  ports:\n    - name: http\n      port: 80\n      targetPort: 8080\n---\n# Headless: DNS returns every ready pod IP, so a gRPC client can balance per request\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: inventory-grpc\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  clusterIP: None\n  selector:\n    app: inventory\n  ports:\n    - name: grpc\n      port: 9090\n      targetPort: 9090\n---\n# Excerpt from the inventory Deployment: only ready pods appear behind either Service\nreadinessProbe:\n  httpGet:\n    path: /actuator/health/readiness\n    port: 8080\n  periodSeconds: 5\n  failureThreshold: 3","note":"Readiness is the registration: a pod that fails its probe, or starts terminating, leaves the endpoints within seconds, which is why graceful shutdown keeps serving briefly while that change propagates."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why do all gRPC calls go to one pod even though the Service has five?","a":"kube-proxy balances TCP connections, and a gRPC client multiplexes every request over one long-lived HTTP/2 connection, so it sticks to the pod it first connected to and pods added later get nothing until it reconnects. Fixes are a headless Service with the gRPC `round_robin` policy over DNS, a service mesh that balances per request, or a maximum connection age on the server so clients reconnect periodically."},{"q":"What happens to callers when the registry goes down?","a":"Clients keep using their cached instance lists, so existing traffic usually continues, but new instances are not discovered and dead ones are not removed. That is why registries run as clusters (Eureka servers replicate to peers and favor availability; Consul servers replicate with Raft and need a quorum to accept changes)."}],"extra":["**Eureka timing:** by default an instance renews every 30 seconds and is evicted after 90 seconds without renewal, and both server and clients cache the registry, so a crashed instance can stay in callers' lists for a minute or more.","**Self-preservation:** when many renewals stop at once, Eureka assumes a network problem and stops evicting, deliberately choosing stale entries over an empty registry.","**Retries need idempotency:** retrying on another instance (`spring.cloud.loadbalancer.retry.enabled`) is safe for reads and for requests with idempotency keys, not for a POST that may already have created the order.","**Zone awareness:** preferring instances in the caller's zone cuts latency and cross-zone cost; Spring Cloud LoadBalancer has zone preference and Kubernetes has topology-aware routing, both falling back to other zones.","**Spring Cloud Kubernetes:** it offers a `DiscoveryClient` backed by the Kubernetes API, so `@LoadBalanced` clients can balance across pod endpoints without a mesh."],"redFlags":["Adds Eureka on Kubernetes by reflex without knowing what a Service provides","treats the instance list as always accurate and skips timeouts","believes kube-proxy balances individual HTTP/2 requests"]},{"id":174,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"One slow downstream API ties up every Tomcat thread and the whole service stops responding. Which pattern prevents that, and how?","level":"Basic","tldr":"A bulkhead gives each dependency or workload its own bounded pool of threads, connections or concurrent calls, so one slow dependency can exhaust only its own compartment while the rest of the service stays available.","answer":["Without isolation, all outbound calls share one resource: the servlet request threads, one HTTP connection pool or one executor. When a single dependency such as a bank API starts answering in 30 seconds, requests to it pile up and hold every thread, and endpoints that never touch the bank (order history, health checks) stop responding too. One slow dependency turns partial degradation into a full outage.","A bulkhead partitions those resources: a separate concurrency limit, thread pool or connection pool per dependency or per workload class. When the bank compartment is full, new calls to it are rejected immediately (return `503` or a `PENDING` status) while everything else keeps its capacity. Variants include a semaphore bulkhead (a cap on concurrent calls on the caller's thread), a thread pool bulkhead with a bounded queue, separate database pools for batch and online traffic, and separate deployments or node pools for critical flows.","It is not a circuit breaker: a breaker decides whether to call based on recent failures, while a bulkhead limits how much capacity calls may occupy, even when they all succeed slowly. They complement each other, with timeouts bounding how long a slot stays taken. Size compartments with Little's Law: concurrency equals throughput times latency, so 50 requests per second at 200 ms needs about 10 slots."],"keyPoints":["give each dependency its own capped pool of threads, connections or permits","a full compartment rejects quickly instead of queueing forever","unrelated endpoints keep working while one dependency is slow","size each limit from expected throughput multiplied by latency"],"examples":[{"title":"Separate Resilience4j bulkheads per dependency","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass BulkheadConfiguration {\n\n    @Bean\n    Bulkhead bankApiBulkhead() {\n        return Bulkhead.of(\"bankApi\", BulkheadConfig.custom()\n                .maxConcurrentCalls(10)                // about 50 rps x 200 ms\n                .maxWaitDuration(Duration.ZERO)         // reject at once, never park request threads\n                .build());\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    Bulkhead fraudApiBulkhead() {                      // separate compartment, separate limit\n        return Bulkhead.of(\"fraudApi\", BulkheadConfig.custom().maxConcurrentCalls(20).build());\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass PayoutService {\n    private final Bulkhead bankApiBulkhead;\n    private final BankClient bank;\n\n    PayoutService(Bulkhead bankApiBulkhead, BankClient bank) {\n        this.bankApiBulkhead = bankApiBulkhead;\n        this.bank = bank;\n    }\n\n    PayoutStatus submit(Payout payout) {\n        try {\n            return bankApiBulkhead.executeSupplier(() -> bank.transfer(payout));\n        } catch (BulkheadFullException e) {\n            return PayoutStatus.queuedForRetry(payout.id());   // degrade, do not block\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"A slow bank can occupy at most 10 concurrent calls; fraud checks and every other endpoint keep their own capacity, and callers get a fast degraded answer instead of a hung request."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Semaphore bulkhead or thread pool bulkhead?","a":"A semaphore bulkhead caps concurrent calls on the caller's own thread: it is cheap and has no hand-off, but it cannot interrupt a blocking call, so it relies on client timeouts. A thread pool bulkhead runs calls on a dedicated bounded pool and queue, isolating caller threads and allowing timeouts through futures, at the cost of context switches and having to propagate MDC and security context. Resilience4j offers both as `Bulkhead` and `ThreadPoolBulkhead`; with virtual threads the semaphore style is usually enough."},{"q":"How can Little's law size a bulkhead?","a":"Calls in flight equal arrival rate times latency, so 200 requests per second at 50 ms needs about 10 concurrent slots. Size from a realistic high percentile latency plus headroom, not the median, and remember the limit applies per instance. When the dependency slows down, the same formula shows how quickly the compartment fills and starts rejecting."}],"extra":["**Virtual threads shift the scarce resource:** with Java 21 virtual threads (`spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` in Spring Boot 3.2 and later), request threads are cheap, so thread pool bulkheads matter less and explicit concurrency limits matter more; unbounded virtual threads can flood a dependency.","**Connection pools are bulkheads:** give each downstream HTTP client its own pool with a per-route maximum, and consider a separate HikariCP pool for reporting or batch jobs so a long query cannot starve checkout of connections.","**Process-level isolation:** separate Kubernetes deployments for critical and non-critical traffic, with resource requests and limits, also isolate memory leaks, GC pauses and noisy neighbors.","**Tenant bulkheads:** per-tenant concurrency limits stop one customer's batch integration from starving everyone else, the same pattern applied to callers instead of dependencies.","**Watch saturation:** monitor available permits and rejection counts per bulkhead; rejections under normal load mean an undersized compartment, rejections only during incidents mean it is doing its job."],"redFlags":["relies on timeouts alone, so threads still pile up until each one fires","sizes a compartment so large that it can still exhaust the server","queues rejected work without any bound"]},{"id":175,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"Teams keep adding pricing rules and order validation to the API gateway. Where would you draw the line?","level":"Basic","tldr":"A gateway owns cross-cutting edge concerns such as routing, TLS, authentication, rate limits and observability; domain rules and business orchestration stay in the services, or the gateway becomes a new monolith.","answer":["An API gateway is the single entry point for external traffic and the right place for concerns identical across services: TLS termination, routing and path rewriting, authentication (validating JWT signature, issuer, audience and expiry, or introspecting opaque tokens), coarse route-level authorization such as required scopes, rate limiting and quotas per client, request size limits, CORS, and consistent access logs, metrics and trace context. Centralizing these removes duplicated code and gives one place to enforce edge security policy.","Business logic stays out: pricing, credit limits, order state checks, and transformations that encode domain knowledge. Once the gateway knows domain rules, every feature needs a gateway change, one team becomes everyone's bottleneck, and each gateway deployment risks every API at once. That is the ESB mistake repeated as a gateway monolith.","The gray zone is client-specific aggregation. If a mobile screen needs data from three services, build a Backend for Frontend owned by the client team, a separate service with its own release cycle, instead of scripting it into the shared gateway. Keep the gateway stateless and highly available since it sits on every request's path, and never make it the only security layer: services still authorize every request themselves."],"keyPoints":["the gateway handles edge concerns that every route shares","token validation, TLS termination, routing and throttling fit there","domain rules and orchestration of business steps live in services","a logic-heavy gateway becomes a shared bottleneck every team must change"],"examples":[{"title":"Spring Cloud Gateway: edge concerns only","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  cloud:\n    gateway:\n      routes:\n        - id: payments\n          uri: http://payments.payments.svc.cluster.local:8080\n          predicates:\n            - Path=/api/payments/**\n          filters:\n            - StripPrefix=1\n            - name: RequestRateLimiter\n              args:\n                redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 50\n                redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 100\n                key-resolver: \"#{@clientIdKeyResolver}\"\n            - name: RequestSize\n              args:\n                maxSize: 1MB\n        - id: orders\n          uri: http://orders.orders.svc.cluster.local:8080\n          predicates:\n            - Path=/api/orders/**\n          filters:\n            - StripPrefix=1\n  security:\n    oauth2:\n      resourceserver:\n        jwt:\n          issuer-uri: https://auth.example.com/realms/shop\n# Stays OUT of the gateway: credit limit checks, discounts, currency conversion","note":"Routing, rate limiting, size limits and JWT validation are declarative config; nothing here knows what an order or a credit limit is. From Spring Cloud 2025.0 the prefix becomes `spring.cloud.gateway.server.webflux`, with the old one supported through the properties migrator."},{"title":"The service still performs object-level authorization","lang":"java","code":"@RestController\nclass PaymentController {\n\n    private final PaymentRepository payments;\n\n    PaymentController(PaymentRepository payments) {\n        this.payments = payments;\n    }\n\n    @GetMapping(\"/payments/{id}\")\n    @PreAuthorize(\"hasAuthority('SCOPE_payments:read')\")\n    PaymentDto get(@PathVariable UUID id, @AuthenticationPrincipal Jwt jwt) {\n        Payment payment = payments.findById(id).orElseThrow(NotFoundException::new);\n        // Only this service knows who owns payment {id}; the gateway cannot check this\n        if (!payment.merchantId().toString().equals(jwt.getClaimAsString(\"merchant_id\"))) {\n            throw new NotFoundException();       // 404 so other merchants' ids are not revealed\n        }\n        return PaymentDto.from(payment);\n    }\n}","note":"The gateway proved who the caller is; the service decides what that caller may see, which is where broken object-level authorization bugs are prevented."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Where should response aggregation for a mobile app live?","a":"In a backend for frontend owned by the mobile team, which composes calls to several services and shapes responses for that client. That keeps presentation logic out of the shared gateway, where every team would otherwise queue up to change it. The BFF still does not own domain rules; it only orchestrates reads and formats data."},{"q":"What kinds of rate limiting fit at the gateway?","a":"Per API key or client, per IP for anonymous traffic, and global ceilings that protect the platform, usually with a token bucket and `429` plus `Retry-After` on rejection. These are coarse, traffic-shaping limits. Business quotas, such as payouts per merchant per day, belong in the owning service, which knows the domain."}],"extra":["**Backend for Frontend:** one BFF per client type (web, mobile, partner API) handles aggregation and response shaping and is owned by the team that owns that client, keeping the edge gateway generic.","**Token exchange at the edge:** gateways often exchange an external access token for a short-lived internal token (OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange, RFC 8693), so internal services never handle raw third-party tokens.","**Gateway versus service mesh:** the gateway handles north-south traffic into the cluster, while a mesh such as Istio or Linkerd handles east-west mTLS, retries and traffic splitting; blurring the two makes it unclear where a policy lives.","**Blast radius:** one bad gateway change can take every API down, so keep routes as reviewed config in Git and roll them out with canaries and automated smoke tests.","**No blind retries:** gateway-level retries on non-idempotent routes can double-submit payments and stack with service retries; restrict them to safe methods."],"redFlags":["trusts the gateway alone, so services skip their own authorization","accepts one team owning a gateway that every release must pass through","cannot explain how a backend for frontend differs from a gateway"]},{"id":176,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"Shipping fails after inventory was reserved and the card charged. Without a distributed transaction, how do you undo the earlier steps?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A saga replaces a distributed transaction with local transactions coordinated by an orchestrator or by events; failures are undone by compensating business actions, which can fail and be retried, not by a rollback.","answer":["With database per service, a process like \"place order\" becomes a sequence of local transactions: create the order as `PENDING`, reserve stock, authorize payment, confirm the order. If payment fails, the saga runs compensations for completed steps in reverse: release the stock, cancel the order. You get eventual atomicity but no isolation, since other transactions can see intermediate states, so you design for them with explicit states and semantic locks. It is not a distributed ACID transaction.","Coordination has two styles. In orchestration, a central orchestrator (a persisted state machine in the order service, or a workflow engine such as Temporal or Camunda) sends commands and tracks progress, which makes the flow visible and easy to change. In choreography, each service reacts to events and emits its own; it is loosely coupled but hard to follow beyond three or four steps.","Undoing a step means running another business operation, not rolling back a transaction. You cannot un-send an email or un-capture money; you issue a cancellation notice or a refund, which may carry fees and side effects. Compensations can fail too, so they must be idempotent, retried with backoff, and escalated to a manual queue when retries run out. Place irreversible steps (capture, shipping) last, after all compensatable steps succeed, and persist saga state so a crashed orchestrator resumes where it stopped."],"keyPoints":["each service commits its own local transaction and emits the outcome","a coordinator or event reactions decide the next step","undoing means a new business operation such as a refund or stock release","compensating steps must be idempotent and retried until they succeed"],"examples":[{"title":"Order saga steps and their compensations","lang":"text","code":"Step  Service     Local transaction              Compensation\n1     ordering    create order PENDING           cancel order (CANCELLED + notify)\n2     inventory   reserve 2 x SKU-881            release reservation\n3     payments    authorize 499 SEK              void the authorization\n4     payments    capture 499 SEK   (pivot)      refund: new transaction, may incur a fee\n5     shipping    create shipment   (retriable)  none: retried until it succeeds","note":"Steps before the pivot are compensatable, the pivot is the point of no return, and steps after it must be retriable because they can no longer be undone cheaply."},{"title":"Orchestrator reacting to replies and failed compensations","lang":"java","code":"@Service\nclass PlaceOrderSaga {\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void on(PaymentAuthorizationFailed event) {\n        SagaState saga = sagas.load(event.orderId());\n        saga.startCompensation(event.reason());\n        // Compensate completed steps in reverse order; every command is idempotent\n        outbox.send(new ReleaseStock(saga.orderId(), saga.stepKey(\"release-stock\")));\n        outbox.send(new CancelOrder(saga.orderId(), \"PAYMENT_DECLINED\"));\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void on(PaymentAuthorized event) {\n        SagaState saga = sagas.load(event.orderId());\n        saga.markPaymentAuthorized(event.authorizationId());\n        // Pivot: capture only after every compensatable step has succeeded\n        outbox.send(new CapturePayment(saga.orderId(), saga.stepKey(\"capture\")));\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void on(CompensationFailed event) {\n        SagaState saga = sagas.load(event.orderId());\n        if (saga.incrementCompensationAttempts() > 5) {\n            saga.markRequiresManualIntervention();          // alert operations, never drop silently\n        } else {\n            outbox.sendDelayed(event.command(), Duration.ofMinutes(1));\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Saga state changes and outgoing commands commit together through the outbox, and step-specific keys make each command safe to redeliver."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Sagas lack isolation. How do you stop other requests from acting on half-finished state?","a":"Use countermeasures such as a semantic lock: mark the record with a state like `PENDING_PAYMENT` so other operations check it and wait, reject or act conservatively. Prefer commutative updates where possible, re-read and verify values before a step that depends on them, and show pending states to readers instead of presenting them as final. These limit, rather than remove, the anomalies a saga allows."},{"q":"Which steps belong at the end of a saga?","a":"Put compensatable steps first, then the pivot, the step after which the saga commits to finishing, such as capturing payment or handing a parcel to the carrier. Steps after the pivot should be retriable until they succeed, because they can no longer be undone. Ordering steps this way minimizes how often irreversible actions need business compensation."}],"extra":["**Step classification:** Chris Richardson's model of compensatable, pivot and retriable transactions gives a practical ordering rule that minimizes irreversible compensation.","**Isolation countermeasures:** because sagas lack isolation, use semantic locks (`PENDING` states), commutative updates, rereading values before acting and version checks to handle dirty reads and lost updates between concurrent sagas.","**Reliable messaging:** each step must update its state and emit its message atomically, which is why sagas are almost always paired with the Transactional Outbox and idempotent consumers.","**Timeouts are outcomes:** a participant that never replies must time out; an unanswered payment call is \"unknown\", so query its status before deciding whether to compensate.","**Workflow engines:** Temporal, Camunda or AWS Step Functions persist state, timers and retries for you; a hand-rolled orchestrator needs the same durability, step timeouts and observability."],"redFlags":["expects a saga to hide intermediate states from other readers","assumes a compensating step can never fail","reaches for two-phase commit across services as the default answer"]},{"id":177,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"Your service commits an order, then publishes `OrderPlaced` to Kafka, and occasionally events go missing. How would you fix this reliably?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A database write plus a broker publish can fail independently; the outbox stores the event in the same local transaction as the state change, and a relay publishes it later, at least once.","answer":["The dual-write problem: a service saves an order, then calls `kafkaTemplate.send(...)`. If the process crashes between the two, the broker is down, or the transaction rolls back after the send, you get an order without an event or an event without an order. Sending inside `@Transactional` does not help because the broker is not part of the database transaction, and XA two-phase commit is rarely available or worth its cost.","With a Transactional Outbox the service inserts the business row and an `outbox` row describing the event in one local ACID transaction, so both exist or neither does. A separate relay publishes outbox rows: either a poller selecting unpublished rows (`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` lets instances share the work) and marking them sent, or change data capture such as Debezium reading the PostgreSQL WAL, which avoids polling load and follows commit order.","The guarantee is at-least-once, never exactly-once. The relay can publish and then crash before marking the row, so the event goes out again. Consumers must therefore be idempotent: record processed event IDs in their own database in the same transaction as their side effects, or make updates naturally idempotent with version checks. Publish with the aggregate ID as the message key to keep per-aggregate ordering, and operate the table: purge published rows and alert on the age of the oldest unpublished one."],"keyPoints":["a database commit and a broker send cannot succeed or fail as one","store the event as a row in the same commit as the business change","a relay or CDC connector forwards those rows to the broker afterward","delivery becomes at least once, so events need IDs and consumers dedupe"],"examples":[{"title":"Dual write versus outbox","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: two systems, no atomicity\n@Transactional\npublic void placeOrderUnsafe(Order order) {\n    orders.save(order);\n    kafka.send(\"order-events\", order.id().toString(), new OrderPlaced(order.id()));\n    // send may succeed and the commit fail afterward, or the process dies before send\n}\n\n// GOOD: the event is part of the same local transaction as the state change\n@Transactional\npublic void placeOrder(Order order) {\n    orders.save(order);\n    outbox.save(new OutboxEvent(\n            UUID.randomUUID(),                  // event id, consumers deduplicate on it\n            \"Order\",\n            order.id().toString(),              // becomes the Kafka key: per-order ordering\n            \"OrderPlaced\",\n            payloadOf(new OrderPlaced(order.id())),\n            Instant.now()));\n}","note":"The good version never talks to Kafka inside the business transaction; publishing is a separate, retryable concern handled by the relay."},{"title":"Outbox table and polling relay in PostgreSQL","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE outbox (\n    id             uuid        PRIMARY KEY,\n    aggregate_type text        NOT NULL,\n    aggregate_id   text        NOT NULL,\n    event_type     text        NOT NULL,\n    payload        jsonb       NOT NULL,\n    created_at     timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    published_at   timestamptz\n);\nCREATE INDEX idx_outbox_pending ON outbox (created_at) WHERE published_at IS NULL;\n\n-- Relay loop: claim a batch without blocking other relay instances\nBEGIN;\nSELECT id, aggregate_id, event_type, payload\nFROM outbox\nWHERE published_at IS NULL\nORDER BY created_at\nLIMIT 100\nFOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED;\n-- publish each row to Kafka keyed by aggregate_id and wait for acks, then:\nUPDATE outbox SET published_at = now() WHERE id = ANY($1);\nCOMMIT;","note":"`SKIP LOCKED` trades strict ordering for throughput: with several relays, two events of one aggregate can be published out of order, so use a single active relay or CDC when per-aggregate order matters."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Polling relay or CDC: how do you choose?","a":"A polling relay is simple and works with any database: it selects unpublished rows with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, publishes them and marks them sent, at the cost of query load and latency equal to the poll interval. CDC such as Debezium reads the WAL or binlog, adds little load, keeps commit order and offers an outbox event router, but needs Kafka Connect and careful operation, since a stalled connector holds a PostgreSQL replication slot and WAL piles up. Start with polling unless volume or latency demands CDC."},{"q":"How do you keep events for one aggregate in order through the outbox?","a":"Publish rows in commit order per aggregate, either with a single relay or with relays partitioned by aggregate ID, and use the aggregate ID as the Kafka message key so those events share a partition. Include a per-aggregate sequence or version in each event so consumers can still detect gaps or stale events. A row that fails to publish must block later rows for the same aggregate rather than letting them skip ahead."}],"extra":["**Debezium outbox router:** Debezium's outbox event router routes outbox rows to topics by aggregate type and uses the aggregate ID as the key; with CDC you can even delete the row in the same transaction, because the WAL still carries the insert.","**Spring Modulith:** its event publication registry persists application events in the business transaction, can republish incomplete ones after a restart and can externalize them to Kafka, an outbox you do not have to hand-write.","**Idempotent producer is not end to end:** Kafka's `enable.idempotence=true` removes duplicates from producer retries within a session, not duplicates from a relay republishing a row after a crash, so consumer deduplication is still required.","**The inbox:** the consumer-side mirror stores incoming event IDs in a table with a unique constraint, inserted in the same transaction as the business update, so a redelivered event becomes a no-op."],"redFlags":["publishes inside the database transaction and assumes a rollback undoes the send","lets the outbox table grow with no cleanup or relay lag monitoring","runs parallel relays without keeping per-aggregate order"]},{"id":178,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"Service A calls B, which calls C. How would you set timeouts, retries and breakers so a slow C doesn't topple everything?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Every attempt gets a timeout, only transient failures of idempotent calls are retried with capped exponential backoff and jitter inside a total deadline, and a circuit breaker stops calls to a clearly failing dependency.","answer":["Timeouts come first: without one, a slow dependency holds your threads and connections until you run out. Set connect and read timeouts per dependency from its latency profile (p99 plus margin), not library defaults, which are often infinite. The timeout bounds a single attempt.","Retry only transient failures (connection errors, `503`, `429` honoring `Retry-After`, timeouts) and only operations that are safe to repeat: reads, or writes carrying an idempotency key. Never retry validation errors or business rejections. Use exponential backoff with jitter so thousands of clients do not retry in lockstep, and cap attempts at two or three.","A circuit breaker tracks failure and slow-call rates; above a threshold it opens and fails fast for a wait period, then half-opens to let a few trial calls through. That protects the struggling dependency, frees your resources and gives you a place for a fallback, such as a cached value or a `PENDING` status.","They share one budget: the caller has a total deadline, and attempts times timeout plus backoff must fit inside it, with the remaining time propagated downstream. Retry in exactly one layer, because gateway, service and client retries multiply. In Resilience4j's default aspect order, retry wraps the circuit breaker, so every attempt is recorded by the breaker and an open breaker stops further attempts."],"keyPoints":["each attempt has its own timeout inside an overall request deadline","repeat only failures that are temporary and safe to run twice","back off exponentially with jitter and cap the number of attempts","an open breaker fails fast and gives the dependency room to recover"],"examples":[{"title":"Resilience4j retry and circuit breaker for a bank API","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass BankApiResilience {\n\n    @Bean\n    Retry bankApiRetry() {\n        return Retry.of(\"bankApi\", RetryConfig.custom()\n                .maxAttempts(3)                                    // total, including the first\n                .intervalFunction(IntervalFunction.ofExponentialRandomBackoff(\n                        Duration.ofMillis(100), 2.0, 0.5))         // 100 ms, 200 ms, +/-50% jitter\n                .retryOnException(BankApiResilience::isTransient)\n                .build());\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    CircuitBreaker bankApiBreaker() {\n        return CircuitBreaker.of(\"bankApi\", CircuitBreakerConfig.custom()\n                .slidingWindowSize(50)\n                .minimumNumberOfCalls(20)\n                .failureRateThreshold(50)\n                .slowCallDurationThreshold(Duration.ofMillis(400))\n                .slowCallRateThreshold(60)\n                .waitDurationInOpenState(Duration.ofSeconds(30))\n                .ignoreExceptions(PaymentDeclinedException.class)   // business result, not a fault\n                .build());\n    }\n\n    static boolean isTransient(Throwable t) {\n        return t instanceof ResourceAccessException                        // I/O error or read timeout\n            || t instanceof HttpServerErrorException.ServiceUnavailable    // 503\n            || t instanceof HttpServerErrorException.GatewayTimeout;       // 504\n    }\n}","note":"Compose them as `Retry.decorateSupplier(retry, CircuitBreaker.decorateSupplier(breaker, call))` with a 500 ms read timeout on the client. `CallNotPermittedException` from an open breaker is not transient, so the retry stops at once instead of hammering a dependency already declared unhealthy."},{"title":"Fitting attempts into a deadline, and how stacking explodes","lang":"text","code":"User request deadline at the gateway:     2000 ms\nPer-attempt read timeout (bank API):       500 ms\nAttempts:                                  3\nBackoff with 50% jitter:                   100 ms and 200 ms, at most 150 + 300 ms\nWorst case: 3 x 500 + 450                = 1950 ms   fits the budget\n\nBAD: gateway retries 3x, service retries 3x, HTTP client retries 3x\n     during an outage every user click becomes up to 27 bank API calls","note":"Derive per-attempt timeouts and retry counts from the end-to-end deadline, not independently per layer."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you carry one deadline across several service hops?","a":"The entry point sets an absolute deadline, and each call passes the remaining budget downstream; gRPC does this natively through the `grpc-timeout` header, while for HTTP you add your own header. Each hop uses the smaller of its own timeout and the remaining budget minus a small margin, and abandons work once the budget is spent. This stops inner services from working on requests whose callers have already given up."},{"q":"In Resilience4j, how should Retry and CircuitBreaker be layered?","a":"Retry should wrap the circuit breaker, which matches the default aspect order: Retry outside CircuitBreaker, which sits outside RateLimiter, TimeLimiter and Bulkhead. Each attempt then passes through the breaker and is recorded, and once it opens, attempts fail fast with `CallNotPermittedException`. Configure Retry to ignore that exception, otherwise it just burns attempts against an open breaker."}],"extra":["**Defaults that bite:** the JDK `HttpClient` has no request timeout unless you set one, `HttpURLConnection` defaults to infinite timeouts, and PgJDBC's `socketTimeout` defaults to 0, meaning no timeout.","**Retry budgets:** cap retries as a fraction of total traffic rather than only per call, as gRPC retry throttling and Envoy retry budgets do, so a broad outage cannot multiply load.","**Deadline propagation:** gRPC propagates deadlines natively; over HTTP, pass the remaining budget in a header so downstream services skip work whose caller has already given up.","**Slow is worse than down:** configure slow-call thresholds, not only error rates, because a dependency answering in ten seconds exhausts resources faster than one failing instantly.","**Respect server hints:** honor `Retry-After` on `429` and `503`, and pair breakers with bulkheads so a half-open probe cannot flood a recovering service."],"redFlags":["gives inner calls longer timeouts than the caller's own","retries non-idempotent POSTs without an idempotency key","counts client errors such as 400 toward opening the breaker"]},{"id":179,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"You're splitting an e-commerce monolith. How would you decide where one service ends and the next begins?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Draw boundaries around business capabilities or bounded contexts that own their data and invariants, change together, belong to one team, and can serve most requests without synchronous calls to other services.","answer":["Start from the domain, not the technology: capabilities such as Payments, Credit Decisioning, Order Fulfillment or Customer Onboarding, refined with Domain-Driven Design bounded contexts in which a term like \"account\" has one consistent meaning. Splitting by technical layer (a UI service, a logic service, a data-access service) or by table (a CustomerService wrapping the customer table) guarantees every feature touches every service and couples them through chatty CRUD calls.","A good boundary encloses cohesive data and invariants: the data needed to enforce \"the credit limit may not be exceeded\" lives in one service and one local transaction. It matches change patterns, so things that change together for the same business reason stay together, which you can verify from version control history. And it has exactly one owning team that can design, deploy and operate it without asking permission.","Finally, a boundary should minimize synchronous chatter. If one request needs three serial calls to other services, you split a cohesive unit, and your availability becomes the product of theirs. Prefer boundaries where collaboration is asynchronous (events like `OrderPlaced`) and where a service keeps a local copy of the reference data it needs. Event Storming is a practical way to find the seams, because domain events and aggregates cluster naturally into contexts."],"keyPoints":["follow business capabilities and their vocabulary, not technical layers","a service owns its data and enforces its own invariants","things that change together and belong to one team stay together","most requests should complete without waiting on other services"],"examples":[{"title":"Layer or table split versus capability split","lang":"text","code":"BAD: split by technical layer or by table\n  order-api        controllers only\n  order-logic      rules; calls order-data and customer-data on every request\n  order-data       CRUD over the orders table\n  customer-data    CRUD over the customers table\n  result: every feature touches 4 services; one schema change breaks 3 deployments\n\nGOOD: split by business capability, each owning data and invariants\n  ordering         owns orders, order_lines; enforces \"cannot ship an unpaid order\"\n                   publishes OrderPlaced, OrderCancelled\n  payments         owns payments, refunds; integrates the PSP; publishes PaymentCaptured\n  credit-decision  owns applications, scores; consumes CustomerRiskProfileChanged\n                   and keeps a local projection of the risk data it needs\n  result: a typical change touches one service and one team","note":"The good split follows reasons for change and ownership of invariants, not the shape of the database."},{"title":"Chatty runtime coupling versus a local projection","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: the boundary cut a cohesive unit; every decision needs 3 blocking calls\npublic Decision decideChatty(CreditApplication app) {\n    Customer customer = customerClient.get(app.customerId());      // network hop 1\n    List<Loan> loans = loanClient.activeLoans(app.customerId());   // network hop 2\n    Income income = incomeClient.latest(app.customerId());         // network hop 3\n    return policy.evaluate(app, customer, loans, income);\n}\n\n// GOOD: credit-decision owns a read model fed by events from other contexts\n@KafkaListener(topics = \"customer-risk-events\", groupId = \"credit-decision\")\nvoid onRiskProfileChanged(CustomerRiskProfileChanged event) {\n    riskProfiles.upsertIfNewer(event.customerId(), event.riskClass(), event.version());\n}\n\npublic Decision decide(CreditApplication app) {\n    RiskProfile profile = riskProfiles.find(app.customerId())\n            .orElseThrow(() -> new ProfileNotYetAvailableException(app.customerId()));\n    return policy.evaluate(app, profile);                          // one local query\n}","note":"The good version survives the customer service being down and removes three network hops from the latency path, at the cost of eventual consistency on risk data."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How can event storming help find service boundaries?","a":"Domain experts and engineers lay out the business's domain events on a timeline, then add the commands, actors and policies that cause them. Clusters of events that share vocabulary and data tend to form bounded contexts, and the hand-off points between clusters suggest where integration events belong. It surfaces boundaries from how the business works rather than from the existing database schema."},{"q":"Two contexts both need customer data. Who owns it?","a":"One context owns the customer record and its rules, typically a customer or account service, and publishes changes as events. Others keep only the fields they need in their own model, such as a shipping address snapshot in fulfillment, updated from those events. Each model is shaped by its own context rather than shared as one universal customer object."}],"extra":["**Aggregates are the minimum unit:** never split one aggregate across services, because its invariants need a single transactional boundary; a bounded context usually contains several aggregates.","**Cognitive load:** Team Topologies sizes a service so one stream-aligned team can fully understand and operate it; a boundary that needs two teams to change it is in the wrong place.","**Context mapping:** make relationships explicit (customer and supplier, conformist, anticorruption layer); an anticorruption layer in front of a legacy core system keeps its model from leaking into your new boundary.","**Start coarse:** splitting a coarse service later is easier than merging fine-grained ones, because a merge means reconciling two data models, two deployments and two ownership lines."],"redFlags":["draws boundaries from the org chart alone without looking at data ownership","accepts a design where one user action needs a long chain of synchronous calls","lets two services write to the same data"]},{"id":180,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"Two services share a schema and keep breaking each other's releases. What does separating their data fix, and what does it cost?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Each service owns its data and nobody else reads or writes it directly, which enables independent change and deployment, but joins, cross-service consistency and reporting move from SQL into APIs, events, sagas and read models.","answer":["Database per service means a service's data is private: other services access it only through its API or the events it publishes. Physically it can be a separate PostgreSQL cluster, a separate database or at minimum a separate schema with its own credentials; the ownership rule is what matters. It gives autonomy (a team can change schema, indexes or even database technology without coordinating), encapsulation (invariants are enforced in one place) and fault isolation, since another team's runaway query cannot lock the payments tables.","The costs are real. Cross-service joins disappear: \"orders with customer name and payment status\" needs API composition or a local read model. Multi-service ACID transactions disappear, so changes spanning services need sagas and the outbox, with eventual consistency the business must accept. Reporting can no longer be one SQL query over everything, and duplicated data needs a clear owner of the authoritative copy.","So share data deliberately: synchronous APIs for current values that must not be cached, domain events so others can maintain their own projections (shipping keeps the customer address it needs), and read models or a data platform for cross-domain queries. Unrestricted cross-service table access, even read-only, is the red line: it turns your schema into a public API nobody can change and recreates the shared database with extra network hops."],"keyPoints":["a private schema lets a team change tables without coordinating releases","other services see the data only through an API or published events","joins across services turn into API composition or local copies","transactions spanning services become sagas with eventual consistency"],"examples":[{"title":"Enforcing ownership in PostgreSQL","lang":"sql","code":"-- Cheap starting point: one cluster, one schema and one role per service\nCREATE ROLE orders_svc LOGIN;\nCREATE ROLE payments_svc LOGIN;\nCREATE SCHEMA orders AUTHORIZATION orders_svc;\nCREATE SCHEMA payments AUTHORIZATION payments_svc;\nREVOKE ALL ON SCHEMA orders, payments FROM PUBLIC;\n\n-- BAD: payments reads orders tables; the orders schema becomes a frozen public API\n-- GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA orders TO payments_svc;\n-- GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA orders TO payments_svc;\n\n-- GOOD: payments keeps its own projection, fed by OrderPlaced events\nCREATE TABLE payments.order_snapshot (\n    order_id     uuid    PRIMARY KEY,\n    customer_id  uuid    NOT NULL,\n    total_minor  bigint  NOT NULL,\n    currency     char(3) NOT NULL,\n    version      bigint  NOT NULL        -- lets consumers ignore stale or duplicate events\n);","note":"Ownership is enforced by the database itself, not by convention; moving a schema to its own cluster later does not change any other service."},{"title":"Idempotent projection update from events","lang":"java","code":"@KafkaListener(topics = \"order-events\", groupId = \"payments\")\npublic void on(OrderPlaced event) {\n    jdbc.update(\"\"\"\n        INSERT INTO payments.order_snapshot (order_id, customer_id, total_minor, currency, version)\n        VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)\n        ON CONFLICT (order_id) DO UPDATE\n           SET customer_id = EXCLUDED.customer_id,\n               total_minor = EXCLUDED.total_minor,\n               currency    = EXCLUDED.currency,\n               version     = EXCLUDED.version\n         WHERE payments.order_snapshot.version < EXCLUDED.version\n        \"\"\", event.orderId(), event.customerId(), event.totalMinor(), event.currency(), event.version());\n}","note":"The version guard makes the update idempotent and safe against out-of-order delivery, so duplicates from an at-least-once outbox are harmless."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you move a shared database toward one owner per table?","a":"Assign every table an owning service first, then move other services' direct reads and writes to that owner's API or events, using database views as a temporary contract where a quick cut is not possible. Next, put the owner's tables in a separate schema with its own credentials so violations fail loudly, and only then consider separate instances. Go table by table and keep copies in sync with CDC during the transition."},{"q":"Does database per service mean one database server per service?","a":"No: ownership is the point, not hardware. Separate schemas or logical databases with separate credentials on a shared cluster are fine as long as no service touches another's data directly. Physical separation is a later choice for scale, isolation or a different storage technology."}],"extra":["**Schemas before clusters:** separate schemas and roles on a shared PostgreSQL instance give ownership at low cost; move a service to its own cluster when it needs independent scaling, maintenance windows or a different engine.","**Migrating off a shared database:** first route all access to a table through its owning module, then replicate to the new store via CDC, switch writes, and finally revoke the old grants.","**Erasure fans out:** duplicated personal data means a GDPR erasure request must reach every projection; define an erasure event and track completion per service.","**Make staleness visible:** store the source event version or timestamp in projections so support tooling and reconciliation jobs can detect lagging copies."],"redFlags":["thinks it requires one database server per service","has no plan for reporting that spans several domains","splits the data but still wants a distributed transaction for every write"]},{"id":181,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"How would you migrate a monolith to microservices without a big-bang rewrite?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Use the strangler fig pattern: put a routing facade in front of the monolith and move one bounded context at a time behind it, with an anti-corruption layer between old and new models. Migrate data in reversible steps (sync, shadow reads, read cutover, write cutover) behind feature flags, and judge each step against a baseline measured first.","answer":["A big-bang rewrite fails predictably: the old system keeps changing while you rebuild it, nothing ships for a year, and every hidden behavior surfaces on cutover day. The **strangler fig** pattern replaces the system incrementally. Put a facade (an API gateway or reverse proxy) in front of the monolith that initially routes everything to it. Extract one capability, ideally a bounded context with clear seams, low inbound coupling and a real reason to move (high change rate, different scaling, a team ready to own it), switch its routes to the new service, and repeat. The cheapest first step happens inside the monolith: enforce module boundaries with Spring Modulith or ArchUnit so the seam is proven before a network is added.","The new service should not inherit the monolith's model: an **anti-corruption layer** translates at the boundary so legacy tables, codes and quirks do not leak in. Data is the hard part, and the rule is one writer at any moment. First the monolith still writes and the new service receives a copy through CDC (for example Debezium). Then run **shadow reads**: read both, compare, keep returning the legacy answer. Then cut reads over per tenant behind a feature flag, and only then move writes, reversing the sync so the monolith can still read and rollback stays possible. Branch by abstraction lets callers inside the monolith switch implementations without knowing.","Measure before and after, or you cannot tell whether it helped: p95/p99 latency, error rate, cost, and delivery metrics such as deploy frequency and lead time for changes in that area, each with a target. Watch for a **distributed monolith**: services that must deploy together, share tables, or call each other in long synchronous chains. If lead time gets worse after an extraction, the boundary is probably wrong, and reversible routing and flags let you back out."],"keyPoints":["route through a facade and move one bounded context at a time","one writer at a time: sync, shadow reads, then read and write cutover","baseline metrics and reversible steps decide when to continue"],"examples":[{"title":"Branch by abstraction with shadow reads and a per-tenant cutover flag","lang":"java","code":"// Inside the monolith: callers depend on this interface, not on where invoices live\npublic interface InvoiceQueries {\n    InvoiceView byId(InvoiceId id);\n}\n\n@Component\n@RequiredArgsConstructor\nclass MigratingInvoiceQueries implements InvoiceQueries {\n    private final LegacyInvoiceQueries legacy;        // the monolith's own tables\n    private final InvoicingServiceAdapter extracted;  // anti-corruption layer over the new API\n    private final FeatureFlags flags;\n    private final MeterRegistry meters;\n\n    @Override\n    public InvoiceView byId(InvoiceId id) {\n        if (flags.isEnabledFor(\"invoices.read-from-service\", id.tenantId())) {\n            return extracted.byId(id);                // per-tenant cutover; flip the flag to roll back\n        }\n        InvoiceView current = legacy.byId(id);\n        if (flags.isEnabled(\"invoices.shadow-read\")) {\n            compare(id, current);                     // callers still get the legacy answer\n        }\n        return current;\n    }\n\n    private void compare(InvoiceId id, InvoiceView expected) {\n        boolean same = expected.equals(extracted.byId(id));\n        meters.counter(\"invoice.shadow.read\", \"outcome\", same ? \"match\" : \"mismatch\").increment();\n    }\n}","note":"Run the shadow call asynchronously or on a sample so it adds no latency, and log mismatching ids: they expose the rounding, time zone and legacy quirks the new service missed."},{"title":"A migration plan with exit criteria for each step","lang":"text","code":"Extracting invoicing from the order monolith\n\n0 Baseline      p99 latency, error rate, deploys per week and lead time for invoice\n                changes, each with a target.\n1 Seams         Invoice code becomes a module; ArchUnit fails the build on calls into\n                its internals from other modules.\n2 Facade        Gateway in front of the monolith; /api/invoices/** still routes there.\n3 New service   invoicing-service owns its schema. Debezium streams the monolith's\n                invoice tables into it. The monolith is still the only writer.\n4 Shadow reads  Every read is compared; fix mismatches until they stay below an agreed\n                rate (for example 0.01% for two weeks).\n5 Read cutover  Flag per tenant: internal tenants, then 5%, 25%, 100% of reads.\n6 Writes        The service becomes the single writer and publishes events through an\n                outbox; sync now flows back so the monolith, and a rollback, can read.\n7 Remove        Delete the legacy invoice code and tables; compare with step 0.","note":"Every step has an exit criterion and a way back. Reads move before writes, and the sync direction flips only when the service becomes the single writer."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you choose the first service to extract?","a":"Look for a bounded context with clear seams, few inbound dependencies and tables that few other modules join against, plus a business reason to move it. Avoid the most entangled core first (often orders or customers) and avoid a trivial edge that teaches nothing; invoicing, notifications or a catalog are common first picks."},{"q":"During the transition, can the monolith and the new service both write the same data?","a":"Avoid it. Two writers on the same rows need conflict resolution, and naive dual writes drift apart the first time one side fails. Keep one writer at each point, sync the other side with CDC or an outbox, and move write ownership in one explicit, flag-controlled step. If dual writing is unavoidable for a short period, name one side the source of truth and reconcile the other against it continuously."}],"extra":["**Port before improving:** move behavior first, quirks included, and pin it with characterization tests; shadow comparison only works while the new service is meant to give the same answers.","**Events out of the monolith:** having the monolith publish domain events through an outbox lets new services build their own data instead of reading its tables.","**Views as a temporary contract:** a database view over legacy tables gives the new service a stable read shape, but it is shared-database coupling and needs an end date.","**Ownership follows the service:** extraction pays off when one team owns the new service end to end; splitting code without splitting ownership tends to produce a distributed monolith.","**Not everything has to move:** stop when the remaining monolith is stable and cheap to change; a well-modularized core that rarely changes is not a problem to solve."],"redFlags":["Proposes rewriting everything and switching over on one date","splits by technical layer instead of business capability","lets both sides write a shared database with no plan for ownership or rollback"]},{"id":182,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"An architect proposes Event Sourcing with CQRS for a simple customer profile service. What would convince you it is worth it?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"CQRS splits write and read models; Event Sourcing makes an append-only event log the source of truth; both add real complexity and pay off only for divergent read shapes, audit or temporal queries.","answer":["CQRS separates the model that handles commands and enforces invariants from one or more models optimized for queries. At its simplest it is two sets of classes over one database; at its fullest, writes go to a normalized PostgreSQL schema while events update denormalized read models in Elasticsearch or dedicated tables, eventually consistent. It is justified when read and write shapes diverge strongly, such as a rich aggregate versus dashboards spanning several contexts, or when read and write loads must scale independently.","Event Sourcing stores state as an append-only sequence of domain events (`AccountOpened`, `FundsDeposited`, `FundsWithdrawn`), and current state is derived by replaying them, usually with snapshots for long streams. You gain a complete audit trail, answers to \"what did we know at time T\", and new projections built by replaying history. The operational price is high: projections and rebuild tooling, fast and safe replay, event schema versioning and upcasting forever since events are immutable, GDPR erasure in an immutable log, and a team comfortable with eventual consistency.","Neither is worth adopting without a concrete need it solves. For ordinary CRUD such as settings or a product catalog they add moving parts, read lag and debugging pain with no payoff. Good fits are ledgers, payments, trading and credit decisions where audit and temporal queries are core requirements, and even then usually for that bounded context only, with a clear operational plan."],"keyPoints":["with CQRS, queries get their own model shaped for reads","an event-sourced system keeps the log as the record and derives current state","projections, replays and evolving stored events are ongoing costs","justified by very different read shapes, audit needs or time-travel queries"],"examples":[{"title":"Event-sourced account aggregate in Java 21","lang":"java","code":"sealed interface AccountEvent permits AccountOpened, FundsDeposited, FundsWithdrawn {}\nrecord AccountOpened(UUID accountId, String currency) implements AccountEvent {}\nrecord FundsDeposited(UUID accountId, long amountMinor) implements AccountEvent {}\nrecord FundsWithdrawn(UUID accountId, long amountMinor) implements AccountEvent {}\n\nfinal class Account {\n    private long balanceMinor;\n    private long version;\n\n    static Account replay(List<AccountEvent> history) {\n        Account account = new Account();\n        history.forEach(account::apply);\n        return account;\n    }\n\n    List<AccountEvent> withdraw(UUID id, long amountMinor) {\n        if (amountMinor > balanceMinor) {                       // invariant on the write model\n            throw new InsufficientFundsException(id, amountMinor, balanceMinor);\n        }\n        return List.of(new FundsWithdrawn(id, amountMinor));    // decide, do not mutate\n    }\n\n    private void apply(AccountEvent event) {\n        switch (event) {                                        // exhaustive over the sealed type\n            case AccountOpened e  -> balanceMinor = 0;\n            case FundsDeposited e -> balanceMinor += e.amountMinor();\n            case FundsWithdrawn e -> balanceMinor -= e.amountMinor();\n        }\n        version++;\n    }\n\n    long version() { return version; }\n}","note":"Commands produce events and state changes only by applying events, so replay rebuilds exactly the same state; the sealed interface makes a forgotten event type a compile error."},{"title":"Event store with optimistic concurrency and a read model","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE account_events (\n    account_id   uuid        NOT NULL,\n    version      bigint      NOT NULL,\n    event_type   text        NOT NULL,\n    schema_ver   int         NOT NULL,        -- enables upcasting of old event shapes\n    payload      jsonb       NOT NULL,\n    recorded_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    PRIMARY KEY (account_id, version)          -- two writers of the same version: one fails\n);\n\n-- Loaded at version 7, append version 8; a unique violation means a concurrent write\nINSERT INTO account_events (account_id, version, event_type, schema_ver, payload)\nVALUES ($1, 8, 'FundsWithdrawn', 1, '{\"amountMinor\": 25000}');\n\n-- Query side, rebuilt at any time by replaying events from version 1\nCREATE TABLE account_balance_view (\n    account_id    uuid   PRIMARY KEY,\n    balance_minor bigint NOT NULL,\n    last_version  bigint NOT NULL\n);","note":"The primary key gives optimistic concurrency for free, and the read model is disposable because it can always be rebuilt from the log."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you evolve events that are already stored?","a":"Stored events are immutable, so changes must stay readable alongside old data: add optional fields with defaults, and use upcasters that transform old event versions into the current shape when they are read. When the meaning changes, introduce a new event type rather than reinterpreting the old one. Rewriting the store is a last resort and needs the same care as a database migration."},{"q":"What role do snapshots play in an event-sourced aggregate?","a":"Long event streams make loading an aggregate slow, so you periodically persist its state, for example every few hundred events, and load the latest snapshot plus the events after it. A snapshot is only a cache: it can be discarded and rebuilt from the log, and it must be invalidated when the aggregate's state shape changes."}],"extra":["**CQRS light:** a separate query path using SQL or Spring Data interface projections on the same database, with commands going through the domain model, captures much of the benefit without any eventual consistency.","**Read your own writes:** with asynchronous projections, return the new version from the command and let the UI poll until the read model reaches it, or read that user's own data from the write side.","**Never rewrite history:** do not change the meaning of a stored event; add new event types or versions and upcast old payloads on read, which is why frameworks like Axon ship upcaster support.","**Replay time is an SLO:** snapshot long streams and measure full projection rebuild time, because a rebuild that takes days turns a projection bug into a multi-day incident.","**Domain events are not integration events:** fine-grained internal events change with the model; publish separate, stable integration events to other services rather than exposing the event store."],"redFlags":["ignores how to change an event's schema once it is stored","hides lagging read models from users who expect to see their own writes","cannot describe how a projection gets rebuilt"]},{"id":183,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"A saved address appears on one screen but not another for seconds. Why, and how should the system handle it?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Replicas and read models may temporarily disagree but converge once updates stop flowing; in practice you design pending states, idempotent versioned updates, reconciliation and lag monitoring, and keep invariants on a strongly consistent store.","answer":["Eventual consistency is a **convergence** guarantee: if no new writes arrive, all replicas eventually return the same value. It shows up everywhere in a backend: async PostgreSQL read replicas, CQRS read models fed by events, caches, search indexes, and data copied between services over Kafka or RabbitMQ. It does not mean no guarantees; it means no bound on staleness unless you add one, plus convergence you must actually engineer.","Design for the window. Model explicit **pending states** (`PENDING_PAYMENT`, a 202 Accepted with a status URL) instead of pretending the result is final. Make projections idempotent and version-guarded so duplicates and reordering still converge. Provide session guarantees where users notice: read-your-writes by returning the created resource from the command, or routing that user to the primary briefly after a write.","Add **reconciliation** because convergence can silently fail: a dropped event, a poison message in a DLQ or a bug leaves a read model permanently wrong. Periodic jobs compare the source of truth with derived copies and repair them, and you monitor replica lag, consumer lag and the age of the oldest unprocessed event against an SLO.","Finally, apply **stronger guarantees where invariants require them**: uniqueness, balances, inventory and idempotency keys live in one authoritative store with transactions or conditional writes; everything derived can be eventual."],"keyPoints":["copies agree again once updates stop, with no fixed deadline","the UI needs explicit pending states instead of pretending writes are instant","updates must be idempotent and versioned so late or repeated ones are harmless","measure lag and run reconciliation to catch copies that never catch up"],"examples":[{"title":"Version-guarded projection that converges despite duplicates and reordering","lang":"java","code":"record OrderStatusChanged(UUID orderId, String status, long version) {}\n\n@Component\nclass OrderSummaryProjector {\n    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;\n\n    OrderSummaryProjector(JdbcTemplate jdbc) {\n        this.jdbc = jdbc;\n    }\n\n    @RabbitListener(queues = \"order-summary.projection\")\n    void on(OrderStatusChanged e) {\n        // GOOD: upsert only if the event is newer than what the read model holds\n        jdbc.update(\"\"\"\n            INSERT INTO order_summary (order_id, status, version, updated_at)\n            VALUES (?, ?, ?, now())\n            ON CONFLICT (order_id) DO UPDATE\n               SET status = EXCLUDED.status,\n                   version = EXCLUDED.version,\n                   updated_at = now()\n             WHERE order_summary.version < EXCLUDED.version\n            \"\"\", e.orderId(), e.status(), e.version());\n    }\n}","note":"The `WHERE order_summary.version < EXCLUDED.version` clause turns duplicates and late events into no-ops, so the read model always converges to the latest source version."},{"title":"Exposing the pending state instead of hiding it","lang":"http","code":"POST /payments HTTP/1.1\nHost: api.example.com\nIdempotency-Key: 7f3c9a2e-1b4d-4c1e-9f7a-2d8e6b0c5a11\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"orderId\": \"ord_1042\", \"amount\": \"149.00\", \"currency\": \"SEK\"}\n\nHTTP/1.1 202 Accepted\nLocation: /payments/pay_88231\nRetry-After: 2\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"id\": \"pay_88231\", \"status\": \"PENDING\"}","note":"The client gets an honest intermediate state and a place to poll, so the UI can say processing rather than showing a stale or wrong final status."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you measure how far behind a read model is?","a":"Stamp each event with its commit time and have the projector record the time and offset of the last event it applied; lag is now minus that commit time, alongside consumer lag in offsets if you use Kafka. Alert against an explicit target, such as 99% of updates visible within five seconds. Exposing an as-of time in the API lets the UI tell users when data may be behind."},{"q":"How do you stop a read model from applying an older update over a newer one?","a":"Carry a version or sequence number per aggregate in each event and make the update conditional, for example `UPDATE ... SET ..., version = :v WHERE id = :id AND version < :v`. A late or duplicate event then changes nothing. For delta events, where order matters, park out-of-order events until the gap is filled instead of discarding them."}],"extra":["**Staleness needs an SLO:** alert on `pg_stat_replication.replay_lag`, Kafka consumer lag and oldest event age; eventually without a number is how a read model ends up hours behind unnoticed.","**Read-your-writes via LSN:** return the commit LSN (`pg_current_wal_lsn()`) to the client and have replica reads wait until `pg_last_wal_replay_lsn()` has passed it, instead of pinning all reads to the primary.","**Replica routing gotcha:** routing `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` to replicas with `AbstractRoutingDataSource` silently breaks read-your-writes for flows that write and then read in separate transactions.","**Conflict resolution:** with multiple writers, last-write-wins by wall clock loses updates under clock skew; prefer a single writer per key, version numbers or CRDTs."],"redFlags":["assumes the delay is always small, so nothing needs monitoring","checks a money invariant against an eventually consistent read model","applies updates in arrival order without versions"]},{"id":184,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"A network split cuts your three-node cluster into two sides. What exactly does CAP say each side can do?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"CAP says that during a network partition a replicated system must either refuse some requests to stay linearizable (CP) or keep answering with possibly stale or divergent data (AP); without partitions you get both.","answer":["Precisely: Gilbert and Lynch proved that no system can provide **linearizability** (every read sees the latest completed write, as if there were one copy), **availability** (every request to a non-failing node gets a non-error response) and **partition tolerance** (correct behavior despite arbitrary message loss between nodes) at the same time. Partitions are not optional: switches fail, cross-AZ links drop, and a long GC pause looks exactly like a partition to peers. So partition tolerance is a given, and the real decision is what each operation does **during** a partition.","A **CP** choice makes the minority side reject or block requests: etcd, ZooKeeper and Raft-based stores only serve writes with a quorum, and PostgreSQL with synchronous replication blocks commits when no synchronous standby answers. An **AP** choice keeps both sides accepting writes and reconciles later with last-write-wins, version vectors or CRDTs (Cassandra at consistency level `ONE`, DynamoDB eventually consistent reads). The choice can be made per operation, not per product.","Two common confusions. CAP consistency is not ACID consistency: ACID C means a transaction preserves invariants such as constraints, while CAP C is a recency and ordering guarantee on single objects. And a partition is not a node crash: a crashed node owes nobody an answer, whereas in a partition both sides are alive and reachable by clients but cannot talk to each other, which is what creates split brain.","In production you pick per use case: debits and uniqueness go CP, catalog browsing and dashboards go AP."],"keyPoints":["the theorem only constrains behavior while a partition is happening","a CP system refuses some requests to stay linearizable","an AP system keeps answering and tolerates stale or divergent data","the C in CAP means linearizability, not the C in ACID"],"examples":[{"title":"Partition between two regions: CP vs AP behavior","lang":"text","code":"Setup: 3 replicas, eu-north (2 nodes) and eu-west (1 node). Link between regions drops.\n\n                    CP store (quorum writes)           AP store (write to any replica)\neu-north (majority) reads and writes succeed           reads and writes succeed\neu-west (minority)  writes fail or time out (503)      writes succeed locally\n                    reads fail or are served stale     reads may return stale data\n                    only if explicitly allowed\nAfter healing       nothing to merge                   conflicting versions must be merged\n                                                       (LWW loses updates, CRDTs merge)\nClients see         errors on one side                 success everywhere, divergence later","note":"The CP system never shows two different truths but refuses service on the minority side; the AP system always answers but pushes conflict resolution into your application or data model."},{"title":"PostgreSQL choosing consistency over availability","lang":"properties","code":"# postgresql.conf on the primary\n# Commit returns only after at least one standby has applied the WAL\nsynchronous_standby_names = 'ANY 1 (replica_a, replica_b)'\nsynchronous_commit = remote_apply\n\n# If both standbys are unreachable, commits wait instead of succeeding:\n# the primary chooses C over A for writes until a standby returns\n# or an operator relaxes the setting.","note":"Synchronous replication is a concrete CP knob: during a partition from its standbys the primary stops acknowledging commits rather than risking acknowledged writes that a failover would lose."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does PACELC add to CAP?","a":"Daniel Abadi's PACELC says that if there is a partition, a system chooses availability or consistency, and else, in normal operation, it chooses between latency and consistency. That captures the everyday trade-off CAP ignores: synchronous replication across zones costs latency on every write even when the network is healthy. Dynamo-style stores with tunable quorums are typically described as PA/EL, while systems like Spanner pay latency for consistency."},{"q":"Is a single PostgreSQL node CA?","a":"Calling it CA is misleading: CAP is about replicated data, and a single node avoids the trade-off by not replicating. Clients can still be cut off from it, and then it is simply unavailable to them. Once you add asynchronous replicas and read from them, those reads are not linearizable, so the CAP question returns."}],"extra":["**PACELC:** even without a partition you trade latency for consistency; synchronous cross-region replication adds a network round trip to every write, which is often the decision that actually matters day to day.","**CAP availability is strict:** it requires every non-failing node to answer, so a CP system that keeps serving from the majority side can still be highly available in the SRE sense of meeting its SLO.","**Quorums are not automatically linearizable:** `R + W > N` makes read and write sets overlap, but sloppy quorums, hinted handoff and concurrent read repair can still return stale or reordered values.","**Tunable per request:** Cassandra consistency levels (`QUORUM` vs `ONE`), DynamoDB `ConsistentRead`, and MongoDB `readConcern`/`writeConcern` `majority` let one system make different CAP choices per query.","**Detection is a timeout:** a node cannot distinguish a slow peer from a partitioned one, which is why leases, fencing tokens and GC pause monitoring matter in CP designs."],"redFlags":["labels a database CP or AP without saying under which settings","uses CAP to explain latency trade-offs when nothing is partitioned","thinks a distributed system can opt out of partitions"]},{"id":185,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"A vendor's broker advertises exactly-once delivery. What would you ask before trusting that for charging customers?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"At-most-once can lose messages, at-least-once can duplicate them, and exactly-once only holds inside a scoped boundary such as Kafka transactions; end to end you get effectively-once from at-least-once plus idempotent processing.","answer":["The semantics come from **when you acknowledge relative to the side effect**. At-most-once acks (or commits the offset) before processing: a crash after the ack loses the message, and a producer that never retries loses on network errors. At-least-once processes first and acks after: a crash between commit and ack causes redelivery, and a producer retrying after a lost confirm publishes twice, so duplicates are normal.","Exactly-once is **scoped**. Kafka's idempotent producer dedupes retries per partition using producer IDs and sequence numbers, and Kafka transactions make consume, transform and produce atomic, including the consumer offset commit, for readers using `read_committed`. That guarantee ends at Kafka's boundary: once the handler writes to PostgreSQL, sends an email or calls a payment provider, the broker cannot make that side effect and the ack atomic. No broker gives arbitrary end-to-end exactly-once, and RabbitMQ does not claim it at all.","The practical design is **at-least-once delivery plus idempotent consumers**: publisher confirms, persistent messages and durable (quorum) queues on the way in, a transactional outbox on the producer side, and a dedupe record written in the same database transaction as the business change on the consumer side. At-most-once is acceptable only where loss is cheap: metrics, telemetry, cache invalidation hints."],"keyPoints":["commit the offset or ack before processing and a crash loses the message","process before committing and a crash repeats it","broker guarantees cover only their own boundary, such as Kafka read-process-write","real side effects need repeated delivery plus idempotent handling"],"examples":[{"title":"Where a crash lands decides loss or duplication","lang":"text","code":"Consumer crash point                 | Ack BEFORE work   | Ack AFTER work\n-------------------------------------+-------------------+----------------------------\nBefore business commit               | message LOST      | redelivered, processed once\nAfter business commit, before ack    | n/a (acked)       | redelivered: DUPLICATE\nAfter ack                            | done              | done\n\nProducer: no retry after timeout     -> possible LOSS\nProducer: retry after lost confirm   -> possible DUPLICATE","note":"Every row has either a loss or a duplicate case, which is why you choose at-least-once and make duplicates harmless instead of trying to eliminate them."},{"title":"Kafka exactly-once scope in Spring Boot","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  kafka:\n    producer:\n      acks: all\n      transaction-id-prefix: ledger-tx-   # enables transactional producer\n      properties:\n        enable.idempotence: true\n    consumer:\n      enable-auto-commit: false\n      isolation-level: read-committed      # skip aborted transactional writes","note":"This makes a Kafka-to-Kafka pipeline atomic; a PostgreSQL write inside the same listener is outside the Kafka transaction and still needs its own idempotency."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does Kafka's exactly-once support actually cover?","a":"The idempotent producer removes duplicates caused by producer retries within a partition, using a producer ID and sequence numbers. Transactions make writes to several partitions and the consumer's offset commit atomic, so a read-process-write pipeline inside Kafka is exactly once for consumers reading with `isolation.level=read_committed`. Anything outside Kafka, such as a database insert or an HTTP call, is not covered and still needs idempotent handling."},{"q":"When is at-most-once delivery the right choice?","a":"When losing an occasional message is cheaper than handling duplicates or retry delays, such as high-volume metrics, telemetry samples or presence updates that the next message supersedes. It is the wrong choice for anything that represents money, orders or user intent. Make the choice explicit in the design rather than getting it by accident through auto-ack."}],"extra":["**Kafka defaults:** since Kafka 3.0 producers default to `enable.idempotence=true` and `acks=all`, which removes producer retry duplicates per partition within a producer session, not duplicates from your own application retries.","**Stable message IDs:** assign the event ID when the business fact is created (for example in the outbox row), not at send time, so every retry and replay carries the same ID for dedupe.","**Dedupe windows are bounded:** broker-side dedupe such as SQS FIFO deduplication works only within a short window (5 minutes for SQS FIFO), so it cannot replace consumer idempotency for replays days later.","**Redelivered flag is a hint:** RabbitMQ sets `redelivered=true` on redelivery, but it does not tell you whether processing completed; only your dedupe record can."],"redFlags":["acks the message before the side effect is durable and calls it reliable","thinks Kafka transactions also cover writes to an external database","has no answer for duplicates beyond hoping they are rare"]},{"id":186,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"Your payment-event consumer sometimes gets the same message twice and double-credits accounts. What would make the handler safe?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Use a stable producer-assigned ID and, in the same transaction as the business change, insert it into an inbox table with a unique constraint, so duplicates fail atomically and are simply acked.","answer":["Start with a **stable ID**: an event ID assigned by the producer when the fact was created, or a natural business key such as `paymentId` plus event type. Do not use broker artifacts like the delivery tag or a Kafka offset, which change when a message is republished or replayed from a DLQ.","Then make **dedupe and the business update atomic**. In one local transaction, `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` into an inbox table keyed by `(message_id, consumer)`, check the affected row count, and only if it is 1 apply the business change. Either both commit or neither does. The classic bugs are the alternatives: an in-memory set of processed IDs is lost on restart and not shared across replicas; check-then-insert (`SELECT` then `INSERT`) races when two consumers get the same redelivered message; and a Redis `SETNX` outside the DB transaction leaves windows where the ID is marked but the business write rolled back, or the reverse.","Where possible, make the operation **naturally idempotent** as a second line of defense: conditional state transitions (`UPDATE ... WHERE status = 'PENDING'`), upserts by business key, or a unique constraint such as one ledger entry per `(payment_id, entry_type)`. Ack only after the transaction commits, retain inbox rows longer than any redelivery or replay window, and handle external side effects separately because they cannot join the transaction."],"keyPoints":["dedupe on an ID assigned by the producer, never on delivery metadata","record the ID and apply the change in one database transaction","a unique index turns a duplicate into a failed insert you can simply ack","a conditional state transition can act as a natural dedupe check"],"examples":[{"title":"Inbox claim and business write in one transaction","lang":"java","code":"record PaymentCaptured(UUID eventId, UUID paymentId, BigDecimal amount, String currency) {}\n\n@Component\npublic class PaymentCapturedConsumer {\n    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;\n    private final LedgerService ledger;\n\n    public PaymentCapturedConsumer(JdbcTemplate jdbc, LedgerService ledger) {\n        this.jdbc = jdbc;\n        this.ledger = ledger;\n    }\n\n    @RabbitListener(queues = \"ledger.payment-captured\")\n    @Transactional\n    public void on(PaymentCaptured event) {\n        // BAD: if (processedIds.contains(event.eventId())) return;  in memory, racy, lost on restart\n\n        // GOOD: atomic claim in the same transaction as the ledger posting\n        int claimed = jdbc.update(\"\"\"\n            INSERT INTO inbox (message_id, consumer, received_at)\n            VALUES (?, 'ledger', now())\n            ON CONFLICT (message_id, consumer) DO NOTHING\n            \"\"\", event.eventId());\n        if (claimed == 0) {\n            return; // duplicate: nothing to do, the container acks it\n        }\n        ledger.postCapture(event.paymentId(), event.amount(), event.currency());\n    }\n}","note":"The listener returns only after the transaction commits, and Spring AMQP acks on return, so a crash at any point either rolls back both writes or leaves a committed claim that makes the redelivery a no-op."},{"title":"Schema: inbox plus a natural business key","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE inbox (\n    message_id  uuid        NOT NULL,\n    consumer    text        NOT NULL,\n    received_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    PRIMARY KEY (message_id, consumer)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE ledger_entry (\n    id          bigserial     PRIMARY KEY,\n    payment_id  uuid          NOT NULL,\n    entry_type  text          NOT NULL,\n    amount      numeric(19,4) NOT NULL,\n    currency    char(3)       NOT NULL,\n    created_at  timestamptz   NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    CONSTRAINT uq_ledger_payment_entry UNIQUE (payment_id, entry_type)\n);","note":"The composite key lets several consumer groups share one inbox table, and the business unique constraint still blocks a double posting even if a message is republished with a new ID."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How long do you need to keep processed message IDs?","a":"At least as long as a duplicate can still arrive: the broker's redelivery and retry window, any DLQ replay horizon, and for Kafka the period over which someone might reset offsets, plus a margin. Prune older entries by time, ideally by dropping time-based partitions. Where the business data already has natural idempotency, such as a version check or a state transition, you may not need a separate table at all."},{"q":"Why not dedupe on the broker's delivery metadata?","a":"In RabbitMQ the delivery tag is a per-channel counter, so a redelivered message gets a new one, and the `redelivered` flag only says the broker may have delivered it before. Kafka offsets are stable, but a producer retry or a republish from an outbox creates a new offset for the same logical event. A producer-assigned event ID is the only key that survives all of these paths."}],"extra":["**Spring AMQP ack timing:** with the default `AcknowledgeMode.AUTO` the container acks after the listener returns and nacks on exception, requeueing by default; set `default-requeue-rejected: false` or throw `AmqpRejectAndDontRequeueException` so failures dead-letter instead of looping.","**Same ID, different payload:** if a duplicate ID arrives with a different body, that is a producer bug; store a payload hash in the inbox and alert on mismatch instead of silently skipping.","**Inbox retention:** keep rows longer than the longest possible redelivery, including DLQ replays days later; partition the table by day and drop old partitions rather than running large `DELETE`s.","**Dedupe is not ordering:** an idempotent consumer still applies events in arrival order, so combine it with version checks when events for one aggregate can be reordered."],"redFlags":["dedupes on the broker's delivery tag, which changes on redelivery","sends emails or calls providers inside the transaction and assumes rollback undoes them","keeps processed IDs with no retention plan, so the table grows without limit"]},{"id":187,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"How does two-phase commit work, and why is it rarely used between microservices?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A coordinator asks every participant to prepare (make the changes durable, keep the locks, vote) and commits only if all vote yes; otherwise everyone rolls back. If the coordinator fails after the votes, participants stay in doubt holding locks, and every participant must be up for any commit, so services use sagas and the outbox instead.","answer":["Two-phase commit (2PC) makes several resource managers (databases, message brokers) commit or abort together. In **phase 1 (prepare)** the coordinator, usually a transaction manager, asks each participant to prepare: it writes everything needed to commit to its durable log, keeps its locks, and votes yes or no. A yes vote is a promise: from then on the participant can neither commit nor abort on its own. In **phase 2** the coordinator durably records the decision and tells everyone to commit if all voted yes, or to roll back otherwise; participants apply it and release their locks. In Java this is XA: JTA (`jakarta.transaction`) with a transaction manager such as Narayana or Atomikos driving `XAResource.prepare`, `commit` and `recover`.","The weakness is the window between the phases. If the coordinator crashes after participants voted yes, they are **in doubt**: they hold row locks and cannot decide until the coordinator recovers and replays its log, which is why 2PC is called blocking. Availability multiplies: the transaction needs the coordinator and every participant, so three systems at 99.9% each give roughly 99.7%. Locks are held across network round trips and forced log writes, so contention and latency grow with each participant.","Between microservices it rarely fits. Services expose HTTP or gRPC APIs, not XA resources, so there is nothing to prepare; Kafka and RabbitMQ do not take part in XA, nor do most managed NoSQL stores; and the protocol ties every service's availability and lock time to the slowest participant, the coupling microservices try to remove. The alternatives accept eventual consistency: a **saga** of local transactions with compensations, the **transactional outbox**, idempotent consumers and reconciliation. 2PC remains reasonable inside one availability domain, such as two databases in one application."],"keyPoints":["prepare is a binding vote; commit applies the logged decision","a coordinator failure leaves participants in doubt and holding locks","between services, use sagas, the outbox and idempotency instead"],"examples":[{"title":"Both phases by hand with PostgreSQL prepared transactions","lang":"sql","code":"-- Participant 1: accounts database (needs max_prepared_transactions > 0; the default is 0)\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100.00\n WHERE id = 'acc-17' AND balance >= 100.00;      -- coordinator checks: 0 rows means vote no\nPREPARE TRANSACTION 'transfer-7f3a.accounts';    -- phase 1: durable, locks kept, vote yes\n\n-- Participant 2: ledger database\nBEGIN;\nINSERT INTO ledger_entries (transfer_id, account_id, amount)\nVALUES ('7f3a', 'acc-17', -100.00);\nPREPARE TRANSACTION 'transfer-7f3a.ledger';\n\n-- Coordinator: both voted yes and the decision is in its own log; phase 2 on each\nCOMMIT PREPARED 'transfer-7f3a.accounts';\nCOMMIT PREPARED 'transfer-7f3a.ledger';\n-- If any prepare failed: ROLLBACK PREPARED on those that did prepare\n\n-- Coordinator crashed between the phases? Later, on accounts:\nSELECT gid, prepared, owner, database FROM pg_prepared_xacts;\n--          gid            |          prepared          |  owner   | database\n-- transfer-7f3a.accounts  | 2026-10-12 03:14:07.12+00  | accounts | accounts\n-- The row lock on acc-17 is still held, and VACUUM cannot clean up behind it.","note":"`PREPARE TRANSACTION` detaches the transaction from the session, so it survives disconnects and even a server restart. That durability is what makes an orphaned one dangerous; alert on the age of rows in `pg_prepared_xacts`."},{"title":"XA inside one application vs a saga with an outbox between services","lang":"java","code":"// XA: a JTA transaction manager runs prepare and commit on both XA data sources\n@Transactional   // backed by JtaTransactionManager (Narayana, Atomikos)\npublic void transfer(Transfer t) {\n    accountsJdbc.update(\"update accounts set balance = balance - ? where id = ?\",\n            t.amount(), t.from());\n    ledgerJdbc.update(\"insert into ledger_entries (transfer_id, account_id, amount) values (?, ?, ?)\",\n            t.id(), t.from(), t.amount().negate());\n}   // both commit or neither, while the coordinator and both databases stay reachable\n\n// Between services: no global transaction. A local transaction plus outbox, then a saga.\n@Transactional   // plain local transaction in the accounts service\npublic void requestTransfer(Transfer t) {\n    accounts.placeHold(t.from(), t.amount(), t.id());               // funds reserved, not moved\n    outbox.add(OutboxEvent.of(\"transfer.hold-placed\", t.id(), t));  // same database commit\n}\n\n// Ledger service (idempotent by transfer id): posts entries, emits transfer.posted or rejected\n// Accounts service: captures the hold on posted, releases it on rejected (the compensation)","note":"The XA version blocks if the coordinator dies mid-commit. The saga holds no lock across services, but the hold is visible while it runs, so the business must accept that intermediate state."}],"followUps":[{"q":"A participant crashes after voting yes. What happens when it restarts?","a":"It comes back with the prepared transaction in its log, still in doubt and holding the locks. The transaction manager's recovery calls `XAResource.recover()` to list in-doubt ids and resolves each from the coordinator's log: commit if a commit was recorded, otherwise roll back. If that log is lost, someone decides by hand and reconciles afterwards."},{"q":"Doesn't three-phase commit solve the blocking problem?","a":"3PC adds a pre-commit round so participants can finish without the coordinator, but it is only safe with bounded message delays; under a network partition the two sides can decide differently. Real systems make the coordinator fault tolerant instead: Spanner runs 2PC over Paxos-replicated groups, so one machine failing leaves nothing in doubt."}],"extra":["**One-phase optimization:** with a single enlisted resource, transaction managers skip prepare, so XA overhead appears only when a second resource joins.","**Presumed abort:** no commit record in the coordinator's log means abort, so coordinators can forget aborted transactions and still answer participants correctly.","**Heuristic outcomes:** forcing an in-doubt participant by hand can leave some committed and others rolled back; JTA reports it as `HeuristicMixedException`, an incident for reconciliation, not a retry.","**Kafka transactions are not XA:** they make Kafka writes and consumer offsets atomic with each other only; synchronizing them with a database transaction just commits one after the other.","**Sagas lack isolation:** intermediate states (a placed hold, a pending order) are visible to others, so sagas need explicit pending states, semantic locks or commutative updates."],"redFlags":["Thinks 2PC stays correct and available when the coordinator fails","suggests XA across REST calls between services","presents sagas as giving the same isolation as a distributed transaction"]},{"id":188,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"Account events flow through a partitioned topic to many consumer instances. What order can each consumer actually rely on?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Distributed systems give ordering per key or partition, not globally; retries, redelivery and parallel consumers reorder events, so route related events by a key and have consumers enforce order with sequence or version checks.","answer":["What you actually get is **scoped ordering**: Kafka orders records within a partition, a RabbitMQ queue delivers FIFO to a single consumer, SQS FIFO orders within a message group, and a database orders commits within itself. A true global order needs a single sequencer (one partition, one leader), which caps throughput and availability, so most systems do not provide it and most business logic does not need it.","Order breaks in predictable places: producers retrying with several requests in flight, multiple producers for the same entity, competing consumers or a thread pool inside one consumer, nack and redelivery, delayed retry queues, DLQ replays, and repartitioning that changes which partition a key maps to. Any of these lets event 12 be processed before event 11.","The design answer has two parts. First, **choose the ordering key** as the aggregate whose invariants depend on order (`accountId`, `orderId`) and use it as the partition or routing key, so all its events share one ordered lane. Second, **make consumers verify order**: the source assigns a per-aggregate sequence or version (the aggregate's optimistic lock version or an outbox sequence), and the consumer applies only the expected next value, ignores older ones and parks gaps. State-carrying events with versions are more forgiving than deltas, because a newer snapshot can simply win."],"keyPoints":["order holds within one key's stream, never across the whole system","redelivery and parallel workers can still shuffle events for a key","use the entity ID as the message key so related events share a partition","consumers check sequence numbers or versions before applying"],"examples":[{"title":"Consumer enforcing per-account sequence","lang":"java","code":"record AccountEvent(UUID accountId, long seq, String type, BigDecimal amount) {}\n\nclass OutOfOrderException extends RuntimeException {\n    OutOfOrderException(UUID account, long expected, long got) {\n        super(\"account \" + account + \" expected seq \" + expected + \" but got \" + got);\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass AccountProjection {\n    private final AccountRepository accounts;\n\n    AccountProjection(AccountRepository accounts) {\n        this.accounts = accounts;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void apply(AccountEvent e) {\n        Account acc = accounts.findByIdForUpdate(e.accountId()).orElseThrow();\n        long expected = acc.getLastSeq() + 1;\n        if (e.seq() < expected) {\n            return; // duplicate or already applied\n        }\n        if (e.seq() > expected) {\n            // gap: 11 is missing, do not apply 12 yet; retry later or park\n            throw new OutOfOrderException(e.accountId(), expected, e.seq());\n        }\n        acc.apply(e.type(), e.amount());\n        acc.setLastSeq(e.seq());\n    }\n}","note":"The row lock plus `lastSeq` makes the consumer the final arbiter of order, so broker reordering can delay processing but can never corrupt the balance."},{"title":"How a retry topic reorders one key","lang":"text","code":"Partition 3, key acct-42, single consumer, retry topic with 30 s delay\n\nt0    consume seq 11 (debit 500)  -> provider timeout -> sent to retry topic\nt1    consume seq 12 (credit 200) -> applied\nt31   consume seq 11 from retry   -> applied after 12\n\nWithout a sequence check: the overdraft rule evaluated at t1 used the wrong balance.\nWith a sequence check: 12 is parked at t1 and applied right after 11 at t31.","note":"The partition itself was perfectly ordered; the retry path is what broke per-key order."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens to per-key ordering if you add partitions to a Kafka topic?","a":"The default partitioner maps a key by hashing it modulo the partition count, so after the change many keys land on a different partition. New events for a key can then be consumed before older events still waiting in its previous partition. Provision enough partitions up front, or drain producers and consumers before resizing, and have consumers check versions as a safety net."},{"q":"Why are timestamps a poor way to order events from different hosts?","a":"Clocks on different machines drift and get adjusted by NTP, so a later event can carry an earlier timestamp, and millisecond ties are common at volume. Order should come from a single writer per entity, such as an aggregate version, a database sequence or the partition offset. When there is no single writer, logical clocks such as Lamport or vector clocks capture causality without trusting wall time."}],"extra":["**Kafka producer ordering:** with idempotence enabled (the default since Kafka 3.0) order is preserved per partition with up to 5 in-flight requests; without it, retries plus `max.in.flight.requests.per.connection` above 1 can reorder.","**Repartitioning:** adding partitions changes the key to partition mapping, so events for one key can straddle two partitions during the switch; size partitions up front or drain producers before changing them.","**Spring Kafka concurrency:** a listener container processes each partition serially, so per-key order holds until you hand records to an async executor; concurrency above the partition count only leaves idle consumers.","**More parallelism than partitions:** the Confluent Parallel Consumer supports key-level ordering, processing different keys from one partition concurrently while keeping each key sequential.","**Timestamps are not order:** clocks skew across hosts, so decide order by source-assigned sequence numbers or versions, never by `occurredAt`."],"redFlags":["orders events by wall-clock timestamps taken on different hosts","adds partitions to a live topic without noticing that keys move","hands one partition to a thread pool and expects order to hold"]},{"id":189,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"What is consistent hashing, and where is it used?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Consistent hashing puts nodes and keys on one hash ring and gives each key to the next node clockwise, so adding or removing a node moves about 1/N of the keys instead of nearly all, as with `hash % N`. Virtual nodes even out the load; Cassandra, Dynamo-style stores, cache clients and load balancers use it, while Redis Cluster uses related fixed hash slots.","answer":["With `hash(key) % N`, changing N changes the result for almost every key: going from 4 to 5 cache nodes moves about 80% of keys, which for a cache means a miss storm on the database and for a datastore a massive reshuffle. Consistent hashing maps both node ids and keys onto one large circular hash space. A key belongs to the first node at or after its position, moving clockwise and wrapping around. When a node joins it takes over only the keys between its predecessor and itself; when it leaves, only its keys move to its successor. On average about 1/N of the keys move, and only to or from the changed node.","With one point per node the arcs are uneven, so some nodes own far more keys than others, and a failed node dumps all its load on a single neighbor. **Virtual nodes** fix both: each physical node is placed at many points (often 100 to 256), which averages out arc sizes, spreads a failed node's keys over many survivors, and lets a bigger machine take more points. Lookup is a binary search over the sorted points. For replication, Dynamo-style systems store a key on the next R distinct physical nodes clockwise, skipping other points of the same machine and ideally of the same zone.","It shows up in Cassandra (token ranges on a Murmur3 ring, `num_tokens` virtual nodes per node), Amazon's Dynamo design and Riak, memcached clients such as ketama, and load balancers that need cache affinity (Envoy's ring hash and Maglev, `hash ... consistent` in NGINX, `hash-type consistent` in HAProxy). Redis Cluster is related but different: 16384 fixed **hash slots**, `CRC16(key) mod 16384`, with slot ranges assigned to primaries explicitly and migrated during resharding. Fixed buckets (also the model of Kafka partitions) trade automatic placement for explicit, observable data movement. And consistent hashing balances keys, not traffic: one hot key still lands on one node."],"keyPoints":["hash % N remaps almost every key when N changes","a ring moves about 1/N of the keys; virtual nodes even out the load","Redis Cluster uses 16384 fixed slots, a related but different scheme"],"examples":[{"title":"A hash ring with virtual nodes, and how many keys move","lang":"java","code":"final class ConsistentHashRing {\n    private final NavigableMap<Long, String> ring = new TreeMap<>();\n    private final int virtualNodes;\n\n    ConsistentHashRing(int virtualNodes) { this.virtualNodes = virtualNodes; }\n\n    void addNode(String node) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < virtualNodes; i++) ring.put(hash(node + \"#\" + i), node);\n    }\n\n    String nodeFor(String key) {\n        Map.Entry<Long, String> owner = ring.ceilingEntry(hash(key));    // first point clockwise\n        return (owner != null ? owner : ring.firstEntry()).getValue();  // wrap around the top\n    }\n\n    private static long hash(String value) {\n        try {\n            byte[] d = MessageDigest.getInstance(\"MD5\").digest(value.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));\n            return ByteBuffer.wrap(d).getLong();    // well spread; not used for security\n        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(e);\n        }\n    }\n}\n// 100,000 keys \"user:0\" to \"user:99999\" on 4 nodes, then a 5th node is added:\n//   hash % N                  80,077 keys move (about 4 in 5)\n//   ring, 160 points per node 20,221 keys move (about 1 in 5, all to the new node)\n//   ring, 1 point per node    per-node load ranged from 9.6% to 38.9% before the change","note":"The numbers come from running this class. Virtual nodes are what make the ring usable: about 1/N of keys move either way, but with one point per node the load is badly skewed."},{"title":"Redis Cluster hash slots and hash tags","lang":"bash","code":"# Redis Cluster does not use a ring: 16384 fixed slots, slot = CRC16(key) mod 16384,\n# and each primary owns explicit slot ranges\nredis-cli -c -h redis-1 CLUSTER KEYSLOT cart:user:42       # (integer) 7950\nredis-cli -c -h redis-1 CLUSTER KEYSLOT orders:user:42     # (integer) 12670\n\n# Hash tags: only the part inside {} is hashed, so related keys share a slot\nredis-cli -c -h redis-1 CLUSTER KEYSLOT 'cart:{user:42}'   # (integer) 15880\nredis-cli -c -h redis-1 CLUSTER KEYSLOT 'orders:{user:42}' # (integer) 15880\nredis-cli -c -h redis-1 MGET 'cart:{user:42}' 'orders:{user:42}'  # works: same slot\nredis-cli -c -h redis-1 MGET cart:user:42 orders:user:42          # (error) CROSSSLOT ...\n\n# Scaling out moves whole slots, not individual keys\nredis-cli --cluster add-node redis-4:6379 redis-1:6379\nredis-cli --cluster rebalance redis-1:6379 --cluster-use-empty-masters\nredis-cli -h redis-1 CLUSTER SHARDS    # which slot ranges each shard owns now","note":"The slot numbers are real CRC16 results. Hash tags make multi-key commands and transactions possible in a cluster, but putting too many keys under one tag recreates a hot slot."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does Redis Cluster use fixed hash slots instead of a ring?","a":"Fixed slots make ownership explicit: each primary owns listed slot ranges, clients cache a small slot-to-node table and get a `MOVED` redirect when it changes, and resharding is a visible operation that migrates slot by slot. 16384 slots keep the slot bitmap in cluster heartbeat messages at 2 KB while still allowing fine-grained moves. The trade-off: rebalancing is an explicit action, not a side effect of adding a node."},{"q":"Does consistent hashing protect you from hot keys?","a":"No. It spreads keys, but a single key always maps to one node, so a celebrity account or a viral product can still saturate it. Mitigations are caching hot keys locally in the application, replicating a hot key under several suffixed names for reads, or bounded-load consistent hashing, which caps each node's share and spills the excess to the next node (HAProxy's `hash-balance-factor` implements it)."}],"extra":["**Everyone must agree:** all clients need the same hash function, point naming and node list, or one key lands on different nodes and the hit rate quietly drops.","**Rendezvous hashing:** an alternative that scores every node with `hash(key, node)` and picks the highest; it also moves only the changed node's keys, needs no ring, and costs O(N) per lookup, fine for tens of nodes.","**Jump consistent hash:** Google's algorithm maps a key to one of N numbered buckets with no memory and minimal movement, but buckets can only be added or removed at the end.","**Cassandra tokens:** Cassandra 4.0 lowered the default `num_tokens` from 256 to 16, paired with a token allocation algorithm, because many virtual nodes per node made repairs and streaming more expensive.","**Changing the hash function:** it remaps everything, like changing N with modulo, so treat it as a migration."],"redFlags":["Uses hash % N for a cache cluster that scales up and down","claims Redis Cluster runs a consistent hash ring","forgets virtual nodes or thinks consistent hashing fixes hot keys"]},{"id":190,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"How do leader election and consensus work at a high level?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Consensus lets a cluster agree on one leader and one ordered log despite crashes and network splits, which prevents split brain. In Raft, a leader is elected per numbered term by majority vote and an entry commits once a majority stores it; in practice you use etcd, ZooKeeper, Consul or KRaft instead of building your own, and you fence stale leaders.","answer":["Many systems need exactly one node to do something: accept writes for a partition, run the scheduler, own a lock. Electing a leader with heartbeats alone fails under a network partition, because both sides stop hearing each other and each picks its own leader: **split brain**, with two nodes accepting conflicting writes. Consensus algorithms (Paxos, Raft, ZooKeeper's ZAB) prevent this by requiring a **majority quorum** for every decision. Any two majorities of the same cluster share at least one node, so two leaders can never both win a majority in the same term, and a minority side simply stops making progress.","Raft is the easiest to explain. Time is divided into **terms**, numbered and only increasing. Followers expect heartbeats from the leader; when a follower's randomized election timeout fires, it becomes a candidate, increments the term, votes for itself and asks the others. Each node votes at most once per term, and only for a candidate whose log is at least as up to date as its own, so the winner holds every committed entry. The leader then handles all writes: it appends each command to its log, replicates it with AppendEntries, and marks it **committed** once a majority has stored it, after which every node applies it in the same order.","This runs underneath etcd (and therefore Kubernetes), Consul servers, Kafka's KRaft controller quorum and RabbitMQ quorum queues. The practical lessons: run 3 or 5 voting members, because 4 tolerates no more failures than 3; spread them over three zones, since with two zones losing the larger one stops the cluster; and expect every write to cost a majority round trip plus an fsync, which makes cross-region quorums slow. Above all, do not build your own. Take leadership from one of these systems (a Kubernetes Lease, an etcd election, a ZooKeeper recipe) and attach a **fencing token** to writes, because a leader stalled in a GC pause can wake up still believing it leads."],"keyPoints":["majority quorums prevent split brain because any two majorities overlap","Raft: terms, randomized elections, commit once a majority stores the entry","use an existing consensus system and fence stale leaders"],"examples":[{"title":"A Raft election and a network partition, step by step","lang":"text","code":"Five nodes A B C D E, majority = 3\n\nTerm 4  A is leader and sends AppendEntries heartbeats every 50 ms\n        A appends #18 \"order-9 status=PAID\" and replicates it to B and C\n        A, B, C store #18: 3 of 5, so #18 is committed and the client gets OK\n\nNetwork splits into [A B] and [C D E]\n        A keeps appending (#19) but reaches only B: 2 of 5, nothing commits\n        C, D, E stop hearing heartbeats; C's randomized timeout fires first\n\nTerm 5  C becomes candidate, votes for itself, asks D and E\n        D and E vote for C (one vote each per term; C's log is at least as up to date)\n        C wins 3 of 5, becomes leader and commits new entries with D and E\n        (had D timed out first, C would refuse it: D lacks #18 and could not reach 3)\n\nPartition heals\n        A sees term 5 > 4 and steps down to follower\n        A and B drop their uncommitted #19 and copy C's log\nResult  #18 survived, the minority committed nothing, no term had two leaders","note":"Randomized timeouts make split votes rare; the up-to-date check on votes guarantees a committed entry is never lost to an election."},{"title":"Fencing a stale leader with an epoch check in storage","lang":"java","code":"// Leadership and an epoch that grows with every new leader come from etcd, ZooKeeper\n// or a Kubernetes Lease. The storage, not the leader, enforces the epoch.\npublic record Leadership(String nodeId, long epoch) {}\n\n@Repository\n@RequiredArgsConstructor\nclass SettlementBatchRepository {\n    private final JdbcClient jdbc;\n\n    /** Returns false if a newer leader already wrote this batch: this node must stop. */\n    boolean markSettled(UUID batchId, Leadership me) {\n        int updated = jdbc.sql(\"\"\"\n                update settlement_batches\n                   set status = 'SETTLED', settled_by = :node, writer_epoch = :epoch\n                 where id = :id\n                   and writer_epoch <= :epoch\n                \"\"\")\n            .param(\"node\", me.nodeId())\n            .param(\"epoch\", me.epoch())\n            .param(\"id\", batchId)\n            .update();\n        return updated == 1;\n    }\n}\n\n// t0  node-a leads with epoch 7 and starts settling batch 42\n// t1  node-a stalls in a 20 s GC pause; its lease expires\n// t2  node-b becomes leader with epoch 8 and settles batch 42 (writer_epoch = 8)\n// t3  node-a wakes, still believes it leads, writes with epoch 7\n//     0 rows updated: node-a learns it was deposed instead of overwriting node-b","note":"The lease decides who should lead; the epoch check decides whose writes count. Without the second part, an expired leader can still overwrite its successor's work."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why use an odd number of nodes?","a":"A majority of 4 is 3, so a 4-node cluster tolerates one failure, the same as 3 nodes, while needing more acknowledgments per write. Five nodes tolerate two failures. Even sizes can also split evenly, 2 and 2, leaving neither side a majority."},{"q":"What happens to writes while a new leader is being elected?","a":"Nothing commits: clients see errors or timeouts for roughly the election timeout plus a voting round, usually seconds at most. A write sent just before the old leader died may or may not have committed, so clients retry, and the retry must be idempotent (the Raft paper recommends unique client request ids for this) or the command could be applied twice."}],"extra":["**Terms are fencing tokens:** Raft terms and Kafka's leader epochs are increasing numbers that let nodes reject a deposed leader's messages; the same idea protects application writes.","**Stale reads:** a leader cut off from the majority can still serve reads from its own state; linearizable reads confirm leadership with a majority first (etcd's default), serializable reads may be stale.","**KRaft:** Kafka's controllers form a Raft quorum whose replicated log is the `__cluster_metadata` topic; the active controller is the quorum leader and in turn picks partition leaders from each partition's ISR.","**FLP result:** no deterministic algorithm guarantees consensus in a fully asynchronous network if one node may crash; Raft and Paxos stay safe but need timeouts to progress, so they stall rather than err.","**Kubernetes leader election:** controllers hold a Lease and client-go stops leading when it cannot renew within the renew deadline, which is shorter than the lease duration; that margin narrows the overlap, only fencing closes it."],"redFlags":["Proposes leader election by lowest IP or a heartbeat table without a quorum","thinks a fourth node improves fault tolerance over three","lets a leader act on an expired lease without fencing its writes"]},{"id":191,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"To stop duplicate payment captures, a teammate wraps the capture call in a Redis lock. What would you use instead, and why?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Enforce correctness in the authoritative datastore with unique constraints, conditional updates and transactions; a distributed lock only reduces duplicate work, because leases expire, holders pause and the lock is not atomic with the write.","answer":["A Redis lock (`SET key value NX PX 10000`) is a **lease**: if the holder stalls longer than the TTL because of a GC pause, a VM freeze or a slow network, the lock expires, a second node acquires it, and both proceed. Redis failover with asynchronous replication can also lose a lock outright. Even a perfect lock lives in a different system from your data, so acquiring it and committing the write are never atomic.","The database, by contrast, enforces invariants **atomically with the write**: a unique constraint or partial unique index (one capture per payment), a conditional state transition (`UPDATE payment SET status = 'CAPTURE_PENDING' WHERE id = ? AND status = 'AUTHORIZED'` and check the row count), optimistic locking with `@Version`, `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, or serializable isolation. Whatever races happen above it, only one writer can win.","Locks are still useful as an **optimization**: stopping two nodes from running the same expensive report, spreading scheduled jobs (ShedLock), or avoiding thundering herds on a cache miss. When a lock must protect an external resource, add **fencing tokens**: a monotonically increasing number issued with the lock that the resource checks, rejecting writes from an older holder.","In interviews and production alike, the rule is: the lock may reduce how often you hit the constraint, but the constraint is what makes it correct."],"keyPoints":["the store holding the data is the only place a rule can be checked atomically","unique indexes, conditional updates and transactions enforce it in the write itself","a lock can expire mid-work and is never part of the commit","keep locks for saving duplicate effort, not for guaranteeing a correct result"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: lock as the only guard; GOOD: database claims the transition","lang":"java","code":"@Service\nclass CaptureService {\n    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;\n    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;\n    private final PaymentProvider provider;\n\n    CaptureService(StringRedisTemplate redis, JdbcTemplate jdbc, PaymentProvider provider) {\n        this.redis = redis;\n        this.jdbc = jdbc;\n        this.provider = provider;\n    }\n\n    // BAD: a GC pause longer than 10 s lets a second node capture as well\n    void captureWithLockOnly(UUID paymentId) {\n        Boolean got = redis.opsForValue()\n            .setIfAbsent(\"capture:\" + paymentId, \"node-a\", Duration.ofSeconds(10));\n        if (Boolean.TRUE.equals(got)) {\n            provider.capture(paymentId, \"capture-\" + paymentId);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: only one caller can move AUTHORIZED to CAPTURE_PENDING\n    void capture(UUID paymentId) {\n        int claimed = jdbc.update(\n            \"UPDATE payment SET status = 'CAPTURE_PENDING' WHERE id = ? AND status = 'AUTHORIZED'\",\n            paymentId);\n        if (claimed == 0) {\n            return; // already captured or in progress elsewhere\n        }\n        provider.capture(paymentId, \"capture-\" + paymentId); // idempotency key\n        jdbc.update(\"UPDATE payment SET status = 'CAPTURED' WHERE id = ? AND status = 'CAPTURE_PENDING'\",\n            paymentId);\n    }\n}","note":"The claim commits before the external call (no long transaction), and the provider idempotency key protects against the one remaining gap: a crash between the provider call and the final update."},{"title":"Database-level guarantees","lang":"sql","code":"-- At most one capture operation per payment, whatever the application does\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX uq_payment_single_capture\n    ON payment_operation (payment_id)\n    WHERE operation_type = 'CAPTURE';\n\n-- Serialize work on one key inside the database, released at commit, no TTL\nBEGIN;\nSELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtextextended('payment:' || '9b2f0c1e-6a7d-4f3b-8c2e-1d5a7e9f0b44', 0));\n-- ... check state and insert the operation ...\nCOMMIT;","note":"The partial unique index is the true invariant; the advisory lock is optional coordination that lives in the same transaction, so it cannot outlive the work the way a Redis lease can."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How should the application handle a duplicate rejected by a unique constraint?","a":"Catch that specific violation (SQLState `23505` in PostgreSQL, surfaced by Spring as `DuplicateKeyException`, a subclass of `DataIntegrityViolationException`) and translate it: return the existing resource for an idempotent retry, or `409 Conflict` with a stable error code for a real duplicate. In PostgreSQL the error aborts the current transaction, so use `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING RETURNING id` or a savepoint when more work must follow. Do not retry blindly, because the conflict will not go away."},{"q":"When is a distributed lock still useful in front of the database?","a":"When it saves expensive or noisy duplicate work, such as two nodes running the same nightly job or generating the same report, and an occasional double run is harmless because the database still rejects conflicting writes. It can also reduce contention on hot rows. If losing the lock would cause a wrong result rather than wasted work, the lock is in the wrong role."}],"extra":["**Redlock critique:** Martin Kleppmann's analysis showed that lease-based locks like Redlock depend on bounded process pauses and clock drift, which JVMs and VMs do not guarantee, so they are unsafe as a correctness mechanism.","**Advisory locks and poolers:** PostgreSQL session-level advisory locks break under PgBouncer transaction pooling because the session changes between transactions; transaction-level `pg_advisory_xact_lock` avoids that.","**ShedLock still needs idempotency:** `lockAtMostFor` expires if a job runs too long, so a second instance can start the same job; the job itself must tolerate overlap.","**Optimistic locking as default:** JPA `@Version` turns lost updates into `ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException` in Spring, a cheap guard for aggregate updates that needs no lock service at all."],"redFlags":["checks for an existing row in code and then inserts","lets a lock timeout decide whether money moves twice","has no plan for the error raised when the index rejects a duplicate"]},{"id":192,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"A payment system has retries, idempotency keys and an outbox. Why still run a daily comparison against the provider's records?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Reconciliation is a scheduled cross-check of separate sources of truth, such as the PSP, your ledger, orders and bank statements, that surfaces lost, doubled or disagreeing entries and feeds them into an owned, audited fix process; it catches the gaps that retries and idempotency leave behind.","answer":["Retries only fix failures you observed. Financial flows also fail silently: a provider charged the card but the response timed out, an event was dropped, a bug posted the wrong amount, an operator changed something manually, or the provider later applied a chargeback, refund, fee or FX adjustment. **Reconciliation** compares independently produced records of the same money movement to find these: missing on one side, duplicated, amount or currency mismatch, or status mismatch. Auditors and regulators expect it as a financial control.","Define **authority per fact** first: the provider or bank is authoritative for whether money moved, the ledger for accounting, the order service for customer intent. Match on stable references (your payment ID sent to the provider as merchant reference, plus the provider transaction ID), compare amount, currency and status, and allow a tolerance window for items still in flight across cutoffs.","Then the **repair workflow**: auto-resolve only well-understood break types, route the rest to an owned ops queue with evidence, and apply fixes as compensating ledger entries, never edits, each linked to the break with who, when and why. That gives an **audit trail**. Track **metrics**: open breaks by type and age, value at risk, auto-resolution rate and time to resolve, and alert on aged or growing breaks because they usually signal a systemic bug."],"keyPoints":["bugs, manual fixes and provider-side changes create drift that no retry sees","match records on one shared reference across every system","decide upfront which source is authoritative for each fact","each break gets an owner, an audit record and tracking by age and amount"],"examples":[{"title":"Daily provider versus ledger match that records breaks","lang":"sql","code":"WITH p AS (\n    SELECT merchant_ref, currency, sum(amount) AS amount, count(*) AS n\n    FROM provider_settlement\n    WHERE settlement_date = DATE '2026-09-25'\n    GROUP BY merchant_ref, currency\n), l AS (\n    SELECT payment_ref, currency, sum(amount) AS amount, count(*) AS n\n    FROM ledger_entry\n    WHERE entry_type = 'CAPTURE' AND booking_date = DATE '2026-09-25'\n    GROUP BY payment_ref, currency\n), matched AS (\n    SELECT coalesce(p.merchant_ref, l.payment_ref) AS ref,\n           p.amount AS provider_amount,\n           l.amount AS ledger_amount,\n           CASE\n             WHEN l.payment_ref IS NULL  THEN 'MISSING_IN_LEDGER'\n             WHEN p.merchant_ref IS NULL THEN 'MISSING_AT_PROVIDER'\n             WHEN l.n > 1 OR p.n > 1     THEN 'DUPLICATE'\n             WHEN p.amount <> l.amount   THEN 'AMOUNT_MISMATCH'\n           END AS break_type\n    FROM p FULL OUTER JOIN l\n      ON l.payment_ref = p.merchant_ref AND l.currency = p.currency\n)\nINSERT INTO recon_break (ref, break_type, provider_amount, ledger_amount, run_date)\nSELECT ref, break_type, provider_amount, ledger_amount, DATE '2026-09-25'\nFROM matched\nWHERE break_type IS NOT NULL;","note":"The `FULL OUTER JOIN` is the heart of it: rows with no partner are exactly the missing items; a real job also widens the date window so late settlements age before they count as breaks."},{"title":"Break types, authority and repair policy","lang":"text","code":"Break type            Authority   Auto repair?              Action\nMISSING_IN_LEDGER     provider    yes, if order is PENDING  post capture entry, mark order PAID\nMISSING_AT_PROVIDER   provider    no                        hold fulfilment, query provider API, ops case\nDUPLICATE             provider    no                        refund at provider, reversing ledger entry\nAMOUNT_MISMATCH       provider    no                        check fees and FX, ops case with evidence\nIN_FLIGHT (< 2 days)  none yet    wait                      re-check next run, age the item","note":"Writing the policy down per break type is what turns reconciliation from a report nobody reads into an operational process with owners."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What kinds of breaks would you expect reconciliation to find?","a":"Missing entries, such as a capture at the provider with no ledger posting after a crash; duplicates, such as two captures for one payment; amount or currency differences from fees, FX or partial refunds; and status disagreements, such as a refund completed at the provider but still pending internally. Timing differences, where settlement lands a day later, are expected and should clear automatically after a defined window. Classifying each break by type decides whether it can be auto-repaired or needs a person."},{"q":"Which system should be treated as authoritative when they disagree?","a":"Decide per fact, in advance: the provider or bank is usually authoritative for whether money actually moved, while your own system is authoritative for business intent, such as which order a payment belongs to. Repairs then bring the other side into line through normal, audited operations like compensating postings, never by editing rows directly."}],"extra":["**Unknown outcome state:** model provider timeouts as `UNKNOWN`, not failed, and resolve them by status query or reconciliation; treating a timeout as failure and retrying as a new request is how double charges happen.","**Control totals first:** compare record counts and sums per file before line matching, and treat a missing or late settlement file as a break in itself.","**Continuous checks:** besides the nightly batch, per-transaction checks shortly after an event (no webhook within 15 minutes, so query status) cut detection time from a day to minutes.","**Four-eyes on manual repairs:** manual adjustments above a threshold should need a second approver, and the engineers who write ledger code should not be the sole approvers of corrections."],"redFlags":["auto-corrects every mismatch with no human review or audit record","compares only daily totals, so offsetting errors hide each other","treats reconciliation as an accounting chore that engineering does not own"]},{"id":193,"category":"Kafka","question":"Explain topics, partitions, consumer groups and offsets in Kafka.","level":"Basic","tldr":"A topic is split into partitions, each an ordered, append-only, replicated log in which every record has an increasing offset. In a consumer group each partition has one owner and the group commits its own offsets, so many groups read the same data independently, and retention, not consumption, deletes records.","answer":["A **topic** is a named stream of records divided into **partitions**. Each partition is an append-only log, and a record's **offset** is its position in that log, meaningful only within that partition. A keyed record goes to `hash(key) % partitions`, so one key always lands in the same partition; keyless records are spread across partitions. Each partition is replicated (typically 3 copies): the **leader** takes the writes, followers copy it, and the **ISR** (in-sync replicas) is the set that is caught up. If the leader's broker fails, the controller promotes an ISR member.","A **consumer group** is a set of consumers sharing a `group.id`. Each partition is assigned to exactly one member, so a group's maximum parallelism equals the partition count: with 6 partitions, a seventh consumer sits idle. Groups are independent, so billing and analytics each receive every record. Progress is a **committed offset** per group and partition, stored in the internal `__consumer_offsets` topic and meaning the next record to read. Reading deletes nothing: records stay until retention (`retention.ms`, 7 days by default) expires them or compaction replaces them with a newer value for the same key, which is what makes replay possible.","In production the partition count is the decision that sticks: it caps consumer parallelism, can be increased but never decreased, and increasing it moves keys to other partitions. The number to watch is **consumer lag**, the log-end offset minus the committed offset per partition. Since Kafka 4.0 there is no ZooKeeper: metadata lives in a Raft-based controller quorum (**KRaft**), and ZooKeeper clusters must migrate to KRaft on a 3.x release before upgrading."],"keyPoints":["a partition is an ordered log addressed by offsets","one consumer per partition within a group; groups are independent","retention, not consumption, removes records"],"examples":[{"title":"Creating a topic and reading leaders, ISR and lag from the CLI","lang":"bash","code":"# 6 partitions, 3 copies of each, writes need 2 in-sync copies\nkafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 --create --topic orders.placed \\\n  --partitions 6 --replication-factor 3 --config min.insync.replicas=2\n\nkafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 --describe --topic orders.placed\n# Topic: orders.placed  PartitionCount: 6  ReplicationFactor: 3  Configs: min.insync.replicas=2\n#   Partition: 0  Leader: 1  Replicas: 1,2,3  Isr: 1,2,3\n#   Partition: 1  Leader: 3  Replicas: 3,1,2  Isr: 3,1      <- broker 2 fell behind\n\n# Where is the group, per partition?\nkafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 --describe --group billing\n# GROUP    TOPIC          PARTITION  CURRENT-OFFSET  LOG-END-OFFSET  LAG\n# billing  orders.placed  0          48211           48230           19\n# billing  orders.placed  1          47998           47998           0\n\n# Replay: move a stopped group back to midnight\nkafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 --group billing \\\n  --topic orders.placed --reset-offsets --to-datetime 2026-10-30T00:00:00.000 --execute","note":"`CURRENT-OFFSET` is the group's committed offset and `LAG` is the distance to the end of the log; both exist only per partition. The offset reset is refused while the group has active members."},{"title":"A plain consumer: group membership, polling and committing after processing","lang":"java","code":"Properties props = new Properties();\nprops.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, \"kafka-1:9092,kafka-2:9092\");\nprops.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, \"billing\");            // same id = share the partitions\nprops.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, false);      // commit only after processing\nprops.put(ConsumerConfig.AUTO_OFFSET_RESET_CONFIG, \"earliest\");  // used only when no offset exists\nprops.put(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class);\nprops.put(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class);\n\ntry (KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props)) {\n    consumer.subscribe(List.of(\"orders.placed\"));   // the coordinator assigns some partitions\n    while (running) {\n        ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500));\n        for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> r : records) {\n            invoices.createFor(r.key(), r.value());   // r.partition() and r.offset() locate it\n        }\n        consumer.commitSync();   // commits the next offset to read for each polled partition\n    }\n}\n// 6 partitions: 1 instance reads all 6; 3 instances read 2 each; 8 instances leave 2 idle.","note":"If the process dies after `createFor` but before `commitSync`, the next owner of the partition reads those records again: committing after processing is at-least-once, so the handler must tolerate duplicates."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens when the broker leading a partition goes down?","a":"The controller detects the lost broker and elects a new leader from the partition's ISR; clients refresh their metadata and continue against it, usually within seconds. Because only in-sync replicas are eligible (unclean leader election is disabled by default), writes acknowledged with `acks=all` survive. If no ISR member is left, the partition stays offline rather than electing a replica that is missing data."},{"q":"How would you get more parallelism than the partition count?","a":"A classic group cannot have more active consumers than partitions, so the options are adding partitions (which remaps keys), processing a partition's records concurrently inside the consumer while keeping per-key order (harder offset tracking), or share groups on recent Kafka versions, which give per-record acknowledgment without ordering. The cheapest fix is enough partitions up front."}],"extra":["**Offset meaning:** the committed offset is the next record to read; with the low-level API, committing `record.offset()` instead of `record.offset() + 1` reprocesses one record per partition after every restart.","**auto.offset.reset:** it applies only when the group has no valid committed offset, and the default `latest` means a brand-new group silently skips everything already in the topic.","**Log compaction:** with `cleanup.policy=compact` Kafka keeps at least the latest record per key and removes a key after a tombstone (null value); `__consumer_offsets` itself is compacted.","**Keyless records:** the producer fills a batch for one partition before moving on (sticky partitioning), which helps batching but gives no ordering between those records.","**KRaft roles:** controller nodes (usually 3 or 5) form the Raft quorum and brokers store partitions; small clusters can run both roles in one process."],"redFlags":["Believes consumed messages are removed from the topic","expects consumers beyond the partition count to add throughput","describes offsets or ordering as topic-wide instead of per partition"]},{"id":194,"category":"Kafka","question":"Kafka vs RabbitMQ: how do you choose between them?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Kafka is a replicated, partitioned log: records stay after they are read, each consumer group tracks its own offsets, and replay, fan-out and throughput are its strengths. RabbitMQ routes messages through exchanges into queues and deletes each one once acknowledged, which suits work distribution, per-message retries and rich routing.","answer":["The core difference is the storage model. Kafka appends records to partitioned logs and keeps them for the retention period whether or not anyone read them; a consumer is just a position in each partition. RabbitMQ classic and quorum queues hold messages until a consumer acknowledges them, then delete them, tracking delivery state per message. Kafka therefore gives **replay** (reset offsets and reprocess last week), cheap fan-out (a new group costs one offset per partition) and high throughput from batching and sequential I/O. RabbitMQ gives **routing flexibility** (direct, topic, fanout and headers exchanges), per-message ack and reject, dead-letter exchanges, TTLs and priorities.","The consumption models differ just as much. In Kafka, ordering is per partition and a group's parallelism is capped by the partition count; a record that keeps failing blocks its partition, because the committed offset cannot move past it, so retries need retry or dead-letter topics. In RabbitMQ any number of competing consumers share a queue and one bad message can be dead-lettered while the rest flow on; the price is that order is lost with several consumers or redeliveries. RabbitMQ **streams**, a replayable append-only queue type, narrow the replay gap, but Kafka's ecosystem (Connect, Kafka Streams, Debezium CDC) remains a differentiator.","So choose by the job. Kafka fits events as shared facts that several teams consume, rebuilding read models after a bug, CDC and data pipelines, and sustained high volume where per-key order is enough. RabbitMQ fits task queues and commands, rich routing, per-message retry delays, priorities and request/reply. Weigh operations too: Kafka needs partition and retention planning, while RabbitMQ suffers when queues grow long and memory alarms block publishers. Many companies run both: Kafka for domain events, RabbitMQ for work dispatch."],"keyPoints":["log with retention and offsets vs queue that deletes on ack","replay and fan-out favor Kafka; routing and per-message handling favor RabbitMQ","Kafka consumer parallelism is bounded by partitions"],"examples":[{"title":"The same order event, published as a Kafka fact and routed as RabbitMQ work","lang":"java","code":"// Kafka: publish once; every consumer group gets all of it and keeps its own offsets\nvoid publish(OrderPlaced event) {\n    kafka.send(\"orders.placed\", event.orderId(), event);  // key keeps one order's events in order\n}\n\n@Component\nclass RevenueProjection {\n    // A group created months later can rebuild its view from the retained log\n    @KafkaListener(topics = \"orders.placed\", groupId = \"revenue-v2\",\n                   properties = \"auto.offset.reset=earliest\")\n    void on(OrderPlaced event) { revenue.add(event); }\n}\n\n// RabbitMQ: route work items into a queue; each message is acknowledged and then gone\n@Configuration\nclass InvoiceQueues {\n    @Bean TopicExchange orders() { return new TopicExchange(\"orders\"); }\n\n    @Bean Queue euInvoices() {\n        return QueueBuilder.durable(\"invoices.eu\").quorum().deadLetterExchange(\"orders.dlx\").build();\n    }\n\n    @Bean Binding euBinding() {\n        return BindingBuilder.bind(euInvoices()).to(orders()).with(\"order.placed.eu.#\");\n    }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass InvoiceWorker {\n    @RabbitListener(queues = \"invoices.eu\", concurrency = \"4-16\")  // workers scale freely\n    void handle(OrderPlaced event) { invoices.create(event); }     // acked on return, rejected on throw\n}","note":"The Kafka side scales by partitions and lets a new group replay history; the RabbitMQ side routes by pattern and lets the worker pool grow regardless of partitioning. Both brokers redeliver, so both handlers must be idempotent."},{"title":"A quick decision table","lang":"text","code":"Need                                              Leans to   Why\n------------------------------------------------  ---------  ----------------------------------------\nSeveral services consume the same domain events   Kafka      one log, one offset per group\nRebuild a projection after a bug                  Kafka      retention plus offset reset\nCDC from a database, data lake feeds              Kafka      Connect and Debezium ecosystem\nSustained very high volume, per-key ordering      Kafka      batching, sequential I/O, partitions\nWork queue with bursty, slow jobs                 RabbitMQ   competing consumers, no partition cap\nRoute by region, type and priority                RabbitMQ   topic and headers exchanges, priorities\nRetry one message later, park poison messages     RabbitMQ   per-message reject, TTL and DLX\nRequest/reply between services                    RabbitMQ   reply-to and correlation ids\nStrict global order across everything             Neither    one partition or one consumer\nExactly-once side effects in other systems        Neither    idempotent consumers either way","note":"If the messages are facts that many readers need, possibly again later, start with Kafka; if they are commands for a pool of workers, RabbitMQ is usually simpler."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can you use Kafka as a job queue?","a":"You can, but with classic consumer groups busy workers are capped by the partition count, there is no per-message acknowledgment, and one slow job delays everything behind it in its partition. Kafka's share groups (KIP-932) add queue semantics with per-record acknowledgment and were declared production-ready in Kafka 4.2; check client and framework support before relying on them."},{"q":"When would RabbitMQ streams be enough instead of Kafka?","a":"If you already run RabbitMQ and need a replayable log at moderate volume, so a new consumer can read recent history, streams avoid running a second platform. They lack Kafka's ecosystem of connectors, stream processing and CDC tooling, so for a central event backbone shared by many teams Kafka remains the usual choice."}],"extra":["**Where state lives:** RabbitMQ tracks every unacknowledged message, Kafka one number per partition per group, which is why a new Kafka consumer group is nearly free while a RabbitMQ fanout puts each message into every bound queue.","**Head-of-line blocking:** with blocking retries, one poison record halts its Kafka partition, so plan the dead-letter topic from day one; RabbitMQ can reject that single message and keep the queue moving.","**Durability is configured:** Kafka needs `acks=all` with `min.insync.replicas=2`; RabbitMQ needs quorum queues, persistent messages and publisher confirms.","**Storage cost:** Kafka disk is sized by ingest rate times retention; a RabbitMQ queue holding millions of messages is a symptom, not a design.","**Per-key order:** Kafka gets it from the partition key; RabbitMQ needs a consistent hash exchange or a single active consumer per queue."],"redFlags":["Picks Kafka only because it is faster","assumes Kafka acknowledges and retries individual messages like a queue","never mentions replay, retention or the partition cap on consumers"]},{"id":195,"category":"Kafka","question":"How do you avoid losing or duplicating messages with Kafka?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Against loss: produce with `acks=all` to topics with replication factor 3 and `min.insync.replicas=2`, check every send result, and commit offsets only after processing. Duplicates cannot be ruled out end to end, so keep producer idempotence on, make consumers idempotent, and use transactions only for read-process-write flows inside Kafka.","answer":["Loss happens at three points. On the producer, `acks=1` lets the leader confirm before followers have the record, so a leader crash loses it; `acks=all` waits for every in-sync replica, but the ISR can shrink to the leader alone, so pair it with replication factor 3 and `min.insync.replicas=2`, and writes then fail with `NotEnoughReplicasException` instead of landing in one copy. `send` is asynchronous: the client retries until `delivery.timeout.ms` (2 minutes by default), then fails the future, so fire-and-forget code loses records unnoticed. On the consumer, committing before processing finishes loses records when the process dies mid-batch.","Duplicates come from retries and replays. `enable.idempotence=true` (the default in modern clients) attaches a producer id and sequence numbers, so the broker discards a retried batch it already wrote, but not the application calling `send` again after a timeout or restart. On the consumer, committing after processing means a crash or rebalance before the commit redelivers records: at-least-once, cured by an **idempotent consumer** that deduplicates by event id or upserts. Kafka **transactions** give exactly-once only for read-process-write inside Kafka: consume, produce and commit the consumed offsets atomically, with readers on `read_committed`. A database write or HTTP call in the middle is outside that transaction.","The reliable setup is boring: durable topic settings, `acks=all` with idempotence, checked sends, commit after processing, idempotent handlers and a dead-letter topic. When a service must update its database and publish an event, do not call `send` inside the `@Transactional` method and hope; the send can succeed while the transaction rolls back, or the reverse. Write the event to an **outbox** table in the same transaction and let a relay (a poller or Debezium) publish it at least once."],"keyPoints":["acks=all with min.insync.replicas=2 and checked sends stop producer-side loss","commit after processing is at-least-once, so consumers must be idempotent","transactions cover Kafka only; use an outbox for database plus Kafka"],"examples":[{"title":"Producer and consumer settings that close the loss windows","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  kafka:\n    producer:\n      acks: all                          # wait for every in-sync replica\n      properties:\n        enable.idempotence: true         # broker drops duplicate retries (producer id + sequence)\n    consumer:\n      group-id: ledger\n      enable-auto-commit: false          # the listener container commits after the listener returns\n      auto-offset-reset: earliest        # a new group starts at the oldest record, not \"now\"\n      isolation-level: read_committed    # never see records from aborted transactions\n    listener:\n      ack-mode: record                   # commit after each record instead of after each poll\n\n# Topic: replication factor 3, min.insync.replicas=2. One broker down is fine; with\n# two down, producers get NotEnoughReplicasException instead of writing one copy.","note":"Spring Kafka already disables auto-commit and by default commits after each poll's records are processed (`AckMode.BATCH`); `record` shrinks the redelivery window after a crash at the cost of more commits."},{"title":"Checked sends, a deduplicating consumer and a transactional read-process-write","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: fire and forget; if the send fails after retries, nobody notices\nkafka.send(\"payments.settled\", payment.id(), PaymentSettled.from(payment));\n\n// GOOD: wait for the broker acknowledgment before reporting success upstream\nkafka.send(\"payments.settled\", payment.id(), PaymentSettled.from(payment))\n     .get(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);           // throws on failure or timeout\n\n// Consumer: at-least-once delivery plus deduplication by event id\n@KafkaListener(topics = \"payments.settled\", groupId = \"ledger\")\n@Transactional\npublic void onSettled(PaymentSettled event) {\n    int inserted = jdbc.sql(\"\"\"\n            insert into processed_events (event_id, processed_at) values (:id, now())\n            on conflict (event_id) do nothing\n            \"\"\").param(\"id\", event.eventId()).update();\n    if (inserted == 1) {\n        ledger.post(event);                // same database transaction as the dedup row\n    }\n}   // the container commits the offset only after this returns\n\n// With spring.kafka.producer.transaction-id-prefix set, the container runs this listener\n// in a Kafka transaction: the send and the consumed offset commit or abort together\n@KafkaListener(topics = \"payments.settled\", groupId = \"fraud-scorer\")\npublic void score(PaymentSettled event) {\n    kafka.send(\"payments.scored\", event.paymentId(), scorer.score(event));\n}","note":"The dedup row and the ledger posting commit in one database transaction, so a redelivered event hits the unique key and is skipped. The transactional listener is exactly-once only for Kafka records, not for a database write inside it."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Does enable.idempotence give you exactly-once delivery?","a":"No. It removes duplicates caused by the client's own retries, per partition, within one producer session. If the application times out and calls `send` again, or restarts and republishes, those are new records. Consumers still see redeliveries after rebalances, so end to end you still need business ids and idempotent processing."},{"q":"Why not just call kafkaTemplate.send inside the @Transactional service method?","a":"If the send succeeds and the database rolls back, consumers act on an event that never happened; if the commit succeeds and the send fails, the event is lost. Synchronizing a Kafka transaction with the database transaction only orders the two commits, it does not make them atomic; the outbox makes the event part of the database commit."}],"extra":["**acks=all alone is not enough:** the default `min.insync.replicas` is 1, so once followers leave the ISR the leader alone satisfies `acks=all`, and one failure can lose acknowledged data.","**Offset expiry:** an empty group's committed offsets are deleted after `offsets.retention.minutes` (7 days by default); a consumer down for longer restarts from `auto.offset.reset`, and with `latest` it skips the backlog.","**Rebalance duplicates:** records processed but not committed when partitions are revoked go to the new owner again, so frequent rebalances mean steady duplicates.","**read_committed stalls:** consumers cannot read past an open transaction, so a producer that hangs mid-transaction stalls them until `transaction.timeout.ms` aborts it.","**Kafka Streams:** `processing.guarantee=exactly_once_v2` applies the same mechanism to whole topologies and their state stores; it still ends at the edge of Kafka."],"redFlags":["Claims Kafka is exactly-once, so consumers need no deduplication","relies on replication factor or acks=all without min.insync.replicas","commits offsets before processing or never checks the send result"]},{"id":196,"category":"Kafka","question":"How does Kafka guarantee ordering, and how do you choose a partition key?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Kafka orders records only within a partition, so records that must stay in order need the same key, which the default partitioner always maps to the same partition. Key by a high-cardinality id that matches the ordering need (usually the aggregate id), keep the partition count stable, and keep the idempotent producer on so retries cannot reorder writes.","answer":["A partition is an append-only log: the leader appends records in the order it receives them and consumers read them in offset order. Across partitions there is no order at all. For a non-null key the default partitioner computes `murmur2(keyBytes) % numPartitions`, so every record with the same key lands in the same partition and keeps its relative order; keyless records are spread over partitions with no ordering relationship. Ordering is therefore something you get per key, by choosing the key. Global order exists only in a single-partition topic, which also limits each consumer group to one active consumer.","The producer can still break the order. With retries and several in-flight requests per connection, batch 1 can fail, batch 2 can succeed, and the retried batch 1 lands after it. The **idempotent producer** (`enable.idempotence=true`, on by default in modern clients unless a conflicting setting such as `acks=1` turns it off) adds a producer id and per-partition sequence numbers, so the broker rejects out-of-sequence batches and order holds with up to 5 in-flight requests. The consumer can break it too: handing records to a thread pool, or non-blocking retry topics that let later records overtake a failed one, both reorder events for the same key.","Choosing the key trades ordering scope against load spread. The key should be the entity whose events must be applied in order, typically the aggregate id (`orderId`, `accountId`), with enough distinct values to spread evenly. Low-cardinality or skewed keys (`country`, `status`, a `tenantId` with one giant tenant) create **hot partitions**: one partition's lag grows while others idle, and more consumers do not help. Size the partition count for future peak load, because adding partitions changes `hash % n` for most keys: a key's older events stay in one partition, newer ones go to another, and consumers can apply them out of order. If you must grow, drain first or migrate to a new topic."],"keyPoints":["order exists per partition, so effectively per key","key by the entity whose history must stay ordered","idempotence keeps order across producer retries"],"examples":[{"title":"Good and bad keys, and what the partitioner does with them","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: no key; Debited and Closed for one account can land on different\n// partitions and be consumed in the wrong order\nvoid publishUnkeyed(AccountEvent e) {\n    kafka.send(\"account-events\", e);\n}\n\n// BAD: low-cardinality key; every Swedish account shares one hot partition\nvoid publishByCountry(AccountEvent e) {\n    kafka.send(\"account-events\", e.country(), e);\n}\n\n// GOOD: key by the entity whose history must stay ordered\nvoid publish(AccountEvent e) {\n    kafka.send(\"account-events\", e.accountId(), e);\n}\n\n// What the default partitioner does with a non-null key:\nstatic int partitionFor(byte[] serializedKey, int numPartitions) {\n    return Utils.toPositive(Utils.murmur2(serializedKey)) % numPartitions;\n}\n// Growing a topic from 6 to 8 partitions sends most keys (about 3 in 4) elsewhere.","note":"The 3 in 4 figure comes from hashing 100,000 sample keys; the exact share depends on the counts, but any change of partition count remaps most keys. Keep `acks=all` and idempotence on so retries preserve the send order."},{"title":"Keeping order on the consumer side, and guarding it with sequence numbers","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: events for one account run concurrently and finish in any order,\n// and the offset is committed before the work is done\n@KafkaListener(topics = \"account-events\", groupId = \"balances\")\nvoid onEventAsync(AccountEvent e) {\n    executor.submit(() -> balances.apply(e));\n}\n\n// GOOD: each partition is processed by one container thread, in offset order\n@KafkaListener(topics = \"account-events\", groupId = \"balances\", concurrency = \"3\")\nvoid onEvent(AccountEvent e) {\n    balances.applyInOrder(e);\n}\n\n@Service\nclass BalanceService {\n    @Transactional\n    public void applyInOrder(AccountEvent e) {\n        Account account = accounts.findByIdForUpdate(e.accountId()).orElseThrow();\n        long expected = account.lastEventSeq() + 1;\n        if (e.seq() < expected) {\n            return;                                  // redelivered duplicate: already applied\n        }\n        if (e.seq() > expected) {\n            throw new EventGapException(e.accountId(), expected, e.seq());  // retry, then DLT\n        }\n        account.apply(e);                            // also sets lastEventSeq = e.seq()\n    }\n}","note":"With 6 partitions, `concurrency = \"3\"` gives each thread 2 partitions and never splits one partition across threads. The sequence check covers what partition order cannot, such as redeliveries or two producer instances racing on one account."}],"followUps":[{"q":"One tenant produces 40% of all traffic. How do you key its events?","a":"Ask what really needs ordering. If it is per account or per order inside the tenant, key by that finer id (or `tenantId:accountId`), which spreads the tenant across partitions while keeping the order that matters. If the tenant truly needs one total order, its throughput is capped by one partition and one consumer, so give it a dedicated topic. Salting the key with a random suffix spreads load but gives up ordering for that tenant."},{"q":"How do consumer-side retries affect ordering?","a":"Blocking retries, the default with Spring Kafka's `DefaultErrorHandler`, seek back to the failed record and retry it before anything after it, so order is kept but the whole partition waits. Non-blocking retries (`@RetryableTopic`) move the failed record to a retry topic and carry on, so later events for the same key can be applied first. Use them only where per-key order does not matter."}],"extra":["**Before idempotence:** the old way to keep order with retries was `max.in.flight.requests.per.connection=1`, which costs throughput; idempotence makes that unnecessary.","**Compacted topics and repartitioning:** compaction works per partition, so after adding partitions a key can keep a stale value in its old partition and a current one in the new partition, and a consumer rebuilding state may apply them in the wrong order.","**Custom partitioners:** they can pin a huge tenant to dedicated partitions, but every producer must run the same logic or ordering silently breaks; a better key is usually simpler.","**Hot partition symptoms:** lag on one partition, one broker with far higher bytes in, and one busy consumer thread while its peers idle.","**Timestamps do not restore order:** sorting downstream by event time fails with clock skew between producers and late arrivals; use per-key sequence numbers or versions when order matters to correctness."],"redFlags":["Says Kafka keeps a whole topic in order","keys by a low-cardinality field without considering skew","adds partitions to a live keyed topic or processes records on a thread pool and still assumes order"]},{"id":197,"category":"Kafka","question":"What causes consumer lag and rebalances, and how do you handle them?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Lag grows when consumers process slower than producers write: traffic spikes, slow downstream calls, hot keys, poison pills or rebalances. Rebalances follow members joining, leaving or exceeding `session.timeout.ms` or `max.poll.interval.ms`; reduce them with bounded processing, cooperative assignment and static membership, and park poison records in a dead-letter topic.","answer":["Lag is per partition: the log-end offset minus the group's committed offset. Measure it with `kafka-consumer-groups.sh`, the client metric `records-lag-max` or an exporter, and alert on lag growth and on time lag, because 50,000 records is seconds on one topic and hours on another. The shape tells you the cause. Lag spread evenly over partitions means capacity: more traffic, or a slower database or external API. Lag on one partition means a hot key or a **poison pill**, a record that fails every time and, with blocking retries, stops its partition. Lag that saw-tooths with gaps in consumption points at rebalances.","A rebalance reassigns partitions. Members join or leave on deploys and scaling, and the coordinator removes a member that stops heartbeating for `session.timeout.ms` (45 s by default) or does not call `poll` within `max.poll.interval.ms` (5 minutes). The second is the classic loop: a batch of `max.poll.records` (500 by default) takes longer than the interval, the consumer is removed, its commit fails, and another member reprocesses the same records and hits the same limit. With eager assignment every rebalance pauses the whole group; the **CooperativeStickyAssignor** revokes only partitions that move. **Static membership** (a stable `group.instance.id`) lets a restarted pod rejoin within the session timeout and keep its partitions.","In production, bound processing time (timeouts on downstream calls, smaller `max.poll.records`) instead of raising `max.poll.interval.ms` to hide a slow consumer, and scale consumers only up to the partition count. For poison pills, wrap the deserializer in `ErrorHandlingDeserializer` and give Spring Kafka's `DefaultErrorHandler` a bounded back-off and a `DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer`, so a bad record goes to `<topic>.DLT` and the partition moves on. Then alert on DLT traffic: an unwatched dead-letter topic is delayed data loss."],"keyPoints":["even lag across partitions is capacity; lag on one partition is a hot key or poison pill","batches slower than max.poll.interval.ms cause rebalance loops","cooperative assignment, static membership and a DLT keep the group moving"],"examples":[{"title":"Consumer settings that keep a group stable","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  kafka:\n    consumer:\n      group-id: payment-settlement\n      max-poll-records: 100              # a batch must finish well inside max.poll.interval.ms\n      value-deserializer: org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.ErrorHandlingDeserializer\n      properties:\n        spring.deserializer.value.delegate.class: com.acme.payments.kafka.PaymentSettledDeserializer\n        max.poll.interval.ms: 300000     # the default; fix slow processing instead of raising it\n        partition.assignment.strategy: org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.CooperativeStickyAssignor\n        group.instance.id: ${HOSTNAME}   # static membership: needs stable names (StatefulSet pods)\n        session.timeout.ms: 60000        # must cover a pod restart, or the member is replaced\n    listener:\n      concurrency: 3                     # threads per instance; total consumers <= partitions","note":"Clients since Kafka 3.0 default to `RangeAssignor` plus `CooperativeStickyAssignor`, so one rolling restart that sets only the cooperative one migrates a live group. With `concurrency` above 1, Spring Kafka suffixes `group.instance.id` with `-1`, `-2` and so on."},{"title":"Bounded retries and a dead-letter topic with DefaultErrorHandler","lang":"java","code":"@Bean   // Spring Boot wires a single CommonErrorHandler bean into the listener factory\nDefaultErrorHandler errorHandler(KafkaTemplate<?, ?> template) {\n    // publishes to payments.settled.DLT, same partition number as the original\n    var recoverer = new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(template);\n\n    var backOff = new ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries(4);   // 0.5 s, 1 s, 2 s, 4 s, then recover\n    backOff.setInitialInterval(500);\n    backOff.setMultiplier(2.0);\n    backOff.setMaxInterval(5_000);                            // sleeps on the consumer thread\n\n    var handler = new DefaultErrorHandler(recoverer, backOff);\n    handler.addNotRetryableExceptions(InvalidPaymentException.class);   // no point retrying\n    return handler;\n}\n\n@KafkaListener(topics = \"payments.settled\", groupId = \"payment-settlement\")\nvoid on(PaymentSettled event) {\n    settlement.apply(event);      // throws: retried, then published to the DLT and committed\n}\n// The DLT copy carries exception headers (class, message, original offset): alert on it","note":"Unconfigured, `DefaultErrorHandler` makes 10 attempts with no delay, then logs and skips the record. `DeserializationException` is not retryable by default, so an unreadable record goes straight to the DLT. Give the DLT at least as many partitions as the source topic."}],"followUps":[{"q":"The group rebalances every few minutes and the same records are processed again and again. What is going on?","a":"Almost always `max.poll.interval.ms`: the logs show the poll timeout expiring, the member leaves, its commit fails and another member repeats the batch. Measure per-batch processing time, then lower `max.poll.records` or add timeouts to slow downstream calls. Raising the interval is the last resort, because it also delays detecting a stuck consumer."},{"q":"Lag is growing. Is adding consumers the fix?","a":"Only if lag is spread across partitions, the group has fewer consumers than partitions, and the consumer itself is the bottleneck. Lag on one partition will not move, and a saturated database only gets worse. Each scale-out is also a rebalance, so lag-based autoscaling (for example KEDA) needs a cap at the partition count and a cool-down."}],"extra":["**Heartbeat vs poll:** heartbeats come from a background thread, so `session.timeout.ms` detects a dead process and `max.poll.interval.ms` a stuck poll loop; a deadlocked listener keeps heartbeating.","**Static membership trade-off:** when a static member really dies, its partitions stall until the session timeout expires, and it needs ids that survive restarts, which Deployment pod names do not.","**New rebalance protocol:** Kafka 4.0 made the KIP-848 protocol generally available (`group.protocol=consumer`): the broker computes assignments incrementally with no group-wide pause, and `partition.assignment.strategy` and `session.timeout.ms` give way to broker-side group configs.","**Pausing instead of sleeping:** Spring Kafka's `ContainerPausingBackOffHandler` pauses the container during long back-offs instead of blocking the thread; non-blocking retry topics avoid the wait at the cost of per-key order.","**Lag in time:** divide lag by the consume rate to get seconds, and alert on what the business tolerates."],"redFlags":["Answers every lag problem with more consumers","raises max.poll.interval.ms to hours instead of bounding processing time","retries a poison record forever or parks it in a DLT nobody monitors"]},{"id":198,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"When would a RabbitMQ publisher choose a fanout exchange over a topic one, and where do direct and headers exchanges fit?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Publishers send to exchanges, which route to queues via bindings: direct matches the exact routing key, topic matches dotted wildcard patterns, fanout copies to every bound queue, headers matches header values.","answer":["The model is publisher to **exchange**, exchange to **queues** via **bindings**, queues to consumers. An exchange stores nothing; it only evaluates bindings. If nothing matches, the message is dropped unless you publish with `mandatory` and handle returns, or configure an alternate exchange. The queue is what buffers and delivers. The default exchange (`\"\"`) is a direct exchange where every queue is bound by its own name.","**Direct** routes when the binding key equals the routing key exactly; good for commands and work queues (`refund.requested`). **Topic** matches dot-separated words where `*` is exactly one word and `#` is zero or more (`payment.*.failed`, `payment.#`); the usual choice for domain events. **Fanout** ignores the routing key and copies to every bound queue; right for true broadcast such as cache invalidation, wrong for targeted delivery because every consumer then receives and discards messages it does not want. **Headers** ignores the routing key and matches message headers with `x-match` set to `all` or `any`; flexible but rarely needed, slower and harder to see.","**Topology should mirror business routing.** A common shape is one durable topic exchange per domain (`payments.events`), routing keys built from stable business attributes (`payment.<event>.<merchant>`), and each consuming service owning its queue and bindings, which gives pub/sub between services and competing consumers inside one. Declare it as code so it is reviewed and versioned."],"keyPoints":["publishers target exchanges, and bindings decide which queues get a copy","direct needs an exact key match, while topic supports `*` and `#` wildcards","fanout ignores the key and copies to every bound queue","headers compares message header values, with `x-match` set to all or any"],"examples":[{"title":"Topic exchange for payment events in Spring AMQP","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass PaymentTopology {\n\n    @Bean\n    Declarables paymentEvents() {\n        TopicExchange events = ExchangeBuilder.topicExchange(\"payments.events\").durable(true).build();\n        FanoutExchange invalidate = ExchangeBuilder.fanoutExchange(\"config.invalidate\").build();\n\n        Queue ledger = QueueBuilder.durable(\"ledger.payment-events\").quorum().build();\n        Queue fraud = QueueBuilder.durable(\"fraud.payment-failures\").quorum().build();\n        Queue localCache = new AnonymousQueue(); // one per instance, deleted on disconnect\n\n        return new Declarables(\n            events, invalidate, ledger, fraud, localCache,\n            // routing key format: payment.<event>.<merchantId>\n            BindingBuilder.bind(ledger).to(events).with(\"payment.captured.*\"),\n            BindingBuilder.bind(ledger).to(events).with(\"payment.refunded.*\"),\n            BindingBuilder.bind(fraud).to(events).with(\"payment.failed.*\"),\n            // true broadcast: every instance must drop its cache\n            BindingBuilder.bind(localCache).to(invalidate));\n    }\n}","note":"Each consuming service declares its own queue and only the keys it needs; fanout is reserved for the one case where every instance really must get every message."},{"title":"Which queue receives which routing key","lang":"text","code":"Published routing key          ledger (payment.captured.*)   fraud (payment.failed.*)\npayment.captured.m_1042        yes                           no\npayment.failed.m_1042          no                            yes\npayment.failed.m_1042.retry    no                            no  (* matches one word only)\npayment.refunded.m_7            yes (payment.refunded.*)      no","note":"The third row is the classic surprise: `*` matches exactly one word, so use `#` when the key may grow extra segments."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens to a message that matches no binding?","a":"By default the exchange silently drops it. Publish with `mandatory=true` and register a return listener so the broker sends it back via `basic.return`, or declare an alternate exchange through the `alternate-exchange` argument or a policy so unroutable messages land in a queue you monitor. With publisher confirms on, the ack still arrives for an unroutable message, after any return, so confirms alone do not reveal it."},{"q":"How does the default exchange work?","a":"It is a pre-declared direct exchange with an empty name, and every queue is automatically bound to it using the queue's name as the routing key. Publishing to it with a queue name as the routing key delivers straight to that queue. It is convenient for simple work queues but hides routing intent, so named exchanges are better once several consumers or event types are involved."}],"extra":["**Unroutable messages:** without `mandatory=true` plus `RabbitTemplate.setReturnsCallback`, or an `alternate-exchange` argument, a typo in a routing key silently discards messages.","**Exchange to exchange bindings:** RabbitMQ can bind exchanges to exchanges, which lets you layer a domain topic exchange into per-team exchanges without publishers knowing about them.","**Streams for replay:** if consumers need to re-read history or you have very high fan-out, RabbitMQ Streams (and partitioned super streams) give an append-only log with non-destructive reads, closer to Kafka.","**Routing keys are not a data contract:** also put the merchant ID and event type in the body or headers, because consumers should not parse routing keys for business data and keys may be restructured."],"redFlags":["does not know that a message matching no binding is dropped by default","uses a headers exchange where a simple routing key would do","assumes topic wildcards can exclude a particular value"]},{"id":199,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"Your consumer uses auto-ack and loses orders when pods restart. How should acks, rejections and requeueing be handled instead?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Switch to manual acknowledgements and confirm a delivery only once its effects are committed; a negative ack either puts the message back on the queue or routes it to a dead-letter exchange (dropping it if none is configured), so return only messages that failed transiently, cap how often, and park poison messages in a DLQ.","answer":["With manual acknowledgement each delivery carries a delivery tag. `basicAck` removes the message; `basicReject(tag, requeue)` rejects one message and `basicNack(tag, multiple, requeue)` is RabbitMQ's extension that can reject many. With `requeue=true` the message goes back to the queue for redelivery; with `requeue=false` it is dead-lettered if the queue has a dead-letter exchange, otherwise discarded. Unacked deliveries are redelivered when the channel or connection closes. Auto-ack mode treats a message as done the moment it is sent, which is at-most-once.","The risks follow directly. **Ack before processing** loses the message if the consumer crashes. **Ack after durable processing** is correct but means a crash between commit and ack produces a duplicate, so the consumer must be idempotent. **Requeue on a permanent error** (deserialization failure, validation error, a bug) creates a hot loop: the same message is redelivered immediately, burns CPU, floods logs and can starve everything behind it. `multiple=true` can ack or nack messages that another thread is still processing.","Guardrails: classify errors, reject permanent ones without requeue into a DLQ, retry transient ones with a delay and a bounded count (retry queues, Spring Retry, or quorum queue delivery limits), and keep processing well under the broker's consumer timeout. In Spring AMQP, note that the default `AUTO` mode requeues on any exception except conversion failures and other fatal errors or an `AmqpRejectAndDontRequeueException`, unless you disable `default-requeue-rejected`."],"keyPoints":["auto-ack hands the message over before your code has done anything","confirm a delivery only after its side effects are committed","a requeued message comes straight back, so a permanent failure spins","rejecting without requeue routes to a dead-letter exchange when one is configured"],"examples":[{"title":"Manual ack with error classification","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass RefundConsumer {\n    private final RefundService refunds;\n    private final MessageConverter converter;\n    private final RabbitTemplate rabbit;\n\n    RefundConsumer(RefundService refunds, MessageConverter converter, RabbitTemplate rabbit) {\n        this.refunds = refunds;\n        this.converter = converter;\n        this.rabbit = rabbit;\n    }\n\n    @RabbitListener(queues = \"refunds.requests\", ackMode = \"MANUAL\")\n    void on(Message message, Channel channel) throws IOException {\n        long tag = message.getMessageProperties().getDeliveryTag();\n        // BAD: channel.basicAck(tag, false) here, before the work, loses the refund on a crash\n        try {\n            refunds.process((RefundRequested) converter.fromMessage(message)); // commits DB tx\n            channel.basicAck(tag, false);            // GOOD: ack after the durable commit\n        } catch (TransientDataAccessException e) {\n            channel.basicNack(tag, false, false);    // DLX routes it to a delayed retry queue\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            rabbit.send(\"refunds\", \"refund.parked\", message); // poison: park it for humans\n            channel.basicAck(tag, false);            // never requeue forever\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Transient and permanent failures take different paths; nothing uses `requeue=true`, so a bad message can never spin in a hot loop."},{"title":"Safer defaults for Spring AMQP auto mode","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  rabbitmq:\n    listener:\n      simple:\n        acknowledge-mode: auto           # ack when the listener returns normally\n        default-requeue-rejected: false  # exceptions dead-letter instead of requeueing\n        prefetch: 20","note":"With `default-requeue-rejected: false` an unexpected exception goes to the queue's dead-letter exchange instead of being redelivered immediately forever."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you cap redeliveries for a message that keeps failing?","a":"Quorum queues count deliveries and support a `delivery-limit`, set by policy or `x-delivery-limit`, after which the message is dead-lettered or dropped; RabbitMQ 4.0 sets a default of 20. For classic queues, reject without requeue into a retry path and count attempts from the `x-death` header or your own header, routing to a parking queue after N attempts. Either way, configure a dead-letter exchange so the message is kept for inspection."},{"q":"What does `consumer_timeout` protect against?","a":"If a delivery stays unacknowledged longer than the timeout, 30 minutes by default, the broker closes the channel with a `PRECONDITION_FAILED` error and requeues its unacked messages. That stops a stuck consumer from holding messages forever. Long-running tasks must finish well within it, or the message should only trigger a job tracked in the database and be acked right away."}],"extra":["**Consumer timeout:** RabbitMQ closes the channel if a delivery stays unacked longer than `consumer_timeout` (30 minutes by default), which redelivers everything that channel held; long-running jobs need a different pattern.","**Quorum queue delivery limit:** quorum queues count redeliveries and dead-letter a message once it exceeds `delivery-limit` (RabbitMQ 4.0 made 20 the default), turning a hot loop into a DLQ entry.","**Tags belong to a channel:** acks must go on the channel that received the delivery; after a reconnect old tags are invalid and acking them closes the channel with an unknown delivery tag error.","**Consumer acks are half the story:** use publisher confirms (`spring.rabbitmq.publisher-confirm-type: correlated`) and persistent messages, otherwise messages can be lost before they ever reach the queue."],"redFlags":["uses `basic.nack` with `multiple=true` without realizing it covers earlier deliveries too","forgets that unacked messages count against prefetch and can stall the consumer"]},{"id":200,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"A downstream API is flaky and some messages are simply invalid. How would you route failures through retries and a dead-letter queue?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Classify failures, retry only transient ones a bounded number of times with backoff (via delayed retry queues), and dead-letter everything else to an owned DLQ with alerting, inspection and safe replay tooling.","answer":["Start with **classification**. Transient failures (timeouts, connection resets, HTTP 503 or 429, lock timeouts) may succeed later; permanent failures (deserialization errors, validation failures, business rule violations, unknown entity, a null pointer bug) never will, so they go straight to the DLQ. Unknown errors are treated as transient but with a bounded attempt count.","RabbitMQ has no built-in retry delay, so the usual pattern is **retry queues with a per-queue TTL**: the main queue dead-letters rejected messages into a retry exchange, the retry queue holds them for its TTL and dead-letters them back to the work exchange. Several tiers (10 s, 1 min, 10 min) give exponential backoff. The consumer counts attempts from the `x-death` header or its own header and, once the limit is hit, parks the message. In-process retry with Spring Retry is simpler for short delays but blocks the consumer thread.","The **DLQ (parking lot)** is an operational product, not a trash can: keep original headers, the exception, attempt count and first failure time; give it an owning team, alerts on depth and oldest message age, a runbook, and a **replay tool** that can inspect, filter and republish to the original exchange at a controlled rate. Replays must go through the same idempotent consumer."],"keyPoints":["separate failures that may pass later from ones that never will","send temporary failures through delay queues with growing TTLs","stop after a fixed attempt count and park the message","the DLQ needs an owner, alerts and a tool to inspect and replay"],"examples":[{"title":"Retry and parking topology","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass RefundRetryTopology {\n\n    @Bean\n    Declarables refundQueues() {\n        DirectExchange work = new DirectExchange(\"refunds\");\n        DirectExchange retry = new DirectExchange(\"refunds.retry\");\n\n        Queue main = QueueBuilder.durable(\"refunds.requests\").quorum()\n            .deadLetterExchange(\"refunds.retry\")   // rejected messages go to the retry tier\n            .build();\n        Queue wait30s = QueueBuilder.durable(\"refunds.retry.30s\")\n            .ttl(30_000)\n            .deadLetterExchange(\"refunds\")          // expired messages return to work\n            .deadLetterRoutingKey(\"refund\")\n            .build();\n        Queue parkingLot = QueueBuilder.durable(\"refunds.parking-lot\").quorum().build();\n\n        return new Declarables(work, retry, main, wait30s, parkingLot,\n            BindingBuilder.bind(main).to(work).with(\"refund\"),\n            BindingBuilder.bind(wait30s).to(retry).with(\"refund\"),\n            BindingBuilder.bind(parkingLot).to(work).with(\"refund.parked\"));\n    }\n}","note":"A rejected message waits 30 s in a queue nobody consumes, then flows back to the main queue; nothing spins, and the broker does the delay."},{"title":"Consumer that bounds attempts and parks poison messages","lang":"java","code":"@RabbitListener(queues = \"refunds.requests\")\nvoid on(Message message) {\n    try {\n        refunds.process((RefundRequested) converter.fromMessage(message));\n    } catch (ProviderUnavailableException | TransientDataAccessException e) {\n        if (attempts(message) >= 5) {\n            park(message, e);   // exhausted: stop retrying, container acks\n            return;\n        }\n        throw new AmqpRejectAndDontRequeueException(\"transient, retry later\", e);\n    } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n        park(message, e);       // permanent: validation error, bug, unknown refund\n    }\n}\n\nprivate long attempts(Message m) {\n    List<Map<String, ?>> deaths = m.getMessageProperties().getXDeathHeader();\n    return deaths == null || deaths.isEmpty() ? 0 : (Long) deaths.get(0).get(\"count\");\n}\n\nprivate void park(Message m, Exception e) {\n    m.getMessageProperties().setHeader(\"x-exception\", e.getClass().getName() + \": \" + e.getMessage());\n    rabbit.send(\"refunds\", \"refund.parked\", m);\n}","note":"Throwing `AmqpRejectAndDontRequeueException` routes the message through the dead-letter exchange into the retry tier; the exception header gives whoever triages the parking lot the context they need."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you build delayed retries in RabbitMQ without a plugin?","a":"Create a retry queue per delay step, each with `x-message-ttl` and an `x-dead-letter-exchange` that points back to the work exchange, so expired messages return for another attempt. On a transient failure the consumer publishes the message to the next delay step and acks the original, tracking attempts in a header or from `x-death`. Use one queue per delay value, because messages only expire at the head of a queue, so mixed per-message TTLs in one queue block each other."},{"q":"What should a dead-lettered message carry?","a":"The original body unchanged, the original exchange and routing key (RabbitMQ records these in `x-death`), the error class and message, the attempt count, timestamps and the trace ID. With that, an operator can understand the failure without digging through logs and can replay the message to its original destination once the cause is fixed."}],"extra":["**Per-message TTL trap:** in classic queues expired messages are dead-lettered only when they reach the head, so mixing different per-message TTLs in one retry queue stalls short delays behind long ones; use one queue per delay tier.","**Delayed message plugin:** `rabbitmq-delayed-message-exchange` supports per-message `x-delay`, but delayed messages are not replicated and it is not meant for large volumes, so TTL tiers are the more predictable choice.","**Spring's recoverers:** with `spring.rabbitmq.listener.simple.retry.enabled`, exhausted retries go to a `MessageRecoverer`; `RepublishMessageRecoverer` republishes to a parking exchange with exception message and stack trace headers.","**Kafka equivalent:** Spring Kafka's `@RetryableTopic` creates delayed retry topics and a dead-letter topic automatically, the same design with different plumbing.","**Replay safety:** replays days later can meet changed schemas and changed state, so replay through the normal idempotent consumer, throttle it, and record who replayed what and why."],"redFlags":["retries validation errors that can never succeed","replays the whole DLQ at full speed into a dependency that just recovered","drops the original error and headers when moving messages to the DLQ"]},{"id":201,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"One consumer sits on hundreds of unacked messages while others idle. Which prefetch and concurrency settings would you change?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Prefetch caps unacked messages buffered per consumer; size it from processing time, latency and memory so consumers stay busy without hoarding, and add consumers only while downstream capacity allows.","answer":["Prefetch is set with `basic.qos`: the broker pushes up to N unacknowledged deliveries to a consumer and then waits for acks. Prefetch 0 means unlimited, so the broker pushes as fast as it can, the consumer's memory grows with the backlog and fairness disappears. Spring AMQP containers default to 250, which is fine for millisecond messages and far too high for slow ones.","**Throughput:** a consumer needs enough messages in flight to hide the round trip between ack and next delivery. For fast handlers (about 1 ms) with a few ms of network latency, a prefetch of tens keeps it busy; for slow tasks (seconds) a prefetch of 1 to 5 is plenty, and more only hoards work. Memory matters on both sides because every unacked message is held by the broker and the client.","**Fairness and redelivery:** RabbitMQ round-robins between consumers, but a consumer with a large prefetch grabs a big batch; a slow or stuck consumer then sits on hundreds of messages while others idle, a newly scaled pod gets nothing until buffers drain, and a crash redelivers the whole batch at once, which shows up as a duplicate and latency burst.","**Concurrency multiplies:** total in flight is instances times consumers times prefetch. Adding consumers beyond what the database pool or a rate-limited provider can absorb just moves the queue into thread pools, causing timeouts, retries and more load."],"keyPoints":["`basic.qos` caps how many unacked deliveries a consumer may hold","a low value spreads slow work evenly; a higher one helps fast, small messages","every buffered message costs memory and becomes a redelivery if the consumer dies","more consumers help only until the database or provider is the bottleneck"],"examples":[{"title":"Slow task queue sized to downstream capacity","lang":"yaml","code":"# Statement PDF generation: about 2 s per message, each needs one DB connection\nspring:\n  rabbitmq:\n    listener:\n      type: simple\n      simple:\n        acknowledge-mode: auto\n        prefetch: 2           # small buffer: fair spreading, small redelivery burst\n        concurrency: 4        # consumers per pod at start\n        max-concurrency: 8    # never more consumers than the pool below can serve\n  datasource:\n    hikari:\n      maximum-pool-size: 10","note":"The ceiling of 8 consumers per pod is derived from the connection pool, not from the queue depth, so scaling out cannot exhaust the database."},{"title":"Why the default can hurt a slow queue","lang":"text","code":"Pods 6, consumers per pod 10, prefetch 250 (Spring AMQP default)\nUnacked in flight     = 6 x 10 x 250 = 15,000 messages\nProcessing time       = 200 ms, so each consumer buffers 50 s of work\nOne pod restarts      = up to 2,500 messages redelivered at once\nNew pod added         = idle while old consumers drain their buffers\n\nPrefetch 5            = 300 in flight, about 1 s buffered per consumer, fair spreading","note":"The same queue with a small prefetch reacts to scaling within seconds instead of minutes and limits the blast radius of a crash."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you pick a starting prefetch value?","a":"Aim to cover the round trip: roughly the time for an ack to reach the broker and a new delivery to arrive, divided by per-message processing time, plus some headroom. For 1 ms messages over a 20 ms round trip that suggests a few dozen; for one-second tasks, 1 or 2. Then check whether consumers ever sit idle waiting for deliveries and adjust from there."},{"q":"How do Spring AMQP's prefetch and concurrency settings combine?","a":"Each listener consumer has its own channel and prefetch, so the messages in flight per instance are roughly the active consumer count, between `concurrentConsumers` and `maxConcurrentConsumers`, times `prefetchCount`. Spring AMQP's default prefetch is 250, which is far too high for slow tasks and should be lowered explicitly. Multiply by the pod count to see how much of the backlog the fleet holds unacked at any moment."}],"extra":["**Per-consumer semantics:** RabbitMQ applies `basic.qos` with `global=false` per consumer, unlike the AMQP spec's per-channel meaning, and quorum queues do not support global QoS at all.","**Consumer utilisation:** the management UI and Prometheus expose how often the queue could deliver immediately; below 100% with a backlog means prefetch is too low or consumers too slow.","**Container choice:** `SimpleMessageListenerContainer` supports dynamic scaling between `concurrency` and `max-concurrency`, while `DirectMessageListenerContainer` uses `consumers-per-queue` and runs the listener on the client library's threads.","**Single active consumer:** with `x-single-active-consumer` for ordering, only one consumer works at a time, so prefetch and handler speed are your only throughput levers."],"redFlags":["leaves auto-ack on, which makes the prefetch setting meaningless","raises prefetch to fix slowness without measuring per-message time","scales consumers on CPU while the backlog keeps growing"]},{"id":202,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"Account events are published in order to one queue with four consumers, yet balances apply out of sequence. Why?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"A single queue delivers FIFO, but multiple consumers, prefetch, requeues and retry queues change processing order; strict order needs one active consumer per ordering key or sequence checks in the consumer.","answer":["RabbitMQ guarantees that messages published on one channel, through one exchange, to one queue are enqueued in publish order, and the queue delivers them FIFO. There is no ordering across different publishers or channels, and delivery order is not processing order.","Processing order breaks as soon as you add **competing consumers** (message 2 on a fast consumer finishes before message 1 on a slow one), **concurrency inside a consumer** with prefetch above 1, **requeue after nack** (the message is redelivered after later ones may already be done), **redelivery after a crash**, **retry queues with TTL** (the message rejoins much later), **DLQ replays**, and priority queues. Running consumers in parallel and keeping a strict processing order inherently conflict.","For strict ordering you have three tools. A **single consumer** with sequential processing, or `x-single-active-consumer` so one consumer is active and others stand by for failover; this caps throughput. **Partitioning by key**: hash the ordering key (account ID) to N queues, each with a single active consumer, giving per-key order with N-way parallelism; the consistent hash exchange or super streams do this in the broker. **Sequence control**: the producer stamps a per-key sequence and the consumer applies only the next expected value, parking gaps. Most designs combine partitioning with sequence checks, because retries can still reorder within a partition."],"keyPoints":["the broker hands out messages from a single queue in FIFO order","competing consumers finish at different speeds, so completion order drifts","requeues and delayed retries let later messages overtake earlier ones","use single active consumer, sharding by key or sequence checks"],"examples":[{"title":"Per-account ordering with hashed queues and single active consumer","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass AccountEventPartitions {\n    static final int PARTITIONS = 8;\n\n    @Bean\n    Declarables accountPartitions() {\n        DirectExchange ex = new DirectExchange(\"accounts.events\");\n        List<Declarable> all = new ArrayList<>(List.of(ex));\n        for (int p = 0; p < PARTITIONS; p++) {\n            Queue q = QueueBuilder.durable(\"accounts.events.\" + p)\n                .quorum()\n                .singleActiveConsumer()   // one consumer processes, others wait for failover\n                .build();\n            all.add(q);\n            all.add(BindingBuilder.bind(q).to(ex).with(String.valueOf(p)));\n        }\n        return new Declarables(all);\n    }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass AccountEventPublisher {\n    private final RabbitTemplate rabbit;\n\n    AccountEventPublisher(RabbitTemplate rabbit) {\n        this.rabbit = rabbit;\n    }\n\n    void publish(AccountEvent e) {\n        int partition = Math.floorMod(e.accountId().hashCode(), AccountEventPartitions.PARTITIONS);\n        rabbit.convertAndSend(\"accounts.events\", String.valueOf(partition), e);\n    }\n}","note":"All events for one account land in the same queue with exactly one active consumer, so they are processed in order while 8 accounts' streams run in parallel; the consumer must still process sequentially (one thread per queue)."},{"title":"How two consumers reorder one account","lang":"text","code":"Queue order:  A1 (debit 500)   A2 (credit 200)   A3 (debit 100)\nConsumer X takes A1, slow DB call   (400 ms)\nConsumer Y takes A2, fast path      (20 ms)   -> A2 committed first\nConsumer Y takes A3                 (20 ms)   -> A3 committed second\nConsumer X finishes A1                        -> A1 committed last","note":"The queue delivered in perfect FIFO order, yet the commits happened as A2, A3, A1."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does single active consumer give you, and at what cost?","a":"With the `x-single-active-consumer` queue argument, many consumers can subscribe but only one receives messages at a time, and the broker fails over to the next when it disconnects. You keep FIFO processing plus high availability without custom leader election. Throughput is capped at one consumer per queue, so combine it with sharding by key across several queues when you need parallelism."},{"q":"How does a consistent hash exchange help with ordering?","a":"The `rabbitmq_consistent_hash_exchange` plugin routes each message by a hash of its routing key, or of a header, across the bound queues, weighted by their binding keys. All messages for one key land in the same queue as long as bindings do not change, so one consumer per queue preserves per-key order. Adding or removing queues remaps some keys, so do it while traffic for the affected keys is drained."}],"extra":["**Consistent hash exchange:** the `rabbitmq_consistent_hash_exchange` plugin routes by a hash of the routing key or a header across bound queues, with the binding key acting as weight, so publishers do not need to know the partition count.","**Super streams:** RabbitMQ Streams support partitioned super streams with single active consumer per partition, giving Kafka-like per-key ordering plus replay.","**Priority queues:** with `x-max-priority`, higher-priority messages overtake lower ones, so FIFO holds only within one priority level.","**Publisher retries:** a publisher that republishes an unconfirmed message after later ones were confirmed creates reordering at the source, which only a per-key sequence number lets the consumer detect."],"redFlags":["blames the publisher instead of the consumer topology","enables single active consumer and expects the same throughput","ignores that newer messages may be processed while an earlier one awaits redelivery"]},{"id":203,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"An alert fires because a RabbitMQ queue has grown to several million messages. What would you examine first, and why?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Treat it as a flow problem: compare publish and ack rates, check consumer health, processing latency, redeliveries, prefetch, downstream saturation and oldest message age before you scale consumers or purge anything.","answer":["First establish the shape: is the queue growing, flat or draining, and since when? Compare **publish rate with ack rate**, split depth into ready versus unacked, check **consumer count** and consumer utilisation, and look at the **age of the oldest message** against the business SLA. Correlate the start time with deploys, traffic spikes and downstream incidents.","Then narrow down the cause. No or few consumers: pods crashed, lost connections, or channels closed by the consumer timeout. Consumers present but slow: **processing latency** went up because a **downstream** dependency is saturated (DB locks or pool exhaustion, a provider returning 429). A high redeliver rate on the same message IDs: a **poison message** hot loop. Huge unacked counts on one consumer while others idle: **prefetch** too high. Publish rate jumped: an upstream bug or retry storm. Publishers blocked: a broker memory or disk alarm.","Act on the bottleneck. Scaling consumers only helps if the downstream has headroom; otherwise it deepens the outage. If fresh messages matter most, divert the backlog to a side queue with a shovel and process new traffic first. **Never purge** without impact analysis: the queue may hold payments or refunds, so export or shovel to an archive first and get the owning team's sign-off. Afterward, add alerts on oldest message age and consumer count, not just depth."],"keyPoints":["a backlog is a rate imbalance, so compare inflow with acknowledged throughput first","message age, not depth, tells you whether the business is affected","redeliveries and prefetch settings reveal stuck or poisoned consumers","a saturated downstream caps what extra consumers can drain"],"examples":[{"title":"First commands during the investigation","lang":"bash","code":"# Depth split, consumers and utilisation per queue\nrabbitmqctl list_queues name messages_ready messages_unacknowledged consumers consumer_utilisation\n\n# Who consumes, with which prefetch and ack mode\nrabbitmqctl list_consumers queue_name channel_pid prefetch_count ack_required\n\n# Broker alarms that block publishers or slow the node\nrabbitmq-diagnostics alarms\nrabbitmq-diagnostics memory_breakdown\n\n# Peek at a few messages to spot poison payloads (requeues them, sets redelivered)\nrabbitmqadmin get queue=refunds.requests count=5 ackmode=ack_requeue_true","note":"Rates come from the management UI or Prometheus; these commands answer the structural questions of who is consuming, how much is in flight, and whether the broker itself is under pressure."},{"title":"Symptom to cause to first action","lang":"text","code":"Signal                                    Likely cause                     First action\npublish rate up, ack rate flat            traffic spike or upstream bug    check producer, rate limit\nack rate dropped, consumers = 0           consumers crashed or timed out   pods, logs, consumer_timeout\nack rate dropped, consumers healthy       downstream slow (DB, provider)   latency, pools, 429s; do not scale\nhigh redeliver rate, same message IDs     poison message hot loop          reject to DLQ, fix handler\nhuge unacked on one consumer              prefetch too high                lower prefetch\npublishers blocked, memory or disk alarm  broker resource alarm            free resources, check other queues","note":"Every row except the first is fixed by something other than adding consumers, which is why scaling is the last step, not the first."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you tell a slow consumer from a poison message stuck in redelivery?","a":"A slow consumer still acks steadily, just below the publish rate, and processing time is elevated across many different messages. A poison message shows up as the same message coming back again and again: the redelivered flag is set, `x-delivery-count` climbs on quorum queues, and error logs repeat one message ID with the same exception while that consumer's ack rate is near zero. The first calls for capacity or downstream tuning; the second calls for a delivery limit or a dead-letter exchange so the message is moved aside."},{"q":"Is purging the queue ever acceptable, and what would you do instead?","a":"Only when the messages are known to be worthless or reproducible, such as stale cache invalidations or metric samples that newer data supersedes, and the owners of that data agree. For business events like payments or orders, move them aside instead (shovel them to a parking queue or dead-letter them) so they can be inspected and replayed later. Record what was removed and when, because a purge cannot be undone."}],"extra":["**Little's law for stakeholders:** backlog divided by net drain rate is time to recovery; 3,000,000 messages draining at a net 500 per second clear in about 100 minutes.","**Measure lag at the consumer:** set the AMQP `timestamp` property when publishing and emit now minus timestamp as a processing lag metric, because oldest message age is not exposed uniformly for every queue type.","**Protect the broker:** very long queues cost memory, disk and recovery time; where dropping or refusing is acceptable, set a TTL, or `x-max-length` with `x-overflow` such as `reject-publish`, so one stuck consumer cannot take down the cluster.","**Autoscaling cap:** scaling consumers with KEDA on backlog needs a maximum replica count tied to downstream capacity, or the autoscaler becomes a denial of service against your own database."],"redFlags":["judges severity by message count without asking how old the oldest message is","never checks whether the same messages are being redelivered in a loop","has no view of what the backlog means for customers while it drains"]},{"id":204,"category":"Observability","question":"Checkout errors spike at night. How would metrics, traces and logs each help you get from alert to cause?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Metrics show that something is wrong and how much (cheap trends for alerting), traces show where across services the time or error occurs, and logs explain exactly what happened in one event.","answer":["**Metrics** answer \"is the system healthy and how is it trending?\": numeric time series such as request rate, error ratio, latency histograms, pool usage and GC time. They are cheap to store and query over long periods, so they drive dashboards, SLOs and alerts, but they are aggregated: they tell you the P99 of `POST /payments` jumped at 14:05, not which request suffered or why.","**Logs** answer \"what exactly happened in this event?\": a discrete, detailed record such as a validation failure with its reason, a stack trace or an audit entry. They can carry high-cardinality detail (order IDs, error messages) but are the most expensive signal per event and poor for trend detection, which is why using logs for all monitoring is a red flag. **Traces** answer \"where did the time go across services for this request?\": a tree of spans with timing, stitched together by trace context (W3C `traceparent`) propagated through HTTP and message headers, showing for instance that 1.8 s of a checkout was spent waiting on the fraud service.","They work best **together** as a workflow: an SLO burn alert fires from metrics, an exemplar or a slow-trace query leads to a representative trace, the trace pinpoints the slow span and service, and the trace ID in that service's structured logs gives the precise error. The glue is consistent resource attributes (service, version, environment) and the trace ID on every log line."],"keyPoints":["metrics are cheap aggregates, ideal for alerting and for seeing when something changed","traces follow one request across services to locate the slow or failing hop","logs carry the detailed context of a single event","shared trace IDs let you jump between the three"],"examples":[{"title":"Which signal answers which question","lang":"text","code":"Signal   Question it answers                         Typical content                          Cost\nMetrics  Healthy? How much? Trending where?          rate, errors, latency histogram, pools   cheap, labels must stay bounded\nTraces   Where did the time go across services?      spans, timings, parent/child, attrs      medium, usually sampled\nLogs     What exactly happened here, and why?        structured JSON with traceId, orderId    expensive per event, full detail\n\nWorkflow for \"checkout is slow\":\n1. Metric alert: P99 of POST /api/checkout above 1.5 s, error budget burning\n2. Exemplar on the latency histogram links to trace 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\n3. Trace shows the fraud-service /score span at 1.3 s, retried twice\n4. Logs filtered by that traceId: \"read timeout after 600 ms, retrying\" in FraudClient","note":"Each step narrows the search: metrics find when and where, traces find which hop, logs find why."},{"title":"One Micrometer Observation produces a metric, a span and linked logs","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class CreditDecisionService {\n\n    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(CreditDecisionService.class);\n    private final ObservationRegistry registry;\n    private final DecisionEngine engine;\n\n    public CreditDecisionService(ObservationRegistry registry, DecisionEngine engine) {\n        this.registry = registry;\n        this.engine = engine;\n    }\n\n    public Decision decide(CreditApplication app) {\n        return Observation.createNotStarted(\"credit.decision\", registry)\n                .lowCardinalityKeyValue(\"product\", app.product().name())        // metric tag and span attribute\n                .highCardinalityKeyValue(\"application.id\", app.id().toString()) // span only, never a metric tag\n                .observe(() -> {\n                    Decision decision = engine.evaluate(app);\n                    // traceId and spanId are in the MDC, so this line links to the trace\n                    log.info(\"credit decision outcome={} applicationId={}\", decision.outcome(), app.id());\n                    return decision;\n                });\n    }\n}","note":"With Spring Boot 3 Actuator and Micrometer Tracing on the classpath, this records a `credit.decision` timer tagged only with bounded values, a span carrying the application ID, and a log line that carries the same trace ID."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does an exemplar give you that a plain histogram does not?","a":"An exemplar attaches a sample trace ID to a histogram observation, so a point on a latency graph links directly to a real request that landed in that bucket. Instead of searching traces by time and guessing, you click from the spike straight to a slow trace and then to its logs. Prometheus and OpenMetrics support exemplars, and Micrometer records them when tracing is enabled."},{"q":"Which signal is cheapest to keep for a year, and why?","a":"Metrics, because each series stores one value per scrape regardless of traffic, so cost scales with the number of series rather than with requests. Logs and traces grow with request volume, which is why they are usually sampled, filtered or retained for days to weeks while metrics can be downsampled and kept far longer."}],"extra":["**Exemplars:** Prometheus and OpenMetrics can attach a trace ID to individual histogram samples, so a Grafana latency panel links straight to a representative slow trace; Micrometer's Prometheus registry adds them when tracing is active.","**Averages lie:** record latency as histograms (for example `management.metrics.distribution.percentiles-histogram.http.server.requests=true`) so percentiles can be aggregated across instances; client-side precomputed percentiles cannot be averaged meaningfully.","**Structured logs:** Spring Boot 3.4 added built-in structured JSON logging (`logging.structured.format.console=ecs`, `logstash` or `gelf`), which makes filtering by `traceId` or `orderId` reliable instead of regex parsing.","**Sampling blind spot:** traces are usually sampled, so a rare failure may only exist in logs and metrics; tail sampling that keeps all errors and slow traces closes most of that gap.","**Fourth signal:** continuous profiling (JFR, async-profiler, the emerging OpenTelemetry profiles signal) answers \"which code is burning CPU or allocating\", which none of the other three shows directly."],"redFlags":["expects to find root cause from a dashboard without looking at individual requests","samples traces uniformly so the failing ones are rarely kept","puts user or order IDs into metric labels"]},{"id":205,"category":"Observability","question":"What makes application logs useful in production?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Useful logs are structured events: JSON lines with consistent fields, levels that mean something, and context such as the trace ID and business IDs added through MDC. Each failure is logged once, with its stack trace, where it is handled, and personal data, secrets and needless volume stay out, because logs are an expensive and widely readable data store.","answer":["A log line is useful when someone can find it, trust it and act on it during an incident, and that starts with levels that mean something. ERROR is an operation that failed and was not handled; WARN is unexpected but handled (a fallback, a retry that later succeeded, a rejected partner payload); INFO records business and lifecycle events (order placed, payment captured, application started); DEBUG is diagnostic detail, off in production and enabled per logger when needed. A client sending invalid input is not a server ERROR; if it is, error alerts turn into noise and people stop reading them.","Write JSON lines with one schema across services (timestamp, level, service, version, logger, trace and span IDs, then business fields) so the backend indexes fields instead of grepping text; Spring Boot 3.4 and later does it with `logging.structured.format.console=ecs` (or `logstash`, `gelf`). Put request context into MDC once at the entry point so every line inherits it; tracing instrumentation adds `traceId` and `spanId`, which join a log line to its trace. Log events, not prose: `payment.capture.failed` with `provider` and `orderId` fields can be counted and alerted on, while a sentence can only be regex-searched. Log an exception once, where it is handled, with its stack trace; lower layers wrap it with context and rethrow, or one failure produces five stack traces and the error counts lie.","Logs are also a data store with a cost and a blast radius: shipped, indexed, retained and readable by far more people than the production database. Log IDs rather than emails, names or card numbers, and never tokens, passwords or `Authorization` headers; under GDPR, personal data in logs carries the same retention and erasure duties as anywhere else. Volume is billed per GB and costs CPU, so a forgotten DEBUG logger or one line per item in a large batch can cost more than the service. Use metrics for counting and traces for latency, sample high-volume success events, and write to stdout for the platform to ship."],"keyPoints":["levels with agreed meanings and structured JSON with consistent fields","context through MDC and trace IDs; events, not prose","log each exception once; keep PII, secrets and noise out"],"examples":[{"title":"Prose and duplicate logging vs one structured event per outcome","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: prose, concatenation, personal data, and the failure logged at every layer\nlog.info(\"Capturing payment for \" + customer.email() + \" card \" + request.cardNumber());\ntry {\n    gateway.capture(request);\n} catch (GatewayException e) {\n    log.error(\"Capture failed: \" + e.getMessage());   // no stack trace, no ids\n    throw e;                                           // logged again upstream\n}\n\n// GOOD: an event with fields; request context (traceId, orderId) is already in MDC\nCaptureResult result = gateway.capture(request);\nlog.atInfo().setMessage(\"payment.captured\")\n    .addKeyValue(\"provider\", result.provider())\n    .addKeyValue(\"amountMinor\", request.amountMinor())\n    .addKeyValue(\"latencyMs\", result.latencyMs())\n    .log();\n\n// Lower layers wrap failures with ids and rethrow; one boundary logs them\n@ExceptionHandler(PaymentCaptureException.class)\nProblemDetail captureFailed(PaymentCaptureException e) {\n    log.atError().setMessage(\"payment.capture.failed\")\n        .addKeyValue(\"orderId\", e.orderId())\n        .addKeyValue(\"provider\", e.provider())\n        .setCause(e)                                   // full stack trace and cause chain\n        .log();\n    return ProblemDetail.forStatus(HttpStatus.BAD_GATEWAY);\n}","note":"The exception carries its own IDs because the code that put them into MDC may already have removed them when the advice runs. The trace ID set by the tracing filter is still present, so the event joins its trace."},{"title":"The failure as one indexed event in the log backend","lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-30T09:14:07.312Z\",\n  \"level\": \"ERROR\",\n  \"service\": \"payments-api\",\n  \"version\": \"2026.10.3\",\n  \"message\": \"payment.capture.failed\",\n  \"traceId\": \"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\",\n  \"spanId\": \"00f067aa0ba902b7\",\n  \"orderId\": \"8f6c1e2a-3b0d-4c55-9a51-0d7e2b6f9a10\",\n  \"provider\": \"cardpay\",\n  \"errorType\": \"com.acme.payments.PaymentCaptureException\",\n  \"stackTrace\": \"PaymentCaptureException: capture rejected\\n\\tat ...CaptureService.capture(CaptureService.java:41)\\nCaused by: java.net.http.HttpTimeoutException: request timed out\"\n}","note":"Shown pretty-printed; in production it is one line. Field names depend on the format (ECS, Logstash, GELF), so pick one for every service, or a query like `provider:cardpay` works for one team and not the next."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you get DEBUG logs for one production problem without redeploying?","a":"Change one logger, not the root, at runtime: Spring Boot Actuator's `/actuator/loggers/{name}` accepts a POST with `{\"configuredLevel\":\"DEBUG\"}` and applies it to that instance until reset or restart, so secure the endpoint. Narrower still, a Logback `DynamicThresholdFilter` enables DEBUG only for requests whose MDC carries a marker, such as one customer's ID. Time-box it; the change applies per pod."},{"q":"How do you keep personal data and secrets out of logs?","a":"At the source: log identifiers instead of values, never dump whole request or response bodies or headers, and watch `toString`, because a record's generated `toString` prints every component, emails and tokens included. Masking in the encoder or the log collector is a backstop, not the plan. Restrict access and retention on log indexes, and treat a leak as a data incident: purge, rotate exposed secrets and fix the code path."}],"extra":["**Trailing throwable:** SLF4J treats a final `Throwable` argument as the exception even with `{}` placeholders, so `log.error(\"capture failed orderId={}\", orderId, e)` keeps the stack trace; logging only `e.getMessage()` loses the cause chain.","**Cheap when disabled:** placeholders skip formatting when the level is off, but arguments are still evaluated; pass a supplier to the fluent API or guard expensive ones with `isDebugEnabled()`.","**Async appender trade-off:** Logback's `AsyncAppender` discards TRACE, DEBUG and INFO events once its queue is 80% full and blocks callers when it is full; `neverBlock=true` drops instead of blocking.","**One event per stack trace:** line-based shippers split a plain-text stack trace into many events; JSON keeps it in one field.","**MDC hygiene:** MDC is thread-local, so remove what you put or a pooled thread carries one request's IDs into the next; it does not follow work to another executor unless context propagation copies it."],"redFlags":["Logs free text with string concatenation and no consistent fields","logs and rethrows at every layer, or logs `e.getMessage()` without the stack trace","writes request bodies, tokens or personal data to logs"]},{"id":206,"category":"Observability","question":"One order passes through HTTP, Kafka and a nightly batch. Why is a trace ID alone not enough to connect it all?","level":"Basic","tldr":"A correlation ID is a key the application defines that ties related work together across many traces and possibly days; a trace ID covers one end-to-end request and each span ID one step inside it; both must be carried across asynchronous hops.","answer":["A **correlation ID** is a grouping key that the application defines: an `X-Correlation-Id` sent by a client, a loan application or order reference, or a support reference that ties together a checkout request, the Kafka events it emits, the settlement batch that runs hours later and the customer's retry the next day. It can outlive any single request, often spans several traces, and is frequently shown to customers or support staff.","A **trace ID** (16 bytes, 32 hex characters in W3C Trace Context) identifies one distributed trace: the causal tree of work triggered by one entry point. Every operation in that tree has its own **span ID** (8 bytes) plus a parent span ID, which is how the backend rebuilds timing and call hierarchy. The tracing SDK creates the trace ID once at the root and every downstream service continues it from the incoming `traceparent` header; generating a fresh trace ID in each service breaks the trace into disconnected fragments.","Both kinds of context must cross **async boundaries**: inject them into Kafka record headers or AMQP message properties when publishing, extract them when consuming, and carry them across thread pools. In Spring Boot 3 the simplest approach is to carry the correlation ID as tracing **baggage**, so it travels with the trace context and lands in the MDC. Keep identifiers opaque: never use an email, national ID or account number as a correlation ID, because it gets logged everywhere and forwarded to downstream parties."],"keyPoints":["trace context is created and carried by tracing libraries using W3C `traceparent`","a correlation ID is owned by the application and can outlive any single trace","batch jobs, retries and later replays usually start new traces but can keep the same correlation key","both belong in message headers and in the MDC so logs can be searched by them"],"examples":[{"title":"Servlet filter that accepts, validates and echoes a correlation ID","lang":"java","code":"@Component\n@Order(Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE)\npublic class CorrelationIdFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {\n\n    static final String HEADER = \"X-Correlation-Id\";\n    private static final Pattern SAFE = Pattern.compile(\"[A-Za-z0-9-]{8,64}\");\n\n    @Override\n    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response,\n                                    FilterChain chain) throws ServletException, IOException {\n        String incoming = request.getHeader(HEADER);\n        // Accept only short opaque values: prevents log injection and PII smuggling\n        String id = (incoming != null && SAFE.matcher(incoming).matches())\n                ? incoming\n                : UUID.randomUUID().toString();\n        MDC.put(\"correlationId\", id);\n        response.setHeader(HEADER, id);\n        try {\n            chain.doFilter(request, response);\n        } finally {\n            MDC.remove(\"correlationId\");\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The correlation ID is independent of the trace ID: a client can reuse it across retries and later calls, while each request still gets its own trace. Echoing it back lets customers and support quote it."},{"title":"Propagating trace context and a correlation field through HTTP, Kafka and RabbitMQ","lang":"yaml","code":"management:\n  tracing:\n    sampling:\n      probability: 0.1\n    baggage:\n      remote-fields: x-correlation-id     # sent to downstream services with the trace context\n      correlation:\n        fields: x-correlation-id          # copied into the MDC for every log line\nspring:\n  kafka:\n    template:\n      observation-enabled: true           # KafkaTemplate writes trace headers\n    listener:\n      observation-enabled: true           # @KafkaListener continues the producer's trace\n  rabbitmq:\n    template:\n      observation-enabled: true\n    listener:\n      simple:\n        observation-enabled: true","note":"With Micrometer Tracing, `remote-fields` makes the value travel as baggage next to `traceparent`, and the observation flags make Spring Kafka and Spring AMQP inject and extract context in message headers."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Should a service accept a correlation ID sent by an external client?","a":"It can, provided it is validated: restrict length and characters so it cannot inject into logs or headers, and generate a fresh one when it is missing or invalid. For trace context from untrusted callers, many teams start a new trace at the edge and add a link to the incoming one instead of continuing it, so outside clients cannot force sampling decisions or pollute traces."},{"q":"How do you make the correlation ID useful to support staff?","a":"Return it in a response header such as `X-Correlation-Id` and include it in error bodies, so a customer or support ticket can quote it. Keep it in the MDC so every log line carries it, and store it with the business record (order, payment) when the flow spans days."}],"extra":["**W3C format:** `traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01` holds version, trace ID, parent span ID and flags (the sampled bit); `tracestate` carries vendor data and the separate `baggage` header carries your key/value pairs.","**Span links for batches:** when one consumer processes a batch of messages from many producers, OpenTelemetry messaging conventions recommend a consumer span with **links** to each producer context rather than arbitrarily choosing one parent.","**Edge trust boundary:** at public ingress decide whether to honor an incoming `traceparent`; a client that sets the sampled flag on every request can inflate tracing cost, so many teams start a new trace at the edge and keep the client's value only as an attribute.","**Baggage leaks:** baggage is sent on every outbound call, including to third-party APIs, so allowlist the fields, keep values small and strip them at egress.","**Give it back to callers:** include the trace or correlation ID in error responses (for example as a property on a `ProblemDetail`) so a customer complaint maps straight to the exact trace and logs."],"redFlags":["uses the correlation ID as a substitute for distributed tracing","drops context at thread pool or message boundaries, so logs stop matching","trusts a client-supplied correlation header without checking its format or length"]},{"id":207,"category":"Observability","question":"Sales promises customers 99% availability. How should engineering set its own targets and measurements underneath that promise?","level":"Basic","tldr":"An SLI is a measured ratio of good to valid events, an SLO is the internal target for it over a window, and an SLA is a looser external contract with financial or legal consequences.","answer":["An **SLI** (service level indicator) is a precisely defined measurement of what users experience, best expressed as good events divided by valid events: the proportion of `POST /credit-decisions` requests that return a non-5xx response within 800 ms, measured at the gateway. An **SLO** (service level objective) is the internal target for that SLI over a window, such as 99.5% over a rolling 28 days. The remainder is the **error budget** (0.5%, about 3.4 hours of full outage at uniform traffic), which the team spends on releases and experiments and protects with an agreed error budget policy.","An **SLA** (service level agreement) is an external commitment in a contract, with consequences such as service credits or penalties. It is owned by business and legal, and it must be looser than the SLO so that you are warned, and can react, long before a contract is breached.","Indicators and windows must be **user-centric**. Plain uptime or \"the pod is running\" misses a service that is up but returning errors or taking 10 s, and it misses partial outages affecting one region or product. Measure as close to the user as practical (load balancer or client), exclude invalid events such as client validation errors, define SLOs per critical user journey, and use a rolling window that matches how users perceive reliability rather than a calendar month that resets the pain on the first."],"keyPoints":["the indicator is a ratio of good events to valid events, measured close to the user","the internal objective sits tighter than the contract to leave a safety margin","the contract carries credits or penalties, so legal and finance care about it","the room between the objective and perfection is the error budget"],"examples":[{"title":"The three terms for one credit decision API","lang":"text","code":"Term  Owner               Example                                                   When missed\nSLI   Engineering         good  = POST /credit-decisions, non-5xx, under 800 ms     (just a measurement)\n                          valid = all requests except 4xx validation rejects\nSLO   Engineering+Product 99.5% of valid requests are good, rolling 28 days         error budget policy:\n                                                                                    freeze risky releases\nSLA   Business, Legal     99.0% monthly availability in the partner contract        service credits paid\n\nError budget: 0.5% of valid requests per 28 days\nAt 2,000,000 requests per 28 days that is 10,000 bad requests","note":"The SLA is deliberately looser than the SLO, and the SLI is a ratio of events rather than server uptime."},{"title":"Prometheus recording rules for a request-based latency SLI","lang":"yaml","code":"# Needs a histogram bucket exactly at 800 ms, for example:\n# management.metrics.distribution.slo.http.server.requests=800ms\ngroups:\n  - name: credit-decision-sli\n    rules:\n      - record: credit_decision:good:rate5m\n        expr: |\n          sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_bucket{uri=\"/credit-decisions\", method=\"POST\",\n                   outcome=\"SUCCESS\", le=\"0.8\"}[5m]))\n      - record: credit_decision:valid:rate5m\n        expr: |\n          sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{uri=\"/credit-decisions\", method=\"POST\",\n                   outcome!=\"CLIENT_ERROR\"}[5m]))\n      - record: credit_decision:sli:ratio_28d\n        expr: |\n          sum_over_time(credit_decision:good:rate5m[28d])\n          /\n          sum_over_time(credit_decision:valid:rate5m[28d])","note":"Good and valid rates are recorded separately and summed over the window before dividing, which weights the SLI by traffic instead of averaging ratios."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens when a service has used up its error budget for the window?","a":"You follow the policy agreed in advance: typically freeze risky feature releases and spend the engineering time on reliability work tied to the incidents that burned the budget, while fixes that improve reliability can still ship. The budget turns the reliability versus velocity debate into a data-driven decision, so the policy must be written down and accepted by product before it is needed."}],"extra":["**Error budget policy:** agree in advance what happens when the budget is exhausted (release freeze, reliability work first, stricter change review); without a policy an SLO is just a dashboard.","**Dependency math:** a service that synchronously needs three dependencies at 99.9% each cannot credibly promise more than about 99.7% itself, which is why SLOs drive timeouts, fallbacks and asynchronous design.","**Not 100%:** a 100% target means no releases and no risk, and users cannot tell 99.99% from 100% when their own mobile network is less reliable than that.","**Thresholds, not averages:** latency SLIs are \"proportion faster than X\", possibly with two thresholds (90% under 300 ms and 99% under 1 s), which maps directly to histogram buckets and to error budget burn.","**Low traffic:** with a few hundred requests a day one failure moves the SLI a lot, so combine request-based SLIs with synthetic probes or longer windows."],"redFlags":["sets the SLO equal to the SLA, leaving no margin before penalties apply","measures only inside the application, missing load balancer and network failures","aims for 100% and so has no budget to spend on change"]},{"id":208,"category":"Observability","question":"Your company wants to switch observability vendors without re-instrumenting fifty services. How does OpenTelemetry make that realistic?","level":"Basic","tldr":"OpenTelemetry offers one open standard (API, SDK, the OTLP protocol and shared semantic conventions) for traces, logs and metrics, with W3C context propagation built in, and its Collector lets you switch or combine backends without touching service code.","answer":["OpenTelemetry (OTel) standardizes the **instrumentation layer**: an API and SDK per language, the OTLP protocol, and W3C Trace Context plus Baggage for **context propagation**, covering traces, metrics and logs. Instrument once and the same data can flow to Tempo, Jaeger, Prometheus, Datadog, Honeycomb or Elastic, so switching vendors or running two in parallel does not touch application code. For Java you get the **Java agent** for zero-code instrumentation of HTTP servers and clients, JDBC, Kafka and more, or Spring Boot 3 with Micrometer Tracing's OTel bridge and an OTLP exporter.","The **Collector** is the other half of the value: a separate process built from receivers, processors and exporters. Services send OTLP to a node-local or gateway collector, which batches, retries, enriches with Kubernetes metadata, redacts PII, applies tail sampling and exports to one or more backends. Backend credentials, routing and cost controls then live in collector configuration owned by the platform team, not in every service.","It is not magic, though. Auto-instrumentation gives transport-level spans, but useful telemetry also needs business spans and attributes (decision outcome, product), consistent naming through **semantic conventions** (`http.request.method`, `http.route`, `http.response.status_code`) so dashboards work across teams and languages, a deliberate **sampling plan** so cost stays predictable, and care with cardinality and sensitive data. Without that you get expensive, inconsistent telemetry nobody can query."],"keyPoints":["services emit telemetry through one API, so vendor SDKs disappear from application code","with OTLP and the Collector, data can go to any backend, or to several at once","shared attribute names make dashboards and queries portable across languages","a single propagation format keeps traces intact across teams and stacks"],"examples":[{"title":"Zero-code instrumentation with the OpenTelemetry Java agent","lang":"bash","code":"java -javaagent:/opt/otel/opentelemetry-javaagent.jar \\\n  -Dotel.service.name=credit-decision-service \\\n  -Dotel.resource.attributes=service.namespace=lending,service.version=1.42.0 \\\n  -Dotel.exporter.otlp.endpoint=http://otel-collector:4318 \\\n  -Dotel.exporter.otlp.protocol=http/protobuf \\\n  -Dotel.traces.sampler=parentbased_traceidratio \\\n  -Dotel.traces.sampler.arg=0.25 \\\n  -jar credit-decision-service.jar","note":"The application only knows the collector address; the parent-based sampler honors the upstream sampling decision so traces are never cut in half."},{"title":"Collector gateway with memory limiter and tail sampling","lang":"yaml","code":"receivers:\n  otlp:\n    protocols:\n      grpc:\n        endpoint: 0.0.0.0:4317\n      http:\n        endpoint: 0.0.0.0:4318\nprocessors:\n  memory_limiter:\n    check_interval: 1s\n    limit_percentage: 80\n    spike_limit_percentage: 20\n  tail_sampling:\n    decision_wait: 10s\n    policies:\n      - name: keep-errors\n        type: status_code\n        status_code: { status_codes: [ERROR] }\n      - name: keep-slow\n        type: latency\n        latency: { threshold_ms: 1000 }\n      - name: baseline\n        type: probabilistic\n        probabilistic: { sampling_percentage: 5 }\n  batch: {}\nexporters:\n  otlp:\n    endpoint: tempo.observability:4317\n    tls: { insecure: true }\nservice:\n  pipelines:\n    traces:\n      receivers: [otlp]\n      processors: [memory_limiter, tail_sampling, batch]\n      exporters: [otlp]","note":"Swapping Tempo for another backend is a change to the exporters block only. `tail_sampling` ships in the contrib distribution and keeps every error and slow trace plus 5% of the rest."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does the Collector do besides forwarding data?","a":"It runs pipelines of receivers, processors and exporters: processors can batch, retry, drop health-check spans, redact or hash sensitive attributes, add Kubernetes metadata and enforce memory limits before anything leaves your network. It can also translate formats, receiving Jaeger or Prometheus data and exporting OTLP, which helps during migrations. Running it as a node agent plus a central gateway keeps that logic out of the services."},{"q":"How does the Java agent compare with instrumenting code by hand?","a":"The agent instruments known libraries at load time (HTTP servers and clients, JDBC, Kafka, Redis) and produces spans and metrics with no code changes. It cannot know your business operations, so manual spans and attributes through the OpenTelemetry API, or `@WithSpan`, are still needed for things like decision steps or a tenant tier. Most teams start with the agent and add manual spans where investigations keep hitting blind spots."}],"extra":["**Ports and protocols:** OTLP uses 4317 for gRPC and 4318 for HTTP/protobuf; the 2.x Java agent defaults to HTTP/protobuf, a common cause of \"no data\" when the collector only listens on gRPC.","**Deployment pattern:** run collectors as a DaemonSet or sidecar for batching and Kubernetes metadata (`k8sattributes` processor), plus a gateway tier for tail sampling and export; because tail sampling needs all spans of a trace on one instance, the first tier routes by trace ID with the `loadbalancing` exporter.","**Metrics before sampling:** derive RED metrics from all spans (the `spanmetrics` connector) or from application metrics before tail sampling drops traces, otherwise latency numbers are biased toward the traces you kept.","**Semantic convention migrations:** HTTP attribute names changed when the conventions stabilized (`http.method` became `http.request.method`); during the transition `OTEL_SEMCONV_STABILITY_OPT_IN=http/dup` let instrumentation emit both so dashboards could migrate.","**Memory limiter first:** put `memory_limiter` at the start of every pipeline so a traffic spike makes the collector refuse data (and senders retry) instead of being OOMKilled and losing everything in flight."],"redFlags":["believes OpenTelemetry is itself a storage or visualization backend","sends telemetry from every pod straight to the vendor with no Collector to batch, filter or redact","mixes vendor-specific propagation headers so traces break at team boundaries"]},{"id":209,"category":"Observability","question":"Your on-call engineers get forty pages a week and ignore most of them. How would you redesign the alerting?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Page only on user-facing symptoms such as SLO burn rate, give every alert a severity, an owning team and a runbook, suppress duplicate downstream alerts, and tune windows so each page is urgent, real and actionable.","answer":["An actionable alert means a human must do something now and knows what to do. So page on **user impact**: error ratio or latency SLO burn rate, failing checkouts, consumer lag measured in time. Causes such as high CPU, a restarted pod or a long GC are not pages by themselves: CPU at 90% while the SLO is healthy just means the system is busy doing its job. Put causes on dashboards or low severity tickets, where they help diagnosis without waking anyone.","Every alert needs metadata: a **severity** that maps to a response (page now vs ticket for the next business day), an **owner** label that routes it to the team able to fix it, and a **runbook** link covering impact, first diagnostic steps, safe mitigations and escalation. The summary should state what users experience (\"card payments failing for 4% of requests\"), not only which metric crossed a line.","Then control noise: group related alerts, use **inhibition** so a database outage does not also fire twenty dependent service alerts, and avoid alerting on several symptoms of the same failure. **Tune windows** with `for:` durations and multi-window burn rates so short blips stay quiet but real incidents are caught quickly. Treat alerts as code: review every page after the on-call shift, delete or demote alerts nobody acted on, and unit test rules with `promtool test rules`."],"keyPoints":["page on symptoms users feel, such as error budget burn, not on internal causes","every page needs a named owner, a severity and a runbook link","group related alerts so one failure produces one notification","anything that can wait until morning becomes a ticket, not a page"],"examples":[{"title":"Multi-window burn-rate alert with owner and runbook","lang":"yaml","code":"# SLO 99.9% over 30 days, so the allowed error ratio is 0.001\ngroups:\n  - name: payments-api-slo-alerts\n    rules:\n      - alert: PaymentsApiErrorBudgetFastBurn\n        # 14.4x burn spends 2% of the monthly budget in one hour\n        expr: |\n          job:http_error_ratio:rate1h{job=\"payments-api\"} > (14.4 * 0.001)\n          and\n          job:http_error_ratio:rate5m{job=\"payments-api\"} > (14.4 * 0.001)\n        labels:\n          severity: page\n          team: payments\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"Payments API failing {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of requests\"\n          runbook_url: \"https://runbooks.internal/payments-api/error-budget-burn\"\n          dashboard: \"https://grafana.internal/d/payments-api-slo\"\n      - alert: PaymentsApiErrorBudgetSlowBurn\n        # 6x burn over 6 hours spends 5% of the budget\n        expr: |\n          job:http_error_ratio:rate6h{job=\"payments-api\"} > (6 * 0.001)\n          and\n          job:http_error_ratio:rate30m{job=\"payments-api\"} > (6 * 0.001)\n        labels:\n          severity: page\n          team: payments\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"Payments API burning its error budget 6x faster than sustainable\"\n          runbook_url: \"https://runbooks.internal/payments-api/error-budget-burn\"","note":"The `job:http_error_ratio:*` series are recording rules computing errors divided by requests over each window. The long window proves significance, the short window makes the alert resolve quickly once the incident is over."},{"title":"Alertmanager routing and inhibition to avoid duplicate pages","lang":"yaml","code":"route:\n  receiver: team-tickets\n  group_by: [alertname, service, cluster]\n  group_wait: 30s\n  group_interval: 5m\n  repeat_interval: 4h\n  routes:\n    - matchers: ['severity=\"page\"', 'team=\"payments\"']\n      receiver: payments-oncall\n    - matchers: ['severity=\"ticket\"']\n      receiver: team-tickets\ninhibit_rules:\n  # The database is down: do not also page for every payments service that depends on it\n  - source_matchers: ['alertname=\"PaymentsDbUnavailable\"']\n    target_matchers: ['team=\"payments\"', 'severity=\"page\"']\n    equal: [cluster]","note":"Routing uses the owner and severity labels from the rule, and inhibition keeps a single root-cause page instead of a storm of symptom pages."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you measure whether your alerting is getting better?","a":"Track pages per on-call shift, the share of pages that led to real action, time to acknowledge, and how many incidents customers reported before any alert fired. Review every page in a weekly on-call handover and delete or downgrade the ones that were not actionable. A falling page count with no rise in customer-detected incidents is the signal you want."},{"q":"Why is high CPU usually a poor paging condition?","a":"High CPU is a cause, not a symptom: a service can run at 90% CPU and still meet its latency objective, or fail users at 30% because a dependency is slow. Paging on it wakes people for non-problems and still misses real ones. Keep it on dashboards and as a ticket-level capacity alert, and page on latency and error objectives instead."}],"extra":["**Burn rate math:** burn rate is the observed error ratio divided by the allowed one (1 minus the SLO); at 14.4x a 30 day budget is gone in about two days, which justifies a page, while 1x only warrants a ticket.","**Dead man's switch:** keep an always-firing `Watchdog` alert routed to an external heartbeat service so you learn when Prometheus or Alertmanager itself is broken, and use `absent()` for metrics that silently disappear.","**Low traffic trap:** at 50 requests an hour a single failure is a 2% error ratio, so require a minimum request count in the expression or rely on synthetic checks for quiet services.","**Measure the alerting:** track pages per shift, time to acknowledge and the share of pages that led to action; the Google SRE book argues for at most about two incidents per 12 hour on-call shift.","**Runbook content:** what the alert means for users, the dashboard, likely causes ranked by frequency, safe mitigations (rollback, feature flag, scale out), how to verify recovery and when to escalate; a runbook that only says \"investigate\" is not a runbook."],"redFlags":["adds alerts after each incident but never deletes any","relies on static thresholds that fire on every brief blip","judges alerting by how many alerts exist rather than how often pages lead to action"]},{"id":210,"category":"Observability","question":"After someone adds `customerId` as a label on a request counter, Prometheus memory climbs steadily. What is going on?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Unbounded label values (user ID, order ID, raw URLs) multiply time series until the backend runs out of memory, slows down or gets very expensive; keep labels bounded and put unique IDs in logs and traces.","answer":["In Prometheus style systems every unique combination of metric name and label values is a separate **time series** with its own index entry and in-memory head chunk. Cardinality is the product of distinct values per label: `http_server_requests_seconds_bucket` with 20 routes, 5 methods, 8 status codes and 30 buckets across 50 pods can already reach 1.2 million series. Add a `customerId` label with 100,000 customers and growth becomes unbounded.","The consequences are operational, not cosmetic: Prometheus memory grows until it is OOMKilled, ingestion and compaction slow down, dashboards and alert queries time out or return partial data, and SaaS vendors that bill per series send a surprising invoice. It hurts the application as well, because Micrometer keeps a meter object per tag combination, so an unbounded tag is a memory leak inside your service too.","The rule is **bounded dimensions only** on metrics: method, route template, status or status class, outcome, exception type, region, a small enum of products or payment methods. Unique identifiers (user, order, request, trace) belong in structured logs and span attributes, and **exemplars** can link a metric sample to a specific trace. The classic trap is tagging with a raw path such as `/orders/98231` instead of the template `/orders/{id}`, usually from a custom filter or hand-built meter."],"keyPoints":["every unique combination of label values becomes its own stored series","series count is the product of each label's distinct values, so one unbounded label dominates","the result is memory pressure, slow queries and a large monitoring bill","per-entity detail belongs in logs, traces or exemplars"],"examples":[{"title":"Bad vs good Micrometer tags, plus a safety net","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: one series per customer and per order, grows forever\nCounter.builder(\"payments.processed\")\n        .tag(\"customerId\", payment.customerId().toString())\n        .tag(\"orderId\", payment.orderId().toString())\n        .register(registry)\n        .increment();\n\n// BAD: raw path as a tag, so /orders/98231 becomes its own series\nTimer.builder(\"orders.api\").tag(\"path\", request.getRequestURI()).register(registry);\n\n// GOOD: bounded tags on the metric, identifiers in the log line and the span\nCounter.builder(\"payments.processed\")\n        .tag(\"method\", payment.method().name())    // CARD, SEPA, SWISH\n        .tag(\"outcome\", result.outcome().name())    // APPROVED, DECLINED, ERROR\n        .register(registry)\n        .increment();\nlog.info(\"payment processed orderId={} customerId={}\", payment.orderId(), payment.customerId());\n\n// GOOD: route template instead of the raw path\nString route = (String) request.getAttribute(HandlerMapping.BEST_MATCHING_PATTERN_ATTRIBUTE);\n\n// Safety net: stop registering new values beyond a cap instead of hurting Prometheus\n@Bean\nMeterFilter paymentMethodCap() {\n    return MeterFilter.maximumAllowableTags(\"payments.processed\", \"method\", 20, MeterFilter.deny());\n}","note":"Every tag value you add multiplies series; the `MeterFilter` turns a future bug into dropped meters instead of an outage of the monitoring system."},{"title":"Finding the offending metric and label in Prometheus","lang":"bash","code":"# Top metrics and labels by series count in the TSDB head block\ncurl -s http://prometheus:9090/api/v1/status/tsdb \\\n  | jq '.data.seriesCountByMetricName[:5], .data.labelValueCountByLabelName[:5]'\n\n# Typical output that points at the culprit:\n# {\"name\": \"payments_processed_total\", \"value\": 412877}\n# {\"name\": \"orderId\", \"value\": 398112}\n\n# Watch total head series over time; alert on sudden growth\ncurl -s 'http://prometheus:9090/api/v1/query?query=prometheus_tsdb_head_series' \\\n  | jq '.data.result[0].value[1]'","note":"The TSDB status endpoint shows which metric names and label names own the most series, which usually identifies the bad deploy within minutes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you find which metric is causing the growth?","a":"Start with the TSDB status: the Prometheus UI's TSDB status page and `/api/v1/status/tsdb` list the metric names and label names with the most series and distinct values. Then count distinct values per label for the suspect metric, for example `count(count by (customerId) (http_requests_total))`, and line up the rise of `prometheus_tsdb_head_series` with recent deploys."},{"q":"How do you protect a metrics backend from the next bad label?","a":"Set per-scrape `sample_limit` and `label_limit` in Prometheus so one target cannot flood it, and drop or rewrite offending labels with `metric_relabel_configs`. On the application side, a Micrometer `MeterFilter` such as `maximumAllowableTags` can cap the number of values for a tag. Review new metrics in code review with an estimated series count."}],"extra":["**Histograms multiply:** each label combination on a histogram costs one series per bucket plus `_sum` and `_count`, so enabling percentile histograms on a meter with a moderately wide tag set can dominate your series count.","**Churn is cardinality too:** labels like `pod` or `container_id` change on every deploy, so frequent rollouts create many short-lived series that still occupy the head block and index for hours.","**Scrape guards:** `sample_limit`, `label_limit` and `label_value_length_limit` in a Prometheus scrape config fail a misbehaving target's scrape instead of letting it exhaust the server.","**Framework defaults help:** Spring MVC server observations tag `uri` with the matched route pattern, and Spring Framework 6 client observations use the URI template (falling back to `none`), so explosions usually come from custom meters rather than the framework.","**Tenant IDs:** a label with tens of tenants is fine and often essential; with thousands it is not, so aggregate by plan or tier and do per-tenant analytics in logs or a columnar store."],"redFlags":["labels HTTP metrics with the raw request path instead of the route template","assumes the backend will simply compress the extra series away","cannot estimate how many series a new metric will produce"]},{"id":211,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"Container vs virtual machine, and image vs container: what is the difference?","level":"Basic","tldr":"A virtual machine runs its own guest kernel on virtual hardware from a hypervisor; a container is an ordinary process on the host kernel, isolated with namespaces and limited with cgroups. An image is the immutable, layered template, and a container is a running instance of it with a thin writable layer that disappears when the container is removed.","answer":["A hypervisor (KVM, ESXi, Hyper-V) virtualizes hardware, and each virtual machine boots a full guest operating system with its own kernel. A container has no kernel of its own: it is a process tree on the host kernel that gets a restricted view of the system through namespaces (its own PID numbering, network stack, mounts, hostname, IPC and user IDs) and resource caps through cgroups (CPU, memory, PIDs, I/O). Capabilities and seccomp narrow which syscalls and privileges it may use, and an overlay filesystem assembles its root filesystem from image layers. It is a process with a restricted view, not a small VM.","The trade-offs follow from the shared kernel. Containers start in milliseconds, cost megabytes instead of gigabytes and pack densely, but the isolation boundary is the kernel's syscall surface: a kernel vulnerability or a privileged, misconfigured container can reach the host and its neighbors. VMs give a hardware-enforced boundary at more overhead, so untrusted multi-tenant code gets a stronger boundary: plain VMs, microVM runtimes such as Kata Containers or Firecracker, or gVisor's user-space kernel. Kubernetes nodes are usually VMs, so most setups use both. For Java the shared kernel is visible: the JVM reads its cgroup limits to size the heap and count CPUs, which is why older, container-unaware JVMs sized themselves for the whole host and were OOMKilled.","An image is an ordered stack of read-only, content-addressed layers plus a config (entrypoint, environment, user), described by an OCI manifest and identified by a `sha256` digest. A container is one instance of it: the runtime mounts the layers read-only, adds a copy-on-write writable layer, applies namespaces and cgroups and starts the process. Ten containers from one image share its layers but each has its own writable layer, lost on removal, so state belongs in volumes or external stores, and a fix means a new image, never patching a running container. Tags are mutable pointers (even `1.4.2` can be re-pushed) while digests are not, so production deploys a unique tag per build or the digest itself: what you tested is exactly what runs and what you roll back to."],"keyPoints":["VMs virtualize hardware and run their own kernel; containers share the host kernel","namespaces isolate what a process sees, cgroups limit what it uses","an image is an immutable template; a container is an instance with a writable layer"],"examples":[{"title":"A container is a host process with its own writable layer","lang":"bash","code":"# Two containers from one image: shared read-only layers, separate writable layers\ndocker run -d --name orders-a --memory 512m registry.acme.io/orders-api:1.4.2\ndocker run -d --name orders-b --memory 512m registry.acme.io/orders-api:1.4.2\n\n# It is an ordinary host process in its own namespaces, with no kernel of its own\nPID=$(docker inspect --format '{{.State.Pid}}' orders-a)\nps -o pid,user,cmd -p \"$PID\"                        # the java process, seen from the host\nsudo readlink /proc/\"$PID\"/ns/pid /proc/1/ns/pid    # two different pid namespaces\ndocker exec orders-a uname -r                       # same kernel version as the host\n\n# The JVM sizes itself from the cgroup limit, not from host RAM\ndocker exec orders-a java -XshowSettings:system -version   # Memory Limit: 512.00M\n\n# Writes land in that container's writable layer only\ndocker exec orders-a sh -c 'echo debug > /tmp/marker'\ndocker diff orders-a                                # includes A /tmp/marker\ndocker exec orders-b ls /tmp/marker                 # No such file or directory\ndocker rm -f orders-a                               # the layer and the file are gone","note":"Nothing here boots an operating system. `-XshowSettings:system` prints the cgroup limits the JVM detected (Linux only), the quickest way to confirm heap and CPU ergonomics inside a container."},{"title":"Deploying by digest instead of a mutable tag","lang":"yaml","code":"# Pod template spec fragment of a Deployment\nspec:\n  containers:\n    - name: app\n      # BAD: a re-pushed 1.4.2 keeps running in its old form on nodes that cached it\n      # (pull policy IfNotPresent) while new nodes pull the new one\n      # image: registry.acme.io/orders-api:1.4.2\n\n      # BAD: re-applying an unchanged manifest triggers no rollout at all\n      # image: registry.acme.io/orders-api:latest\n\n      # GOOD: readable tag for humans, digest for the runtime (the digest wins)\n      image: registry.acme.io/orders-api:1.4.2-9f3c2ab@sha256:6c1f0e9d4b7a2c3e8f5d1a0b9c7e6d5f4a3b2c1d0e9f8a7b6c5d4e3f2a1b0c9d\n      securityContext:\n        runAsNonRoot: true\n        readOnlyRootFilesystem: true   # the writable layer is not a data store\n      volumeMounts:\n        - { name: tmp, mountPath: /tmp }\n  volumes:\n    - { name: tmp, emptyDir: {} }","note":"CI resolves the digest after the push and writes it into the manifest. A read-only root filesystem makes reliance on the writable layer fail in testing, not lose data in production."}],"followUps":[{"q":"If containers share the kernel, how do you make them reasonably safe?","a":"Shrink what the process can do: run as non-root, drop all capabilities and add back only what is needed, use the `RuntimeDefault` seccomp profile, forbid privileged mode and host namespaces, and mount the root filesystem read-only; Pod Security Admission's `restricted` profile enforces most of this. Keep images minimal and patched, and put untrusted code behind a VM or a sandboxed runtime."},{"q":"An image built on a developer's Mac fails on the cluster with exec format error. Why?","a":"Containers run directly on the host CPU, so an image built on Apple silicon is `arm64` and cannot execute on `amd64` nodes without emulation. Build in CI for the target platform, or publish a multi-platform image with `docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64`, whose image index lets each node pull the variant for its architecture."}],"extra":["**Docker is not the Kubernetes runtime:** Kubernetes removed dockershim in 1.24 and nodes run containerd or CRI-O; images built with Docker are standard OCI images and run unchanged.","**Layer sharing:** replicas on one node share read-only layers on disk, and a common base image shortens pulls.","**Copy-on-write cost:** changing a file from an image layer first copies the whole file into the writable layer, so write-heavy paths belong on volumes.","**Fast container, slow JVM:** the container starts in milliseconds, but class loading, JIT warm-up and Spring startup still take seconds; class data sharing or native images address that part.","**Stopped is not removed:** an exited container keeps its writable layer until removal, so `docker cp` can still recover a heap dump from it."],"redFlags":["Calls a container a lightweight VM with its own kernel","patches running containers by hand instead of rebuilding the image","deploys `latest` or other mutable tags to production"]},{"id":212,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"What do a Pod, Deployment, Service and Ingress each do in Kubernetes?","level":"Basic","tldr":"A Pod runs one or more tightly coupled containers that share an IP and volumes; a Deployment keeps a desired number of identical Pods running through ReplicaSets and rolls out changes. A Service gives a changing set of Pods a stable virtual IP and DNS name, and an Ingress routes external HTTP by host and path to Services through an ingress controller.","answer":["A Pod is the smallest unit Kubernetes schedules: containers that run together on one node, share a network namespace (one IP, `localhost` between them) and can share volumes, usually one application container plus optional init or sidecar containers. Pods are disposable: a replacement gets a new name and IP, so nothing should address one directly. A Deployment declares the desired state of a stateless app (pod template, replica count, update strategy) and delegates: each version of the template gets a ReplicaSet, which keeps exactly N matching pods alive. Changing the template starts a rollout that scales the new ReplicaSet up and the old one down within `maxSurge` and `maxUnavailable`; the old one stays at zero replicas, which is what `kubectl rollout undo` returns to.","A Service solves the moving-IP problem. It selects pods by label and gives them one stable virtual IP and DNS name (`orders-api.orders.svc.cluster.local`). The control plane keeps EndpointSlices listing the selected pods, and only pods passing their readiness probe get traffic; kube-proxy rules or an eBPF dataplane on each node translate the virtual IP to one of those pod IPs. `ClusterIP` is the internal default, `NodePort` and `LoadBalancer` expose a Service outside the cluster, and a headless Service returns pod IPs directly from DNS. Balancing is per connection at layer 4, which matters for long-lived connections.","An Ingress holds layer 7 rules (host, path, TLS certificate) and does nothing by itself: an ingress controller (NGINX, Traefik, a cloud load balancer controller) watches Ingress objects and configures its proxy. A request flows from DNS to the external load balancer, to the controller, which matches host and path and picks the backend Service, then to a ready pod IP on the Service's `targetPort`. Many controllers send traffic straight to pod IPs and use the Service only for discovery, so readiness timing matters at the edge too. Gateway API is the successor; the Ingress API is frozen."],"keyPoints":["pods are disposable units of containers sharing one IP and volumes","a Deployment manages ReplicaSets to keep replicas running and roll out changes","a Service gives pods a stable name; an Ingress routes external HTTP to Services"],"examples":[{"title":"Deployment, Service and Ingress for one HTTP service","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata: { name: orders-api }\nspec:\n  replicas: 3                            # the ReplicaSet keeps 3 pods alive\n  selector:\n    matchLabels: { app: orders-api }     # must match the template labels\n  template:                              # editing the template starts a rollout\n    metadata:\n      labels: { app: orders-api }\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: app\n          image: registry.acme.io/orders-api:1.4.2\n          ports: [{ name: http, containerPort: 8080 }]\n          readinessProbe:                # only ready pods become endpoints\n            httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/readiness, port: http }\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata: { name: orders-api }           # DNS: orders-api.<namespace>.svc\nspec:                                    # type defaults to ClusterIP (internal)\n  selector: { app: orders-api }          # matches pods by label, never by name\n  ports: [{ name: http, port: 80, targetPort: http }]\n---\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata: { name: public-api }\nspec:\n  ingressClassName: external             # which controller implements the rules\n  tls: [{ hosts: [api.acme.io], secretName: api-acme-io-tls }]\n  rules:\n    - host: api.acme.io\n      http:\n        paths:\n          - path: /orders\n            pathType: Prefix\n            backend: { service: { name: orders-api, port: { name: http } } }","note":"The API server rejects a Deployment whose selector does not match its template labels, but it accepts a Service whose selector or `targetPort` matches nothing; the Service simply has no endpoints, a classic cause of 503s at the Ingress."},{"title":"Walking a 503 from the Ingress to the Pod","lang":"bash","code":"# Work outside in and stop at the first broken link\nkubectl -n orders describe ingress public-api       # rule admitted? which Service and port?\nkubectl -n orders get svc orders-api -o wide         # SELECTOR app=orders-api\nkubectl -n orders get pods -l app=orders-api         # empty output means a label mismatch\n\n# Selected pods appear as endpoints; failing readiness shows Ready: false\nkubectl -n orders describe endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=orders-api\n\n# Stuck rollout? Old and new ReplicaSet side by side\nkubectl -n orders get rs -l app=orders-api\n\n# Bypass the Ingress: does the Service answer inside the cluster?\nkubectl -n orders run curl --rm -it --restart=Never --image=curlimages/curl --command -- \\\n  curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' http://orders-api/actuator/health/readiness","note":"A 503 from the ingress controller usually means it found the Service but no ready endpoints; a 404 usually means no rule matched the host or path."}],"followUps":[{"q":"The Service load-balances, yet one pod gets most of the traffic. Why?","a":"kube-proxy balances connections, not requests. Keep-alive pools and gRPC over HTTP/2 send many requests over a few long-lived connections, so clients that connected while one pod was ready stay on it after a scale-out. Fix it with layer 7 balancing (a service mesh, or gRPC client-side balancing over a headless Service) or by capping connection age so clients reconnect."},{"q":"When would you use a StatefulSet instead of a Deployment?","a":"When pods need a stable identity: ordinal names (`kafka-0`, `kafka-1`), per-pod DNS names through a headless Service, a PersistentVolumeClaim that follows each pod across rescheduling, and ordered startup and scaling. That fits databases and Kafka brokers; stateless HTTP services stay on Deployments."}],"extra":["**ReplicaSet ownership:** a `pod-template-hash` label keeps old and new ReplicaSets from claiming each other's pods; `revisionHistoryLimit` (default 10) sets how many remain for rollback.","**Immutable selector:** a Deployment's `spec.selector` cannot change after creation, so choose stable labels.","**Native sidecars:** init containers with `restartPolicy: Always` start before the app container and stop after it, which suits proxies and log shippers.","**One entry point:** each `LoadBalancer` Service gets its own cloud load balancer; Services can share one Ingress.","**Gateway API:** it splits roles into GatewayClass, Gateway and HTTPRoute and supports header matching and weighted splits without annotations; the retirement of community ingress-nginx in March 2026 pushed many teams to it."],"redFlags":["Thinks pods have stable IPs or that a Service runs the application","cannot explain how a Service finds pods through labels and readiness","expects an Ingress to work without an ingress controller"]},{"id":213,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"Every commit re-downloads all Maven dependencies during the Docker build, and the image is 900 MB. How would you restructure it?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Each `RUN`, `COPY` and `ADD` instruction creates a cached, immutable layer, so put stable dependency layers before source code, use multi-stage builds on small trusted bases, run as non-root, and never put secrets in any layer.","answer":["Every `RUN`, `COPY` and `ADD` instruction produces an immutable, content-addressed layer, and the build cache reuses a layer only while its instruction and inputs are unchanged. The first cache miss invalidates every layer after it, so order instructions from least to most frequently changing: base image, OS packages, dependency manifests (`pom.xml`, `build.gradle`) plus a dependency resolution step, and only then the source code. Copying the whole repository before resolving dependencies means a one-line change re-downloads the entire Maven repository on every build.","Layers are additive, so size only goes up: deleting files in a later layer hides them but still ships the bytes. **Multi-stage builds** fix this by compiling with a full JDK and build tools in a builder stage and copying only the jar into a small runtime base such as `eclipse-temurin:21-jre` or a distroless Java image. Smaller images pull faster, which directly shortens scale-out and node recovery time.","For security, every layer is readable by anyone who can pull the image, so credentials must never appear in a copied file, `ENV` or `ARG`, even if removed later. Use BuildKit secret mounts (`RUN --mount=type=secret`) for build-time credentials and inject runtime secrets at deploy time. Prefer a minimal, trusted, pinned base (fewer packages, fewer CVEs), run as a **non-root user**, keep a `.dockerignore` so `.git`, `target/` and local `.env` files never enter the build context, and scan the final image in CI."],"keyPoints":["the build cache is reused only up to the first changed layer, so order matters","copy the build file and resolve dependencies before copying source","a multi-stage build ships only the runtime and the jar, not the JDK and the Maven cache","anything written to a layer, including credentials, stays in the image"],"examples":[{"title":"Bad vs good Dockerfile for a Spring Boot service","lang":"dockerfile","code":"# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\n\n# BAD: any source change busts the dependency cache, JDK and Maven ship to prod,\n# the password lives forever in image metadata, and the process runs as root\n# FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-21\n# COPY . /app\n# ENV DB_PASSWORD=s3cr3t\n# RUN mvn -f /app/pom.xml package\n# CMD java -jar /app/target/orders-service.jar\n\n# GOOD: dependency layer cached, build tools left behind, non-root runtime\nFROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-21 AS build\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY pom.xml .\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.m2 mvn -B -q dependency:go-offline\nCOPY src ./src\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.m2 mvn -B -q package -DskipTests\n\nFROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre-alpine\nRUN addgroup -S app && adduser -S app -G app\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=build /src/target/orders-service.jar app.jar\nUSER app\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75\", \"-jar\", \"app.jar\"]","note":"Only `pom.xml` is copied before dependency resolution, so code edits reuse the cached dependency layer, and the BuildKit cache mount keeps `~/.m2` between builds without writing it into any layer. The exec-form `ENTRYPOINT` makes the JVM PID 1 so it receives SIGTERM directly."},{"title":"Build-time secret done right, and proof that a deleted secret still ships","lang":"bash","code":"# GOOD: the secret is mounted for a single RUN step and never written to a layer\n# Dockerfile line:\n#   RUN --mount=type=secret,id=maven_settings,target=/root/.m2/settings.xml mvn -B package\ndocker build --secret id=maven_settings,src=\"$HOME/.m2/settings.xml\" -t orders-service:1.4.2 .\n\n# BAD image: settings.xml was COPYed, then removed by a later \"RUN rm\"\ndocker history --no-trunc orders-service:bad     # ENV and ARG values are visible here\ndocker save orders-service:bad -o orders-bad.tar\nmkdir -p bad && tar -xf orders-bad.tar -C bad\nfor layer in $(find bad -type f); do\n  tar -tf \"$layer\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q 'settings.xml' && echo \"secret still in $layer\"\ndone","note":"The loop finds the file inside the earlier layer archive even though the final filesystem view no longer shows it. Anyone with pull access to the registry can do the same."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do Spring Boot layered jars improve caching?","a":"Spring Boot can split the fat jar into layers (dependencies, spring-boot-loader, snapshot-dependencies, application), extracted with `java -Djarmode=tools -jar app.jar extract --layers --launcher` in Boot 3.3 and later, or `-Djarmode=layertools` before that, and the Dockerfile copies each into its own image layer. Dependencies change rarely, so a code change rebuilds and pushes only the small application layer, which also speeds up pulls on nodes. Buildpacks through `spring-boot:build-image` do this automatically."},{"q":"How do you give the build a private repository token safely?","a":"Use a BuildKit secret mount: `RUN --mount=type=secret,id=maven_settings,target=/root/.m2/settings.xml mvn -B package`, passing the file with `docker build --secret id=maven_settings,src=settings.xml .`. The file exists only during that `RUN` step and is never written to the image or its history, unlike values passed through `ARG` or `ENV`."}],"extra":["**Pin by digest:** `FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre@sha256:...` makes builds reproducible because tags are mutable; let Renovate or Dependabot bump the digest so you still receive security patches.","**Spring Boot layered jars:** Boot can split the fat jar into dependencies, loader, snapshot-dependencies and application layers (`-Djarmode=tools` since Boot 3.3, `layertools` before), so a code change pushes only the small application layer; Buildpacks (`spring-boot:build-image`) and Jib do this automatically.","**Container-aware JVM:** JDK 21 reads cgroup v1 and v2 limits, so size the heap with `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` instead of a fixed `-Xmx` that ignores the pod memory limit.","**Distroless trade-off:** no shell or package manager shrinks the attack surface and CVE count, but you debug with `kubectl debug` ephemeral containers instead of `exec` into a shell.","**Leaked means rotate:** once a secret was pushed in any layer, rebuilding does not help because old digests stay pullable from registries, mirrors and caches; rotate the credential first, then purge the images."],"redFlags":["ships a full JDK and build tools in the runtime image","pins the base image to `latest` and never rebuilds for patches","runs the process as root because it is the default"]},{"id":214,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"A Spring service checks the database in all three Kubernetes probes. Why is that risky, and how should they differ?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Startup probes gate slow boot, readiness decides whether the pod receives Service traffic, and liveness restarts a truly stuck process; only readiness may reflect hard dependencies, and liveness must stay local and cheap.","answer":["All three are HTTP, TCP, gRPC or exec checks that the kubelet runs periodically against each container. The **startup probe** runs first; until it succeeds, liveness and readiness are disabled, and if it fails `failureThreshold × periodSeconds` in a row the container is restarted. It protects slow initialization (JVM boot, Flyway migrations, cache warmup) so you need neither a huge `initialDelaySeconds` nor a lax liveness probe.","The **readiness probe** runs for the container's whole life. Failure removes the pod from the Service's EndpointSlices, so it stops receiving new traffic, but it is not restarted. Use it for \"cannot serve right now\": still warming up, draining at shutdown, or a hard dependency the pod genuinely cannot work without.","The **liveness probe** also runs for the whole life once startup has succeeded, and failure makes the kubelet kill and restart the container. It should detect only states a restart fixes: deadlocked threads, a wedged event loop, an unrecoverable internal error. It must not check the database, Kafka or downstream APIs, because a slow dependency would fail liveness on every replica at once and trigger mass restarts. Optional dependencies should not even fail readiness; degrade with fallbacks and circuit breakers instead.","Spring Boot 3 exposes `/actuator/health/liveness` and `/actuator/health/readiness`, backed by `ApplicationAvailability` and enabled automatically on Kubernetes. By default neither group includes external checks, and that default is usually right."],"keyPoints":["the startup probe holds off other probes until a slow boot completes","a failing readiness check only removes the pod from Service endpoints, which suits dependency trouble","a failing liveness check kills the container, so it should test only the process itself","dedicated liveness and readiness health groups in Spring Boot keep the checks apart"],"examples":[{"title":"Three probes for a JVM service","lang":"yaml","code":"containers:\n  - name: payments-api\n    image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.8.3\n    ports:\n      - containerPort: 8080\n    startupProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/liveness, port: 8080 }\n      periodSeconds: 5\n      failureThreshold: 36      # up to 180s to boot before a restart\n    readinessProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/readiness, port: 8080 }\n      periodSeconds: 5\n      failureThreshold: 2       # leave the load balancer quickly\n    livenessProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/liveness, port: 8080 }\n      periodSeconds: 10\n      timeoutSeconds: 2\n      failureThreshold: 3       # tolerate a GC pause or a throttled period","note":"Startup and liveness can share the cheap liveness endpoint; the startup probe simply allows a much larger failure budget. Readiness reacts fast, liveness is deliberately tolerant."},{"title":"Spring Boot probe groups","lang":"properties","code":"# Probe groups are auto-enabled on Kubernetes; this enables them locally too\nmanagement.endpoint.health.probes.enabled=true\n# With a separate management port, also expose /livez and /readyz on the main port\nmanagement.endpoint.health.probes.add-additional-paths=true\n# Liveness: internal state only, never external systems\nmanagement.endpoint.health.group.liveness.include=livenessState\n# Readiness: add a dependency only if the pod is useless without it\nmanagement.endpoint.health.group.readiness.include=readinessState,db","note":"Including `db` in readiness is a deliberate choice for a service that can do nothing without its database. Because all replicas share that database, an outage empties the Service of endpoints, so leave it out when the app can still serve cached reads or return controlled errors."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does Spring Boot Actuator map its health groups onto Kubernetes probes?","a":"When it detects Kubernetes, or with `management.endpoint.health.probes.enabled=true`, Actuator exposes `/actuator/health/liveness` and `/actuator/health/readiness`, backed by the `LivenessState` and `ReadinessState` availability states. By default neither group includes external checks such as the database; you can opt in for readiness with `management.endpoint.health.group.readiness.include=readinessState,db`. During shutdown Boot switches readiness to refusing traffic automatically."}],"extra":["**Detection math:** time to act is roughly `periodSeconds × failureThreshold` (plus `timeoutSeconds`); a liveness failure restarts only the container, not the pod, and repeated failures lead to CrashLoopBackOff with exponential backoff.","**Separate management port gotcha:** with `management.server.port` the probe hits a different connector and thread pool, so liveness can pass while request threads are exhausted; `add-additional-paths=true` serves `/livez` and `/readyz` on the main port.","**Shared dependency in readiness:** if every replica fails readiness because of the same database outage, the Service has zero endpoints and clients get connection errors instead of meaningful 503 responses from your app.","**Native gRPC probes:** Kubernetes supports `grpc` probes natively (GA in 1.27), so gRPC services no longer need the `grpc_health_probe` binary; exec probes fork a process every period and are costly, especially with JVM tools.","**Custom liveness signals:** publish `AvailabilityChangeEvent` with `LivenessState.BROKEN` when the app detects an unrecoverable state (for example a poisoned internal cache), letting Kubernetes restart it cleanly."],"redFlags":["thinks a readiness failure restarts the pod","sets a one-second liveness timeout on a JVM with long GC pauses","skips the startup probe and compensates with a huge `initialDelaySeconds`"]},{"id":215,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"A team builds a separate Docker image per environment with config baked in. How would you restructure it with Kubernetes primitives?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Keep images environment-agnostic and inject non-sensitive settings from ConfigMaps and credentials from Secrets, remembering that Secrets are only base64 encoded, so real protection comes from etcd encryption, tight RBAC and an external secret manager.","answer":["Build one immutable image and promote it unchanged through environments; everything environment-specific is injected at deploy time. Baking config into images forces a rebuild per environment (so production runs a binary you never tested) and pushes secrets into the registry. **ConfigMaps** hold non-sensitive settings: URLs, feature flags, pool sizes, log levels. **Secrets** hold credentials, API keys, TLS keys and keystores.","Secrets are **not inherently secure**. Values are only base64 encoded, stored unencrypted in etcd unless encryption at rest is configured, and readable by anyone with `get` on secrets or permission to create a pod in the namespace. Minimum hardening: etcd encryption with a KMS provider, least-privilege RBAC (no `list` or `watch` on secrets for humans or CI), a dedicated service account per workload, and audit logging. Better still, keep the source of truth in Vault or a cloud secret manager and sync it with External Secrets Operator or the Secrets Store CSI driver, which adds rotation and audit. Never commit plain Secret manifests; use Sealed Secrets or SOPS in GitOps repositories.","In the application, never log injected values or dump the environment: Spring Boot 3 masks values on `/actuator/env` by default, and `management.endpoint.env.show-values` should stay `NEVER`. Spring reads environment variables through relaxed binding and mounted secret files through `spring.config.import=configtree:`. Make ConfigMaps and Secrets immutable with a content hash in the name, so a config change triggers a controlled rollout instead of silent drift."],"keyPoints":["one image moves unchanged through every environment; only injected config differs","plain settings come from ConfigMaps, credentials from Secrets","base64 is an encoding, so protection relies on etcd encryption at rest and RBAC","an external secret store synced into the cluster keeps the source of truth outside it"],"examples":[{"title":"ConfigMap for settings, Secret mounted as files","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-config\ndata:\n  SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE: prod\n  PAYMENTS_PSP_BASE_URL: https://psp.example.com\n  SPRING_DATASOURCE_HIKARI_MAXIMUM_POOL_SIZE: \"20\"\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-db\ntype: Opaque\nstringData:          # in real clusters created by External Secrets, never committed to Git\n  username: payments_app\n  password: change-me\n---\n# Pod template excerpt from the Deployment\nspec:\n  serviceAccountName: payments-api\n  containers:\n    - name: app\n      image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.8.3\n      envFrom:\n        - configMapRef: { name: payments-config }\n      volumeMounts:\n        - name: db-creds\n          mountPath: /etc/secrets/db\n          readOnly: true\n  volumes:\n    - name: db-creds\n      secret:\n        secretName: payments-db","note":"Plain settings arrive as environment variables that Spring binds automatically; credentials arrive as files on a tmpfs volume, one file per key."},{"title":"Reading mounted secrets in Spring Boot without exposing them","lang":"properties","code":"# /etc/secrets/db/username becomes the property db.username\nspring.config.import=optional:configtree:/etc/secrets/\nspring.datasource.url=jdbc:postgresql://payments-db:5432/payments\nspring.datasource.username=${db.username}\nspring.datasource.password=${db.password}\n# Never return raw values from actuator\nmanagement.endpoint.env.show-values=NEVER\nmanagement.endpoint.configprops.show-values=NEVER","note":"The `configtree` import maps directory and file names to property names, so the same image works with any secret backend that can produce files."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you keep Secret manifests out of plain Git while still using GitOps?","a":"Either encrypt them before committing with Sealed Secrets or SOPS, so only the cluster controller or authorized keys can decrypt them, or commit only a reference and let the External Secrets Operator pull the real value from Vault or a cloud secret manager. The second keeps rotation and audit in the secret manager, which is usually preferable for production credentials."},{"q":"Does editing a ConfigMap change a running pod?","a":"Environment variables never change after the container starts, so those pods must be restarted. Mounted volumes are refreshed by the kubelet after a sync delay (except with `subPath`), but the application still has to re-read the file. A common pattern is a checksum annotation on the pod template so any config change triggers a rolling restart."}],"extra":["**Update propagation:** environment variables are fixed at container start; mounted volumes are refreshed by the kubelet after a delay, except `subPath` mounts, which never update. Spring reloads neither automatically, so a hash-triggered rollout (Kustomize `configMapGenerator`, Helm checksum annotation) is the reliable pattern.","**Size limit:** ConfigMaps and Secrets are capped at 1 MiB because they live in etcd; large reference data belongs elsewhere.","**Pod creation is a read path:** anyone who can create pods or Deployments in a namespace can mount any Secret there, so RBAC on workloads matters as much as RBAC on secrets.","**Workload identity over static keys:** IRSA on EKS, Workload Identity on GKE or Azure Workload Identity give pods short-lived cloud credentials, removing long-lived access keys from Secrets entirely.","**Dynamic database credentials:** Vault's database secrets engine can issue per-application credentials with a TTL; the app must renew the lease or rebuild its connection pool before expiry, so test that path before relying on it."],"redFlags":["thinks base64 in a Secret manifest is encryption","commits Secret manifests to Git in plain form","lets every service account read all Secrets in the namespace"]},{"id":216,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"You are releasing a risky change to a payment service. Would you use a rolling, blue-green or canary rollout, and why?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Rolling updates replace pods gradually with little extra capacity, blue-green switches all traffic between two full environments for instant rollback at double cost, and canary exposes a small slice while metrics decide promotion.","answer":["**Rolling** (the Deployment default) replaces pods in batches bounded by `maxSurge` and `maxUnavailable`, so it needs almost no extra capacity and no extra tooling. The costs: old and new versions serve side by side for the whole rollout, rollback is just another rolling update, and readiness is the only gate, so a version that is ready but wrong (higher error rate, bad pricing logic) rolls out fully unless someone is watching.","**Blue-green** runs the new version at full capacity next to the old one, verifies it with smoke tests on a private route, then switches all traffic at once through a Service selector, load balancer target or DNS. Rollback is switching back, which takes seconds as long as blue is still running. The price is double capacity during the switch and exposing every user at the same moment.","**Canary** sends a small share of traffic (1%, 5%, 25%) to the new version and promotes step by step only if predefined criteria hold against the stable baseline: error rate, p99 latency, saturation and business signals such as payment authorization rate. It needs weighted traffic control (service mesh, ingress, Argo Rollouts or Flagger) and reliable metrics; one canary pod behind a Service with nobody evaluating it is just an unmonitored rolling update.","Rule of thumb: rolling for low-risk internal services, blue-green when you need an atomic cutover, canary for high-traffic, high-risk paths. In all three, two versions coexist at some point, so APIs, events and schemas must stay backward compatible."],"keyPoints":["rolling needs little spare capacity but mixes versions for a while","blue-green keeps the old stack warm, so rollback is a traffic switch","canary puts a small share of real traffic on the new version and promotes on evidence","every strategy depends on schema changes both versions can run against"],"examples":[{"title":"Canary with automated analysis (Argo Rollouts)","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Rollout\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\nspec:\n  replicas: 10\n  workloadRef:                  # reuse the pod template of the existing Deployment\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: payments-api\n  strategy:\n    canary:\n      canaryService: payments-api-canary\n      stableService: payments-api-stable\n      trafficRouting:\n        nginx:\n          stableIngress: payments-api\n      analysis:                 # runs in the background; failure aborts and shifts traffic back\n        templates:\n          - templateName: payments-5xx-ratio\n        startingStep: 1\n      steps:\n        - setWeight: 5\n        - pause: { duration: 10m }\n        - setWeight: 25\n        - pause: { duration: 15m }\n---\napiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: AnalysisTemplate\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-5xx-ratio\nspec:\n  metrics:\n    - name: error-ratio\n      interval: 1m\n      failureLimit: 2\n      successCondition: result[0] < 0.01\n      provider:\n        prometheus:\n          address: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090\n          query: >\n            sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{service=\"payments-api-canary\",status=~\"5..\"}[2m]))\n            / sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{service=\"payments-api-canary\"}[2m]))","note":"The canary is judged by an explicit success condition (under 1% server errors) evaluated every minute; a third failed measurement (more than `failureLimit: 2`) aborts the rollout automatically. Real setups add latency and business metrics to the same template."},{"title":"Trade-offs at a glance","lang":"text","code":"Strategy     Extra capacity     Rollback             Blast radius           Needs\nRolling      maxSurge only      minutes (re-roll)    grows batch by batch   good readiness probes\nBlue-green   2x during switch   seconds (flip back)  100% at once           switchable routing, smoke tests\nCanary       small slice        seconds (weight 0)   1% to 5% at first      weighted routing, SLO metrics, analysis","note":"No strategy removes the need for backward compatible changes; they only change how many users see a bad version and how fast you can undo it."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Which signals would decide whether a canary gets promoted?","a":"Compare the canary with a baseline running the old version at the same time, not with yesterday: error rate, latency percentiles, saturation and business signals such as payment authorization success rate. Tools like Argo Rollouts or Flagger run these analyses at each step and abort automatically when the canary is worse beyond a threshold. Make sure the canary gets enough traffic for the comparison to be statistically meaningful."}],"extra":["**Rolling knobs:** `maxUnavailable: 0` with `maxSurge: 1` keeps full capacity throughout; `progressDeadlineSeconds` marks a stuck rollout as failed but does not roll back by itself, so pair it with `kubectl rollout undo` or a progressive delivery controller.","**Deploy is not release:** feature flags let you ship code dark and enable it per tenant or percentage, so a behavioral rollback becomes a flag flip instead of a redeploy.","**Canary statistics:** at 1% traffic metrics are noisy; compare canary against a baseline over the same window, require a minimum request count, and prefer ratios over absolute counts.","**Consumers and workers:** Kafka consumers receive partitions, not traffic weights, so a canary consumer gets a partition share of messages; blue-green for consumers must ensure only one color consumes, typically by scaling the idle color to zero.","**Traffic mirroring:** a mesh can shadow production requests to the new version and discard its responses, which is great for read paths but dangerous for payments unless side effects are disabled."],"redFlags":["ignores that two versions run side by side during most rollouts","has no automated rollback trigger","picks blue-green without accounting for shared database state"]},{"id":217,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"Your team deploys straight from main with only unit tests. Which automated checks would you add, and where?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Use layered, risk-based gates: fast compile, unit, static and secret checks on every PR, then integration and contract tests, a scanned and signed immutable artifact promoted unchanged, and post-deploy verification with automated rollback.","answer":["Order gates by cost and signal so feedback stays fast. On every pull request, within minutes: compile, unit tests, static analysis on new code (Error Prone, SpotBugs, Sonar), formatting, dependency vulnerability and license checks, and **secret scanning**, ideally also as push protection so secrets never reach the remote. Then integration tests with Testcontainers and **contract verification**, which catch boundary bugs unit tests cannot. Failing tests fail the build; a flaky test is quarantined with an owner, never retried until green.","Build the artifact **once**: an immutable version (semver plus git SHA), an image scanned for vulnerabilities, an SBOM, and a signature with provenance. Promote the same digest from staging to production. Deploying a mutable `latest` tag breaks rollback, audit and reproducibility, and different nodes can end up running different code.","Deployment gates protect the environment: migration safety (backward compatible, tested against a production-like schema), an admission policy that accepts only signed digests, smoke tests after deploy, canary analysis on SLO metrics, and automated rollback when error rate or latency breaches. Manual approval and change windows belong on high-risk services such as payments, not on every internal tool.","The underlying idea is that checks are **risk-based**, rather than a single enormous gate. A 60-minute pipeline teaches people to batch changes and bypass controls. Parallelize and cache, keep the merge path to roughly 10 to 15 minutes, and move expensive suites (full E2E, performance, DAST) to nightly or release-candidate stages."],"keyPoints":["cheap, deterministic checks run on every pull request so feedback stays fast","slower suites gate release candidates instead of every merge","the exact artifact that was tested and scanned is the one promoted to production","verification after deploy, with automatic rollback, closes the loop"],"examples":[{"title":"Gate map for a payments service","lang":"text","code":"Stage            Gate                                          Blocks\nPR (< 10 min)    compile, unit tests, static analysis          merge\n                 formatting, secret scan, dependency policy    merge\nPR / main        Testcontainers ITs, contract verification     merge\n                 migration check on prod-like schema           merge\nBuild once       image scan, SBOM, signature, provenance       publish and promotion\nDeploy           admission policy: signed digest only          deployment\n                 smoke tests, canary SLO analysis              promotion, triggers auto rollback\nNightly / RC     full E2E, load test, DAST                     release candidate, not every merge","note":"Each gate sits at the cheapest point where it can catch its class of problem, and only the fast deterministic ones block merges."},{"title":"Build once, scan, sign, deploy by digest","lang":"bash","code":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nVERSION=\"1.12.${BUILD_NUMBER}-$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\"\nIMAGE=\"registry.example.com/payments/orders-service:${VERSION}\"\n\ndocker build -t \"$IMAGE\" .\n# Gate: fail on fixable critical or high CVEs before anything is pushed\ntrivy image --exit-code 1 --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --ignore-unfixed \"$IMAGE\"\ndocker push \"$IMAGE\"\n\n# Resolve the immutable digest; everything downstream uses it, never a tag\nDIGEST=$(docker inspect --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' \"$IMAGE\")\nsyft \"$DIGEST\" -o spdx-json > sbom.spdx.json\ncosign sign --yes \"$DIGEST\"\ncosign attest --yes --type spdxjson --predicate sbom.spdx.json \"$DIGEST\"\n\n# Promote the same digest; the cluster admission policy verifies the signature\nkubectl -n payments set image deployment/orders-service app=\"$DIGEST\"\nkubectl -n payments rollout status deployment/orders-service --timeout=5m \\\n  || { kubectl -n payments rollout undo deployment/orders-service; exit 1; }","note":"The tag is only a human-readable label; the digest is what gets signed, attested and deployed, so rollback and audit always refer to exact bytes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why promote the same image digest instead of rebuilding per environment?","a":"A rebuild can pull different dependency versions or base image patches, so what reaches production is not what passed the tests and scans. Promoting by digest, not by a tag that can be moved, guarantees byte-for-byte identity and lets signatures and provenance attestations verify it at admission. Environment differences then live only in configuration."}],"extra":["**Pin CI dependencies:** third-party CI actions and plugins run with your deploy credentials, so pin them by commit SHA and use OIDC federation to the cloud instead of long-lived deploy keys.","**Coverage on changed lines:** a global coverage threshold rewards junk tests; gate on coverage of new code and use mutation testing (PIT) on critical modules to check that tests actually assert.","**Rescan at promotion:** new CVEs are published after an image was built, so rescan images in the registry continuously and at promotion time, not only at build.","**Migrations as a separate step:** run Flyway or Liquibase as a pre-deploy job with its own gate, so a failed migration stops the rollout before any new pod starts.","**Measure the pipeline:** track the DORA metrics (lead time, deployment frequency, change failure rate, time to restore) plus pipeline duration and flake rate to judge whether gates add value or only delay."],"redFlags":["rebuilds the artifact separately for each environment","lets the pipeline pass once a flaky test is retried until green","makes every check merge-blocking, so developers wait an hour per change"]},{"id":218,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"A Java pod has a tiny CPU request and a tight memory limit. What problems would you expect in production?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Requests are what the scheduler reserves and what HPA percentages are based on; limits are enforced at runtime, where exceeding CPU means throttling and exceeding memory means OOMKill, so size both from measured usage.","answer":["**Requests** are what the scheduler reserves: a pod lands only on a node whose unreserved allocatable capacity covers its requests, regardless of actual usage. They also set the QoS class (Guaranteed, Burstable, BestEffort), which decides eviction order under node pressure, and CPU requests become the cgroup CPU weight used to share contended CPU. Requests set too low overpack nodes, causing noisy neighbors and evictions; set too high, they waste capacity and leave pods Pending. HPA utilization targets are percentages of requests too.","**Limits** are enforced by the kernel at runtime. CPU is compressible: a container that uses its quota within a CFS period (100 ms by default) is **throttled** until the next period, never killed. A JVM with many GC and JIT threads can burn a 1 CPU quota in 30 ms and then stall for 70 ms, which shows up as p99 latency spikes while average CPU looks fine. Memory is incompressible: exceeding the limit invokes the OOM killer and the container restarts with reason `OOMKilled` and exit code 137.","For Java, the memory limit must cover heap plus metaspace, thread stacks, code cache, direct buffers and GC structures, so size the heap with `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` around 70 to 75 rather than heap equal to limit. A common production stance: memory request equal to limit, CPU request at measured p95 under realistic load, and a generous or no CPU limit for latency-sensitive services, as cluster policy allows. Derive numbers from metrics, load tests and VPA recommendations, then revisit after major changes."],"keyPoints":["the scheduler places pods by requests, so a tiny request overpacks nodes","the HPA measures utilization as a percentage of the request, so the request distorts scaling too","a CPU limit throttles the container; a memory limit kills it","sizing should come from observed usage under realistic load"],"examples":[{"title":"Resources sized from measurements for a JVM service","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: credit-decision\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels: { app: credit-decision }\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels: { app: credit-decision }\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: app\n          image: registry.example.com/credit-decision:2.4.1\n          env:\n            - name: JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS\n              value: \"-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75 -XX:ActiveProcessorCount=2\"\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"1\"          # measured p95 is about 0.7 cores under peak load\n              memory: 1Gi\n            limits:\n              memory: 1Gi       # equal to request: no memory overcommit\n              # no CPU limit: bursts are not throttled (if cluster policy allows)","note":"Heap is capped at 75% of the 1Gi limit, leaving room for native memory. `ActiveProcessorCount` stops the JVM from sizing GC and ForkJoin pools for every core on the node when no CPU limit is set."},{"title":"Spotting OOMKill and CPU throttling","lang":"bash","code":"# OOMKill: the kernel killed the container for exceeding its memory limit\nkubectl describe pod credit-decision-7d9f8c-x2k4p | grep -A4 \"Last State\"\n#   Last State:  Terminated\n#     Reason:    OOMKilled\n#     Exit Code: 137\nkubectl get pod credit-decision-7d9f8c-x2k4p \\\n  -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.reason}'\n\n# Throttling: cgroup v2 counters from inside the container\nkubectl exec credit-decision-7d9f8c-x2k4p -- cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat\n#   nr_periods 120000\n#   nr_throttled 38000        (about 32% of periods throttled)\n#   throttled_usec 912000000\n\n# Same signal fleet-wide in Prometheus:\n#   sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total{namespace=\"credit\"}[5m]))\n#   / sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_periods_total{namespace=\"credit\"}[5m]))","note":"A throttled-period ratio above a few percent on a latency-sensitive service is worth acting on even if average CPU usage is far below the limit."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How should JVM memory settings relate to the container limit?","a":"The JVM reads the cgroup limit, so size the heap as a percentage with `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` (often 60 to 75) rather than a fixed `-Xmx`, leaving room for metaspace, code cache, thread stacks, direct buffers and GC structures. If total process memory exceeds the limit, the kernel OOM killer ends the container with exit code 137 even though the heap never filled. Native Memory Tracking helps measure the non-heap share for your service."},{"q":"What do QoS classes mean for eviction under node pressure?","a":"Pods where every container sets requests equal to limits for both CPU and memory are `Guaranteed`, pods with some requests or limits are `Burstable`, and pods with none are `BestEffort`. Under node memory pressure the kubelet evicts `BestEffort` pods first, then `Burstable` pods using the most memory above their requests, and `Guaranteed` pods last."}],"extra":["**QoS and eviction:** under node memory pressure the kubelet evicts BestEffort pods first, then Burstable pods using the most above their requests, and Guaranteed last; eviction terminates the whole pod (phase `Failed`, reason `Evicted`) and its controller creates a replacement, while an OOMKill restarts the container in place.","**JVM processor count:** modern JDKs no longer derive `availableProcessors()` from CPU requests (shares), so without a CPU limit the JVM sizes GC threads and the common `ForkJoinPool` for the whole node; pin it with `-XX:ActiveProcessorCount`.","**Where native memory goes:** `-XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary` plus `jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary` breaks RSS into heap, metaspace, threads, code cache and GC, which explains OOMKills that happen with a healthy heap.","**LimitRange surprises:** a namespace LimitRange can silently inject default requests and limits into pods that declare none, for example a small CPU limit that throttles every JVM; check with `kubectl describe limitrange`.","**Startup bursts:** JIT compilation and class loading need far more CPU in the first minute than steady state, so tight CPU limits make startup slow and can trip startup or liveness probes."],"redFlags":["sets a memory limit without fitting heap, metaspace and thread stacks inside it","believes a pod using more CPU than its request gets evicted"]},{"id":219,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"During every deployment, clients see a burst of 502s and some Kafka messages get processed twice. How would you fix shutdown?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"On SIGTERM, fail readiness, keep serving while endpoint removal propagates, stop accepting new work, finish or cancel in-flight requests and messages within the grace period, then close consumers, executors and pools before exiting.","answer":["On pod deletion two things start in parallel: the control plane removes the pod from EndpointSlices, and the kubelet runs the `preStop` hook and then sends SIGTERM to PID 1. Kube-proxy, ingress controllers and meshes learn about the endpoint removal asynchronously, a few seconds later, so an app that exits instantly on SIGTERM still receives requests and clients see resets and 502s. When `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (default 30, counted from the start and including preStop) expires, the kubelet sends SIGKILL, which is the failure mode, not the plan.","The sequence a Java service should follow: fail readiness (Kubernetes marks terminating endpoints not ready, and publishing `ReadinessState.REFUSING_TRAFFIC` also covers external load balancers), keep serving during a short preStop delay, then stop accepting new work. SIGTERM runs JVM shutdown hooks and Spring Boot closes the context: with `server.shutdown=graceful` (the default since Boot 3.4) the web server stops accepting connections and waits for in-flight requests up to `spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase`.","Background work matters as much: stop Kafka listener containers so no new records are polled, finish or abandon the current batch (unprocessed records are redelivered, so handlers must be idempotent), commit offsets, shut down executors with `shutdown()` plus `awaitTermination()` and `shutdownNow()` on timeout, then close pools and flush telemetry. Budget it so preStop delay plus drain fits inside the grace period, and use an exec-form `ENTRYPOINT` so the JVM, not a shell, receives SIGTERM."],"keyPoints":["endpoint removal is asynchronous, so the pod must keep serving briefly after SIGTERM","graceful shutdown in Spring Boot lets in-flight requests finish within a timeout","consumers should stop polling and commit offsets for completed work before closing","the whole sequence must fit inside the pod's grace period"],"examples":[{"title":"Pod spec and Spring config sized together","lang":"yaml","code":"# Deployment pod template excerpt\nspec:\n  terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 45       # preStop 10s + app drain 25s + margin\n  containers:\n    - name: orders-api\n      image: registry.example.com/orders-api:3.2.0\n      lifecycle:\n        preStop:\n          exec:\n            command: [\"sleep\", \"10\"]      # let endpoint removal propagate before SIGTERM\n      readinessProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/readiness, port: 8080 }\n        periodSeconds: 5\n---\n# application.yaml of orders-api\nserver:\n  shutdown: graceful                      # explicit, although it is the default since Boot 3.4\nspring:\n  lifecycle:\n    timeout-per-shutdown-phase: 25s","note":"The grace period is derived from its parts rather than guessed. An `exec` sleep needs a `sleep` binary in the image; distroless images can use the native `preStop.sleep` action available in newer Kubernetes versions."},{"title":"Draining a Kafka consumer and a worker pool on shutdown","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass SettlementWorker implements SmartLifecycle {\n\n    private final ExecutorService workers = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8);\n    private final KafkaListenerEndpointRegistry registry;\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;\n    private volatile boolean running;\n\n    SettlementWorker(KafkaListenerEndpointRegistry registry, ApplicationEventPublisher events) {\n        this.registry = registry;\n        this.events = events;\n    }\n\n    @Override public void start() { running = true; }\n    @Override public boolean isRunning() { return running; }\n\n    // Default phase (Integer.MAX_VALUE) stops before the web server and listener containers\n    @Override\n    public void stop() {\n        AvailabilityChangeEvent.publish(events, this, ReadinessState.REFUSING_TRAFFIC);\n        registry.getListenerContainer(\"settlements\").stop();  // no new polls, offsets committed\n        workers.shutdown();                                   // reject new jobs, finish queued ones\n        try {\n            if (!workers.awaitTermination(20, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {\n                workers.shutdownNow();                        // interrupt; redelivery covers the rest\n            }\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            workers.shutdownNow();\n            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n        } finally {\n            running = false;\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Spring calls `stop()` when the context closes after SIGTERM, before beans such as the DataSource are destroyed. The order is: refuse traffic, stop intake, drain with a deadline, cancel the rest and rely on idempotent redelivery."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why might a Java process in a container ignore SIGTERM?","a":"If the entrypoint uses the shell form or a wrapper script, the shell is PID 1 and may not forward signals, so the JVM never runs its shutdown hooks and is SIGKILLed when the grace period ends. Use the exec form (`ENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"app.jar\"]`), `exec java ...` at the end of the script, or a tiny init such as tini. Then verify by watching for Spring's shutdown log lines during a rollout."},{"q":"How does a Spring Kafka listener stop cleanly?","a":"Listener containers are lifecycle beans, so on context close they stop polling, let the current batch finish within `shutdownTimeout`, commit offsets for processed records and leave the group. Keep processing idempotent anyway, because a rebalance or a forced kill can still redeliver records whose offsets were not committed."}],"extra":["**PID 1 and signals:** shell-form `ENTRYPOINT java -jar app.jar` runs under `/bin/sh -c`, which does not forward SIGTERM, so the JVM only ever sees SIGKILL; use exec form, `exec java` in scripts, or a tiny init such as tini.","**Kafka rebalances on deploy:** every stopping consumer triggers a rebalance; the cooperative sticky assignor and static membership (`group.instance.id`) reduce stop-the-world rebalances during rolling updates.","**Long jobs:** a grace period of minutes cannot protect hour-long work, so design jobs to checkpoint, hold leases and resume idempotently after interruption instead of stretching `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`.","**Node drains use the same path:** evictions during cluster upgrades trigger the same termination sequence, and a PodDisruptionBudget limits how many replicas drain at once.","**Test it:** run `kubectl rollout restart` under load and watch ingress 5xx, client connection resets and consumer lag; any spike means the shutdown sequence has a gap."],"redFlags":["assumes the pod stops receiving traffic the instant SIGTERM arrives","runs the JVM under a shell script that never forwards the signal","commits offsets before processing, losing messages on shutdown"]},{"id":220,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"Traffic doubled and the autoscaler added pods, yet latency barely improved. What might explain that, given how HPA calculates replicas?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"HPA computes desired replicas as current replicas times current metric over target, with CPU and memory measured against requests, so wrong requests, slow startup, missing metrics or downstream bottlenecks make it ineffective.","answer":["The HPA controller runs a control loop (every 15 seconds by default), reads the metrics APIs (metrics-server for CPU and memory, a Prometheus adapter or KEDA for custom and external metrics) and computes `desired = ceil(currentReplicas × currentValue / targetValue)`. Resource targets are **relative to requests**: 70% CPU means 70% of the requested CPU averaged across pods, not of the node. With several metrics it takes the highest result, it ignores changes within a 10% tolerance, and scale-down is damped by a stabilization window configurable in `behavior`.","Things that make it ineffective: **wrong requests** (far below real usage pins it at max, far above means it never triggers); **slow startup** (a JVM that needs a minute plus warmup arrives after the spike, and JIT CPU can cause extra scaling); **missing metrics** (a broken adapter or pods without metrics mean no or conservative decisions); the **wrong signal** (CPU for an I/O-bound service waiting on a database); and `maxReplicas` or cluster capacity, where pending pods wait minutes for the cluster autoscaler.","Crucially, HPA only adds replicas of this service. If the bottleneck is the database or a rate-limited provider, more pods mean more connections and concurrent queries, which deepens the saturation; Hikari pool size times `maxReplicas` must fit the database's `max_connections`. Scale on a signal that reflects real work (requests per pod, queue lag), keep `minReplicas` with headroom for sudden spikes, and combine autoscaling with rate limiting and load shedding, because HPA reacts in minutes, not seconds."],"keyPoints":["desired replicas scale with the ratio of observed metric to target","resource utilization is measured against requests, so bad requests skew decisions","metric delay, stabilization windows and slow JVM startup add minutes of lag","extra pods cannot help when a shared database or rate-limited API is the bottleneck"],"examples":[{"title":"HPA on CPU and requests per second with tuned behavior","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: autoscaling/v2\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: orders-api\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: orders-api\n  minReplicas: 4\n  maxReplicas: 20              # bounded by the DB: 20 pods x 10 Hikari connections = 200\n  metrics:\n    - type: Resource\n      resource:\n        name: cpu\n        target:\n          type: Utilization\n          averageUtilization: 65     # percent of the CPU request, not of the node\n    - type: Pods\n      pods:\n        metric:\n          name: http_server_requests_per_second   # served by a Prometheus adapter rule\n        target:\n          type: AverageValue\n          averageValue: \"150\"\n  behavior:\n    scaleUp:\n      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 0\n      policies:\n        - { type: Percent, value: 100, periodSeconds: 60 }\n    scaleDown:\n      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300\n      policies:\n        - { type: Pods, value: 2, periodSeconds: 60 }","note":"Scale-up is aggressive (double per minute) while scale-down is slow and gradual, which avoids flapping. `maxReplicas` is a capacity decision about the database, not just about the cluster."},{"title":"How a wrong request breaks the calculation","lang":"text","code":"4 replicas, CPU request 500m, target 65%\nObserved average usage 455m per pod = 91% of request\ndesired = ceil(4 x 91 / 65) = ceil(5.6) = 6 replicas      (scales as intended)\n\nSame load, request mistakenly set to 2000m\n455m = 23% of request\ndesired = ceil(4 x 23 / 65) = ceil(1.4) = 2  -> clamped to minReplicas 4\nLatency is climbing, yet HPA sees idle pods and never scales.","note":"Because utilization is measured against requests, the request value is effectively part of the autoscaling policy."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why can scale-down be more dangerous than scale-up?","a":"Removing pods too quickly after a spike can leave too little capacity for the next burst, and every terminated pod must drain its in-flight work. The HPA's default scale-down stabilization window of 300 seconds uses the highest recommendation within it to avoid flapping, and `behavior.scaleDown` policies can limit removal to a percentage per period. Pair it with proper graceful shutdown so removed pods do not drop requests."},{"q":"How does replica count interact with database connection pools?","a":"Each pod opens its own pool, so total connections equal replicas times pool size: 40 pods with a pool of 20 means 800 connections, which can exceed PostgreSQL's `max_connections` or simply slow the database down. Cap `maxReplicas` with that budget in mind, shrink per-pod pools, or put a pooler such as PgBouncer in front."}],"extra":["**Reaction time adds up:** metric scrape interval, HPA sync, scheduling, image pull, JVM start and readiness commonly total one to three minutes, so pre-scale for known peaks (a KEDA cron scaler or a scheduled `minReplicas` bump).","**Startup CPU is discounted:** the controller treats CPU samples from pods that just started or are not yet ready conservatively, which only works if readiness is accurate; AppCDS, CRaC or GraalVM native images shrink the startup gap itself.","**Memory is a poor signal for Java:** the heap rarely shrinks after load, so memory-based scaling goes up and never comes back down.","**HPA and VPA:** do not let both act on the same resource metric for one workload; use VPA in recommendation mode to size requests and HPA for the replica count.","**Scale to zero:** a plain HPA keeps at least one replica by default; KEDA can scale event-driven workloads to zero and manages an HPA underneath once there is work."],"redFlags":["scales on CPU for an I/O-bound service","sets `maxReplicas` without checking database connection limits","does not know HPA needs resource requests to compute utilization"]},{"id":221,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"An outage ended when someone restarted the pods. Your manager asks whether the incident can be closed. What do you say?","level":"Basic","tldr":"No: a restart is a mitigation that resets accumulated state; capture evidence first, find what accumulated (memory, threads, connections, locks, caches), fix the cause, and add detection and a runbook so it cannot silently return.","answer":["A restart that makes the symptom disappear proves only that the problem lives in **process state**: something accumulated or got stuck and the restart wiped it. The cause is still there and will come back, often at a worse moment, and every restart destroys the evidence needed to find it. So treat a restart as **mitigation, not root cause**: keep the incident open and make the root cause a tracked action item with an owner.","**Preserve evidence first** whenever you can: take the instance out of rotation instead of killing it, then capture thread dumps, a heap histogram or dump, the JFR recording, GC logs, pool, thread and file descriptor counts, and the logs and events. If users are hurting badly, restart the other replicas and keep one sick instance isolated for analysis.","Then look for **state or resource accumulation**: heap live set, native memory, threads, file descriptors, HTTP or database connections, a growing cache, a lock or stuck scheduled job, a DNS or connection cache pointing at a failed-over endpoint, or credentials and certificates that expired in memory. Plot them against time since start: a problem that appears N hours after every restart is a strong accumulation signal. Finally define **prevention** (bound the cache, add the timeout, fix the leak), add **detection** on the leading indicator so you alert before users notice, and write a **runbook** covering evidence capture and safe mitigation. Scheduled restarts are acceptable only as a documented stopgap with a ticket."],"keyPoints":["a restart wipes accumulated state, which hides the cause rather than removing it","dumps and logs must be grabbed before the restart or they are gone","look for something that builds up over time: memory, threads, connections, locks or cache entries","follow up with a fix, an alert that catches the build-up early, and a runbook"],"examples":[{"title":"Evidence capture script to run before restarting a sick pod","lang":"bash","code":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nNS=payments; POD=$1; OUT=evidence-$POD-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)\nmkdir -p \"$OUT\"\n\n# Remove the selector label: the Service stops routing to it, the ReplicaSet starts a replacement\nkubectl -n $NS label pod \"$POD\" app- quarantine=true\n\nkubectl -n $NS describe pod \"$POD\" > \"$OUT/describe.txt\"\nkubectl -n $NS get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=\"$POD\" > \"$OUT/events.txt\"\nkubectl -n $NS logs \"$POD\" --since=2h > \"$OUT/app.log\"\nkubectl -n $NS logs \"$POD\" --previous > \"$OUT/app-previous.log\" 2>/dev/null || true\n\nfor i in 1 2 3; do\n  kubectl -n $NS exec \"$POD\" -- jcmd 1 Thread.print > \"$OUT/threads-$i.txt\"\n  sleep 10\ndone\nkubectl -n $NS exec \"$POD\" -- jcmd 1 GC.class_histogram > \"$OUT/histogram.txt\"\nkubectl -n $NS exec \"$POD\" -- jcmd 1 JFR.dump name=1 filename=/tmp/incident.jfr\nkubectl -n $NS cp \"$POD:/tmp/incident.jfr\" \"$OUT/incident.jfr\"\nkubectl -n $NS exec \"$POD\" -- sh -c 'ls /proc/1/fd | wc -l' > \"$OUT/open-fds.txt\"","note":"The quarantined pod keeps its broken state for analysis while capacity is restored by the replacement. `JFR.dump` assumes a continuous recording started with `-XX:StartFlightRecording`; delete the quarantined pod when done."},{"title":"Closing the incident properly: evidence, finding, actions","lang":"text","code":"Incident: payments-api P99 above 5 s, mitigated by restart at 14:32\nStatus after restart: MITIGATED, not resolved\n\nEvidence                            Finding\nthreads-1..3.txt                    180 request threads waiting in HikariPool.getConnection\nhistogram.txt                       heap stable, no growth\nhikaricp.connections.active         at max (20) for 40 minutes, 150 pending\napplication logs                    ReportExportJob opens connections and never closes them\n\nAction item                                                Owner      Type\nClose connections in ReportExportJob (try-with-resources)  payments   prevention\nEnable leakDetectionThreshold=30000 on the pool            payments   detection\nAlert: hikaricp pending > 10 for 5 minutes                 payments   detection\nRunbook: run evidence script before any restart            sre        runbook","note":"The incident is resolved only when prevention, detection and runbook items exist and are owned; the restart merely bought time."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When are scheduled restarts an acceptable stopgap?","a":"When the accumulation is understood well enough to know the interval is safe, a real fix is planned with an owner and a date, and the restarts roll so capacity never drops. It must be monitored, because a faster leak will outrun the schedule. It is never acceptable as a silent replacement for root-cause work."},{"q":"How do you make the next occurrence easy to diagnose?","a":"Keep a continuous JFR recording with a bounded size, enable `-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError` with a persistent volume as the target, and export pool, thread, file descriptor and memory metrics per instance. Add an alert on the accumulating resource well before it hurts users. Then the evidence already exists next time, even if someone restarts in a hurry."}],"extra":["**Time to failure:** record uptime at the moment of failure across incidents; a consistent interval after restart (for example about three days) strongly suggests a leak, exhaustion or counter overflow, while random timing suggests an external trigger.","**Kubernetes hides restarts:** liveness probes and OOMKills restart pods automatically, so a problem can persist for months unnoticed; alert on restart rate and last termination reason, not only on availability.","**Continuous JFR:** running with `-XX:StartFlightRecording` and bounded `maxage` or `maxsize` keeps the last minutes before an incident in a ring buffer ready to dump, at low overhead.","**Leading indicators:** alert on the accumulating resource (post-GC live set trend, pool pending, thread or FD count) rather than on the eventual user-facing failure, so the next occurrence is caught hours earlier.","**Blameless postmortem:** record the timeline, the evidence, why detection did not fire earlier, and concrete action items with owners and dates; \"restarted, monitoring\" is not a closure."],"redFlags":["cannot name what state a restart resets","schedules nightly restarts as the permanent fix","writes a postmortem with no follow-up actions or owners"]},{"id":222,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"`kubectl get pods` shows the restart count climbing steadily for one deployment. Which evidence tells you why the containers die?","level":"Basic","tldr":"Read the pod's events and last termination state (reason, exit code, OOMKilled), the previous container's logs, probe failures and resource limits, then check startup timing, dependencies and graceful shutdown.","answer":["Start with `kubectl describe pod`: **Last State** shows the termination **reason** and **exit code**, and **Events** show probe failures, back-off, scheduling and eviction. Exit code 137 means SIGKILL: with reason `OOMKilled` the container exceeded its memory limit, otherwise it was killed after the grace period. Exit code 143 means the JVM handled SIGTERM (typical after a liveness failure), and 1 or another small code means the application exited on its own, usually a startup failure. Then read `kubectl logs --previous`, because the current container's logs begin after the crash and hide the cause.","Map causes to those signals. **OOMKilled**: heap plus metaspace, thread stacks and direct buffers exceed the limit; fix sizing with `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` and a realistic limit. **Liveness failures**: a tight probe timeout during GC pauses or CPU throttling, or a liveness check that includes the database, so one DB blip restarts every pod. **Startup timing**: Spring Boot starting slowly under a low CPU limit exceeds the liveness delay; use a `startupProbe`. **Dependencies and config**: a missing secret, an unreachable database or a failed Flyway migration makes the app exit, ending in `CrashLoopBackOff`.","Finally check **graceful shutdown** and limits: if SIGTERM is not completed within `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`, the kubelet sends SIGKILL, in-flight requests fail and you see 137 without an OOM. Compare requests and limits with real usage, and check whether restarts correlate with deploys, node events or traffic peaks."],"keyPoints":["the last termination state gives the reason and exit code in one place","the `--previous` logs show the final output of the container that died","events reveal probe failures, evictions and scheduling problems","compare memory and startup duration against limits and probe timings"],"examples":[{"title":"Where to look, in order","lang":"bash","code":"NS=orders; POD=orders-api-7b9c-kx2lp\n\nkubectl -n $NS get pods -l app=orders-api            # RESTARTS column, CrashLoopBackOff?\nkubectl -n $NS describe pod $POD                     # Last State, Reason, Exit Code, Events\n\n# Termination details of the previous container as JSON\nkubectl -n $NS get pod $POD \\\n  -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated}'\n# {\"exitCode\":137,\"reason\":\"OOMKilled\",\"startedAt\":\"...\",\"finishedAt\":\"...\"}\n\n# Logs of the container that died, not the fresh one\nkubectl -n $NS logs $POD --previous --tail=200\n\n# Probe failures, kills and back-off for this pod only\nkubectl -n $NS get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=$POD --sort-by=.lastTimestamp","note":"The termination reason and exit code usually decide the branch of the investigation within a minute: OOM, probe kill, application crash or shutdown timeout."},{"title":"Probes, memory and shutdown configured to avoid restart loops","lang":"yaml","code":"spec:\n  terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 45       # longer than preStop plus Spring's shutdown phase\n  containers:\n    - name: orders-api\n      image: registry.internal/orders-api:1.18.3\n      resources:\n        requests: { cpu: \"1\", memory: 1Gi }\n        limits: { memory: 1Gi }\n      env:\n        - name: JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS\n          value: \"-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=70 -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError\"\n      startupProbe:                         # protects slow starts from liveness kills\n        httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/liveness, port: 8080 }\n        periodSeconds: 5\n        failureThreshold: 36                # up to 3 minutes\n      livenessProbe:                        # process health only, no database check\n        httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/liveness, port: 8080 }\n        periodSeconds: 10\n        timeoutSeconds: 3\n        failureThreshold: 3\n      readinessProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/readiness, port: 8080 }\n        periodSeconds: 5\n      lifecycle:\n        preStop:\n          exec: { command: [\"sleep\", \"5\"] }  # let endpoints update before shutdown","note":"Heap is capped at 70% of the container limit to leave room for native memory, the startup probe covers slow boots, and liveness never depends on external systems."}],"followUps":[{"q":"The app needs 90 seconds to start but the liveness probe begins at 30. What happens, and how do you fix it?","a":"The kubelet starts probing liveness while Spring is still initializing, the probe fails `failureThreshold` times, and the container is killed before it ever becomes ready, which ends in CrashLoopBackOff. Add a `startupProbe` with enough budget (`failureThreshold` times `periodSeconds` above the worst observed startup time); liveness and readiness probes do not run until it succeeds. Then work on startup time itself, for example with lazy initialization or fewer eager connections."}],"extra":["**Back-off timing:** the kubelet restart delay doubles from 10 s up to a 5 minute cap and resets after the container runs cleanly for 10 minutes, so `CrashLoopBackOff` pods look idle between attempts.","**Alert on it:** kube-state-metrics exposes `kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total` and `kube_pod_container_status_last_terminated_reason`, so you can alert on restart rate per reason instead of finding restarts by accident.","**Java OOM vs kernel OOM:** a heap `OutOfMemoryError` appears in the logs and leaves the JVM in an undefined state, so `-XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError` makes it exit and restart cleanly; a kernel OOMKill leaves no Java stack trace at all.","**Probes on the right port:** if Actuator runs on a separate management port, `management.endpoint.health.probes.add-additional-paths=true` also serves `/livez` and `/readyz` on the main port, so probes reflect the server that actually handles traffic.","**Graceful shutdown in Spring Boot:** it is on by default since 3.4 and bounded by `spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase` (30 s by default), so `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` must exceed that plus any `preStop` sleep."],"redFlags":["assumes every restart is an application crash rather than the kubelet acting on a probe","raises probe timeouts without understanding why they failed","does not know what exit codes 137 and 143 mean"]},{"id":223,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"At 14:05 the P95 of your orders API triples. Walk through the first fifteen minutes of your response.","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Confirm scope and start time, correlate with deploys, config and traffic changes, read RED metrics per endpoint and instance, use traces to find the slow span or dependency, check saturation, mitigate first, then find root cause.","answer":["First **confirm and scope**: which endpoints, which percentile (averages hide tail problems), since when, all instances or one pod, one region or tenant, and whether the server sees it or only a client reports it. Then line it up against a **change timeline**: deploys, config and feature flag changes, traffic shape (a new client, a batch job, a retry storm), dependency incidents, node pool or database maintenance.","Next read **RED metrics** (rate, errors, duration) per endpoint and per instance, and **USE** metrics (utilization, saturation, errors) for resources: CPU throttling, GC pause time, Tomcat or executor queueing, HikariCP pending connections and acquire time, database and downstream latency. Pull **traces** of slow requests: either one span grew (a query, a dependency) or there is an unexplained gap, which usually means waiting on a pool, a lock, GC or CPU. Separate **saturation** (queueing because something is full) from **errors** (timeouts and retries that inflate latency).","**Mitigate before you root cause**: roll back the suspect deploy, disable the flag, shed or rate limit the abusive traffic, scale the saturated tier. Change one thing at a time and watch the metric respond, and capture evidence (thread dumps, slow traces, pool stats) before any restart, because a restart destroys state. Once users are safe, find the root cause and add whatever alert or dashboard would have shortened the investigation."],"keyPoints":["establish blast radius first: which endpoints, instances, regions and tenants","line up the start time with deploys, flag flips, config changes and traffic shifts","traces show which span or dependency absorbed the extra time","restore service with rollback or traffic shifting before deep root-cause work"],"examples":[{"title":"Investigation command sequence on Kubernetes and Spring Boot","lang":"bash","code":"# 1. Scope: what changed, and is it every pod or one?\nkubectl -n payments rollout history deploy/payments-api\nkubectl -n payments get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -20\nkubectl -n payments top pods -l app=payments-api\n\n# 2. Saturation inside the JVM (Spring Boot Actuator)\ncurl -s localhost:8080/actuator/metrics/hikaricp.connections.pending | jq '.measurements'\ncurl -s localhost:8080/actuator/metrics/jvm.gc.pause | jq '.measurements'\ncurl -s 'localhost:8080/actuator/metrics/http.server.requests?tag=uri:/api/payments' | jq '.measurements'\n\n# 3. Thread state on a slow pod: blocked on the pool, a lock, or a remote call?\nkubectl -n payments exec payments-api-7d9f-abcde -- jcmd 1 Thread.print > threads-1.txt\nsleep 10\nkubectl -n payments exec payments-api-7d9f-abcde -- jcmd 1 Thread.print > threads-2.txt\n\n# 4. If a deploy correlates, mitigate first and keep investigating afterwards\nkubectl -n payments rollout undo deploy/payments-api","note":"Evidence (events, pool metrics, two thread dumps) is captured before the rollback, so the root cause analysis still has data after users are safe."},{"title":"Reading a slow trace: the gap is the clue","lang":"text","code":"POST /api/payments    total 2.41 s    (same endpoint last week: 180 ms)\npayments-api  handler                            2.41 s\n  (no child span)                                2.05 s   <== unexplained: pool wait, lock, GC or CPU throttling\n  SELECT payment ... (JDBC)                      0.09 s\n  POST fraud-service /score                      0.21 s\n\nNext checks for the gap:\n  hikaricp.connections.pending > 0 and acquire time up  -> pool exhaustion\n  jvm.gc.pause spikes at the same timestamps            -> GC\n  container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total rising      -> CPU limit","note":"When no child span explains the time, the request was waiting for a resource inside the service, which is a saturation problem rather than slow code or a slow dependency."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you tell whether a dependency or your own service is responsible?","a":"Compare the client-side timing of the outbound call with the dependency's own server-side latency: if both rose, the dependency is slow; if only your side rose, the time is going into pool waits, DNS, TLS handshakes or thread starvation. Pool metrics such as HikariCP's `hikaricp_connections_pending` and gaps inside client spans make this visible. Also check whether every caller of that dependency is affected or only you."},{"q":"When is rollback the right first move?","a":"When the latency change lines up with a deploy or config change and rolling back is safe and fast, roll back before diagnosing, because the previous version is a known good state. It is not safe if the release included a database migration the old code cannot handle, which is why migrations must stay backward compatible. Keep the bad build's artifacts and telemetry so the investigation can continue afterward."}],"extra":["**Little's law:** concurrency equals throughput times latency, so if latency doubles at constant traffic the in-flight requests double, which can exhaust thread and connection pools and turn one slow dependency into a full outage.","**Latency at the timeout value:** a cluster of requests at exactly 1 s or 2 s usually means a timeout followed by a retry somewhere; look for the configured timeout, not for slow code.","**Coordinated omission:** load generators and clients that wait for each response before sending the next request under-report tail latency, so trust server histograms and gateway timings over client averages.","**Virtual threads (Java 21):** they remove the servlet thread pool as a queueing point, so saturation moves to the HikariCP pool or the downstream; on JDK 21 detect carrier pinning in `synchronized` blocks with the JFR `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` event (JEP 491 removed most pinning in JDK 24).","**Compare with a baseline:** the same hour last week, or a healthy canary or region, often shows the difference faster than absolute numbers."],"redFlags":["jumps to a favorite theory before checking what changed","ignores saturation signals such as pool wait time and thread queueing","keeps debugging live while customers wait, instead of mitigating first"]},{"id":224,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"A service's container memory creeps up for days until it is OOMKilled. How do you find out what is accumulating?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"First determine whether the heap live set, native memory or a cache is growing, confirm with GC and NMT data, then take comparable heap dumps hours apart and compare dominators and paths to GC roots.","answer":["Start by identifying **which memory** grows. Container working set or RSS includes Java heap, metaspace, code cache, thread stacks, direct and mapped buffers and malloc arenas. Compare `jvm.memory.used` per pool and the **live set after GC** (old generation after a full or mixed collection, or `jvm.gc.live.data.size`) with the container metric. A heap sawtooth is normal; a post-GC floor that rises every day is a heap leak. A flat heap floor with rising RSS points to **native memory**: enable Native Memory Tracking and diff summaries, and check direct buffers, thread count and metaspace (class loader leaks from dynamic proxies or scripting).","For a heap problem, confirm the trend in GC logs (`-Xlog:gc*`), then take **comparable heap dumps**: same instance, similar load, a few hours apart, live objects only. One dump shows what is big, not what is growing; two or three show the delta. In Eclipse MAT compare the histograms, open the **dominator tree** for the largest retained sizes, and follow **paths to GC roots** (excluding weak and soft references) to the static field, cache, `ThreadLocal`, listener list or meter registry holding the objects.","Typical culprits are unbounded maps used as caches, per-request `ThreadLocal` values on pooled threads, Micrometer meters with unbounded tags, a Hibernate persistence context in a long batch, or listeners never deregistered. Raising `-Xmx` only delays the restart and makes each dump bigger. A controlled rolling restart is fine as a mitigation while you investigate, not as the fix."],"keyPoints":["decide first whether the growth is Java heap, native memory or off-heap buffers","the post-GC live set, not raw heap usage, shows real retention","two heap dumps taken hours apart reveal which objects keep growing","the path to GC roots names the field holding the memory"],"examples":[{"title":"Separating heap, native and live-set growth with jcmd","lang":"bash","code":"POD=orders-api-6c8d9-x2k4p\n\n# Which one grows: container memory or the heap?\nkubectl exec $POD -- cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.current      # cgroup v2\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 GC.heap_info\n\n# Native memory (JVM started with -XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary)\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 VM.native_memory baseline\n# ... several hours of normal traffic later ...\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 VM.native_memory summary.diff\n\n# Cheap trend: live-object histogram (forces a full GC), repeated later, then diff\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 GC.class_histogram > histo-morning.txt\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 GC.class_histogram > histo-evening.txt\n\n# Comparable heap dumps of live objects; take the pod out of rotation first\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 GC.heap_dump /dumps/orders-morning.hprof\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 GC.heap_dump /dumps/orders-evening.hprof","note":"`summary.diff` shows which native category (threads, class, internal, other) grew since the baseline; the two histograms or dumps turn \"what is big\" into \"what is growing\"."},{"title":"A classic leak and its bounded replacement","lang":"java","code":"@Component\npublic class FxRateService {\n\n    // BAD: static map keyed by pair and day, gains new entries forever\n    private static final Map<String, BigDecimal> RATES = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    // GOOD: bounded, expiring and observable, so its size plateaus\n    private final Cache<String, BigDecimal> rates = Caffeine.newBuilder()\n            .maximumSize(50_000)\n            .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofHours(12))\n            .recordStats()\n            .build();\n\n    private final FxClient client;\n\n    public FxRateService(FxClient client, MeterRegistry registry) {\n        this.client = client;\n        CaffeineCacheMetrics.monitor(registry, rates, \"fx-rates\");\n    }\n\n    public BigDecimal rate(String pair, LocalDate day) {\n        return rates.get(pair + \":\" + day, key -> client.fetch(pair, day));\n    }\n}","note":"In a heap dump the static `ConcurrentHashMap` shows up in the dominator tree with a GC root path through a class; the Caffeine cache exposes size, hit ratio and evictions so you can prove it is bounded."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Heap looks flat but container memory keeps rising. What next?","a":"The growth is outside the Java heap, so start the JVM with `-XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary`, record `jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory baseline`, and later run `jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary.diff` to see whether threads, metaspace, code cache or direct buffers grow. If NMT stays flat too, suspect allocations by native libraries or glibc malloc arena fragmentation; setting `MALLOC_ARENA_MAX` or switching to jemalloc often confirms it. The `BufferPool` MXBeans also show direct `ByteBuffer` usage from Netty or NIO."}],"extra":["**Low overhead leak profiling:** JFR's `OldObjectSample` event, dumped with `path-to-gc-roots=true`, samples long-lived objects with their allocation stack and reference chain, cheap enough for production when a full heap dump is too disruptive.","**Dumps are disruptive and sensitive:** a heap dump pauses the JVM for seconds and contains customer data, so remove the pod from rotation first, write to a volume with enough space, and store the file encrypted with restricted access.","**Native surprises:** glibc malloc arenas can inflate RSS with many threads (`MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2` is a common mitigation), and Netty or NIO direct buffers are capped by `-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize`, not by `-Xmx`.","**Container sizing:** with `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` around 70 to 75 there must still be room for non-heap memory; if native growth crosses the container limit the kernel OOMKills the pod without any `OutOfMemoryError` in the logs.","**Automatic capture:** run with `-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=/dumps` on a persistent volume, so the next failure leaves evidence instead of a clean restart."],"redFlags":["reads total container RSS and assumes it is all heap","never turns on Native Memory Tracking, so off-heap growth (class metadata, per-thread memory, NIO buffers) stays invisible","takes a heap dump in production without considering the pause or the customer data inside it"]},{"id":225,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"Your PostgreSQL primary is pinned near full CPU and API latency is climbing. What do you look at first?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Find which queries consume the CPU (pg_stat_statements by total time, pg_stat_activity right now), check plan regressions, stale statistics, locks, connection storms and recent deploy or traffic changes, and stop the expensive work before scaling.","answer":["First establish **what changed and when**: a deploy, a new batch or reporting job, a traffic spike, a cache outage, a data migration or bulk load. Then find **where the CPU goes**: `pg_stat_statements` ordered by `total_exec_time` (not mean time) shows which statements consume the most in aggregate, with calls, rows and buffers touched, while `pg_stat_activity` shows what runs right now and whether sessions are on CPU (`wait_event` is null) or waiting on locks or I/O.","For the top statements look for a **plan change** with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`: a sequential scan or a hash join over millions of rows where an index scan used to run, caused by **stale statistics** after a bulk load, a shifted data distribution, a dropped index or a prepared statement switching to a generic plan. Compare rows scanned with rows returned per call. Also check **locks** (`pg_blocking_pids`) and the **connection count**: hundreds of active sessions on a 16 core server create contention and context switching, so raising pool sizes makes it worse.","**Stop the expensive work first**: cancel runaway queries, pause the batch or reporting job, disable the feature flag, rate limit the endpoint, move analytics to a replica. Then fix the cause: `ANALYZE`, rewrite the query or add a targeted index validated with `EXPLAIN`, remove N+1 access in code. Scaling the instance up is a last resort: it usually needs a failover, costs money and does not fix a query whose cost grows with the data."],"keyPoints":["rank statements by total execution time, since many cheap calls can outweigh one slow query","compare current plans with known good ones to catch a regression after an analyze or a deploy","look at what is running right now, including lock waits and connection counts","relieve the load at its source before buying a bigger instance"],"examples":[{"title":"Finding the CPU consumers in PostgreSQL","lang":"sql","code":"-- Top statements by total execution time (PostgreSQL 13+ column names)\nSELECT queryid,\n       calls,\n       round(total_exec_time::numeric)       AS total_ms,\n       round(mean_exec_time::numeric, 2)     AS mean_ms,\n       rows / NULLIF(calls, 0)               AS rows_per_call,\n       shared_blks_hit + shared_blks_read    AS blocks_touched,\n       left(query, 100)                      AS query\nFROM pg_stat_statements\nORDER BY total_exec_time DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n\n-- What runs right now: on CPU (wait_event IS NULL) or blocked?\nSELECT pid, now() - query_start AS runtime, state, wait_event_type, wait_event,\n       pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by, left(query, 80) AS query\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE state <> 'idle' AND backend_type = 'client backend'\nORDER BY runtime DESC;\n\n-- Stale statistics or unexpected sequential scans?\nSELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_mod_since_analyze, last_autoanalyze, seq_scan, idx_scan\nFROM pg_stat_user_tables\nORDER BY n_mod_since_analyze DESC\nLIMIT 10;","note":"Sorting by total time finds both the slow statement and the fast one executed a million times; `blocks_touched` divided by rows returned reveals scans that read far more than they return."},{"title":"Stopping the bleeding, then checking the plan","lang":"sql","code":"-- Cancel long running reporting queries (cancel first, terminate only if needed)\nSELECT pg_cancel_backend(pid)\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE usename = 'reporting'\n  AND state = 'active'\n  AND now() - query_start > interval '2 minutes';\n\n-- Guardrail for new sessions of that role\nALTER ROLE reporting SET statement_timeout = '60s';\n\n-- Plan check with real row counts and buffer usage (this executes the query)\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT id, amount, created_at\nFROM payment\nWHERE merchant_id = 4711 AND status = 'PENDING'\nORDER BY created_at\nLIMIT 50;\n\n-- Estimates far from actual rows: refresh statistics\nANALYZE payment;","note":"Mitigation comes before optimization: the role-level timeout keeps one reporting tool from saturating the primary again while you fix the underlying plan."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How can stale statistics cause a sudden CPU spike?","a":"The planner estimates row counts from table statistics; if a table grew or its data distribution shifted since the last `ANALYZE`, it may pick a nested loop or sequential scan that suited small numbers and is now disastrous. The symptom is a query whose `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE)` shows estimated rows far from actual rows. Running `ANALYZE` on the table, and tuning autovacuum's analyze thresholds for large tables, usually restores the good plan."},{"q":"Which PostgreSQL views would you open during the incident?","a":"`pg_stat_activity` for what is running now, its wait events and how long each query has been active; `pg_stat_statements` for cumulative cost per normalized query; and `pg_locks` with `pg_blocking_pids()` for blocking chains. `pg_stat_user_tables` shows sequential scan counts and dead tuples, which hint at missing indexes or vacuum trouble."}],"extra":["**Generic plan trap:** PostgreSQL uses custom plans for the first five executions of a prepared statement and may then switch to a cached generic plan that ignores skewed parameter values; pgJDBC switches to server-side prepared statements after `prepareThreshold` (default 5) uses, so a plan can go bad minutes after a deploy, and `plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan` is a targeted fix.","**auto_explain:** setting `auto_explain.log_min_duration` logs plans of slow statements as they happen, which catches intermittent plan flips you cannot reproduce later.","**Windowed statistics:** `pg_stat_statements` is cumulative since the last reset, so snapshot it every few minutes (or call `pg_stat_statements_reset()` when the investigation starts) to see today's top consumers rather than last month's.","**Pool sizing:** HikariCP's guidance is a small pool, roughly cores times two plus effective spindles on the database, shared across all application instances; 40 pods with 20 connections each is 800 sessions, far beyond what a 16 core server runs efficiently.","**Other CPU sinks:** autovacuum on heavily updated tables, JIT compilation for complex queries (the `jit` setting), and bloated tables or indexes that make every scan touch more pages."],"redFlags":["only looks at the slowest single query and misses the high-frequency one","kills sessions at random without knowing which client they belong to","upgrades the instance size and never finds the regression"]},{"id":226,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"Three of four replicas respond in 50 ms, but one takes 2 seconds. What would you suspect?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"When just one replica lags, look for something local to it: a throttled container or noisy neighbor node, GC trouble, a broken connection in its pool, a cold cache, skewed traffic, disk or network faults, blocked threads or a config mismatch; then diff its metrics against healthy peers.","answer":["If one replica is slow while the others run the same code on the same traffic mix, look for something **local**. On the node side: a noisy neighbor, CPU steal, **CFS throttling** from a tight CPU limit, memory pressure, a slow or full local disk, a flaky NIC, a full conntrack table, DNS trouble, or simply a node in a different availability zone from the database. On the pod side: **GC pressure** because its heap is fuller, a **stuck or deadlocked** thread pool, a pooled **connection** to a degraded database replica or a stale DNS entry, a **cold cache** or unwarmed JIT after a recent restart, or **version and config drift** (a different image digest, ConfigMap or feature flag).","**Uneven load** is the other big category: long-lived HTTP/2 or gRPC connections pinned to one pod by L4 load balancing, sticky sessions, a hot Kafka partition assigned to that consumer, or one heavy tenant routed there.","Investigate by **comparing pod-level signals** side by side: latency and error rate by `pod`, request rate by `pod` (is it simply getting more?), CPU usage and throttling, GC pause time, heap after GC, thread and connection pool metrics, uptime and restarts, node and zone, image digest. Capture a thread dump and a short JFR recording from the slow pod before touching it, then take it out of rotation, or cordon the node if the problem follows the node. Restarting alone erases the evidence and the problem often returns."],"keyPoints":["identical code on every replica points to the environment, not the logic","node-level contention and CPU quota throttling are common culprits","per-pod state such as one broken pooled connection or a cold cache can differ","side-by-side pod metrics isolate the outlier quickly"],"examples":[{"title":"Comparing the slow pod with its siblings","lang":"bash","code":"NS=credit; SLOW=credit-api-5f7c-q8z2m\n\n# Same image, same node type? Where does each replica run?\nCOLS='POD:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName,IMAGE:.status.containerStatuses[0].imageID,RESTARTS:.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount'\nkubectl -n $NS get pods -l app=credit-api -o custom-columns=\"$COLS\"\n\n# Node pressure and neighbors\nNODE=$(kubectl -n $NS get pod $SLOW -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}')\nkubectl describe node \"$NODE\" | grep -A8 -E \"Conditions|Allocated resources\"\nkubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu | head -15\n\n# CPU throttling from inside the container (cgroup v2)\nkubectl -n $NS exec $SLOW -- cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat\n\n# JVM view on the slow pod only: heap, GC and blocked threads\nkubectl -n $NS exec $SLOW -- jcmd 1 GC.heap_info\nkubectl -n $NS exec $SLOW -- jcmd 1 Thread.print > slow-threads.txt\ngrep -c \"java.lang.Thread.State: BLOCKED\" slow-threads.txt","note":"The goal is a diff between the slow pod and a healthy one: image digest, node, throttling counters and thread states usually expose the local cause quickly."},{"title":"Alerting on per-pod CPU throttling","lang":"yaml","code":"groups:\n  - name: credit-api-pods\n    rules:\n      - alert: CreditApiPodCpuThrottled\n        expr: |\n          sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total{namespace=\"credit\", container=\"credit-api\"}[5m]))\n          /\n          sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_periods_total{namespace=\"credit\", container=\"credit-api\"}[5m]))\n          > 0.25\n        for: 10m\n        labels:\n          severity: ticket\n          team: credit\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"{{ $labels.pod }} is throttled in more than 25% of CFS periods\"\n          runbook_url: \"https://runbooks.internal/credit-api/cpu-throttling\"","note":"Grouping by `pod` is the point: an aggregate over all replicas hides the one pod that is throttled while the others are fine."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you take a misbehaving pod out of traffic without losing evidence?","a":"Remove it from the Service's endpoints instead of deleting it: change a label so the selectors no longer match (the ReplicaSet then creates a replacement), or make its readiness fail through an admin endpoint. The pod keeps running, so you can take thread dumps, heap histograms and JFR recordings and inspect its node without time pressure. Delete it once the evidence is saved."},{"q":"Why can one pod receive more load than its siblings?","a":"Long-lived connections are the usual reason: HTTP/2 or gRPC clients and keep-alive pools stick to the pods that existed when they connected, so one pod can keep an unfair share. Session affinity, consistent hashing on a hot key, or a Kafka partition with a busy key cause the same skew. Request-level balancing (a mesh or client-side load balancing) and a maximum connection age spread the load out."}],"extra":["**Tail from one pod:** with round-robin across 10 replicas, one bad pod affects about 10% of requests, which shows up as a normal P50 and a terrible P99 in the aggregate; always break latency down by `pod` before blaming the code.","**JVM CPU awareness:** the JVM sizes GC threads, JIT compiler threads and `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()` from the container CPU limit, and parallel GC phases can burn a whole quota period in milliseconds; many teams remove CPU limits while keeping requests, or set `-XX:ActiveProcessorCount` explicitly.","**Quarantine instead of kill:** removing the Service selector label from the pod makes the ReplicaSet start a replacement while the sick pod stays alive, out of the endpoints, for debugging.","**Outlier ejection:** a service mesh or proxy with outlier detection (Envoy, Istio `outlierDetection`) automatically ejects a replica returning consecutive errors, limiting blast radius while you investigate.","**Node signals:** node-exporter's CPU `steal` mode, pressure stall information (PSI) and kubelet eviction events reveal node trouble that pod-level metrics hide."],"redFlags":["looks only at service-wide averages, where one bad pod disappears","deletes the pod before capturing a thread dump or its node details","never checks which node the pod landed on"]},{"id":227,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"Your payment provider's API starts taking 30 seconds per call. How do you keep your own service healthy?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Fail fast and contain it: strict timeouts within an overall deadline, few retries with backoff for idempotent calls only, a circuit breaker, a bulkhead capping concurrency, a degraded fallback, and per-dependency metrics.","answer":["A slow dependency is more dangerous than a dead one, because every waiting request holds a thread, a pooled HTTP connection and sometimes a database connection. By Little's law, if the API goes from 100 ms to 10 s at the same traffic, in-flight calls grow a hundredfold and your service falls over as well. **Bound the damage**: set **connect and read timeouts** on every client (the JDK `HttpClient` and several request factories wait indefinitely by default) and derive them from an overall **deadline** for the inbound request, so a call never outlives its caller's own timeout.","**Retry** only transient failures of idempotent operations, at one layer, with one or two extra attempts, exponential backoff and jitter; retrying a slow service synchronously multiplies its load exactly when it is struggling. A **circuit breaker** (Resilience4j, using both failure rate and slow-call rate) opens after sustained problems so calls fail immediately and the dependency gets room to recover, then probes in half-open state. A **bulkhead** (semaphore or dedicated pool) caps concurrent calls to that dependency so it can only consume its own slice of capacity, which still matters with Java 21 virtual threads because connection pools and memory remain finite.","Choose the **fallback** per use case: cached data, a default, a degraded response, or queue the work and answer asynchronously (a credit application goes to manual review instead of failing). Never hold a database transaction open around the remote call. Emit **metrics** per dependency: latency histogram, timeouts, breaker state, bulkhead rejections and fallback rate."],"keyPoints":["a short timeout per attempt and an overall deadline stop threads from piling up","a circuit breaker stops calling a dependency that is clearly failing","a dedicated bulkhead keeps the slow integration from starving other endpoints","a degraded response or a queued retry beats a hanging request"],"examples":[{"title":"Resilience4j configuration for a slow credit bureau","lang":"yaml","code":"resilience4j:\n  circuitbreaker:\n    instances:\n      creditBureau:\n        sliding-window-type: COUNT_BASED\n        sliding-window-size: 50\n        minimum-number-of-calls: 20\n        failure-rate-threshold: 50\n        slow-call-duration-threshold: 500ms\n        slow-call-rate-threshold: 60\n        wait-duration-in-open-state: 20s\n        permitted-number-of-calls-in-half-open-state: 5\n  retry:\n    instances:\n      creditBureau:\n        max-attempts: 2                    # one retry only\n        wait-duration: 200ms\n        enable-exponential-backoff: true\n        exponential-backoff-multiplier: 2\n        retry-exceptions:\n          - org.springframework.web.client.ResourceAccessException\n          - org.springframework.web.client.HttpServerErrorException$ServiceUnavailable\n  bulkhead:\n    instances:\n      creditBureau:\n        max-concurrent-calls: 30           # this dependency can never hold more than 30 threads\n        max-wait-duration: 0ms             # reject immediately instead of queueing","note":"The breaker opens on slowness, not only on errors, and the bulkhead rejects instantly when full, so the bureau can never consume more than a fixed share of your capacity."},{"title":"Client with timeouts, retry, breaker, bulkhead and fallback","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class CreditBureauClient {\n\n    private final RestClient restClient;\n\n    public CreditBureauClient(RestClient.Builder builder) {\n        var factory = new JdkClientHttpRequestFactory(\n                HttpClient.newBuilder().connectTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(300)).build());\n        factory.setReadTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(800));\n        this.restClient = builder.baseUrl(\"https://bureau.example.com\")\n                .requestFactory(factory)\n                .build();\n    }\n\n    // Fallback on the outermost aspect (Retry), so inner failures still reach the retry logic\n    @Retry(name = \"creditBureau\", fallbackMethod = \"manualReview\")\n    @CircuitBreaker(name = \"creditBureau\")\n    @Bulkhead(name = \"creditBureau\")\n    public BureauScore score(String applicationId, String customerRef) {\n        return restClient.get()\n                .uri(\"/v2/scores/{ref}\", customerRef)\n                .retrieve()\n                .body(BureauScore.class);\n    }\n\n    private BureauScore manualReview(String applicationId, String customerRef, Throwable cause) {\n        // Degraded answer: the decision engine routes the application to manual review\n        return BureauScore.unavailable(applicationId);\n    }\n}","note":"Worst case is bounded: 300 ms connect plus 800 ms read, at most two attempts, and an open breaker or full bulkhead goes straight to the fallback without touching the network."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you size a bulkhead for this dependency?","a":"Use Little's law: concurrent calls equal request rate times latency, so 20 calls per second at a normal 300 ms needs about 6 in flight; allow headroom, say 10 to 15, and reject or queue beyond that. When the provider slows to 30 seconds, the bulkhead fills and extra requests fail fast instead of consuming every servlet thread. Resilience4j's `Bulkhead` or `ThreadPoolBulkhead`, or a dedicated connection pool with a small maximum size, enforces it."},{"q":"What should callers see while the circuit is open?","a":"Something honest and recoverable: for a payment, accept the order as pending and confirm asynchronously, or say payment is temporarily unavailable with a retry option, rather than showing a timeout. Read paths can serve cached or partial data. The response should be fast, API clients should get a clear status such as 503 with `Retry-After`, and the fallback itself must never call the failing dependency."}],"extra":["**Retry amplification:** three layers each making three attempts turn one user request into up to 27 calls to the bottom dependency, so retry at one layer only and consider a retry budget (for example retries capped at 10% of normal traffic).","**Pool acquire timeouts count too:** set a connection request timeout on pooled HTTP clients and keep HikariCP's `connectionTimeout` short, otherwise requests queue invisibly for a connection before the read timeout even starts.","**Aspect order:** Resilience4j's Spring Boot default order is Retry around CircuitBreaker around RateLimiter around TimeLimiter around Bulkhead, so a fallback on an inner annotation swallows the exception and Retry never retries.","**Hedged requests:** for idempotent reads against replicated backends, sending a second request after roughly the P95 latency and taking the first answer cuts tail latency, at the price of extra load.","**Respect backpressure:** honor `Retry-After` on 429 and 503, and apply load shedding or adaptive concurrency on your own inbound side so you degrade gracefully instead of queueing."],"redFlags":["retries non-idempotent payment calls without an idempotency key","shares one connection pool and thread pool across all dependencies","has no per-dependency latency metric, so the cause is found late"]},{"id":228,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"Consumer lag on an order-events topic has climbed all day without ever recovering. Where would you start looking?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Compare ingress rate with processing rate and watch the oldest message's age, then check consumer errors and rebalances, downstream saturation, partition or queue skew, poison messages and whether capacity really exceeds peak load.","answer":["Depth alone says little: a million messages may be two minutes of normal work or two days. First compare the **ingress rate** with the **processing rate** (acks or committed offsets per second): a backlog only grows while ingress exceeds throughput, so find out whether producers spiked (a campaign, a replay, an upstream batch) or consumption dropped. Then track the **age of the oldest message**, or lag expressed in time, which is what the business feels and what you should alert on.","If throughput dropped, inspect the consumers: **error and retry rates**, redeliveries, Kafka **rebalances** (processing longer than `max.poll.interval.ms` gets a consumer evicted and its batch redone), how many consumers are alive, and their CPU, GC and time per message. Very often the real bottleneck is **downstream saturation**: the database, an external API or a lock every message needs, and adding consumers then only adds contention.","Check **distribution**: lag concentrated on one partition means a hot key or a stuck consumer, and Kafka cannot use more consumers than partitions in a group; in RabbitMQ a large prefetch lets one slow consumer hoard messages. Look for **poison messages** that fail forever and block a partition or cycle through redelivery. Finally check **capacity**: per-consumer throughput times consumer count must exceed peak ingress with headroom, and drain time is backlog divided by (consume rate minus ingress rate). Scale consumers only as far as the downstream can absorb."],"keyPoints":["growing lag means arrivals outpace completions, so measure both","the oldest unprocessed event shows how far behind the business really is","rebalances, errors and retries can keep consumers busy while finishing little","uneven partitions or a slow downstream cap throughput however many consumers run"],"examples":[{"title":"Where is the lag, and is it moving?","lang":"bash","code":"# Kafka: lag per partition for the consumer group\nkafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server kafka:9092 \\\n  --describe --group payment-settlement\n\n# GROUP               TOPIC     PARTITION  CURRENT-OFFSET  LOG-END-OFFSET  LAG      CONSUMER-ID\n# payment-settlement  payments  0          1840211         1840390         179      consumer-1\n# payment-settlement  payments  7          922004          1391887         469883   consumer-4\n\n# Run it again a minute later: if CURRENT-OFFSET on partition 7 has not moved,\n# suspect a poison record or a stuck consumer; if it moves slowly, suspect a hot key.\n\n# RabbitMQ: ready vs unacknowledged messages and consumers per queue\nrabbitmqctl list_queues name messages_ready messages_unacknowledged consumers","note":"Lag concentrated on one partition is a distribution or poison problem, not a capacity problem, and adding consumers will not help it."},{"title":"Bounded retries and a dead-letter topic in Spring Kafka","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\npublic class SettlementConsumerConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    DefaultErrorHandler settlementErrorHandler(KafkaTemplate<Object, Object> template) {\n        // After retries are exhausted, publish the record to payments.DLT and move on\n        var recoverer = new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(template);\n\n        var backOff = new ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries(3);\n        backOff.setInitialInterval(500);\n        backOff.setMultiplier(2.0);\n\n        var handler = new DefaultErrorHandler(recoverer, backOff);\n        // Retrying cannot fix bad data: send these to the DLT immediately\n        handler.addNotRetryableExceptions(InvalidSettlementException.class);\n        return handler;\n    }\n}","note":"Spring Boot wires a single `CommonErrorHandler` bean into the auto-configured listener container factory. Keep total backoff well below `max.poll.interval.ms`, or use `@RetryableTopic` for non-blocking retries."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why might adding consumers not reduce lag on a Kafka topic?","a":"A consumer group has at most one active consumer per partition, so instances beyond the partition count sit idle. Even below that, a hot key can concentrate load in one partition, and if the real limit is the database or an external API, more consumers only add contention there. Check per-partition lag and downstream saturation before scaling."},{"q":"How do frequent rebalances hurt throughput?","a":"During a rebalance consumers stop fetching while partitions are reassigned, and with the eager protocol every member of the group pauses. If processing a batch takes longer than `max.poll.interval.ms`, the consumer is removed, its partitions move, uncommitted work is redone and the cycle repeats. Smaller `max.poll.records`, faster processing, static membership and the cooperative sticky assignor reduce the damage."}],"extra":["**Kafka poll defaults:** `max.poll.records` defaults to 500 and `max.poll.interval.ms` to 5 minutes; if 500 records against a slow downstream exceed the interval, the group rebalances repeatedly and nothing commits, which looks like a stuck backlog with busy consumers.","**Lag in time:** tools such as Burrow or kafka-lag-exporter, or SQS `ApproximateAgeOfOldestMessage`, turn offsets into seconds, which enables SLOs like \"settlement events processed within 5 minutes\".","**Autoscaling guard:** KEDA can scale consumers on lag, but set a maximum replica count derived from downstream capacity such as database connections, or the autoscaler will overload your own database.","**Rebalance hygiene:** the cooperative sticky assignor and static membership (`group.instance.id`) reduce stop-the-world rebalances during rolling deploys, which otherwise pause consumption across the whole group.","**RabbitMQ specifics:** quorum queues support a `delivery-limit` that dead-letters a message after repeated redeliveries, and Spring AMQP's default prefetch of 250 can let one slow consumer sit on work others could process."],"redFlags":["adds partitions mid-incident without considering key ordering","never checks for repeated rebalances","assumes the backlog is temporary without comparing peak inflow to capacity"]},{"id":229,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"Error rates spike right after a deployment that included a database migration. What do you do?","level":"Advanced","tldr":"Confirm the spike lines up with the new version or the migration, stop the rollout and check the schema's state. If the schema change is backward compatible, roll back the application and keep the schema; if it is destructive, do not run down scripts blindly but roll forward a fix or switch the new path off with a flag.","answer":["Confirm and contain first. Did errors start when new pods took traffic, or when the migration ran, possibly minutes earlier in a pipeline step? Split errors by version: if only new pods fail with `column does not exist` or mapping errors, code and schema disagree; if all pods time out, suspect locks or load from the migration itself. Then pause the rollout or abort the canary, open an incident with one person coordinating and one posting status updates, and do not push an untested hotfix. The deciding question: can the previous version run on the schema as it is now?","If the migration was additive (a new nullable column, table or index), the previous version runs on it, so roll back the application and leave the schema; that fast path exists only because the migration was backward compatible. If the migration is still running, cancel it: in PostgreSQL an `ALTER TABLE` queued for its exclusive lock blocks every later query on the table, and the cancelled change rolls back cleanly. If it was destructive (a dropped or renamed column, a type change, a data rewrite), the old version breaks too, and a down script cannot restore dropped data or undo later writes. Roll forward a small compatibility fix (rename back, add a default, relax a constraint) or switch the new path off with a feature flag; a point-in-time restore is a business decision, never a reflex.","The postmortem lesson is nearly always that one deploy coupled an incompatible schema change with the code that needed it. Use expand/contract (parallel change): add new structures, deploy code that writes both shapes, backfill in batches, switch reads, and drop the old structure in a later release after the rollback window, so every migration works with versions N and N-1. Run migrations as their own pipeline step with `lock_timeout`, rehearse them on production-sized data, lint for destructive DDL in CI, and ship risky behavior behind flags so release is decoupled from deploy. Write a blameless postmortem."],"keyPoints":["confirm the correlation, stop the rollout and check the schema state first","roll back the app only when the schema is backward compatible; never reverse destructive migrations blindly","expand/contract migrations and feature flags keep rollback safe"],"examples":[{"title":"A breaking migration vs expand/contract across releases (PostgreSQL)","lang":"sql","code":"-- BAD: one release renames a column and tightens a constraint\nALTER TABLE payments RENAME COLUMN status TO payment_status;  -- old pods fail at once\nALTER TABLE payments ALTER COLUMN provider_ref SET NOT NULL;  -- scans under exclusive lock\n-- Rolling back the app does not help: the old code still selects \"status\".\n\n-- GOOD: expand, migrate, contract\n-- Release N (expand): additive only; N writes both columns and still reads status\nSET lock_timeout = '3s';      -- fail fast instead of queueing every query behind the DDL\nALTER TABLE payments ADD COLUMN payment_status text;\n\n-- After N is fully rolled out: backfill in batches until 0 rows\nUPDATE payments SET payment_status = status\nWHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM payments WHERE payment_status IS NULL LIMIT 5000);\n\n-- Release N+1: reads payment_status, still writes both, so rollback to N is safe\n-- Release N+2: stops writing status (drop its NOT NULL first, if any)\n-- Release N+3 (contract), after the rollback window has passed:\nALTER TABLE payments DROP COLUMN status;","note":"Every step leaves a schema that both the current and the previous version can use, so `rollout undo` is always safe. Renames are the classic trap: `RENAME COLUMN` is instant in PostgreSQL yet breaks every old pod still running."},{"title":"First minutes of the incident: confirm, contain, decide","lang":"bash","code":"# 1. Confirm: did errors start when the new ReplicaSet took traffic?\nkubectl -n payments get rs -l app=payments-api     # new vs old ReplicaSet, age, ready\n\n# 2. Contain: freeze the rollout (with a canary controller, abort it)\nkubectl -n payments rollout pause deploy/payments-api\n\n# 3. What did the migration change, and is anything still running or blocked?\npsql \"$DB_URL\" -c \"select version, description, success\n  from flyway_schema_history order by installed_rank desc limit 3;\"\npsql \"$DB_URL\" -c \"select pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid), left(query, 60)\n  from pg_stat_activity\n  where wait_event_type = 'Lock' or query ilike 'alter table%';\"\n\n# 4a. Migration stuck on locks: cancel it (pid from the query above)\npsql \"$DB_URL\" -c \"select pg_cancel_backend(48213);\"\n\n# 4b. Schema is additive: roll the app back (undo refuses a paused Deployment)\nkubectl -n payments rollout resume deploy/payments-api\nkubectl -n payments rollout undo deploy/payments-api","note":"Pausing stops further progress but moves no traffic back. The rollback is one command because the previous ReplicaSet still exists, yet it is safe only once step 3 shows the old version runs on the current schema."}],"followUps":[{"q":"The migration made an existing column NOT NULL, and inserts from old pods now fail. What is the fastest safe fix?","a":"Make the schema tolerant again instead of rolling anything back: in PostgreSQL `ALTER TABLE payments ALTER COLUMN provider_ref DROP NOT NULL` only changes the catalog, so old pods' inserts succeed within moments; run it with a short `lock_timeout`, since it still needs a brief exclusive lock. Reintroduce the constraint later, once every writer sets the column."},{"q":"When is rolling forward better than rolling back?","a":"When the previous version cannot run safely: the migration was destructive, or the new version already wrote data the old one cannot read, such as a new enum value or event version. Also when the cause is understood and the fix is small enough to pass the normal pipeline within minutes. If the cause is unclear, roll back whenever it is safe; a rushed hotfix that skips the pipeline turns one incident into two."}],"extra":["**Fail at startup:** `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate` stops a pod whose mappings disagree with the schema, so the rollout stalls while old pods serve.","**Transactional DDL differs:** PostgreSQL rolls back a failed migration as a unit; MySQL commits each DDL statement implicitly, leaving partial changes to fix by hand before `flyway repair`.","**Both versions run at once:** during a rolling update or canary, old and new pods share the schema, so compatibility matters for the whole rollout.","**Migrations at startup:** Spring Boot runs Flyway or Liquibase on startup when present; run slow ones as a separate step or Job so probes do not kill pods midway.","**Automated canary analysis:** Argo Rollouts or Flagger compare error rates by version and abort a bad release early."],"redFlags":["Debugs root cause before stopping the rollout or mitigating","rolls back the app without checking that the old version works with the new schema","runs down migrations or restores a backup without weighing the data written since"]}];
